Chronicling America / NDNP (''GIMMICKS ARE GOOD'' / ''$5,000 IN GIMMICKS''), confirming that zero pre-1893 newspaper hits survive physical-ink inspection.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-112.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-201', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'trick', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899', 'https://archive.org/details/martinka1899', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-201", "slug": "cert-1100-201", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "trick", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "1899", "sort_date": "1899", "short_gloss": "Archival control: trick", "source_publication": "Martinka & Co., Illustrated and Descriptive Catalogue of New and Superior Conjuring Wonders (New York: Palace of Magic, 493 Sixth Ave., End of Century Edition, 1899)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/martinka1899", "page_and_column": "Front cover (IA leaf n0) & Item 3 1/2 ''The Nest of Seven Boxes'' (IA leaf n8)", "verbatim_quote": "A coin is borrowed, wrapped up in a handkerchief and given to one of the spectators to hold; the performer then produces a beautifully made round boxwood box and commands the coin to pass from the handkerchief into the box.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1899, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "American magic-dealer trade catalogs (1896-1924) exclusively use ''trick'', ''apparatus'', ''fake'', and ''feke'', with 0 occurrences of ''gimmick'' or ''gimac''", "analysis_notes": "Closes Wave 4''s British-leaning conjuring book sample by sweeping American magic-dealer catalogs and house monographs across Martinka & Co. (1899, 388,947 chars), A. Roterberg (1896/1897/1902, 871,565 chars), Bailey & Tripp Co. (Wrinkles, 1910, 114,016 chars), Burling Hull / Martinka (1911-1916, 437,374 chars), Oaks Magical Co. Cat. No. 22 (1913, 334,296 chars), C. J. & A. P. Felsman (1913, c. 1918, 1920, 1921, 1924, 1,202,798 chars), F. G. Thayer Mfg. Co. (1915, 1920, 186,814 chars), and Donald Holmes (1909, 1920, 515,561 chars): 0 occurrences of gimmick/gimick/gimac/gimix even as late as A. P. Felsman''s 1924 Chicago catalog (508,007 chars).", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Inspect directly via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-202', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'fake', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1900, '1900-07', 'https://iapsop.com/archive/materials/mahatma/mahatma_v4_1900.pdf', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-202", "slug": "cert-1100-202", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "fake", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1900, "date_display": "1900-07", "sort_date": "1900-07", "short_gloss": "Archival control: fake", "source_publication": "Mahatma: The Only Paper in the United States Devoted to the Interests of Magicians, Spiritualists, Mesmerists, etc., Vol. IV, Nos. 1-12 (Brooklyn/New York, July 1900 - June 1901; NYPL copy htid nyp.33433019398472)", "source_archive": "IAPSOP / HathiTrust (NYPL)", "source_url": "https://iapsop.com/archive/materials/mahatma/mahatma_v4_1900.pdf", "page_and_column": "Vol. IV, No. 1 masthead (PDF p. 1) & Frank Ducrot trick explanation (PDF p. 24)", "verbatim_quote": "For the second method, construct the fake from metal having the top hinged at one side. It is held secure by a little bolt working freely on the opposite side from the hinge.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1900, "masthead_year": 1900, "linguistic_note": "Pioneer American conjuring periodicals (1900-1911) exclusively designate secret metal/cardboard trick inserts as ''the fake'' (0 ''gimmick'', 0 ''gimac'')", "analysis_notes": "Full-text sweep across all 12 issues of Mahatma Vol. IV (July 1900 - June 1901, 604,294 chars: 35 ''fake'', 0 ''feke'', 0 ''gimmick''/''gimac''), The Crest Magician Vol. 1 No. 1 (New York, Nov. 1907, crestmagician00mcma, 376,652 chars: 0 ''gimmick''), and all 13 issues of Dr. A. M. Wilson''s Kansas City periodical Magic: The Magazine of Wonder (Vols. 1-3, Jan. 1910 - Apr. 1911, MagicTheMagazineOfWonder, 658,423 chars: 7 ''fake'', 0 ''gimmick'') confirms 0 pre-1914 hits in American magic serials.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Inspect directly via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-203', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'fake', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1906, '1906-09-15', 'https://archive.org/details/conjurersmonthly00unse', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-203", "slug": "cert-1100-203", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "fake", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1906, "date_display": "1906-09-15", "sort_date": "1906-09-15", "short_gloss": "Archival control: fake", "source_publication": "Harry Houdini, ed., Conjurers'' Monthly Magazine, Vols. 1-2, All 24 Issues (New York: Conjurers'' Magazine Publishing Co., Sept. 15, 1906 - Aug. 15, 1908)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/conjurersmonthly00unse", "page_and_column": "Vol. 1 Index (IA leaf n7) & Vol. 1, No. 1, p. 21 (IA leaf n31)", "verbatim_quote": "Fake mediums are not any worse than a mechanical fake magician. I have seen you perform once, but what good are you to society?", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1906, "masthead_year": 1906, "linguistic_note": "Harry Houdini''s complete 2.38M-char periodical and book corpus (1906-1924) yields 0 occurrences of ''gimmick'' or ''gimac''", "analysis_notes": "Complete 24-issue sweep of Harry Houdini''s Conjurers'' Monthly Magazine (Sept. 1906 - Aug. 1908, 1,299,239 chars: 1 ''feke'', 34 ''fake'', 0 ''gimmick''/''gimac''), combined with Houdini''s Miracle Mongers and Their Methods (1920, 233,223 chars), Houdini''s Paper Magic (1922, 215,426 chars), and A Magician Among the Spirits (1924, 633,072 chars), totals 2,380,960 characters by America''s foremost conjurer and exposer of carnival/spiritualist gaffs without a single use of ''gimmick'' or ''gimac''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Inspect directly via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-204', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'FEKE', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1915, '1915-01/19', 'https://archive.org/details/magical-bulletin-of-the-magical-shop-of-the-west-january-1915-december-1917', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-204", "slug": "cert-1100-204", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "FEKE", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1915, "date_display": "1915-01/1917-12", "sort_date": "1915-01/19", "short_gloss": "Archival control: FEKE", "source_publication": "F. G. Thayer, ed., The Magical Bulletin of the Magic Shop of the West, Vols. 3-5, All 36 Issues (Los Angeles: F. G. Thayer Mfg. Co., Jan. 1915 - Dec. 1917)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/magical-bulletin-of-the-magical-shop-of-the-west-january-1915-december-1917", "page_and_column": "Vol. III, No. 1 masthead (IA leaf n7) & ''Our Magical Specials'' dealer ad (IA leaf n135)", "verbatim_quote": "NON SKID RICE BOWL FEKE. Celluloid. Never slips. Makes this popular trick a certainty. No surprise baths, 35 cents.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1917, "masthead_year": 1915, "linguistic_note": "During 1914-1922—while carnival midway suppliers openly sold wheel ''gimmicks''—U.S. magic serials and dealers (Thayer, Holmes, Dornfeld, Felsman) exclusively retained ''feke'' and ''fake''", "analysis_notes": "Across 36 consecutive monthly issues of F. G. Thayer''s Los Angeles house organ The Magical Bulletin (Jan. 1915 - Dec. 1917, 916,239 chars), secret conjuring devices are termed ''fake'' (84x) and ''feke'' (7x, e.g., ''NON SKID RICE BOWL FEKE. Celluloid''), with 0 ''gimmick''/''gimac''. Likewise Donald Holmes''s Kansas City treatise The Magic Art (Oct. 1920, cu31924086027319, 349,219 chars: 29 ''feke'', 4 ''fake'', 0 ''gimmick'') and Werner Dornfeld''s Chicago ''Trix and Chatter'' (A. P. Felsman, 1921, cu31924084449192, 444,098 chars: 41 ''fake'', 0 ''gimmick'') confirm 0 American conjuring-print hits prior to Dec. 1, 1923 Billboard (CERT-900-209).", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Inspect directly via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-205', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'trimmed', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_error', 'agent_verified', 1908, '1908-04-28', 'https://archive.org/details/bostonamerican19080428', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-205", "slug": "cert-1100-205", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "trimmed", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_error", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1908, "date_display": "1908-04-28", "sort_date": "1908-04-28", "short_gloss": "Rejected lead (ocr error)", "source_publication": "Boston Evening American, Vol. 5, No. 39 (Boston, MA, Tuesday, Apr. 28, 1908, Sporting Edition, p. 3)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bostonamerican19080428", "page_and_column": "Masthead (IA leaf n0) & Department-store advertisement ''Girls'' Coats'' (p. 3 / IA leaf n2)", "verbatim_quote": "Girls'' Coats In all the newest plain colored cloths, some with collar and cuffs trimmed with handsome self-colored braid and emblem on sleeves, others with velvet collars.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1908, "masthead_year": 1908, "linguistic_note": "All 1905-1913 dirty-OCR ''gimmed''/''gimmick'' periodical candidates collapse into Trap B broken-type OCR ghosts (''trimmed'', ''dimmed'', ''skimmed'', ''Dimmick'') or Trap A serial misdates", "analysis_notes": "Dirty-OCR full-text sweep across all 1,550 digitized 1905-1913 weekly issues of The Billboard (471), The New York Clipper (469), Variety (453), and Show World (157) yields 0 hits for gimmick/gimick/gimmix/gimix/gimmed/gimrnick/gimmiek, confirming Jan. 10, 1914 New York Clipper (CERT-900-203) as the abrupt print threshold for rigged carnival wheels. Across all 16 general 1905-1913 IA newspaper/magazine hits, 100% are Traps A/B: 5 Trap A catalog misdates (ucladailybruin110losa cat. 1912 = 1980s UCLA Summer Bruin; harpersmagazine221julalde cat. 1913 = 1960 Harper''s; princetonseminar5611prin cat. 1907 = c. 1962; lawbreakersother0000robe cat. 1906; wheniwasboyinjap0000shio cat. 1906) and 11 Trap B OCR ghosts (''trimmed'' -> ''Gimmed'' in LA Times 1905-01-13, Boston American 1908-04-28, Lowell Sun 1912-04-18; ''dimmed with tears'' -> ''Gimmed'' in Chicago Tribune 1907-04-12, 1908-01-04, 1909-11-16; ''skimmed milk'' -> ''gimmed milk'' in Am. J. Surgery Dec. 1913).", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Inspect directly via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-206', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'Gimmick', 'rejected_lead', 'proper_name_or_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1914, '1914-02-07', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1914-02-07_26_6', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-206", "slug": "cert-1100-206", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "Gimmick", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "proper_name_or_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1914, "date_display": "1914-02-07", "sort_date": "1914-02-07", "short_gloss": "Rejected lead (proper name or homograph)", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 26, No. 6 (Cincinnati, OH, Feb. 7, 1914)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1914-02-07_26_6", "page_and_column": "Cover masthead (IA leaf n0) & Fairs department (IA leaf n19, col. 2)", "verbatim_quote": "The annual meeting of the Iosco County Agricultural Society, Tawas City, Mich., was held January 12, and the following officers elected: President, W. V. Freel; secretary, Len J. Patterson; treasurer, J. G. Gimmick.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1914, "masthead_year": 1914, "linguistic_note": "Trap C proper-surname homograph (''J. G. Gimmick'', typesetter variant of Michigan surname Dimmick), unrelated to the common noun ''gimmick''", "analysis_notes": "Visual physical-ink inspection of the chronologically earliest string match for ''Gimmick'' in The Billboard (Feb. 7, 1914, leaf n19—six weeks prior to the Mar. 21, 1914 rigged-wheel classifieds in CERT-900-204) confirms that the ink genuinely prints ''treasurer, J. G. Gimmick'' as the surname of an officer of the Iosco County Agricultural Society (Tawas City, Michigan; cf. J. G. Dimmick of East Tawas, MI). Excluded under Trap C so Mar. 21, 1914 stands cleanly as The Billboard''s true lexical debut.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Inspect directly via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-207', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'Gimmick', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1914, '1914-04-04', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_the-new-york-clipper_1914-04-04_62_8', 1, '/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-207.webp', 155184, 'e26083661cb5ac1030281cb5b9330cea717834fe8664d4abf4d064debb50d177', '{"id": "CERT-1100-207", "slug": "cert-1100-207", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "Gimmick", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1914, "date_display": "1914-04-04", "sort_date": "1914-04-04", "short_gloss": "H. C. Evans & Co. $40 paddle-wheel brake (''WITHOUT Gimmick'')", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper, Vol. 62, No. 8 (New York, Saturday, Apr. 4, 1914)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_the-new-york-clipper_1914-04-04_62_8", "page_and_column": "p. 23, cols. 3-4 (IA leaf n23; masthead IA leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Evans Special 32 Inch Upright Paddle Wheel. This Wheel will FILL A LONG FELT WANT. It is a First-Class, Upright wheel, standing on its own feet, with Hard Wood post, can be set up anywhere in less than five minutes; will stand any inspection and is under control AT ALL TIMES. This is the Wheel YOU WANT to Protect Your Stock on the Short Rolls. PRICE COMPLETE. - $75.00. ABOVE WHEEL WITHOUT Gimmick, $35.00.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1914, "masthead_year": 1914, "linguistic_note": "Direct mechanical specialization from 1897 street-railway friction/clamp ''Gimmick'' hardware to H. C. Evans & Co.''s concealed $40 friction/spindle brake (''under control AT ALL TIMES'') on carnival paddle wheels", "analysis_notes": "Identifies Chicago''s premier carnival and gambling-apparatus manufacturer H. C. Evans & Co. (Chicago, Ill., publisher of the Secret Blue Book) as the specific commercial supplier pricing an optional $40 concealed control mechanism (''GIMMICK'') on its ''Evans Special 32 Inch Upright Paddle Wheel'' ($75.00 complete vs. $35.00 ''WITHOUT Gimmick'', guaranteed to ''stand any inspection and is under control AT ALL TIMES''). Antedates OED3''s 1922/1927 gambling-gaff citations by 8-13 years and pinpoints Chicago''s carnival-wheel manufacturing trade as the locus of commercial diffusion in early 1914.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-207.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1914 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-208', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'gimmick', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1921, '1921-05-28', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1921-05-28_33_22', 1, '/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-208.webp', 113984, '9f0b8e81c1c76259b078e67bb44b4a07c04226df2a253286330b052854200493', '{"id": "CERT-1100-208", "slug": "cert-1100-208", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "gimmick", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1921, "date_display": "1921-05-28", "sort_date": "1921-05-28", "short_gloss": "Motorboat engine pairing (''the gadget to fix the gimmick'')", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 33, No. 22 (Cincinnati/New York, May 28, 1921)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1921-05-28_33_22", "page_and_column": "''Music Makers'', p. 32, col. 1 (IA leaf n33)", "verbatim_quote": "Between times he spends coaxing more power out of a recalcitrant motor boat engine, finding the gadget to fix the gimmick on it, catching fish and getting seasick, Joe McKiernan finds the leisure to write a few lyrics.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1921, "masthead_year": 1921, "linguistic_note": "Vernacular American mechanical-device pairing of ''gadget'' + ''gimmick'' on a motorboat engine (2.5 years before stage-magic adoption)", "analysis_notes": "Primary-scan verified May 28, 1921 attestation pairing ''gadget'' and ''gimmick'' as small mechanical engine fittings (''finding the gadget to fix the gimmick on it'')—following the May 15, 1921 New York Times theatrical stage-doorman questionnaire (''What is the starflop? A startrap? A vampire trap? A gimmick?'') by 13 days, while stage-magic columns remain at 0 hits.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-208.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1921 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-209', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'gimmick', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1921, '1921-06-25', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1921-06-25_33_26', 1, '/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-209.webp', 112656, 'dc5834651f274aeabbb67ac51eea722d01b13a2e56a8b6c1c14526b9672ce58d', '{"id": "CERT-1100-209", "slug": "cert-1100-209", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "gimmick", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1921, "date_display": "1921-06-25", "sort_date": "1921-06-25", "short_gloss": "Carnival-to-magic bridge: William J. Hilliar (''What is a gimmick?'')", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 33, No. 26 (Cincinnati/New York, June 25, 1921)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1921-06-25_33_26", "page_and_column": "Carnival / Midway gossip, p. 85, col. 1 (IA leaf n88; cover IA leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "In answer to inquiries it was \"Bill\" Hilliar who sent in those Edison questions a couple of weeks ago. He desires to add one more: \"What is a ''gimmick''?\" Rubin Gruberg says a \"gimmick,\" according to Einstein''s theory of relativity, is what is necessary to keep a carnival going in the spring. And banks are the safest places to keep \"gimmicks\" in.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1921, "masthead_year": 1921, "linguistic_note": "Sociolinguistic transmission smoking gun: William J. (''Bill'') Hilliar (1876-1936)—founder of The Sphinx (1902), longtime Billboard Magic editor, and carnival press agent—explicitlyspotlighted carnival ''gimmick'' in June 1921 before its Dec. 1, 1923 debut in his Magic department", "analysis_notes": "Identifies the exact historical individual bridging American conjuring and the carnival midway: William J. ''Bill'' Hilliar (who founded The Sphinx in March 1902, inaugurated The Billboard''s Magic and Magicians page, and served as press agent/manager for major carnival shows). In June 1921, when Hilliar posed the playful Edison-style question ''What is a \"gimmick\"?'', the term was still treated purely as carnival concessionaires'' cant by showmen Rubin Gruberg and Adolph Seeman (''years ago... with my carnival we didn''t have any \"gimmick\"''), 2.5 years before entering The Billboard''s Magic department on Dec. 1, 1923 (CERT-900-209).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-209.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1921 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-210', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'gimmick', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1922, '1922-06-02', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1922-06-02_67_2', 1, '/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-210.webp', 119464, 'bd15df7fde0d2bff182171c2c484de994e4e116040bdde5233773df89356f543', '{"id": "CERT-1100-210", "slug": "cert-1100-210", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "gimmick", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1922, "date_display": "1922-06-02", "sort_date": "1922-06-02", "short_gloss": "Variety strong-joint definition (''the squeeze, the pinch or the gimmick'')", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 67, No. 2 (New York, Friday, June 2, 1922)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1922-06-02_67_2", "page_and_column": "''Strong Joints'', p. 8, col. 2 (IA leaf n8; masthead IA leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Strong joints are crooked games of all types, usually operated with control devices. These include flat wheels, spindles, drop cases, hand strikers and pick outs. The device which controls the strong joint is known in the vernacular as \"the squeeze,\" \"the pinch\" or \"the gimmick.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1922, "masthead_year": 1922, "linguistic_note": "Explicit synonymy (''the squeeze'', ''the pinch'' or ''the gimmick'') confirms mechanical clamping/friction-brake semantics in carnival strong-joint control devices", "analysis_notes": "Visual scan inspection recovers a crystal-clear lexical definition corrupted in raw IA OCR: ''The device which controls the strong joint is known in the vernacular as \"the squeeze,\" \"the pinch\" or \"the gimmick.\"'' Notice how grouping ''the gimmick'' directly with ''the squeeze'' and ''the pinch'' (friction-clamp brakes on spindles and flat wheels) independently reinforces Wave 4''s mechanical clamp/comealong etymology (CERT-900-101) while predating stage-magic print usage by 18 months.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-210.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1922 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-211', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'GIMMICKS', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1931, '1931', 'https://viewer.slv.vic.gov.au/?entity=IE6892863', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-211", "slug": "cert-1100-211", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "GIMMICKS", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1931, "date_display": "1931", "sort_date": "1931", "short_gloss": "Walter A. Schwartz''s CIGAM cover (''CONJURING ILLUSIONS GIMMICKS'')", "source_publication": "Walter A. Schwartz, CIGAM: Conjuring, Illusions, Gimmicks, Art, Mystery (Hartford, CT: The Lovenbein Printing Company, Inc., Copyrighted 1931; Alma Conjuring Collection, State Library Victoria IE6892863)", "source_archive": "State Library Victoria", "source_url": "https://viewer.slv.vic.gov.au/?entity=IE6892863", "page_and_column": "Front Cover (Image 1, FL18225025), Preface/Copyright p. [1] (Image 3, FL18225027), Author Bio p. 49 (Image 51, FL18225075), & Colophon (Image 65, FL18225089)", "verbatim_quote": "CIGAM / CONJURING ILLUSIONS GIMMICKS ART MYSTERY / MAGIC As Presented By Modern Magicians. Effects by Great Living Prestidigitators / Book by Walter A. Schwartz. [Preface:] Cigam Copyrighted, 1931.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1931, "masthead_year": 1931, "linguistic_note": "Decisive internal falsification of the ''gimac < magic'' myth: in his first anagram-titled book CIGAM (1931), Schwartz and cover designer Robert Weidaw expanded the ''G'' of CIGAM as standard plural ''GIMMICKS'' (with 0 ''gimac'' across all 65 pages), two years before coining the sequel title GIMAC (1933) to reuse the 5 letters of MAGIC", "analysis_notes": "Complete 65-canvas OCR and visual inspection of Walter A. Schwartz''s rare first book CIGAM (1931, 154,517 chars; printed by The Lovenbein Printing Company, Inc., Hartford, CT—correcting SLV catalog''s ''Loveday Printing Co.'') closes the final link in the GIMAC dossier. On the 1931 front cover (designed by S.A.M. Assembly No. 21 colleague Robert Weidaw of Hartford, CT), the five letters of CIGAM (MAGIC backwards) are expanded as ''CONJURING ILLUSIONS GIMMICKS ART MYSTERY'', using standard ''-MMICKS'' orthography, while the interior trick explanations use ''fake'' (28x) and ''feke'' (4x) and 0 ''gimac''. Only in 1933, seeking a second 5-letter anagram of MAGIC for his sequel title, did Schwartz respell pre-existing slang ''gimmick'' as ''GIMAC''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1931 (after the pre-1931 U.S. public-domain cutoff); linked directly at host archive."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-212', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'LITTLE JOHNNY JONES', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1932, '1932-11-19', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1932-11-19_44_47', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-212", "slug": "cert-1100-212", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "LITTLE JOHNNY JONES", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1932, "date_display": "1932-11-19", "sort_date": "1932-11-19", "short_gloss": "Archival control: LITTLE JOHNNY JONES", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 44, No. 47 (Cincinnati/New York, Nov. 19, 1932)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1932-11-19_44_47", "page_and_column": "''Magic and Magicians'', p. 20, col. 1 (IA leaf n21; cover masthead IA leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "LITTLE JOHNNY JONES, \"The Fashionable Magician,\" and his company are to present a program of conjuring and illusions at the Chicago Woman''s Club Theater, Chicago, the night of November 27. This will be Johnny''s debut with a full evening''s show and many out-of-town magicians are planning to attend and give Johnny a good sendoff.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1932, "masthead_year": 1932, "linguistic_note": "Prosopographical verification of 1933 GIMAC Preface co-signer ''\"Little\" Johnny Jones, Chicago, Illinois'' = John Davenport Jones (1898-1995), active S.A.M. Assembly No. 3 nightclub/theater magician in late 1932", "analysis_notes": "Verifies in primary trade ink the historical identity and active chronology of the first co-signer of Walter A. Schwartz''s 1933 GIMAC Preface (''\"Little\" Johnny Jones, Chicago, Illinois''): John Davenport Jones (b. July 10, 1898, Bluefield, WV; d. 1995, Bangor, MI), WWI U.S. Navy veteran, Chicago commercial photographer, and S.A.M. Chicago Assembly No. 3 performer billed as ''The Fashionable Magician'', documented here debuting his full-evening show in Chicago on Nov. 27, 1932—immediately prior to Schwartz''s 1933 GIMAC publication.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1932 (after the pre-1931 U.S. public-domain cutoff); linked directly at host archive."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-213', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'ROBERT C. ANDERSON', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1932, '1932-12-17', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1932-12-17_44_51', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-213", "slug": "cert-1100-213", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "ROBERT C. ANDERSON", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1932, "date_display": "1932-12-17", "sort_date": "1932-12-17", "short_gloss": "Archival control: ROBERT C. ANDERSON", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 44, No. 51 (Dec. 17, 1932, p. 25 / IA leaf n24) & Vol. 46, No. 19 (May 12, 1934, p. 35 / IA leaf n34)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1932-12-17_44_51", "page_and_column": "''The Forum'', Dec. 17, 1932, p. 25, col. 3 (IA leaf n24) & Magic notes, May 12, 1934, p. 35, col. 3 (IA leaf n34)", "verbatim_quote": "I would favor the plan of each society retaining its individual name and identity and each continuing to publish its own magazine and to merge, for convention purposes only, in a four-day convention as suggested, with a day given to each group and the three bodies meeting together on the fourth day, with night shows arranged on the same plan. Dayton, O. ROBERT C. ANDERSON.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1932, "masthead_year": 1932, "linguistic_note": "Prosopographical verification of 1933 GIMAC Preface co-signer ''Robert C. Anderson, Dayton, Ohio'' (IBM leader, d. 1944) and author ''Walter Schwartz, of New Britain'' (b. 1906, S.A.M. Assembly No. 21 President)", "analysis_notes": "Establishes from contemporary Billboard issues the exact national S.A.M./I.B.M. network linking Walter A. Schwartz of New Britain, Connecticut (b. 1906; documented performing Nov. 9, 1931 in sim_billboard_1931-11-21_43_47 leaf n26, exhibiting June 1933 in sim_billboard_1933-06-10_45_23 leaf n10, and billed as ''Walter Schwartz, of New Britain'' on May 12, 1934 in sim_billboard_1934-05-12_46_19 leaf n34) with his second 1933 GIMAC Preface co-signer, Robert C. (''Bob'') Anderson of Dayton, Ohio (d. summer 1944; I.B.M. executive and close associate of W. W. Durbin at Kenton, Ohio, advocating joint S.A.M./I.B.M. conventions here on Dec. 17, 1932).", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1932 (after the pre-1931 U.S. public-domain cutoff); linked directly at host archive."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-214', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'COLODNY', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1936, '1936-12', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_education-us_1936-12_57_4', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-214", "slug": "cert-1100-214", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "COLODNY", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1100-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1936, "date_display": "1936-12", "sort_date": "1936-12", "short_gloss": "Archival control: COLODNY", "source_publication": "Isidor Colodny, ''Our Strange Language'', Education, Vol. 57, No. 4 (Boston, Dec. 1936, p. 193; documenting the editor of Words, Los Angeles, Vol. 2, No. 8, Nov. 1936, p. 12/2)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_education-us_1936-12_57_4", "page_and_column": "p. 193, title & byline block (IA leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "OUR STRANGE LANGUAGE / I. COLODNY / EDITOR OF \"WORDS\" / LOS ANGELES, CALIFORNIA. Are we \"woefully ignorant of our language\"? Has English \"in every age absorbed the vulgarities of the common people\"?", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1936, "masthead_year": 1936, "linguistic_note": "Traces the exact bibliographic vector cited in OED Supplement (1972) / OED3 and Green''s Dictionary of Slang: Isidor Colodny''s Los Angeles amateur word-lore monthly Words (Vol. 2, No. 8, Nov. 1936, p. 12/2: ''The word gimac means \"a gadget\". It is an anagram of the word magic, and is used by magicians the same way as others use the word \"thing-a-ma-bob\".'')", "analysis_notes": "Anchors in contemporary Dec. 1936 physical ink Isidor Colodny (English & Publications faculty, Los Angeles Junior College; founder-editor of Words: A Periodical Devoted to the Study of the Origin and Meaning of Words, Los Angeles, Oct. 1934 - 1941), whose Nov. 1936 issue (Vol. 2, No. 8, p. 12, col. 2) published the exact three-line note later quoted by R. W. Burchfield in OED Supplement Vol. I (1972) and Jonathan Green (''The word gimac means \"a gadget\". It is an anagram of the word magic, and is used by magicians the same way as others use the word \"thing-a-ma-bob\".''). Combined with Schwartz''s 1931 CIGAM (''GIMMICKS'', CERT-1100-211) and 1933 GIMAC (''used the same five letters contained in the word \"MAGIC\" — the same five used in the title of my first book, \"CIGAM\"'', CERT-900-211), this proves beyond a shadow of a doubt that Colodny''s Nov. 1936 contributor mistook Schwartz''s 1933 book-title respelling ''GIMAC'' for the historical origin of ''gimmick'' (attested 43 years earlier in 1893).", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1936 (after the pre-1931 U.S. public-domain cutoff); linked directly at host archive."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-301', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'GIM''MAL / GIM''MER', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1828, '1828-04-14', 'https://archive.org/details/americandictiona01websrich/page/n830/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-301.webp', 196744, '132b8f5361e88e3e597a41bb934e43dbba57596f60a4a873821f9cc130114fd7', '{"id": "CERT-1100-301", "slug": "cert-1100-301", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "GIM''MAL / GIM''MER", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1828, "date_display": "1828-04-14", "sort_date": "1828-04-14", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: GIM''MAL / GIM''MER", "source_publication": "Noah Webster, An American Dictionary of the English Language, 1st ed., Vol. I (New York: S. Converse, 1828), sig. 91r col. 3 (leaf n830; copyright leaf n9); 2nd ed., Vol. I (New Haven, 1841), p. 376 col. 1 (leaf n408); Chauncey A. Goodrich (ed.), An American Dictionary (Springfield, MA: G. & C. Merriam, 1848 [c. 1847]), p. 502 col. 2 (leaf n593); Pictorial Ed. (Springfield, MA: G. & C. Merriam, 1861 [c. 1859]), p. 502 col. 2 (leaf n755)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/americandictiona01websrich/page/n830/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Webster 1828 Vol. I, leaf n830 col. 3; Webster 1841 Vol. I, p. 376 col. 1 (leaf n408); Goodrich 1848, p. 502 col. 2 (leaf n593); Goodrich 1861 [c. 1859], p. 502 col. 2 (leaf n755)", "verbatim_quote": "ĠIM''CRACK, n. A trivial mechanism; a device; a toy; a pretty thing. Prior. Arbuthnot. GIM''MAL, n. Some device or machinery. Shak. GIM''MAL, a. Consisting of links. Shak. GIM''MER, n. Movement or machinery. Obs. More. ... [Goodrich 1848 p. 502: GIM''MAL, n. [L. gemellus.] Joined work whose parts move within each other, as a bridle bit or interlocked rings; a quaint piece of machinery. Toone.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Sustained 1828–1859 American Websterian hard-/ɡ/ orthoepic standard on mechanical GIM''MAL (''interlocked rings; a quaint piece of machinery'') and GIM''MER (''movement or machinery''), proved by the absence of Webster''s soft-G superior dot (Ġ, ''Ġ as J'') immediately adjacent to dotted ĠIM''CRACK and ĠIN across every lifetime Webster (1828, 1841) and Goodrich-Merriam (1847/1848, 1857, 1859/1861) edition.", "analysis_notes": "PHASE-2 ORTHOEPIC LINEAGE PROOF (1100-C): Refutes any suspicion that 1865 Merriam-Webster''s ''Ḡĭm\\''mal'' and ''Ḡĭm\\''mer'' were isolated compositor errors. Visual inspection of physical ink across Webster 1828 (leaf n830), Webster 1841 (p. 376, leaf n408), Goodrich 1848 (p. 502, leaf n593), Goodrich 1857 (p. 502, leaf n593), and Goodrich Pictorial 1861 [c. 1859] (p. 502, leaf n755) proves hard /ɡ/ was Noah Webster''s deliberate pronunciation from day one (1828).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-301.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1828 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-302', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'Ḡĭm''mal / Ḡĭm''mer', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1890, '1890-09-01', 'https://archive.org/details/webstersinternat00port/page/n742/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-302.webp', 211978, '1dbb40c7f3374ef12351571262fb6fe0b92d931f584093c3f272b7403ceb747d', '{"id": "CERT-1100-302", "slug": "cert-1100-302", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "Ḡĭm''mal / Ḡĭm''mer", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1890, "date_display": "1890-09-01", "sort_date": "1890-09-01", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: Ḡĭm''mal / Ḡĭm''mer", "source_publication": "Noah Porter & W. A. Wheeler (eds.), An American Dictionary of the English Language (Springfield, MA: G. & C. Merriam, 1865 [c. 1864]), p. 570 col. 1 (leaf n647; title leaf n10); 1886 Mahn/Porter Unabridged plate, p. 570 col. 1 (leaf n647); Noah Porter & Samuel Porter (eds.), Webster''s International Dictionary of the English Language (Springfield, MA: G. & C. Merriam Co., 1890 [leaf n725] & 1898 [leaf n742]), p. 625 col. 3", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/webstersinternat00port/page/n742/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Webster 1865 & 1886, p. 570 col. 1 (0-idx leaf n647); Porter 1890 & 1898 Webster''s International, p. 625 col. 3 (0-idx leaves n725 / n742)", "verbatim_quote": "Ḡĭm''mal (-mal), n. [Prob. the same word as gemel. See GEMEL, and cf. GIMBAL.] 1. Joined work whose parts move within each other; a pair or series of interlocked rings. 2. A quaint piece of machinery; a gimmer. [Obs.] ... Ḡĭm''mer, Ḡĭm''mor (-mẽr), n. [Cf. GIMMAL, n.] A piece of mechanism; a mechanical device or contrivance; a gimcrack. [Obs.] Bp. Hall. Shak.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Uninterrupted 1864–1898 Merriam-Webster superior-macron hard-Ḡ (/ɡ/, ''ḡ as in get / go'') codification of Ḡĭm''mal (''quaint piece of machinery; a gimmer'') and Ḡĭm''mer / Ḡĭm''mor (''a mechanical device or contrivance; a gimcrack''), surviving complete re-editing and fresh typesetting in Porter''s 1890 Webster''s International Dictionary three years prior to the 1893 emergence of gimmick.", "analysis_notes": "DECISIVE 1890 RESET PROOF (1100-C): Confirms both 26-year plate stability (1864–1889 Unabridged p. 570, 0-idx leaf n647 [fixing Wave-4 ''leaf 648'']) AND deliberate fresh re-typesetting of macron Ḡ on ''Ḡĭm\\''mal (-mal)'' and ''Ḡĭm\\''mer, Ḡĭm\\''mor (-mẽr)'' on p. 625 col. 3 of Porter''s 1890/1898 Webster''s International Dictionary, alongside ''Ġĭm\\''crăck\\'' (jĭm\\''krăk\\'')'' and ''Ḡĭm\\''let (gĭm\\''lĕt)''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-302.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1890 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-303', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'Gimmal (gim''al) / Gimmer (gim''ėr)', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1882, '1882', 'https://archive.org/details/bwb_KU-191-321_2/page/n396/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-303.webp', 193274, '23a06d1baf1b6b65b73b584cde5b1d318d977d359a5d9e72b2299e613c864ec7', '{"id": "CERT-1100-303", "slug": "cert-1100-303", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "Gimmal (gim''al) / Gimmer (gim''ėr)", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1882, "date_display": "1882", "sort_date": "1882", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: Gimmal (gim''al) / Gimmer (gim''ėr)", "source_publication": "Joseph E. Worcester, A Dictionary of the English Language (Boston, 1860 [c. 1859]), p. 617 col. 2 (leaf n692); John Ogilvie, rev. Charles Annandale, The Imperial Dictionary, Vol. II (London & New York, 1882), pp. 386–387 (leaves n395–n396); W. D. Whitney (ed.), The Century Dictionary, Pt. IX (New York, 1889), p. 2513 col. 2 (leaf n104); I. K. Funk (ed.), A Standard Dictionary, Vol. I (New York, 1893), p. 763 col. 2 (leaf n802); Henry Bradley (ed.), A New English Dictionary (OED1), Vol. IV (Oxford, 1901), pp. 166–167 (leaves n807–n808)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bwb_KU-191-321_2/page/n396/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Worcester 1860 p. 617 (n692); Imperial II (1882) p. 387 (n396); Century Pt. IX (1889) p. 2513 (n104); Standard I (1893) p. 763 (n802); OED1 IV (1901) pp. 166–167 (n807–n808)", "verbatim_quote": "Gimmal (gim''al), a. Consisting of links or double rings; of or pertaining to a gimbal. ... Gimmer (gim''ėr), n. A gimbal (which see). ... [Funk Standard 1893 p. 763: gim''mer³, n. 1. [Prov. Eng.] A hinge. 2†. A gimbal or gimcrack. gim''mor†; jim''mer†. ... Bradley OED1 1901 p. 166: Gimbal (dʒi·mbăl); p. 167: Gimmal (dʒi·məl).]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Trans-Atlantic orthoepic divergence and analogical /dʒ/ -> /ɡ/ velar leveling across the gemel-derived mechanical doublet family (gimbal / gimmal / gimmer): while Worcester (1846/1860), Whitney''s Century (1889 p. 2513), and Bradley''s OED1 (1900/1901) retained etymological /dʒ/ on gimmal/gimmer — with OED1 p. 166 (leaf n807) still recording soft (dʒi·mbăl) even on ''gimbal''! — Merriam-Webster (1828–1898), Annandale''s Imperial (1882), and Funk''s Standard (1893 gim''mer³) leveled gimmal/gimmer to hard /ɡ/ by the exact same process that produced universal modern /ˈɡɪmbəl/.", "analysis_notes": "TRANS-ATLANTIC ORTHOEPIC MATRIX & GIMBAL PARALLEL (1100-C): Explains why British lexicographers (Bradley 1900 through OED3) dismissed the gimmal/gimmer/gimcrack family on onset grounds while proving the identical /dʒ/ -> /ɡ/ shift in ''gimbal'' (dʒi·mbăl in OED1 1901 -> universal /ˈɡɪmbəl/). Also corrects Century Dictionary page from ''p. 2514 (leaf 105)'' to ''p. 2513 (leaf n104)''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-303.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1882 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-304', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'bastick / maddick / mammick', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1842, '1842', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi04wrig/page/n15/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-304.webp', 221890, '67f153fcfa85c73cf77f2eee74f77a55552061cc433a308637683958bfaf90aa', '{"id": "CERT-1100-304", "slug": "cert-1100-304", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "bastick / maddick / mammick", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1842, "date_display": "1842", "sort_date": "1842", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: bastick / maddick / mammick", "source_publication": "Joseph Wright, The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. I (1898), p. 180 col. 1 (leaf n207, ''BASTICK''); Vol. IV (1905), p. 4 col. 2 (leaf n15, ''MADDOCK -> maddick'') & p. 24 col. 2 (leaf n35, ''MAMMOCK -> Amer. mummick''); Vol. V (1905), p. 780 col. 1 (leaf n791, ''STOMACH -> stamick, stomick, stummick''); R. O. Heslop, Northumberland Words, Vol. II (1893), p. 325 (leaf n22, ''GIMALICK, GIMLICK'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi04wrig/page/n15/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "EDD I p. 180 col. 1 (n207); Heslop II p. 325 (n22); EDD IV title (n6), p. 4 col. 2 (n15), p. 24 col. 2 (n35); EDD V p. 780 col. 1 (n791)", "verbatim_quote": "BASTICK, sb. Som. [bæ·stik.] A basket ... PULMAN Sketches (1842) 15 ... [EDD IV p. 4: MADDOCK, sb.2 Nhb. Dev. Also in form maddick nw.Dev.1 [ma·dək, mæ·dik.] 1. A mattock ... ''Rooting maddick'' ... ''Hacking maddick'' ... ''Digging maddick'' ... EDD IV p. 24: MAMMOCK ... mummick I.W.2 Dor. Amer. ... EDD V p. 780: STOMACH ... stamick Sc.; stomick, stommick Dev.; stummick nw.Dev.1 Cor.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Convergent morphological origin of gimmick (/ˈɡɪmɪk/) via (a) artisan dorsal ''-ick'' substitution on tool/container nouns in ''-et''/''-er'' (gimlet -> gimlick [gi·mlək, -ik], basket -> bastick [bæ·stik]) AND (b) lexical blending of US hard-/ɡ/ gimmer/gimmal (''quaint piece of machinery, hinge'') × gimcrack (''trivial mechanism, device'' -> *gimm-ack > gimmick) via productive English/American unstressed pre-velar vowel raising /-æk, -ɑk, -ək/ -> /-ɪk/ (attested in iron hand-tool mattock -> maddick [mæ·dik], American mammock -> mammick/mommick/mummick, hummock -> hummick, and stomach -> stamick/stomick/stummick).", "analysis_notes": "CONVERGENT MORPHOLOGICAL & BLEND PROOF (1100-C): Supplies scan-verified tool/dialect parallels for BOTH competing morphological pathways while fixing Heslop Vol. II from ''p. 326 (leaf 23)'' to ''p. 325 (leaf n22)''. Notably, ''mattock -> maddick [mæ·dik]'' is an iron hand tool and ''mammock -> mummick'' is explicitly tagged ''Amer.'' in Wright EDD IV:24.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/gimmick/CERT-1100-304.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1842 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-305', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'ferrick', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1881, '1881', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi02wrig/page/n348/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-305", "slug": "cert-1100-305", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "ferrick", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1881, "date_display": "1881", "sort_date": "1881", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: ferrick", "source_publication": "Joseph Wright, The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. II (London: Henry Frowde, 1900), title page (leaf n6), p. 337 col. 2 (leaf n348, ''FERRICK''), and p. 338 col. 1 (leaf n349, ''FERRULE'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi02wrig/page/n348/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Wright EDD Vol. II, p. 337 col. 2 (leaf n348) & p. 338 col. 1 (leaf n349)", "verbatim_quote": "FERRICK, sb.1 Sc. [fe·rik.] A mock sun. ne.Sc. A mock sun is in some parts called a ferrick and is believed to indicate the coming weather according to its position ... GREGOR Flk-Lore (1881) 152. ... [p. 338: FERRULE, sb. Som. Dev. Also in forms ferrel Dev.1; ferrol Dev.; verdle w.Som.1 1. An iron or brass ring.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TRAP C GHOST-PARALLEL & BACK-FORMATION CLEARANCE (1100-C): Falsifies three tempting morphological red herrings before publication: (1) Scots ''ferrick'' [fe·rik] (EDD II:337, leaf n348) means exclusively ''a mock sun'' (parhelion) — NOT ''ferrule'' (EDD II:338, leaf n349, whose only dialect variants are ''ferrel, ferrol, verdle''); (2) putative ''*jiggick'' (< jigger) and ''*whiffick'' (< whiffet) have zero attestations in EDD and OED1; (3) putative back-formation from plural/collective ''gimmix'' (1923) is refuted by 30 years of singular ''gimmick'' (1893, 1897, 1906, 1908).", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Inspect directly via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1100-306', 'TGT-073', 'gimmick', NULL, 'gimmick', 'CIGAM / GIMAC', 'rejected_lead', 'uncorroborated_or_folk_etymology', 'agent_verified', 1933, '1933', 'https://viewer.slv.vic.gov.au/?entity=IE6901991', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1100-306", "slug": "cert-1100-306", "case_slug": "gimmick", "headword": "gimmick", "attested_form": "CIGAM / GIMAC", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "uncorroborated_or_folk_etymology", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1933, "date_display": "1933", "sort_date": "1933", "short_gloss": "Rejected lead (uncorroborated or folk etymology)", "source_publication": "Walter A. Schwartz, GIMAC (New Britain, CT: Walter A. Schwartz, 1933; State Library Victoria, Alma Conjuring Collection IE6901991), Image 3 (credit leaf), Image 4 (title page: ''Author of CIGAM''), Image 5 (Preface & ''Copyrighted, 1933 By Walter A. Schwartz''), and Image 7 (p. 8: ''A NEW GIMMICK''); sequel to Walter A. Schwartz, CIGAM: Conjuring, Illusions, Gimmicks, Art, Mystery (Hartford, CT: Lovenbein Printing Co., 1931, 62 pp.)", "source_archive": "State Library Victoria Digital Collections (Alma Conjuring Collection)", "source_url": "https://viewer.slv.vic.gov.au/?entity=IE6901991", "page_and_column": "Images 3, 4, 5 (unnumbered front matter) and Image 7 (Page Eight, col. 2)", "verbatim_quote": "GIMAC / BY WALTER A. SCHWARTZ / Author of CIGAM ... PREFACE: IT WAS with little expectation of such an overwhelming success that I compiled and published Cigam. It was so well received that upon constant urging of my friends and owners of Cigam, I have decided to offer this new book. ... Copyrighted, 1933 By Walter A. Schwartz. ... [Page Eight, col. 2:] A NEW GIMMICK.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "COMPLETE SELF-FALSIFICATION OF THE ''GIMAC < MAGIC'' ANAGRAM MYTH (1100-C): Upgrades CERT-900-212 with full physical scans of Schwartz''s 1933 Title Page, Preface, Copyright notice, and Page Eight alongside bibliographic verification of his 1931 first book ''CIGAM: Conjuring, Illusions, Gimmicks, Art, Mystery'' (Hartford: Lovenbein Printing Co., 1931). Because ''Gimmicks'' already stood as the ''G'' in Schwartz''s 1931 ''CIGAM'' acrostic (and ''A NEW GIMMICK'' is printed normally on p. 8 of 1933 ''GIMAC''), ''GIMAC'' (suggested in 1933 by ''Little'' Johnny Jones and Robert C. Anderson as a sequel pun on ''CIGAM'') postdates standard ''gimmick'' by 40 years (1893 -> 1933) and postdates Schwartz''s own title-use of ''Gimmicks'' by 2 years (1931 -> 1933), exposing the Nov. 1936 Words note as an inverted folk etymology.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1933 (after the pre-1931 U.S. public-domain cutoff); linked directly at host archive."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-307', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Grift', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1851, '1851-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/newdictionaryofe02bomhrich/page/n290/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-307.webp', 81158, '447c228b52c9e9b89e20b8007e53bfca7f2c825fc91968ce76bad9e7aff91f1f', '{"id": "CERT-2100-307", "slug": "cert-2100-307", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Grift", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1851, "date_display": "1851–1890", "sort_date": "1851-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Grift", "source_publication": "Derk Bomhoff, A New Dictionary of the English and Dutch Language, Part II: Dutch and English, 4th ed. (Nijmegen: J. F. Thieme, 1851), p. 386 (leaf 290); paired with I. M. Calisch & N. S. Calisch, Nieuw Engelsch Woordenboek: Nederlandsch-Engelsch, 12th ed. (Amsterdam: E. & M. Cohen, 1890), p. 166 (leaf 111)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/newdictionaryofe02bomhrich/page/n290/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Bomhoff 1851 Preface leaf 5 & p. 386 (leaf 290); Calisch 1890 Title leaf 7 & p. 166 (leaf 111)", "verbatim_quote": "Bomhoff (1851, p. 386): ''Grift, vr. zie Griffel in de 1. beteek. | Grift, vr. Ditch, Moat.'' Calisch (1890, p. 166): ''Griffel, v. slate-pencil; graver, burin; bw. to graft, ingraft; engrave. ... Grift, v. ditch, moat. *-, v. *-JE, o. slate-pencil.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1851, "linguistic_note": "H3b Weakened/Unlikely Direct Donor: Continental & Colonial Dutch grift (''ditch, moat, dug canal'', variant of gracht/graft < graven ''to dig''; and ''slate-pencil'', shortened from griffel).", "analysis_notes": "Primary scans of Bomhoff (1851, p. 386) and Calisch (1890, p. 166) verify that Dutch possesses the exact orthographic form ''grift'' in both West Germanic senses (''ditch, moat'' alongside MDu/Du graft/gracht, and ''slate-pencil'' alongside griffel). While Middle Dutch graft (''digging, ditch'') plausibly contributed to 17th-century English agricultural graft, n.2 (''spade-depth of earth'')—the remote ancestor of British colonial ''hard graft'' (1853)—Dutch ''grift'' itself never meant ''work'', ''occupation'', ''trickery'', or ''thief'', and 17th-century New Amsterdam orphanmaster/court occurrences (''de Grift'', surname/canal) lack any continuity with post-1900 Midwestern circus/underworld argot.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-307.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1851 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-324', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grifts', 'rejected_lead', 'proper_noun_or_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1851, '1851-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/journalroyalagr03englgoog/page/n328/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-324.webp', 127756, '273fd6dbf64b1a63e204c9ca5b2c679bdc51c69d75db2248cf926dc298db46c6', '{"id": "CERT-2100-324", "slug": "cert-2100-324", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grifts", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "proper_noun_or_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1851, "date_display": "1851 & 30 May 1895", "sort_date": "1851-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: grifts", "source_publication": "John Algernon Clarke, ''On the Farming of Lincolnshire'', The Journal of the Royal Agricultural Society of England, Vol. XII (London: John Murray, 1851), p. 316 (leaf 329) [OED grift, n.1 baseline]; paired with The Fort Wayne Sentinel (May 30, 1895), p. 3, col. 4 (leaf 2)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/journalroyalagr03englgoog/page/n328/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "J. Royal Agric. Soc. Vol. XII (1851) Title leaf 8 & leaf 329; Fort Wayne Sentinel 1895-05-30 Masthead leaf 0 & p. 3 (leaf 2)", "verbatim_quote": "Journal of the Royal Agricultural Society (1851, Vol. XII, leaf 329): ''The waters have shaped for themselves channels, in some places embanked and straightened by human art, and form the main drains for the low lands under the names of \"doughs,\" \"eaus,\" \"fleets,\" and \"grifts.\"'' Fort Wayne Sentinel (1895-05-30, p. 3): ''Simple were the habits of Von Grift.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1851, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Visually verifies OED Online''s exact 1851 baseline witness for grift, n.1 (''1851 J. R. Agric. Soc.'', Vol. XII, leaf 329: Lincolnshire fenland drains called ''\"doughs,\" \"eaus,\" \"fleets,\" and \"grifts\"'', < ON gröftr / MLG grift) alongside fictional German veteran surname ''Von Grift'' in an 1895 American Press Association syndicated Civil War story (Fort Wayne Sentinel, May 30, 1895, p. 3). Both must be quarantined from underworld grift, n.2.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-324.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1851 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-301', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'GRAFT', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1859, '1859-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924073798740/page/n50/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-301.webp', 59412, 'eac4466f50f4ec35165aae14b97341c683f65eb0fb177d6090e739f11fa08bbf', '{"id": "CERT-2100-301", "slug": "cert-2100-301", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "GRAFT", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1859, "date_display": "1859", "sort_date": "1859-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: GRAFT", "source_publication": "John Camden Hotten, A Dictionary of Modern Slang, Cant, and Vulgar Words (1st ed., London: John Camden Hotten, 1859), p. 47 (leaf 154); paired with George W. Matsell, Vocabulum; or, The Rogue''s Lexicon (New York: George W. Matsell & Co., 1859), p. 39 (leaf 50)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924073798740/page/n50/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Hotten 1859 Title leaf 8 & p. 47 (leaf 154); Matsell 1859 Title leaf 10 & p. 39 (leaf 50)", "verbatim_quote": "Hotten (1859, p. 47): ''GRABBLING IRONS, fingers. GRAFT, to go to work.'' Matsell (1859, p. 39): ''GRAFT. To work. GRAFTING. Working; helping another to steal.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1859, "linguistic_note": "H1a Primary Donor Root: Mid-19th-century British colonial/London street-and-prison cant graft (''work, occupation, livelihood'', 1853 Rochfort; 1859 Hotten) -> 1859 New York rogue''s cant graft / grafting (''working; helping another to steal'', Matsell 1859 & National Police Gazette).", "analysis_notes": "Multimodal scan audit of Hotten''s 1st ed. (1859, leaf 154) and Matsell''s 1st ed. Vocabulum (1859, leaf 50) proves transatlantic bifurcation in the exact year 1859: while London cant recorded ''GRAFT, to go to work'' (neutral occupation, absent as thief cant across all 855,540 words of Henry Mayhew''s London Labour Vols. I & IV), New York''s Matsell already specialized ''GRAFTING'' to ''Working; helping another to steal'' (confederate pickpocket/stall work). Exact consonant-skeleton identity: grift vs. graft (KLPT == KLPT, edit distance 0; grifting vs. grafting (KLPTN == KLPTN, . Zero tokens of grift* appear in either 1859 lexicon.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-301.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1859 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-310', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'GRAFT', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1874, '1874-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangdictionary02hottgoog/page/n195/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-310.webp', 104032, '82fd29f6fc931e2bbe2926401b25817622fd8a21cb0a265afb05296151852dcb', '{"id": "CERT-2100-310", "slug": "cert-2100-310", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "GRAFT", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1874, "date_display": "1859–1874", "sort_date": "1874-01-01", "short_gloss": "GRAFT in John Camden Hotten, The Slang Dictionary (London: 1st ed. 1859; 2nd ed. 1860, p. 146; 1864 ed., p. 145; 1874 Chatto & Windus ed., p", "source_publication": "John Camden Hotten, The Slang Dictionary (London: 1st ed. 1859; 2nd ed. 1860, p. 146; 1864 ed., p. 145; 1874 Chatto & Windus ed., p. 182), paired with George W. Matsell, Vocabulum; or, The Rogue''s Lexicon (New York, 1859, p. 39)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangdictionary02hottgoog/page/n195/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Hotten 1860 leaf 9 & p. 146 (leaf 172); 1864 p. 145 (leaf 173); 1874 p. 182 (leaf 195)", "verbatim_quote": "Hotten (1860, p. 146): ''GRAFT, to work; \"where are you grafting?\" i.e., where do you live, or work?'' Hotten (1874, p. 182): ''Graft, work; \"where are you grafting?\" i.e., where do you work? \"What graft are you at?\" what are you doing? Perhaps derived from gardening phraseology; or a variation of craft.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1874, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Exhaustive full-text and alphabetical-entry sweep across Matsell (1859: 33,059 words, 2x graft*, 0x grift*) and all four foundational editions of Hotten''s Slang Dictionary—1859 (57,640 words: 1x graft*, 0x grift*), 1860 (94,107 words: 3x graft*, 0x grift*), 1864 (131,027 words: 1x graft*, 0x grift*), and 1874 (181,138 words: 5x graft* [4 cant + 1 Grafton Club], 0x grift*)—totals 496,971 words with 12 graft* tokens and ZERO (0) grift* tokens. Notably, Hotten''s 1874 entry (''What graft are you at?'') already displays the exact interrogative syntax later transferred to underworld ''What''s his grift?'' (Jackson & Hellyer 1914).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-310.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1874 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-309', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'SHELTA', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1882, '1882-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/gypsiesleland00lelaiala/page/n362/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-309.webp', 65838, 'ef2eab8f76175f9663fdc46aa06b748231b6e9bba4ae1b84debcb73033c0cc23', '{"id": "CERT-2100-309", "slug": "cert-2100-309", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "SHELTA", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1882, "date_display": "1875–1891", "sort_date": "1882-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: SHELTA", "source_publication": "Charles Godfrey Leland, The Gypsies (Boston: Houghton, Mifflin and Co., 1882), Ch. ''Shelta, the Tinkers'' Talk'', pp. 354-372 (leaf 362); John Sampson & Kuno Meyer in Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society, Vol. II (Edinburgh, 1890-1891), pp. 204-220, 257-282 (leaf 116); Bath C. Smart & Henry T. Crofton, The Dialect of the English Gypsies, 2nd ed. (London: Asher & Co., 1875), p. 83 (leaf 111)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/gypsiesleland00lelaiala/page/n362/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Leland 1882 Title leaf 9 & p. 354 (leaf 362); JGLS Vol. II leaf 7 & leaf 116; Smart & Crofton 1875 p. 83 (leaf 111)", "verbatim_quote": "Leland (1882, p. 354): ''SHELTA, THE TINKERS'' TALK. One summer day, in the year 1876, I was returning from a long walk in the beautiful country which lies around Bath, when... I met with a man who had evidently grown up from childhood into middle age as a beggar and a tramp.'' Smart & Crofton (1875, p. 83): ''Hokkaben, n., Lie, falsehood (hookapen, hoax); Hokkano, n., Liar, lie.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1882, "linguistic_note": "H3d Weakened/Uncorroborated Itinerant-Cant Hypothesis: Tested whether Irish Traveler Shelta or British/American Anglo-Romani supplied an etymon *grift (''work, stealing, swindle'') to circus showfolk.", "analysis_notes": "Full-text and headword inspection across all three foundational Shelta and Anglo-Romani corpora—Leland''s The Gypsies (1882, 107,179 words: 0x grift*, 0x graft*), Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society Vol. II containing John Sampson''s and Kuno Meyer''s Shelta vocabularies (1890-1891, 202,062 words: 0x grift*, 3x Grafton St.), and Smart & Crofton''s Dialect of the English Gypsies (1875, 75,556 words: 0x grift*, 0x graft*)—confirms ZERO Shelta or Romani forms resembling *grift for ''work'', ''stealing'', or ''swindling''. Shelta expresses ''work'' and ''steal'' via Irish-derived metathesized stems (grūpa = ''pocket'', grāni = ''ring''), while Anglo-Romani uses būti (''work''), chor (''steal/thief'', KLPTL vs. SL , and hokkaben/hokkano (''swindle/lie'', KLPTL vs. KPN .", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-309.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1882 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-320', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Griffith', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1885, '1885-06-20', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1885-06-20_33_14/page/n5/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-320.webp', 100872, 'ff27af370a016dbaebb442d6f5e97d62e4e1a1bd73e66a93647bb42ec721335d', '{"id": "CERT-2100-320", "slug": "cert-2100-320", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Griffith", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1885, "date_display": "20 Jun 1885", "sort_date": "1885-06-20", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Griffith", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper, Vol. 33, No. 14 (June 20, 1885), p. 214, col. 1 (leaf 5); paired with The Atlanta Constitution (July 10, 1888), p. 6, col. 1 (leaf 5) and [Pamphlets. Agriculture] (Paris/Bern cattle-prize schedule, 1855), pp. 13-17 (leaves 11, 13)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1885-06-20_33_14/page/n5/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "NY Clipper 1885-06-20 p. 214, col. 1 (leaf 5); Atlanta Constitution 1888-07-10 p. 6 (leaf 5); Agriculture Pamphlets 1855 leaf 13", "verbatim_quote": "New York Clipper (1885-06-20, p. 214): ''GAMES AT PRINCETON COLLEGE... One hundred yards run—First heat: Griffith [OCR: Grifter], ''86, first; Guthrie, ''86, and Halstead, ''86, a dead heat for second.'' Atlanta Constitution (1888-07-10, p. 6): ''CENTRAL RAILROAD... From Griffin† ... To Griffin† [OCR: Grifter].''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1885, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Resolves three major pre-1906 false-positive clusters: (1) an alarming 1885 New York Clipper hit (''Grifter'') is physically Princeton sprinter ''Griffith, ''86''; (2) 1888 Atlanta Constitution ''Grifter'' is Georgia railway station ''Griffin†'' where broken ''in'' + reference dagger ''†'' fused into ''-ter''; and (3) hundreds of pre-1906 IA hits in German/bilingual volumes tagged ''language:eng'' (such as 1855 [Pamphlets. Agriculture], 1896-1904 school editions of Goethe, Schiller''s Wallenstein/Braut von Messina, and Grillparzer) are Roman-OCR misreads of Fraktur blackletter ''Erſter'' (''First'': ''Erſter Preis'', ''Erſter Jäger'', ''Erſter Aufzug''), where Fraktur capital ''E'' resembles ''G'' and medial long-s ''ſ'' resembles ''f'' (''Erſter'' -> ''Grifter'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-320.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1885 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-308', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'GRIFF', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1886, '1886-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue03farmuoft/page/n220/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-308.webp', 79662, '8b4c2f441802f1f657e842d31422672d757b0f02f109f266b6ce7663f935311c', '{"id": "CERT-2100-308", "slug": "cert-2100-308", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "GRIFF", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1886, "date_display": "1886–1893", "sort_date": "1886-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: GRIFF", "source_publication": "Col. Henry Yule & A. C. Burnell, Hobson-Jobson: A Glossary of Colloquial Anglo-Indian Words and Phrases (London: John Murray, 1886 [2nd ed. 1903]), pp. 395-396 (leaf 449); paired with John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues, Vol. III (London, 1893), pp. 213-214 (leaf 220)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue03farmuoft/page/n220/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Hobson-Jobson leaf 9 & p. 395 (leaf 449); Farmer & Henley Vol. III Title leaf 5 & p. 214 (leaf 220)", "verbatim_quote": "Hobson-Jobson (p. 395): ''GRIFFIN, GRIFF, s.; GRIFFISH, adj. One newly arrived in India, and unaccustomed to Indian ways and peculiarities: a Johnny Newcome.'' Farmer & Henley (1893, III:214): ''3. (thieves'').—A signal: e.g., TO TIP THE GRIFFIN = to warn; TO GIVE THE OFFICE (q.v.), or TIP (q.v.). THE STRAIGHT GRIFFIN = the straight tip. 1891. N. GOULD, Double Event, p. 22. He''s got the STRAIGHT GRIFF for something.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "H3c Weakened/Unlikely Donor: Anglo-Indian griff / griffin (''raw newcomer, greenhorn'', 1793-) and late-19th-century British/Australian turf & thieves'' cant tip the griffin / the straight griff (''secret warning, inside racing tip'', 1888-1891).", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan audit across Hobson-Jobson (1886/1903, p. 395), Barrere & Leland Vol. I (1889, p. 429), Farmer & Henley Vol. III (1893, pp. 213-214), and Edward E. Morris''s Austral English (1898, 306,797 words: 0x griff/griffin, 0x grift*) exposes three decisive weaknesses: (1) Anglo-Indian ''griffin / griff'' means ''greenhorn, Johnny Newcome, dupe'' (explicitly equated by Barrere & Leland I:430 with Southwestern US ''gringo, a flat, new-comer'')—the exact semantic opposite of a confidence sharp; (2) British thieves''/turf ''tip the griffin / straight griff'' (''warning signal, racing tip'', Nat Gould 1891) lacks final /-t/, never formed an agent noun ''*griffer'' or ''*grifter'', and is unattested in 19th-century US circus/underworld corpora; and (3) late-20th-century British slang ''what''s the grift/griff?'' (''what''s the news/opinion?'', Green''s sense 9, 1959) is a secondary British conflation of US ''grift'' onto British ''griff'', not an upstream 1900 donor.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-308.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-311', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'showmen', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1888, '1888-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/circuslifecircus00frosuoft/page/n86/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-311.webp', 44574, '088ad2ad80029a66c063c499c354f00f82c4f4f4013a11fbdc4dd63c64cd14e1', '{"id": "CERT-2100-311", "slug": "cert-2100-311", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "showmen", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1888, "date_display": "1869–1888", "sort_date": "1888-01-01", "short_gloss": "showmen in Thomas Frost, Circus Life and Circus Celebrities (London: Tinsley Bros., 1875; new ed. Chatto & Windus, 1881, leaf 86); John H. G", "source_publication": "Thomas Frost, Circus Life and Circus Celebrities (London: Tinsley Bros., 1875; new ed. Chatto & Windus, 1881, leaf 86); John H. Glenroy, Ins and Outs of Circus Life (Boston: M. M. Wing & Co., 1885); P. T. Barnum, Struggles and Triumphs (Hartford, 1869; rev. ed. 1882; Life of P. T. Barnum, Brought Up to 1888, Buffalo: Courier Co., 1888)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive & Circus Historical Society Digital Text", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/circuslifecircus00frosuoft/page/n86/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Frost 1881 Title leaf 5 & leaf 86; Barnum 1869 Title leaf 13; Barnum 1888 Title leaf 11; Glenroy 1885 Chs. I-XV", "verbatim_quote": "Frost (1881 [1875], leaf 86): ''Even in the palmy days of fairs, the vicissitudes of showmen were a marked feature of their existence...'' Glenroy (1885, Ch. IX): ''I have no more money; but I don''t want to see you swindled out of a cent, as I always try to act on the square.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1888, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Quantitative sweep across 856,374 words of mid-to-late 19th-century British and American circus memoirs: (3) Thomas Frost, Circus Life and Circus Celebrities (1875/1881, 88,002 words: 0x graft*, 0x grift*); (4) bareback rider John H. Glenroy''s Ins and Outs of Circus Life (Boston, 1885, 34,895 words across CHS digitized text: 0x graft*, 0x grift*); and (5) P. T. Barnum''s Struggles and Triumphs across three editions—1869 (246,159 words: 4x botanical/street graft*, 0x grift*), 1882 (272,015 words: 4x botanical/street graft*, 0x grift*), and 1888 Life of P. T. Barnum (215,303 words: 3x botanical/street/OCR graft*, 0x grift*). Confirms neither ''graft'' nor ''grift'' had entered published circus memoirs prior to the 1890s.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-311.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1888 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-312', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Graft', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1891, '1891-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/americanslangdic00maitiala/page/n135/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-312.webp', 62458, '00d04420dce721e3fca2daa530479a6122976877a87e58eca1799bb84e0b445c', '{"id": "CERT-2100-312", "slug": "cert-2100-312", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Graft", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1891, "date_display": "1889–1891", "sort_date": "1891-01-01", "short_gloss": "Graft in Albert Barrere & Charles Godfrey Leland, A Dictionary of Slang, Jargon & Cant, Vols. I-II (Ballantyne Press, 1889-1890), Vol. I, p.", "source_publication": "Albert Barrere & Charles Godfrey Leland, A Dictionary of Slang, Jargon & Cant, Vols. I-II (Ballantyne Press, 1889-1890), Vol. I, p. 423 (leaf 451); paired with James Maitland, The American Slang Dictionary (Chicago: R. J. Kittredge & Co., 1891), p. 131 (leaf 135)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/americanslangdic00maitiala/page/n135/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Barrere & Leland Vol. I Title leaf 7 & p. 423 (leaf 451); Maitland 1891 Title leaf 7 & p. 131 (leaf 135)", "verbatim_quote": "Barrere & Leland (1889, Vol. I, p. 423): ''Graft (prison and popular), work; to graft, to work. To graf is a provincialism for \"to dig\" (graft being a trench). Hence the slang signification.'' Maitland (Chicago, 1891, p. 131): ''Graft, work; anything done; \"great graft\" is anything satisfactory.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1891, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Full-text and alphabetical audit across Barrere & Leland''s two-volume Dictionary of Slang, Jargon & Cant (Vol. I 1889: 244,241 words, 7x graft*, 0x grift*; Vol. II 1890: 193,540 words, 0x graft*, 0x grift*) and Chicago journalist James Maitland''s American Slang Dictionary (1891: 68,280 words, 2x graft*, 0x grift*). Crucially, Maitland was compiled in Chicago—the exact rail/circus hub where ''grifter'' later surfaced—yet in 1891 records only ''Graft, work; anything done; \"great graft\" is anything satisfactory'', with zero trace of ''grift'' or ''grifter''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-312.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1891 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-321', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Gifted', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1892, '1892-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/worksoliverwend21holmgoog/page/n244/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-321.webp', 88440, '5549c975782608f7d057928bd9dbf03dc88edeb29ab8e3ff660f9547329e4f65', '{"id": "CERT-2100-321", "slug": "cert-2100-321", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Gifted", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1892, "date_display": "1892 & 10 Jun 1902", "sort_date": "1892-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Gifted", "source_publication": "Oliver Wendell Holmes, The Guardian Angel, Vol. VI of The Works of Oliver Wendell Holmes, Standard Library / Riverside Edition (Boston & New York: Houghton, Mifflin and Co., 1892 [1st ed. 1867]), p. 204 (leaf 245); paired with The Chicago Daily Tribune, Vol. 61, No. 161 (June 10, 1902), p. 16 (leaf 15)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/worksoliverwend21holmgoog/page/n244/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Holmes 1892 Title leaf 12 & p. 204 (leaf 245); Chicago Daily Tribune 1902-06-10 Masthead leaf 0 & p. 16 (leaf 15)", "verbatim_quote": "Holmes (1892 [1867], p. 204): ''\"What a pity,\" he said to himself, \"that this Gifted [OCR: Grifted] Hopkins has n''t got the brains of that William Murray Bradshaw!\"'' Chicago Daily Tribune (1902-06-10, p. 16): ''A favorite model with our professional staff of fitters [OCR: grifters] is made of lightest batiste...''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1892, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "In Google-digitized editions of Oliver Wendell Holmes''s The Guardian Angel (worksoliverwend21holmgoog, writingsoliverw01unkngoog, guardianangel02holmgoog), the wide-serifed Riverside capital ''G'' in young poet ''Gifted Hopkins'' is misread 13 separate times per volume as ''Grifted Hopkins'', creating dozens of spurious pre-1902 hits for past-tense ''grifted'' (just days before the genuine June 3, 1902 St. Paul Globe ''grifted a benny''!). Likewise, the June 10, 1902 Chicago Daily Tribune corset advertisement fuses ''of fitters'' across a shaded halftone border into ''grifters''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-321.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1892 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-313', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'GRAFT', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1893, '1893-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue03farmuoft/page/n198/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-313.webp', 68972, 'ab7eca297e0742b2f3df18090452070caf0be8a5924ec05f45ceba678693175e', '{"id": "CERT-2100-313", "slug": "cert-2100-313", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "GRAFT", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1893, "date_display": "1893", "sort_date": "1893-01-01", "short_gloss": "GRAFT in John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues Past and Present, Vol. III: Fla.-Hyps. (London: Printed for Subscribers Only", "source_publication": "John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues Past and Present, Vol. III: Fla.-Hyps. (London: Printed for Subscribers Only, 1893), pp. 192-193 (leaf 198) & p. 214 (leaf 220)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue03farmuoft/page/n198/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf 5; p. 192 (leaf 198, s.v. GRAFT); p. 214 (leaf 220, GRIFF-METOLL -> GRIG)", "verbatim_quote": "GRAFT, subs. (common).—Work; employment; LAY (q.v.): e.g. What GRAFT are you on now? GREAT-GRAFT = profitable labour; GOOD BIZ (q.v.). Also GRAFTING and ELBOW-GREASE. ... Verb (common).—1. To work. ... 2. (American).—To steal.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Definitive 1893 lexicographical negative proof across 215,785 words of Farmer & Henley Vol. III (Fla.-Hyps.): on pp. 192-193 (leaves 198-199), Farmer & Henley explicitly document both common ''GRAFT... Work; employment; LAY: e.g. What GRAFT are you on now?'' and ''Verb... 2. (American).—To steal'' (15 total graft* tokens across the volume). Twenty-two pages later, on p. 214 (leaf 220), the alphabetical headword sequence passes directly from ''GRIFFIN (or GRIFF)'' and ''GRIFF-METOLL, subs. (old).—Sixpence'' to ''GRIG, subs. (old)''—proving 100% absence of grift, grifter, and grifting in 1893.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-313.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-322', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Vandegrift', 'rejected_lead', 'proper_noun_or_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1898, '1898-10-20', 'https://archive.org/details/bristol-bucks-county-gazette-1898-10-20/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-322.webp', 157368, '94cef7361cff85299627e1141c23aafead4c7c52eca0dab65026e3a1a586933d', '{"id": "CERT-2100-322", "slug": "cert-2100-322", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Vandegrift", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "proper_noun_or_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1898, "date_display": "1882–1898", "sort_date": "1898-10-20", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Vandegrift", "source_publication": "The Bucks County Gazette (Bristol, PA), Vol. XXVI, No. 15 (Oct. 20, 1898), p. 3, cols. 2-3 (leaf 2); paired with The Dial (Chicago), Vol. 3, No. 31 (Nov. 1882), p. 156 (leaf 28) and The New York Dramatic Mirror, Vol. 32, No. 808 (June 23, 1894), p. 9 (leaf 8)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bristol-bucks-county-gazette-1898-10-20/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Bucks County Gazette 1898-10-20 Masthead leaf 0 & p. 3 (leaf 2); The Dial Nov. 1882 leaf 28; NY Dramatic Mirror 1894-06-23 p. 9 (leaf 8)", "verbatim_quote": "Bucks County Gazette (1898-10-20, p. 3): ''Mr. Howard Vandegrift and wife, Miss Anna Richardson, of Andalusia; Mr. Mahlon Vande-grift, of Ogontz... Thomas Vande-grift and George Vandegrift will lead the company...'' NY Dramatic Mirror (1894-06-23, p. 9): ''and Van de Grift, musical director.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1898, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Out of 1,730 pre-1906 English newspaper full-text hits for standalone ''grift'' on Internet Archive, the single largest class consists of space-split or end-of-line hyphenated tokens of the Delaware Valley Dutch-American surname and Westmoreland/Bucks County, PA toponym ''Vandegrift / Vandergrift / Van de Grift'' (descended from 1640s New Amsterdam settler Jacob Leendertsen van der Grift)—including Bucks County Gazette (''Vande- / grift''), Lima Times-Democrat photographer ''Van De Grift'' (1895), literary pen name ''Margaret Vande- / grift'' (The Dial, 1882), Fanny Van de Grift Stevenson & Nellie Van de Grift Sanchez, and theatrical musical director ''Van de Grift'' in The New York Dramatic Mirror (June 23, 1894).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-322.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1898 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-302', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Grafter', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/trampingwithtra00willgoog/page/n394/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-302.webp', 98374, 'd626bc68f8a47e7c8612158dffa015f1f50f8a1ff032ca5e62dfc9e1228b2f22', '{"id": "CERT-2100-302", "slug": "cert-2100-302", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Grafter", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "1899–1901", "sort_date": "1899-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Grafter", "source_publication": "Josiah Flynt [Willard], Tramping with Tramps (New York: The Century Co., 1899), Glossary p. 394 (leaf 394); paired with Josiah Flynt, The World of Graft (New York: McClure, Phillips & Co., 1901), Introductory p. 4 (leaf 19) & Glossary p. 221 (leaf 236)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/trampingwithtra00willgoog/page/n394/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Flynt 1899 leaf 394; Flynt 1901 Title leaf 8, p. 4 (leaf 19), & p. 221 (leaf 236)", "verbatim_quote": "Flynt (1899, p. 394): ''Graft: a line of business; synonymous with \"spiel.\" Grafter: a pickpocket.'' Flynt (1901, p. 4): ''In regard to the word \"graft,\" which is used freely in the text, I desire to state that it is a generic slang term for all kinds of theft and illegal practices generally.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "H1b Sociolinguistic Trigger: Josiah Flynt''s McClure''s articles and The World of Graft (1901: 249 graft* tokens, 38 grafter* tokens, 0 grift* tokens) popularized underworld ''graft / grafter'' nationwide in the civic-reform sense ''municipal political corruption and police bribery'', bleaching its subcultural exclusivity.", "analysis_notes": "Quantitative progression across Josiah Flynt''s trilogy pinpoints the exact semantic-bleaching shock: in Tramping with Tramps (1899, 100,510 words: 3x graft*, 1x grafter, 0x grift*), ''Grafter'' still meant specifically ''a pickpocket'' and ''Graft'' was ''synonymous with \"spiel\"''. In The Powers That Prey (1900, 57,802 words: 22x graft*, 5x grafter*, 0x grift*) and especially The World of Graft (1901, 52,652 words: 249x graft*, 38x grafter*, 0x grift*), Flynt generalized ''graft'' to municipal Tammany/Chicago aldermanic and police boodle (''Metropolitan Grafting''). Within months (1901-1903), mainstream newspapers flooded headlines with political ''graft/grafters'', creating acute functional pressure inside itinerant circus/midway and non-violent con argot to coin a distinct in-group form.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-302.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-102', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grafter', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafter', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-02-14', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_1899-02-14_50_182/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-102.webp', 96626, '81287a1fbbf8791de85d9f8deab6f600b7ca9ea09ced28cf5212e84ba45deb78', '{"id": "CERT-2100-102", "slug": "cert-2100-102", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grafter", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafter", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "14 Feb 1899", "sort_date": "1899-02-14", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: grafter", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_st-louis-post-dispatch_1899-02-14_50_182)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_1899-02-14_50_182/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 4", "verbatim_quote": "Then he soliloquized: \"If I hadn''t taken so much whiskey I wouldn''t be in this fix.\" The little \"grafter\" go-between for corrupt officials and those who wished to profit by the corruption, who boasted of his sales of engineers'' licenses and intimidated—for a time at least—the Major Committee, is as humble as any common person caught by the law.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "Falsified Trap B OCR ghost: physical lead type prints ''\"grafter\"'', not ''\"grifter\"''.", "analysis_notes": "Earliest pre-1900 Internet Archive full-text search hit for ''grifter'' (''The little \"grifter\" go-between''). Multimodal inspection of Vol. 50, No. 182 masthead (''TUESDAY EVENING—ST. LOUIS—FEBRUARY 14, 1899'') and 4x ink macro proves the token is ''\"grafter\"'' with a faint diagonal ink fleck over a complete lowercase ''a'' bowl.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-102.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-323', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'on the Grift', 'rejected_lead', 'proper_noun_or_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-07-07', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-times-magazine_the-new-york-times_1899-07-07_48_15442/page/n5/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-323.webp', 106346, '3e95b4c2049f6c1d35e6f1425176d472d5cfe8231bdd1639435d93bc44ccc684', '{"id": "CERT-2100-323", "slug": "cert-2100-323", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "on the Grift", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "proper_noun_or_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "7 Jul 1899", "sort_date": "1899-07-07", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: on the Grift", "source_publication": "The New York Times, Vol. XLVIII, No. 15,442 (July 7, 1899), p. 6, col. 5 (leaf 5); paired with Karl Baedeker, Belgium and Holland, Including the Grand-Duchy of Luxembourg: Handbook for Travellers, 11th ed. (Leipzig: Karl Baedeker, 1894), p. 363 (leaf 513)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-times-magazine_the-new-york-times_1899-07-07_48_15442/page/n5/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "NY Times 1899-07-07 Masthead leaf 0 & p. 6, col. 5 (leaf 5); Baedeker 1894 Title leaf 9 & p. 363 (leaf 513)", "verbatim_quote": "New York Times (July 7, 1899, p. 6): ''THE HAGUE, July 6.—... Express trains left soon after 10 to convey the delegates to The Hague and the Queens to Chateau Loo, near Apeldoorn, on the Grift.'' Baedeker (1894, p. 363): ''Apeldoorn ... a prosperous village with 3000 inhab., is picturesquely situated on the Grift and the Dieren Canal.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Across all 62 English-language pre-1912 IA full-text matches for the exact three-word phrase ''\"on the grift\"'', over 20 pre-1906 hits occur in Murray''s and Baedeker''s Belgium and Holland travel handbooks (1876, 1885, 1888, 1894, 1897, 1901, 1905) and The New York Times''s July 7, 1899 Hague Peace Conference dispatch describing Royal Palace Het Loo at Apeldoorn, situated ''on the Grift'' (the Gelderland canalized river De Grift / Apeldoornse Kanaal; cf. Valleikanaal / Bisschop Davidsgrift). Zero relation to underworld ''on the grift''!", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-323.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-306', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'GRIFT', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1900, '1900-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi02wrig/page/n740/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-306.webp', 59356, '9cec72e7e8b7d8dea0334ab6657edef1c83faedbbc770c4b26fd94703b2bbfd0', '{"id": "CERT-2100-306", "slug": "cert-2100-306", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "GRIFT", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1900, "date_display": "1889–1900", "sort_date": "1900-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: GRIFT", "source_publication": "Joseph Wright, ed., The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. II: D-G (London: Henry Frowde, 1900), pp. 726-727 (leaf 740), s.v. GRIFT, sb.1 & GRIFT, sb.2; paired with Edward Peacock, A Glossary of Words Used in the Wapentakes of Manley and Corringham, Lincolnshire, 2nd ed. (London: English Dialect Society, 1889), p. 244 (leaf 271)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi02wrig/page/n740/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Wright EDD Vol. II Title leaf 7 & pp. 726-727 (leaf 740); Peacock 1889 Title leaf 9 & p. 244 (leaf 271)", "verbatim_quote": "GRIFT, sb.1 n.Lin.1 [grift.] A channel shaped out by water for itself, a runnel. Cf. griff, sb.2 | GRIFT, sb.2 Ess. Suf. [grift.] A slate-pencil. Ess. When Ess. children go to school, they take with them their slates and their grifts, N. & Q. (1890) 7th S. ix. 67; (J.F.); Gl. (1851); Ess.1, Suf.1 [OFr. grefe (mod. greffe), ''stylet''.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1900, "linguistic_note": "H3a Weakened/Unlikely Direct Donor: British regional dialect grift, sb.1 (North Lincolnshire ''water-channel, runnel, fen drain'' < ON gröftr / MLG grift) and grift, sb.2 (Essex & Suffolk ''slate-pencil'' < OFr greffe / English graft). Valuable solely as a structural phonological parallel for /ɡræft, ɡrɛft/ -> /ɡrɪft/ vowel raising before /-ft/.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan audit of Wright''s EDD Vol. II (1900, pp. 726-727) and Peacock''s Lincolnshire Glossary (1889, p. 244) confirms two genuine pre-1900 British dialect nouns spelled ''grift'' (plus Derbyshire ''gruft'', narrow valley, p. 750, and Southern Irish ''grafán / griftaun'', grubbing-axe, p. 700). However, neither North Lincolnshire fen-drainage ''grift'' (''runnel'') nor East Anglian schoolroom ''grift'' (''slate-pencil'', attested 1851 Essex Gl. & 1890 Notes & Queries) ever developed occupational, cant, or swindling senses or agentive ''*grifter''. Crucially, East Anglian ''grift'' (< OFr greffe / Eng. graft ''stylus'') independently demonstrates English front-vowel raising to /ɪ/ in the exact /ɡr_ft/ phonotactic environment, corroborating the naturalness of H1 without being its lexical ancestor.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-306.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1900 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-101', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grafter', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafter', 'agent_verified', 1900, '1900-11-04', 'https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053684/1900-11-04/ed-1/seq-21/', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-101.webp', 74822, 'dfa1d514b073b6f01967dc4bd4acdc70782931fbc7b1167fd40dffc08cbc1a3b', '{"id": "CERT-2100-101", "slug": "cert-2100-101", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grafter", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafter", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1900, "date_display": "4 Nov 1900", "sort_date": "1900-11-04", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: grafter", "source_publication": "Savannah Morning News (Savannah, GA)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America / Georgia Historic Newspapers (LCCN sn89053684)", "source_url": "https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053684/1900-11-04/ed-1/seq-21/", "page_and_column": "p. 21, col. 4", "verbatim_quote": "\"I remember,\" said the grafter, reflectively, \"once when I came tru Savannah, twenty years ago. I was goin'' up de country dat night and I struck up wid de butcher on de train. He was a wise one,\" said the grafter, with great admiration, \"and dey had wise ones butcherin'' on de trains in dem days.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1900, "linguistic_note": "Falsified Trap B OCR ghost: physical lead type prints ''grafter'', not ''grifter''.", "analysis_notes": "Mandatory re-verification of killed Wave 1 candidate CERT-2100-101 (corroborating Subagent 700-A2). Both p. 1 masthead (''Savannah Morning News. SAVANNAH, GA., SUNDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1900.'') and p. 21 running header (''THE MORNING NEWS: SUNDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1900. 21'') verified on LoC NDNP scan (service:ndnp:gu:batch_gu_gadget_ver01:data:sn89053684:00414182318:1900110401:0747). Inside feature ''THE WAYS OF THE GRAFTER'', Open-ONI raw OCR ''paid tne grifter, reflec* 11v Iy'' corresponds to physical lead type ''said the grafter, reflectively'', with a full lowercase ''a'' bowl identical to ''said the grafter, with great admiration'' five lines below.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-101.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1900 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-208', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grafters', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_boundary', 'agent_verified', 1901, '1901-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924030315943', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-208.webp', 80486, 'd91398e9fc3adbb6122adeb220d3358580bc61b5d0c3e6f28081be20cada04b6', '{"id": "CERT-2100-208", "slug": "cert-2100-208", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grafters", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_boundary", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1901, "date_display": "1901", "sort_date": "1901-01-01", "short_gloss": "grafters in Josiah Flynt [Frank Willard], The World of Graft (New York: McClure, Phillips & Co., 1901), p. 10 & Glossary", "source_publication": "Josiah Flynt [Frank Willard], The World of Graft (New York: McClure, Phillips & Co., 1901), p. 10 & Glossary", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924030315943", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "consensus of opinion among the grafters with whom I talked was that Chicago was the best stopping-place for tramps and thieves in the United States.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1901, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Pivotal chronological boundary: across all 6 Josiah Flynt books (1899-1908, 2.37M chars), Flynt uses graft/grafter 302x and grift/grifter 0x. On p. 10 he calls ''Wyoming Slivers'' ''a tramp'', refuting modern secondary summaries calling Slivers an ''ex-grifter''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-208.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1901 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-314', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'fakirs', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1901, '1901-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sawdustspangless00coupiala/page/n79/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-314.webp', 92488, '2d821e957a50b356c280180a0eb9eba0460978b06c51d16eb999452b89dc9c9e', '{"id": "CERT-2100-314", "slug": "cert-2100-314", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "fakirs", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1901, "date_display": "1901", "sort_date": "1901-01-01", "short_gloss": "fakirs in W. C. Coup, Sawdust & Spangles: Stories & Secrets of the Circus (Chicago: Herbert S. Stone and Company, 1901), Ch. V (''Freaks and", "source_publication": "W. C. Coup, Sawdust & Spangles: Stories & Secrets of the Circus (Chicago: Herbert S. Stone and Company, 1901), Ch. V (''Freaks and Fakes''), p. 55 (leaf 79)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sawdustspangless00coupiala/page/n79/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf 7 (''MDCCCCI''); p. 55 (leaf 79)", "verbatim_quote": "From the viewpoint of the showmen there are \"fakers\" and \"fakirs.\" Under the former head we class the men who conceive and manufacture fakes of the kind already described. The fakirs are altogether of a different kind, being the camp-followers who hang on the heels of a circus for the purpose of swindling the public by every variety of device known to the \"blackleg fraternity.\" Frequently a number of illegitimate shows start out, and, before doing so, announce that faking privileges are to be leased.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1901, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "W. C. Coup (1837-1895, P. T. Barnum''s partner who leased side-show privileges from 1861 onward; posthumously edited and published in Chicago by H. S. Stone in 1901, 57,494 words) devotes entire pages (pp. 55-62, leaves 79-86) to the exact social referent of ''grifter'': leased circus swindling concessions, three-card monte, shell-games, short-change men, and ticket-wagon ''square squeals'' deposits. Yet Coup''s entire 57,494-word volume contains ZERO (0) tokens of grift* AND ZERO (0) tokens of graft*, calling these operators exclusively ''fakirs'', ''camp-followers'', ''blackleg fraternity'', and ''faking privileges'' (while using ''whole shebang'' on the same page!). Confirms ''grifter'' was NOT pre-1895 circus parlance and crystallized right at the turn of the 20th century (1900-1902).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-314.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1901 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-226', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grafters', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_b_ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1901, '1901-10-24', 'https://archive.org/details/victoriadailytimes19011024', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-226.webp', 61694, '6f140954190e3b1e5f3121fb6aa6280b8edcd3af7f0176cab73386b2dfbc6f0c', '{"id": "CERT-2100-226", "slug": "cert-2100-226", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grafters", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_b_ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1901, "date_display": "24 Oct 1901 & 25 Oct 1905", "sort_date": "1901-10-24", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: grafters", "source_publication": "Victoria Daily Times (Oct. 24, 1901, p. 4) & The New York Times (Oct. 25, 1905, p. 9)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/victoriadailytimes19011024", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "boosted by a lot of Liberal paid grafters. [1901] | getting the city out of the clutch of grafters and plunderers [1905]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1901, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TRAP B KILL: Full-text OCR returns ''grifters'' in both Oct. 24, 1901 Victoria Daily Times (p. 4) and Oct. 25, 1905 New York Times (p. 9, https://archive.org/details/sim_new-york-times_the-new-york-times_1905-10-25_55_17441). Visual ink inspection proves both print ''grafters'' with partially ink-starved lower bowls of ''a''. Also falsified Swedish-American Skaffaren/Minnesota Stats Tidning homograph ''grifter'' = plural of Swedish grift (''grave'', Trap C).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-226.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1901 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-319', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'drift-ers', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1901, '1901-12-30', 'https://archive.org/details/per_atlanta-constitution_1901-12-30_34/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-319.webp', 165238, '4655740fd2432e42282caa720df7c2f910fe68a82a61d3264ea764d7e210e07c', '{"id": "CERT-2100-319", "slug": "cert-2100-319", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "drift-ers", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1901, "date_display": "30 Dec 1901", "sort_date": "1901-12-30", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: drift-ers", "source_publication": "The Atlanta Constitution, Vol. 34 (Dec. 30, 1901), p. 3, col. 2 (leaf 2); paired with Chicago Daily Tribune, Vol. 11, No. 106 (Oct. 9, 1857), p. 2 (leaf 1) and The Los Angeles Times (Oct. 2, 1900), p. 11 (leaf 10)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_atlanta-constitution_1901-12-30_34/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Atlanta Constitution 1901-12-30 p. 3 (leaf 2); Chicago Tribune 1857-10-09 p. 2 (leaf 1); LA Times 1900-10-02 p. 11 (leaf 10)", "verbatim_quote": "Atlanta Constitution (1901-12-30, p. 3): ''Young as is the day, the whole length of the markets and neighboring piers and quays are already fringed with drift-ers [OCR: grifters], and as yet there is no break in the belt of incoming boats...'' Chicago Tribune (1857-10-09): ''While drifting [OCR: Grifting] about at the mercy of the waves...''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1901, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Across pre-1906 newspaper full-text indexes (442 ''grifting'' hits and 413 ''grifted'' hits), over 85% of English-language matches are broken-ascender ''d-'' -> ''G-/g-'' OCR misreads of ''drifting'', ''drifted'', and ''drifters'' (including Hong Kong China Mail and US shipping columns). Visual scan proof confirms Dec. 30, 1901 Atlanta Constitution prints hyphenated ''drift- / ers'' (Lowestoft North Sea herring-drifter vessels), Oct. 9, 1857 Chicago Daily Tribune prints ''drifting about at the mercy of the waves'', and Oct. 2, 1900 Los Angeles Times prints ''San Pedro, commerce drifted back to its old moorings''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-319.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1901 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-315', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Graft', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/newdictionaryofa00clapuoft/page/n231/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-315.webp', 60940, '038ff0ac56bf4234208300497e9254eeb82c1ec0ae13ed5066c399d6762ae695', '{"id": "CERT-2100-315", "slug": "cert-2100-315", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Graft", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "1902", "sort_date": "1902-01-01", "short_gloss": "Graft in Sylva Clapin, A New Dictionary of Americanisms: Being a Glossary of Words Supposed to Be Peculiar to the United States and the Domi", "source_publication": "Sylva Clapin, A New Dictionary of Americanisms: Being a Glossary of Words Supposed to Be Peculiar to the United States and the Dominion of Canada (New York: Louis Weiss & Co., [1902]), p. 211 (leaf 231)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/newdictionaryofa00clapuoft/page/n231/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf 7; p. 211 (leaf 231, s.v. Graft)", "verbatim_quote": "Graft. (1) A shoemaker''s word used in sense of adding new soles to a pair of boots. (2) In thieves'' parlance, to pick pockets. To help another to steal.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Exhaustive full-text sweep across all 190,593 words of Sylva Clapin''s A New Dictionary of Americanisms (1902) yields exactly 1 token of graft*—p. 211 (leaf 231): ''Graft... (2) In thieves'' parlance, to pick pockets. To help another to steal''—and ZERO (0) tokens of grift, grifter, or grifting (passing directly from ''Griffe'' to ''Grimalkin'' / ''Gringo''). Across all 10 required Negative-Corpus Control Works (16 volumes/editions, 1859-1902, totaling 2,323,278 words), grift* = 0 tokens (100% absent), establishing a rock-solid quantitative terminus post quem at the 1900-1902 threshold.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-315.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-103', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grifter', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'discovered', 1902, '1902-04-14', 'https://www.merriam-webster.com/grammar/is-it-grifter-or-grafter-usage-difference-grift-graft', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-2100-103", "slug": "cert-2100-103", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grifter", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "discovered", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "14 Apr 1902", "sort_date": "1902-04-14", "short_gloss": "grifter in The Waterloo Courier (Waterloo, IA)", "source_publication": "The Waterloo Courier (Waterloo, IA)", "source_archive": "Merriam-Webster Usage Guide (''Is it grifter or grafter?'', citing commercial newspaper archive)", "source_url": "https://www.merriam-webster.com/grammar/is-it-grifter-or-grafter-usage-difference-grift-graft", "page_and_column": "unpaginated in secondary citation", "verbatim_quote": "The special ''write-up'' grifter is getting in his work, ever at Des Moines, and we are surprised to see the Register allow its pages to be used.", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "Deliberate American underworld, railroad-depot, and circus-midway cant vowel-ablaut / blend alteration of grafter, n. (''corrupt official or swindler'', 1865) + graft, n., reinforced by midway drifter / shift among traveling ''sure-thing'' flat-joint and privilege operators c. 1900-1904 as ''graft/grafter'' bleached into mainstream municipal politics.", "analysis_notes": "Secondary lexicographical citation reported by Merriam-Webster (''Is it grifter or grafter?'') from The Waterloo Daily Courier (Waterloo, IA, 14 Apr 1902). Because the 1902 Waterloo newsprint page is held behind a commercial newspaper paywall rather than an open public-domain repository, it is credited as BASELINE-LITERATURE (verification_level: discovered) while our 3 Jun 1902 St. Paul Globe (''grifted'') and 12 Oct 1904 St. Louis Post-Dispatch (''grifters'') plates supply the earliest open-scan primary evidence.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): inspect original scan via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-104', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grifted', 'attestation', 'earliest_primary_scan_verified_word_family_verb', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-06-03', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn90059523/1902-06-03/ed-1/?sp=5', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-104.webp', 70220, '7f909e33b8cf9b4187ff15f8e97d7e551e21cad8f9fb193eb29ce372369e332d', '{"id": "CERT-2100-104", "slug": "cert-2100-104", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grifted", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "earliest_primary_scan_verified_word_family_verb", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "3 Jun 1902", "sort_date": "1902-06-03", "short_gloss": "grifted in The Saint Paul Globe (St. Paul, MN) [reprinting Kansas City Journal]", "source_publication": "The Saint Paul Globe (St. Paul, MN) [reprinting Kansas City Journal]", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (LCCN sn90059523)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn90059523/1902-06-03/ed-1/?sp=5", "page_and_column": "p. 5, col. 6", "verbatim_quote": "\"Poor Fatty! I''m leary that he''ll croak in the stir. Say, did you hear about Dutch Charlie? He got on his uppers an'' grifted a benny an'' was sent to the hand house.\" \"Dutch Charlie''s a dead one. What''s Jimmie Burke''s graft now?\" \"Jimmie''s a stall for a dip. Him an'' his pal got a jacket last week.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "Deliberate American underworld, railroad-depot, and circus-midway cant vowel-ablaut / blend alteration of grafter, n. (''corrupt official or swindler'', 1865) + graft, n., reinforced by midway drifter / shift among traveling ''sure-thing'' flat-joint and privilege operators c. 1900-1904 as ''graft/grafter'' bleached into mainstream municipal politics.", "analysis_notes": "Primary-scan-verified transitive verb ''grifted'' (''stole [an overcoat]''), antedating TGT-100 baseline (1906) by 4 years and OED Online ''grift, v.'' (1926, Flynn''s) by 24 years. Explicitly credits prior art in Green''s Dictionary of Slang (''grift, v.'', sense 1, 1902) and Merriam-Webster (''First Known Use: 1902''). Newly establishes the underlying primary source: an article (''LANGUAGE OF THIEVES'') compiled from Kansas City Union Depot Detective Charles Sanderson for the Kansas City Journal, glossed lower in the same feature (''He was broke and stole an overcoat and was sent to the workhouse''), and contrasts ''grifted'' (verb) directly with ''Jimmie Burke''s graft'' (noun) two lines below.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-104.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-105', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grifted', 'attestation', 'independent_typesetting_corroboration_1902_verb', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-07-06', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn89053684/1902-07-06/ed-1/?sp=7', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-105.webp', 128952, 'aed936bffa1e5758bdece4b7053921b4dd87cbad31b55e36e8aa8b64a256ed97', '{"id": "CERT-2100-105", "slug": "cert-2100-105", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grifted", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "independent_typesetting_corroboration_1902_verb", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "6 Jul 1902", "sort_date": "1902-07-06", "short_gloss": "grifted in Savannah Morning News (Savannah, GA) [crediting ''From the Kansas City Journal'']", "source_publication": "Savannah Morning News (Savannah, GA) [crediting ''From the Kansas City Journal'']", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America / Digital Library of Georgia (LCCN sn89053684)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn89053684/1902-07-06/ed-1/?sp=7", "page_and_column": "p. 7, col. 3", "verbatim_quote": "From the Kansas City Journal. [...] \"Poor Fatty! I''m leary that he''ll croak in the stir. Say, did you hear about Dutch Charlie? He got on his upper an'' grifted a benny an'' was sent to the band house.\" \"Dutch Charlie''s a dead one. What''s Jimmie Burke''s graft now?\" \"Jimmie''s a stall for a dip. Him an'' his pal got a packet last week.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "Deliberate American underworld, railroad-depot, and circus-midway cant vowel-ablaut / blend alteration of grafter, n. (''corrupt official or swindler'', 1865) + graft, n., reinforced by midway drifter / shift among traveling ''sure-thing'' flat-joint and privilege operators c. 1900-1904 as ''graft/grafter'' bleached into mainstream municipal politics.", "analysis_notes": "Novel independent typesetting discovery (-24 years vs OED 1926 ''grift, v.''; -4 years vs TGT-100 1906 baseline). Running header (''SAVANNAH MORNING NEWS: SUNDAY, JULY 6, 1902. 7'') and explicit byline (''From the Kansas City Journal.'') verified on high-res NDNP scan. Because Savannah linotypists independently reset the copy (correcting St. Paul''s typos ''hand house'' -> ''band house'' and ''Jimmit''es'' -> ''Jimmie''s'' while preserving dotted-''i'' ''grifted'' right above ''graft''), this rules out a single-compositor St. Paul misprint and proves ''grifted'' stood in the original May/June 1902 Kansas City Journal copy.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-105.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-106', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Vander- grift', 'rejected_lead', 'line_break_toponym_homograph_vandergrift', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-09-27', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1902-09-27_50_31/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-106.webp', 73926, '2095be63100dc5067f51870fb09c754fe26ef2eee06eb20818dce6b0cba2b1fb', '{"id": "CERT-2100-106", "slug": "cert-2100-106", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Vander- grift", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "line_break_toponym_homograph_vandergrift", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "27 Sep 1902", "sort_date": "1902-09-27", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Vander- grift", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1902-09-27_50_31)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1902-09-27_50_31/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 50, No. 31, p. 664 [leaf 4], col. 1", "verbatim_quote": "Dilger-Cornell Co. Notes: We opened the seventh week of our season at Vander- grift, Pa., to S. R. O., breaking the house record for repertory openings, playing to capacity the remainder of our stay there.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "Trap C proper-noun/toponym homograph: hyphenated line wrap of Vandergrift, Pennsylvania (founded 1895, named after Capt. Jacob Jay Vandergrift).", "analysis_notes": "Exemplar Trap C kill representing >120 false-positive full-text hits for ''grift'' across 1897-1915 theatrical/circus route columns in The New York Clipper, The Billboard, The Show World, and Variety (alongside surnames ''F. B. Pedgrift'', ''Van de Grift'', ''Grift Brothers'', ''Jack Grift'', and Dutch canal ''on the Grift'' in 1899-07-07 NYT).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-106.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-304', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'drifter', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-09-27', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1902-09-27_14_39/page/n24/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-304.webp', 91662, '63b98e5dc7256414414431203300d153d653d7f85ce36b91da59b1187bd5364f', '{"id": "CERT-2100-304", "slug": "cert-2100-304", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "drifter", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "27 Sep 1902", "sort_date": "1902-09-27", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: drifter", "source_publication": "The Billboard (Cincinnati, OH), Vol. 14, No. 39 (Sept. 27, 1902), p. 25 (leaf 24, misnumbered ''32'' on corner) & Vol. 14, No. 47 (Nov. 22, 1902), p. 24 (leaf 23), Singer Brothers (82 Bowery, New York City) advertisement", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1902-09-27_14_39/page/n24/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Billboard 1902-09-27 Cover leaf 0 & leaf 24; 1902-11-22 Cover leaf 0 & leaf 23", "verbatim_quote": "DRIFTERS, ATTENTION! WE ARE THE \"REAL THING\" FOR YOU. We not only carry, but sell anything and everything a drifter can make money on. We are the cheapest in price, promptest in shipping, and carry the largest assortment of everything made in White Stones, Watches, Glims, Notions, Cutlery, Flat Ware, Jewelry and Novelties for Diamond Palaces, Spindle Wheel, Fish Ponds, Give-away, etc., also latest Novelties for every excitement.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "H2a Lexical Blending Catalyst: Turn-of-the-century midway/fairground trade cant drifter (''itinerant street pitchman, fake-jewelry slum worker, and spindle-wheel operator'', Billboard 1902-1903) & railroad-tramp on the drift blended with grafter / on the graft -> grifter / on the grift.", "analysis_notes": "Newly discovered primary evidence in The Billboard (Sept. 27, Nov. 8, Nov. 22, 1902; Jan. 24 & Mar. 14, 1903): New York wholesale slum house Singer Brothers (82 Bowery) headlines its carnival/streetfair supply catalog ''DRIFTERS, ATTENTION! ... anything and everything a drifter can make money on ... White Stones [paste ''Arizona diamonds''!], Watches, Glims [spectacles], ... Spindle Wheel, Fish Ponds, Give-away, etc., for every excitement.'' Compare CERT-2100-316 (''A grifter with flash slum is a gentleman who sells Arizona diamonds on street corners'') and Green''s 1911 Mathews baseline (''A grifter ... Gallery, canes and wheel''). This proves that in 1902 midway trade argot, the exact itinerant flat-joint/slum operator was called BOTH a ''grafter'' AND a ''drifter''! Phonological contact between grifter (KLPTL) and drifter (TLPTL) is (edit distance 1, ; on the grift vs. on the drift , explaining why grafter + drifter converged specifically on /-ɪftər/ (''grifter'') and prepositional ''on the grift''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-304.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-107', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'greeted', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ink_ghost_for_greeted', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-10-12', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030272/1902-10-12/ed-1/?sp=18', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-107.webp', 151072, 'aaece78fc0f92970e3eb277742757f19ba2427193e7db8439e853db291cbbbd3', '{"id": "CERT-2100-107", "slug": "cert-2100-107", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "greeted", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ink_ghost_for_greeted", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "12 Oct 1902", "sort_date": "1902-10-12", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: greeted", "source_publication": "The Sun (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (LCCN sn83030272)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030272/1902-10-12/ed-1/?sp=18", "page_and_column": "Sec. 3, p. 4 [sp=18], col. 2", "verbatim_quote": "\"Dippy was sitting by the front window reading the charts of the afternoon''s races in a paper and looking miserable. \"''Say, wouldn''t it bone-spavin you—wouldn''t it make you wish you lived two blocks from Mont Pelee?'' was the way Dippy greeted me.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "Falsified Trap B OCR ghost: physical lead type prints ''greeted'', misread by NDNP OCR as ''grifted'' (ee -> if).", "analysis_notes": "Crucial Trap B falsification inside a racetrack slang story (''The Wedding Ring of Mrs. Dippy McGlone'', which uses genuine ''graft'' and ''grafter'' in col. 1). NDNP raw OCR produced ''was thn way Dippy grifted me'', a deceptive semantic near-miss; 4x ink inspection proves the physical type reads ''Dippy greeted me.''", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-107.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-305', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'SHIFTER', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1903, '1903-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue06farmuoft/page/n176/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-305.webp', 134164, '02da77742dc21fc4af2f3f49507a8c31fc187d2c6245b06550b6ce7c7d3098ce', '{"id": "CERT-2100-305", "slug": "cert-2100-305", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "SHIFTER", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1903, "date_display": "1785–1903", "sort_date": "1903-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: SHIFTER", "source_publication": "Francis Grose, A Classical Dictionary of the Vulgar Tongue (1st ed., London: S. Hooper, 1785), leaf 179; Samuel Johnson, Dictionary of the English Language (London, 1827 [1755]), leaf 507; The Century Dictionary (New York, 1895 [1890]), p. 5569 (leaf 267); John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues, Vol. VI (London, 1903), p. 174 (leaf 176)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue06farmuoft/page/n176/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Grose 1785 leaf 179; Johnson 1827 leaf 507; Century Dict. p. 5569 (leaf 267); Farmer & Henley Vol. VI p. 174 (leaf 176)", "verbatim_quote": "Farmer & Henley (1903, Vol. VI, p. 174): ''SHIFTER, subs. (old).—1. An intriguer: SHIFTY-COVE = a trickster (GROSE). Also (2) = a thief; (3) a sharper; and (4) a drunkard. Whence SHIFTY (or SHIFTING) = tricky (now recognised); SHIFTING = (1) shuffling, stealing, swindling.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1903, "linguistic_note": "H2b Secondary Lexical-Rime Attractor: Historical English cant/colloquial shift (''fraudulent expedient, trick, dodge'') and shifter (''cony-catcher, cozener, sharper, thief'', 1567 Awdeley; 1598 Florio; 1755 Johnson; 1785 Grose; 1890 Century; 1903 Farmer & Henley) reinforced the /-ɪft / -ɪftər/ rime with core ''trickery/swindler'' semantics.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scans across four centuries of English lexicography verify that shift (''petty trick, artifice, fraud'') and shifter (''one who plays tricks; a cosener; a trickster; a sharper; a thief'') remained active in 19th-century dictionaries (Century Dictionary p. 5569: ''one who resorts to petty shifts or expedients; one who practises artifice; a dodger; a trickster; a cozener''; Farmer & Henley VI:174: ''SHIFTY-COVE = a trickster... SHIFTING = (1) shuffling, stealing, swindling''). Skeleton similarity is on agentive grifter vs. shifter (KLPTL vs. SPTL, d=2, ) and on gerundial grifting vs. shifting. While ''shifter'' alone could not generate initial /ɡr-/, the three-way convergence of onset+coda ''grafter'' (/ɡr-...-ftər/) + midway itinerant ''drifter'' (/-rɪftər/, + trickster ''shifter'' (/-ɪftər/, ; plus ''lift/shoplifter'') overdetermined the exact vowel /ɪ/ over */ɡrɒftər/ or */ɡrʌftər/.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-305.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1903 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-108', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grafting', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafting', 'agent_verified', 1903, '1903-07-13', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn87093220/1903-07-13/ed-1/?sp=4', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-108.webp', 82818, '4a8b9265cbaf64d20b14bc15bd54a062e4d8599c5564fcb3ace6abdddcffd863', '{"id": "CERT-2100-108", "slug": "cert-2100-108", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grafting", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafting", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1903, "date_display": "13 Jul 1903", "sort_date": "1903-07-13", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: grafting", "source_publication": "Aberdeen Herald (Aberdeen, WA)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (LCCN sn87093220)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn87093220/1903-07-13/ed-1/?sp=4", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 1", "verbatim_quote": "That it would cost the Polk Co., a few more dollars, might be true, but they would be Chehalis county dollars, paid to Chehalis county printers, by the very Chehalis county people the Polk folks are grafting.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1903, "linguistic_note": "Falsified Trap B OCR ghost: physical lead type prints ''grafting.'', not ''grifting.''", "analysis_notes": "Earliest pre-1904 Chronicling America hit for ''grifting''. Running header (''ABERDEEN HERALD, MONDAY, JULY 13, 1903'') and 4x ink zoom verify a clear lowercase ''a'' in ''grafting.'' whose slightly pinched top arch triggered NDNP a -> i OCR confusion.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-108.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1903 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-109', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grafters', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafters', 'agent_verified', 1903, '1903-08-20', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn86064149/1903-08-20/ed-1/?sp=4', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-109.webp', 83402, '8b099a375f6a2b05163270d90a4ba5a1948508c609764ffcd1e034f43e2e710d', '{"id": "CERT-2100-109", "slug": "cert-2100-109", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grafters", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafters", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1903, "date_display": "20 Aug 1903", "sort_date": "1903-08-20", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: grafters", "source_publication": "The Vinita Weekly Chieftain (Vinita, Indian Territory [OK])", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (LCCN sn86064149)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn86064149/1903-08-20/ed-1/?sp=4", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 3", "verbatim_quote": "Hot? Yes, but let government affairs be investigated thoroughly and properly, and it will be so much hotter for some of the grafters that h—l will look like a summer resort.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1903, "linguistic_note": "Falsified Trap B OCR ghost: physical lead type prints ''grafters'', not ''grifters''.", "analysis_notes": "Pre-1904 Indian Territory candidate from Chronicling America (''some of tbe grifters''); 4x physical ink zoom confirms ''the grafters'' (matching ''\"grafters\"'' higher in the same column).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-109.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1903 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-110', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grafter', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafter', 'agent_verified', 1903, '1903-11-12', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn96088281/1903-11-12/ed-1/?sp=2', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-110.webp', 133456, '45cf9ad2c221531dae47be31b6ba9ccea148645aa460c9d66db2210a7864125e', '{"id": "CERT-2100-110", "slug": "cert-2100-110", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grafter", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ink_ghost_for_grafter", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1903, "date_display": "12 Nov 1903", "sort_date": "1903-11-12", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: grafter", "source_publication": "Rogue River Courier (Grants Pass, OR)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (LCCN sn96088281)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn96088281/1903-11-12/ed-1/?sp=2", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 2", "verbatim_quote": "\"Grafting is by no means confined to the petty and wholesale robbing of the community by public officials. There is the gr[a]fter who is false to the interests of his employer; the grafter who uses his position with a corporation or company to a dishonest end. In every branch and ramification of the business, financial and industrial world is found the grafter, smug of face, discreet of tongue...", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1903, "linguistic_note": "Falsified Trap B under-inked sort: Brooklyn Eagle reprint on ''Grafting'' prints ''grafter'' throughout, with one under-inked ''a'' bowl misread by OCR as ''grifter''.", "analysis_notes": "Running header (''ROGUE RIVER COURIER, GRANTS PASS, OREGON, NOVEMBER 12, 1903'') and column scan verified. Demonstrates classic lead-press dry-sort dropout (''gr[a]fter'' with full ''a''-width kerning gap, immediately followed by crisp ''the grafter'' twice in the same paragraph).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-110.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1903 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-111', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grifters', 'attestation', 'novel_antedating_worlds_fair_confession', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-10-12', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-12_57_52/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-111.webp', 80590, '9deebf9770e78f5cb92e317356f9b00276e04cc9bf964447854ea19ceccc4556', '{"id": "CERT-2100-111", "slug": "cert-2100-111", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grifters", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "novel_antedating_worlds_fair_confession", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "12 Oct 1904", "sort_date": "1904-10-12", "short_gloss": "grifters in St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-12_57_52)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-12_57_52/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 57, No. 52, p. 1, col. 4", "verbatim_quote": "He declared that all such \"work\" had been done by men who had \"butted in\" on the \"grifters\" and was not countenanced by the \"kings\" or the county officials. [...] O''Reilly declared the only game the \"grifters\" were working was the \"sure thing\" game, and their only bait to victims was the offer of a chance to get all the money in sight.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "Deliberate American underworld, railroad-depot, and circus-midway cant vowel-ablaut / blend alteration of grafter, n. (''corrupt official or swindler'', 1865) + graft, n., reinforced by midway drifter / shift among traveling ''sure-thing'' flat-joint and privilege operators c. 1900-1904 as ''graft/grafter'' bleached into mainstream municipal politics.", "analysis_notes": "Earliest open-scan attestation of grifter, n. (plural ''grifters''), antedating OED Online (1915) by 11 years, Green''s Dictionary of Slang (1911) by 7 years, the 1906 target-board baseline by 1 year, 8 months, and Merriam-Webster''s 17 Oct 1904 St. Louis Republic editorial by 5 days. Reports the 11 Oct 1904 arrest of self-confessed World''s Fair ''steerer'' and ''booster'' Wallace (''Booster'') O''Reilly alongside John A. Morton and Clyde S. Jenks while steering Rev. Thomas Powell Clark of Lexington, Tennessee, into a ''drop suit case'' sure-thing game near Delmar Garden.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-111.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-112', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'GRIFTERS', 'attestation', 'lexical_split_headline_contrast', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-10-13', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-13_57_53/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-112.webp', 80312, 'f38273d3d3582934080f87cafc3f947c251d3e2f770f1e381ad6b7c568d1b32b', '{"id": "CERT-2100-112", "slug": "cert-2100-112", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "GRIFTERS", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "lexical_split_headline_contrast", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "13 Oct 1904", "sort_date": "1904-10-13", "short_gloss": "GRIFTERS in St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-13_57_53)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-13_57_53/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 57, No. 53, p. 1, cols. 1-2", "verbatim_quote": "\"GRIFTERS\" AND \"GRAFTERS\" ARE NEAR EXPOSURE. Grand Jury May Reassemble Monday to Hear Self-Confessed Booster O''Reilly''s Complete Story of How Sure-Thing Gamblers Paid for Protection in St. Louis County.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "Deliberate American underworld, railroad-depot, and circus-midway cant vowel-ablaut / blend alteration of grafter, n. (''corrupt official or swindler'', 1865) + graft, n., reinforced by midway drifter / shift among traveling ''sure-thing'' flat-joint and privilege operators c. 1900-1904 as ''graft/grafter'' bleached into mainstream municipal politics.", "analysis_notes": "Page-1 banner headline contrasting ''\"GRIFTERS\" AND \"GRAFTERS\" ARE NEAR EXPOSURE'' as St. Louis County Grand Jury Foreman Henry H. Helmering prepares to reassemble jurors to hear Wallace ''Booster'' O''Reilly''s testimony on official protection payments at Delmar Garden and Creve Coeur Lake.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-112.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-113', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Grifter', 'attestation', 'metalinguistic_neologism_testimony', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-10-14', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-14_57_54/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-113.webp', 97712, '7d489b9dc2bc612085ac4c02d91ddd1190b881d03378743025119e4e7026c8e3', '{"id": "CERT-2100-113", "slug": "cert-2100-113", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Grifter", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "metalinguistic_neologism_testimony", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "14 Oct 1904", "sort_date": "1904-10-14", "short_gloss": "Grifter in St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-14_57_54)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-14_57_54/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 57, No. 54, p. 2 [leaf 1], cols. 2-3", "verbatim_quote": "R. L. Johnston, County Prosecuting Attorney, Will Take the Evidence of Self-Confessed County \"Grifters.\" [...] O''Reilly, the \"Sure-Thing\" Gambler, Who Told of Protection in St. Louis County and Coined New Slang Word \"Grifter\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "Deliberate American underworld, railroad-depot, and circus-midway cant vowel-ablaut / blend alteration of grafter, n. (''corrupt official or swindler'', 1865) + graft, n., reinforced by midway drifter / shift among traveling ''sure-thing'' flat-joint and privilege operators c. 1900-1904 as ''graft/grafter'' bleached into mainstream municipal politics.", "analysis_notes": "Earliest open-scan singular attestation (''Grifter''), reporting St. Louis County Prosecuting Attorney R. L. Johnston''s subpoena of Wallace ''Booster'' O''Reilly before the Grand Jury and printing the explicit metalinguistic caption ''Who ... Coined New Slang Word \"Grifter\"'' to distinguish protected World''s Fair sure-thing operators from municipal or violent ''grafters''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-113.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-303', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Grifter', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-10-14', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-14_57_54/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-303.webp', 59656, '9f234cdce32f539b8a915c8fb373d683b8ab665db301a02af1c4e50fca32002b', '{"id": "CERT-2100-303", "slug": "cert-2100-303", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Grifter", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "14 Oct 1904", "sort_date": "1904-10-14", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Grifter", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch, Vol. 57, No. 54 (Oct. 14, 1904), p. 2 (leaf 1); paired with O. Henry, The Gentle Grafter (New York, 1908 [1919 repr.], leaf 12: 151x graft*, 0x grift*) and Louis E. Jackson & C. R. Hellyer, A Vocabulary of Criminal Slang (Portland, OR, 1914), p. 39 (leaf 48)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-14_57_54/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch 1904-10-14 p. 2, cols. 2-4 (leaf 1); Jackson & Hellyer 1914 Title leaf 9 & p. 39 (leaf 48)", "verbatim_quote": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (Oct. 14, 1904, p. 2): ''R. L. Johnston, County Prosecuting Attorney, Will Take the Evidence of Self-Confessed County \"Grifter\" Before Grandjurors. NAMES OF \"GRAFTERS\" FURNISHED PROSECUTOR ... O''Reilly, the \"Sure Thing\" Gambler, Who Told of Protection in St. Louis County and Coined New Slang Word \"Grifter\".''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "H1c Morphophonological Differentiation: Expressive high-front vowel raising / ablaut (/ɡræft(ər)/ -> /ɡrɪft(ər)/, cf. drip/drop, snip/snap, flip/flap, tip/top) isolating deft, non-violent skill-based swindling (''the gentle grift'': flat-joint wheels, short-change, pickpocketing, con games) from both municipal ''graft'' and violent safe-blowing (''yegg'' work).", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan of the Oct. 14, 1904 St. Louis Post-Dispatch (published during the 1904 St. Louis World''s Fair) captures ''sure-thing'' Delmar Garden / Creve Coeur Lake flat-joint booster Wallace O''Reilly explicitly contrasting county sure-thing gamblers (''grifters'') against corrupt officials taking protection money (''grafters'')—the exact news event that prompted the Oct. 17, 1904 St. Louis Republic editorial (''We now have both grafters and grifters'') cited by Merriam-Webster. Morphophonologically, raising low-front /æ/ to high-front lax /ɪ/ preserves the entire CCVCC(ər) consonant frame (KLPT == KLPT, KLPTL == KLPTL, while deploying English phonaesthetic vowel symbolism (/ɪ/ = light, quick, dexterous touch vs. heavy /æ, ɑ/ in ''graft'', ''yegg'', ''strong-arm''; cf. Maurer 1940: ''The grift has a gentle touch''). Literary lag is confirmed by O. Henry''s The Gentle Grafter (1908: 151x graft*, 116x grafter*, 0x grift*), whereas Jackson & Hellyer (1914, p. 39, OED''s grift, n.2 baseline) record ''GRIFT, Noun. General usage. Graft; an opportunity for plying criminal talents.''", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-303.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-114', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grifter', 'attestation', 'earliest_explicit_semantic_definition', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-10-15', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-15_57_55/page/n3/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-114.webp', 73904, '05ee2397d4050ea19924936260f090861d7fef3c8952227a76169ae325ba69bc', '{"id": "CERT-2100-114", "slug": "cert-2100-114", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grifter", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "earliest_explicit_semantic_definition", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "15 Oct 1904", "sort_date": "1904-10-15", "short_gloss": "grifter in St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-15_57_55)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1904-10-15_57_55/page/n3/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 57, No. 55, p. 4 [leaf 3], col. 2", "verbatim_quote": "\"Booster\" O''Reilly says that a grifter is a gambler who takes your money on a \"sure thing\" game, and a \"grafter\" is a robber who grabs it. This is a distinction without a difference.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "Deliberate American underworld, railroad-depot, and circus-midway cant vowel-ablaut / blend alteration of grafter, n. (''corrupt official or swindler'', 1865) + graft, n., reinforced by midway drifter / shift among traveling ''sure-thing'' flat-joint and privilege operators c. 1900-1904 as ''graft/grafter'' bleached into mainstream municipal politics.", "analysis_notes": "NOVEL DISCOVERY — Earliest known explicit print definition contrasting ''grifter'' (''a gambler who takes your money on a \"sure thing\" game'', i.e. non-violent confidence/flat-joint swindler) against ''grafter'' (''a robber who grabs it''). Masthead and p. 4 running header (''SATURDAY, OCTOBER 15, 1904.'') verified.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-114.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-115', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'Grifting', 'attestation', 'earliest_verified_grifting_verbal_noun', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-10-17', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84020274/1904-10-17/ed-1/?sp=6', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-115.webp', 166142, 'e60cc8c2f64281663a2b7e82747d7d673fb69556cb11b9e77cb8eb025e2bab3f', '{"id": "CERT-2100-115", "slug": "cert-2100-115", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "Grifting", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "earliest_verified_grifting_verbal_noun", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "17 Oct 1904", "sort_date": "1904-10-17", "short_gloss": "Grifting in The St. Louis Republic (St. Louis, MO)", "source_publication": "The St. Louis Republic (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (LCCN sn84020274)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84020274/1904-10-17/ed-1/?sp=6", "page_and_column": "p. 6, col. 2", "verbatim_quote": "\"GRIFTERS.\" We now have both grafters and grifters. [...] Grifting is far more sensational and spectacular than grafting, and commands less respect. Among the pursuits of grifting are gold-bricking, double-crossing, conning, faking and the distribution of packages; bamboozling, trimming, three-shelling, four-flushing, crimping, rolling a sleeper, short-changing, selling green goods, working the panel, slipping in a phony, lock tricks and generally separating the innocent from his money by means of sure things; taking easy money, exploiting the Rube...", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "Deliberate American underworld, railroad-depot, and circus-midway cant vowel-ablaut / blend alteration of grafter, n. (''corrupt official or swindler'', 1865) + graft, n., reinforced by midway drifter / shift among traveling ''sure-thing'' flat-joint and privilege operators c. 1900-1904 as ''graft/grafter'' bleached into mainstream municipal politics.", "analysis_notes": "Antedates OED and Green''s Dictionary of Slang for verbal noun grifting (1915, Bronson-Howard) by 11 years (and OED grift, v. 1926 by 22 years). Triggered directly by Wallace O''Reilly''s 12–15 Oct 1904 Post-Dispatch Grand Jury disclosures; catalogs ''gold-bricking, double-crossing, conning, faking ... three-shelling, four-flushing, crimping, rolling a sleeper, short-changing, selling green goods, working the panel, slipping in a phony, lock tricks ... exploiting the Rube'' and notes that ''the main forte and prime characteristic of grifting is to keep solid with the officials'' (paragraph 1 ''grifters'' previously cited by Merriam-Webster).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-115.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2100-116', 'TGT-100', 'grifter', NULL, 'grifter', 'grift', 'attestation', 'earliest_verified_bare_noun_grift', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-10-17', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84020274/1904-10-17/ed-1/?sp=6', 1, '/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-116.webp', 159458, '8b2b22caadb6e211d4094df1da9ee44d9dae2b3856154026ac201ebd03ef88b6', '{"id": "CERT-2100-116", "slug": "cert-2100-116", "case_slug": "grifter", "headword": "grifter", "attested_form": "grift", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "earliest_verified_bare_noun_grift", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "17 Oct 1904", "sort_date": "1904-10-17", "short_gloss": "grift in The St. Louis Republic (St. Louis, MO)", "source_publication": "The St. Louis Republic (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (LCCN sn84020274)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84020274/1904-10-17/ed-1/?sp=6", "page_and_column": "p. 6, col. 2 (closing paragraph)", "verbatim_quote": "The common, or garden, variety of grifter now observed in such rank abundance at Creve Coeur Lake and vicinity is exceedingly interesting. But, after all, what can ever fill the place in the public interest of our dear, old, familiar boodlers? All other grift and graft is cheap indeed compared to the kind that has made St. Looey famous—through being run out of our said town.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "Deliberate American underworld, railroad-depot, and circus-midway cant vowel-ablaut / blend alteration of grafter, n. (''corrupt official or swindler'', 1865) + graft, n., reinforced by midway drifter / shift among traveling ''sure-thing'' flat-joint and privilege operators c. 1900-1904 as ''graft/grafter'' bleached into mainstream municipal politics.", "analysis_notes": "Major 10-year antedating of bare noun grift, n.2 vs. OED Online and Green''s Dictionary of Slang (both 1914, Jackson & Hellyer), and 2 years earlier than the 1906 target-board baseline. In the closing paragraph of its 17 Oct 1904 editorial (p. 6, col. 2), The St. Louis Republic coordinates bare singular ''grift'' directly with ''graft'' (''All other grift and graft is cheap indeed compared to the kind that has made St. Looey famous'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/grifter/CERT-2100-116.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
