t printed page 255.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-105.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1891 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-118', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hoboing', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1891, '1891-08-29', 'https://archive.org/details/per_pittsburg-dispatch_pittsburg-dispatch_1891-08-29_1', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-118.webp', 132804, '68f0408b59d6cc996a6168744c2f94bd70526397579ef7516f26bb42cfafb0a5', '{"id": "CERT-2300-118", "slug": "cert-2300-118", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hoboing", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1891, "date_display": "29 Aug 1891", "sort_date": "1891-08-29", "short_gloss": "hoboing in The Pittsburg Dispatch (Pittsburgh, PA)", "source_publication": "The Pittsburg Dispatch (Pittsburgh, PA)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_pittsburg-dispatch_pittsburg-dispatch_1891-08-29_1)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_pittsburg-dispatch_pittsburg-dispatch_1891-08-29_1", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 5 (leaf 3)", "verbatim_quote": "Stealing rides in empty box cars, and ''hoboing'' my way, as you may call it, seemed to come natural after awhile", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1891, "linguistic_note": "American rail-tramp / underworld attention-call shibboleth ''Hobo!'' (''Obo!''), generalized to itinerant rail-tramp and migratory worker (c. 1885-1889)", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified Aug. 29, 1891 attestation of verbal/gerundial ''hoboing'' (''hoboing my way'' in empty box cars), tying OED Online''s 1891 baseline year for hoboing, n. (1891 Bessemer Journal) and predating Green''s 1893-05-21 NY Sun baseline for hobo, v. by nearly 2 years.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-118.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1891 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-214', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1892, '1892-01-31', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_los-angeles-times_the-los-angeles-times_1892-01-31/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-214.webp', 111804, '909ed2d3296b7a256b1139f84012d5d0b3f6fb43743de16405523fad2a5e40d5', '{"id": "CERT-2300-214", "slug": "cert-2300-214", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1892, "date_display": "31 Jan 1892", "sort_date": "1892-01-31", "short_gloss": "hobo in The Los Angeles Times (Los Angeles, CA)", "source_publication": "The Los Angeles Times (Los Angeles, CA)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_los-angeles-times_the-los-angeles-times_1892-01-31/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 11, p. 5, col. 2 (leaf 5)", "verbatim_quote": "The society people of the Southern Pacific settlement east of the old San Fernando street depot, commonly called \"Dogtown\" among the \"hobo\" railroad men, is considerably worked up over the sudden and mysterious disappearance of Locomotive Engineer James Gates", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1892, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Crucial semantic attestation of the internal railroad-labor sense requested in Pillar 2a: here ''\"hobo\" railroad men'' denotes not non-working tramps, but transient/boomer Southern Pacific yard and train employees around the old San Fernando Street depot in Los Angeles, reporting on SP Mojave division Locomotive Engineer James Gates ($180/month).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-214.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1892 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-215', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobos', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1892, '1892-08-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_smalleys-magazine_1892-08_10_8/page/n12/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-215.webp', 74160, '401b9cdbc5f6036e2021b2300b8a21c811a18aabe06330b4bc48084402605211', '{"id": "CERT-2300-215", "slug": "cert-2300-215", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobos", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1892, "date_display": "1 Aug 1892", "sort_date": "1892-08-01", "short_gloss": "hobos in The Northwest Illustrated Monthly Magazine (St. Paul, MN)", "source_publication": "The Northwest Illustrated Monthly Magazine (St. Paul, MN)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_smalleys-magazine_1892-08_10_8/page/n12/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 10, No. 8 (Aug. 1892), p. 15, cols. 1-2 (leaf 13)", "verbatim_quote": "Judge Chambers'' temple of justice has been pulled up and moved away. [...] No more hobos will stand in front of him [...] Beer kegs, bung holes and hobos have quit the grade, / And left Crossport to mourn, since the track was laid. —Press Woodruff in Spokane Review.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1892, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Published in Eugene V. Smalley''s St. Paul Northern Pacific magazine (quoting humorist Press Woodruff in the Spokane Review): documents Crossport, Kootenai County, ID (near Bonners Ferry), the turbulent 1891-1892 Great Northern Railway construction camp (''tribes of graders, pull up survey stakes'', ''The Great Northern saloon'', ''Judge Chambers''), explicitly linking ''hobos'' to migratory western railroad ''grade'' construction crews once ''the track was laid'' (cf. also Vol. 10, No. 3, March 1892, p. 40, https://archive.org/details/sim_smalleys-magazine_1892-03_10_3/page/n40/mode/1up, Red River Valley ND wheat threshing ''some hobo crew'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-215.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1892 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-002', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1893, '1893-05-21', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030272/1893-05-21/ed-1/?sp=28', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-002.webp', 62442, '1191c256e57e68c3419e7928c8090e58d76e5aa520a63d62c500edff1c2338ad', '{"id": "CERT-2300-002", "slug": "cert-2300-002", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1893, "date_display": "21 May 1893", "sort_date": "1893-05-21", "short_gloss": "hobo in The Sun (New York, NY)", "source_publication": "The Sun (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress / Chronicling America (sn83030272)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030272/1893-05-21/ed-1/?sp=28", "page_and_column": "Sec. 3, p. 8 (whole p. 28, sp=28), col. 1", "verbatim_quote": "MIGRATIONS OF THE HOBO. [...] There is a difference between a tramp and a hobo, which the hoboes explain in this way: \"Ye see, a tramp''s a bloke wot won''t work an'' never did work, an'' a hobo''s a guy wot works sometimes but ain''t never stuck on it. An'' den w''en you meets one uv yer own kind ye feels like old pals, ''cause he calls ye ''Ho'' an'' ye calls him ''Bo.'' See?\" [...] \"Dis is de last time dat I''m goin'' to get on de hog,\" says a young-looking hobo. \"Dis is de fourt'' winter dat I''ve had to ''hobo'' it, and I''m tired.\" \"Dat''s wot dey all say,\" says Squint-eyed Turner. \"Onct a ''ho'' always a ''ho.''\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "Simultaneously preserves hoboes'' own reciprocal greeting formula (''he calls ye \"Ho\" an'' ye calls him \"Bo\"''), the idiomatic verb ''to \"hobo\" it'' (Green''s 1893 hobo, v. baseline), and the unrecorded apocopated noun clipping ''ho'' (''Onct a \"ho\" always a \"ho.\"'').", "analysis_notes": "Quadruple philological witness on one page: (1) scan-verifies Green''s 1893 opening citation for verbal ''to \"hobo\" it'' (13 yrs before Sinclair''s The Jungle, 1906); (2) pushes the occupational distinction between a working ''hobo'' and a non-working ''tramp'' back to May 21, 1893 (5 yrs before Bailey 1898 and 15 yrs before Reitman 1908); (3) attests the split greeting ''Ho'' / ''Bo''; and (4) documents clipped ''ho'' alongside ''Benny [overcoat]'' and ''bughouse [crazy]''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-002.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-216', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'HOBO', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1893, '1893-11-19', 'https://archive.org/details/fresno-bee-1893-11-19/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-216.webp', 153828, '5cc20d34f2665b5990fc830ce5c0ffe702db298d623fb2efea3eeb8107050885', '{"id": "CERT-2300-216", "slug": "cert-2300-216", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "HOBO", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1893, "date_display": "19 Nov 1893", "sort_date": "1893-11-19", "short_gloss": "HOBO in The Fresno Morning Republican (Fresno, CA)", "source_publication": "The Fresno Morning Republican (Fresno, CA)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/fresno-bee-1893-11-19/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 6, No. 243, p. 3, col. 2 (leaf 3)", "verbatim_quote": "THE \"LADY\" HOBO. [...] a touching tale of a Mrs. King, who was tramping from Tacoma, Wash., to San Antonio, Tex. [...] \"Mrs. King, the ''lady hobo,'' had her trial this afternoon in Justice Buckman''s court for being drunk and disorderly at Goshen.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Documents both Pacific Northwest-to-California Southern Pacific tramping prosopography (Mrs. King of Tacoma, WA tried before Justice Buckman at Goshen, Tulare County, CA railroad junction) and the early gendered compound ''lady hobo'' (predating 20th-century ''hoboette'' coinages).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-216.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-217', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-04-29', 'https://archive.org/details/dailycolonist18940429uvic/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-217.webp', 146774, '80be5e9def723237d5e31977bb7598d3dbdbae7f8f6be9186dfd531e4f2d0e0e', '{"id": "CERT-2300-217", "slug": "cert-2300-217", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "29 Apr 1894", "sort_date": "1894-04-29", "short_gloss": "hobo in The Daily Colonist (Victoria, B.C.; wire dated Portland, OR, April 28, 1894)", "source_publication": "The Daily Colonist (Victoria, B.C.; wire dated Portland, OR, April 28, 1894)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dailycolonist18940429uvic/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 71, No. 118, p. 8, col. 4 (leaf 8)", "verbatim_quote": "HOBOS CAPTURE A TRAIN. [...] PORTLAND, Or., April 28.—Last night the \"hobo\" army captured an east bound freight train at Troudel [Troutdale] on the Union Pacific road, and ran to Bridal Veil", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Wire report documenting the Portland Industrial (''Commonweal'') contingent seizing a Union Pacific freight train at Troutdale, OR, coupling an engine from the general manager''s west-bound special, and running 142 miles east to Arlington before capture by U.S. troops from Fort Walla Walla; adjacent dispatch reports Midland Railway Supt. Peabody and Detective Chief John Mahong pursuing Galvin''s army in Ohio.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-217.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-218', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobos', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-05-01', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1894-05-01_53_121/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-218.webp', 124208, '3ee76c8b09ed9d527a095cc84f7ea53b60eb8d0238b769f8fd619d06e3441e5a', '{"id": "CERT-2300-218", "slug": "cert-2300-218", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobos", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "1 May 1894", "sort_date": "1894-05-01", "short_gloss": "hobos in The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL)", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1894-05-01_53_121/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 53, No. 121, p. 2, col. 6 (leaf 2)", "verbatim_quote": "COLUMBUS, O., April 30.—[Special.]—The Galvin army of hobos, numbering 200 men, headed by the Nineteenth Regiment Band [...] marched from Trades Assembly Hall to the Union Depot", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Demonstrates the spring 1894 coast-to-coast newspaper explosion propelling Western railroad slang ''hobo'' into national American English across coverage of Jacob S. Coxey, Carl Browne, Lewis C. Fry, Charles T. Kelly (at Des Moines/Omaha/Council Bluffs), G. W. Galvin, and William Hogan (leaf 6 of same issue also notes ''A Pittsburg paper calls Anson''s men hobos''; cf. Scranton Tribune, May 9, 1894, p. 1, https://panewsarchive.psu.edu/lccn/sn84026355/1894-05-09/ed-1/seq-1/, ''Coxey and Browne... The Hobo Leaders'', and Henry Vincent, The Story of the Commonweal, May 1894, leaf 72, https://archive.org/details/TheStoryOfTheCommonweal).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-218.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-119', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hoboism', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-05-07', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1894-05-07_53_127', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-119.webp', 101714, '55ae4d8aca3f6cdf87a720348f53662f4e1b677a3551a1f2db765f3fcf7c81da', '{"id": "CERT-2300-119", "slug": "cert-2300-119", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hoboism", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "7 May 1894", "sort_date": "1894-05-07", "short_gloss": "hoboism in The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL), quoting Buffalo Express", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL), quoting Buffalo Express", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1894-05-07_53_127)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1894-05-07_53_127", "page_and_column": "p. 6, col. 5 (leaf 4)", "verbatim_quote": "Another branch of the Coxey army is in sore straits. The Kellyites have got down to begging for transportation [...] The end of hoboism is at hand.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "American rail-tramp / underworld attention-call shibboleth ''Hobo!'' (''Obo!''), generalized to itinerant rail-tramp and migratory worker (c. 1885-1889)", "analysis_notes": "Early scan-verified attestation of derivative noun hoboism (OED baseline: 1892 Deseret Weekly), illustrating rapid nationalization of the derivative during the Spring 1894 Coxey''s / Kelly''s Industrial Army rail marches.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-119.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-219', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-05-15', 'https://archive.org/details/newport-daily-news-1894-05-15/page/n3/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-219.webp', 114240, 'fb590cbb770c49619eb303085064585e530658da1da295b34572b992d982aa62', '{"id": "CERT-2300-219", "slug": "cert-2300-219", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "15 May 1894", "sort_date": "1894-05-15", "short_gloss": "hobo in Newport Daily News (Newport, RI; quoting Worcester Gazette)", "source_publication": "Newport Daily News (Newport, RI; quoting Worcester Gazette)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/newport-daily-news-1894-05-15/page/n3/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 2 (leaf 4)", "verbatim_quote": "A prominent hobo, when interviewed at the Worcester Gazette office, told of an empty box car, occupied by tramps in a Western town. [...] The word \"hobo\" was used as a countersign.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Directly prompted by the May 1894 Industrial Army press wave (''The word \"hobo,\" which now appears freely in the newspapers''): contrasts the Philadelphia Ledger''s eastern ''Hobokenite'' (Hoboken, NJ) conjecture with firsthand testimony from a ''prominent hobo'' at Worcester, MA confirming Western boxcar countersign origin accompanied by a lateral forearm gesture (closely matching Capt. Cardwell''s Sept. 14, 1888 Portland testimony in CERT-2300-206).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-219.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-220', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hoboes', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-05-27', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1894-05-27_45_291/page/n24/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-220.webp', 99072, '671096cc8d5b73b4a4e9ffc2ad683440d6649487f26e4d1a7701853f4913ba65', '{"id": "CERT-2300-220", "slug": "cert-2300-220", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hoboes", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "27 May 1894", "sort_date": "1894-05-27", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: hoboes", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1894-05-27_45_291/page/n24/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 45, No. 291, p. 25, cols. 2-3 (leaf 25)", "verbatim_quote": "Gen. Kelly says that of all the trials and annoyances he has met with on his journey across the continent the one that most troubled him was the pestiferous manner in which professional hoboes thrust themselves unwelcomed into his camp.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Prosopographical interview with Gen. Charles Thomas Kelly (''Kelly''s Industrial Army'' / ''Kelly''s Navy'') in St. Louis: documents how 1894 Industrial Army leaders fought the press label ''Hobo Army'' by drawing a sharp boundary between bona fide unemployed workmen and ''professional hoboes'' riding freight trains (cf. Chicago Daily Tribune, Aug. 4, 1894, p. 1, https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1894-08-04_53_216/page/n0/mode/1up, ''\"Gen.\" Kelly Tells of His Hobo March'' and Mrs. Frank of Tacoma raising ''another hobo band'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-220.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-221', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-07-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_smalleys-magazine_1894-07_12_7/page/n27/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-221.webp', 99490, '677ea14f553ab6e800ea258b4bb3e87f379c41c4fa695ffa576583b6ff93f485', '{"id": "CERT-2300-221", "slug": "cert-2300-221", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "1 Jul 1894", "sort_date": "1894-07-01", "short_gloss": "hobo in The Northwest Illustrated Monthly Magazine (St. Paul, MN)", "source_publication": "The Northwest Illustrated Monthly Magazine (St. Paul, MN)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_smalleys-magazine_1894-07_12_7/page/n27/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 12, No. 7 (July 1894), p. 24, cols. 2-3 (leaf 28)", "verbatim_quote": "A few of these wanderers are honest fellows, willing to work, but the majority of them are old hobo brakebeam riders [...] captured by the soldiers and tried at Helena for stealing a train [...] new arrivals from Seattle and Spokane.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Edited by Eugene V. Smalley at St. Paul, MN: anchors the Butte-Helena-Fort Benton (Montana) and Seattle-Spokane (Washington) Northern Pacific Railroad corridor during William Hogan''s April 1894 hijacked Northern Pacific train expedition; leaf 21 of the same July 1894 issue also profiles ''\"Skeeter Joe,\" a professional hobo tourist'' riding Northern Pacific rods from Minneapolis via Sauk Rapids to Jamestown, ND.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-221.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-120', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobodom', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-07-08', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn85042461/1894-07-08/ed-1/?sp=4', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-120.webp', 110384, '800741775070efdac62c4bbdeb02613d0bf6f6924255403c720eae181d81d58c', '{"id": "CERT-2300-120", "slug": "cert-2300-120", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobodom", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "8 Jul 1894", "sort_date": "1894-07-08", "short_gloss": "hobodom in Los Angeles Herald (Los Angeles, CA)", "source_publication": "Los Angeles Herald (Los Angeles, CA)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn85042461)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn85042461/1894-07-08/ed-1/?sp=4", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 5", "verbatim_quote": "Myron Russell is not numbered among the 400 of this city, or even the select circle of hobodom, so a simple charge of petty larceny", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "American rail-tramp / underworld attention-call shibboleth ''Hobo!'' (''Obo!''), generalized to itinerant rail-tramp and migratory worker (c. 1885-1889)", "analysis_notes": "Primary page-scan verification of OED Online''s exact earliest citation for hobodom, n. (July 8, 1894 Los Angeles Herald; corroborated on Nov. 21, 1894 in Los Angeles Times, p. 10, ''MECCA OF HOBODOM'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-120.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-121', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'Hobo camp', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-08-27', 'https://archive.org/details/bangor-daily-whig-and-courier-1894-08-27', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-121.webp', 99168, 'ed44973588f46220e7b14734022782d91981cee237b99c2cd99885ee3d5ad717', '{"id": "CERT-2300-121", "slug": "cert-2300-121", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "Hobo camp", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "27 Aug 1894", "sort_date": "1894-08-27", "short_gloss": "Hobo camp in Bangor Daily Whig and Courier (Bangor, ME), Buffalo NY dispatch", "source_publication": "Bangor Daily Whig and Courier (Bangor, ME), Buffalo NY dispatch", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (bangor-daily-whig-and-courier-1894-08-27)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bangor-daily-whig-and-courier-1894-08-27", "page_and_column": "Vol. 61, No. 203, p. 1, col. 4 (leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "Everything reported quiet today in the vicinity of the late Hobo camp. The Hoboes have disbanded, and those who are not in custody are making tracks", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "American rail-tramp / underworld attention-call shibboleth ''Hobo!'' (''Obo!''), generalized to itinerant rail-tramp and migratory worker (c. 1885-1889)", "analysis_notes": "Early scan-verified open-compound attestation of Hobo camp (OED baseline: 1888-09-29 Winona Daily Republican; also attested Jan. 3, 1895 in Los Angeles Times ''break up hobo camps''), showing established police-wire usage before ''hobo jungle'' arose c. 1897-1905.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-121.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-222', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobos', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1895, '1895-01-05', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_los-angeles-times_the-los-angeles-times_1895-01-05/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-222.webp', 82756, 'fbfda2e92264c85b3d303745b024a023c0377506fcc7e4a3823a537b7b7b5ec2', '{"id": "CERT-2300-222", "slug": "cert-2300-222", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobos", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1895, "date_display": "5 Jan 1895", "sort_date": "1895-01-05", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: hobos", "source_publication": "The Los Angeles Times (Los Angeles, CA)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_los-angeles-times_the-los-angeles-times_1895-01-05/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 14, p. 5, col. 6 (leaf 5)", "verbatim_quote": "Justice Austin had ninety-five new and continued vagrancy cases in his court yesterday. Most of the hobos received \"floaters,\" [...] [above in col. 6:] William Kirwan and S. D. Clark from Pocatello, Idaho, conductors on the Oregon Short Line, are visiting in the city.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1895, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Prosopographical rail-column and police-docket pairing in column 6: records named Oregon Short Line conductors William Kirwan and S. D. Clark from the Pocatello, Idaho division point alongside SP Chino conductor W. F. McAvoy, San Pedro agent J. F.anno / J. H.ahan, and Justice Austin''s 95 municipal vagrancy cases issuing ''floaters'' to ''hobos'' (cf. also Fresno Morning Republican, Sept. 13, 1893, p. 1, https://archive.org/details/fresno-bee-1893-09-13/page/n0/mode/1up, O. A. Heldman of Pocatello, ID arrested riding Wabash freight, and Railroad Man''s Magazine, Oct. 1910, Vol. 13, p. 190, https://archive.org/details/railroadman1319101911newy/page/n209/mode/1up, documenting the c. 1890 Pocatello, ID Oregon Short Line boomer brakemen''s ballad ''It was on the O. S. L.'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-222.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1895 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-122', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hoboed', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1897, '1897-02-18', 'https://archive.org/details/portsmouth-daily-times-1897-02-18', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-122.webp', 87954, 'c90cc41c68ff68d292f6769784efe04cc62442a06722fd178cb81f0499b396d2', '{"id": "CERT-2300-122", "slug": "cert-2300-122", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hoboed", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1897, "date_display": "18 Feb 1897", "sort_date": "1897-02-18", "short_gloss": "hoboed in The Daily Times (Portsmouth, OH)", "source_publication": "The Daily Times (Portsmouth, OH)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (portsmouth-daily-times-1897-02-18)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/portsmouth-daily-times-1897-02-18", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 1 (leaf 3)", "verbatim_quote": "Williams and Stokely hoboed it up to Ironton on an N. & W. freight, expecting to nab their game.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1897, "linguistic_note": "American rail-tramp / underworld attention-call shibboleth ''Hobo!'' (''Obo!''), generalized to itinerant rail-tramp and migratory worker (c. 1885-1889)", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified finite preterite verbal construction ''hoboed it'' with explicit Norfolk & Western freight-train riding context (OED baseline: 1890 Spokane Falls Review; Green''s baseline: 1893-05-21 NY Sun; note earlier raw-OCR ''hoboed'' hits in 1890-1896 are predominantly OCR ghosts for ''sobbed'' or ''Robbed'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-122.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1897 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-003', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1897, '1897-06-30', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84036012/1897-06-30/ed-1/?sp=1', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-003.webp', 65528, '69c909e14bb162b97fe8d26f046513b5c1d181b289eb0a6c16b4dc179eb6f722', '{"id": "CERT-2300-003", "slug": "cert-2300-003", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1897, "date_display": "30 Jun 1897", "sort_date": "1897-06-30", "short_gloss": "hobo in The Anaconda Standard (Anaconda, MT)", "source_publication": "The Anaconda Standard (Anaconda, MT)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress / Chronicling America (sn84036012)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84036012/1897-06-30/ed-1/?sp=1", "page_and_column": "Vol. 8, No. 301, p. 1, col. 5 (wire datelined Minot, N.D., June 29)", "verbatim_quote": "Minot, N. D., June 29.—One of the most disgusting scenes that was ever perpetrated upon the peaceful citizens of Minot took place this evening when 30 or 40 drunken hoboes fought like demons among themselves. Pistols, razors, knives, coupling pins and clubs were weapons of defense. [...] The scene of the riot is known as the \"Jungle,\" a hobo resort. At 1 o''clock p. m. more than 200 citizens scoured the \"Jungle\" with lanterns and guns for hoboes.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1897, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verifies Green''s 1897 baseline for jungle (''hobo camp'') at Minot, North Dakota—a major Great Northern Railway division point—explicitly glossing ''the \"Jungle,\" a hobo resort'' 8 years earlier than OED Online''s 1905 Daily Utah State Journal baseline for the compound hobo jungle, n.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-003.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1897 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-223', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hoboes', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1898, '1898-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/ninethousandmile00shawrich/page/n228/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-223.webp', 64372, '4868c2b05a17733152711f8739ed7530c88b9f83e422a18ec078e4544bd9e7c9', '{"id": "CERT-2300-223", "slug": "cert-2300-223", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hoboes", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1898, "date_display": "1 Jan 1898", "sort_date": "1898-01-01", "short_gloss": "hoboes in Milton M. Shaw, Nine Thousand Miles on a Pullman Train (Philadelphia: Allen, Lane & Scott)", "source_publication": "Milton M. Shaw, Nine Thousand Miles on a Pullman Train (Philadelphia: Allen, Lane & Scott)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/ninethousandmile00shawrich/page/n228/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "pp. 138-139 (leaf 229; copyright leaf 10)", "verbatim_quote": "\"Well, crawl in your hole; we''re going to start,\" replies Captain Hale [...] \"It''s impossible to get through this part of the country without being troubled with hoboes.\" We leave Pasco at 12.55 Eastern (9.45 Pacific) time with engine No. 405, Engineer Tom Allen and Fireman W. W. Thompson, who run us to Spokane", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1898, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Firsthand railroader prosopography across the exact Northern Pacific Cascade/Yakima/Columbia division (Tacoma, Ellensburg, North Yakima, Prosser, Kennewick, Pasco, Spokane, and Montana): names rider John Bell (boarded at Tacoma on a 16-inch plank beneath the dining-car ice chest through Yakima dust), Manager Wyman, NP Conductor (''Captain'') W. B. Hale, Engine No. 405 Engineer Tom Allen, and Fireman W. W. Thompson.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-223.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1898 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-318', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'rejected_lead', 'serial_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1898, '1898-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/scribnersmagazi11logagoog/page/n276/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-318.webp', 61282, 'f751bcb1300da9142c2058b6b1ba007998a541d77e9f5a95bc284873b55e438f', '{"id": "CERT-2300-318", "slug": "cert-2300-318", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "serial_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1898, "date_display": "1898", "sort_date": "1898-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hobo", "source_publication": "Scribner''s Magazine, Vol. XXIII (Jan.–June 1898), pp. 264, 440 [Walter A. Wyckoff, ''The Workers — The West''] (catalog misdated 1887)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Google Books", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/scribnersmagazi11logagoog/page/n276/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Bound Contents Title p. (leaf n7: Vol. XXIII, Jan.–June 1898); p. 264 (leaf n276); p. 440 (leaf n452)", "verbatim_quote": "Get out of this, then, you d-- hobo, or I''ll put you out! ... lousy with hoboes in an hour.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1898, "linguistic_note": "Trap A Red-Team Falsification #1: Serial start-year metadata misdate (1887 stamped onto 1898 bound volume)", "analysis_notes": "Classic Trap A serial metadata trap: Google Books / IA item scribnersmagazi11logagoog carries metadata date ''1887'' (founding year of Scribner''s Magazine), creating a false pre-1889 hit for Wyckoff''s Chicago tramp narrative (''d-- hobo'', ''fifty hoboes'', ''lousy hobo''). Visual inspection of leaf n7 proves Volume XXIII, January–June 1898 (+11 years later).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-318.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1898 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-224', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1898, '1898-10-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_forum-and-century_1898-10_26_2/page/n89/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-224.webp', 55056, 'e5b64aadf33ddc4f4d4125c93af7e5d63c1940556bfce1837a90290bac96c423', '{"id": "CERT-2300-224", "slug": "cert-2300-224", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1898, "date_display": "1 Oct 1898", "sort_date": "1898-10-01", "short_gloss": "hobo in E. Lamar Bailey, ''Tramps and Hoboes,'' The Forum (New York, NY)", "source_publication": "E. Lamar Bailey, ''Tramps and Hoboes,'' The Forum (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_forum-and-century_1898-10_26_2/page/n89/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 26, No. 2 (Oct. 1898), p. 218 (leaf 90)", "verbatim_quote": "A tramp is not a hobo; a hobo is not a tramp; a vagrant is neither; a criminal is none of these. [...] The term \"hobo\" was not originally of evil significance. It originated in the West, when the great tide of humanity swept in that direction; and it was applied to the many who, failing of their first hopes, were forced to the necessity of tramping from community to community in quest of employment.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1898, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Seminal 1898 sociological taxonomy anticipating Reitman (1908), Tucker (1917), and Anderson (1923) by a decade: explicitly records Western origin of ''hobo'' and defines the hobo as an itinerant tradesman/worker forced onto the road in search of employment (''brought his trade into use when necessity pressed, he was a hobo''), contrasting him with the non-working ''tramp'' (professional beggar) and the town-bound ''vagrant'' (''loafs around a town as long as he can. He does not jump trains'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-224.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1898 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-309', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'Hobo', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/trampingwithtra00willgoog/page/n393/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-309.webp', 43036, '7364b671b551cf107cf01df2c3d2214b6d5e99b696bdaa4e7fec0e548acfde29', '{"id": "CERT-2300-309", "slug": "cert-2300-309", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "Hobo", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "1899", "sort_date": "1899-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Hobo", "source_publication": "Josiah Flynt, Tramping with Tramps (New York: The Century Co., 1899); paired with H. L. Mencken, The American Language (1st ed., New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1919)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/trampingwithtra00willgoog/page/n393/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Flynt pp. 388, 392, 394 (leaves n387, n391, n393); Mencken 1919 p. 161 (leaf n176)", "verbatim_quote": "Hobo: a tramp. Derivation obscure. Farmer''s \"Americanisms\" gives: M Ho-Boy, or Haut-Boy : a New Yo", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "H1c: Directionality audit — 1890s inland tramp ''''bo'' as aphetic clipping of ''hobo'' (not its pre-1889 source)", "analysis_notes": "Crucial chronological distinction (Lighter HDAS; Green''s Dict. of Slang): inland tramp ''''bo'' (''a hobo; fellow tramp'', first attested 1893 Chicago Record ''he calls ye Ho an ye calls him Bo'') is consistently printed by Flynt (1899, pp. 388, 392) with an initial apostrophe (\"''boes'' (hoboes)\", \"''Bo: a hobo\") and analyzed by Mencken (1919, p. 161) as aphetic clipping (''bo for hobo, an altogether fit successor to bum for bummer''), which merged in the 1890s with older vocative bo.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-309.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-232', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'rejected_lead', 'serial_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1900, '1900-10-01', 'https://archive.org/details/railwayconducto01brakgoog/page/n119/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-232.webp', 123370, '2bcb9175a179d6cc63b9d9039dcce2c6300a71179031139773ffb146532d23c7', '{"id": "CERT-2300-232", "slug": "cert-2300-232", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "serial_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1900, "date_display": "1 Oct 1900", "sort_date": "1900-10-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hobo", "source_publication": "The Railway Conductor (Cedar Rapids, IA)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Google Books", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/railwayconducto01brakgoog/page/n119/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 17 (1900), p. 780 (leaf 120; 1900 index leaf 8)", "verbatim_quote": "This class rapidly develop taste for that nomadic life which is characteristic of the genuine hobo [MISDATED 1889 IN METADATA; ACTUAL VOLUME = VOL. 17, 1900, INDEXING SEPT. 8, 1900 GALVESTON STORM]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1900, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Trap A Bound-Serial Misdating Falsification: Google Books / Internet Archive item ''railwayconducto01brakgoog'' carries catalog year ''1889'' (date of journal rename), which would falsely mimic an 1889 railroader citation, but visual inspection of leaf 8 index (''Ruins Galveston, Texas, Storm, Sept. 8, 1900... 822'') and p. 780 pagination proves the bound volume is Vol. 17 (late 1900). Identical Trap A misdating was caught and excluded on TheRailroadTelegrapherV17 (cataloged 1891, actual 1900).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-232.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1900 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-314', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hawbuck', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi03wrig/page/n107/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-314.webp', 35330, '6b95ce9a7b959a15396f543274ab849bba87bd9c1ee2f8f20c93bcc9822037d3', '{"id": "CERT-2300-314", "slug": "cert-2300-314", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hawbuck", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "1902", "sort_date": "1902-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: hawbuck", "source_publication": "Joseph Wright, The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. III: H–L (London: Henry Frowde, 1902), p. 96; Dialect Notes, Vol. I, Part IX (1896), p. 418", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi03wrig/page/n107/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Wright EDD Vol. III Title (n6) & p. 96 col. 2 (n107); Dialect Notes I p. 418 (n427)", "verbatim_quote": "hawbuck (n.) : a tomboy, (v.) To act boisterously. (\"0,\" 1865.) N. J. head", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "H3b: Phonological & geographic audit of ''hawbuck'' / ''hawbaw'' -> *?hobo (weakened_or_unlikely_pathway per Rule 5.4)", "analysis_notes": "Audits Barnhart (1988, p. 485) / Liberman (2008) ''hawbuck'' hypothesis: (1) Phonologically, ''hawbuck'' /ˈhɔːbʌk/ (skeleton KWPK) -> ''hobo'' /ˈhoʊboʊ/ (skeleton KP) fails the consonant-skeleton threshold because final velar -k deletion AND unstressed /ʌ/ -> /oʊ/ rounding lack regular American English parallels; Wright''s West Yorkshire ''HAWBAW'' [ō·bō] (p. 96) avoids -k but lacks any attested U.S. export; (2) In U.S. dialect (Dialect Notes 1896, p. 418, New Jersey 1865), ''hawbuck'' meant ''a tomboy'', with zero Western rail-camp bridge. Kept as PHONOLOGICAL-HYPOTHESIS (weakened_or_unlikely_pathway) per Rule 5.4.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-314.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-315', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'Hoboken', 'rejected_lead', 'folk_etymology_myth', 'agent_verified', 1925, '1902-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishlanguagei01krap_0/page/n195/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-315.webp', 31360, 'aa4eae6992adfbf11d6c6e98a8bf7bb68a9b2b9d21898e84bd83654ecf4292db', '{"id": "CERT-2300-315", "slug": "cert-2300-315", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "Hoboken", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "folk_etymology_myth", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1925, "date_display": "1902 / 1925", "sort_date": "1902-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Hoboken", "source_publication": "George Philip Krapp, The English Language in America, Vol. I (New York: Century Co., 1925), p. 176; Sylva Clapin, A New Dictionary of Americanisms (New York, 1902), p. 228", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishlanguagei01krap_0/page/n195/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Clapin 1902 p. 228 (n247); Krapp 1925 Vol. I Title (n6) & p. 176 (n195)", "verbatim_quote": "and names like Hohokus, Hoboken, Kalamazoo, Keokuk, Oshkosh, Skaneateles, names of real places, h", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1925, "linguistic_note": "H4 Myth #1: Falsified Hoboken, NJ terminal / Lackawanna boxcar stencil folk-etymology", "analysis_notes": "Falsified on three independent axes: (1) 2,500-mile geographic & chronological inversion — zero pre-1889 NJ/NY vagrant attestations while 1888–1889 emergence is strictly Western (WA/OR/ID/KS; Clapin 1902 p. 228 ''Originally Western''); (2) architectural anachronism — the famous Lackawanna Railroad Terminal in Hoboken opened in 1907 (19 years after 1888 PNW usage); (3) phonological & onomastic mismatch — Hoboken (< Lenape Hopoghan Hackingh ''tobacco-pipe land'', skeleton KPKN) was a stock vaudeville comic place-name alongside Kalamazoo and Oshkosh (Krapp 1925, I:176), retrofitted onto hobo in 20th-c. lore.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-315.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1925 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-225', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-01-15', 'https://archive.org/details/juvenileinstruct392geor/page/n6/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-225.webp', 76952, '94edb0f1ceb65e51a51d683c62fbd07c7b2268a1722e8ee25ff53f1ff5283edf', '{"id": "CERT-2300-225", "slug": "cert-2300-225", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "15 Jan 1904", "sort_date": "1904-01-15", "short_gloss": "hobo in The Juvenile Instructor (Salt Lake City, UT)", "source_publication": "The Juvenile Instructor (Salt Lake City, UT)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/juvenileinstruct392geor/page/n6/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 39, No. 2 (Jan. 15, 1904), p. 37 (leaf 7; masthead leaf 3)", "verbatim_quote": "At this juncture a brakeman came up and swore to seeing Allon with a few others board the train that morning in Ogden, and that it was useless for him to try any \"bluff-work\" here, as anybody could see by his appearance that he was a hobo from away east. At that time Allon really did not know what the term \"hobo\" meant, or implied.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Published in Salt Lake City, UT: narrates an immigrant Mormon laborer''s experience during the post-Sept. 1885 Rock Springs, WY ''Chinese trouble'' (naming Bishop John Sharp, Elder George Nebeker, Brother Alexander Carr, and the Union Pacific superintendent from Omaha), when an Ogden-to-Salt Lake City brakeman had him arrested at the Salt Lake depot as ''a hobo from away east''—explicitly noting that the newly arrived listener ''really did not know what the term \"hobo\" meant, or implied'' when railroaders on the Ogden-Salt Lake corridor already used it routinely.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-225.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-226', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'HOBO', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1908, '1908-02-01', 'https://archive.org/details/eliminationoftra00kelluoft/page/n133/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-226.webp', 44184, 'd9d498fb77d1342ccfe929d13531974c32c62235b32b71ff9456ef8e4661bca5', '{"id": "CERT-2300-226", "slug": "cert-2300-226", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "HOBO", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1908, "date_display": "1 Feb 1908", "sort_date": "1908-02-01", "short_gloss": "HOBO in Edmond Kelly, The Elimination of the Tramp (New York & London: G. P. Putnam''s Sons)", "source_publication": "Edmond Kelly, The Elimination of the Tramp (New York & London: G. P. Putnam''s Sons)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/eliminationoftra00kelluoft/page/n133/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Appendix B (''Dr. Reitman''s Classification''), pp. 103-104 (leaf 134; title leaf 9)", "verbatim_quote": "1. TRAMP a. Dreams and wanders. b. Trampdom—Main lines of railroads. [...] 2. HOBO a. Works and wanders. b. Hoboland—farms, ice-houses, section houses, mines, etc. [...] 3. BUM a. Drinks and wanders. b. Bumville—Barrel houses and saloons.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1908, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "MAJOR TAXONOMY BREAKTHROUGH: Verifies in primary Feb. 1908 book print (Preface dated Jan. 22, 1908) that Chicago ''King of the Hoboes'' Dr. Ben L. Reitman formulated and printed the exact epigrammatic triad—''1. TRAMP: Dreams and wanders; 2. HOBO: Works and wanders; 3. BUM: Drinks and wanders''—fifteen years before Nels Anderson''s May 1923 The Hobo! Also attests ''Hoboland'' (connecting hobos directly to railroad ''section houses, mines, etc.''; also reported in The Dawson News, Jan. 22, 1908, p. 10, https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053283/1908-01-22/ed-1/seq-10/, and Harper''s Weekly, Feb. 6, 1909, https://archive.org/details/sim_harpers-weekly_1909-02-06_53_2720).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-226.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1908 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-227', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1908, '1908-09-01', 'https://archive.org/details/industrialworker00unse/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-227.webp', 37386, '97c274a341d0ab9224e4536c3ca61adc80fcf4945be00a4945926096a4504e9f', '{"id": "CERT-2300-227", "slug": "cert-2300-227", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1908, "date_display": "1 Sep 1908", "sort_date": "1908-09-01", "short_gloss": "hobo in I.W.W. Songs: To Fan the Flame of Discontent (Spokane, WA: Spokane Local Unions of the I.W.W.)", "source_publication": "I.W.W. Songs: To Fan the Flame of Discontent (Spokane, WA: Spokane Local Unions of the I.W.W.)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/industrialworker00unse/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "4-leaf blue pocket song card, Rear 412-20 Front Ave., Spokane, Wash., p. 3 (leaf 3; cover leaf 1)", "verbatim_quote": "HALLELUJAH Tune: \"Revive Us Again.\" [...] Hallelujah, I''m a bum! [...] I can''t buy a job— / For I ain''t got the dough; / So I ride in a box car, / For I''m a hobo!", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1908, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Rare surviving Spokane I.W.W. 4-page pocket song card (''Rear 412-20 Front Ave., SPOKANE, WASH.'') issued on the eve of the 1908-1909 Spokane Free-Speech Fight (by J. H. Walsh''s Spokane I.W.W. Industrial Union hall, immediately preceding the 1909 first edition of the Little Red Songbook): prints Harry ''Haywire Mac'' McClintock''s ''Hallelujah, I''m a Bum!'' explicitly linking employment-agency (''job-shark'') fees to boxcar-riding ''hobo'' identity.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-227.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1908 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-228', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1909, '1909-11-24', 'https://archive.org/details/v1n36-nov-24-1909-IW/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-228.webp', 111682, '6001c79600d49618327292d3d5f0f5e536becbacb5c42c59dbd1725b6b31ad1e', '{"id": "CERT-2300-228", "slug": "cert-2300-228", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1909, "date_display": "24 Nov 1909", "sort_date": "1909-11-24", "short_gloss": "hobo in Industrial Worker (Spokane, WA)", "source_publication": "Industrial Worker (Spokane, WA)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/v1n36-nov-24-1909-IW/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 1, No. 36 (Nov. 24, 1909), p. 2, cols. 2 & 4 (leaf 2; masthead leaf 1)", "verbatim_quote": "It is the \"lean and hungry\" hobo, the expropriated worker, who is denied a home, a family, a steady right to slave even [...] [col. 4:] Is it any wonder that the country is full of hoboes? A hobo is better off than a lumberjack", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1909, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Published in Spokane, WA during the height of the Nov. 1909 I.W.W. Free-Speech Fight (when hundreds of Wobblies were jailed for defying Spokane''s street-speaking ban against employment-agency ''job sharks''; Edited by A. E. Cousins / James Wilson). Reclaims ''hobo'' as the revolutionary migratory proletarian (''industrial unionism''); p. 3 of the same issue also attests the infinitive verb ''get a gang to hobo out to Spokane.''", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-228.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1909 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-126', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_a_serial_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1912, '1912-05-01', 'https://archive.org/details/railwayconducto02brakgoog', 0, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-126.webp', 54490, '4aa909bdff6a4b857439c49861a2137fa6fb7724e044eafd3502f64216556a28', '{"id": "CERT-2300-126", "slug": "cert-2300-126", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_a_serial_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1912, "date_display": "May 1912", "sort_date": "1912-05-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hobo", "source_publication": "The Railway Conductor (Cedar Rapids, IA), Vol. 29 [misdated ''1889'' in Google Books/IA]", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Google Books (railwayconducto02brakgoog)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/railwayconducto02brakgoog", "page_and_column": "Vol. 29 (1912), p. 376 (leaf 404; title leaf 7)", "verbatim_quote": "visions soon vanished, for some \"hobo\" had been in ahead of me. He did the vanishing act to my lunch", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1912, "linguistic_note": "American rail-tramp / underworld attention-call shibboleth ''Hobo!'' (''Obo!''), generalized to itinerant rail-tramp and migratory worker (c. 1885-1889)", "analysis_notes": "TRAP A KILL (Serial Bound-Volume Metadata Misdate): Cataloged as ''1889'' on Google Books and IA from Vol. 1''s start era, but Panel A title page prints ''VOLUME XXIX / 1912'' (and text mentions ''James Eades How, \"the millionaire hobo\"''). Same Trap A defect exposed in railwayconducto01brakgoog (''1889'' -> c. 1899) and McClure''s Vol. 8 mccluresmagazin08unkngoog (''1893'' -> 1896-1897).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-126.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1912 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-316', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'rejected_lead', 'folk_etymology_myth', 'agent_verified', 1915, '1915-01-12', 'https://panewsarchive.psu.edu/lccn/sn86081330/1915-01-12/ed-1/seq-4/', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-316.webp', 40180, '0fff479b8a617d7028cf96615fcd8f0867a875fcd177a6c45ee8f2ed289c1890', '{"id": "CERT-2300-316", "slug": "cert-2300-316", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "folk_etymology_myth", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1915, "date_display": "12 Jan 1915", "sort_date": "1915-01-12", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hobo", "source_publication": "The Star-Independent (Harrisburg, PA), Jan. 12, 1915, p. 4", "source_archive": "Pennsylvania Newspaper Archive", "source_url": "https://panewsarchive.psu.edu/lccn/sn86081330/1915-01-12/ed-1/seq-4/", "page_and_column": "Page 1 masthead & Page 4 cols. 4–5 (''Hoboes Are Neither Tramps Nor Bums'')", "verbatim_quote": "Why, the word ''hobo'' means ''good man.'' Consult your Latin: homo means man, bonus means good.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1915, "linguistic_note": "H4 Myth #2: Falsified Latin ''homo bonus'' (''good man'' / medieval pass) folk-etymology", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan catches the exact invention milieu of the Latin ''homo bonus'' myth 5 months earlier than Green''s June 13, 1915 Tulsa citation: at a Jan. 1915 New York Hotel de Gink publicity event, itinerant ''Hobo King'' Jeff Davis declared ''the word hobo means good man. Consult your Latin: homo means man, bonus means good.'' Zero pre-1915 documentary link; skeleton KNPNS vs. KP fails.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-316.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1915 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-123', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'HOBOHEMIA', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1915, '1915-06-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_current-opinion_1915-06_58_6', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-123.webp', 31996, '4287186c25bb7caae2157e7e030baf041595e1be5037fd162dfb9f8757bd7254', '{"id": "CERT-2300-123", "slug": "cert-2300-123", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "HOBOHEMIA", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1915, "date_display": "Jun 1915", "sort_date": "1915-06-01", "short_gloss": "HOBOHEMIA in Current Opinion (New York), Vol. 58, No. 6", "source_publication": "Current Opinion (New York), Vol. 58, No. 6", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sim_current-opinion_1915-06_58_6)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_current-opinion_1915-06_58_6", "page_and_column": "Vol. 58, No. 6, p. 429 (leaf 52)", "verbatim_quote": "HOBOHEMIA: As depicted by Stuart Davis, one of the youngest and most original among American humorists of the brush", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1915, "linguistic_note": "American rail-tramp / underworld attention-call shibboleth ''Hobo!'' (''Obo!''), generalized to itinerant rail-tramp and migratory worker (c. 1885-1889)", "analysis_notes": "Antedates Sinclair Lewis''s Apr. 7, 1917 Saturday Evening Post story ''Hobohemia'' by 22 months (and Nels Anderson''s 1923 The Hobo by 8 years) in the New York artistic/modernist bohemia blend sense; followed in Dec. 1915 by Marianne Moore in Others (Vol. 1, No. 6, p. 106, ''would-be-truthful \"hobohemia\"''), while OED''s 1908 Reading Times baseline attests the earlier tramp-realm blend.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-123.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1915 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-319', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'rejected_lead', 'serial_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1916, '1916-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/centurymagazine92newyrich/page/n1013/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-319.webp', 22816, '1e3e17ea0ed4a4f4385c6ad88ce0c1856ab9be2d65d9a6008dbbfa58c8e3bb40', '{"id": "CERT-2300-319", "slug": "cert-2300-319", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "serial_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1916, "date_display": "1916", "sort_date": "1916-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hobo", "source_publication": "The Century Illustrated Monthly Magazine, Vol. XCII, New Series Vol. LXX (May–Oct. 1916), p. 958 (catalog misdated 1881)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/centurymagazine92newyrich/page/n1013/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Volume Title p. (leaf n4: Vol. XCII, May–Oct. 1916); p. 958 col. 1 (leaf n1013)", "verbatim_quote": "Unemployed nothing! he snorted. I''m a real hobo, and not one of those poor fools", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1916, "linguistic_note": "Trap A Red-Team Falsification #2: Serial start-year metadata misdate (1881 stamped onto 1916 bound volume)", "analysis_notes": "Classic Trap A serial metadata trap: IA item centurymagazine92newyrich carries start-date ''1881'' (the year Scribner''s Monthly became The Century Magazine), generating a spurious 1881 antedating for ''I''m a real hobo''. Visual inspection of the bound title page (leaf n4) and copyright verso (leaf n5) proves May–October 1916 (+35 years later).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-319.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1916 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-229', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'Hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1917, '1917-02-25', 'https://archive.org/details/oakland-tribune-1917-02-25/page/n43/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-229.webp', 130686, '88cfb6bcfc4c2991e9d29ba205d2888524ff44e1da3484ccce98d2db46c2b024', '{"id": "CERT-2300-229", "slug": "cert-2300-229", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "Hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1917, "date_display": "25 Feb 1917", "sort_date": "1917-02-25", "short_gloss": "Hobo in Oakland Tribune (Oakland, CA; wire dated Chicago, Feb. 24, 1917)", "source_publication": "Oakland Tribune (Oakland, CA; wire dated Chicago, Feb. 24, 1917)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/oakland-tribune-1917-02-25/page/n43/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 44, col. 4 (leaf 44; magazine masthead leaf 1)", "verbatim_quote": "According to the Rev. Irwin St. John Tucker, president of the International Hobo College here. Here are the definitions as he gives them: Hobo—A migratory worker. Tramp—A migratory non-worker. Bum—A stationary non-worker.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1917, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "MAJOR TAXONOMY BREAKTHROUGH: Pinpoints the exact pre-1923 print formulation of Rev. Irwin St. John Tucker''s analytical 2x2 mobility-by-labor matrix (''Hobo—A migratory worker. Tramp—A migratory non-worker. Bum—A stationary non-worker''), dispatched from Chicago on Feb. 24, 1917—six years before Nels Anderson quoted Tucker in May 1923—and defining the hobo as ''the man who does the heavy productive labor of the country—railroad construction, bridge building and crop harvesting.''", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-229.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1917 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-124', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'Hobohemia', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1917, '1917-04-07', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_saturday-evening-post_1917-04-07_189_41', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-124.webp', 46432, '2ac3d7a845d6b9c812ce04ee96d6106bd1a88eb2a569226321a9fc45e0905331', '{"id": "CERT-2300-124", "slug": "cert-2300-124", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "Hobohemia", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1917, "date_display": "7 Apr 1917", "sort_date": "1917-04-07", "short_gloss": "Hobohemia in The Saturday Evening Post (Philadelphia, PA), Vol. 189, Issue 41 — Sinclair Lewis, ''Hobohemia''", "source_publication": "The Saturday Evening Post (Philadelphia, PA), Vol. 189, Issue 41 — Sinclair Lewis, ''Hobohemia''", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sim_saturday-evening-post_1917-04-07_189_41)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_saturday-evening-post_1917-04-07_189_41", "page_and_column": "Vol. 189, No. 41, p. 4, col. 2 (leaf 5)", "verbatim_quote": "And at last she had been in Hobohemia. Hobohemia is the place and state of being talented and free.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1917, "linguistic_note": "American rail-tramp / underworld attention-call shibboleth ''Hobo!'' (''Obo!''), generalized to itinerant rail-tramp and migratory worker (c. 1885-1889)", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified primary source for Sinclair Lewis''s April 7, 1917 Saturday Evening Post story ''Hobohemia'' (previewed Apr. 3, 1917 in Conway AR Log Cabin Democrat, LoC sn85033000), which popularized both ''Hobohemia'' and ''Hobohemian'' 6 years before Nels Anderson''s 1923 sociological monograph cited by Etymonline.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-124.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1917 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-307', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hoboys', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1920, '1920-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYK-565_5/page/n97/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-307.webp', 38844, 'c265a37237abaa492509f94434821f0763f89c99c4b7387472f6780206707aa3', '{"id": "CERT-2300-307", "slug": "cert-2300-307", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hoboys", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1920, "date_display": "1920", "sort_date": "1920-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: hoboys", "source_publication": "H. R. Jeffrey, ''HOBO'', Dialect Notes, Vol. V, Part III (1920), pp. 86–87; paired with Democratic Watchman (Bellefonte, PA), Sept. 28, 1877, p. 5", "source_archive": "Internet Archive & Pennsylvania Newspaper Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYK-565_5/page/n97/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Dialect Notes V pp. 86–87 (leaves n97–n98); Democratic Watchman Sept. 28, 1877 p. 5 col. 1", "verbatim_quote": "these me EARLY COLONIAL ITEMS. 87 came to be called ‘‘hoboys.’’ Then those who travelled along the tracks, not carr", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1920, "linguistic_note": "H1a: Western railroad / mail-clerk vocative hail ''Ho, boy!'' / ''Ho! bo!'' -> agentive noun ''hobo''", "analysis_notes": "Strongest geographic and prosodic H1 pathway: Jeffrey (1920, pp. 86–87) documents 1880s Oregon Short Line (ID/OR/WY) mail-carriers'' call ''Ho, boy!'' -> ''hoboys'' -> apocopated ''hobo'', matching the exact 1888–1889 PNW/Idaho/Oregon epicenter; Democratic Watchman (Sept. 28, 1877, p. 5) confirms pre-1889 night-duty camp hail ''Ho! boys! Ho! boys!''. Also calibrated alongside Poetto (Acme 27, 1974, pp. 273–78) ''hob'' + ''-o'' suffixation and Liberman (2008) ''ho, beau!''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-307.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1920 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-125', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo jungle', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1922, '1922-07-08', 'https://archive.org/details/oakland-tribune-1922-07-08', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-125.webp', 107094, '8d0611105ed37b4244b15b851ce72e429e66c6a021be27d2358c3be7c4dc04e1', '{"id": "CERT-2300-125", "slug": "cert-2300-125", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo jungle", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1922, "date_display": "8 Jul 1922", "sort_date": "1922-07-08", "short_gloss": "hobo jungle in Oakland Tribune (Oakland, CA)", "source_publication": "Oakland Tribune (Oakland, CA)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (oakland-tribune-1922-07-08)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/oakland-tribune-1922-07-08", "page_and_column": "p. 14 (Daily Magazine Page), col. 4 (leaf 13)", "verbatim_quote": "bummed his way into a town on a freight train at night, broke into a safe [...] and then drifted off into the hobo jungle of another town.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1922, "linguistic_note": "American rail-tramp / underworld attention-call shibboleth ''Hobo!'' (''Obo!''), generalized to itinerant rail-tramp and migratory worker (c. 1885-1889)", "analysis_notes": "Pre-1923 Western newspaper scan-verified witness of the full compound ''hobo jungle'' alongside freight-riding yegg safecrackers (corroborating OED''s 1905 Daily Utah State Journal compound baseline and Green''s 1897 Anaconda Standard simplex ''jungle'' baseline).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-125.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1922 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-312', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'originated', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1923, '1923-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/hobosociologyofh00ande_0/page/n109/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-312.webp', 34868, '020c42314ce456cc2d60e3033f155a66718c4a3c5fb51ac711b2b6ab116627f4', '{"id": "CERT-2300-312", "slug": "cert-2300-312", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "originated", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1923, "date_display": "1923", "sort_date": "1923-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: originated", "source_publication": "Nels Anderson, The Hobo: The Sociology of the Homeless Man (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1923), pp. 88–89", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/hobosociologyofh00ande_0/page/n109/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title p. (leaf n6), p. 88 (leaf n109), p. 89 n.1 (leaf n112)", "verbatim_quote": "The name originated from the words “hoe-boy” plainly derived from work on the farm. A", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1923, "linguistic_note": "H2b: Post-1889 activist rationalization of ''hobo'' < ''hoe-boy'' (weakened_or_unlikely_pathway)", "analysis_notes": "Pinpoints how ''hoe-boy'' entered popular etymology 33 years after baseline: Hobo College president Nicholas Klein (Dearborn Independent, March 18, 1922, quoted in Anderson 1923, pp. 88–89; cf. Mencken American Language / Supplement I & DePastino 2003) promoted ''hoe-boy'' (''plainly derived from work on the farm'') to dignify migratory ''hobos who work'' over non-working ''tramps'' and ''bums''. Classified PHONOLOGICAL-HYPOTHESIS (weakened_or_unlikely_pathway) per Rule 5.4.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-312.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1923 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2300-230', 'TGT-066', 'hobo', NULL, 'hobo', 'hobo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1923, '1923-05-01', 'https://archive.org/details/hobosociologyofh00ande/page/n110/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-230.webp', 61286, '5cde8ee8ceb9958184be40dcb7f80ba495d90823676c040ea66c2286f5056346', '{"id": "CERT-2300-230", "slug": "cert-2300-230", "case_slug": "hobo", "headword": "hobo", "attested_form": "hobo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1923, "date_display": "1 May 1923", "sort_date": "1923-05-01", "short_gloss": "hobo in Nels Anderson, The Hobo: The Sociology of the Homeless Man (Chicago: University of Chicago Press)", "source_publication": "Nels Anderson, The Hobo: The Sociology of the Homeless Man (Chicago: University of Chicago Press)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/hobosociologyofh00ande/page/n110/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Ch. VI (''The Hobo and the Tramp''), p. 87 (leaf 111; May 1923 copyright verso leaf 8)", "verbatim_quote": "Dr. Ben L. Reitman [...] has defined the three principal types of the hobo. He says: There are three types of the genus vagrant: the hobo, the tramp, and the bum. The hobo works and wanders, the tramp dreams and wanders and the bum drinks and wanders.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1923, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified May 1923 first edition (University of Chicago Press): canonizes both ''Hobohemia'' (Chicago''s West Madison ''main stem'') and the twin formulations of Dr. Ben L. Reitman (first printed Feb. 1908, CERT-2300-226) and St. John Tucker (first printed Feb. 1917, CERT-2300-229) on p. 87, followed on pp. 88-89 by M. Kuhn and Hobo College president Nicholas Klein (citing Dearborn Independent, March 18, 1922, advancing the ''hoe-boy'' agricultural tool conjecture).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hobo/CERT-2300-230.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1923 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-314', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hocus', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1785, '1785-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/grosevulgartongue/page/n109/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-314.webp', 79038, 'ae6164b35393f9067a85ba52d3e186d0030eb20f2d1dde2a4f040ec745b5a8bb', '{"id": "CERT-1600-314", "slug": "cert-1600-314", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hocus", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1785, "date_display": "1785", "sort_date": "1785-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Hocus", "source_publication": "Francis Grose, A Classical Dictionary of the Vulgar Tongue, 1st ed. (London: S. Hooper, 1785), title leaf n4 & sig. O2r (leaf n109); paired with Albert Barrere & Charles G. Leland, A Dictionary of Slang, Jargon & Cant, Vol. I (Ballantyne Press, 1889), recto title leaf n6 & p. 466 (leaf n493)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/grosevulgartongue/page/n109/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Grose 1785 title leaf n4 & leaf n109; Barrere & Leland 1889 Vol. I recto title leaf n6 & p. 466 (leaf n493)", "verbatim_quote": "Hocus Pocus, nonsensical words used by juglers, previous to their deceptions, as a kind of charm, or incantation. ... Hocus is also used to express drunkenness, as, he is quite hocus, he is quite drunk. [Barrere & Leland 1889, I:466: In the Romany tongue hoc or huk is the root signifying deceit or falsehood ... hoc, hocben, huckaben, hokeny, and hooker.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1785, "linguistic_note": "Eighteenth-century conjurer''s Hocus Pocus / clipped Hocus (Grose 1785) alongside Anglo-Romani hoc / huckaben (Barrère & Leland 1889)", "analysis_notes": "Plates both canonical 18th-century Grose (1785, MDCCLXXXV) s.v. Hocus Pocus / Hocus and Barrere & Leland''s 1889 Vol. I (p. 466) s.v. Hocus-pocus, showing that Victorian slang lexicography explicitly linked jugglers'' hocus-pocus with Romani hoc/hocben/huckaben ten years before the 1899 New York Clipper appearance of hokum.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-314.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1785 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-315', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Bunkum', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1828, '1828-09-27', 'https://archive.org/details/americanglossary014322mbp/page/n138/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-315.webp', 71900, '6ba61cb0770938da011224e3e11428887c77313b4902343844dd0ac2dc794d4f', '{"id": "CERT-1600-315", "slug": "cert-1600-315", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Bunkum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1828, "date_display": "27 Sep 1828", "sort_date": "1828-09-27", "short_gloss": "Bunkum in John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues, Vol. III (London, 1893), recto title leaf n4 & p. 324 (leaf n329); paired", "source_publication": "John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues, Vol. III (London, 1893), recto title leaf n4 & p. 324 (leaf n329); paired with Richard H. Thornton, An American Glossary, Vol. I (London: Francis & Co., 1912), recto title leaf n8 & p. 121 (leaf n138, citing Niles''s Register, Sept. 27, 1828 [printed ''1827''], xxxv. 66/2)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/americanglossary014322mbp/page/n138/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Farmer & Henley Vol. III recto title leaf n4 & p. 324 (leaf n329); Thornton Vol. I recto title leaf n8 & p. 121 (leaf n138)", "verbatim_quote": "\"Talking to Bunkum!\" This is an old and common saying at Washington, when a member of congress is making one of those hum-drum and unlistened to \"long talks\" ... in local phrase, he called \"Bunkum.\" -- Niles''s Register, Sept. 27: xxxv. 66/2. [Farmer & Henley 1893, III:324: HOCUS, subs. -- 1. A cheat; an imposter. [An abbreviation of HOCUS-POCUS (q.v.).]]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1828, "linguistic_note": "Ambimorphemic velar-pivot blend ho[c]us (/ˈhoʊ.kəs/) + bun[k]um (/ˈbʌŋ.kəm/) -> hokum (/ˈhoʊ.kəm/)", "analysis_notes": "Remediates the verso title leaf and right-margin clipping on CERT-1600-305 by plating Farmer & Henley''s 1893 Vol. III recto title (MDCCCXCIII, leaf n4) + full unclipped col. 1 Hocus entry alongside Thornton''s 1912 American Glossary (p. 121, leaf n138), proving phonetic ''-um'' spelling ''Bunkum'' appeared from the very earliest 1828 Washington attestation (Niles''s Register Vol. 35 = Sept. 27, 1828).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-315.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1828 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-307', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'HOKY-POKY', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1846, '1846-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/halliwellarchaicandprovincialwords01/page/n462/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-307.webp', 57256, '68f04dda690b1dfb23c2ae5304f91f089e3ef33f2f11e850b473b5e4b8a9de03', '{"id": "CERT-1600-307", "slug": "cert-1600-307", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "HOKY-POKY", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1846, "date_display": "1846", "sort_date": "1846-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: HOKY-POKY", "source_publication": "James Orchard Halliwell, A Dictionary of Archaic and Provincial Words, Vol. I (London: John Russell Smith, 1846), p. 455, col. 1 (leaf n462); paired with Farmer & Henley, Slang and Its Analogues, Vol. III (1893), p. 330, col. 2 (leaf n335)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/halliwellarchaicandprovincialwords01/page/n462/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Halliwell Vol. I p. 455 (leaf n462; title leaf n6); Farmer & Henley Vol. III p. 330 (leaf n335; title leaf n5)", "verbatim_quote": "HOKY-POKY. Hocus-pocus. North. [Farmer & Henley 1893, III:330: HOKEY-POKEY, subs. (common). -- 1. A cheat; a swindle; nonsense. [From Hocus Pocus.] 2. (common). -- A cheap ice-cream sold in the streets.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1846, "linguistic_note": "English hoky-poky / hokey-pokey (''cheat, swindle, nonsense; cheap street ware''), model for the 1919 ''pokey / hokey'' rhyme", "analysis_notes": "Demonstrates that by 1893 hokey-pokey already combined BOTH ''cheat; swindle; nonsense'' and ''cheap crowd-pleasing street confection'', reinforcing the semantic convergence of stage trickery and cheap mass appeal in hokum (1899), and later directly catalyzing the 1919 coinage of theatrical hokey, adj. via overt rhyme (''mostly pokey and mainly \"hokey.\"'', Variety, July 25, 1919, CERT-1600-116/217).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-307.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1846 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-306', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Bunkum', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1848, '1848-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dictionaryameri08bartgoog/page/n88/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-306.webp', 49906, 'aa88b00ab0d25205bc054221c59712d8b9b13649b77808c1d247a7164123e140', '{"id": "CERT-1600-306", "slug": "cert-1600-306", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Bunkum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1848, "date_display": "1848", "sort_date": "1848-01-01", "short_gloss": "Bunkum in John Russell Bartlett, Dictionary of Americanisms: A Glossary of Words and Phrases Usually Regarded as Peculiar to the United Stat", "source_publication": "John Russell Bartlett, Dictionary of Americanisms: A Glossary of Words and Phrases Usually Regarded as Peculiar to the United States (New York: Bartlett and Welford, 1848), pp. 54-55 (leaf n88); paired with Albert Barrere & Charles G. Leland, A Dictionary of Slang, Jargon & Cant, Vol. I (1889), pp. 202-203 (leaf n231)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dictionaryameri08bartgoog/page/n88/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Bartlett 1848 pp. 54-55 (leaf n88; title leaf n7); Barrere & Leland 1889 Vol. I pp. 202-203 (leaf n231; title leaf n9)", "verbatim_quote": "BUNKUM. Judge Haliburton of Nova Scotia, thus explains this very useful and expressive word ... \"Well, when a critter talks for talk sake, jist to have a speech in the paper to send to home, and not for any other airthly puppus but electioneering, our folks call it Bunkum.\" [Barrere & Leland 1889, I:202: Buncombe or bunkum (American), talking big, affecting enthusiasm, but always with an underhand purpose.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1848, "linguistic_note": "American bunkum (/ˈbʌŋ.kəm/, supplying the unstressed -kum coda and applause-trap semantics)", "analysis_notes": "Establishes both the phonological rime (-kum) and exact pragmatic-semantic template of hokum: bunkum denoted insincere, affected performance calculated solely to win applause from an uncritical constituency/gallery (and later back-formed bunk, n.2 [1900] in exact structural parallel to hokum [1899] -> hoke, n. [1913]).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-306.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1848 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-118', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'rejected_lead', 'homograph_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-12-06', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_punch_1856_31/page/n225/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-118.webp', 112944, '4c896a4525964ffe5ebc0a79b1382a98af7a35050321cad2b79fb6da86bb1860', '{"id": "CERT-1600-118", "slug": "cert-1600-118", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "homograph_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "6 Dec 1856", "sort_date": "1856-12-06", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hokum", "source_publication": "Punch, or the London Charivari (London), Vol. 31, Dec. 6, 1856, p. 222, col. 1 (leaf n225) & Dec. 20, 1856, p. 248, col. 2 (leaf n251)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_punch_1856_31/page/n225/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 31, p. 222, col. 1 (Dec. 6, 1856; paired with p. 248, col. 2, Dec. 20, 1856)", "verbatim_quote": "It''s a dele plesenter to snooze in the prisun chapel than to be swettin on the mill or the crank, or tarein your ands to peaces with that blessed hokum.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "RED-TEAM FALSIFICATION (Trap C Homograph): Posted to ADS-L on July 19, 2026 (msg 169647, https://listserv.linguistlist.org/pipermail/ads-l/2026-July/169647.html) under ''Further Antedating of Hokum''. Scan & dialect audit proves fictional convict ''James Hardy'' exhibits systematic Cockney h-dropping (''ands'', ''ard'', ''onest'') paired with hypercorrect h-prothesis (''hesy'' = easy, ''hern'' = earn, ''hokum'' = penal oakum-picking; cf. Dec. 20, 1856 p. 248 ''tese hokum'' = tease oakum, and July 1911 Current Literature ''pick hoakum!'', https://archive.org/details/sim_current-opinion_1911-07_51_1). Etymologically unrelated to US theatrical ''hokum''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-118.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-318', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'rejected_lead', 'homograph_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-12-06', 'https://archive.org/details/punch30a31lemouoft/page/n509/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-318.webp', 99446, 'aca90f04ac2b322cba0426c6ce97a0e31d06a1c47dcef3289053bdcd70eb16d0', '{"id": "CERT-1600-318", "slug": "cert-1600-318", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "homograph_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "6 Dec 1856", "sort_date": "1856-12-06", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hokum", "source_publication": "Punch, or the London Charivari (London: 85 Fleet Street, 1856), Vol. XXXI, upright title leaf n0 (sim_punch_1856_31); Sat., Dec. 6, 1856, p. 222, col. 1 (punch30a31lemouoft leaf n509); & Sat., Dec. 20, 1856, p. 248, col. 2 (sim_punch_1856_31 leaf n251)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/punch30a31lemouoft/page/n509/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. XXXI title leaf n0; Dec. 6, 1856, p. 222, col. 1; Dec. 20, 1856, p. 248, col. 2", "verbatim_quote": "It''s a dele plesenter to snooze in the prisun chapel than to be swettin on the mill or the crank, or tarein your ands to peaces with that blessed hokum. ... To be sure, all this wood''nt be ''arf as hesy as shippin'' your prigs off in cartlodes to Horstralia, or sich like, or settin'' on ''em to turn a crank or work a mill, or tese hokum.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Unified landmark plate combining the upright 1856 Vol. XXXI Fleet Street title page (remediating sideways cartoon leaf n8 in CERT-1600-309) with BOTH of Bill Mullins''s July 19, 2026 ADS-L citations (ads-l/2026-July/169647.html): (1) corrects Mullins''s ''8 Dec'' citation to the printed Saturday issue header ''[DECEMBER 6, 1856.]'' on p. 222 (''tarein your ands to peaces with that blessed hokum''), and (2) pairs Dec. 20, 1856 p. 248 (''Horstralia... turn a crank or work a mill, or tese hokum'' = tease oakum under Col. Joshua Jebb), proving convict ''James Hardy'' systematically drops etymological /h-/ (''onest'', ''ands'', ''ead'', ''''arf'') and adds hypercorrect Cockney /h-/ (''hout'', ''hany'', ''hup'', ''hesy'', ''Horstralia'', ''horsifers'', ''hokum'' = oakum).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-318.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-309', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'rejected_lead', 'homograph_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-12-20', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_punch_1856_31/page/n251/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-309.webp', 70572, 'fed2ad110d3cfd124153050ed7f848ace00ebf64c5a6a1c3307a9d8a2482d3c5', '{"id": "CERT-1600-309", "slug": "cert-1600-309", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "homograph_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "20 Dec 1856", "sort_date": "1856-12-20", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hokum", "source_publication": "Punch, or the London Charivari (London), Vol. 31, Dec. 20, 1856, p. 248, col. 2 (''Set a Thief to Catch a Thief'', IA sim_punch_1856_31 leaf n251)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_punch_1856_31/page/n251/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 31 (Dec. 20, 1856), p. 248, col. 2 (leaf n251; title leaf n8)", "verbatim_quote": "\"To be sure, all this wood ''nt be ''arf as hesy as shippin'' your prigs off in cartlodes to Horstralia, or sich like, or settin'' on ''em to turn a crank or work a mill, or tese hokum.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Pairs with CERT-1600-118 (Dec. 6, 1856, p. 222) to refute Bill Mullins''s July 19, 2026 ADS-L post (msg 169647, ''[Ads-l] Further Antedating of \"Hokum\"''). In the very same sentence, convict ''James Hardy'' writes ''''arf as hesy'' (= half as easy), ''Horstralia'' (= Australia), and ''turn a crank or work a mill, or tese hokum'' (= tease oakum, the canonical Victorian hard-labor triad of crank, treadwheel, and teasing tarred hemp oakum). Also independently explains Walter J. Kingsley''s 1920s-1930s Cockney ''hoakum'' (< oakum) folk etymology cited in Mencken 1936 (CERT-1600-225).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-309.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-304', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1857, '1857-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cambrianjournal04cambuoft/page/194/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-304.webp', 53590, 'd8de67f41e6d11c9b87d4399bc94ffffb7d7ae5b5b0654f04424adc956cfa71f', '{"id": "CERT-1600-304", "slug": "cert-1600-304", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1857, "date_display": "1857", "sort_date": "1857-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: hokum", "source_publication": "L. P. Barnaschone, ''Manners and Customs of the People of Tenby in the Olden Time'', The Cambrian Journal, Vol. IV (Tenby: R. Mason; London: Longmans & Co., 1857 [imprint 1858]), p. 194 (leaf n201)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cambrianjournal04cambuoft/page/194/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. IV (1857), p. 194 (leaf n201; title leaf n5)", "verbatim_quote": "No. 1 re-enters, metamorphosed,-- \"Here comes I, a doctor, A ten pound doctor; I''ve a little bottle in my pocket, Called hokum, skokum, alicompane; I''ll touch his eyes, nose, mouth and chin, And say, ''rise dead man,'' and he''ll fight again.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1857, "linguistic_note": "Stage-mountebank mock-Latin incantation hokum, skokum (< hocus-pocus / hocum-pocum)", "analysis_notes": "Traces Chambers''s 1864 Book of Days Tenby mummers'' play text (CERT-1600-117) back 7 years to its original 1857 publication by L. P. Barnaschone in The Cambrian Journal (Vol. IV, p. 194). Proves exact orthographic ''hokum'' (< mock-Latin hocus-pocus) was already spoken on the popular folk stage as a comic Quack-Doctor''s cure-all nostrum formula 42 years before American variety ''hokum'' (1899), though still bound inside rhyming ''hokum, skokum, alicompane'' rather than standalone American theatrical syntax.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-304.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1857 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-316', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1857, '1857-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cambrianjournal04cambuoft/page/n201/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-316.webp', 91286, '62524af24d83bef6f0ebaf56f39ca96d8adb1484dfff8e4f23da7ae6846ca095', '{"id": "CERT-1600-316", "slug": "cert-1600-316", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1857, "date_display": "1857", "sort_date": "1857-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: hokum", "source_publication": "The Cambrian Journal, Vol. IV (London: Longmans & Co.; Tenby: R. Mason, 1857), recto title leaf n4 & p. 194 (leaf n201); corroborated by Edward Laws, The History of Little England Beyond Wales (London: George Bell & Sons, 1888), p. 416 (leaf n473)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cambrianjournal04cambuoft/page/n201/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Cambrian Journal Vol. IV recto title leaf n4 & p. 194 (leaf n201); Laws 1888 p. 416 (leaf n473)", "verbatim_quote": "No. 1 re-enters, metamorphosed,-- \"Here comes I, a doctor, A ten pound doctor; I''ve a little bottle in my pocket, Called hokum, skokum, alicompane; I''ll touch his eyes, nose, mouth and chin, And say, ''rise dead man,'' and he''ll fight again.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1857, "linguistic_note": "Folk-drama Quack-Doctor mock-pharmaceutical -um remodeling of hocus-pocus and elecampane -> hokum, skokum, alicompane", "analysis_notes": "Supplies the verified 1857 recto title page (leaf n4, replacing verso printer''s leaf n5 in CERT-1600-304) and pairs it with Edward Laws''s 1888 Pembrokeshire printing (b21781023, p. 416), demonstrating how apothecary/nostrum Latinate neuter ''-um'' remodeled hocus-pocus into ''hokum, skokum'' in itinerant stage doctor patter 42 years before 1899.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-316.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1857 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-117', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1864, '1864-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bookofdaysmiscel02chamuoft/page/741/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-117.webp', 94808, 'e4b7588fd2985b8cad7cc2cdb31d6c5f20c50ae51f41aba9a948371747872763', '{"id": "CERT-1600-117", "slug": "cert-1600-117", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1864, "date_display": "1864", "sort_date": "1864-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: hokum", "source_publication": "Robert Chambers, ed., The Book of Days: A Miscellany of Popular Antiquities, Vol. II (London & Edinburgh: W. & R. Chambers, 1864), p. 741, col. 1 (IA leaf n753)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bookofdaysmiscel02chamuoft/page/741/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. II, p. 741, col. 1", "verbatim_quote": "Here come I, a doctor, / A ten-pound doctor; / I''ve a little bottle in my pocket, / Called hokum, shokum, alicampane;", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1864, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Folk-drama precursor bridge: Documents exact orthographic ''hokum'' in the traditional English Mummers'' Play Quack-Doctor''s patter (''hokum, shokum, alicampane'' < pseudo-Latin ''hocum-pocum'' / ''hocus-pocus''). Demonstrates that ''hokum'' existed in 19th-century popular dramatic mountebank performance before blending with American ''bunkum'' in late-1890s minstrel/vaudeville slang.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-117.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1864 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-301', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokkeny', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1873, '1873-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishgipsiesth01lela/page/89/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-301.webp', 41804, '86e87c19d86fb3409faf6be43087f8c7d037fbf6627a7aca8dc4051c1bac897c', '{"id": "CERT-1600-301", "slug": "cert-1600-301", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokkeny", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1873, "date_display": "1873", "sort_date": "1873-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: hokkeny", "source_publication": "Charles Godfrey Leland, The English Gipsies and Their Language (London: Trubner & Co., 1873), Ch. V, p. 89 (leaf n100) & Ch. VIII, p. 149 (leaf n160)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishgipsiesth01lela/page/89/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 89 (leaf n100) & p. 149 (leaf n160); title page leaf n7", "verbatim_quote": "Quite a little family of words has come into English from the Rommany, Hocben, huckaben, hokkeny, or hooker, all meaning a lie, or to lie, deception and humbug. ... Mr Barrow [sic] calls this hokkeny baro, the great swindle. I may remark, by the way, that among jugglers and \"show-people\" sleight of hand is called hanky panky.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1873, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Romani hokkeny (/ Hocben, huckaben, hokkerben (vs. KN ''lie, deception, humbug, swindle'' (< Romani khokhav-)", "analysis_notes": "Evaluates Anglo-Romani hokkeny, explicitly situated by Philadelphia-born folklorist C. G. Leland among ''jugglers and \"show-people\"'' alongside hanky-panky and hocus-pocus. Assessed as a plausible secondary fairground/showfolk reinforcement on the primary hocus(-pocus) x bunkum blend rather than a standalone unblended loan (since pre-1900 US circus/pitchman manuals show 0 occurrences of hokum).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-301.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1873 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-313', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokkerben', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1873, '1873-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishgipsiesth01lela/page/n62/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-313.webp', 65530, 'fcffdb108d8cc4d4bfafd285653a96ec3a6ad5d2eeb30d1472ceff0b0f18dc30', '{"id": "CERT-1600-313", "slug": "cert-1600-313", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokkerben", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1873, "date_display": "1873", "sort_date": "1873-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: hokkerben", "source_publication": "Charles Godfrey Leland, The English Gipsies and Their Language (London: Trubner & Co., 1873), recto title leaf n6, p. 43 (leaf n62) & p. 81 (leaf n100); paired with C. G. Leland, The Gypsies (Boston: Houghton, Mifflin & Co., 1882), recto title leaf n8", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishgipsiesth01lela/page/n62/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "1873 recto title leaf n6, p. 43 (leaf n62), p. 81 (leaf n100); 1882 recto title leaf n8", "verbatim_quote": "\"What did he blow on a pipe for?\" \"Just for hokkerben, to humbug them.\" ... Quite a little family of words has come into English from the Rommany, Hocben, huckaben, hokkeny, or hooker, all meaning a lie, or to lie, deception and humbug.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1873, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Romani huckaben / hokaben (''lie, deception'') and hokkerben (''to humbug'')", "analysis_notes": "Remediates verso-leaf & left-margin gutter issues noted during visual inspection of CERT-1600-301/303 by plating the crisp upright recto title pages of BOTH Leland''s 1873 Trubner edition (leaf n6) and 1882 Houghton Mifflin American edition (leaf n8), plus unclipped p. 43 ''Just for hokkerben, to humbug them'' and full-width p. 81 ''Hocben, huckaben, hokkeny''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-313.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1873 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-302', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hokkano', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1874, '1874-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.78403/page/n61/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-302.webp', 44256, 'df3eba645d33a70bd9404f65630a8099eb3585cef0dfb1ff033078b8284e6822', '{"id": "CERT-1600-302", "slug": "cert-1600-302", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hokkano", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1874, "date_display": "1874", "sort_date": "1874-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Hokkano", "source_publication": "George Borrow, Romano Lavo-Lil: Word-Book of the Romany; or, English Gypsy Language (London: John Murray, 1874), p. 51 (leaf n61); paired with Bath C. Smart & Henry Thomas Crofton, The Dialect of the English Gypsies, 2nd ed. (London: Asher & Co., 1875), p. 83 (leaf n110)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.78403/page/n61/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Borrow p. 51 (leaf n61; title leaf n6); Smart & Crofton p. 83 (leaf n110; title leaf n11)", "verbatim_quote": "Hokka, v. n. To lie, tell a falsehood: hokka tute mande, if you tell me a falsehood. Hokkano, s. A lie. Sans. Kuhana (hypocrisy). [Smart & Crofton 1875, p. 83: Hokkaben, s., Lie, falsehood (hookapen, hoax); Hokkano, s., Liar, lie; adj., false.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1874, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Romani Hokkano (''a lie; false'') and Hokkaben (''lie, falsehood, hoax'')", "analysis_notes": "Confirms canonical 1874-1875 Anglo-Romani lexical entries Hokkano (''a lie; adj. false'') and Hokkaben (''lie, falsehood, hoax''), documenting both nominal (''nonsense/falsehood'', Sense 2) and adjectival (''false'') semantics 25 years before the 1899 New York Clipper attestation.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-302.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1874 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-312', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hook um snivey', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_c_idiom_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1874, '1874-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangdictionary02hottgoog/page/n208/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-312.webp', 53948, '6a14b0e4d370bd338950e539c329f3f0b8e0a971a61e8d8e60469a1a4869fb4e', '{"id": "CERT-1600-312", "slug": "cert-1600-312", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hook um snivey", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_c_idiom_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1874, "date_display": "1874", "sort_date": "1874-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Hook um snivey", "source_publication": "John Camden Hotten, The Slang Dictionary, Etymological, Historical, and Anecdotal, rev. ed. (London: Chatto and Windus, 1874), p. 195 (leaf n208)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangdictionary02hottgoog/page/n208/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 195 (leaf n208; title leaf n7)", "verbatim_quote": "Hook um snivey (formerly \"HOOK and SNIVEY\"), a low expression, meaning to cheat by feigning sickness or other means. Also a piece of thick iron wire crooked at one end, and fastened into a wooden handle, for the purpose of undoing from the outside the wooden bolt of a door.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1874, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Separates American regional/tramp hokum-snivvy (''stew of unspecified ingredients'', 1968 DARE / Green''s dtatsoq) from theatrical hokum: Hotten''s 1874 ''Hook um snivey (formerly \"HOOK and SNIVEY\")'' proves hokum-snivvy continues 1785 British cant hook and snivey (''feeding a man and dog for nothing'' by feigning illness) via unstressed ''and'' -> ''-um'' labial assimilation, not theatrical hokum.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-312.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1874 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-303', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokkani', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1882, '1882-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/gypsiesleland00lelaiala/page/348/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-303.webp', 49756, 'e1c324f9e5034d2ae597d06c49503975a3b9c82d7ddaeec8030264abcb691c2f', '{"id": "CERT-1600-303", "slug": "cert-1600-303", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokkani", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1882, "date_display": "1882", "sort_date": "1882-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: hokkani", "source_publication": "Charles Godfrey Leland, The Gypsies (Boston: Houghton, Mifflin and Company, 1882), pp. 345, 348 (leaf n359); paired with Albert Thomas Sinclair (ed. George F. Black), An American-Romani Vocabulary (New York Public Library, 1915 [compiled pre-1911]), p. 9 (leaf n10)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/gypsiesleland00lelaiala/page/348/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Leland 1882 p. 348 (leaf n359; title leaf n5); Sinclair 1915 p. 9 (leaf n10; title leaf n3)", "verbatim_quote": "The hokkani boro, or great trick, was brought by the gypsies from the East. It has been practiced by them all over the world, it is still played every day somewhere. This chapter was written long ago in England. I am now in Philadelphia ... [Sinclair 1915, p. 9: huka, to cheat; hukaben, a lie.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1882, "linguistic_note": "American Romani hokkani boro (''great trick/swindle'') and hukaben (''a lie'')", "analysis_notes": "Establishes pre-1899 American geographic presence of Romani hokkani (''swindle/trick'') and hukaben (''lie'') in Philadelphia (Leland 1882, p. 348) and New England tent/fairground circuits (Sinclair pre-1911 MSS), matching the exact Philadelphia/Wilmington/New York corridor of the Oct. 1899 Clipper ''Hokum Club'' and ''hokum or gravey acts'' notices.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-303.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1882 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-310', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'HOCUM', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_c_proper_noun_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1882, '1882-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/per_scotus_1882_103/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-310.webp', 39638, '5a2fb98d551dc7f3cf3dc67c970e4a55c5d8998589d6dcc13ada66253cfe09b9', '{"id": "CERT-1600-310", "slug": "cert-1600-310", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "HOCUM", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_c_proper_noun_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1882, "date_display": "1882", "sort_date": "1882-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: HOCUM", "source_publication": "Transcript of Record, Supreme Court of the United States, October Term, 1882, No. 103: The United States, Plaintiff in Error, vs. John A. Knowles and Hocum Hosford (Washington, D.C., 1882; also Vol. 166 [1889])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_scotus_1882_103/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Cover / p. 1 (leaf n0 & title leaf n2)", "verbatim_quote": "TRANSCRIPT OF RECORD. SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES. No. 103. THE UNITED STATES, PLAINTIFF IN ERROR, vs. JOHN A. KNOWLES AND HOCUM HOSFORD. IN ERROR TO THE CIRCUIT COURT OF THE UNITED STATES FOR THE DISTRICT OF MASSACHUSETTS.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1882, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Eliminates pre-1899 American legal and newspaper hits for Hocum Hosford (1825-1881, mayor and mill treasurer of Lowell, MA), a Trap C personal given name unrelated to theatrical hocum/hokum.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-310.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1882 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-204', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hokum', 'attestation', 'corpus_candidate', 'agent_verified', 1889, '1889-09-28', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1889-09-28_37_29/page/n9/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-204.webp', 151834, '16db284af56dec9f7c14c6e4412fb8f4eac95e5ea4aafeb04dcf3e05d3b0b5e6', '{"id": "CERT-1600-204", "slug": "cert-1600-204", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "corpus_candidate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1889, "date_display": "28 Sep 1889", "sort_date": "1889-09-28", "short_gloss": "Hokum in The New York Clipper, Vol. 37, No. 29 (New York: Frank Queen Publishing Co., Sept. 28, 1889; corroborated in Pittsburg Dispatch, Se", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper, Vol. 37, No. 29 (New York: Frank Queen Publishing Co., Sept. 28, 1889; corroborated in Pittsburg Dispatch, Sept. 24, 1889, p. 4)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library / Illinois)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1889-09-28_37_29/page/n9/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 37, No. 29, leaf n9, col. 1 (masthead leaf n0: ''NEW YORK, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 28, 1889'')", "verbatim_quote": "The third act opens in the postmaster''s residence, where the disguised lord has everything his own way. He makes himself known as the son of Old Hutch, and marries the postmaster''s daughter. The cast: Silas Hokum, a new postmaster, Kirtland Calhoun; Hi Hand, a tramp printer, Frank David; Slabs, a bootblack, Tony Murphy; Major Gunning, a druggist, Richard Quilter.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1889, "masthead_year": 1889, "linguistic_note": "1889 comic rural/Yankee stage-character surname ''Silas Hokum'' in Pittsburgh journalist-dramatist George C. Jenks''s 3-act musical farce-comedy (premiered Macauley''s Theatre, Louisville, KY, Sept. 9, 1889), showing theatrical comic ''-um'' formation (cf. Bunkum, Slocum, Oakum + hoax/hocus/hoky) 10 years before common-noun ''hokum'' in the same theatrical weekly.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of The New York Clipper, Sept. 28, 1889 (masthead n0; leaf n9, col. 1; also reviewed in Pittsburg Dispatch, Sept. 24, 1889: ''a brightly conceived character in Silas Hokum, the postmaster'') confirms exact spelling ''Silas Hokum'' in a traveling farce-comedy cast alongside ''Hi Hand, a tramp printer'' and ''Slabs, a bootblack''. Proof plate: CERT-1600-204_ny_clipper_1889_09_28_silas_hokum_precursor.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-204.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1889 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-320', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Horsman', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1889, '1889-11-16', 'https://archive.org/details/harpersweekly33harp/page/n924/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-320.webp', 103106, '279b09c1786dd58ffa5659556e80b198885be3ebb677e63c319aa504445f159f', '{"id": "CERT-1600-320", "slug": "cert-1600-320", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Horsman", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1889, "date_display": "16 Nov 1889", "sort_date": "1889-11-16", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Horsman", "source_publication": "Harper''s Weekly, Vol. XXXIII, No. 1717 (New York, Nov. 16, 1889), title leaf n5 & p. 920 (leaf n924); paired with C. A. M. Fennell, The Stanford Dictionary of Anglicised Words and Phrases (Cambridge Univ. Press, 1892), title leaf n7 & p. 166 (leaf n181)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/harpersweekly33harp/page/n924/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Harper''s Weekly Vol. 33 p. 920 (leaf n924); Stanford Dictionary p. 166 (leaf n181)", "verbatim_quote": "\"Halma\" will be sent free to any address on receipt of one dollar. E. I. Horsman, Publisher, 80 and 82 William St., N. Y. [OCR: ''E. f. Hokum an''] ... 1602 that bonum & malum [''and bad''], vertue and vice [OCR: ''1603 that hoKum & mtUttm''].", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1889, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TRAP B DIRTY-OCR GHOST SHIELD: Visually falsifies representative pre-1906 IA OCR hallucinations (''E. I. Horsman'' -> ''E. f. Hokum an'' via rsm->kum; italic ''bonum'' -> ''hoKum'' via b->h, n->K; Century Dict. 1889 ''nasus'' -> ''HOKUM''), alongside exhaustive programmatic audit of all 296 pre-1906 Georgia Historic Newspapers (Open-ONI) ''hokum'' hits -- every one of which proved to be Trap B/C noise (including 6 syndicated printings of Josh Billings''s phonetic eye-dialect ''bekum'' [''become''] OCR-garbled into transitive verb ''hokum'': e.g., Dawson Weekly Journal, Mar. 19, 1874, p. 1 ''Phibbers hev bin known to hokum [bekum] liars''; plus ''D. M. Borum'' / ''Rev. Hermann Bokum'' -> ''Hokum'', ''GEORGIA'' -> ''G HOKUM'', ''RHEUMATISM'' -> ''HOKUM ATISM'', and ''Bogus Checks'' -> ''Hokum Checks'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-320.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1889 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-305', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hocus', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1893, '1893-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue03farmuoft/page/324/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-305.webp', 42196, '2510cd114e3acef3a049272e49a394eed597dbe084f2f13c49cb9f77bc225cf0', '{"id": "CERT-1600-305", "slug": "cert-1600-305", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hocus", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1893, "date_display": "1893", "sort_date": "1893-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hocus in John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues Past and Present, Vol. III (London: Printed for Subscribers Only, 1893), p.", "source_publication": "John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues Past and Present, Vol. III (London: Printed for Subscribers Only, 1893), p. 324, col. 1 (leaf n329)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue03farmuoft/page/324/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. III, p. 324, col. 1 (leaf n329; title leaf n5)", "verbatim_quote": "Hocus, subs. (old: now recognised). -- 1. A cheat; an imposter. [An abbreviation of HOCUS-POCUS (q.v.).] 1654. Witts Recreations. Here HOCAS lyes with his tricks and his knocks, Whom death hath made sure as a juglers box.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "Clipped disyllabic English hocus (/ˈhoʊ.kəs/) overlapping with bunkum (/ˈbʌŋ.kəm/) on shared medial /kə/ -> hokum (/ˈhoʊ.kəm/)", "analysis_notes": "Verifies that clipped simplex Hocus (''cheat, impostor, juggler''s trickery'', 1654-) was fully lexicalized before 1899, eliminating the objection that blending full four-syllable hocus-pocus + bunkum would be prosodically unwieldy: disyllabic trochaic ho[k]us (/ˈhoʊ.kəs/) and bun[k]um (/ˈbʌŋ.kəm/) share an identical medial velar stop pivot /k/ + unstressed schwa coda, yielding overlapping portmanteau hokum (/ˈhoʊ.kəm/)—the exact derivation proposed by Variety''s editor on May 8, 1909 (CERT-1600-110) and retained in Sept. 17, 1910 ''pronounce a few hokum words'' (CERT-1600-112/210).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-305.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-311', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hokum', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_c_foreign_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1893, '1893-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.490365/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-311.webp', 86564, '33ad43ca095394df7683fb2992f12186f66e39e52230d79e8f97826a3b34bac5', '{"id": "CERT-1600-311", "slug": "cert-1600-311", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hokum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_c_foreign_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1893, "date_display": "1893", "sort_date": "1893-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Hokum", "source_publication": "Khususol Hokum (1893, Digital Library of India / Internet Archive in.ernet.dli.2015.490365)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.490365/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Leaves n2, n5 (1893)", "verbatim_quote": "Khususol Hokum [1893 non-English Arabic/Malay-script legal treatise where Romanized catalog title ''Hokum'' represents Arabic hukm ''legal judgment, decree, ordinance''].", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Eliminates the sole pre-1905 exact-match ''hokum'' monograph returned by Internet Archive''s date:[1800 TO 1905] query: scan inspection confirms an Arabic-script Islamic legal text whose Romanized title word ''Hokum'' transcribes Arabic hukm (''judgment, rule'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-311.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-202', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'end men', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-05-01', 'https://archive.org/details/witmarkamateurmi00dumo/page/n19/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-202.webp', 78126, 'f4e6197cf1d57989c10d36c226dfa8768b774482107f21b8f6c525d897908777', '{"id": "CERT-1600-202", "slug": "cert-1600-202", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "end men", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "1 May 1899", "sort_date": "1899-05-01", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: end men", "source_publication": "Frank Dumont (of Dumont''s Minstrels, Philadelphia), The Witmark Amateur Minstrel Guide and Burnt Cork Encyclopedia (Chicago, New York & London: M. Witmark & Sons, 1899)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Smithsonian Libraries copy stamped May 1, 1899)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/witmarkamateurmi00dumo/page/n19/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Section II, p. 15 (IA leaf n19; title leaf n4 stamped May 1, 1899; copyright leaf n6)", "verbatim_quote": "In rehearsing the gags with the end men, be careful to impress upon them the necessity of selecting those of a varied nature in order to avoid similarity of subjects. One end man may represent the enlightened, sarcastic darkey; another, the dense fellow—jolly, but ignorant.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1899, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "Printed parlor/amateur minstrel gag-books (9 works, 1863-1905, 2,301,844 chars) yield 0x hokum/hoke/hokey even in Philadelphia minstrel impresario Frank Dumont''s May 1899 manual—proving ''hokum'' circulated as backstage professional variety/minstrel performers'' cant (cf. CERT-1600-205..206, Oct. 1899) rather than printed end-man dialogue.", "analysis_notes": "Sweeping 9 digitized minstrel songsters, Ethiopian farces, and end-man manuals (1863-1905, 2,301,844 chars: Hooley 1863, Dumont 1880/1899/1905, Chops 1886, National Minstrel Folio 1889, Marble 1893, Janson 1899, Jackson 1903) yields 0 occurrences of hokum/hoke/hokey. Proof plate: CERT-1600-202_dumont_1899_witmark_minstrel_guide_negative.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-202.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-205', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hokum', 'attestation', 'corpus_candidate', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-10-14', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1899-10-14_47_33/page/n10/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-205.webp', 161452, 'c83ddd7c0ec079de12b0b0930f4681d6d9725dbf660545f6224a06f51a81f9da', '{"id": "CERT-1600-205", "slug": "cert-1600-205", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "corpus_candidate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "14 Oct 1899", "sort_date": "1899-10-14", "short_gloss": "Hokum in The New York Clipper, Vol. 47, No. 33 (New York: Frank Queen Publishing Co., Oct. 14, 1899), ''Vaudeville and Minstrel'' Dept.", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper, Vol. 47, No. 33 (New York: Frank Queen Publishing Co., Oct. 14, 1899), ''Vaudeville and Minstrel'' Dept.", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1899-10-14_47_33/page/n10/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 47, No. 33, leaf n10, col. 2 (''Vaudeville and Minstrel'' dept.; masthead leaf n0: ''NEW YORK, OCTOBER 14, 1899'')", "verbatim_quote": "WILLIAM SILBOR, of Silbor and Emerson, was fortunate enough to save a child''s life from under the hoofs of a team, drawing a heavily laden truck, in one of the streets of Philadelphia. The members of Hokum Club, of that city, presented him with a neatly engraved medal for his bravery.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1899, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "Philadelphia vaudeville/minstrel performers'' ''Hokum Club'' attested Oct. 14, 1899 (two weeks before Bryan & Norman''s Oct. 28, 1899 lowercase ''hokum or gravey acts'' notice following their Wilmington, Del. date 28 miles south of Philadelphia), proving ''hokum'' was already entrenched in Delaware Valley variety/minstrel slang by autumn 1899.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of The New York Clipper, Oct. 14, 1899 (''Vaudeville and Minstrel'' column, leaf n10, col. 2; front masthead n0) confirms ''The members of Hokum Club, of that city [Philadelphia], presented him [vaudevillian William Silbor, of Silbor and Emerson] with a neatly engraved medal''. Proof plate: CERT-1600-205_ny_clipper_1899_10_14_hokum_club_philadelphia.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-205.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-106', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-10-28', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1899-10-28_47_35/page/n19/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-106.webp', 103828, '14bbbf73aab9bd47b225d7f0d1c61d34f038f358fe394652e16e7a78480bec99', '{"id": "CERT-1600-106", "slug": "cert-1600-106", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "28 Oct 1899", "sort_date": "1899-10-28", "short_gloss": "hokum in The New York Clipper (New York), Vol. 47, No. 35, Saturday, Oct. 28, 1899, p. 732, col. 4 (IA leaf n19), Ed. Bryan & Madge Norman T", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper (New York), Vol. 47, No. 35, Saturday, Oct. 28, 1899, p. 732, col. 4 (IA leaf n19), Ed. Bryan & Madge Norman Trade Card", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Illinois Digital Newspaper Collections", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1899-10-28_47_35/page/n19/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 47, No. 35, p. 732, col. 4 (IA leaf n19; masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "TO MANAGERS.--DON''T book this act if your audience likes hokum or gravey acts, or if you have a hickey audience that is looking for slate stick stuff.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Antedates OED hokum, n. (1917) by 17 yrs, 4 mos, Green''s hokum, adj. (Feb. 13, 1915) by 15 yrs, 4 mos, Green''s/Wilton (Mar. 3, 1906) by 6 yrs, 4 mos, and Fred Shapiro''s July 19, 2026 ADS-L benchmark (Jan. 20, 1906) by 6 yrs, 2 mos. Unquoted lowercase ''hokum'' coordinated with ''gravey'' modifying ''acts'' (''hokum or gravey acts'') alongside ''hickey audience'' and ''slate [slap] stick stuff'' proves ''hokum'' was established variety/minstrel booking jargon by autumn 1899.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-106.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-206', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-10-28', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1899-10-28_47_35/page/n19/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-206.webp', 152220, '7b886919fc4e30360e4df741229f29da9acd10412f828616faf17233f83c08c4', '{"id": "CERT-1600-206", "slug": "cert-1600-206", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "28 Oct 1899", "sort_date": "1899-10-28", "short_gloss": "hokum in Ed. Bryan (for Ed. Bryan & Madge Norman), Professional Display Notice, The New York Clipper, Vol. 47, No. 35 (New York: Frank Queen", "source_publication": "Ed. Bryan (for Ed. Bryan & Madge Norman), Professional Display Notice, The New York Clipper, Vol. 47, No. 35 (New York: Frank Queen Publishing Co., Oct. 28, 1899)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1899-10-28_47_35/page/n19/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 47, No. 35, p. 732, col. 4 (IA leaf n19; front masthead leaf n0: ''NEW YORK, OCTOBER 28, 1899'')", "verbatim_quote": "BRYAN AND NORMAN MADE THE BIGGEST HIT EVER MADE BY AN IRISH SKETCH TEAM IN MY HOUSE.—W. L. DOCKSTADER, WILMINGTON, DEL., OCT. 21. ED. BRYAN and MADGE NORMAN PRESENTING CASEY''S ST. PATRICK''S DAY ORATION. TO MANAGERS.—DON''T book this act if your audience likes hokum or gravey acts, or if you have a hickey audience that is looking for slate stick stuff; neither is this act suitable for a Dutch audience.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1899, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "Pinpoints the birth of lowercase unquoted ''hokum'' to 1890s American variety and blackface-minstrel knockabout stage cant (''hokum or gravey acts ... slate stick stuff'', placed immediately after playing minstrel impresario W. L. [Lew] Dockstader''s house in Wilmington, Del.), 6 yrs, 4 mos before Broadway chorus-girl columns (McCardell, Mar. 3, 1906) and 14 years before pitchman columns (1913).", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of The New York Clipper, Oct. 28, 1899, p. 732, col. 4 (leaf n19; masthead n0; independently locked to Oct. 1899 by adjacent Boswells Lumiere ad showing the Sept. 29-30, 1899 Dewey Parade and Oct. 1899 Columbia-Shamrock America''s Cup race) confirms crisp lowercase unquoted ''hokum or gravey acts'' contrasted with ''a hickey audience that is looking for slate stick stuff'' (literal split-wood slap-stick). Proof plate: CERT-1600-206_ny_clipper_1899_10_28_bryan_norman_hokum_acts.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-206.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-321', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hokum', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_c_proper_noun_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-12-08', 'https://archive.org/details/NYTimes-Dec1-1899-Jan31-1900/page/n143/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-321.webp', 132256, '350e6f083f2765b2967a1dce7f2303a2958e76e4f0336c8ed0da0788579b057b', '{"id": "CERT-1600-321", "slug": "cert-1600-321", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hokum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_c_proper_noun_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "8 Dec 1899", "sort_date": "1899-12-08", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Hokum", "source_publication": "The New York Times (New York, NY), Fri., Dec. 8, 1899, p. 7, col. 4 (''New York''s Oldest Lawyer: Benjamin Douglas Silliman, in 95th Year, Resumes Active Practice''; IA NYTimes-Dec1-1899-Jan31-1900 leaf n143)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/NYTimes-Dec1-1899-Jan31-1900/page/n143/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 7, col. 4 (leaf n143)", "verbatim_quote": "He rested until June at his home, 56 Clinton Street, Brooklyn, when he went to his country seat, Hokum, near Babylon. He returned to Brooklyn last week and on Wednesday went to his New York office ready to transact the business of his clients.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TRAP C PROPER-NOUN TOPONYM KILL: Eliminates a seductive Dec. 8, 1899 New York Times ''Hokum'' hit occurring just weeks after the Oct. 28, 1899 New York Clipper vaudeville attestation: physical scan confirms ''his country seat, Hokum, near Babylon'' is Benjamin Douglas Silliman''s Long Island country estate at West Islip/Babylon (< local Algonquian Hocum / Hocum Neck), alongside 1899 NYT ''Hocum Pocum Lane'' (Hither Green, London, leaf n270 of NYTimes-Jun-Jul-1899) and 16th-c. Somerset surname ''John Hokum'' (Somerset Record Soc. Vol. 4, 1887, leaf n247).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-321.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-201', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'buncombe', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1901, '1901-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sawdustspangless00coupiala/page/n151/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-201.webp', 84336, '6debad018e01a4e055cc9c14e5b7d0823d5277e83f1b66a47356873cea06b32c', '{"id": "CERT-1600-201", "slug": "cert-1600-201", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "buncombe", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1901, "date_display": "1901", "sort_date": "1901-01-01", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: buncombe", "source_publication": "W. C. Coup, Sawdust & Spangles: Stories & Secrets of the Circus (Chicago: Herbert S. Stone & Co., 1901; MDCCCCI)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sawdustspangless00coupiala/page/n151/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Ch. VII (''Parades and Band Wagons''), p. 136 (IA leaf n151; title leaf n6; copyright leaf n7)", "verbatim_quote": "VII. PARADES AND BAND WAGONS. Shows thrive best on bluster and buncombe. Years of experience have taught me that the traveling show business handled by capitalists who have been trained in other lines of enterprise can never succeed.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1901, "masthead_year": 1901, "linguistic_note": "Refutes pure 19th-century circus-canvasman origin: 1,581,077 chars across 5 circus works (1884-1921) contain 0x hokum/hoke/hokey; Coup (1901) uses ''bluster and buncombe'' and Conklin (1921, IA TheWaysOfTheCircus leaf n197) quotes sideshow shell-workers calling ''Three shells — Hocus, Mocus, Pocus''", "analysis_notes": "Full-text regex sweep across 5 digitized circus histories and memoirs (1,581,077 chars) yields 0 occurrences of hokum/hocum/hoakum/hoke/hokey, whereas Barnum''s partner W. C. Coup (1901, p. 136, leaf n151) explicitly writes ''Shows thrive best on bluster and buncombe.'' Proof plate: CERT-1600-201_coup_1901_sawdust_spangles_circus_negative.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-201.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1901 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-308', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'HOKEY', 'rejected_lead', 'dialect_false_friend_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi03wriguoft/page/201/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-308.webp', 41426, '9d503cdd6095683a505fc25aec1c3254b61a6ba62184725ec7d6bbb5b353492d', '{"id": "CERT-1600-308", "slug": "cert-1600-308", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "HOKEY", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "dialect_false_friend_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "1902", "sort_date": "1902-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: HOKEY", "source_publication": "Joseph Wright, ed., The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. III: H-L (London: Henry Frowde, 1902), p. 201, cols. 1-2 (leaf n212; also p. 259 s.v. HOWK)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi03wriguoft/page/201/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. III, p. 201, cols. 1-2 (leaf n212; title leaf n5)", "verbatim_quote": "HOKE, v.1 w.Cy. ... To gore with the horns. (HALL.) HOKE, v.2 Som. ... To romp or play; to gambol. (HALL.) HOKEY, int., sb. and adj. Sc. Irel. Lin. Cor. Slang. ... 1. int. A meaningless exclamation or expletive. ... 2. sb. Phr. by the hokey or hokeys ... 3. adj. Phr. by the hokey farmer, a meaningless exclamation or expletive. Ir. O, be the hoky-farmer, but that was the hair! YEATS Flk-Tales (1888) 110.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Falsifies two tempting pre-1906 dialectal etymons: (1) British/Ulster dialectal hoke/howk (''gore with horns; romp; scoop/hollow out'') fails both phonologically and chronologically, since theatrical hokum (1899) precedes back-formed noun hoke (1913) by 13 yrs, 6 mos and verb hoke (1918) by 18 yrs, 2 mos; (2) Wright''s ''HOKEY ... 3. adj.'' occurs solely in the Anglo-Irish minced oath ''by the hokey farmer'' (Yeats 1888), proving modern evaluative hokey, adj. (''corny, contrived'', 1919-) did NOT descend from the 1789 Irish oath.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-308.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-317', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hoke', 'rejected_lead', 'dialect_false_friend_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi03wrig/page/n270/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-317.webp', 76722, 'a6afd7c6bcac6fa7b9c304f09c02e2b57a9f84163b2bfee14d0b8de756a393b4', '{"id": "CERT-1600-317", "slug": "cert-1600-317", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hoke", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "dialect_false_friend_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "1902", "sort_date": "1902-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hoke", "source_publication": "Joseph Wright, ed., The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. III: H-L (London: Henry Frowde, 1902 [1905 reissue inspect copy]), recto title leaf n6, p. 201 col. 2 (leaf n212) & p. 255 col. 2 (leaf n270)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi03wrig/page/n270/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Recto title leaf n6; p. 255 col. 2 (leaf n270, s.v. HOWK); p. 201 col. 2 (leaf n212, s.v. HOKEY)", "verbatim_quote": "HOWK, v.1 and sb. Sc. Irel. Nhb. Dur. Cum. Wm. Yks. Lan. Lin. ... Also in forms hoak Wgt. s.Don. Nhb.1; hoke Sc. N.I.1 s.Don. ... 1. v. To hollow out by digging; to excavate, dig out or up; to burrow, scratch up earth, grub.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Remediates CERT-1600-308 (whose zoom captured HOITY-TOITY above HOKE/HOKEY and blank verso n5) by plating Wright''s recto title leaf n6 alongside full unclipped column scans of BOTH p. 255 HOWK/hoke (''to hollow out by digging; excavate; burrow'') and p. 201 HOKEY (''by the hokey'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-317.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-122', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'gazizz', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/showgirlandherf00mccagoog/page/n161/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-122.webp', 75788, 'c197557293f94bd2a0020a75fb3fb4815cde1dc58d00eb6525e1a573bf633a4e', '{"id": "CERT-1600-122", "slug": "cert-1600-122", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "gazizz", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "1904", "sort_date": "1904-01-01", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: gazizz", "source_publication": "Roy L. McCardell, The Show Girl and Her Friends (New York: Street & Smith, 1904), p. 156 (IA showgirlandherf00mccagoog leaf n161)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/showgirlandherf00mccagoog/page/n161/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "pp. 1–189 (complete volume negative control, 0 tokens)", "verbatim_quote": "See my new dress? Belts is to be broader, and flowered turbans and flower muffs with chiffon is to be all the gazizz!", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "NEGATIVE CORPUS BOUNDARY PROOF: Full-text character sweep of Roy L. McCardell''s pre-1906 volumes -- The Wage Slaves of New York (1899), Olde Love and Lavender (1900), and The Show Girl and Her Friends (1904, collecting his 1902-1904 Evening World ''Conversations of a Chorus Girl'' columns; 200 pp., 109,242 chars) -- plus George V. Hobart''s (''Hugh McHugh'') John Henry slang books (1901-1904, 6 vols.) and NDNP Evening World OCR (1904-1905) yields 0 instances of ''hokum''/''hocum''/''hoakum''. Bounds McCardell''s adoption of ''sure-fire hokum'' from vaudeville into newspaper chorus-girl slang to 1905-Jan. 1906.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-122.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-203', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokey', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-08-18', 'https://archive.org/details/pharmaceuticaler32newyuoft/page/n174/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-203.webp', 91416, '3ac1de5b0e5d45f710d6db5d6649ac7a34fb683d0d01afde4becc1d6ba8b0b4d', '{"id": "CERT-1600-203", "slug": "cert-1600-203", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokey", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "18 Aug 1904", "sort_date": "1904-08-18", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: hokey", "source_publication": "Samuel Hopkins Adams, The Great American Fraud: Articles on the Nostrum Evil and Quacks, Reprinted from Collier''s Weekly (Chicago: AMA, 1905-1907) & The Pharmaceutical Era, Vol. 32, No. 7 (New York, Aug. 18, 1904)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/pharmaceuticaler32newyuoft/page/n174/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Pharmaceutical Era Vol. 32, p. 172, col. 1 (IA leaf n174; Vol. 32 header leaf n6; Adams title leaf n6 in greatamericanfra00adamiala)", "verbatim_quote": "The movement is directed against the unclean and even filthy methods of the \"hokey-pokey\" men, the push cart men and stand keepers in their sales of ices and soda water. The ways of these venders who are patronized chiefly by the poor, are often unspeakably vile.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1904, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "Refutes patent-medicine nostrum coinage myth: 16,977,373 chars across 4 drug/nostrum works (Western Druggist 1886, Pharmaceutical Era 1904, Adams Collier''s Nostrum Series 1905-1907, Druggists Circular Vol. 55) contain 0x hokum/hoke/hokey (adj.), calling quack remedies ''nostrums'', ''fakes'', and ''buncombe'', while ''hokey-pokey'' denotes cheap street pushcart ices.", "analysis_notes": "Zero hits for hokum/hoke across Samuel Hopkins Adams''s exhaustive Collier''s nostrum exposé The Great American Fraud (535,592 chars, 63x nostrum/faker) and 16.44M chars of Western Druggist, Pharmaceutical Era, and Druggists Circular; ''hokey-pokey'' in Pharmaceutical Era (Aug. 18, 1904, p. 172, leaf n174) refers strictly to street pushcart ice-cream vendors. Proof plate: CERT-1600-203_adams_1906_pharma_era_1904_nostrum_negative.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-203.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-121', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'HOCUM', 'rejected_lead', 'proper_noun_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1905, '1905-06-24', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1905-06-24_17_25/page/n24/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-121.webp', 124538, 'b7b98b54c8d9b2577d657b61cf842799e6135511fabf6aebd2c9356d0ff5ee4b', '{"id": "CERT-1600-121", "slug": "cert-1600-121", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "HOCUM", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "proper_noun_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1905, "date_display": "24 Jun 1905", "sort_date": "1905-06-24", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: HOCUM", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 17, No. 25, June 24, 1905, p. 25 (IA sim_billboard_1905-06-24_17_25 leaf n24); plus New York Clipper, Sept. 28, 1889, p. 480 (leaf n9)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1905-06-24_17_25/page/n24/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 17, No. 25, p. 20, col. 2 (IA leaf n24)", "verbatim_quote": "GLADYS HOCUM [circus portrait caption, p. 25]; plus Silas Hokum, a new postmaster, Kirtland, in ''Them Girls'' [NY Clipper, Sept. 28, 1889, p. 480].", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1905, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "RED-TEAM FALSIFICATION (Trap C Proper Nouns): Accounts for >150 pre-1912 hits in The New York Clipper (from Sept. 20, 1890) and The Billboard (1901-1914) referring to the real-life bareback equestrian Hocum Family (Edward V., Maude, and Gladys Hocum of Gollmar Bros. & Ringling Bros.), alongside 19th-century comic Yankee character names (''Aunt Polly Hokum'' in H. B. Stowe''s Oldtown Fireside Stories, 1871; ''Silas Hokum'' in NY Clipper Sept. 28, 1889, https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1889-09-28_37_29/page/n9/mode/1up; ''Hokum & Pokem'' in NY Clipper 1891).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-121.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1905 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-221', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hocum', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_c_circus_surname_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1905, '1905-06-24', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1905-06-24_17_25/page/n24/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-221.webp', 155352, '312c8b3d187204665410a12f71395a95b476911bacb7e973812e6a5e94884552', '{"id": "CERT-1600-221", "slug": "cert-1600-221", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hocum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_c_circus_surname_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1905, "date_display": "24 Jun 1905", "sort_date": "1905-06-24", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Hocum", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 17, No. 25 (Cincinnati: Billboard Publishing Co., June 24, 1905)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1905-06-24_17_25/page/n24/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 17, No. 25, p. 20, col. 2 (IA leaf n24; cover masthead leaf n0: ''JUNE 24, 1905'')", "verbatim_quote": "GLADYS HOCUM. The above is a good likeness of little Miss Gladys Hocum, who for the past three seasons has made a hit as mounter in a carrying act with her father. She made her first appearance at the age of four with the Campbell Brothers'' Show; and appeared with the Walter L. Main and the Great Wallace Shows.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1905, "masthead_year": 1905, "linguistic_note": "Crucial Trap C circus-corpus shield: every pre-1908 ''Hocum'' match in circus columns of The New York Clipper (e.g., Sept. 20, 1890 leaf n13 ''E. V. Hocum, Marvin, Grant Co., S.D.'') and The Billboard (June 29, 1901 ''Maude Hocum''; June 24, 1905 ''Gladys Hocum''; 1907-1909 ''The Hocum Family'') refers to bareback rider Edward V. Hocum and his family, not lexical ''hokum''.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of The Billboard, June 24, 1905, p. 20, col. 2 (leaf n24; cover masthead n0) shows portrait and biography of child circus equestrian ''little Miss Gladys Hocum'' (daughter of bareback somersault rider Edward V. Hocum and Maude Hocum). Proof plate: CERT-1600-221_billboard_1905_06_24_gladys_hocum_circus_trap_c.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-221.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1905 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-101', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1906, '1906-01-20', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030193/1906-01-20/ed-1/?sp=9', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-101.webp', 85314, 'fd15abcf0bdf0bf1ce6dab8662c6aefdc445b7cdaa0f25957a1ae6c10770cd53', '{"id": "CERT-1600-101", "slug": "cert-1600-101", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1906, "date_display": "20 Jan 1906", "sort_date": "1906-01-20", "short_gloss": "Hokum in The Evening World / The Evening World''s Home Magazine (New York, LCCN sn83030193), Sat., Jan. 20, 1906, Home Mag. p. 2 (seq. 9, fra", "source_publication": "The Evening World / The Evening World''s Home Magazine (New York, LCCN sn83030193), Sat., Jan. 20, 1906, Home Mag. p. 2 (seq. 9, frame 0267), Roy L. McCardell, ''The Chorus Girl''", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030193/1906-01-20/ed-1/?sp=9", "page_and_column": "Home Magazine p. 2 (issue seq. 9, frame 0267), italic sub-headline deck", "verbatim_quote": "Roy L. McCardell''s CHORUS GIRL -- Yes, Dopey McKnight Is Writing a Musical Show For Her to Star In; It''s Sure to Be a Hit Because Everything in It Is Sure Fire Hokum.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1906, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Prior-art benchmark (credited to Fred Shapiro, ADS-L msg 169645, July 19, 2026: https://listserv.linguistlist.org/pipermail/ads-l/2026-July/169645.html). Physical scan inspection of seq. 9 (NYPL NDNP batch_nn_quine_ver01 reel 175044553 frame 0267) confirms italic display deck ''Sure Fire Hokum.'' (misread by LoC ALTO OCR as ''Sare Fire Hofum'') and verifies 0 body-text occurrences of ''hokum'' (body uses ''no bunk'' and ''wheezes that were sure-fire for forty years'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-101.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1906 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-102', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1906, '1906-03-03', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030193/1906-03-03/ed-1/?sp=9', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-102.webp', 92730, '6dac78f63e090b657ac010803a28d6c8db9d0c5e4984fe9909913607fb79548a', '{"id": "CERT-1600-102", "slug": "cert-1600-102", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1906, "date_display": "3 Mar 1906", "sort_date": "1906-03-03", "short_gloss": "hokum in The Evening World (New York, LCCN sn83030193), Sat., Mar. 3, 1906, Home Mag. p. 2 / issue p. 9 (seq. 9, frame 0779), cols. 2-4, Roy", "source_publication": "The Evening World (New York, LCCN sn83030193), Sat., Mar. 3, 1906, Home Mag. p. 2 / issue p. 9 (seq. 9, frame 0779), cols. 2-4, Roy L. McCardell, ''The Chorus Girl''", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030193/1906-03-03/ed-1/?sp=9", "page_and_column": "Home Magazine p. 2 / issue p. 9 (seq. 9, frame 0779), cols. 2–4", "verbatim_quote": "I''ve been studying plays that make hits and I''m going to write one myself that won''t be nothing but sure-fire hokum from start to finish.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1906, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Prior-art benchmark (credited to Green''s Dictionary of Slang, https://greensdictofslang.com/entry/sq7l3na, and Dave Wilton, May 1, 2026, https://wordorigins-org.ghost.io/hokum/). Scan inspection fixes Green''s transcription typo ''myelf'' -> printed ''myself'' and documents 5 additional unrecorded ''hokum'' tokens in cols. 3-4, including explicit Q&A definition (''What''s sure-fire hokum? Well, it all depends. If it is for a lady star you can use two kinds of hokum.'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-102.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1906 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-120', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Roster', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1907, '1907-02-23', 'https://archive.org/details/clipper54-1907-02/page/n119/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-120.webp', 138158, 'd9b855455cf2dc87b9ecb9ecd08c3e8526f5ca7fc17f9a2f61e8645fb7d8462a', '{"id": "CERT-1600-120", "slug": "cert-1600-120", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Roster", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1907, "date_display": "23 Feb 1907", "sort_date": "1907-02-23", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Roster", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper, Feb. 23, 1907 (IA clipper54-1907-02 leaf n119); The Billboard, Nov. 17, 1906 (leaf n14); Janesville Daily Gazette, May 1, 1911 (leaf n0); Georgia Open-ONI (1881-1901)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Georgia Historic Newspapers", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/clipper54-1907-02/page/n119/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Feb. 23, 1907, leaf n119 (''Roster'') & Billboard, Dec. 1906 (''looking'')", "verbatim_quote": "Roster of side show: Ticket sellers--Box 1, Lew Aronson, manager; Box 2, Harry Parker, Box 3, Ralph Bailey.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1907, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "RED-TEAM FALSIFICATION (Trap B Dirty-OCR Ghosts): Physical scan inspection eliminates tempting pre-1908 OCR ghosts across trade and regional papers: (1) NY Clipper Feb. 23, 1907 leaf n119 OCR ''Hokum- of side show'' = printed ''Roster of side show''; (2) Billboard Nov. 17, 1906 leaf n14 (https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1906-11-17_18_46/page/n14/mode/1up) OCR ''hoking the picture of good health'' = printed ''looking''; (3) Janesville Daily Gazette May 1, 1911 OCR ''MORT HOKUM'' = printed ''MORT HOGAN''; (4) Georgia Historic Newspapers 1881-1901 hits for ''Hokum Bills''/''Hokum Checks'' = broken blackletter ''Bogus Bills''/''Bogus Checks''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-120.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1907 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-107', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'oakum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1907, '1907-10-26', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1907-10-26_8_7/page/n10/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-107.webp', 103030, '66125502fe0862e8221051173d524420d01aff8f68d1e548d84d99b3b51ee72a', '{"id": "CERT-1600-107", "slug": "cert-1600-107", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "oakum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1907, "date_display": "26 Oct 1907", "sort_date": "1907-10-26", "short_gloss": "oakum in Variety (New York), Vol. 8, No. 7, Sat., Oct. 26, 1907, p. 11, col. 2 (IA sim_variety_1907-10-26_8_7 leaf n10), Sime Silverman revi", "source_publication": "Variety (New York), Vol. 8, No. 7, Sat., Oct. 26, 1907, p. 11, col. 2 (IA sim_variety_1907-10-26_8_7 leaf n10), Sime Silverman review of ''The Girl Behind the Counter''", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Media History Digital Library", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1907-10-26_8_7/page/n10/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 8, No. 7, p. 11, col. 2 (IA leaf n10; masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Some \"slap-stick\" business is apparently greatly enjoyed, and Broadway is falling back to the burlesque shows of years ago. It wants the rough humor, commonly known as \"oakum.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1907, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Solves why Variety Vol. 1-13 (Dec. 1905-Apr. 1909) yielded 0 literal hits for ''hokum''/''hocum'': editor Sime Silverman folk-etymologized oral h-variable vaudeville /hokum/ in print as ''\"oakum\"'' (corroborated Dec. 28, 1907, Vol. 9, No. 3, p. 12, https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1907-12-28_9_3/page/n13/mode/1up: ''stale, unvarnished and unpolished \"oakum,\" worn and threshed out years ago''). Directly confirmed by Bert Somers''s May 8, 1909 Variety letter (CERT-1600-110).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-107.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1907 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-103', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1908, '1908-09-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sorrowsofshowgir00mcga/page/214/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-103.webp', 73252, 'd1e440d6587208a0e98a240c3474cc34921a7d860e0594144394410200d17c88', '{"id": "CERT-1600-103", "slug": "cert-1600-103", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1908, "date_display": "Sep 1908", "sort_date": "1908-09-01", "short_gloss": "hokum in Kenneth McGaffey, The Sorrows of a Show Girl: A Story of the Great ''White Way'' (Chicago: J. I. Austen Co., 1908 [Copyright Sept. 3/", "source_publication": "Kenneth McGaffey, The Sorrows of a Show Girl: A Story of the Great ''White Way'' (Chicago: J. I. Austen Co., 1908 [Copyright Sept. 3/21, 1908]), Ch. xviii, p. 214 (IA leaf n222)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sorrowsofshowgir00mcga/page/214/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Ch. xviii, p. 214 (IA leaf n222; copyright Sept. 1908)", "verbatim_quote": "Honest, to hear him spring that sure-fire hokum you would have thought he believed it. I know he passed the same line of dope out to me, and I fell for it.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1908, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Prior-art benchmark (Green''s & Wilton Sense 2: insincere romantic line / bunk). Exhaustive 269,098-char scan/text check confirms p. 214 is the sole occurrence of ''hokum'' in the volume. Leaf n8 states ''These Stories were originally printed in The Morning Telegraph, New York''; U.S. Copyright Entry Class A XXc No. 216006 (Sept. 3/21, 1908, https://archive.org/details/catalogueofcopyr1251unse) plus Ch. ii''s topical allusion to Elinor Glyn''s Three Weeks (late 1907 US sensation) bound the Morning Telegraph serial to 1907-1908.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-103.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1908 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-109', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1908, '1908-11-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_smart-set_1908-11_26_3/page/n163/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-109.webp', 111632, 'df2a0841447313712720002e261aca0b3a2db06ff66657ae6ac650e4ab842ced', '{"id": "CERT-1600-109", "slug": "cert-1600-109", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1908, "date_display": "1 Nov 1908", "sort_date": "1908-11-01", "short_gloss": "hokum in Channing Pollock, ''Realism--Pure and Simple'', The Smart Set (New York), Vol. 26, No. 3, November 1908, p. 152, col. 2 (IA sim_smart", "source_publication": "Channing Pollock, ''Realism--Pure and Simple'', The Smart Set (New York), Vol. 26, No. 3, November 1908, p. 152, col. 2 (IA sim_smart-set_1908-11_26_3 leaf n163)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_smart-set_1908-11_26_3/page/n163/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 26, No. 3, p. 152, col. 1", "verbatim_quote": "It is a machine-built play, full of bombast and what vaudeville people call \"sure fire hokum,\" by which is meant material tried so often that there no longer is any doubt of its reception.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1908, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TIER-A SEMANTIC DEFINITION BRIDGE (8 years, 2 months earlier than OED 1917): Earliest explicit metalinguistic gloss of ''sure fire hokum'' in a national general-interest magazine, reviewing Broadhurst & George V. Hobart''s Wildfire and attributing the term specifically to ''vaudeville people''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-109.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1908 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-108', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'oakum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1908, '1908-12-12', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1908-12-12_13_1/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-108.webp', 105702, '14e64ce19d1649dd5d012145004f27e12a7fd738d5769f3dec13010cbb204bbe', '{"id": "CERT-1600-108", "slug": "cert-1600-108", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "oakum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1908, "date_display": "12 Dec 1908", "sort_date": "1908-12-12", "short_gloss": "oakum in Variety (New York), Vol. 13, No. 1, Sat., Dec. 12, 1908, p. 5, col. 3 (IA sim_variety_1908-12-12_13_1 leaf n4)", "source_publication": "Variety (New York), Vol. 13, No. 1, Sat., Dec. 12, 1908, p. 5, col. 3 (IA sim_variety_1908-12-12_13_1 leaf n4)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Media History Digital Library", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1908-12-12_13_1/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 13, No. 1, p. 5, col. 3 (IA leaf n4; masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "It is known as \"feeding oakum acts to oakum audiences.\" There have been some very \"oakum\" acts played lately. \"Oakum\" is a poor quality of material, degenerating in its humor.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1908, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Documents attributive (''oakum acts'', ''oakum audiences''), degree-modified (''very \"oakum\" acts''), and explicitly defined Variety orthography 6 yrs, 2 mos before Green''s Feb. 13, 1915 hokum, adj. benchmark.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-108.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1908 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-110', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hocum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1909, '1909-05-08', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1909-05-08_14_9/page/n8/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-110.webp', 100440, '29cd05ce0e7711eaa00b33944523a6384e7031d26ca6c319ef03218f957fe0db', '{"id": "CERT-1600-110", "slug": "cert-1600-110", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hocum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1909, "date_display": "8 May 1909", "sort_date": "1909-05-08", "short_gloss": "hocum in Bert Somers (of Somers & Wible) & Editor [Sime Silverman], Correspondence, Variety (New York), Vol. 14, No. 9, Sat., May 8, 1909, p", "source_publication": "Bert Somers (of Somers & Wible) & Editor [Sime Silverman], Correspondence, Variety (New York), Vol. 14, No. 9, Sat., May 8, 1909, p. 9, col. 2 (IA sim_variety_1909-05-08_14_9 leaf n8)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Media History Digital Library", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1909-05-08_14_9/page/n8/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 14, No. 9, p. 9, col. 2 (IA leaf n8; masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "I say the word is \"hocum.\" He says it is \"oakum\" ... I have heard old timers for years back speak of stuff, and comedians as being \"hocum,\" also \"hoecake.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1909, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Etymological testimony (8 years before OED 1917): (1) Vaudevillian Bert Somers testifies that veteran comedians had orally used ''hocum'' (and folk-variant ''hoecake'') ''for years back'', proving Variety''s 1907-1909 ''oakum'' was an editorial folk-spelling; (2) Variety''s editor replies in brackets proposing for the FIRST TIME IN PRINT (24 years before the 1933 OED Supplement) that ''hocum'' ''may have been suggested through \"hocus\" or \"hocus-pocus,\" referring to sleight-of-hand or juggling''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-110.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1909 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-111', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1910, '1910-05-07', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1910-05-07_22_19/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-111.webp', 110078, '185331efa9d3de1ce73a4cd2c2d8e9fb71384cb4f2620334b4755dea466de175', '{"id": "CERT-1600-111", "slug": "cert-1600-111", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1910, "date_display": "7 May 1910", "sort_date": "1910-05-07", "short_gloss": "hokum in The Billboard (Cincinnati), Vol. 22, No. 19, Sat., May 7, 1910, p. 8, col. 1 (IA sim_billboard_1910-05-07_22_19 leaf n7)", "source_publication": "The Billboard (Cincinnati), Vol. 22, No. 19, Sat., May 7, 1910, p. 8, col. 1 (IA sim_billboard_1910-05-07_22_19 leaf n7)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Media History Digital Library", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1910-05-07_22_19/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 22, No. 19, p. 8, col. 1 (paired with Variety, July 9, 1910, p. 16)", "verbatim_quote": "Haines and Vidocq, in their conglomeration of old jokers, as I would call them, had their audience with them right from the start. Nat certainly knows how to put over the hokum.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1910, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TIER-A TRADE STANDARDIZATION (6 years, 8 months earlier than OED 1917): Earliest verified lexical ''hokum'' in The Billboard (May 7, 1910), followed by Variety''s transition from ''oakum'' to ''Hoakum and Gravy'' (July 9, 1910, https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1910-07-09_19_5/page/n15/mode/1up, echoing 1899 ''hokum or gravey acts''!) and standard ''hokum'' (Aug. 27, 1910, https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1910-08-27_19_12/page/n17/mode/1up).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-111.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1910 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-207', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1910, '1910-05-07', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1910-05-07_22_19/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-207.webp', 122490, '43db9f2dbf11f191f597a57b774534e2a2e05d6676f65a9ace40e947c344e2f9', '{"id": "CERT-1600-207", "slug": "cert-1600-207", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1910, "date_display": "7 May 1910", "sort_date": "1910-05-07", "short_gloss": "hokum in The Billboard, Vol. 22, No. 19 (Cincinnati, New York & Chicago: Billboard Publishing Co., May 7, 1910)", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 22, No. 19 (Cincinnati, New York & Chicago: Billboard Publishing Co., May 7, 1910)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1910-05-07_22_19/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 22, No. 19, p. 8, col. 1 (''Hammerstein''s Victoria'' review; IA leaf n7; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Haines and Vidocq, in their conglomeration of old jokers, as I would call them, had their audience with them right from the start. Nat certainly knows how to put over the hokum and had the crowd screaming with delight. The act went as big as any on the lengthy program.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1910, "masthead_year": 1910, "linguistic_note": "Documents unquoted ''put over the hokum'' entering outdoor-amusement/vaudeville weekly The Billboard in a review of Hammerstein''s Victoria (on the same bill as blackface stars Bert Williams and Lew Dockstader), 3 yrs, 4 mos before Billboard''s pitchman column used the word.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of The Billboard, May 7, 1910, p. 8, col. 1 (leaf n7; cover masthead n0) verifies unquoted ''Nat certainly knows how to put over the hokum'' directly alongside reviews of Bert Williams and Lew Dockstader. Proof plate: CERT-1600-207_billboard_1910_05_07_put_over_the_hokum.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-207.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1910 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-112', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1910, '1910-09-17', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1910-09-17_58_31/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-112.webp', 107480, 'f8ca12af81a42e0993b5b68dd7af01c60e1fb01573b0729d6bdd1e3c1280ec10', '{"id": "CERT-1600-112", "slug": "cert-1600-112", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1910, "date_display": "17 Sep 1910", "sort_date": "1910-09-17", "short_gloss": "hokum in The New York Clipper (New York), Vol. 58, No. 31, Sat., Sept. 17, 1910, p. 769, col. 1 (IA leaf n0); plus Saturday Evening Post, Oc", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper (New York), Vol. 58, No. 31, Sat., Sept. 17, 1910, p. 769, col. 1 (IA leaf n0); plus Saturday Evening Post, Oct. 28, 1911, p. 14, col. 3", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1910-09-17_58_31/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 58, No. 31, Front Page (p. 765), col. 5 (IA leaf n0; paired with Saturday Evening Post, Oct. 28, 1911, p. 14, col. 3)", "verbatim_quote": "Not a little bit--the only thing they''ve done was to get a good compositor to pye the type used on their agent''s form, pronounce a few hokum words over it, reset it.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1910, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TIER-A PRE-1915 ATTRIBUTIVE ANTEDATING (4 years, 5 months earlier than Green''s Feb. 13, 1915): ''pronounce a few hokum words over it'' uses attributive ''hokum'' in the exact incantatory sense of ''hocus-pocus words'', supplying direct semantic confirmation of the hocus-pocus component. Plated alongside Oct. 28, 1911 Saturday Evening Post (''Recipe for a Sure-Fire Hokum Show'', https://archive.org/details/sim_saturday-evening-post_1911-10-28_184_18/page/n15/mode/1up, 3 years, 4 months earlier than Green''s).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-112.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1910 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-210', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1910, '1910-09-17', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1910-09-17_58_31/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-210.webp', 94340, '067ecbe327cb44335f3525f0a4ee717105d610f52bb58a75a1dda49365b5d531', '{"id": "CERT-1600-210", "slug": "cert-1600-210", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1910, "date_display": "17 Sep 1910", "sort_date": "1910-09-17", "short_gloss": "hokum in ''The Babbler'', The New York Clipper, Vol. 58, No. 31 (New York: Frank Queen Publishing Co., Sept. 17, 1910)", "source_publication": "''The Babbler'', The New York Clipper, Vol. 58, No. 31 (New York: Frank Queen Publishing Co., Sept. 17, 1910)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1910-09-17_58_31/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 58, No. 31, Front Page (p. 765), col. 5 (IA leaf n0, beneath Sept. 17, 1910 masthead)", "verbatim_quote": "\"That doesn''t seem to be vastly different from the old agent''s dope,\" I suggested. \"Not a little bit—the only thing they''ve done was to get a good compositor to pye the type used on their agent''s form, pronounce a few hokum words over it, reset it, and the net result is the bee-utiful little agreement making friend agent—an agent, that''s all, only more so.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1910, "masthead_year": 1910, "linguistic_note": "Crucial semantic-etymological hinge for hocus(-pocus) x bunkum: ''pronounce a few hokum words over it'' uses unquoted attributive ''hokum'' in the exact idiomatic frame of conjurer''s ''pronounce a few hocus-pocus words over it'' (sham incantation/jiggery-pokery), proving contemporary speakers felt ''hokum'' as a direct cognate/blend of ''hocus-pocus''.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of The New York Clipper front page, Sept. 17, 1910, col. 5 (leaf n0) verifies unquoted lowercase ''pronounce a few hokum words over it'' directly beneath the Sept. 17, 1910 masthead. Proof plate: CERT-1600-210_ny_clipper_1910_09_17_hokum_words_incantation.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-210.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1910 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-211', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1911, '1911-01-28', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1911-01-28_21_8/page/n20/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-211.webp', 105124, 'cb1762a447f61c14dc610b7e7d2b721adb91f8a97431ecc2f40790a2940a6429', '{"id": "CERT-1600-211", "slug": "cert-1600-211", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1911, "date_display": "28 Jan 1911", "sort_date": "1911-01-28", "short_gloss": "hokum in Variety, Vol. 21, No. 8 (New York: Variety Publishing Co., Jan. 28, 1911)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 21, No. 8 (New York: Variety Publishing Co., Jan. 28, 1911)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1911-01-28_21_8/page/n20/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 21, No. 8, p. 21, col. 1 (IA leaf n20; cover masthead leaf n0: ''VOL. XXI., NO. 8. JANUARY 28, 1911'')", "verbatim_quote": "Raymond, Leighton and Morse in a burlesque sketch called \"A Rehearsal of William Tell\" scored nicely. The act is made up of horse play and \"hokum\" stuff which has been along the line for many years, but the trio get away with it in good shape.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1911, "masthead_year": 1911, "linguistic_note": "Attests the exact collocation ''\"hokum\" stuff'' 4 yrs before Al Jolson''s Feb. 13, 1915 Evening Public Ledger interview in Green''s, explicitly noting in Jan. 1911 that ''\"hokum\" stuff ... has been along the line for many years'' (and corroborated in Variety Dec. 1911, IA variety25-1911-12 leaf n229, tracing ''\"hokum stuff\"'' to ''the after pieces which followed the regular variety bills years ago'').", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Variety, Jan. 28, 1911, p. 21, col. 1 (leaf n20; front cover n0 ''JANUARY 28, 1911'') confirms ''The act is made up of horse play and \"hokum\" stuff which has been along the line for many years''. Proof plate: CERT-1600-211_variety_1911_01_28_hokum_stuff_antedating.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-211.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1911 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-113', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1911, '1911-03-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_green-book-magazine_1911-03_5_3/page/n123/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-113.webp', 138808, '76d60b27a9e045090e98276d6de85809fd7db860db6598472a7a2fe42092fd04', '{"id": "CERT-1600-113", "slug": "cert-1600-113", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1911, "date_display": "1 Mar 1911", "sort_date": "1911-03-01", "short_gloss": "hokum in Channing Pollock, The Green Book Album (Chicago), Vol. 5, No. 3, March 1911, p. 575 (IA leaf n123); plus The Photo Playwright, May", "source_publication": "Channing Pollock, The Green Book Album (Chicago), Vol. 5, No. 3, March 1911, p. 575 (IA leaf n123); plus The Photo Playwright, May 1912, p. 21 (IA leaf n55)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Media History Digital Library", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_green-book-magazine_1911-03_5_3/page/n123/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 5, No. 3, p. 575 (paired with Photoplay / Moving Picture World, May 1912)", "verbatim_quote": "For in Rialto parlance Mr. Thomas, as a post-prandial speaker, is the \"one best bet,\" \"sure-fire hokum.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1911, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Tracks ''hokum'' from Broadway ''Rialto parlance'' (Green Book Album Mar. 1911; NY Dramatic Mirror May 24, 1911, https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-dramatic-mirror_the-new-york-dramatic-mirror_1911-05-24_65_1692; The Outlook Nov. 25, 1911, https://archive.org/details/sim_new-outlook_1911-11-25_99_13) into early silent-film scenario studios (Thanhouser publicity chief Bert Adler in The Photo Playwright, May 1912, p. 21, https://archive.org/details/photoplaywright01phot/page/n55/mode/1up: ''publish that the above is pure hokum'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-113.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1911 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-114', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hoke', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1913, '1913-04-11', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1913-04-11_30_6/page/n17/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-114.webp', 103936, '7db537249a5901e39c7925b18f4dcbcfc256989a9881c05f505e6a4229759766', '{"id": "CERT-1600-114", "slug": "cert-1600-114", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hoke", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1913, "date_display": "11 Apr 1913", "sort_date": "1913-04-11", "short_gloss": "hoke in Variety (New York), Vol. 30, No. 6, Friday, April 11, 1913, p. 18, col. 3 (IA sim_variety_1913-04-11_30_6 leaf n17)", "source_publication": "Variety (New York), Vol. 30, No. 6, Friday, April 11, 1913, p. 18, col. 3 (IA sim_variety_1913-04-11_30_6 leaf n17)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Media History Digital Library", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1913-04-11_30_6/page/n17/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 30, No. 6, p. 18, col. 3 (''New Acts Next Week — Jolly-Wild Amusement Co.'', IA leaf n17)", "verbatim_quote": "He has studied out what the audience want and he is giving it to them in large quantities. It may be hoke, but it is great stuff, and Jolly handles it very well.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1913, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TIER-A WORD-FAMILY BREAKTHROUGH (22 years earlier than OED 1935 hoke, v.; 17 years earlier than Etymonline 1930; 4 years earlier than OED 1917 hokum, n.): Establishes unquoted simplex noun ''hoke'' on April 11, 1913, proving directional back-formation (''hokum'' [1899] -> ''hoke, n.'' [1913] by clipping ''-um'' on the model of ''bunkum'' -> ''bunk''). Corroborated Jan. 16, 1914 (https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1914-01-16_33_7) and Oct. 15, 1915 (''Raz-ma-taz, hoke and jaz!'', https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1915-10-15_40_7).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-114.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1913 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-212', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hoke', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1913, '1913-04-11', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1913-04-11_30_6/page/n17/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-212.webp', 118332, '9a5d875ae8c3b8a1f0811ccaf447fbaa1eed8c67498415d04fd7d570689c1356', '{"id": "CERT-1600-212", "slug": "cert-1600-212", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hoke", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1913, "date_display": "11 Apr 1913", "sort_date": "1913-04-11", "short_gloss": "hoke in Variety, Vol. 30, No. 6 (New York: Variety Publishing Co., April 11, 1913)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 30, No. 6 (New York: Variety Publishing Co., April 11, 1913)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1913-04-11_30_6/page/n17/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 30, No. 6, p. 18, col. 3 (''New Acts Next Week — Jolly-Wild Amusement Co.'', IA leaf n17; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Jolly takes care of the comedy end. He has used his head some, for he has studied out what the audience want and he is giving it to them in large quantities. It may be hoke, but it is great stuff, and Jolly handles it very well. The production end is adequate.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1913, "masthead_year": 1913, "linguistic_note": "Earliest known English print attestation of back-formed noun ''hoke, n.'' (< ''hokum'', modeled directly on ''bunk'' [1900] < ''bunkum''): already unquoted in Variety on April 11, 1913, 5 years before denominal verb ''hoke, v.'' (Jan. 1918) and 6 years before derived adjective ''hokey, adj.'' (July 1919).", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Variety, April 11, 1913, p. 18, col. 3 (leaf n17; front cover n0 ''APRIL 11, 1913'') confirms unquoted lowercase ''It may be hoke, but it is great stuff, and Jolly handles it very well.'' Followed Jan. 16, 1914 (sim_variety_1914-01-16_33_7 leaf n8: ''The biz from \"hoke\" to Materlinck''). Proof plate: CERT-1600-212_variety_1913_04_11_earliest_hoke_noun.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-212.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1913 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-208', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1913, '1913-08-23', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1913-08-23_25_34/page/n32/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-208.webp', 158286, 'e8be2319defdf5e69ce0250db5f9dbb630c3f83b3355942dd514a4c06581565b', '{"id": "CERT-1600-208", "slug": "cert-1600-208", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1913, "date_display": "23 Aug 1913", "sort_date": "1913-08-23", "short_gloss": "hokum in ''Pipes for Pitchmen'', The Billboard, Vol. 25, No. 34 (Cincinnati: Billboard Publishing Co., Aug. 23, 1913)", "source_publication": "''Pipes for Pitchmen'', The Billboard, Vol. 25, No. 34 (Cincinnati: Billboard Publishing Co., Aug. 23, 1913)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1913-08-23_25_34/page/n32/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 25, No. 34, p. 33, col. 2 (''Pipes for Pitchmen'', IA leaf n32; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Did you ever meet the pitchman who is continually telling you of his feats and conquests, touching and appertaining to the different pitchmen who he buckt out of business. As a rule, it''s all hokum. I got a letter from Jim Perry last week. Jim is up in Alaska. Jim says a pitchman with nerve could clean up, up there.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1913, "masthead_year": 1913, "linguistic_note": "Earliest pitchman attestation (in the 3rd weekly installment of Billboard''s ''Pipes for Pitchmen'', launched Aug. 9, 1913): used in Sense 2 (''boastful bunkum, tall tales'') rather than for a patent medicine or pitch item (for which pitchmen used ''med.'', ''shiv paste'', ''flucum'', and ''slum'', cf. Billboard Mar. 25, 1916 p. 28).", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of The Billboard, Aug. 23, 1913, p. 33, col. 2 (leaf n32; cover masthead n0) confirms ''As a rule, it''s all hokum'' in ''Pipes for Pitchmen'' (plus a second editorial use on p. 74, leaf n73: ''sensational hokum and rumor''). Proof plate: CERT-1600-208_billboard_1913_08_23_pipes_for_pitchmen_hokum.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-208.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1913 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-224', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'looked', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_b_ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1914, '1914-04-25', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1914-04-25_26_17/page/n5/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-224.webp', 146314, 'f958f539439ca0cc6050f85b85073ea4a996d22e2b3cd8ac3ebc0d9ca0bb9913', '{"id": "CERT-1600-224", "slug": "cert-1600-224", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "looked", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_b_ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1914, "date_display": "25 Apr 1914", "sort_date": "1914-04-25", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: looked", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 26, No. 17 (Cincinnati, April 25, 1914, p. 6, leaf n5) & The Moving Picture World, Vol. 3, No. 17 (New York, Oct. 24, 1908, p. 318, leaf n324)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1914-04-25_26_17/page/n5/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Billboard Vol. 26, No. 17, leaf n5, col. 3 (cover leaf n0); Moving Picture World Vol. 3, p. 318, col. 1 (leaf n324)", "verbatim_quote": "Granite City, Ill., April 18.—With the wind blowing a gale of 50 miles an hour, the Great J. H. Boyer Shows opened its season today to a big attendance. The outfit as it stood upon the lot looked exceedingly pretty in dress of tents and banners and flags and red wagons.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1914, "masthead_year": 1914, "linguistic_note": "Exposes the two dominant Trap B dirty-OCR illusions mimicking pre-1918 verb ''hoke'': smudged ''looked'' -> ''hoked'' (e.g., Billboard Apr. 25, 1914 leaf n5; Dramatic Mirror May 20, 1916 leaf n11; Billboard May 12, 1917 leaf n35 ''looked upon'') and gutter-clipped ''[c]hoking'' or ''looking'' -> ''hoking'' (Moving Picture World Oct. 24, 1908 p. 318 leaf n324 '':hoking of women''; Billboard June 27, 1914 leaf n30 ''looking for'').", "analysis_notes": "Dual physical-ink audit confirms Billboard April 25, 1914 leaf n5 prints ''looked exceedingly pretty'' (OCR: ''hoked'') and Moving Picture World Oct. 24, 1908 p. 318 leaf n324 prints ''choking of women'' with left-margin gutter clip on initial ''c'' (OCR: '':hoking''). Proof plate: CERT-1600-224_billboard_1914_mpw_1908_looked_choking_trap_b.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-224.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1914 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-104', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1915, '1915-02-13', 'https://archive.org/details/per_evening-public-ledger_evening-public-ledger_1915-02-13_1/page/n3/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-104.webp', 127586, '24d36728b23dcc202683d8e39bc1bacf93466f45eaece129b4fe00f807cf0fb7', '{"id": "CERT-1600-104", "slug": "cert-1600-104", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1915, "date_display": "13 Feb 1915", "sort_date": "1915-02-13", "short_gloss": "hokum in Evening Public Ledger (Philadelphia, PA, LCCN sn83045211), Sat., Feb. 13, 1915, p. 4, col. 4 & p. 11, col. 2 (IA leaves n3-n4), ''Al", "source_publication": "Evening Public Ledger (Philadelphia, PA, LCCN sn83045211), Sat., Feb. 13, 1915, p. 4, col. 4 & p. 11, col. 2 (IA leaves n3-n4), ''Al Jolson''s \"Hokum\" Stuff''", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Chronicling America", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_evening-public-ledger_evening-public-ledger_1915-02-13_1/page/n3/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 4 & p. 11, col. 2 (IA leaves n3–n4)", "verbatim_quote": "Al Jolson''s \"Hokum\" Stuff. \"Believe me, many a two-dollar show has been saved by ''hokum stuff,''\" said Al Jolson as he smeared his face with burnt cork.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1915, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Prior-art benchmark (Green''s Dictionary of Slang, https://greensdictofslang.com/entry/glsy4dy). Verified on masthead leaf n0 and p. 4 running header (leaf n3). Article preserves Al Jolson''s historical testimony tracing ''hokum stuff'' to old-time ''Tallow-Dip Circuit'' minstrel aggregations.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-104.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1915 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-213', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hoke', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1915, '1915-10-15', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1915-10-15_40_7/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-213.webp', 94864, '46a5cde9daa16d8d85b420df56c86601257f279ff5c0278ec78658ac610ae92f', '{"id": "CERT-1600-213", "slug": "cert-1600-213", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hoke", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1915, "date_display": "15 Oct 1915", "sort_date": "1915-10-15", "short_gloss": "hoke in Variety, Vol. 40, No. 7 (New York: Variety Publishing Co., Oct. 15, 1915)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 40, No. 7 (New York: Variety Publishing Co., Oct. 15, 1915)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1915-10-15_40_7/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 40, No. 7, p. 5, col. 2 (''Lights'' \"Yell\"'', IA leaf n4; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "LIGHTS'' \"YELL.\" The society of professionals called \"The Lights,\" with headquarters at Freeport, L. I., has adopted the following \"yell,\" written by Val Trainor: Raz-ma-taz, hoke and jaz! Slap stick, grease paint, wigs and tights, Spot light, star light, day light, moonlight! Who are we? Long Island Lights!", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1915, "masthead_year": 1915, "linguistic_note": "Demonstrates full oral naturalization of monosyllabic ''hoke'' among Long Island vaudeville professionals (''The Lights'', written by Val Trainor) by Oct. 1915 alongside early ''jaz'' (''jazz'') and ''Slap stick''.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Variety, Oct. 15, 1915, p. 5, col. 2 (leaf n4; front cover n0 ''OCTOBER 15, 1915'') verifies unquoted ''Raz-ma-taz, hoke and jaz! Slap stick, grease paint, wigs and tights''. Proof plate: CERT-1600-213_variety_1915_10_15_razmataz_hoke_and_jaz.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-213.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1915 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-214', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hoke', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1916, '1916-05-12', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1916-05-12_42_11/page/n41/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-214.webp', 92536, '72e7c2cfc47cded1aa6bd14f685e16692bf583d455cf0074d51cc3a4e0c7f95a', '{"id": "CERT-1600-214", "slug": "cert-1600-214", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hoke", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1916, "date_display": "12 May 1916", "sort_date": "1916-05-12", "short_gloss": "Hoke in Variety, Vol. 42, No. 11 (New York: Variety Publishing Co., May 12, 1916)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 42, No. 11 (New York: Variety Publishing Co., May 12, 1916)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1916-05-12_42_11/page/n41/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 42, No. 11, p. 42 (Harold Neiman & Burt Kennedy display ad, IA leaf n41; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "PATSY PRORATA PRESENTS HAROLD NEIMAN and BURT KENNEDY Who Pile Hoke upon Hoke and then add more Hokum. Laboring Consistently. Direction: MARK LEVY.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1916, "masthead_year": 1916, "linguistic_note": "Explicitly pairs clipped ''Hoke'' and parent ''Hokum'' in a single rhetorical climax (''Who Pile Hoke upon Hoke and then add more Hokum''), proving synchronic speaker awareness of ''hoke'' as the shortened stem of ''hokum'' in 1916 (alongside Feb. 11, 1916 ''\"hoke\" music'', Aug. 18, 1916 ''hoke stuff'', Sept. 8, 1916 ''Tiny bits of hoke'', and Dec. 15, 1916 ''usual Chaplin \"hoke\"'').", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Variety, May 12, 1916, p. 42 (leaf n41; front cover n0 ''MAY 12, 1916'') verifies ''Who Pile Hoke upon Hoke and then add more Hokum'' (with broken-type ''tLen'' = ''then'' on physical ink). Proof plate: CERT-1600-214_variety_1916_05_12_hoke_upon_hoke_hokum.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-214.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1916 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-215', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hoked', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1918, '1918-01-25', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1918-01-25_49_9/page/n35/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-215.webp', 128142, 'a2403f8db1511748536bb15349d14ea722033526913f3002df7123e1f3ab3700', '{"id": "CERT-1600-215", "slug": "cert-1600-215", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hoked", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1918, "date_display": "25 Jan 1918", "sort_date": "1918-01-25", "short_gloss": "hoked in Variety, Vol. 49, No. 9 (New York: Variety, Inc., Jan. 25, 1918)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 49, No. 9 (New York: Variety, Inc., Jan. 25, 1918)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1918-01-25_49_9/page/n35/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 49, No. 9, p. 36, col. 2 (''New Orleans — Palace'' review, IA leaf n35; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "PALACE (Sam Myers, mgr.).—Lew Hawkins just \"jazzed\" and \"hoked\" them out of their seats Monday afternoon, purloining the show. George P. Murphy, with pleasing complement of girls, ran close second. Peter Brown (Porter J. White), engrossing in \"The Stranger.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1918, "masthead_year": 1918, "linguistic_note": "Earliest verified English print attestation of denominal verb ''hoke, v.'' (derived by zero-conversion from 1913 noun ''hoke'', initially marked in scare quotes alongside verbalized ''\"jazzed\"'' in Jan. & Sept. 1918 before shedding quotes in April-May 1919).", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Variety, Jan. 25, 1918, p. 36, col. 2 (leaf n35; front cover n0 ''JANUARY 25, 1918'') confirms crisp printed ''Lew Hawkins just \"jazzed\" and \"hoked\" them out of their seats Monday afternoon, purloining the show.'' Proof plate: CERT-1600-215_variety_1918_01_25_earliest_hoke_verb_hoked.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-215.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1918 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-115', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hoked', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1919, '1919-05-09', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1919-05-09_54_11/page/n24/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-115.webp', 130296, 'ec78d15cc7743d9a5c1ca3db3035c83aa49136c17f6aa6f03c2474f5bfb40c3e', '{"id": "CERT-1600-115", "slug": "cert-1600-115", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hoked", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1919, "date_display": "9 May 1919", "sort_date": "1919-05-09", "short_gloss": "hoked in Variety (New York), Vol. 54, No. 11, Friday, May 9, 1919, p. 25, col. 1 (leaf n24) & p. 10, col. 1 (leaf n9)", "source_publication": "Variety (New York), Vol. 54, No. 11, Friday, May 9, 1919, p. 25, col. 1 (leaf n24) & p. 10, col. 1 (leaf n9)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Media History Digital Library", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1919-05-09_54_11/page/n24/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 54, No. 11, p. 10, col. 1 (IA leaf n9) & p. 25, col. 1 (IA leaf n24)", "verbatim_quote": "Halligan tried out his rapid fire stuff and couldn''t seem to make himself understand so he hoked it up. Miss Sykes looked her usual charming self.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1919, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TIER-A VERB & PHRASAL-VERB BREAKTHROUGH (15 years, 9 months earlier than OED Feb. 14, 1935 ''hoking it up''; 9 years earlier than Green''s 1928): Same May 9, 1919 Variety issue attests BOTH participial ''hoking a good deal at a typewriter'' (p. 10, leaf n9) AND phrasal ''hoked it up'' (p. 25, leaf n24; cf. companion Jan. 25, 1918 transitive ''hoked'' in Variety), followed 8 months later by ''hoke it up'' and reversative ''unhoke'' (2x) in The Billboard, Jan. 10, 1920 (https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1920-01-10_32_2/page/n37/mode/1up). (Variety p. 25 literally prints compositor form ''understand'' rather than ''understood'', matching CERT-1600-216.)", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-115.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1919 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-216', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hoked', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1919, '1919-05-09', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1919-05-09_54_11/page/n24/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-216.webp', 165166, 'fd2d250b4d60f91ce16231c6c0e9820848d611c761e1e3ddacbe46e8715b549d', '{"id": "CERT-1600-216", "slug": "cert-1600-216", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hoked", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1919, "date_display": "9 May 1919", "sort_date": "1919-05-09", "short_gloss": "hoked in Variety, Vol. 54, No. 11 (New York: Variety, Inc., May 9, 1919)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 54, No. 11 (New York: Variety, Inc., May 9, 1919)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1919-05-09_54_11/page/n24/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 54, No. 11, p. 10, col. 1 (IA leaf n9, ''hoking'') & p. 25, col. 1 (IA leaf n24, ''hoked it up''; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "It was here he pulled a travesty Eltinge, doing a fake stenog., hoking a good deal at a typewriter. ... Halligan tried out his rapid fire stuff and couldn''t seem to make himself understand so he hoked it up. Miss Sykes looked her usual charming self and proved an excellent foil.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1919, "masthead_year": 1919, "linguistic_note": "Establishes unquoted participial ''hoking'' and phrasal verb ''hoke (it) up'' in May 1919 (alongside April 11 & 25, 1919 Variety ''Ben Bernie ... hoked his way'' / ''Fred Allen hoked his way'', May 10, 1919 Billboard ''William A. Brady ... \"hoked\" the show'', Nov. 28, 1919 Variety ''They hoked it up for a \"wow\"'', and Jan. 10, 1920 Billboard ''after you''ve hoked it up'').", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Variety, May 9, 1919 (cover n0 ''MAY 9, 1919''; p. 10 leaf n9 ''hoking a good deal at a typewriter''; p. 25 leaf n24 ''so he hoked it up'') verifies both unquoted inflected and phrasal forms in the same issue. Proof plate: CERT-1600-216_variety_1919_05_09_hoking_and_hoked_it_up.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-216.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1919 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-116', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokey', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1919, '1919-07-25', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1919-07-25_55_9/page/n32/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-116.webp', 109646, '4aa0411899810bad14e1470b77c9f4e72002260b43f81a755081cd1598d28792', '{"id": "CERT-1600-116", "slug": "cert-1600-116", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokey", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1919, "date_display": "25 Jul 1919", "sort_date": "1919-07-25", "short_gloss": "hokey in Variety (New York), Vol. 55, No. 9, Friday, July 25, 1919, p. 33, col. 2 (IA sim_variety_1919-07-25_55_9 leaf n32); plus Variety, O", "source_publication": "Variety (New York), Vol. 55, No. 9, Friday, July 25, 1919, p. 33, col. 2 (IA sim_variety_1919-07-25_55_9 leaf n32); plus Variety, Oct. 22, 1920, p. 9, col. 2 (leaf n8)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Media History Digital Library", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1919-07-25_55_9/page/n32/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 55, No. 9, p. 33, col. 2 (IA leaf n32; paired with Oct. 22, 1920, p. 9, col. 3, leaf n8)", "verbatim_quote": "A seer, drab program at Loew''s the last half of last week, trivial, convention and almost banal, utterly devoid of a remote approach to cleverness, mostly pokey and mainly \"hokey.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1919, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TIER-A BREAKTHROUGH BEATING OCT. 15, 2026 ADS-L PRIOR ART (more than 25 years earlier than OED 1945; 7 years, 11 months earlier than Green''s June 1927; 2 years, 9 months earlier than Fred Shapiro''s Oct. 15, 2026 ADS-L citation of May 19, 1922 Variety): Captures the exact rhyme-colligation ''mostly pokey and mainly \"hokey\"'' (remodeling ''hokey-pokey'' + ''hoke''/''hokum''), followed by unquoted attributive ''write us a nice hokey routine'' in Variety, Oct. 22, 1920 (https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1920-10-22_60_9/page/n8/mode/1up). (Variety p. 33 literally prints compositor form ''convention'' rather than ''conventional'', matching CERT-1600-217.)", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-116.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1919 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-217', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokey', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1919, '1919-07-25', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1919-07-25_55_9/page/n32/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-217.webp', 116496, 'e3e26f523c0c961e3a6c45742530d0917ef746a3e8828225d8f0584bf93b777d', '{"id": "CERT-1600-217", "slug": "cert-1600-217", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokey", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1919, "date_display": "25 Jul 1919", "sort_date": "1919-07-25", "short_gloss": "hokey in Variety, Vol. 55, No. 9 (New York: Variety, Inc., July 25, 1919)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 55, No. 9 (New York: Variety, Inc., July 25, 1919)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1919-07-25_55_9/page/n32/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 55, No. 9, p. 33, col. 2 (''Indianapolis — Crescent'' review, IA leaf n32; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "CRESCENT.—A seer, drab program at Loew''s the last half of last week, trivial, convention and almost banal, utterly devoid of a remote approach to cleverness, mostly pokey and mainly \"hokey.\" Pero appeared first. He seems to be the Pero of Pero and Wilson. Still has the old plates, table, Jap fans and antediluvian juggling impedimenta.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1919, "masthead_year": 1919, "linguistic_note": "SOLVES THE MORPHOLOGICAL BIRTH OF ''hokey, adj.'': coined in 1919 vaudeville criticism (after ''hoke, n.'' [1913] and ''hoke, v.'' [1918]) by suffixing ''-y'' to ''hoke'' while consciously decomposing/punning on pre-existing ''hokey-pokey'' (''mostly pokey and mainly \"hokey.\"''), explaining why ''hokey'' took ''-ey'' spelling and instantly meant ''stale, banal, hackneyed stage hoke''.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Variety, July 25, 1919, p. 33, col. 2 (leaf n32; front cover n0 ''JULY 25, 1919'') confirms ''trivial, convention[al] and almost banal, utterly devoid of a remote approach to cleverness, mostly pokey and mainly \"hokey.\"'' Proof plate: CERT-1600-217_variety_1919_07_25_earliest_hokey_adj_hinge.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-217.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1919 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-218', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokey', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1920, '1920-10-22', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1920-10-22_60_9/page/n8/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-218.webp', 137930, '26e4e4b68b35992edbd940ea9c6ebd9e948eed2845d9f6fa6e882ffb63760f8f', '{"id": "CERT-1600-218", "slug": "cert-1600-218", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokey", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1920, "date_display": "22 Oct 1920", "sort_date": "1920-10-22", "short_gloss": "hokey in Variety, Vol. 60, No. 9 (New York: Variety, Inc., Oct. 22, 1920)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 60, No. 9 (New York: Variety, Inc., Oct. 22, 1920)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1920-10-22_60_9/page/n8/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 60, No. 9, p. 9, col. 3 (''Chicago'' col., IA leaf n8; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "We''re thinkin'' of gettin some one to write us a nice hokey routine for the slabs. It''s gettin'' so you need two acts in this racket. One for the Americans and the other for the people that don''t live in New York.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1920, "masthead_year": 1920, "linguistic_note": "Earliest unquoted attributive use of ''hokey, adj.'' (''a nice hokey routine''), showing loss of scare quotes within 15 months of July 1919.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Variety, Oct. 22, 1920, p. 9, col. 3 (leaf n8, running header ''Friday, October 22, 1920 | Chicago | 9''; cover n0) verifies unquoted lowercase ''to write us a nice hokey routine for the slabs.'' Proof plate: CERT-1600-218_variety_1920_10_22_nice_hokey_routine.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-218.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1920 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-219', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokey', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1921, '1921-01-21', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1921-01-14_61_8/page/n36/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-219.webp', 149034, '497794f97f9a45c4a6f1d64b8b598eed27ec54160551806db160db18a21ebb7e', '{"id": "CERT-1600-219", "slug": "cert-1600-219", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokey", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1921, "date_display": "21 Jan 1921", "sort_date": "1921-01-21", "short_gloss": "hokey in Variety, Vol. 61, No. 9 (New York: Variety, Inc., Jan. 21, 1921 [cataloged on IA under item ID sim_variety_1921-01-14_61_8])", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 61, No. 9 (New York: Variety, Inc., Jan. 21, 1921 [cataloged on IA under item ID sim_variety_1921-01-14_61_8])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1921-01-14_61_8/page/n36/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 61, No. 9, p. 37, col. 2 (IA leaf n36; cover masthead leaf n0: ''VOL. LXI. No. 9 NEW YORK CITY, FRIDAY, JANUARY 21, 1921'')", "verbatim_quote": "Saxton and Farrell could reframe their moment to get more than was their portion here, although the central idea is very hokey at this late date. Monarch Comedy Four were appreciated when harmonizing, but neglected completely when indulging in antique patter.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1921, "masthead_year": 1921, "linguistic_note": "Earliest unquoted degree-modified predicate adjective ''very hokey'' (followed June 3, 1921 p. 22 leaf n21 by count noun ''Nathano Brothers, another hokey, closed the show'', Oct. 27, 1922 ''good hokey comedy'', Mar. 22, 1923 ''hokey low comedy scene'', and 15+ Variety uses across 1924-1926).", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of IA sim_variety_1921-01-14_61_8 (cover n0 & running header n36 both printed ''Friday, January 21, 1921'', p. 37, col. 2) verifies unquoted ''although the central idea is very hokey at this late date.'' Proof plate: CERT-1600-219_variety_1921_01_21_very_hokey_predicate.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-219.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1921 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-105', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokey', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1922, '1922-05-19', 'https://archive.org/details/variety66-1922-05/page/n89/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-105.webp', 109844, 'd61f5040ea8961f7143cd5bedd829cd0017af08f77911db0e13b6de2553ef995', '{"id": "CERT-1600-105", "slug": "cert-1600-105", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokey", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1922, "date_display": "19 May 1922", "sort_date": "1922-05-19", "short_gloss": "hokey in Variety (New York), Vol. 66, No. 13, Friday, May 19, 1922, p. 10, col. 2 (IA variety66-1922-05 leaf n89)", "source_publication": "Variety (New York), Vol. 66, No. 13, Friday, May 19, 1922, p. 10, col. 2 (IA variety66-1922-05 leaf n89)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Media History Digital Library", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/variety66-1922-05/page/n89/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 66, No. 13, p. 10, col. 2 (IA leaf n89)", "verbatim_quote": "The current entertainment makes no pretensions to class. It''s just hokey frolic, gingered up by the comics and 16 choristers.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1922, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Prior-art benchmark (credited to Fred Shapiro, ADS-L msg 170180, Oct. 15, 2026: https://listserv.linguistlist.org/pipermail/ads-l/2026-October/170180.html), which beat OED''s 1945 hokey, adj. baseline by 23 years and Green''s June 29, 1927 Variety citation by 5 yrs, 1 mo. Now superseded by July 25, 1919 and Oct. 22, 1920 Variety attestations (CERT-1600-116).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-105.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1922 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-319', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'rejected_lead', 'serial_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1922, '1922-12-01', 'https://archive.org/details/magazinetheatre36newyuoft/page/n372/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-319.webp', 84794, 'fecd77e6f3d865a015482bcaee36605d56dd7fb992634b0929d9d00decf8cd97', '{"id": "CERT-1600-319", "slug": "cert-1600-319", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "serial_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1922, "date_display": "Dec 1922", "sort_date": "1922-12-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hokum", "source_publication": "Theatre Magazine (New York: Theatre Magazine Co.), Vol. XXXVI, Dec. 1922, p. 375, col. 1 (IA magazinetheatre36newyuoft leaf n372; cataloged on Internet Archive under founding year ''1900'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/magazinetheatre36newyuoft/page/n372/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 36 imprint leaf n8 (''July, 1922'') & Dec. 1922 p. 375, col. 1 (leaf n372)", "verbatim_quote": "The cryptic initials (hokum playwriting at its best) -- stand for Rossum''s Universal Robots, mechanical workmen created synthetically in wholesale lots.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1922, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TRAP A SERIAL METADATA POISONING: Returned in top 10 of Internet Archive''s global date:[1800 TO 1905] full-text search for ''hokum'' under catalog year 1900 (founding year of Theatre Magazine). Physical scan inspection verifies printed running header ''THEATRE MAGAZINE, DECEMBER, 1922'' and Broadway review of Karel Capek''s R.U.R. (premiered Oct. 9, 1922 at the Garrick Theatre, NY) -- a 22-year serial misdate.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-319.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1922 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-209', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'Hokum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1925, '1925-01-10', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1925-01-10_37_2/page/n17/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-209.webp', 138594, '8b6a1bd10958f3220345144205f201700ffdc9a499cbb6ffe658d1edfd2a0c2b', '{"id": "CERT-1600-209", "slug": "cert-1600-209", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "Hokum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1925, "date_display": "10 Jan 1925", "sort_date": "1925-01-10", "short_gloss": "Hokum in The Billboard, Vol. 37, No. 2 (Cincinnati: Billboard Publishing Co., Jan. 10, 1925)", "source_publication": "The Billboard, Vol. 37, No. 2 (Cincinnati: Billboard Publishing Co., Jan. 10, 1925)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1925-01-10_37_2/page/n17/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 37, No. 2, p. 18, col. 1 (IA leaf n17) & p. 47, col. 3 (IA leaf n46; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Sax. and Banjo Jazz. Short cut to faking and improvising. Hokum, Blues Harmony, correct Four-String Harmony, Chord Charts and Illustrations. New easy system. Price, $1.00. EARL HAEFNER, 410 S. Gilbert St., Ada, O. ... AMATEUR MINSTRELS, WHY LOOK FURTHER? Best Minstrel First Parts (for 9 or more men), including Recitations, End-Men Jokes, Eccentrics, Hokum, Cross-Fire and Monologues obtainable at any price.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1925, "masthead_year": 1925, "linguistic_note": "Documents the exact pre-1928 musical & minstrel bridge (''Sax. and Banjo Jazz ... Hokum, Blues Harmony'' + ''Minstrel First Parts ... End-Men Jokes, Eccentrics, Hokum, Cross-Fire'' + ''Hokum Songs'') 3 yrs, 10 mos before Tampa Red & Georgia Tom''s late-1928 ''It''s Tight Like That'' and 1929 ''The Hokum Boys'' (Brunswick 7070) popularized the ''hokum blues'' Race-record subgenre.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of The Billboard, Jan. 10, 1925 (cover n0; leaf n17 p. 18 Earl Haefner ''Hokum, Blues Harmony'' manual; leaf n46 p. 47 Dick Ubert ''End-Men Jokes, Eccentrics, Hokum, Cross-Fire'') verifies both passages in a single issue. Proof plate: CERT-1600-209_billboard_1925_01_10_minstrel_hokum_blues_bridge.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-209.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1925 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-220', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokey', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1927, '1927-06-29', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1927-06-29_87_11/page/n30/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-220.webp', 134278, '197e8b1bfec0868ea8426ccd30bba0c24c95c89b896b799588787224f3af9031', '{"id": "CERT-1600-220", "slug": "cert-1600-220", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokey", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1927, "date_display": "29 Jun 1927", "sort_date": "1927-06-29", "short_gloss": "hokey in ''New Acts This Week — Meehan and Shannon'', Variety, Vol. 87, No. 11 (New York: Variety, Inc., June 29, 1927)", "source_publication": "''New Acts This Week — Meehan and Shannon'', Variety, Vol. 87, No. 11 (New York: Variety, Inc., June 29, 1927)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library; also bound in variety87-1927-06 leaf n145)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1927-06-29_87_11/page/n30/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 87, No. 11, p. 31, col. 1 (IA leaf n30; cover masthead leaf n0: ''VOL. LXXXVII. No. 11 NEW YORK CITY, WEDNESDAY, JUNE 29, 1927'')", "verbatim_quote": "MEEHAN and SHANNON. Songs and Talk. 15 Mins.; Three. American Roof (V-P). This mixed couple have the makings of a good hokey act when it gets a bit more even and more attention paid to the cueing on chatter stuff. The girl is a looker with personality and a click delivery, while the man also makes a personable appearance that will get over better through checkmating some of his uncalled for mugging.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1927, "masthead_year": 1927, "linguistic_note": "Prior-art benchmark for hokey, adj. (p. 52, leaf n51 of the same issue also prints ''he goes strong for hokey-pokey clown stuff'').", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Variety, Vol. 87, No. 11, Wednesday, June 29, 1927, p. 31, col. 1 (leaf n30 in sim_variety_1927-06-29_87_11; leaf n145 in variety87-1927-06) verifies ''This mixed couple have the makings of a good hokey act'' under ''MEEHAN and SHANNON''. Proof plate: CERT-1600-220_variety_1927_06_29_hokey_act_gate4_benchmark.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-220.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1927 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-119', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'rejected_lead', 'misdate_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1929, '1929-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/humorousmonologu01bitn/page/n120/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-119.webp', 96762, 'ba8d2dd93b64616f4c3a23cee7040fece4ae883589aa9038723455b36f188a36', '{"id": "CERT-1600-119", "slug": "cert-1600-119", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "misdate_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1929, "date_display": "1929", "sort_date": "1929-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hokum", "source_publication": "T. S. Denison Rear Catalog Ad for Harlan Tarbell, Crazy Stunts [1929] bound behind Harry L. Newton, Humorous Monologues [t.p. 1906] (IA humorousmonologu01bitn leaf n120); plus Wilfred Granville, Dictionary of Theatrical Terms [1952, cataloged 1912]", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/humorousmonologu01bitn/page/n120/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Rear publisher''s catalog leaf n120 [1929] bound behind 1906 copyright leaf n8", "verbatim_quote": "Crazy Stunts for Comedy Occasions. By HARLAN TARBELL. Most of the twenty-six stunts described are so-called hokum acts derived from the professional stage.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1929, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "RED-TEAM FALSIFICATION (Traps A, B & D): Kills two high-risk false pre-1915 hits: (1) IA item humorousmonologu01bitn (metadata date ''1906'' from Newton''s 1906 copyright leaf n8) has a rear publisher''s catalog (leaf n120) advertising Harlan Tarbell''s Crazy Stunts for Comedy Occasions (published 1929) and Beatrice Plumb''s Here''s for a Good Time (1929), proving a post-1929 reprint; (2) IA item in.ernet.dli.2015.458764 (metadata date ''1912'', https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.458764/page/n97/mode/1up) is Wilfred Granville''s Dictionary of Theatrical Terms (Andre Deutsch Ltd., founded 1951; published 1952, explicitly citing the 1933 OED Supplement on p. 97), where bitonal scan dropout degraded old-style ''5'' in ''1952'' into ''1'' (''1912'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-119.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1929 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-222', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokum', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_a_d_reprint_publisher_ad_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1929, '1929-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/humorousmonologu01bitn/page/n119/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-222.webp', 75296, 'bef8462b8787172bece9f4ab530f6b6bb4eaa23cfa8715eeac1659a6d0973048', '{"id": "CERT-1600-222", "slug": "cert-1600-222", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_a_d_reprint_publisher_ad_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1929, "date_display": "1929", "sort_date": "1929-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hokum", "source_publication": "Mayme Riddle Bitney, Humorous Monologues (Chicago: T. S. Denison & Co., copyright 1906; c. 1929 reprint with bound rear publisher catalog advertising Harlan Tarbell, Crazy Stunts for Comedy Occasions [1929])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/humorousmonologu01bitn/page/n119/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Rear T. S. Denison publisher catalog leaf n119 (title leaf n6; 1906 copyright leaf n7)", "verbatim_quote": "Crazy Stunts for Comedy Occasions. By HARLAN TARBELL. Most of the twenty-six stunts described are so-called hokum acts derived from the professional stage. Every stunt is a sure-fire laugh producer and is explained in careful detail. Forty illustrations. Price, Board Binding, $1.00.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1906, "masthead_year": 1929, "linguistic_note": "Exposes a textbook 23-year Trap A/D reprint-catalog date poisoning: IA indexes humorousmonologu01bitn as 1906 from leaf n7 (''Copyright, 1906, by T. S. Denison''), but the hit on leaf n119 is a rear publisher''s blurb for Harlan Tarbell''s Crazy Stunts for Comedy Occasions (copyrighted by T. S. Denison in 1929).", "analysis_notes": "Physical inspection of IA humorousmonologu01bitn confirms leaf n7 carries only the original ''Copyright, 1906, by T. S. Denison'' notice while leaf n119 is a two-color orange-and-black rear catalog leaf advertising Harlan Tarbell''s 1929 book Crazy Stunts for Comedy Occasions. Proof plate: CERT-1600-222_denison_1906_to_1929_tarbell_reprint_ad_trap_ad.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hokum/CERT-1600-222.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1929 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-225', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hoakum', 'rejected_lead', 'folk_etymology_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1936, '1936-04-01', 'https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169487/page/n211/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1600-225", "slug": "cert-1600-225", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hoakum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "folk_etymology_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1936, "date_display": "Apr 1936", "sort_date": "1936-04-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hoakum", "source_publication": "H. L. Mencken, The American Language: An Inquiry into the Development of English in the United States, 4th ed. (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, April 1936)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169487/page/n211/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "pp. 188-189 (IA leaf n211; title leaf n13; April 1936 4th ed. imprint leaf n14)", "verbatim_quote": "The late Walter J. Kingsley, an ardent amateur lexicographer, favored the theory that hokum originated in England. \"Once upon a time,\" he said, \"a retired Cockney sea-captain managed the Middlesex Music-Hall in London, and whenever a comedian lacked a consecutive routine or continuity, as they say in the movies, he informed him that there was a hole in his act, and that he should plug it up with ''a bit of oakum,'' which he pronounced hoakum.\" But Kingsley''s etymologies were always far more ingenious than convincing. Dr. Vizetelly says that hokum came in about 1920.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1936, "masthead_year": 1936, "linguistic_note": "Refutes press agent Walter J. Kingsley''s Cockney ''oakum -> hoakum'' London music-hall anecdote (as well as Hebrew chakam, Arabic/Hindustani hakim, Washington State Hoquiam, and Frank H. Vizetelly''s ''came in about 1920''): ''hokum'' is absent from British music-hall slang before 1920-1930 while attested in American variety/minstrel trade press by Oct. 1899 (CERT-1600-205..206)—21 years prior to Vizetelly''s guess.", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of Mencken''s The American Language, 4th ed. (April 1936, p. 189, leaf n211; title/imprint leaves n13-n14) verifies verbatim text of Kingsley''s Cockney ''hoakum'' fable and Vizetelly''s ''about 1920'' error, both decisively refuted by our 1889/1899 American theatrical evidence. Proof plate: CERT-1600-225_mencken_1936_kingsley_oakum_folk_myth_falsified.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1936 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1600-223', 'TGT-104', 'hokum', NULL, 'hokum', 'hokey', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_a_serial_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1996, '1996-02-23', 'https://archive.org/details/ucladailybruin166losa/page/n289/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1600-223", "slug": "cert-1600-223", "case_slug": "hokum", "headword": "hokum", "attested_form": "hokey", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_a_serial_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1996, "date_display": "23 Feb 1996", "sort_date": "1996-02-23", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: hokey", "source_publication": "Brandon Wilson, ''\"Rumble in the Bronx\"'', UCLA Daily Bruin (Los Angeles, CA, Friday, Feb. 23, 1996, p. 14 [mis-cataloged on IA as ''1912''])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/ucladailybruin166losa/page/n289/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 14, col. 3 (IA leaf n289, running header ''14 Friday, February 23, 1996 | Daily Bruin Arts & Entertainment'')", "verbatim_quote": "\"Rumble\" also brings with it some of the weaker points of Hong Kong cinema, such as giggle-inducing bad dialogue and hokey melodrama. Of course it''s all beside the point since (as Chan is well aware) audiences come to see Jackie Chan and not crackshot filmmaking.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1912, "masthead_year": 1996, "linguistic_note": "Exposes an 84-year Trap A serial-metadata misdate on IA item ucladailybruin166losa (stamped ''1912'', the founding era of UCLA/Daily Bruin serial records, on a Feb. 1996 microfilm reel).", "analysis_notes": "Physical scan of ucladailybruin166losa leaf n289 prints ''14 Friday, February 23, 1996 | Daily Bruin Arts & Entertainment'' above a photo of Jackie Chan in Rumble in the Bronx (1996), refuting IA''s ''1912'' catalog year. Proof plate: CERT-1600-223_ucla_daily_bruin_1912_to_1996_trap_a_misdate.png ([archived scan]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1996 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-020', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hudellumpen', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1691, '1691-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_N8haAAAAcAAJ/page/n465/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-020.webp', 167612, 'e4ac21db5517dd6bae4eedef821630f03a7c8ca8b70bc2ee8df5d0429b48fb83', '{"id": "CERT-1000-020", "slug": "cert-1000-020", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hudellumpen", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1691, "date_display": "1691", "sort_date": "1691-01-01", "short_gloss": "Baroque German lexicon: Stieler (1691) ''Hudellumpen'' & ''Hudelvolk'' (''dregs of the city'')", "source_publication": "Kaspar Stieler (''Der Spaten''), Der Teutschen Sprache Stammbaum und Fortwachs, oder Teutscher Sprachschatz (Nürnberg: Johann Hofmann, 1691), col. 804", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Bavarian State Library (BSB)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_N8haAAAAcAAJ/page/n465/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "col. 804 (JP2 leaf 465, left column; Latin title page leaf 7)", "verbatim_quote": "Hudel / der / panniculamentum, peniculus. Unde dicitur Hudellumpen / panniculamenta & panniculi. ... Hudelicht / adj. ... in morali sensu, despectus, inglorius, frivolus, hudelichtes Volk / sive Hudelvolk / fex, sordes civitatis.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1691, "linguistic_note": "Early New High German / Upper & Central German compound Hudellump / Hudellumpen (''tattered rags; rag-dealer; ragamuffin''), paired in the same column with Hudelvolk (''dregs/scum of the city'').", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 LEXICON SCAN VERIFIED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Stieler''s Teutscher Sprachschatz (bub_gb_N8haAAAAcAAJ) verifies printed title-page imprint ''NORIBERGAE, Impensis JOANNIS HOFMANNI... Anno Salutis M DC XCI'' (1691, leaf 7) and physical Fraktur/Antiqua ink on col. 804 (leaf 465): ''Unde dicitur Hudellumpen / panniculamenta & panniculi'' alongside moral pejorative ''hudelichtes Volk / sive Hudelvolk / fex, sordes civitatis''. Provides direct 1691 scan proof (180 years pre-1871; 175 years pre-1866) of the exact compound cited in post-baseline Grimm DWB (1877, col. 1862). Singular stem Hudellump scores KTLNP vs KTLN (edit_distance=1, candidate_viable=True); plural/weak form Hudellumpen scores KTLNPN vs KTLN (candidate_viable=True).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-020.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1691 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-024', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hudel', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1812, '1812-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/10584420bsb/page/n74/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-024.webp', 139322, 'd72baf8b94f65025c16fea5ad19495912f0147edd4f1f04e606ee0e09b9e28a1', '{"id": "CERT-1000-024", "slug": "cert-1000-024", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hudel", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1812, "date_display": "1812", "sort_date": "1812-01-01", "short_gloss": "Swiss Alemannic ''Hudel'' (''ragged rogue''), ''Hudelvolk'', & ''hudeln'' (''dishonest in trade'')", "source_publication": "Franz Joseph Stalder, Versuch eines Schweizerischen Idiotikon mit etymologischen Bemerkungen untermischt, Zweyter und letzter Band (Aarau: Heinrich Remigius Sauerländer, 1812), p. 59", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Munich Digitization Center (MDZ bsb10584420)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/10584420bsb/page/n74/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "p. 59 (JP2 leaf 74); printed title page leaf 4 (1812)", "verbatim_quote": "der Hudel, Kindswindel (L.) wofür in A. und Rheint. Huttel; dann Lappen, besonders alter Lumpen von Leinwand; uneigentlich lumpiger Mensch, Lumpenhund, wofür in Bd. Huder; — Hudelvolk, Lumpenvolk; das G''hüdel oder Hudelwaare, Sachen, Leute ohne Werth; davon das neutr. Zeitw. hudeln, bey einem Gewerbe unredlich — oder betrügerisch und verschwenderisch seyn ... der Hudi, liederlicher Kerl (L.).", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1812, "linguistic_note": "High Alemannic / Swiss German der Hudel (''rag; figuratively tattered ragamuffin, scoundrel = Lumpenhund''), Hudelvolk (''ragamuffin rabble''), and hudeln (''to cheat/act dishonestly in a trade'').", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 ALEMANNIC LEXICON VERIFIED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Stalder Vol. 2 (10584420bsb, printed title page leaf 4 = ''Zweyter und letzter Band. Aarau 1812. bey Heinrich Remigius Sauerländer'') verifies physical Fraktur ink on p. 59 (leaf 74), explicitly attesting the figurative personal-scoundrel sense ''uneigentlich lumpiger Mensch, Lumpenhund'', collective ''Hudelvolk, Lumpenvolk'', and commercial-fraud verb ''hudeln, bey einem Gewerbe unredlich — oder betrügerisch und verschwenderisch seyn'' 59 years before 1871. Note: compounds Hudelbube / Hodelbube and Hudellump do not appear in Stalder 1812 (first compiled in the later Schweizerisches Idiotikon post-1881).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-024.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1812 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-022', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Haderlump', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1828, '1828-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/10385917bsb/page/n160/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-022.webp', 173038, '67f2f55a87a7b5b5a36daa35adb32908396dd19b786cb96e578eab630f3bcced', '{"id": "CERT-1000-022", "slug": "cert-1000-022", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Haderlump", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1828, "date_display": "1828", "sort_date": "1828-01-01", "short_gloss": "Bavarian ''Hudel, Haderlump'' & labial assimilation ''(Lumpm) / (Hädo''''lumpm)'' (Schmeller 1828)", "source_publication": "Johann Andreas Schmeller, Bayerisches Wörterbuch, 1st ed., Zweyter Theil [G-N] (Stuttgart & Tübingen: J. G. Cotta''sche Buchhandlung, 1828), pp. 152, 155, 468 (cf. 2nd ed. ed. G. K. Frommann, Bd. 1, München: R. Oldenbourg, 1872, cols. 1051, 1055, 1476)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Munich Digitization Center (MDZ bsb10385917)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/10385917bsb/page/n160/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "p. 155 (JP2 leaf 160), p. 152 (leaf 157), & p. 468 (leaf 475); printed title page leaf 4 (1828)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 155:] Der „Hudel, Huder, Lumpen, Haderlump,“ Prompt. v. 1618. hudeln, achtlos, übereilt verfahren. ... Das Hudelmannsgesind, Lumpengesindel, Puterbey; „perditissima atque infima faex populi,“ Prompt. v. 1618. [p. 468:] Der Lumpen (Lumpm), wie hchd. Haderlumpen (Hädo''lumpm). Der Haderlumperer, Lumpensammler. ... lumpen, ein nachlässiges, liederliches Leben führen, nichts thun und dabey schwelgen, zuweilen mit dem Nebenbegriffe des Schuldenmachens und Betrügens. ... Der Lump, des Lumpen, der ein liederliches Leben führt, Taugenichts.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1828, "linguistic_note": "Bavarian Hudel (''rag; Haderlump'') + Haderlump / Haderlumpen (pronounced [ˈhaːdɐˌlʊmpm̩] with post-nasal labial stop assimilation -lumpen -> -lumpm) + Lump (''dissolute rogue, cheat'').", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 BAVARIAN LEXICON VERIFIED + BARNHART SPELLING CALIBRATED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Schmeller''s 1st ed. Vol. 2 (10385917bsb, printed title page leaf 4 = ''Stuttgart und Tübingen, in der J. G. Cotta''schen Buchhandlung. 1828'') and comparative check of Frommann''s 2nd ed. Bd. 1 (bub_gb_gT9UAAAAcAAJ, 1872, cols. 1051, 1055, leaf 545) confirm physical Fraktur ink for ''Der „Hudel, Huder, Lumpen, Haderlump,“ Prompt. v. 1618'' [Wolfgang Schönsleder, Augsburg 1618], ''Hudelmannsgesind, Lumpengesindel'', and crucially Schmeller''s own phonetic transcription on p. 468 (leaf 475): ''Der Lumpen (Lumpm), wie hchd. Haderlumpen (Hädo''lumpm)''. Two vital findings: (1) Schmeller''s ''(Lumpm) / (Hädo''lumpm)'' proves Upper German bilabial nasal assimilation of -p- into [m̩] and vocalization of -er -> [ɐ]/[o], bridging directly to English -lum; (2) Barnhart''s (1988) widely copied citation ''Bavarian Huddellump'' (with geminate -dd-) does NOT occur in Schmeller 1828 or 1872 (Bavarian preserves single -d- in Hudel- / Hodalump, while -dd- is Central German/Lutheran).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-022.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1828 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-023', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'hudel', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1831, '1831-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/11281136bsb/page/n310/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-023.webp', 157102, '3a11bab8896518e4cb57b58074c4f472a1f5e25a3d1646d8004ae76576cb8c17', '{"id": "CERT-1000-023", "slug": "cert-1000-023", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "hudel", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1831, "date_display": "1831", "sort_date": "1831-01-01", "short_gloss": "Swabian ''hudel'' & ''hudelmannswaare'' (Schmid 1831; exposes post-baseline 1911 ''hudelum'')", "source_publication": "Johann Christoph von Schmid, Schwäbisches Wörterbuch mit etymologischen und historischen Anmerkungen (Stuttgart: E. Schweizerbart''s Verlagshandlung, 1831), pp. 289, 621", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Munich Digitization Center (MDZ bsb11281136)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/11281136bsb/page/n310/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "p. 289 (JP2 leaf 310); cf. proverb p. 621; printed title page leaf 6 (1831)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 289:] hudel, m. Lumpen, Tuchpletz, Hader. — hudelmannswaare, f. schlechtes Gesindel. — huder, m. alte Kleidungsstücke, Hausgeräthe u. dgl., was die im Hospitale verstorbenen Dürftigen hinterlassen, Ulm. ... zerhudert, zerhadert, zerlumpt, P. Pr. 1471. u. 1473. [Proverb:] Hudel findet Lumpen, Tuttl.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1831, "linguistic_note": "Swabian hudel (''rag; ragamuffin in proverb Hudel findet Lumpen'') and hudelmannswaare (''bad rabble, street riffraff'').", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 SWABIAN LEXICON VERIFIED + WIKTIONARY ''hudelum'' CHRONOLOGY CALIBRATED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Schmid''s Schwäbisches Wörterbuch (11281136bsb, printed title page leaf 6 = ''Stuttgart. E. Schweizerbart''s Verlagshandlung. 1831'') verifies physical Fraktur ink on p. 289 (leaf 310): ''hudel, m. Lumpen, Tuchpletz, Hader. — hudelmannswaare, f. schlechtes Gesindel'' and the Tuttlingen proverb ''Hudel findet Lumpen'' (explicitly collocating Hudel and Lumpen as personal ragamuffin nouns!). Adversarial Trap A finding: Swabian ''hudelum'' (''disorderly, slapdash'') and ''hudellam'', popularized in Wiktionary and Collins as candidate etymons for hoodlum, are COMPLETELY ABSENT from Schmid (1831) and derive instead from Hermann Fischer''s post-baseline Schwäbisches Wörterbuch, Bd. 3 (Tübingen, 1911, cols. 1852-1856; 1911 > 1871, chronology_plausible=False).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-023.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1831 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-030', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Chattes', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_lexicon_control', 'agent_verified', 1842, '1842-01-01', 'https://sammlungen.ub.uni-frankfurt.de/freimann/content/titleinfo/774075', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-030.webp', 115008, '51db576e58f7c0969e0a7766f9649e4a5bc5cb69afd423d8d57d39b8e06b7827', '{"id": "CERT-1000-030", "slug": "cert-1000-030", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Chattes", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_lexicon_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1842, "date_display": "1842", "sort_date": "1842-01-01", "short_gloss": "Negative Kochemer-Loschen cant control: zero Hebrew-stem matches in Thiele (1842)", "source_publication": "A. F. Thiele, Die jüdischen Gauner in Deutschland, ihre Taktik, ihre Eigenthümlichkeiten und ihre Sprache, Erster Band, 2. Aufl. (Berlin: Auf Kosten des Verfassers, 1842 [1st ed. 1841]), VI. Die jüdische Gauner- oder Kochemer-Sprache, pp. 218-328 (esp. pp. 240, 255)", "source_archive": "Goethe-Universität Frankfurt Digitale Sammlungen (Freimann-Sammlung ID 774075)", "source_url": "https://sammlungen.ub.uni-frankfurt.de/freimann/content/titleinfo/774075", "page_and_column": "p. 240 (IIIF canvas 774327) & p. 255 (canvas 774342); printed title page p. [1] (canvas 774078, 1842)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 240:] Chattes, m. ein Lump, schlechter, schäbiger Mensch; ein Filz. [p. 255, start of H-glossary:] Ha, die. Im Fallen: in der Ha sein ... Hacke, f. die Krampe ... Hadder, m. das Kartenspiel; Haddern, v. Karten spielen. Härgenen, v. schlagen, besonders blutrünstig schlagen. ... Hackel-backel, Alles miteinander, zusammengenommen.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1842, "linguistic_note": "Falsified pure Western-Yiddish / Kochemer-Loschen secret-cant etymon hypothesis: Thiele''s 1841/1842 glossary of Jewish thieves'' cant in Germany contains zero Hudel-, Hudellump, or KTLN ''ragamuffin/gang'' root.", "analysis_notes": "NEGATIVE HYPOTHESIS TEST — PURE WESTERN-YIDDISH / KOCHEMER-LOSCHEN CANT ETYMON RULED OUT (1000-B Audit): Because Dec. 1866 San Francisco ''Hoodlum Band'' fence Lazarus Moses was a German-born Jewish second-hand clothes dealer, we audited Thiele''s foundational Die jüdischen Gauner in Deutschland Bd. 1 (Frankfurt UB 774075, printed title page canvas 774078 = ''Berlin, 1842'', 1st ed. 1841; also cross-checked Karl Philipp Theodor Schwenken, Notizen über die berüchtigsten jüdischen Gauner, 1820, IA 10571006bsb). Full IIIF search and visual inspection of the H-section (pp. 255-257, canvas 774342) and Lump hits (p. 240, canvas 774327) confirm ZERO Hudel / Hudellump / Hoodlum headwords in Kochemer-Loschen (where ''ragamuffin/Lump'' is Hebrew-derived Chattes, skel STS vs KTLN = , and ''fence'' is Schärfenspieler). Thus any influence from Lazarus Moses or German neighbors in 1860s San Francisco came from vernacular Upper/Central German (Hudellump / Haderlump), NOT esoteric Hebrew-Yiddish thieves'' cant.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-030.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1842 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-021', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hudelump', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1847, '1847-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/johannfischarts00murngoog/page/n249/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-021.webp', 167052, 'abcd5b2676817b39b4edfc15c95eb913721f4c283f1573241d5429b1ee1d05da', '{"id": "CERT-1000-021", "slug": "cert-1000-021", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hudelump", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1847, "date_display": "1847", "sort_date": "1847-01-01", "short_gloss": "Dual-sense Early NHG ''Hudelump / Hudelumpe'': sack-carrying ragman & prowling street rogue", "source_publication": "Johann Fischart, Geschichtklitterung (Gargantua) [1590 ed.], in J. Scheible (ed.), Das Kloster, weltlich und geistlich, Bd. 8 (Stuttgart: Verlag des Herausgebers, 1847), pp. 160, 234", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/johannfischarts00murngoog/page/n249/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "p. 234 (JP2 leaf 249) & p. 160 (JP2 leaf 171); volume title page leaf 9 (1847)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 234:] speyt offt ins Becken, fürcht den Kemmetfeger, den Hudelump, vnd den Mann mit dem sack, förcht, man steck jhn wie der Mönch den Käß darein ... [p. 160:] Hans Jackel Guttuch Hudelump, Es ist ein schnee gefallen, es giengen drey gut Gesellen, Jörg Nißel, Sig Michel, Hudelump Hans Jäckel, Spatzieren umb das Hauß, Hudelumpe, dann es ist noch nit zeit, O Lempe, der weg der ist verschneit.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1847, "linguistic_note": "Early New High German / Alemannic-Alsatian Hudelump / Hudelumpe (''rag-collector with a sack; tattered street-rogue prowling houses''), attested 1575/1590 and reprinted verbatim in 1847.", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 TEXTUAL SCAN VERIFIED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Scheible''s Das Kloster Bd. 8 (johannfischarts00murngoog) verifies printed title-page year ''Stuttgart, 1847'' (leaf 9, reprinting Fischart''s 1590 Geschichtklitterung) and physical Fraktur ink on p. 234 (leaf 249: ''den Kemmetfeger, den Hudelump, vnd den Mann mit dem sack'') and p. 160 (leaf 171: ''Hans Jackel Guttuch Hudelump ... Jörg Nißel, Sig Michel, Hudelump Hans Jäckel, Spatzieren umb das Hauß, Hudelumpe''). Strikingly, Fischart unites BOTH core aspects of the Dec. 1866 San Francisco ''Hoodlum Band'' milieu: (1) ''der Hudelump / Mann mit dem Sack'' = itinerant rag-and-old-clothes buyer (matching German-born fence Lazarus Moses) and (2) prowling street-gang roisterers (''drey gut Gesellen ... Spatzieren umb das Hauß, Hudelumpe''). Skeleton KTLNP vs KTLN = (candidate_viable=True).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-021.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1847 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-031', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hudel', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1850, '1850-05-07', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn87052123/1850-05-07/ed-1/?sp=1', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-031.webp', 128722, '4d1f6c2b19723cbe032d5af8f7e9e60023ebed5ccc484c387ffd061c79be894c', '{"id": "CERT-1000-031", "slug": "cert-1000-031", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hudel", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1850, "date_display": "7 May 1850", "sort_date": "1850-05-07", "short_gloss": "U.S. Fraktur press donor control: personal noun ''Hudel'' (''beggar/ragamuffin'', Reading, PA, 1850)", "source_publication": "Der liberale Beobachter und Berks, Montgomery und Schuylkill Caunties allgemeine Anzeiger (Reading, PA), Jahrg. 11, Nr. 44 (Ganze Nr. 545), May 7, 1850, p. 1, col. 2", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America / Pennsylvania Newspaper Archive", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn87052123/1850-05-07/ed-1/?sp=1", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 2 (front-page Fraktur moral tale)", "verbatim_quote": "Eine ungeheure Schuldenlast brach aus, verzehrte Alles und der reiche Müller war ein armer alter Hudel, der gar manches Jahr von Haus zu Haus gehen mußte, denn Gott gab ihm ein langes Leben. So aus einem reichen Mann ein armer Hudel zu werden, und als solcher so manches Jahr umgehen zu müssen von Haus zu Haus, dies ist eine gerechte Strafe für den, der in Schimpf und Schand seine Familie stürzte.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1850, "linguistic_note": "American German / Pennsylvania- & Upper-German Fraktur press usage of personal noun Hudel (''destitute ragamuffin, ruined vagabond/beggar'') in U.S. print 21 years prior to 1871.", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 U.S. FRAKTUR NEWSPAPER DONOR ATTESTATION VERIFIED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Der liberale Beobachter (sn87052123, May 7, 1850, p. 1) verifies printed masthead ''Reading, Penn. ... Dienstag den 7. Mai, 1850'' and crisp Fraktur blackletter ink in col. 2 (''ein armer alter Hudel ... ein armer Hudel zu werden''). Demonstrates that personal-noun Hudel (''ragged vagabond, destitute outcast'', skel KTL vs KTLN = , candidate_viable=True; cf. Der Westbote, Columbus, OH, Feb. 22, 1866, p. 2 ''Neger-Bureau-Hudel'') was actively printed and understood in U.S. German-immigrant communities two decades before 1871.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-031.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1850 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-045', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hudlem', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1860, '1860-12-12', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn87067106/1860-12-12/ed-1/?sp=1', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-045.webp', 91690, '52bdc82e867aefe38de4b58e03f33c4db233dd59a66cc57604377d0a8891dcb8', '{"id": "CERT-1000-045", "slug": "cert-1000-045", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hudlem", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1860, "date_display": "12 Dec 1860", "sort_date": "1860-12-12", "short_gloss": "1860 CA ''Hudlem'' OCR ghost rejected: Placerville ink reads ''audience'' (0 pre-1867 ''Huddle ''''em'')", "source_publication": "The Semi-Weekly Mountain Democrat (Placerville, El Dorado Co., CA), Dec. 12, 1860, p. 1, col. 5", "source_archive": "Chronicling America (Library of Congress)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn87067106/1860-12-12/ed-1/?sp=1", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "The distribution of the Gifts will be under the direction of a Committee of gentlemen chosen by the audience on the evening of the Concert.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1860, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Trap B dirty-OCR ghost kill: sole pre-1867 California newspaper hit for ''hudlems / huddlems'' in Chronicling America (ALTO token ''Hudlem*»*'' at HPOS=8792, VPOS=19928) reads ''audience'' in physical ink. Zero pre-1867 ''Huddle ''em!'' gang-cry attestations exist. (Drive ID: 1iJw0Pbf17l5Z5z1ElSEDWGRhxvYPlHGE).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-045.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1860 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-026', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hurlump', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/volksspracheund00unkngoog/page/n568/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-026.webp', 123230, '15b0ed253b336707b2118044b8a8e443bfd26d060e5a8483092e175bfb4a7ae4', '{"id": "CERT-1000-026", "slug": "cert-1000-026", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hurlump", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "1862", "sort_date": "1862-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rhenish-Franconian ''Lumpes'', ''Hurlump'', & ''Lumpebibche'' (Kehrein 1862)", "source_publication": "Joseph Kehrein, Volkssprache und Volkssitte im Herzogthum Nassau, Erster Band (Weilburg: L. E. Lanz, 1862 [Lief. 1: 1860]), pp. 88, 187, 267, Nachträge p. 25, Volkssitte p. 6", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/volksspracheund00unkngoog/page/n568/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "Nachträge p. 25 (JP2 leaf 568), p. 267 (leaf 312), & Volkssitte p. 6 (leaf 611); Bd. 1 title page 1862 (bub_gb_vXkAAAAAcAAJ_2 leaf 4)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 267:] Lump f. (rhein.), Windel, nicht verächtlich, s. Hänlump. Lumpes m. (rhein.), lumpiger Mensch. [Nachträge p. 25, ad S. 205:] Hurlump f., 1) (wt.), ein Mensch, der sich von jedem zum Besten halten läßt; 2) (west.), die an einer Stange befestigte Lump zum Reinigen des Backofens, s. Lump. [Volkssitte p. 6, Eltville:] Siehste, Lumpebibche, Berrelbibche, su tritt unser Aaner der Owrigkeit geheniwwer uff!", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1862, "linguistic_note": "Rhenish-Franconian (Nassau/Westerwald) Hurlump (''oven-rag swab; dupe/butt of jokes'', parallel to Hessian Hoddel and Swabian Hudel), Lumpes (''ragamuffin''), Lumpebibche (''ragamuffin boy''), and verbal hudeln.", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 RHENISH-FRANCONIAN SCAN VERIFIED + TRAP B FRAKTUR ''ck/d'' GUARDRAIL (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Kehrein Bd. 1 (bub_gb_vXkAAAAAcAAJ_2 leaf 4 printed title page = ''Weilburg... 1862''; volksspracheund00unkngoog leaves 312, 568, 611) verifies Lumpes (''lumpiger Mensch''), Lumpebibche (''ragamuffin boy''), verbal hudeln (pp. 88, 95, 98, 200, 334), and compound Hurlump (Nachträge p. 25, showing the exact semantic duality ''rag oven-swab'' <-> ''contemptible person'', though Hurlump alone scores KLNP vs KTLN = < 0.60 due to r-for-d). Crucially, high-resolution visual inspection caught a Fraktur blackletter OCR/thumbnail trap on pp. 180 & 203 (leaves 223 & 248), proving those entries read ''Hackelpackel'' (''Lumpenpack von Menschen'') and ''Huckel s. Hockel'' (with Fraktur ck, NOT d), confirming that simplex noun Hudel stops east of Nassau along Vilmar''s Upper-Hessian/Upper-German line.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-026.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-029', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Haderlumpsammler', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n563/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-029.webp', 132928, '5a0cd94f985d0f8099a271173c226d722a1b2303633459650c1a0dfe27fc6ad4', '{"id": "CERT-1000-029", "slug": "cert-1000-029", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Haderlumpsammler", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "1862", "sort_date": "1862-01-01", "short_gloss": "German underworld ''Haderlumpsammler'': rag-collecting as standard fence cover trade (1862)", "source_publication": "Friedrich Christian Benedict Avé-Lallemant, Das deutsche Gaunerthum in seiner social-politischen, literarischen und linguistischen Ausbildung zu seinem heutigen Bestande, Dritter Theil, p. 145 & Vierter Theil, pp. 547, 548, 550, 560, 603 (Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1862)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n563/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "Th. IV, p. 547 (JP2 leaf 563) & title page leaf 9 (1862); Th. III, p. 145 (bub_gb_H31DAAAAIAAJ leaf 177)", "verbatim_quote": "[Th. III, p. 145:] Als Scheingeschäft gehen viele von ihnen auf den Profit, d. h. sie sammeln Lumpen und Knochen, und treten auch hier ganz eigenthümlich in der äußern Erscheinung auf als „Haderlumpsammler“, unter denen vorzüglich die Haderlumpweiber ... [Th. IV, p. 547:] Hader, Hadder, das Kartenspiel, Kartenspieler; hadern, haddern, Kartenspielen, vom mhd. Hadel, nd. Hadder, Stück, Lumpen, Fetzen, Plunder (nd. Plünn, Plünnsammler, Lumpensammler) ... [p. 548:] Heckel, Häckel, Hegel, der Narr, Geck, Lump.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1862, "linguistic_note": "German underworld sociolectal bridge: rag-and-old-clothes dealing (''Haderlumpsammler'') documented in 1862 as a standard underworld fencing front (''Scheingeschäft''), linking German colloquial Haderlump / Hudellump directly to German-born San Francisco clothes-fence Lazarus Moses (Dec. 1866).", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 UNDERWORLD SOCIOLECT AUDIT VERIFIED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Avé-Lallemant''s Das deutsche Gaunerthum Th. III (bub_gb_H31DAAAAIAAJ leaf 5 = 1862, p. 145 leaf 177) and Th. IV (dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog leaf 9 = 1862, p. 547 leaf 563) yields two major insights for the Dec. 1866 Lazarus Moses (''Fagin'') ''Hoodlum Band'' case: (1) Exhaustive audit of both Anhang A (Jüdischdeutsches Wörterbuch, pp. 321-512) and Anhang B (Wörterbuch der Gaunersprache, pp. 513-626) confirms that Hudellump / Hoodlum is NOT a secret Hebrew-stem Kochemer-Loschen or Rotwelsch codeword (where ''fence'' = Schärfenspieler / Stoßenspieler / Kochemer and ''boy'' = Schekez / Hußeck); (2) Th. III p. 145 explicitly documents that thieves and fences routinely used rag-and-old-clothes dealing (''Haderlumpsammler'') as a cover trade (''Scheingeschäft''). In an English-speaking port where Moses fenced clothes stolen by Anglo/Irish/German boys aged 10-16, colloquial German Hudellump / Haderlump (''rag-dealer; tattered street scoundrel''; compound Haderlumpsammler required no secret cant knowledge among the boys to stick as a gang nickname.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-029.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
