ligan'') scores KLKN < 0.60). (Drive ID: 1PnMbHnPfhBrgtGtL036fY95e_g7wMJjJ).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-048.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1864 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-034', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hudels', 'rejected_lead', 'false_positive_rejection', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-02-21', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn85025148/1866-02-21/ed-1/?sp=1', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-034.webp', 156002, '1a6f519ddd1d58f87e51f4dcc20e840f4bd1e51bac336dfe796341f9cb493ccf', '{"id": "CERT-1000-034", "slug": "cert-1000-034", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hudels", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "false_positive_rejection", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "21 Feb 1866", "sort_date": "1866-02-21", "short_gloss": "False positives rejected: 1866 ''Hudels-'' (foul-case ''Handels-'') & 1883 surname ''Miss Hudelum''", "source_publication": "Baltimore Wecker (Baltimore, MD), Jahrg. 17, No. 45, February 21, 1866, p. 1, col. 4 (paired with The Cleveland Gazette, Vol. 1, No. 16, December 8, 1883, p. 1, col. 6)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn85025148/1866-02-21/ed-1/?sp=1", "page_and_column": "Baltimore Wecker p. 1, col. 4 (1866-02-21); Cleveland Gazette p. 1, col. 6 (1883-12-08, https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83035386/1883-12-08/ed-1/?sp=1)", "verbatim_quote": "[Baltimore Wecker, Feb. 21, 1866, p. 1:] Einladung zur Subscription für Ausgewählte werthvolle Stadt-Bauplätze, Farmen und Cottage Farm Lots in der aufblühenden deutschen Hudels-, Fabri- und Hafenstadt Egg Harbor City ... [Cleveland Gazette, Dec. 8, 1883, p. 1:] observed the following: That Miss Josie C. Hudelum, of Chicago, was the best lady dancer in the hall ... Captain Brown and Miss Hudelum took the cake when dancing the Herr Schmidt.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Adversarial Trap B & Trap C rejections: (1) 1866 Fraktur typesetter foul-case error ''Hudels-'' for ''Handels-''; (2) 1883 proper surname ''Hudelum'' unrelated to Swabian dialectal ''hudelum''.", "analysis_notes": "ADVERSARIAL TRAP B & TRAP C FALSIFICATIONS VERIFIED ON SCAN (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection eliminates the two most seductive false positives in the 1850-1890 U.S. newspaper corpus: (1) In Baltimore Wecker (Feb. 21, 1866, p. 1, col. 4), physical Fraktur lead type literally reads ''deutschen Hudels-, Fabri- und Hafenstadt Egg Harbor City''—a compositor''s foul-case typographical error substituting ''u'' for ''an'' in ''Handels-'' alongside dropped ''k'' in ''Fabrik-'' (and similarly Tennessee Staatszeitung July 23, 1867 p. 4 ''HUdelsSchul'' = OCR garble for ''Handels-Schulen'', plus Minnesota Staats-Zeitung May-Oct. 1865 ''Nachfolger von Hudel'' = St. Paul shoemaker surname); (2) Across the entire 1850-1890 all-language Chronicling America corpus, the sole hit for ''Hudelum'' (The Cleveland Gazette, Dec. 8, 1883, p. 1, col. 6) is a proper surname (''Miss Josie C. Hudelum, of Chicago''), ruling out any U.S. newspaper attestation of Swabian hudelum.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-034.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-017', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'soothing', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-11-09', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn86072040/1866-11-09/ed-1/?sp=3', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-017.webp', 170906, '4a296c45f74b74fc2bbe909a3c03e12f60a233a4f682c7da15d82b79d8cc93e7', '{"id": "CERT-1000-017", "slug": "cert-1000-017", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "soothing", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "9 Nov 1866", "sort_date": "1866-11-09", "short_gloss": "Pre-Dec. 1866 OCR ghost rejected: Nov. 9, 1866 LoC ''hoodlum'' reads ''soothing'' in syrup ad", "source_publication": "Walla Walla Statesman (Walla Walla, Washington Territory), Vol. V, No. 47", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn86072040)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn86072040/1866-11-09/ed-1/?sp=3", "page_and_column": "p. 3, col. 2 (Newell''s Pulmonary Syrup advertisement)", "verbatim_quote": "Newell''s Pulmonary Syrup ... It is composed of purely vegetable substances, harmless in its nature, and pleasant to the taste; soothing, healing and strengthening in its effects, and entirely free from everything of a debilitating or deleterious quality.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Mandatory Trap B audit of the #1 pre-December-1866 Chronicling America hit for ''hoodlum'' (Nov. 9, 1866, five weeks before the San Francisco Daily Evening Bulletin attestation). Native-resolution pct:100 scan inspection (:0217) proves smudged italic/roman type in ''soothing, healing'' was hallucinated by LoC OCR as ''hoodlum''. Preserves Dec. 14, 1866 as the true terminus a quo. [Bracketed Chronicling America ALTO OCR misread (''hoodlum'' for printed ink ''soothing'') moved out of verbatim_quote into analysis_notes.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-017.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-001', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'externally_recognized', 1866, '1866-12-14', 'https://www.frederickbee.com/hoodlum.pdf', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-001.webp', 265456, '68e9fe7310f9e489e187bf4fef994afefd5cc4df6dd2797a73221ac841b54dcc', '{"id": "CERT-1000-001", "slug": "cert-1000-001", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "externally_recognized", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": "Adopted as opening Sense 1 quotation in Oxford English Dictionary (3rd ed., revised Sept. 2026, s.v. hoodlum, n.), originally surfaced by Fred Shapiro and the Frederick Bee History Project (2015–2016).", "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "14 Dec 1866", "sort_date": "1866-12-14", "short_gloss": "OED3 opening witness: ''Hoodlum Band'', ''a \"Hoodlum\"'', ''the \"Hoodlums\"'' (SF Police Court)", "source_publication": "Daily Evening Bulletin (San Francisco, CA), Police Court column (''JUVENILE VAGABONDS'')", "source_archive": "San Francisco Public Library Microfilm via Frederick Bee History Project + Library of Congress Chronicling America & Internet Archive corroboration", "source_url": "https://www.frederickbee.com/hoodlum.pdf", "page_and_column": "Police Court column (''JUVENILE VAGABONDS'')", "verbatim_quote": "It is doubtful if any thieves ever did a more driving business than these young fellows, for all of their plunder has been stolen since last Saturday night. They call themselves the \"Hoodlum Band,\" and unless their thieving propensities are nipped in the bud, the reputation of Orliniski will fade before that of a \"Hoodlum.\" The worst feature of the case is that the boys were encouraged to steal by an old \"Fagan,\" who purchased whatever they brought him, and promised them that the police should never get any information from his place. This man will be prosecuted immediately. Among other exploits of the \"Hoodlums\" was the stealing of a lot of keys from the pockets of a clerk in an office on Montgomery street, to enter the office and unlock and rob the safe of $2 in silver.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Upper/Central German dialect compound Hudellump / Hodalump / Hoddel-Lump (''ragamuffin, rag-dealer, scoundrel'') in the Barbary Coast second-hand clothing fence milieu of Prussian-born tailor/merchant Lazarus Moses (822-824 Montgomery St. at Pacific St.)", "analysis_notes": "Earliest verified print attestation in English, exhibiting all three morphological stages inside one paragraph: self-styled gang name ''the \"Hoodlum Band\"'', singular generic noun ''a \"Hoodlum\"'', and plural ''the \"Hoodlums\"''. Trap A/D verification: modern typed annotation above column rule discounted; exact Dec. 14, 1866 date independently verified via (1) adjacent column item ''THE \"GOLDEN RULE\" WRECKING CASE'', (2) Dec. 13 arrest scan (https://www.frederickbee.com/boysarrest.pdf) naming William Sullivan, William Frattiger, Christopher J. Conley, John Hamilton, and John Hudson for robbing the Chicago, International, and American Exchange Hotels and Empire Lodging, (3) Dec. 14 arrest scan of 13-year-old Tommy Lloyd by Officer [Frederick O.] Fuller at 3rd & Jessie Sts. (https://www.frederickbee.com/lloyd.pdf), (4) SF Municipal Reports 1866-67 p. 298 Table I recording 5 boys committed to the Industrial School in December 1866, and (5) The Carson Daily Appeal full masthead scan of Dec. 21, 1866 (CERT-1000-005) citing the Bulletin''s ''Hoodlum Band'' report. [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-B vs. OED3 Sept. 2026 (which adopted this Dec. 14, 1866 SF Evening Bulletin citation surfaced by Fred Shapiro / Frederick Bee Project in 2015-2016); -5y antedating vs. TGT-053 1871 baseline (OED1/OED2/Etymonline). Microfilm column excerpt lacks top-of-page Bulletin masthead (modern typed date label discounted per Trap D); Dec. 1866 date triply corroborated by Carson Daily Appeal Dec. 21, 1866 full masthead (CERT-1000-005), SF Municipal Reports 1866-67 p. 298, and OED3 Sept. 2026.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-001.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-002', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum Band', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-12-15', 'https://www.frederickbee.com/faginarticle.png', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-002.webp', 202968, 'c75cdcf19a6da6a9ee236ef6532bfd9f4da02d24b467699d1521f96f0d544175', '{"id": "CERT-1000-002", "slug": "cert-1000-002", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum Band", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "15 Dec 1866", "sort_date": "1866-12-15", "short_gloss": "Arrest of clothes-fence Lazarus Moses (''alleged Fagan of the Hoodlum Band'')", "source_publication": "Daily Evening Bulletin (San Francisco, CA), Police items (''FAGAN ARRESTED'')", "source_archive": "San Francisco Public Library Microfilm via Frederick Bee History Project + Internet Archive Langley''s 1867 Directory corroboration", "source_url": "https://www.frederickbee.com/faginarticle.png", "page_and_column": "Local intelligence column (''FAGAN ARRESTED'')", "verbatim_quote": "FAGAN ARRESTED.—Lazarus Moses, the alleged Fagan of the Hoodlum Band of young thieves, was arrested last evening on a charge of receiving stolen goods, knowing them to have been stolen. The boys say that he encouraged them in their criminal course.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Prussian-born second-hand clothes dealer Lazarus Moses (822-824 Montgomery St. at Pacific St.) identified by name as the ''Fagan'' fencing stolen garments from the juvenile ''Hoodlum Band''.", "analysis_notes": "Physical microfilm image visually inspected. Directly links the juvenile ''Hoodlum Band'' to Prussian-born second-hand clothing merchant Lazarus Moses (born c. 1826-27 in Prussia; registered July 23, 1866 at 822 Montgomery St. in the 1866 SF Great Register, Line 5142; listed at 824 Montgomery St. in Langley''s 1864-1868 SF Directories). Date corroborated by Dec. 14 ''will be prosecuted immediately'' notice, Dec. 15 Daily Alta California parallel report, Dec. 20 conviction scan, and SF Chief of Police FY1866-67 report (p. 166: only 3 arrests citywide for ''Receiving Stolen Goods''). [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-B OED3 Prosopographical Corroboration: Verbatim Dec. 15, 1866 SF Evening Bulletin arrest report naming Lazarus Moses as ''alleged Fagan of the Hoodlum Band'' (summarized without verbatim quotation in OED3 Sept. 2026 Etymology Note; -5y vs. TGT-053 1871 baseline).]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-002.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-003', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum Gang', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'discovered', 1866, '1866-12-15', 'https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hoodlum', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1000-003", "slug": "cert-1000-003", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum Gang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "discovered", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "15 Dec 1866", "sort_date": "1866-12-15", "short_gloss": "Corroborating SF notice: ''the \"Hoodlum Gang\" of juvenile thieves'' (Daily Alta California)", "source_publication": "Daily Alta California (San Francisco, CA), Vol. 18, No. 7021 (''Police Court'')", "source_archive": "California Digital Newspaper Collection (CDNC) via Wiktionary / OED3 prior-art quotation", "source_url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hoodlum", "page_and_column": "Vol. 18, No. 7021, p. 1 (local intelligence)", "verbatim_quote": "a dealer in second-hand clothing was arrested, yesterday, on the charges of receiving stolen goods from the \"Hoodlum Gang\" of juvenile thieves.", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Corroborates second-hand clothing trade context (''a dealer in second-hand clothing'') and variation between ''Hoodlum Band'' (Bulletin) and ''Hoodlum Gang'' (Alta) on the same day.", "analysis_notes": "Logged strictly as BASELINE-LITERATURE with scan_inspected=False per 01_AGENTS.md because CDNC (cdnc.ucr.edu) returned a Cloudflare HTTP 403 JS challenge from the headless VM environment, preventing direct pixel inspection of the Alta scan. Independently corroborated on physical ink by the same-day Dec. 15, 1866 San Francisco Daily Evening Bulletin scan (CERT-1000-002). [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-B Uninspected Baseline Citation: Dec. 15, 1866 Daily Alta California (''Hoodlum Gang'' & ''dealer in second-hand clothing''); kept at BASELINE-LITERATURE (scan_inspected=False) due to CDNC Cloudflare HTTP 403.] [Secondary-literature baseline witness (Wiktionary citing Daily Alta California, Vol. 18, No. 7021, Dec. 15, 1866; direct CDNC page scan blocked by Cloudflare HTTP 403). Bracketed identification of the arrested second-hand clothing dealer as Lazarus Moses moved from quotation field to analysis_notes.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): scan not bundled locally; consult external source URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-004', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum Band', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-12-20', 'https://www.frederickbee.com/faginverdict.pdf', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-004.webp', 106180, 'dc79addcbd6c13007a2d84cb9407c47d120bf495e482c532b75aa06d5eea7b6c', '{"id": "CERT-1000-004", "slug": "cert-1000-004", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum Band", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "20 Dec 1866", "sort_date": "1866-12-20", "short_gloss": "Five-minute Police Court jury conviction of Lazarus Moses (''Fagin of the Hoodlum Band'')", "source_publication": "Daily Evening Bulletin (San Francisco, CA), Police Court column (''FAGIN CONVICTED'')", "source_archive": "San Francisco Public Library Microfilm via Frederick Bee History Project + Internet Archive SF Municipal Reports 1866-67 corroboration", "source_url": "https://www.frederickbee.com/faginverdict.pdf", "page_and_column": "Local court intelligence (''FAGIN CONVICTED'')", "verbatim_quote": "FAGIN CONVICTED.—Lazarus Moses, the Fagin of the Hoodlum Band, was convicted in the Police Court, yesterday afternoon, the jury returning a verdict of guilty after an absence of only five minutes. The boys testified against him, and their evidence was corroborated by that of the Police, who found stolen property in his possession. The defendant testified in his own behalf that he did not know the goods were stolen when he purchased them.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Confirms repeated face-to-face commercial transactions and courtroom confrontation between Prussian-born clothes dealer Lazarus Moses and the juvenile ''Hoodlum Band''.", "analysis_notes": "Physical microfilm column scan visually inspected, including adjacent same-column legal item (''INSOLVENT.—Frederick Roskamp filed his petition of insolvency to-day.'', dated Dec. 20, 1866 in SF insolvency dockets). Proves Police found the boys'' stolen hotel clothing directly in Lazarus Moses''s Montgomery/Pacific St. shop and that the boys testified in person against him. [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-B OED3 Prosopographical Corroboration: Verbatim Dec. 20, 1866 SF Evening Bulletin Police Court jury conviction of Lazarus Moses after five-minute deliberation (-5y vs. TGT-053 1871 baseline).]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-004.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-005', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum Band', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-12-21', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84022040/1866-12-21/ed-1/?sp=2', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-005.webp', 174574, '81dfdcc04adbbc31f001b0f56a5c39182b27635094ce92d14688e8271dcbf4eb', '{"id": "CERT-1000-005", "slug": "cert-1000-005", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum Band", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "21 Dec 1866", "sort_date": "1866-12-21", "short_gloss": "Earliest out-of-state witness & full LoC masthead: ''the \"Hoodlum Band\"'' (Carson City, NV)", "source_publication": "The Carson Daily Appeal (Carson City, NV), Vol. IV, No. 30", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (NDNP batch_nvln_jackrabbit_ver01, sn84022040)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84022040/1866-12-21/ed-1/?sp=2", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 2 (''BIG THIEVES PESTERED BY LITTLE ONES'')", "verbatim_quote": "BIG THIEVES PESTERED BY LITTLE ONES.—The San Francisco police has lately discovered the existence of a gang of boy-thieves in that city who style themselves the \"Hoodlum Band\" who have been doing a wholesale business in the pilfering way. This fact having leaked out, the big thieves who \"destroy widows houses\" and grind the faces of the poor, have set up a terrible wail, and the boys are to be prosecuted in accordance with their devilish proficiencies. That virtuous dragon, the Bulletin, has lost no time in consigning the youthful scamps to the fathomless depths of its awful damnation.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Major Tier-A archival find. Both Page 1 masthead (''CARSON DAILY APPEAL. VOL. IV. CARSON CITY, NEVADA, FRIDAY MORNING, DECEMBER 21, 1866. NO. 30.'') and Page 2 running header (''Carson Daily Appeal. HENRY R. MIGHELS, Editor. FRIDAY, DEC. 21, 1866.'') + native-resolution pct:100 physical-ink column image visually inspected on LoC IIIF (:0468 / :0469). Simultaneously (1) antedates the 1871 target-board baseline by 5 years, (2) proves immediate Comstock/Nevada diffusion within 7 days of the Dec. 14 Bulletin report, and (3) provides unimpeachable external masthead proof of the Dec. 1866 San Francisco Bulletin ''Hoodlum Band'' articles. [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-A NOVEL Post-OED3 Discovery: Dec. 21, 1866 Carson Daily Appeal (Vol. IV, No. 30, p. 2, col. 2) — uncited in OED3 (Sept. 2026), Green''s, HDAS, Tamony, or Frederick Bee Project; earliest known attestation of ''Hoodlum Band'' outside California (7 days after SF Bulletin) and earliest citation verified from a complete Library of Congress full-sheet masthead.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-005.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-006', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum band', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-12-27', 'https://www.frederickbee.com/faginfine.pdf', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-006.webp', 107706, '689a4aec1d612ca866bd1304b22d97bf965cde129924457c1138c910a3643779', '{"id": "CERT-1000-006", "slug": "cert-1000-006", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum band", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "27 Dec 1866", "sort_date": "1866-12-27", "short_gloss": "Judge Alfred Rix fines Lazarus Moses $300 for fencing goods of the ''Hoodlum band''", "source_publication": "Daily Evening Bulletin (San Francisco, CA), Police Court column (''FINED'')", "source_archive": "San Francisco Public Library Microfilm via Frederick Bee History Project + Langley''s 1867 Directory & 1866 Great Register corroboration", "source_url": "https://www.frederickbee.com/faginfine.pdf", "page_and_column": "Local court intelligence (''FINED'')", "verbatim_quote": "FINED.—Lazarus Moses, the Fagan of the Hoodlum band, was fined $300 by Judge Rix yesterday, with the alternative of four months'' imprisonment in the County Jail, for receiving stolen goods. He paid his fine.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Demonstrates Lazarus Moses''s immediate cash liquidity ($300 gold/silver fine paid on the spot to avoid 4 months in County Jail) from his Barbary Coast clothing trade.", "analysis_notes": "Physical microfilm scan visually inspected. Identifies the sentencing magistrate as Police Judge Alfred Rix (verified in 1866 SF Great Register as age 44, born New Hampshire, Police Judge, residing at 737 Pine St.; Langley''s 1867 Directory p. 609 confirms Alfred Rix sat as SF Police Judge in 1866 before Judge W. D. Sawyer assumed the bench in 1867). Note lowercase ''band'' in ''Hoodlum band'', showing incipient lexicalization. [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-B OED3 Prosopographical Corroboration: Verbatim Dec. 27, 1866 SF Evening Bulletin sentencing of Lazarus Moses ($300 fine paid before Police Judge Alfred Rix; -5y vs. TGT-053 1871 baseline).]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-006.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-032', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Haderlump', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1867, '1867-11-10', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045582/1867-11-10/ed-1/?sp=3', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-032.webp', 187018, '959c7ab84b7d60249eb136ca847bc6044bbb82f4c0745bbfb3580eb455b64c25', '{"id": "CERT-1000-032", "slug": "cert-1000-032", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Haderlump", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1867, "date_display": "10 Nov 1867", "sort_date": "1867-11-10", "short_gloss": "U.S. Fraktur press donor control: ''der Haderlump'' (''rag-buyer'', Cincinnati, OH, 1867)", "source_publication": "Westliche Blätter: Sonntagsblatt des Cincinnati Volksblattes (Cincinnati, OH), Jahrg. 3, No. 109, November 10, 1867, p. 3, col. 4", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045582/1867-11-10/ed-1/?sp=3", "page_and_column": "p. 3, col. 4 (with p. 1 masthead verified)", "verbatim_quote": "Der Regen kündigt sich durch die mannigfaltigsten Anzeichen an, unter Anderm auch dadurch, daß „Wagenschmierhändler in ein Dorf kommen“ (anderwärts regnet es auch, wenn „der Haderlump“ schreit, d. h. der Mann, welcher Hadern einkauft).", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1867, "linguistic_note": "U.S. German-language Fraktur press attestation of Haderlump (''street rag-buyer''; also attested in the same paper Oct. 5, 1873 p. 9 as pejorative ''Du bist ein Haderlump! Ein leichtsinniger Haderlump bist'' and in Katholische Volkszeitung July 4, 1874 p. 1 ''Ein oberbayerischer Haderlump'').", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 U.S. FRAKTUR NEWSPAPER DONOR ATTESTATION VERIFIED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Westliche Blätter (Sunday literary edition of Tägliches Cincinnatier Volksblatt, sn83045582) confirms printed masthead ''Jahrgang 3. Sonntag, den 10. November 1867. No. 109'' (p. 1), running header ''Sonntagsblatt des Cincinnati Volksblattes. 3'', and physical Fraktur ink on p. 3, col. 4: ''wenn „der Haderlump“ schreit, d. h. der Mann, welcher Hadern einkauft''. Together with subsequent Cincinnati and Baltimore Fraktur occurrences in the scoundrel sense (Westliche Blätter Nov. 17, 1872 p. 1; Oct. 5, 1873 p. 9; Katholische Volkszeitung July 4, 1874 p. 1; Der Deutsche Correspondent June 16, 1877 p. 5), this proves living German-American currency of Haderlump (KTLNP vs KTLN, candidate_viable=True).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-032.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1867 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-025', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hudel', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1868, '1868-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/idiotikonvonkurh00vilmuoft/page/n191/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-025.webp', 172204, 'd76ea2955621cfc04a258b4ee8052c4518963bd744dc7bd1fbb73a84876d04db', '{"id": "CERT-1000-025", "slug": "cert-1000-025", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hudel", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1868, "date_display": "1868", "sort_date": "1868-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hessian ''Hudel ~ Hoddel ~ Haddel'' & Upper/East-Hessian vs. Low-German isogloss (Vilmar 1868)", "source_publication": "August Friedrich Christian Vilmar, Idiotikon von Kurhessen (Marburg & Leipzig: N. G. Elwert''sche Universitäts-Buchhandlung, 1868), p. 177", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/idiotikonvonkurh00vilmuoft/page/n191/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "p. 177 (JP2 leaf 191); printed title page leaf 7 (1868)", "verbatim_quote": "Hudel msc., Stück grober Leinwand, Lappe, Lumpen. Das Wort ist im fuldaischen, im hersfeldischen, in der Obergrafschaft Hanau und in Oberhessen üblich, in Niederhessen nicht einmal bekannt und verstanden, geschweige denn gebräuchlich. ... In Oberhessen ist am gebräuchlichsten die Composition Handerhodel (Handerhaddel), Handtuch. In Schmalkalden ist Hudel nur zerrissenes Kleidungsstück, Lumpen. Eben so ist im hersfeldischen Hoddel der mit Lumpen umwickelte Backofenfeger ... Durch die Formen Haddel und Hoddel schließt sich dieses Wort an das gemeinhochdeutsch gewordene Hader, Lumpen, an. Das Wort ist entschieden oberdeutsch ... Hudler msc., bekanntes älteres, noch jetzt in Oberhessen in der Form Huller übliches Schimpfwort = Lump.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1868, "linguistic_note": "Upper Hessian / Rhine-Main Central German Hudel ~ Hoddel ~ Haddel (''tattered garment, rag'') and Hudler (''abusive epithet = Lump''), defining the exact northern Upper/Central German boundary.", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 DIALECT ISOGLOSS & VOCALISM VERIFIED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Vilmar''s Idiotikon von Kurhessen (idiotikonvonkurh00vilmuoft, printed title page leaf 7 = ''Marburg und Leipzig. N. G. Elwert''sche Universitäts-Buchhandlung. 1868'') verifies p. 177 (leaf 191). Establishes two critical philological facts: (1) Hudel / Hoddel / Haddel is strictly an Upper German + Upper/East-Hessian (Fulda, Hersfeld, Hanau, Schmalkalden) word, completely unknown in Low German / Lower Hesse (''in Niederhessen nicht einmal bekannt''); and (2) both geminate -dd- (Hoddel, Haddel) and personal insult Hudler = Lump (citing a 1604 Rauschenberg court fine) were active in southern Hesse / Rhine-Main, a major source region of 1850s-1860s German-Jewish and German Forty-Niner migration to California.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-025.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1868 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-027', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hudel', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1870, '1870-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/deutschessprich00sprigoog/page/n421/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-027.webp', 105612, '820994fe094f68cd7bb707a8694b09da958170a64fff79214efd98e2b57a0dc6', '{"id": "CERT-1000-027", "slug": "cert-1000-027", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hudel", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1870, "date_display": "1870", "sort_date": "1870-01-01", "short_gloss": "German proverbs: ''Hudel findet Lumpen'', ''Hudelmann'', & ''Haderlumpen'' (Wander 1870)", "source_publication": "Karl Friedrich Wilhelm Wander, Deutsches Sprichwörter-Lexikon: Ein Hausschatz für das deutsche Volk, Zweiter Band: Gott bis Lehren (Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1870), cols. 251, 796", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/deutschessprich00sprigoog/page/n421/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "col. 796 (JP2 leaf 421, right column) & col. 251 (leaf 148, left column); printed title page leaf 10 (1870)", "verbatim_quote": "[col. 796:] Hudel. 1 Hudel findet Hudel. (S. Lump.) (Nürtingen.) 2 Hudel findet Lumpen. (Schwäb.) — Körte, 2187. Ein liederlicher Mann gesellt sich auch gern ein liederliches Weib zu. ... 1) Hudel, in Luzern Kindswindel, wofür in Aarau Huttel, dann Lappen, besonders alter Lumpen von Leinwand, uneigentlich lumpiger Mensch. Hudelvolk = Lumpenvolk ... Hudelmann. 1 Das ist hudelmans gesind, werckt langsamb, aber trinckt geschwind. — Gruter, III, 13. ... „und des unnützen Huddelmansgesindlin und gross Geschlepp kommen.“ (Luther''s Werke, VIII, Jsl. 273.) [col. 251:] Haderlumpen. 1 Offt werden alte haderlumpen auffs new zu marck gebracht. — Lehmann, 415, 19.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1870, "linguistic_note": "Pre-1871 German paremiological pairing Hudel + Lumpen (''dissolute ragamuffins''), Luther''s geminate Huddel- (''Huddelmansgesindlin''), and Haderlumpen.", "analysis_notes": "PRIMARY PRE-1871 PAREMIOLOGICAL SCAN VERIFIED + TRAP A/D REPRINT EXPOSURE (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of the original Google-digitized Brockhaus edition (deutschessprich00sprigoog, leaf 10) confirms the true printed title-page date ''Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus. 1870'' (1870 <= 1871 baseline) and physical ink on col. 796 (leaf 421) & col. 251 (leaf 148). Simultaneously exposes a dual Trap A/D hazard on IA''s rival upload deutsches-sprichworter-lexikon-band-2-gott-bis-lehren, which is a 1987 Weltbild Verlag Augsburg photomechanical reprint carrying a false modern copyright-page note (''Nachdruck der Ausgabe Leipzig 1867'' — confusing Bd. 1 [1867] with Bd. 2 [1870]).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-027.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1870 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-018', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Goodhue', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1870, '1870-07-21', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn78000873/1870-07-21/ed-1/?sp=2', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-018.webp', 209838, '3ba70bd21bf2c13fac046528d03164c9f5cdd0934169240815b75db8d8afea12', '{"id": "CERT-1000-018", "slug": "cert-1000-018", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Goodhue", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1870, "date_display": "21 Jul 1870", "sort_date": "1870-07-21", "short_gloss": "Pre-1871 East Coast OCR ghost rejected: 1870 LoC ''Master Hoodlum'' reads ''Master Goodhue''", "source_publication": "The Republican Journal (Belfast, ME), Vol. 41, No. 2", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn78000873)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn78000873/1870-07-21/ed-1/?sp=2", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 5", "verbatim_quote": "The boys of the primary school were marshaled in fine order under the eye of Master Goodhue.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1870, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Mandatory Trap B/C audit of the sole pre-1871 East Coast Chronicling America hit for ''Hoodlum'' (July 21, 1870, Belfast, Maine). Native-resolution pct:100 scan inspection (:0324) proves the physical ink prints the New England surname ''Master Goodhue'', misread by LoC OCR as ''Master Hoodlum''. Confirms zero pre-1871 East Coast diffusion. [Bracketed Chronicling America ALTO OCR misread (''Master Hoodlum'' for printed ink ''Master Goodhue'') moved out of verbatim_quote into analysis_notes.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-018.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1870 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-007', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlums', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1871, '1871-04-15', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84022046/1871-04-15/ed-1/?sp=2', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-007.webp', 230406, '5a8e43aa6ee1eb1a018b95c8d216807953e33b464de56708d3715b6d8c68dabc', '{"id": "CERT-1000-007", "slug": "cert-1000-007", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlums", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1871, "date_display": "15 Apr 1871", "sort_date": "1871-04-15", "short_gloss": "Generic plural ''the Hoodlums'' at SF Swiss Guard Sunday picnic (bridges OED3 1866–1877 gap)", "source_publication": "Gold Hill Daily News (Gold Hill, NV), Vol. XVI, No. 2321", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn84022046)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84022046/1871-04-15/ed-1/?sp=2", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 3", "verbatim_quote": "Had there been a Bill Tell amongst the Swiss Guard on Sunday, this would have been done; but unfortunately, that patriot''s spirit did not animate any of his countrymen, and in consequence the Hoodlums had it pretty nearly all their own way. For the next few months the heading, \"Disgraceful Row at a Picnic,\" will be a regular feature of Monday''s newspaper; and as Police Judge Sawyer has Congressional aspirations, and now makes a friend of every culprit brought before him by inflicting only nominal penalties, it is useless to expect that he will do anything to abate them.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1871, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Full Page 1 masthead (''GOLD HILL DAILY NEWS. VOL. XVI. GOLD HILL, NEVADA: SATURDAY EVENING, APRIL 15, 1871. NO. 2321.'') and Page 2 col. 3 ink scan visually verified (:0359/:0360). Documents the completed semantic broadening from the specific 1866-1868 juvenile burglary ring (''Hoodlum Band'') to the generic plural noun ''the Hoodlums'' (without quotation marks) disrupting a San Francisco Swiss Guard Sunday picnic, naming sitting SF Police Judge W. D. Sawyer. [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-A NOVEL OED3 Sense-1 Quotation Gap-Filler: April 15, 1871 Gold Hill Daily News (''the Hoodlums'' disrupting SF Swiss Guard picnic) — fills OED3 Sept. 2026''s 11-year Sense-1 quotation gap (1866 Dec. 14 -> 1877 Aug.) and predates OED2/DA Sept. 6, 1871 baseline by nearly 5 months.] [Diplomatic quote audited directly against p. 2, col. 3 scan (preceded on the column top by ''...of every excursion during the Summer. The only wonder to me is that the peaceable majority do not make an example of one or two of the ruffians, and pitch them overboard.''). Editorial summary describing the row at the San Francisco Swiss Guard Sunday picnic is kept strictly in analysis_notes rather than quoted text.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-007.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1871 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-008', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlums', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1871, '1871-04-17', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84022046/1871-04-17/ed-1/?sp=2', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-008.webp', 255880, 'f3c21adfbbf23190191ba1bffff4b079545cbbffa0a4c087b769915c31950c8b', '{"id": "CERT-1000-008", "slug": "cert-1000-008", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlums", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1871, "date_display": "17 Apr 1871", "sort_date": "1871-04-17", "short_gloss": "Western Union SF wire dispatch: ''a gang of Hoodlums'' on Shields Guard Petaluma steamer", "source_publication": "Gold Hill Daily News (Gold Hill, NV), Vol. XVI, No. 2322", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn84022046)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84022046/1871-04-17/ed-1/?sp=2", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 2 (''BY TELEGRAPH — FROM SAN FRANCISCO'')", "verbatim_quote": "SAN FRANCISCO, April 17—1 P. M.—At the Shields Guard excursion to Petaluma yesterday, a gang of Hoodlums, who had accompanied the excursionists, commenced a row by smashing crockery at the restaurants, etc. The Guard interfered, and Mike Thompson, one of the roughs, stabbed John P. Yates, of the Guard, twice in the breast, inflicting slight wounds. He was arrested and held in $1,000 bail.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1871, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Full Page 1 masthead (''MONDAY EVENING, APRIL 17, 1871. NO. 2322.'') and Page 2 col. 2 telegraph dispatch ink scan visually verified (:0363/:0364). Shows ''a gang of Hoodlums'' transmitted unmarked over Western Union press wire from San Francisco to Nevada. [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-A NOVEL OED3 Sense-1 Quotation Gap-Filler: April 17, 1871 Gold Hill Daily News SF telegraph dispatch (''a gang of Hoodlums'' on Shields Guard Petaluma steamer excursion), proving lowercase/generic gang-class pluralization in spring 1871.] [Diplomatic quote audited directly against p. 2, col. 2 scan beneath the centered column headers ''LATEST TELEGRAMS / [SPECIAL TO THE GOLD HILL DAILY NEWS BY THE WESTERN UNION TELEGRAPH LINK.] / FROM SAN FRANCISCO'' (synthetic ''FROM SAN FRANCISCO.'' prefix removed from verbatim_quote; exact printed text confirms Mike Thompson stabbing Guardsman John P. Yates rather than any drowning summary).]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-008.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1871 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-009', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1871, '1871-05-01', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84022046/1871-05-01/ed-1/?sp=2', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-009.webp', 232914, '424b0c73b5ebd3463089010d1d0e56f1bea7fdbeeee787d4dd48fa70a69389a1', '{"id": "CERT-1000-009", "slug": "cert-1000-009", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1871, "date_display": "1 May 1871", "sort_date": "1871-05-01", "short_gloss": "Earliest attributive compound: ''\"Hoodlum neck-tie\"'' (''hemp noose'', 11 yrs before OED3 1882)", "source_publication": "Gold Hill Daily News (Gold Hill, NV), Vol. XVI, No. 2334", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn84022046)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84022046/1871-05-01/ed-1/?sp=2", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 2 (''A NEAT THING IN NECK-TIES'')", "verbatim_quote": "A NEAT THING IN NECK-TIES.—Neck-tie festivals being now all the rage, the Butler (Kansas) vigilantes, not to be behind the fashion, held one a few days since. The neck-tie used was made of hemp, and the young man who wore it will need no more neck-ties. This should be the \"Hoodlum neck-tie.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1871, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Full Page 1 masthead (''MONDAY EVENING, MAY 1, 1871. NO. 2334.'') and Page 2 col. 2 ink scan visually verified (:0411/:0412). Attests productive attributive compounding (''Hoodlum neck-tie'' = vigilante hangman''s noose) by May 1, 1871. [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-A NOVEL OED3 Attributive Compound Discovery: May 1, 1871 Gold Hill Daily News (''Hoodlum neck-tie'' = vigilante hemp hangman''s noose) — unrecorded in OED3 Sept. 2026, predating OED3''s earliest listed compound (hoodlum wagon, n., 1882) by 11 years and predating OED3 hoodlumism (late 1871), hoodlumish (1872), and hoodluming (1874).]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-009.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1871 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-010', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlums', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1871, '1871-05-18', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84020345/1871-05-18/ed-1/?sp=2', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-010.webp', 139922, 'af2dc805b1d5c32392a9f05069d1429a3c435d9df3b3c685e794f82c15069653', '{"id": "CERT-1000-010", "slug": "cert-1000-010", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlums", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1871, "date_display": "18 May 1871", "sort_date": "1871-05-18", "short_gloss": "Generic plural ''Some of the Hoodlums of San Francisco'' in municipal chain-gang", "source_publication": "The Daily State Register (Carson City, NV), Vol. I, No. 121", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn84020345)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84020345/1871-05-18/ed-1/?sp=2", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 1 (''GOOD!'')", "verbatim_quote": "GOOD!—Some of the Hoodlums of San Francisco have quit annoying decent people, and gone quietly to work in the chain-gang.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1871, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Full Page 1 masthead (''The Daily State Register. VOL. I. CARSON CITY, NEVADA, THURSDAY MORNING, MAY 18, 1871. NO. 121.'') and Page 2 col. 1 ink scan visually verified (:0250/:0251). Attests ''the Hoodlums of San Francisco'' as an established regional designation. [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-A NOVEL OED3 Sense-1 Quotation Gap-Filler: May 18, 1871 Carson City Daily State Register (''Some of the Hoodlums of San Francisco ... in the chain-gang''), documenting spring 1871 municipal chain-gang enforcement.] [Diplomatic quote audited directly against p. 2, col. 1 scan: isolated strictly to the standalone paragraph between horizontal column rules (trimming the unrelated preceding Sacramento frost brieflet and following Ely Record Pioche building brieflet into contextual notes).]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-010.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1871 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-011', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlums', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1871, '1871-07-01', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn85025134/1871-07-01/ed-1/?sp=1', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-011.webp', 237234, '22c658d626fb18f2fc9d2c9c3dbe9a85b100702e8042ff6d53bd9dd0bb32f84b', '{"id": "CERT-1000-011", "slug": "cert-1000-011", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlums", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1871, "date_display": "1 Jul 1871", "sort_date": "1871-07-01", "short_gloss": "SF Chief Patrick Crowley bars ''\"Hoodlums\"'' from Martinez bay excursion steamer", "source_publication": "The Union Democrat (Sonora, Tuolumne County, CA), Vol. XVIII, No. 1", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn85025134)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn85025134/1871-07-01/ed-1/?sp=1", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 5", "verbatim_quote": "It was the most respectable crowd of picnic-ers that ever left San Francisco. Four policemen had been detailed, by Chief Crowley, to accompany the excursionists and preserve order. But their services were never once required. No \"Hoodlums\" were allowed on board and not a single drunk was reported during the whole trip. Everbody went up to Martinez that day to enjoy themselves and did do so.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1871, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Single-sheet Page 1 masthead (''THE UNION DEMOCRAT. VOLUME XVIII. SONORA, TUOLUMNE COUNTY, JULY 1, 1871. NUMBER 1.'') and col. 5 ink scan visually verified (:0510). Names San Francisco Police Chief Patrick Crowley (who served 1866-1873) detailing officers to bar ''Hoodlums'' from a San Francisco-to-Martinez steamboat excursion. [Chief Orchestrator Calibration: Tier-A NOVEL OED3 Sense-1 Quotation Gap-Filler: July 1, 1871 Sonora Union Democrat (''No \"Hoodlums\" were allowed on board'', policed by SF Chief Patrick Crowley), 2 months prior to Sept. 6, 1871 DA/OED2 baseline.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-011.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1871 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-041', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'externally_recognized', 1871, '1871-08-01', 'https://archive.org/details/overlandmonthly7176sanfrich', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-041.webp', 112102, '61e2c48c4aad3b8028c82436a7ceb33d4b2f0dba1a62a72b6e942e04bb655d68', '{"id": "CERT-1000-041", "slug": "cert-1000-041", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "externally_recognized", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": "Recorded in Jonathon Green, Green''s Dictionary of Slang, s.v. hoodlum, n.", "publication_year": 1871, "date_display": "Aug 1871", "sort_date": "1871-08-01", "short_gloss": "Aug. 1871 Overland Monthly witness (''the young \"Hoodlum\"'', cited in Green''s)", "source_publication": "The Overland Monthly (San Francisco), Vol. 7, No. 2 (August 1871), ''The Darwinian Eden'', p. 163", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/overlandmonthly7176sanfrich", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "If the young \"Hoodlum\" merely takes up his stand at the corner of the streets and there displays all the glories of his Turkish pantaloons and stub-toed boots, we hurry him off to prison.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1871, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verified (Vol. 7, No. 2, Aug. 1871 masthead + p. 163 col. 2); predates DAE/DA/OED2 Sept. 6, 1871 Cincinnati Commercial baseline quote by one month and is already recorded in Green''s Dictionary of Slang. (Drive ID: 13EKw5-hk9ceQM3wq2KlkSJxdpy6mefku).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-041.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1871 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-019', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'HOODLUMS', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1873, '1873-08-11', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030214/1873-08-11/ed-1/?sp=1', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-019.webp', 227628, '92c599c8dbf26390f0aef08468673bb88c34205e35c23e96689918ff7103af2b', '{"id": "CERT-1000-019", "slug": "cert-1000-019", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "HOODLUMS", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1873, "date_display": "11 Aug 1873", "sort_date": "1873-08-11", "short_gloss": "1873 ''NOODLUMS'' OCR ghost rejected: NY Tribune front-page ink reads ''THE HOODLUMS''", "source_publication": "New-York Tribune (New York, NY), Vol. XXXIII, No. 10,095", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn83030214)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83030214/1873-08-11/ed-1/?sp=1", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 1 (front-page San Francisco dispatch deck)", "verbatim_quote": "THE ANTI-CHINESE MOVEMENT. GROWING OPPOSITION TO COOLIE IMMIGRATION ON THE PACIFIC COAST—THE HOODLUMS CHIEFLY CONCERNED ABOUT THE CHINESE.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1873, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Crucial Trap B test of the Muldoon/Noodlum hypothesis: a Chronicling America search for ''noodlums'' returns this Aug. 11, 1873 New-York Tribune front page, which if real would predate the 1877 Muldoon myth by 4 years! Visual inspection of the native-resolution pct:100 scan (:0769) proves the all-caps deck clearly prints ''THE HOODLUMS CHIEFLY CONCERNED ABOUT THE CHINESE'' (with broken crossbars misread by OCR as ''TRE NOODLUMS''). Zero genuine pre-1877 attestations of ''noodlum(s)'' exist. [Bracketed Chronicling America ALTO OCR misread (''TRE NOODLUMS'' for printed ink ''THE HOODLUMS'') moved out of verbatim_quote into analysis_notes.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-019.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1873 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-047', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Honour bright / Hook', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_lexicon_control', 'agent_verified', 1874, '1874-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924086360488', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-047.webp', 102532, 'e504526ef77d12df1df6d6a460dfb302f760dbd48b56e4f0221289635dd7a287', '{"id": "CERT-1000-047", "slug": "cert-1000-047", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Honour bright / Hook", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_lexicon_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1874, "date_display": "1874", "sort_date": "1874-01-01", "short_gloss": "Negative British cant control: ''hoodlum'' absent across Hotten''s Slang Dictionary (1859–1874)", "source_publication": "John Camden Hotten, The Slang Dictionary (London, 1864 ed., p. 155; rev. Chatto & Windus ed., 1874, p. 194)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924086360488", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Honour bright, an asseveration which means literally, \"by my honour, which is bright and unsullied.\" ... Hook, an expression at Oxford, implying doubt.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1874, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Comparative scan audit across Hotten''s 1859, 1860, 1864 (p. 155: HONDEY -> HOOK), 1869, and 1874 (p. 194: Honour bright -> Hook) editions proves total absence of ''hoodlum'' in British underworld cant and London street slang both before and immediately after 1871. (Drive ID: 1n8rxBeWnVeXFXJxpxWGBQYX3JZXjf02E).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-047.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1874 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-033', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlums', 'contextual_evidence', 'prior_art_or_reception_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1874, '1874-06-01', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84026744/1874-06-01/ed-1/?sp=4', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-033.webp', 109732, '8f661bc3f44e78b2e0420e599b8075a5362ed7bcf4a6e067356a3332bf400393', '{"id": "CERT-1000-033", "slug": "cert-1000-033", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlums", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "prior_art_or_reception_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1874, "date_display": "1 Jun 1874", "sort_date": "1874-06-01", "short_gloss": "SF Abend-Post (1874) sets ''Hoodlums'' in Antiqua inside Fraktur as an opaque English loanword", "source_publication": "Washingtoner Journal (Washington, D.C.), Jahrg. 2, Nr. 139, June 1, 1874, p. 4, col. 2 (reprinting San Francisco Abend-Post; paired with Minnesota Staats-Zeitung Oct. 12, 1871 p. 7, Täglicher Baltimore Wecker Feb. 21, 1872 p. 2, & Der Nordstern Sept. 20, 1877 p. 6 quoting California Demokrat)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn84026744/1874-06-01/ed-1/?sp=4", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 2 (with p. 1 masthead verified)", "verbatim_quote": "Die San Francisco „Abend Post“ schreibt: An allen Orten, wo Picnics abgehalten werden, beklagen sich den Picnic-Plätzen nahewohnende Bewohner über die frechen, frivolen und unerträglichen Aufführungen der Hoodlums. Nicht nur dringen dieselben mit ihrer weiblichen Begleitung ohne Weiteres in die Blumengärten und verwüsten dieselben ... sondern sie führen auch in unverschämtester Weise solche unsittliche Handlungen auf, die man anstandshalber nicht näher bezeichnen darf.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1874, "linguistic_note": "1871-1878 German-American press borrowing of Californian English ''Hoodlum(s)'' as an unanalyzed English loanword (58 LoC German press hits), demonstrating early phonological opacity between English ''Hoodlum'' and Upper German ''Hudellump / Haderlump''.", "analysis_notes": "1871-1878 GERMAN-AMERICAN PRESS RECEPTION AUDIT VERIFIED (1000-B Audit): Visual inspection of Washingtoner Journal (sn84026744, June 1, 1874, p. 1 masthead ''Montag, 1. Juni 1874'' & p. 4 col. 2 verbatim excerpt from San Francisco Abend-Post) alongside all 58 LoC German-language newspaper hits from 1871-1878—including Minnesota Staats-Zeitung (Oct. 12, 1871, p. 7: ''Taschendieben und Hoodlums''), Täglicher Baltimore Wecker (Feb. 21, 1872, p. 2: ''Die Thätigkeit unserer „Hoodlums“''), Westliche Blätter (Oct. 20, 1872, p. 7: ''aus dem der edle Hoodlum sich entwickelt''; Aug. 23, 1874, p. 1: ''Die kalifornischen Straßenjungen, dort „Hoodlums“ genannt''), Illinois Staats-Zeitung (July 29, 1878, p. 2: ''californischen Taugenichtse oder Hoodlums''), and Der Nordstern (Sept. 20, 1877, p. 6, quoting California Demokrat on ''den Hoodlum-Krawall'')—proves that contemporary German-speaking Californians and German-American editors treated ''Hoodlum'' strictly as a local San Francisco Englishism, never equating it in print during 1871-1878 with Hudellump or Haderlump.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-033.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1874 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-042', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'hoodlum', 'contextual_evidence', 'prior_art_or_reception_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1876, '1876-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/californians00fish', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-042.webp', 131808, '8492de6b96cb46217cabaafa4499d1170653fc6d139001746eabfd7cfde2caab', '{"id": "CERT-1000-042", "slug": "cert-1000-042", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "hoodlum", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "prior_art_or_reception_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1876, "date_display": "1876", "sort_date": "1876-01-01", "short_gloss": "Walter M. Fisher (1876) confirms ''hoodlum'' derivation already uncertain in California", "source_publication": "Walter M. Fisher, The Californians (London: Macmillan and Co., 1876), Ch. V, pp. 79-80", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/californians00fish", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "What the derivation of the word \"hoodlum\" is we could never satisfactorily ascertain, though several derivations have been proposed; and it would appear that the word has not been very many years in use.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1876, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verified (1876 title page + pp. 79-80); establishes that Californian observers already regarded the derivation as uncertain prior to the 1877 Eastern newspaper myth wave, and cites Samuel Williams in Scribner''s Monthly (July 1875). (Drive ID: 1fFDEZZa4FWRrunQb_j3Y__-uN4BDCosH).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-042.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1876 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-012', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum', 'contextual_evidence', 'prior_art_or_reception_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1876, '1876-09-01', 'https://archive.org/details/lightsshadesinsa00lloy', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-012.webp', 90298, 'de66bdfffd395b74f3d6f9880594f91b40cc9707fe550ff8a815bba286bc6d3d', '{"id": "CERT-1000-012", "slug": "cert-1000-012", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "prior_art_or_reception_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1876, "date_display": "1 Sep 1876", "sort_date": "1876-09-01", "short_gloss": "Pre-riot SF baseline (B. E. Lloyd): local origin of ''hoodlum'' already ''not obvious''", "source_publication": "B. E. Lloyd, Lights and Shades in San Francisco (San Francisco: A. L. Bancroft & Co., 1876), Ch. XLI (''THE HOODLUM''), pp. 297-300", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (lightsshadesinsa00lloy, leaves n5, n6, n320)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/lightsshadesinsa00lloy", "page_and_column": "Ch. XLI, p. 297", "verbatim_quote": "THE Hoodlum had his origin in San Francisco. He is the offspring of San Francisco society. What particular phase in social life possesses the necessary fertility to produce such fruit is not obvious. It is certain, however, that the seed has been sown in productive soil, for the harvest is abundant. ... It is really wonderful—this growth of hoodlumism. A few years ago it was unknown; now it is met in every department of social life.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1876, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Author''s dated preface (''SAN FRANCISCO, September 1, 1876''), engraved frontispiece, and Ch. XLI (pp. 297-300) visually inspected on Internet Archive. Crucial negative evidence against the 1877 ''Muldoon'' and ''Huddle em'' myths: in Sept. 1876, a well-informed San Francisco chronicler devoting an entire chapter to ''THE HOODLUM'' knew neither any ''Muldoon'' typesetter story nor any ''Huddle em'' cry, confirming those stories were concocted only after the July 1877 San Francisco anti-Chinese / labor riots made ''hoodlum'' national front-page news.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-012.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1876 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-044', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Hoodlum', 'rejected_lead', 'rival_theory_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1877, '1877-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dictionaryameri01bartgoog', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-044.webp', 122138, '0d3f4013e53d2c5a3cd6e2b9414405e0264bb44877b983a729ee96cac75bfd47', '{"id": "CERT-1000-044", "slug": "cert-1000-044", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Hoodlum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "rival_theory_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1877, "date_display": "1877", "sort_date": "1877-01-01", "short_gloss": "Bartlett 2nd ed. (1859, absent) vs. 4th ed. (1877) canonizing Muldoon, Huddle ''em, & Hoods myths", "source_publication": "John Russell Bartlett, Dictionary of Americanisms, 2nd ed. (1859, p. 201) vs. 4th ed. (1877, p. 293 & Addenda p. 785)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dictionaryameri01bartgoog", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "These boys had a place of rendezvous; and, when danger threatened them, their words of warning were, \"Huddle ''em, Huddle ''em!\" ... The name applied to them was soon contracted into \"Hoodlum.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1877, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Side-by-side scan comparison proves ''hoodlum'' is absent in Bartlett''s 1859 2nd ed. (p. 201 jumps HONORABLE -> HOOK) and 1860 3rd ed., whereas the 1877 4th ed. canonized three contradictory Sept.-Oct. 1877 newspaper fables: ''Muldoon -> noodlums'' (p. 293), ''Huddle ''em!'' (p. 785), and Zouave fez ''hoods'' / ''hoodlum girls'' (p. 785). (Drive ID: 1PyWDEsZhIGdUpqaIXWs860pfFWOCfvOs).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-044.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1877 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-028', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'HUDELLUMP', 'rejected_lead', 'phonological_donor_match', 'agent_verified', 1877, '1877-04-16', 'https://archive.org/details/deutscheswrter42grimuoft/page/n943/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-028.webp', 109154, '4770949333ea12544c16de593c1b310cf786530320ddc804514445fa9cd14288', '{"id": "CERT-1000-028", "slug": "cert-1000-028", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "HUDELLUMP", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "phonological_donor_match", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1877, "date_display": "16 Apr 1877", "sort_date": "1877-04-16", "short_gloss": "Catalog misdate rejected (Trap A): IA ''1854'' Grimm DWB Bd. 4.2 (''HUDELLUMP'') is an 1877 imprint", "source_publication": "Jacob Grimm & Wilhelm Grimm (bearb. von Moriz Heyne), Deutsches Wörterbuch, Vierten Bandes Zweite Abtheilung: H. I. J. (Leipzig: Verlag von S. Hirzel, 1877), col. 1862 (s.v. HUDELLUMP, -LUMPE) & cols. 115-116 (s.v. HADERLUMPE, -LUMP, -LUMPEN)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/deutscheswrter42grimuoft/page/n943/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "col. 1862 (JP2 leaf 943, right column) & col. 116 (leaf 70, right column); printed title page leaf 9 (1877) & preface leaf 11 (April 16, 1877)", "verbatim_quote": "HUDELLUMP, -LUMPE, m. wie haderlump sp. 115. 1) lumpe, fetzen: hudellumpen, panniculamenta, panniculi STIELER 804. 2) vgl. haderlump 2, der mit lumpen handelt, lumpensammler: (der kleine Gargantua) forcht den kemmetfeger, den hudelump, und den mann mit dem sack. Garg. 129b; dafür auch hudelumper. groszm. 49; auch der in lumpen einhergeht, und hier als kehrreim von schelmenliedern: ... Hans Jackel Guttuch hudelump! Garg. 92b ... spazieren umb das hausz, hudelumpe!", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1877, "linguistic_note": "Post-1871 lexicographical compilation (1877 volume imprint, falsely cataloged as ''1854'' on Internet Archive); embedded 1590 Fischart and 1691 Stieler primary sources independently verified in CERT-1000-020 and CERT-1000-021.", "analysis_notes": "DEMOTED TO FALSIFIED-MYTH AS DIRECT PRE-1871 IMPRINT — CLASSIC TRAP A SERIAL METADATA MISDATE EXPOSED (1000-B Audit): Both Internet Archive records for DWB Bd. 4, Abt. 2 (deutscheswrter42grimuoft and b33779211_0010) stamp ''year: 1854'' (the publication year of DWB Vol. 1) onto Moriz Heyne''s H-I-J volume. Visual inspection of leaf 9 (''LEIPZIG VERLAG VON S. HIRZEL 1877'') and leaf 11 (Heyne''s Vorwort signed ''Basel den 16. April 1877'') proves a 23-year Trap A serial-metadata error. Fascicle audit confirms Lief. 1 (H-Hafen, cols. 1-240, including col. 115-116 HADERLUMPE, leaf 70) appeared in 1868 (<1871), whereas col. 1862 HUDELLUMP (leaf 943) appeared in Lief. 8/9 (1874-1875, bound 1877 > 1871; chronology_plausible=False as an 1877 imprint). Therefore DWB col. 1862 cannot be cited as a pre-1871 primary source itself, whereas its embedded 1590 Fischart and 1691 Stieler citations are verified directly on pre-1871 scans in CERT-1000-020 and CERT-1000-021.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-028.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1877 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-013', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'noodlums', 'rejected_lead', 'folk_etymology_hoax', 'agent_verified', 1877, '1877-09-08', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn86092182/1877-09-08/ed-1/?sp=1', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-013.webp', 262506, 'd81c4f325e86ad876bbf1d94a10cc8a751262d0eb2f5ca1d8d36eca83b09ea50', '{"id": "CERT-1000-013", "slug": "cert-1000-013", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "noodlums", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "folk_etymology_hoax", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1877, "date_display": "8 Sep 1877", "sort_date": "1877-09-08", "short_gloss": "Earliest print source of ''Muldoon -> noodlums -> hoodlum'' hoax (credited to NY Sun)", "source_publication": "The New Dominion (Morgantown, WV), Vol. III, No. 21 (explicitly crediting ''NEW YORK Sun'')", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn86092182)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn86092182/1877-09-08/ed-1/?sp=1", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 4", "verbatim_quote": "NEW YORK Sun: A slang word in common use, \"hoodlum,\" was maltreated in its birth in this way: A newspaper man in San Francisco, in attempting to coin a word to designate a gang of street Arabs under the beck of one Muldoon, hit on the idea of dubbing them the noodlums; that is, simply reversing the leader''s name. In writing the word the strokes of the n did not correspond in height, and the compositor took it for hoodlum. Hoodlum it is, and probably ever will be.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1877, "linguistic_note": "Falsified ''Muldoon -> noodlums -> hoodlum'' backward-spelling compositor hoax (Stage 1: anonymous San Francisco newspaperman & ''Muldoon'' street-Arab gang, coined in the New York Sun in Aug. 1877 and cribbed uncredited by The Congregationalist on Sept. 26, 1877).", "analysis_notes": "Page 1 masthead (''SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 8, 1877'') and col. 4 ink scan visually verified (:0093). Solves a 150-year bibliographic puzzle: Bartlett (1877, p. 293) and every subsequent reference work credited this exact paragraph to The Congregationalist (Sept. 26, 1877), but The New Dominion printed it verbatim 18 days earlier with explicit attribution to ''NEW YORK Sun'' (corroborated by Deseret Evening News, Aug. 23, 1877). Demolished empirically by (1) Dec. 1866 Police Court records proving the boys already ''call themselves the \"Hoodlum Band\"'' before any reporter coined a name, (2) zero gang leader named Muldoon among the Dec. 1866 arrests (Sullivan, Frattiger, Conley, Hamilton, Hudson, Lloyd, Miller), and (3) Langley''s Sept. 1867 SF Directory (p. 360) showing only 4 Muldoon adults in all of San Francisco (widow Ellen, drayman Patrick, laborer Peter, brass-finisher Thomas J.).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-013.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1877 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-014', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'Noodlum', 'rejected_lead', 'folk_etymology_hoax', 'agent_verified', 1877, '1877-09-30', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn96061149/1877-09-30/ed-1/?sp=2', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-014.webp', 121894, '330e69fc5831e2437437aaeef638eacb60851d0f1e7f324670d9dc07f243fa88', '{"id": "CERT-1000-014", "slug": "cert-1000-014", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "Noodlum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "folk_etymology_hoax", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1877, "date_display": "30 Sep 1877", "sort_date": "1877-09-30", "short_gloss": "Rapid Sept. 1877 mutation of Muldoon from street-gang leader into newspaper essayist", "source_publication": "The Daily Astorian (Astoria, OR), Vol. 3, No. 100 (''The Muldoon Theory'')", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn96061149)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn96061149/1877-09-30/ed-1/?sp=2", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 1 (''The Muldoon Theory'')", "verbatim_quote": "The Muldoon Theory. A California correspondent not long ago furnished a plausible theory respecting the genesis of the term \"hood- lum,\" that mysterious word which has perplexed, yet fascinated, philologists ever since it began to pass current. According to his argument, the word was derived from the name of one Muldoon, who, if we remember rightly, wrote a series of aggressive articles for a San Francisco newspaper, and for a signature adopted an anagram reversing the letters of his name—thus: Noodlum. By a typographical error an h was substituted for the initial n.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1877, "linguistic_note": "Falsified ''Muldoon -> Noodlum -> hoodlum'' hoax (Stage 2 mutation: Muldoon transformed from street-gang leader into a pseudonymous San Francisco newspaper polemicist).", "analysis_notes": "Page 1 masthead (''Sunday Morning, September 30, 1877'') and Page 2 col. 1 ink scan visually verified (:0100/:0101). Demonstrates rapid folkloric instability within weeks of the Aug. 1877 New York Sun fabrication: here Muldoon is no longer a gang leader at all, while the same editorial also quotes and ridicules the rival Aug. 25, 1877 Los Angeles Express ''Huddle em! huddle em!'' wharf-grain-thieves origin story.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-014.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1877 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-015', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'hoodlum', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1878, '1878-03-22', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045160/1878-03-22/ed-1/?sp=4', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-015.webp', 311736, '1b7d91baa414b59bc7831c9584e87d67330e53ddec4723dc5ef0b3d94b04e4c4', '{"id": "CERT-1000-015", "slug": "cert-1000-015", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "hoodlum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1878, "date_display": "22 Mar 1878", "sort_date": "1878-03-22", "short_gloss": "OCR ghost rejected: LoC ''noodlum'' reads ''Memphis hoodlum'' on physical ink", "source_publication": "The Memphis Daily Appeal (Memphis, TN), Vol. XXXVII, No. 68", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn83045160)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045160/1878-03-22/ed-1/?sp=4", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 2", "verbatim_quote": "Judicious punishment, not too severe, but never-failing, might make a man of the young Memphis hoodlum. You can see the youthful Memphian late at night, half drunk on beer, and frequenting places most objectionable.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1878, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Trap B verification: Chronicling America full-text search for ''noodlum'' returns this issue, which could deceive an uncritical researcher into thinking ''noodlum'' actually existed in living American slang outside Muldoon origin tales. Visual inspection of the native-resolution pct:100 scan (:0266) proves the physical lead type prints ''hoodlum'' (with slight ink wear on the upper ascender of ''h''), confirming ''noodlum'' never existed outside meta-etymological hoax articles. [Bracketed Chronicling America ALTO OCR misread (''noodlum'' for printed ink ''hoodlum'') moved out of verbatim_quote into analysis_notes.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-015.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1878 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-016', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'hoodlum', 'rejected_lead', 'folk_etymology_hoax', 'agent_verified', 1879, '1879-10-30', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83026413/1879-10-30/ed-1/?sp=1', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-016.webp', 294846, '81eca10a5f72be1264aef02301b7ea9c6e36ecaa3d3586933a37db56b793d8d4', '{"id": "CERT-1000-016", "slug": "cert-1000-016", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "hoodlum", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "folk_etymology_hoax", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1879, "date_display": "30 Oct 1879", "sort_date": "1879-10-30", "short_gloss": "Three contradictory Aug.–Oct. 1877 origin fables printed side by side (Muldoon, Huddle ''em, Hoods)", "source_publication": "The New Orleans Daily Democrat (New Orleans, LA), Vol. IV, No. 315 (''What Is a Hoodlum?'')", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Chronicling America (sn83026413)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83026413/1879-10-30/ed-1/?sp=1", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 5 (''What Is a Hoodlum?'')", "verbatim_quote": "What Is a Hoodlum? A newspaper man in San Francisco, in attempting to coin a word to designate a gang of young street Arabs under the beck of one named \"Muldoon,\" hit upon the idea of dubbing them \"noodlums,\" ... printed it \"hoodlum.\"—[The Congregationalist, September 26, 1877. A gang of bad boys ... their words of warning were, \"Huddle ''em, huddle ''em!\" An article headed \"Huddle ''em,\" describing the gang and their plan of operations, was published in the San Francisco Times. ...—[Los Angeles (Cal.) Express, August 25, 1877. Before the late war there appeared in San Francisco a man whose dress was very peculiar. ... headdress resembled the fez ... called it a \"hood,\" and the company became known as the \"hoods.\" ... designated as \"hoodlums.\"—[San Francisco Morning Call, October 27, 1877.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1879, "linguistic_note": "Demonstrates simultaneous circulation of three irreconcilable 1877 newspaper inventions (Muldoon/noodlums, Huddle ''em in the SF Times, and pre-Civil War Zouave fez ''hoods'' in the SF Call).", "analysis_notes": "Page 1 masthead (''THURSDAY, OCTOBER 30, 1879'') and col. 5 ink scan visually verified (:0248). Reprinting Bartlett''s 4th-edition Addenda cluster, this single column exposes the 1877 folk-etymology explosion triggered by the July 1877 SF riots: three incompatible stories invented within 65 days of one another (Aug. 23/25 to Oct. 27, 1877), none aware of the December 1866 Police Court prosecution of Lazarus Moses and the juvenile ''Hoodlum Band''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-016.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1879 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-043', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'HOODLUM', 'contextual_evidence', 'prior_art_or_reception_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1888, '1888-06-16', 'https://archive.org/details/americannotesque01philuoft', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-043.webp', 169752, 'd1f06295fa23bca1588fe90360e56266c428565c1f5e8f9826d7563d0dee2b3d', '{"id": "CERT-1000-043", "slug": "cert-1000-043", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "HOODLUM", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "prior_art_or_reception_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1888, "date_display": "16 Jun 1888", "sort_date": "1888-06-16", "short_gloss": "Earliest German-origin scholarship: ''C. G.'' connects ''hoodlum'' to ''hudeln / hudel'' (16 June 1888)", "source_publication": "American Notes and Queries (Philadelphia: Wm. S. Walsh), Vol. 1, No. 6 (June 9, 1888), pp. 65-66 & No. 7 (June 16, 1888), p. 83", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/americannotesque01philuoft", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "HOODLUM (A. N. and Q. Vol. 1 p. 65.) it seems likely is related to, or derived from, the German hudeln, to be idle, to \"loaf,\" hudler, and idler, hudel, trash, a vagabond; just as bummer is the German bummler, an idler. C. G.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1888, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Crucial scholarly prior-art calibration: contributor C. G. proposed deriving ''hoodlum'' from German ''hudeln / hudler / hudel'' on June 16, 1888 (p. 83), 81 years before Peter Tamony (1969). (Drive ID: 13vU4fxFvcKUFa-Cv5yUC800obAVQzci2).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-043.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1888 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-049', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'hood lahnt', 'rejected_lead', 'rival_theory_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1889, '1889-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangjargoncant01barriala', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-049.webp', 89314, 'c7bbd88fb0e6b95ccb09d2d3cef545ad1b6822394aa27c89c4325bbbbceab883', '{"id": "CERT-1000-049", "slug": "cert-1000-049", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "hood lahnt", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "rival_theory_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1889, "date_display": "1889", "sort_date": "1889-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rival pidgin conjecture rejected: Barrère & Leland''s 1889 ''hood lahnt'' (''very lazy'')", "source_publication": "Albert Barrere & Charles G. Leland, A Dictionary of Slang, Jargon & Cant, Vol. I (Ballantyne Press, 1889), p. 470 (paired with Farmer & Henley, Vol. III, 1893, p. 339)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangjargoncant01barriala", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Hoodlum (American), a vagabond or rough, a loafer. It was for a long time a Californian word, and is probably of Spanish origin. It may possibly be the pidgin English, hood lahnt, good, i.e., very lazy; lahnt''o, Mandarin.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1889, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Falsifies Barrere & Leland''s 1889 Spanish / Chinese Pidgin ''hood lahnt'' conjecture: unattested prior to 1889 (1889 > 1871, candidate_viable: False), skeleton KTLKNT lacks final labial/nasal ''-m'', and gloss ''very lazy'' conflicts with 1866 juvenile burglary gang usage. (Drive ID: 15oJTuQIM89sHr2CEOEimWKzZSDuT4RvJ).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-049.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1889 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-040', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'hoodlums', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'externally_recognized', 1899, '1899-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/oxforddictionaryv5p1unse', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-040.webp', 104696, '852cdd962f5bdcc587c69f8471e9eb94ba2a089c51b9cc5f3d6740b05b792aeb', '{"id": "CERT-1000-040", "slug": "cert-1000-040", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "hoodlums", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "externally_recognized", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": "Oxford English Dictionary, 1st ed. (H fascicle, 1899; Vol. V, 1901, p. 371), historical baseline entry.", "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "1899 (quoting 24 Feb 1872)", "sort_date": "1899-01-01", "short_gloss": "OED1 (1899 H fascicle) baseline: 24 Feb. 1872 earliest quote, ''origin unknown'', zero ''hooligan''", "source_publication": "A New English Dictionary on Historical Principles (OED1), Vol. V (H-K, 1901; H fascicle 1899), p. 371", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/oxforddictionaryv5p1unse", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "1872 Sacramento Weekly Union 24 Feb. 2 (Farmer) All the boys to be trained as scriveners..clerks, pettifoggers, polite loafers, street-hounds, hoodlums, and bummers.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Calibrates OED1 (1899) earliest quotation (Feb. 24, 1872) and unrevised etymology note placing origin in San Francisco c. 1870-72 with newspaper fictions proliferating c. 1877; confirms 0 entries for ''hooligan'' in 1899 H fascicle. (Drive ID: 14yci-PEbXrp9rCZijMXzDBdTVwv3XUSJ).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-040.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-046', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'HOODLE', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_lexicon_control', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi03wriguoft', 1, '/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-046.webp', 135232, '9b442fc53f80ded32f4a777f224dff4fdb3c19502d7d167d7e6537ccdd1049b2', '{"id": "CERT-1000-046", "slug": "cert-1000-046", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "HOODLE", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_lexicon_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "1902", "sort_date": "1902-01-01", "short_gloss": "Negative British dialect control: ''hoodlum'' absent in Wright''s English Dialect Dictionary (1902)", "source_publication": "Joseph Wright, The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. III (H-L, London: Henry Frowde, 1902), pp. 221-223", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi03wriguoft", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "HOODLE, see Huddle. HOODLE-CUM-BLIND, sb. Nhp.1 The game of ''blind-man''s buff.'' HOODLING HOW, phr. Obs. Sc. A kind of cap.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Exhaustive inspection of Wright''s EDD Vol. III (pp. 221-223) confirms ''hoodlum'' is completely absent across all British and Anglo-Irish regional dialects; ''hoodle'' (skel KTL is solely a cross-reference to ''huddle'' plus Northamptonshire ''hoodle-cum-blind''. (Drive ID: 1NxoeEPqaocLNQM8XtYfqkSXd1z5l5mYg).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/hoodlum/CERT-1000-046.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1000-050', 'TGT-053', 'hoodlum', NULL, 'hoodlum', 'hoodlum', 'contextual_evidence', 'prior_art_or_reception_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1969, '1969-01-01', 'https://files.shsmo.org/manuscripts/columbia/C3939.pdf', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1000-050", "slug": "cert-1000-050", "case_slug": "hoodlum", "headword": "hoodlum", "attested_form": "hoodlum", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "prior_art_or_reception_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1000-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1969, "date_display": "Jan 1969", "sort_date": "1969-01-01", "short_gloss": "Peter Tamony (Western Folklore, Jan. 1969): 1871/1872 baseline & Bavarian ''hodalum / huddellump''", "source_publication": "Peter Tamony, ''Hoodlums and Folk Etymology'', Western Folklore, Vol. 28, No. 1 (Jan. 1969), pp. 44-48", "source_archive": "Western Folklore / State Historical Society of Missouri (C3939, Box 13, Folder 15)", "source_url": "https://files.shsmo.org/manuscripts/columbia/C3939.pdf", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1969, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Calibrates Peter Tamony''s pivotal 1969 article and SHSMO C3939 archival folder: Tamony knew no pre-1871 California attestations and championed Bavarian ''hodalum / huddellump'' over 1877 S.F. press fables. (Drive ID: 1aAShpK5uwwO6QEOj2YE0Kv6wVm7P0LkJ). [Post-1930 copyrighted scholarship (Western Folklore 28.1, Jan. 1969, pp. 44–48; SHSMO C3939): full-page scan not hosted publicly, and editorial summary moved from verbatim_quote into analysis_notes. Tamony cited Sept. 6, 1871 (Cincinnati Commercial) and Feb. 24, 1872 (Sacramento Weekly Union) as earliest attestations and endorsed Bavarian ''hodalum / huddellump'' following George Gessner and John T. Krumpelmann while rejecting 1877 San Francisco press fables.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1969 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-313', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'chaloupe', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1611, '1611-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_a-dictionarie-of-the-fre_cotgrave-randle_1611/page/n170/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-313.webp', 164908, 'b74943d7d656ee2167057507cad0268775b4b4285e154a3881f096297b9f281e', '{"id": "CERT-1900-313", "slug": "cert-1900-313", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "chaloupe", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1611, "date_display": "1611", "sort_date": "1611-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: chaloupe (Randle Cotgrave, A Dictionarie of the French and English Tongues (London: Adam Islip, 1611), leaf n170, col. 1; August", "source_publication": "Randle Cotgrave, A Dictionarie of the French and English Tongues (London: Adam Islip, 1611), leaf n170, col. 1; Auguste Jal, Glossaire nautique: répertoire polyglotte de termes de marine anciens et modernes (Paris: Firmin Didot, 1848), pp. 452-453 (leaves n453-n454); and Émile Littré, Dictionnaire de la langue française, Vol. I (Paris: Hachette, 1873), pp. 541-542 (leaves n608-n609)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_a-dictionarie-of-the-fre_cotgrave-randle_1611/page/n170/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Cotgrave 1611 leaf n170 col. 1; Jal 1848 p. 453 col. 1 (leaf n454); Littré 1873 Vol. I p. 542 col. 1 (leaf n609)", "verbatim_quote": "[Cotgrave 1611, leaf n170:] Chaloupe: f. A Shallop, or small boat. || [Jal 1848, p. 453:] CHALOUPE, fr. s. f. (Du holl. Sloep.) ... ital. Barca, Barcone, Barcaccia ... Embarcation ... || [Littré 1873, Vol. I, p. 542:] CHALOUPE (cha-lou-p''), s. f. 1° Petit bâtiment à voile et à rames, non ponté, que l''on emmène pour le service d''un grand vaisseau. Chaloupe de pêche.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1611, "masthead_year": 1611, "linguistic_note": "Proposed maritime-to-garage hypothesis (Italian/Sicilian scialuppa ~ cialuppa and North American French chaloupe mapped onto pre-1924 U.S. garage ''old boat / old tub'' slang, with possible reinforcement from Scots gilpy and Yiddish shlape/shlap). Brookline''s 1921–1925 fictional prep-school names (''East Jolopy / Jalopy / Gillopie Prep.'') and local Ford-trade context are classified separately from the 1927–1930 automotive attestations; neither proves the source of the first syllable.", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit PATHWAY 1A — UPGRADES CERT-500-026] Multi-lexicon primary scan upgrade (Cotgrave 1611 + Jal 1848 + Littré 1873). Deterministic score_phonological_contact(''jalopy'', ''chaloupe'', 1611, 1924) = three-quarters consonant-class match (SLPW vs SLP, viable=True; exact consonant-class match against early -i spelling ''jaloppi''). Uniquely explains early high-back rounded vowel spellings (''jaloupy'', ''jaloopy'', ''jalupie'') from French <ou> [u], though French /ʃaˈlup/ requires both initial postalveolar voicing (/ʃ/ -> /dʒ/) and addition of English hypocoristic -y/-ie to gain a third syllable.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-313.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1611 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-305', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'jalop', 'rejected_lead', 'homograph_medicinal_jalap', 'agent_verified', 1815, '1815-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/39002086312460.med.yale.edu/page/n14/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-305.webp', 181606, '02fb5deb3a84970e9ab84dc3658e58230efe04009960efc6fb7fcdf010856543', '{"id": "CERT-1900-305", "slug": "cert-1900-305", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "jalop", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "homograph_medicinal_jalap", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1815, "date_display": "1815", "sort_date": "1815-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: jalop (Ennalls Martin, An Essay on the Epidemics of the Winters of 1813 and 1814, in Talbot & Queen-Anne''s Counties in the St", "source_publication": "Ennalls Martin, An Essay on the Epidemics of the Winters of 1813 and 1814, in Talbot & Queen-Anne''s Counties in the State of Maryland (Baltimore: Joseph Robinson, 1815), p. 13 (leaf n14); paired with Gazeta Lwowska, No. 30 (March 11, 1830, p. 183, leaf n4)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Yale Medical Library / Jagiellonian Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/39002086312460.med.yale.edu/page/n14/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Martin 1815 p. 13 (leaf n14); Gazeta Lwowska 1830 p. 183, col. 2 (leaf n4)", "verbatim_quote": "Mr. ___ was called in because he could bleed, and give calomel and jalop. She was bled in the morning, and took calomel and jalop, which operated violently. She ceased to exist long before the beginning of another day. || [Gazeta Lwowska, 1830, p. 183:] oraz cała prowincyja San Luis de Potozi przystąpiły do aktu Jałapy.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1815, "masthead_year": 1815, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit TRAP C KILL] Accounts for 1,118 pre-1924 IA full-text hits for ''jalop'' and 253 hits for ''jalapy'': (1) English/French ''jalop'' is a routine 18th/19th-century spelling of pharmaceutical jalap (Ipomoea purga, purgative tuber named from Xalapa, Mexico, administered with calomel); (2) ''jalapy'' hits in Central European serials are Polish genitive singulars of botanical jalapa (''korzeń jalapy'') or Mexican toponym Jałapa (''do aktu Jałapy'', Bustamante''s Dec. 1829 Plan of Jalapa).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-305.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1815 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-304', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'lallopy', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1868, '1868-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/10581999bsb/page/n363/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-304.webp', 129090, '2079f088c633a874d84e5746ceb0dcc52ed0371f90e4660bc29f9cf90474a8ca', '{"id": "CERT-1900-304", "slug": "cert-1900-304", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "lallopy", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1868, "date_display": "1868", "sort_date": "1868-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: lallopy (Rev. J. C. Atkinson, A Glossary of the Cleveland Dialect (London: John Russell Smith, 1868), p. 304 (leaf n363); pai", "source_publication": "Rev. J. C. Atkinson, A Glossary of the Cleveland Dialect (London: John Russell Smith, 1868), p. 304 (leaf n363); paired with Xenia Evening Gazette (Xenia, OH, July 26, 1924, p. 15, leaf n15) and The Bradford Reporter (Towanda, PA, March 31, 1870, p. 3)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/10581999bsb/page/n363/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Atkinson 1868 p. 304 (leaf n363); Xenia Evening Gazette July 26, 1924 p. 15, col. 5 (leaf n15)", "verbatim_quote": "[Atkinson 1868, p. 304 ink:] Lallopy, adj. Lounging, idle and slovenly in gait and habits. ''A lang lallopy lass, as lazy as she''s lang.'' Wb. Gl. [OCR ghost: ''Jallopy''] || [Xenia Evening Gazette, July 26, 1924, p. 15 ink:] For months I had a tired, sleepy feeling all day, and when night would come I would be so nervous I couldn''t stay in bed. [OCR ghost: ''jaloppy'']", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1868, "masthead_year": 1868, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit TRAP B KILL] Visual ink inspection rules out three deceptive full-text OCR hits: (1) Atkinson 1868 p. 304 prints italic ''lallopy'' (mis-OCRed as ''Jallopy''); (2) Xenia Evening Gazette (July 26, 1924, p. 15, Lydia E. Pinkham Vegetable Compound testimonial of Mrs. J. B. Holleman) prints ''a tired, sleepy feeling all day'' (smudged ''sl-'' mis-OCRed as ''jaloppy''—a critical false-1924 trap!); and (3) Bradford Reporter (Mar. 31, 1870) display type generated OCR gibberish ''NO Jaloppy 1 NO RVMBVG 1'' in Alex. Solomon''s store ad.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-304.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1868 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-319', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'scialuppa', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1868, '1868-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_qIBWAAAAcAAJ/page/n901/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-319.webp', 126142, 'd2133e6a2edd320cb012daad83dfdd139beb5f852f069d774bee0254c652dbf3', '{"id": "CERT-1900-319", "slug": "cert-1900-319", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "scialuppa", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1868, "date_display": "1868", "sort_date": "1868-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: scialuppa (Antonino Traina, Nuovo vocabolario siciliano-italiano (Palermo: Giuseppe Pedone Lauriel, 1868), pp. 889-890 (leaves n", "source_publication": "Antonino Traina, Nuovo vocabolario siciliano-italiano (Palermo: Giuseppe Pedone Lauriel, 1868), pp. 889-890 (leaves n901-n902, s.v. Scialuppa, Sciarappa); Vincenzo Mortillaro, Nuovo dizionario siciliano-italiano, 3rd ed. (Palermo, 1876, p. 983); Alfred Hoare, An Italian Dictionary (Cambridge: University Press, 1915, pp. 545, 675, leaves n564, n695); and La Stampa (Turin, September 23, 1901, p. 1, ''cialuppa'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_qIBWAAAAcAAJ/page/n901/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Traina 1868 p. 889 col. 1 (leaf n901); Hoare 1915 p. 545 col. 3 (leaf n564); La Stampa Sept. 23, 1901 p. 1 (leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "[Traina 1868, p. 889:] Scialuppa. s. m. T. mar. La maggiore delle barche destinate al servizio dei vascelli da guerra: scialuppa. || Schifo: scialuppa. || [Hoare 1915, p. 545:] Scialuppa f. (< Fr. chaloupe or Span. chalupa, which are < Dutch sloep); (mar.) launch. [p. 675: Barge; barca, scialuppa, barcone.] || [La Stampa, Sept. 23, 1901, p. 1:] cialuppa era già piena...", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1868, "masthead_year": 1868, "linguistic_note": "Proposed maritime-to-garage hypothesis (Italian/Sicilian scialuppa ~ cialuppa and North American French chaloupe mapped onto pre-1924 U.S. garage ''old boat / old tub'' slang, with possible reinforcement from Scots gilpy and Yiddish shlape/shlap). Brookline''s 1921–1925 fictional prep-school names (''East Jolopy / Jalopy / Gillopie Prep.'') and local Ford-trade context are classified separately from the 1927–1930 automotive attestations; neither proves the source of the first syllable.", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit PATHWAY 3A — HIGHEST PROSODIC & PHONOLOGICAL FIT] Resolves the two structural weaknesses of standalone French chaloupe (/ʃaˈlup/): Italian/Sicilian scialuppa ~ dialectal affricate cialuppa ([ʃaˈluppa ~ tʃaˈluppa], attested in period Italian newsprint La Stampa 1901) ALREADY supplies (1) an exact 3-syllable amphibrachic template CV-ˈCVC-CV with final unstressed vowel (-a/-e regularly raised to -i/-y in Italian-American English, cf. Bologna -> baloney, culminating in Chicago 1929 ''Jaloppi'' where cialuppa scores an EXACT exact consonant-class match SLP==SLP match!), and (2) an initial palatal affricate [tʃ] ~ [dʒ]. Demographically reinforced across NYC, Chicago, and New Orleans (where 1920 Census Table 75 counts 7,633 Italian-born residents—over 6x the Mexican-born population!).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-319.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1868 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-317', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'Schlappe', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1883, '1883-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_CA8JAAAAQAAJ/page/n286/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-317.webp', 155328, '72740c6dd3e9250d04206a52d53f576294af2e19f2cfc11bce8f985579f4e02a', '{"id": "CERT-1900-317", "slug": "cert-1900-317", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "Schlappe", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1883, "date_display": "1883", "sort_date": "1883-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Schlappe (Jacob Grimm & Wilhelm Grimm (ed. Moriz Heyne), Deutsches Wörterbuch, Bd. 9 [Schiefeln–Seele] (Leipzig: S. Hirzel, 1899", "source_publication": "Jacob Grimm & Wilhelm Grimm (ed. Moriz Heyne), Deutsches Wörterbuch, Bd. 9 [Schiefeln–Seele] (Leipzig: S. Hirzel, 1899), cols. 479-496 (leaves n240, n247, n255); and Hermann Frischbier, Preussisches Wörterbuch: Ost- und westpreussische Provinzialismen, Bd. 2 [L–Z] (Berlin: Th. Chr. Fr. Enslin, 1883), pp. 279-287 (leaves n284-n289)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_CA8JAAAAQAAJ/page/n286/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Frischbier 1883 Bd. 2 p. 282 (leaf n286) & p. 279 (leaf n283); Grimm DWB Bd. 9 (1899) cols. 493-496 (leaves n247, n255)", "verbatim_quote": "[Frischbier 1883, Bd. 2, p. 282:] schlapp, adj. u. adv. 1. schlaff, welk, weich. Schlapper Hut. Old, kold, slapp on met wedderlichem Schmeerbuk. ... Schlappe, s. Schlabbe ... Schlorre: 1. Pantoffel, namentlich alter, ausgeweiteter; auch niedergetretener Schuh. || [Grimm DWB Bd. 9, 1899, cols. 479-495:] SCHLAPP ... SCHLAPPE, f. Pantoffel, schlaffer Schuh.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1883, "masthead_year": 1883, "linguistic_note": "Proposed maritime-to-garage hypothesis (Italian/Sicilian scialuppa ~ cialuppa and North American French chaloupe mapped onto pre-1924 U.S. garage ''old boat / old tub'' slang, with possible reinforcement from Scots gilpy and Yiddish shlape/shlap). Brookline''s 1921–1925 fictional prep-school names (''East Jolopy / Jalopy / Gillopie Prep.'') and local Ford-trade context are classified separately from the 1927–1930 automotive attestations; neither proves the source of the first syllable.", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit PATHWAY 2B] Primary scans of Grimm DWB Bd. 9 (1899) and Frischbier Bd. 2 (1883) verify German Schlappe (''worn-out trodden slipper'', SLP vs SLPW = three-quarters consonant-class match, viable=True) and schlapp (''slack, limp, worn''). Note that in Frischbier (1883), the primary East Prussian term for ''altes, mageres Pferd'' is Luder (p. 38), whereas Schlappe/Schlorre center on down-at-heel footwear and limp gear; colloquial German salopp ([zaˈlɔp], SLP=three-quarters consonant-class match) provides a 2-syllable stress-final parallel without #ʃl- cluster epenthesis.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-317.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1883 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-318', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'szłapać', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1890, '1890-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dokadnysownikpo00chodgoog/page/n380/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-318.webp', 163434, 'b445f85014e8070ca2a916b3e0255da5d692180aee7368d108812275d40c9249', '{"id": "CERT-1900-318", "slug": "cert-1900-318", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "szłapać", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1890, "date_display": "1890", "sort_date": "1890-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: szłapać (Aleksander Chodźko, Dokładny słownik polsko-angielski i angielsko-polski (Chicago, IL: Nakładem i drukiem Władysława Dy", "source_publication": "Aleksander Chodźko, Dokładny słownik polsko-angielski i angielsko-polski (Chicago, IL: Nakładem i drukiem Władysława Dyniewicza, 1890 [1st ed. 1874]), p. 370 (leaf n379, s.v. SZKAPA, SZKAPSKO) & p. 371 (leaf n380, s.v. SZŁAPAĆ, SZŁAPAK); paired with W. Kierst & O. Callier, Burt''s Polish-English Dictionary (New York, c. 1900, p. 197, leaf n633, s.v. jade = szkapa)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dokadnysownikpo00chodgoog/page/n380/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Chodźko (Chicago, 1890) p. 371 col. 2 (leaf n380) & p. 370 col. 2 (leaf n379); Kierst & Callier (1900) leaf n633", "verbatim_quote": "[Chodźko, Chicago 1890, p. 371:] SZŁAPAĆ, PIĘ, v. n. vulg., to jog, to walk heavily or slowly. SZŁAPAK, a, s. m. jogger, an ambler or pacer, an Austrian foot-soldier. || [p. 370:] SZKAPA, Y, s. f., SZKAPSKO, A, s. n. jade, hack, hackney. || [Kierst & Callier 1900, leaf n633:] jade, szkapa f, szkapsko.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1890, "masthead_year": 1890, "linguistic_note": "Proposed maritime-to-garage hypothesis (Italian/Sicilian scialuppa ~ cialuppa and North American French chaloupe mapped onto pre-1924 U.S. garage ''old boat / old tub'' slang, with possible reinforcement from Scots gilpy and Yiddish shlape/shlap). Brookline''s 1921–1925 fictional prep-school names (''East Jolopy / Jalopy / Gillopie Prep.'') and local Ford-trade context are classified separately from the 1927–1930 automotive attestations; neither proves the source of the first syllable.", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit PATHWAY 2C] Primary scan of Dyniewicz''s 1890 Chicago Polish-English dictionary cleanly bifurcates the two Polish candidates: (1) Polish szłapać / człapać (''jog, walk heavily'', SLPS vs SLPW = three-quarters consonant-class match, viable=True) is phonologically viable (mostly as the Slavic source/reinforcer of Yiddish shlapen); whereas (2) Polish szkapa / szkapina (''wretched nag, jade'', SKP / SKPN vs SLPW = half consonant-class match (below viability threshold) < 0.60, viable=False) is deterministically FALSIFIED because medial velar /k/ cannot yield English lateral /l/.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-318.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1890 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-307', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'Gilopy', 'rejected_lead', 'proper_noun_surname', 'agent_verified', 1892, '1892-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/historyof67threg00scot/page/n145/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-307.webp', 134354, 'f2d853edd3f3d3a48adadc19dcdf1a3750ce194865e1fcb9ad2b72b6ccb3325d', '{"id": "CERT-1900-307", "slug": "cert-1900-307", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "Gilopy", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "proper_noun_surname", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1892, "date_display": "1892", "sort_date": "1892-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Gilopy (Reuben B. Scott, The History of the 67th Regiment Indiana Infantry Volunteers, War of the Rebellion (Bedford, IN: Her", "source_publication": "Reuben B. Scott, The History of the 67th Regiment Indiana Infantry Volunteers, War of the Rebellion (Bedford, IN: Herald Book and Job Print, 1892), p. 128 (leaf n145; matching Report of the Adjutant General of the State of Indiana, Vol. VI, 1866, leaf n81); paired with Lowell Sun (August 24, 1898, p. 4) and Janesville Daily Gazette (August 23, 1910, p. 4)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/historyof67threg00scot/page/n145/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Scott 1892 p. 128 (leaf n145); Lowell Sun Aug. 24, 1898 p. 4 (leaf n3); Janesville Daily Gazette Aug. 23, 1910 p. 4", "verbatim_quote": "SIXTY-SEVENTH REGIMENT INFANTRY. ENLISTED MEN OF COMPANY ''C.'' ... Gilopy, John S. | Date of Muster: Aug. 19, ''62 | Remarks: Mustered out July 19, ''65. || [Lowell Sun, Aug. 24, 1898, p. 4:] Rank errors by Pickett and Gallopy [OCR: ''(Jallopy''], latest additions to the Springfields, enabled the Syracuses to win the game in the seventh inning.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1892, "masthead_year": 1892, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit TRAP C KILL] Every pre-1924 hit for ''gilopy''/''gillopy'' is a personal surname (Pvt. John S. Gilopy, Co. C, 67th Indiana Infantry; Boston ''List of Residents'' ''GILLOPY'', misdated 1921 for 1969), while 1898 Lowell Sun (Eastern League infielder Pete Gallopy) and 1910 Janesville Daily Gazette (Milwaukee A.O.H. delegate John H. Gallopy) are capital-G -> ''(J'' OCR misreads of Irish surname Gallopy.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-307.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1892 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-320', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'gilpy', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi02wrig/page/n624/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-320.webp', 142674, '05b487e8c3f4105598358afa2f86bd7b13ae21c2b1174cf65632936957fcbf2d', '{"id": "CERT-1900-320", "slug": "cert-1900-320", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "gilpy", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "1902", "sort_date": "1902-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: gilpy (Joseph Wright, The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. II [D–G] (London: Henry Frowde, 1900), p. 615 (leaf n624, s.v. GILPY ", "source_publication": "Joseph Wright, The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. II [D–G] (London: Henry Frowde, 1900), p. 615 (leaf n624, s.v. GILPY [giˈlpi]) & Vol. III [H–L] (1902), p. 344 (leaf n355, s.v. JALAP / jallop [dʒaˈləp]), p. 380 (leaf n389, s.v. JOLLOP), p. 511 (leaf n521, s.v. LALLOPY), and pp. 661-663 (leaves n671-n673, s.v. LOPPY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi02wrig/page/n624/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "EDD Vol. II (1900) p. 615 col. 2 (leaf n624); Vol. III (1902) p. 344 col. 2 (leaf n355) & p. 661 col. 2 (leaf n671)", "verbatim_quote": "[EDD Vol. II, p. 615:] GILPY, sb. Sc. Also in form gilpin Bnff. Gall. [giˈlpi.] 1. A lively young fellow; a roguish boy; also, a soft, stupid person. || [EDD Vol. III, p. 344:] JALAP ... Also in forms jalop Som.; jallop Sc. Yks. Nhp. War. Dev. Cor. ... [dʒaˈləp.] || [p. 661:] LOPPY ... (4) Lopper, sb. a lame person, one who limps ... (6) Loppy, sb. a lame dog.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1902, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "Proposed maritime-to-garage hypothesis (Italian/Sicilian scialuppa ~ cialuppa and North American French chaloupe mapped onto pre-1924 U.S. garage ''old boat / old tub'' slang, with possible reinforcement from Scots gilpy and Yiddish shlape/shlap). Brookline''s 1921–1925 fictional prep-school names (''East Jolopy / Jalopy / Gillopie Prep.'') and local Ford-trade context are classified separately from the 1927–1930 automotive attestations; neither proves the source of the first syllable.", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit PATHWAY 3B] While no English/Scots dialect lexeme denoted a vehicle before 1924, Wright''s EDD documents exact phonological attractors that shaped American oral variation: Scots gilpy [ˈɡɪlpi] achieves an exact exact consonant-class match skeleton match (KLPW==KLPW) with early hard-velar ''gillopy'' (Steinbeck 1936), while dialectal jallop [dʒaˈləp] (three-quarters consonant-class match), lallopy (''slovenly in gait'', three-quarters consonant-class match), and loppy (''limping, lame dog; choppy sea'', three-quarters consonant-class match)—alongside American automotive jitney + sloppy/floppy—supplied native phonaesthetic reinforcement lowering stressed /u/ -> /ɑ ~ ɒ/ (''jaloupy'' -> ''jaloppy'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-320.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-218', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'jallopy', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_c_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1903, '1903-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/roundanvilrockro00bankiala/page/213/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-218.webp', 121520, 'ee8a94f1593a89e24147b8470129e7e1d2aa5bca5951feb5bc7832544db88460', '{"id": "CERT-1900-218", "slug": "cert-1900-218", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "jallopy", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_c_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1903, "date_display": "1903", "sort_date": "1903-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: jallopy (Nancy Huston Banks, Round Anvil Rock: A Romance (New York: The Macmillan Company, 1903), p. 213 (leaf n237))", "source_publication": "Nancy Huston Banks, Round Anvil Rock: A Romance (New York: The Macmillan Company, 1903), p. 213 (leaf n237)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/roundanvilrockro00bankiala/page/213/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 213 (IA leaf n226)", "verbatim_quote": "On first coming he had found more people being killed by calomel and jalap than by the plague. At every turn he encountered this bane of the country which was called callomy-jallopy, and at that moment he was utterly worn out, body and soul", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1903, "masthead_year": 1903, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Trap C Medical Homograph Shield: Physical page 213 prints ''callomy-jallopy'', a genuine 19th-century Kentucky frontier folk-rhyming compound for the purgative dose ''calomel and jalap'' (where ''jalap'' itself derives from Xalapa/Jalapa, Mexico). Falsifies pre-1924 automotive antedating while documenting that ''jallopy'' already existed in rural American speech as a humorous reduplicative/medical word.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-218.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1903 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-314', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'chaloupe', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1909, '1909-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/leparlerpopulair00dionuoft/page/n158/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-314.webp', 127606, 'bf2c0c0c9ee9709233903fd955612a477b10e3f5be3cebc27dac02478127dbcb', '{"id": "CERT-1900-314", "slug": "cert-1900-314", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "chaloupe", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1909, "date_display": "1909", "sort_date": "1909-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: chaloupe (Sylva Clapin, Dictionnaire canadien-français (Montreal & Boston: C. O. Beauchemin & Fils, 1894), leaf n129 (s.v. Chalo", "source_publication": "Sylva Clapin, Dictionnaire canadien-français (Montreal & Boston: C. O. Beauchemin & Fils, 1894), leaf n129 (s.v. Chaloupier); Narcisse-Eutrope Dionne, Le parler populaire des Canadiens français (Quebec: Laflamme & Proulx, 1909), pp. 3, 131, 260 (leaf n158); and Colonial Louisiana Superior Council records (1729) in The Louisiana Historical Quarterly, Vol. 7, No. 3 (July 1924), p. 501 (leaf n16)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/leparlerpopulair00dionuoft/page/n158/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Dionne 1909 p. 131 (leaf n158); Clapin 1894 leaf n129; LA Hist. Quarterly July 1924 p. 501 (leaf n16)", "verbatim_quote": "[Clapin 1894, leaf n129:] Chaloupier, s. m., Qui exerce le métier de conducteur de chaloupe, le long des rives du Saint-Laurent, pour des fins de petit cabotage. || [Dionne 1909, p. 131:] Chaloupée, n. f. — La charge d''une chaloupe. Chaloupier. — Qui conduit une chaloupe. [p. 3: Ma chaloupe abîme l''eau; p. 260: Notre chaloupe fait l''eau.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1909, "masthead_year": 1909, "linguistic_note": "Proposed maritime-to-garage hypothesis (Italian/Sicilian scialuppa ~ cialuppa and North American French chaloupe mapped onto pre-1924 U.S. garage ''old boat / old tub'' slang, with possible reinforcement from Scots gilpy and Yiddish shlape/shlap). Brookline''s 1921–1925 fictional prep-school names (''East Jolopy / Jalopy / Gillopie Prep.'') and local Ford-trade context are classified separately from the 1927–1930 automotive attestations; neither proves the source of the first syllable.", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit PATHWAY 1B] Demonstrates living pre-1924 North American vernacular continuity of chaloupe across both the St. Lawrence / Upstate NY / New England French-Canadian corridor (Clapin 1894; Dionne 1909, including leaky-boat idioms ''Ma chaloupe abîme l''eau'' and ''Notre chaloupe fait l''eau'') and the Mississippi / New Orleans Gulf corridor (1729 Superior Council records; cf. Gulf Spanish chalupa [tʃaˈlupa], SLP=three-quarters consonant-class match).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-314.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1909 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1900-316', 'TGT-011', 'jalopy', NULL, 'jalopy', 'shlape', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1910, '1910-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/completeenglishj00harkuoft/page/n804/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-316.webp', 166650, 'ac471f4a274724dc6f7e1833d01295bf032775357b10e8843f884bbf0469aef5', '{"id": "CERT-1900-316", "slug": "cert-1900-316", "case_slug": "jalopy", "headword": "jalopy", "attested_form": "shlape", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1910, "date_display": "1910", "sort_date": "1910-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: shlape (Alexander Harkavy, Complete English-Jewish Dictionary / Yiddish-English Dictionary, 6th rev. ed. (New York: Hebrew Publi", "source_publication": "Alexander Harkavy, Complete English-Jewish Dictionary / Yiddish-English Dictionary, 6th rev. ed. (New York: Hebrew Publishing Co., 1910 [c.1891/1898]), Part II, p. 343 (leaf n796, s.v. שקאַפּע shkape) & p. 351 (leaf n804, s.v. שלאַפּ shlap, שלאַפּע shlape); and Yiddish-English-Hebrew Dictionary (1925 [1898], p. 504, leaf n511, s.v. שלאַפּ shlap, שלאַפּען shlapen)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/completeenglishj00harkuoft/page/n804/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Harkavy 1910 Part II p. 351 col. 1 (leaf n804) & p. 343 col. 2 (leaf n796); Harkavy 1925 p. 504 col. 1 (leaf n511)", "verbatim_quote": "[Harkavy 1910, p. 351:] שלאַפּ [shlap], adj. weak, feeble. | שלאַפּע [shlape], f. (pl. -ס) slipper. || [Harkavy 1925, p. 504:] שלאַפּען [shlapen] to wade through snow or mud v. n. || [Harkavy 1910, p. 343:] שקאַפּע [shkape], f. (pl. -ס) 1) jade, worthless horse; 2) old woman.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1910, "masthead_year": 1910, "linguistic_note": "Proposed maritime-to-garage hypothesis (Italian/Sicilian scialuppa ~ cialuppa and North American French chaloupe mapped onto pre-1924 U.S. garage ''old boat / old tub'' slang, with possible reinforcement from Scots gilpy and Yiddish shlape/shlap). Brookline''s 1921–1925 fictional prep-school names (''East Jolopy / Jalopy / Gillopie Prep.'') and local Ford-trade context are classified separately from the 1927–1930 automotive attestations; neither proves the source of the first syllable.", "analysis_notes": "[archival audit PATHWAY 2A — UPGRADES CERT-500-027 & SOLVES QUINION CONFLATION] Visual audit of Harkavy 1910 pp. 343 & 351 exposes how Michael Quinion''s secondary report (''Yiddish shlappe, a term for an old horse that actually derives from Polish'') conflated two separate Yiddish headwords: (1) שלאַפּע shlape (''slipper'', [ˈʃlapə], SLP=three-quarters consonant-class match, viable=True) + שלאַפּ/שלאַפּען (''weak, feeble; trudge in mud'', SLP/SLPN=three-quarters consonant-class match), and (2) שקאַפּע shkape (''jade, worthless horse'' < Polish szkapa, SKP=half consonant-class match (below viability threshold) < 0.60, viable=False). While Yiddish urban demography in NYC/Chicago is strong, #ʃl- cluster break-up (#ʃl- -> dʒəl-) requires non-trivial onset voicing + vowel epenthesis.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/jalopy/CERT-1900-316.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1910 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
