 (''money'') across the entire digitized run of Variety (zero hits 1905-1940), appearing April 23, 1941 in a 52nd Street Swing Alley nightclub review of former world light-heavyweight boxing champion ''Slapsie'' Maxie Rosenbloom at Leon & Eddie''s—uniting boxing, 52nd St. jazz clubs, and Broadway trade slang. [Cross-verified independently by CERT-1300-019: April 23, 1941 Variety p. 44 Maxie Rosenbloom ''heavy moolah'' debut.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1941 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-020', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moola', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1941, '1941-05-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1941-05-01_8_9/page/n8/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-020", "slug": "cert-1300-020", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moola", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1941, "date_display": "1 May 1941", "sort_date": "1941-05-01", "short_gloss": "''moola'' in Down Beat (1 May 1941)", "source_publication": "Down Beat, Vol. 8, No. 9 (Chicago, May 1, 1941)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1941-05-01_8_9/page/n8/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 9 [IA leaf n8], col. 2, ''Scramble to Form New Orks Unprecedented; All Seek the Jackpot'' (''9 Out of 10 Fail!'')", "verbatim_quote": "There is still another class of bands which, although not in the gravy moola class, is made up of leaders whose crews are making money and working steadily in the better hotels and summer spots.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1941, "masthead_year": 1941, "linguistic_note": "Swing/dance-band trade adoption (''in the gravy moola class''): marks the entry of ''moola'' into Down Beat in May 1941, contemporaneous with 1941 Pittsburgh Courier clipped ''moo''", "analysis_notes": "[1300-A DOWN BEAT''S FIRST VERIFIED ''MOOLA'' ATTESTATION] Masthead (n0, May 1, 1941) and target column (n8) verified on physical scan. After 0 hits across 1937-1940 (CERT-1300-003), Down Beat prints ''moola'' four times in May-Dec. 1941 (May 1 ''gravy moola''; Oct. 15 leaf n18 ''Thinks of Moola 2nd''; Nov. 15 leaf n8 ''more moola''; Dec. 15 leaves n3 & n34 ''coining moola'' / ''Biggest Moola''). Proof plate: CERT-1300-020_down_beat_1941_05_01_first_gravy_moola.png ( [Chief Orchestrator Visual Audit: Running header atop IA leaf n8 reads ''Chicago, May 1, 1941 | News | DOWN BEAT | 9'' (verified on companion plate CERT-1300-038).]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1941 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-038', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moola', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1941, '1941-05-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1941-05-01_8_9/page/n8/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-038", "slug": "cert-1300-038", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moola", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1941, "date_display": "1 May 1941", "sort_date": "1941-05-01", "short_gloss": "''moola'' in Down Beat (Chicago) (1 May 1941)", "source_publication": "Down Beat (Chicago), Vol. 8, No. 9 (May 1, 1941), p. 9 (''Scramble to Form New Orks Unprecedented; All Seek the Jackpot'', leaf 8)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1941-05-01_8_9/page/n8/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 9 [IA leaf n8], col. 2 (''9 Out of 10 Fail!'')", "verbatim_quote": "There is still another class of bands which, although not in the gravy moola class, is made up of leaders whose crews are making money and working steadily in the better hotels and summer spots.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1941, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Earliest verified attestation of ''moola'' in Chicago swing-musician biweekly Down Beat (May 1, 1941, p. 9), appearing within 8 days of Variety''s April 23, 1941 debut—confirming rapid spring-1941 diffusion from Broadway/52nd Street into national big-band booking jargon.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1941 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-021', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moolah', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1941, '1941-05-07', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1941-05-07_142_9/page/n14/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-021", "slug": "cert-1300-021", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moolah", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1941, "date_display": "7 May 1941", "sort_date": "1941-05-07", "short_gloss": "''moolah'' in Variety (7 May 1941)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 142, No. 9 (New York, Wednesday, May 7, 1941)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_variety_1941-05-07_142_9/page/n14/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 15, col. 2, Broadway tavern reminiscence (IA leaf n14; masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Me and Aggie just finished reading an article by my old pal Damon Runyon, and brother, how he can put words together! ... Dowling''s on 43d and Broadway had a nice layout of free grub, a place where a guy could walk in with a little moolah, meet the gang and fill himself up with laughs and lunch and paint his nose red.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1941, "masthead_year": 1941, "linguistic_note": "Runyonesque Broadway/Times Square saloon register: explicitly invokes Damon Runyon and Billy LaHiff''s Gaiety saloon (46th & Broadway, hangout of Runyon, Winchell, and boxing writers) alongside ''moolah''", "analysis_notes": "[1300-A DAMON RUNYON / BROADWAY SALOON MILIEU BRIDGE] Masthead (n0) and p. 15 (n14) verified for May 7, 1941 (Variety''s second occurrence, 14 days after CERT-1300-019; followed May 28, 1941 leaf n15 by ''moolah to the extent of 1,900 G''s''). Directly situates ''moolah'' in the Runyon/LaHiff Times Square sportswriter-and-vaudevillian social network three years prior to Green''s 1944 Runyon book quote. Proof plate: CERT-1300-021_variety_1941_05_07_broadway_little_moolah.png ( [Cross-verified independently by CERT-1300-039: May 7, 1941 Variety p. 15 Joe Laurie Jr. / Damon Runyon ''little moolah''.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1941 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-039', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moolah', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1941, '1941-05-07', 'https://archive.org/details/variety142-1941-05/page/n14/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-039", "slug": "cert-1300-039", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moolah", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1941, "date_display": "7 May 1941", "sort_date": "1941-05-07", "short_gloss": "''moolah'' in Variety (7 May 1941)", "source_publication": "Variety, Vol. 142, No. 9 (Wednesday, May 7, 1941), p. 15 (''Damon Runyon''s Piece Uncorks Lefty''s Memory Files on Free Lunch That Wuz'' by Joe Laurie, Jr., leaf 14)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Media History Digital Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/variety142-1941-05/page/n14/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 142, No. 9, p. 15, col. 2, Joe Laurie, Jr. Broadway reminiscence (IA leaf n14)", "verbatim_quote": "Me and Aggie just finished reading an article by my old pal Damon Runyon, and brother, how he can put words together! ... Dowling''s on 43d and Broadway had a nice layout of free grub, a place where a guy could walk in with a little moolah, meet the gang and fill himself up with laughs and lunch and paint his nose red.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1941, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Written by veteran vaudeville comedian and historian Joe Laurie, Jr. explicitly mimicking Damon Runyon''s Broadway-and-43rd Street argot (''my old pal Damon Runyon... a guy could walk in with a little moolah''), directly linking vaudevillians and Runyon''s Times Square turf/saloon register. [Cross-verified independently by CERT-1300-021: May 7, 1941 Variety p. 15 Joe Laurie Jr. / Damon Runyon ''little moolah''.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1941 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-022', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moola', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1941, '1941-05-26', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_newsweek-us_1941-05-26_17_21/page/n73/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-022", "slug": "cert-1300-022", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moola", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1941, "date_display": "26 May 1941", "sort_date": "1941-05-26", "short_gloss": "''moola'' in Newsweek (US Edition) (26 May 1941)", "source_publication": "Newsweek (US Edition), Vol. 17, No. 21 (New York, May 26, 1941)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_newsweek-us_1941-05-26_17_21/page/n73/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 72, col. 2, John O''Hara ''Entertainment Week'' review of ''Major Barbara'' (IA leaf n73; cover leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Rex Harrison, for my moola, is a poor man''s Leslie Howard, which is poverty indeed. Wendy Hiller is so forgettable that I have to worry about the spelling of her name.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1941, "masthead_year": 1941, "linguistic_note": "Mainstream national-newsmagazine lexicalization: John O''Hara substitutes ''moola'' directly into the fixed English idiom ''for my money'' -> ''for my moola'' without gloss or scare quotes", "analysis_notes": "[1300-A FIRST NATIONAL NEWSMAGAZINE ATTESTATION & IDIOMATIC CALQUE] Cover (n0, May 26, 1941) and p. 72 (n73) verified on physical scan. Seven months after publishing Pal Joey (Oct. 1940, p. 52: ''No matter how much moola I had''), John O''Hara carries ''moola'' into national middlebrow journalism in Newsweek. Proof plate: CERT-1300-022_newsweek_1941_05_26_ohara_for_my_moola.png (", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1941 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-023', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moola', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1941, '1941-06-30', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_newsweek-us_1941-06-30_17_26/page/n59/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-023", "slug": "cert-1300-023", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moola", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1941, "date_display": "30 Jun 1941", "sort_date": "1941-06-30", "short_gloss": "''moola'' in Newsweek (US Edition) (30 Jun 1941)", "source_publication": "Newsweek (US Edition), Vol. 17, No. 26 (New York, June 30, 1941)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_newsweek-us_1941-06-30_17_26/page/n59/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 58, col. 2, ''Entertainment Week'' by John O''Hara (IA leaf n59; cover leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "By all means under the head of entertainment this week past come the performances of a guy named Joe. This Joe, whose name is Joe E. (for Everglades) Lewis, is one of the greatest entertainers in the business. The night after another Joe, surnamed Louis, sent Billy Conn to the floor and lined my pockets with moola, Joe E. Lewis opened at one of the Hollywood night clubs.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1941, "masthead_year": 1941, "linguistic_note": "Championship boxing-wagering & nightclub-comedy convergence: directly links ringside betting winnings on Joe Louis''s 13th-round KO of Billy Conn (Polo Grounds, NYC, June 18, 1941) with nightclub comedian Joe E. Lewis", "analysis_notes": "[1300-A CHAMPIONSHIP BOXING WAGERING & NIGHTCLUB CONVERGENCE] Cover (n0, June 30, 1941) and p. 58 (n59, ''by JOHN O''HARA'') verified on physical scan. Captures ''moola'' as ringside fight-betting cash (''sent Billy Conn to the floor and lined my pockets with moola'') in the immediate aftermath of the historic June 18, 1941 Louis-Conn heavyweight title bout. Proof plate: CERT-1300-023_newsweek_1941_06_30_ohara_louis_conn_boxing_moola.png (", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1941 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-040', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'Moola', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1941, '1941-12-15', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1941-12-15_8_24/page/n34/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-040", "slug": "cert-1300-040", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "Moola", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1941, "date_display": "15 Dec 1941", "sort_date": "1941-12-15", "short_gloss": "''Moola'' in Down Beat (Chicago) (15 Dec 1941)", "source_publication": "Down Beat (Chicago), Vol. 8, No. 24 (Dec. 15, 1941), p. 33 (''Kyser''s Kaycee Date Lures Biggest Moola'' by John Scott Kerns, leaf 34)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1941-12-15_8_24/page/n34/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 8, No. 24, p. 35, Kay Kyser big-band gross headline (IA leaf n34)", "verbatim_quote": "Kyser''s Kaycee Date Lures Biggest Moola / by JOHN SCOTT KERNS / Kansas City—Biggest hunk of dough ever grossed in Kaycee by a band''s one-night stand was carted away by Kay Kyser here, whose matinee performance and dance session at the Muny Auditorium lured in 10,000 persons for $8,300.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1941, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "By Dec. 15, 1941, ''moola'' had graduated into boldface headlines in Down Beat (alongside a second hit on p. 3 of the same issue: Jack Teagarden ''coining moola'' at Chicago''s Hotel Sherman Panther Room), marking full naturalization across Chicago and Kansas City swing circuits.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1941 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-024', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moo-lah', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1941, '1941-12-28', 'https://archive.org/details/per_atlanta-constitution_1941-12-28_74_197/page/n71/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-024", "slug": "cert-1300-024", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moo-lah", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1941, "date_display": "28 Dec 1941", "sort_date": "1941-12-28", "short_gloss": "''moo-lah'' in The Atlanta Constitution (28 Dec 1941)", "source_publication": "The Atlanta Constitution, Vol. 74, No. 197 (Atlanta, GA, Sunday Morning, December 28, 1941)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_atlanta-constitution_1941-12-28_74_197/page/n71/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Sunday Magazine / Fiction supplement, col. 1 (IA leaf n71; masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "\"Well, Captain—I mean, Major—\" O''Brien was leaning contentedly back—\"we are happily met, it seems, this evening. You just about saved our lives. You see, we spent a lot of moo- lah getting around the town, and —\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1941, "masthead_year": 1941, "linguistic_note": "Morpho-prosodic bridge to clipped ''moo'' (OED moo, n.2, 1941 Pittsburgh Courier): hyphenated spelling ''moo-lah'' (matching Winchell''s Feb. 14, 1939 ''moo-lah'') marks explicit speaker reanalysis of ''moo-'' as detachable stressed initial syllable", "analysis_notes": "[1300-A PROSODIC SEGMENTATION BRIDGE TO OED ''moo, n.2'' (1941)] Masthead (n0, Dec. 28, 1941) and fiction supplement (n71) verified on physical scan. Together with Walter Winchell''s Feb. 14, 1939 ''moo-lah'' (cited in Green''s Dictionary of Slang), the hyphenated orthography ''moo- lah'' explains how trochaic /ˈmuː.lɑː/ underwent monosyllabic back-clipping to ''moo'' (''money'') in 1941 African American press usage (OED moo, n.2: 1941 Pittsburgh Courier). Proof plate: CERT-1300-024_atlanta_constitution_1941_12_28_hyphenated_moo_lah.png (", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1941 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-011', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moola', 'rejected_lead', 'antedating', 'agent_verified', 1942, '1942-01-24', 'https://archive.org/details/colliers109janspri/page/n224/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-011", "slug": "cert-1300-011", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moola", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "antedating", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1942, "date_display": "24 Jan 1942", "sort_date": "1942-01-24", "short_gloss": "Rejected lead (antedating): moola (24 Jan 1942)", "source_publication": "Collier''s, Vol. 109 (IA ''colliers109janspri'': Catalog Year 1905 vs. Printed Header January 24, 1942)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/colliers109janspri/page/n224/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 54, col. 1 (IA leaf n224, printed header ''Collier''s for January 24, 1942'')", "verbatim_quote": "Swell place, Miami. Plenty goin'' on. Them big buildings you see, they''re mostly the hotels on the beach. Cost plenty moola, them jernts. But you oughta see the private houses . . .", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1905, "masthead_year": 1942, "linguistic_note": "Rejected Trap A bound-serial metadata misdate (+37-year shift: cataloged as ''1905'' in IA search index, actual issue printed January 24, 1942)", "analysis_notes": "[1300-A TRAP A SERIAL METADATA MISDATE #1: 1905 -> 1942] Surfaces at the very top of chronological IA full-text searches for ''moola'' under year 1905. Visual inspection of p. 54 running header atop leaf n224 (''Collier''s for January 24, 1942'') proves a 37-year bound-serial misdate (paralleling ''esquire140julchic'' stamped 1933 = July 2003, ''mademoiselle91jannewy'' stamped 1935 = Jan. 1985, and ''life26janluce'' stamped 1936 = Jan. 1949). Proof plate: CERT-1300-011_colliers_1905_to_1942_trap_a_misdate.png (", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1942 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-041', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'Moo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1943, '1943-03-15', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1943-03-15_10_6/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-041", "slug": "cert-1300-041", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "Moo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1943, "date_display": "15 Mar 1943", "sort_date": "1943-03-15", "short_gloss": "''Moo'' in Down Beat (Chicago) (15 Mar 1943)", "source_publication": "Down Beat (Chicago), Vol. 10, No. 6 (March 15, 1943), p. 2 (''No Moo Ceiling Repeal, Leaders Bite Their Nails'', leaf 1)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1943-03-15_10_6/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 10, No. 6, p. 2, col. 1, headline ''No Moo Ceiling Repeal, Leaders Bite Their Nails'' (IA leaf n1)", "verbatim_quote": "No Moo Ceiling Repeal, Leaders Bite Their Nails / Baton Boys Stuck Between Form B And 25G Limit / New York—At press time, band-leaders interested in learning what possible revisions congress would make in the present salary limitations were still biting their nails.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1943, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan of clipped ''moo'' (''money, salary'') in a March 15, 1943 Down Beat headline (two years earlier than OED2''s 1945 Frank Fay baseline and Weingarten 1954, and confirming rapid spread after OED3''s 1941 Pittsburgh Courier debut, alongside May 9, 1943 NY Times jive glossary ''coo-for-moo''). [Chief Orchestrator Visual Note: Headline physical ink reads ''No Moo Ceiling / Repeal, Leaders / Bite Their Nails'' cleanly on p. 2 col. 1 (red highlight box sits on adjacent word ''Ceiling''). Antedates OED2''s 1945 baseline by 2 years; postdates OED3''s 1941 Pittsburgh Courier baseline.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1943 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-042', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moo', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1943, '1943-09-15', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1943-09-15_10_18/page/n11/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-042", "slug": "cert-1300-042", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moo", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-B", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1943, "date_display": "15 Sep 1943", "sort_date": "1943-09-15", "short_gloss": "''moo'' in Down Beat (Chicago) (15 Sep 1943)", "source_publication": "Down Beat (Chicago), Vol. 10, No. 18 (Sept. 15, 1943), p. 12 (''Along Melody Row'', leaf 11)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_down-beat_1943-09-15_10_18/page/n11/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 10, No. 18, p. 12, col. 2, ''Along Melody Row'' (IA leaf n11)", "verbatim_quote": "Chappell''s Hy Kanter is pushing Sammy Cahn''s and Julie Styne''s new one called Victory Polka. . . . Joe Dracca is presenting boss Broadway Music Co. with a nice batch of moo. Money maker is Put Your Arms Around Me.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1943, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Confirms unquoted, casual Tin Pan Alley / Broadway music-publishing usage of clipped ''moo'' (''a nice batch of moo'') by Sept. 15, 1943 (the same issue also puns on p. 15: ''gets a great deal of what we quaintly call \"moo\" for doing it''). [Chief Orchestrator Visual Note: Physical ink in col. 2 reads ''Dracca''s presenting boss Broadway Music Co. with a nice batch of moo. Money maker is Put Your Arms Around Me'' cleanly two lines below the red marker box.]", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1943 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-069', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'mazoola', 'rejected_lead', 'rejected_derivation', 'agent_verified', 1943, '1943-10-12', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045462/1943-10-12/ed-1/?sp=13', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-069", "slug": "cert-1300-069", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "mazoola", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "rejected_derivation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1943, "date_display": "12 Oct 1943", "sort_date": "1943-10-12", "short_gloss": "Analytical rejected-derivation record: mazoola -> moolah chronology test (see CERT-1300-076 & CERT-1300-077)", "source_publication": "The Evening Star (Washington, D.C.), Oct. 12, 1943, p. A-13, col. 1 (Ned Nordness AP World Series column) & Victoria Daily Times, Nov. 7, 1944, p. 8 (''mazoola''); cf. Dixon Evening Telegraph, Oct. 30, 1945, p. 7", "source_archive": "Library of Congress (Chronicling America) & Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045462/1943-10-12/ed-1/?sp=13", "page_and_column": "The Evening Star (Washington, D.C.), 12 Oct. 1943, p. A-13, col. 1; Victoria Daily Times, 7 Nov. 1944, p. 8 (IA leaf n8)", "verbatim_quote": "The Redbird management is letting a lot of dough slip through its fingers—but of necessity. Refunds to holders of tickets marked Game No. 6 will be started Saturday morning. And there is all that mazoola gone to waste on tickets already prepared for sale for the seventh game. [Cf. Victoria Daily Times, Nov. 7, 1944, p. 8: ''the junior squad can garner more mazoola than the pros.'']", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1943, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Analytical record testing the reverse-clipping claim (mazoola -> moolah; for positive ink-verified mazoola citations, see CERT-1300-076 and CERT-1300-077): searches of Chronicling America (1770–1945) and Internet Archive periodicals (1800–1946) found no pre-1936 tokens of mazoola (pre-1940 ''mazoo'' hits are line-wrap hyphenations of Kala-mazoo), whereas moolah/Mulah is attested from Feb. 1936 (CERT-1300-025) and reported mazoola printings appear in 1943–1946 (12 Oct. 1943 Evening Star, CERT-1300-076; indexed-only 19 Apr. 1944 Prince Albert Daily Herald p. 6 paraphrasing Jack Park; 7 Nov. 1944 Victoria Daily Times p. 8 ''garner more mazoola than the pros'' alongside Syd Thomas''s likely same-dispatch Canadian Press variant ''offer him more mazoola than the pros'' in the 7 Nov. 1944 Prince Albert Daily Herald p. 5; and 14 May 1946 Lethbridge Herald, CERT-1300-077). While this sequence favors Lilian Mermin Feinsilver''s (1970/1980, pp. 229–230) mazuma + moola blend proposal—or Michael Quinion''s (2007) treatment of mazoola as a mazuma variant—over a reverse clipping from mazoola to moolah, chronology alone does not prove either word-formation mechanism.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1943 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-012', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'moola', 'rejected_lead', 'antedating', 'agent_verified', 1948, '1948-12-08', 'https://archive.org/details/LONGWOOD_COLLEGE_ROTUNDA_1941-10_to_1949-06/page/n902/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-012", "slug": "cert-1300-012", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "moola", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "antedating", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1948, "date_display": "8 Dec 1948", "sort_date": "1948-12-08", "short_gloss": "Rejected lead (antedating): moola (8 Dec 1948)", "source_publication": "The Rotunda, Longwood College (IA ''LONGWOOD_COLLEGE_ROTUNDA_1941-10_to_1949-06'': Catalog Year 1920 vs. Printed Header December 8, 1948)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/LONGWOOD_COLLEGE_ROTUNDA_1941-10_to_1949-06/page/n902/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 3, ''The Sophisticated Rat'' (IA leaf n902, printed header ''WEDNESDAY, DECEMBER 8, 1948'')", "verbatim_quote": "up filthy moola to plunk down for tickets. What the freshman class needs is some benevolent philanthropist.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1920, "masthead_year": 1948, "linguistic_note": "Rejected Trap A serial metadata misdate (+28-year shift: stamped ''1920-01-01'' in IA search metadata, directly contaminating unverified ''c. 1920'' automated date counts)", "analysis_notes": "[1300-A TRAP A SERIAL METADATA MISDATE #2: ''1920'' CUTOFF POISONING] Indexed in IA full-text search with date ''1920-01-01'', creating a false-positive hit directly on TGT-038''s 1920 cutoff year. Visual inspection of the printed running header atop leaf n902 reads ''THE ROTUNDA, WEDNESDAY, DECEMBER 8, 1948''—exposing how serial-founding metadata misdates generate illusory ''c. 1920'' attestations. Proof plate: CERT-1300-012_longwood_rotunda_1920_to_1948_trap_a_misdate.png (", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1948 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-013', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'Moola', 'rejected_lead', 'antedating', 'agent_verified', 1959, '1959-07-16', 'https://archive.org/details/per_gl9scsg3k-gc_pittsburgh-post-gazette_1939-07-16/page/n3/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-013", "slug": "cert-1300-013", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "Moola", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "antedating", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1300-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1959, "date_display": "16 Jul 1959", "sort_date": "1959-07-16", "short_gloss": "Rejected lead (antedating): Moola (16 Jul 1959)", "source_publication": "Pittsburgh Post-Gazette (IA ''per_gl9scsg3k-gc_pittsburgh-post-gazette_1939-07-16'': Catalog Date 1939-07-16 vs. Printed Masthead July 16, 1959)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_gl9scsg3k-gc_pittsburgh-post-gazette_1939-07-16/page/n3/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 3, ''It''s in the Bag, Money That Is, for GOP'' by Pat O''Neill (IA leaf n3)", "verbatim_quote": "Campaign Moola Promised Candidates By Finance Group. Republican candidates moved a step closer to fiscal bliss late yesterday when members of Division C of the GOP Finance Committee met with candidates to talk about spending.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1939, "masthead_year": 1959, "linguistic_note": "Rejected Trap A microfilm reel misdate (+20-year shift: cataloged as July 16, 1939 on IA/Google News, actual issue printed Thursday, July 16, 1959)", "analysis_notes": "[1300-A TRAP A MICROFILM DIGITIZATION MISDATE #3: 1939 -> 1959] Appears to be a major July 16, 1939 daily-newspaper headline (''Campaign Moola Promised Candidates''), but physical scan inspection of the running header atop p. 4 (leaf n3) unmistakably reads ''PITTSBURGH POST-GAZETTE: THURSDAY, JULY 16, 1959'' (corroborated by ''KDKA-TV CH. 2'' listings and 1959 Deborah Kerr divorce story). Proof plate: CERT-1300-013_pittsburgh_post_gazette_1939_to_1959_trap_a_misdate.png (", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1959 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-076', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'mazoola', 'attestation', 'related_form_mazoola', 'agent_verified', 1943, '1943-10-12', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045462/1943-10-12/ed-1/?sp=13', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-076", "slug": "cert-1300-076", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "mazoola", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "related_form_mazoola", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1943, "date_display": "12 Oct 1943", "sort_date": "1943-10-12", "short_gloss": "Positive ''mazoola'' citation (separate lexeme, not ''moolah'') in Ned Nordness AP column, The Evening Star (Washington, D.C.), 12 Oct. 1943, p. A-13", "source_publication": "Ned Nordness (Associated Press), World Series column, The Evening Star (Washington, D.C.), Tuesday, October 12, 1943, p. A-13, col. 1", "source_archive": "Library of Congress (Chronicling America, LCCN sn83045462)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045462/1943-10-12/ed-1/?sp=13", "page_and_column": "Sports, p. A-13, col. 1, ''Nordness (Continued From Page A-12.)'' (LoC sequence sp=13, batch dlc_1young_ver01, tile 0617)", "verbatim_quote": "The Redbird management is letting a lot of dough slip through its fingers—but of necessity. Refunds to holders of tickets marked Game No. 6 will be started Saturday morning. And there is all that mazoola gone to waste on tickets already prepared for sale for the seventh game. Optimism is a wonderful thing.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1943, "masthead_year": 1943, "linguistic_note": "Positive attestation of mazoola, n. (distinct from moolah, n.; linked to analytical derivation record CERT-1300-069).", "analysis_notes": "Separate positive primary-scan record for mazoola, n. (linked to CERT-1300-069; not an attestation of moolah, n.): printed running header (''Evening Star Sports | TUESDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1943—A—13'') and column 1 text visually checked on Library of Congress IIIF tile 0617. Precedes the 1944 Winnipeg Free Press date cited for OED3 mazoola, n. in secondary notes, though both the live OED entry and the exact 1944 Winnipeg date should be verified directly before claiming a formal dictionary antedating, and searched archives do not establish that this AP dispatch was the absolute first printing.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1943 (> 1930): page image not hosted in repository; inspect via Library of Congress Chronicling America link."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1300-077', 'TGT-038', 'moolah', NULL, 'moolah', 'mazoola', 'attestation', 'related_form_mazoola', 'agent_verified', 1946, '1946-05-14', 'https://digitallibrary.uleth.ca/digital/api/collection/herald/id/19300/download', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1300-077", "slug": "cert-1300-077", "case_slug": "moolah", "headword": "moolah", "attested_form": "mazoola", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "related_form_mazoola", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1946, "date_display": "14 May 1946", "sort_date": "1946-05-14", "short_gloss": "Positive ''mazoola'' citation (separate lexeme, not ''moolah'') in Lorimer Fenwick, ''Out on a Limb,'' Lethbridge Herald, 14 May 1946, p. 9, col. 1", "source_publication": "Lorimer Fenwick, ''Out on a Limb,'' The Lethbridge Herald (Lethbridge, Alberta), May 14, 1946, p. 9, col. 1", "source_archive": "University of Lethbridge Digital Library", "source_url": "https://digitallibrary.uleth.ca/digital/api/collection/herald/id/19300/download", "page_and_column": "p. 9, col. 1", "verbatim_quote": "This does not include the amount of mazoola that was bet on the sly with the bookies.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1946, "masthead_year": 1946, "linguistic_note": "Positive attestation of mazoola, n. (distinct from moolah, n.) documenting independent postwar Canadian sports-page usage.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-checked positive citation for mazoola, n. (not moolah, n., and not an antedating): physical ink, date (May 14, 1946), p. 9, col. 1, and Lorimer Fenwick''s byline visually verified on the University of Lethbridge archival scan. Demonstrates independent Canadian sports-column circulation beyond Canadian Press wire items.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1946 (> 1930): page image not redistributed; inspect directly via University of Lethbridge Digital Library."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-301', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'FAIN & Fainne, a ring; rectius ain'', ainne a circle, a ring', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1768, '1768-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bub_gb__B3aET_ihI4C/page/n294/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-301.webp', 49254, '5b72369aab5680199c8dd7a19bd7d6a35eaeadbcebd6cd349f1312348f46cfef', '{"id": "CERT-2200-301", "slug": "cert-2200-301", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "FAIN & Fainne, a ring; rectius ain'', ainne a circle, a ring", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1768, "date_display": "1768", "sort_date": "1768-01-01", "short_gloss": "Earliest printed Irish-English dictionary entry for Irish fainne (''a ring; a circle, a ring'') in Bishop John O''Brien''s Paris Focaloir (1768), thirteen years before Parker''s 1781 ''Fawney Rig''", "source_publication": "John O''Brien, Focaloir Gaoidhilge-Sax-Bhearla, or an Irish-English Dictionary (Paris: Printed by Nicolas-Francis Valleyre, for the Author, 1768 [M. DCC. LXVIII])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bub_gb__B3aET_ihI4C/page/n294/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf (n8, ''M. DCC. LXVIII''), p. 228, col. 1 (''FAIN & Fainne, a ring'', leaf n294) & col. 2 (''FAINNE, ignorance'', leaf n294)", "verbatim_quote": "FAIN & Fainne, a ring; rectius ain'', ainne a circle, a ring. vid. remarks on A. | [col. 2:] FAINNE, ignorance.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1768, "masthead_year": 1768, "linguistic_note": "Ultimate Celtic donor lemma: Old and Middle Irish ainne (''finger-ring'', diminutive/derivative of ain ''ring, circle''; eDIL dil.ie/1351 & dil.ie/21121) acquired prothetic f- via regular lenition reanalysis after the masculine genitive/dative article and prepositions, yielding Classical and Modern Irish fainne [fa:n''e], realized in Munster and southern Irish with strong labial-velar rounding before tense palatalized nasal -nn- as [fo:n''e] — the exact phonetic model for 18th-century St Giles Rookery / Anglo-Irish cant fawney [fo:ni] (''finger-ring'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the 1768 Paris first edition (title page M. DCC. LXVIII, leaf n8; p. 228, leaf n294). Bishop John O''Brien of Cloyne and Ross (County Cork, Munster) records both the spoken vernacular form ''Fainne, a ring'' with its etymological cross-reference to historical vowel-initial ''ainne, a circle, a ring'' (col. 1) and the distinct short-vowel abstract noun ''FAINNE, ignorance'' (< fann ''weak'', col. 2) on the very same page. Published thirteen years before George Parker''s 1781 London cant attestation (''The FAWNEY RIG'' / ''Fawney''), O''Brien''s Munster lexicon anchors the donor form chronologically, semantically, and dialectally.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-301.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1768 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-305', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'The FAWNEY RIG / THE Fawney / going on the Fawney', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1781, '1781-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_a-view-of-society-and-ma_parker-george_1781_2/page/n173/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-305.webp', 53464, '8c98a128007a370a4ed5af1c3e3124a683af5e1acd6ba89a3c6f139aacc7a09b', '{"id": "CERT-2200-305", "slug": "cert-2200-305", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "The FAWNEY RIG / THE Fawney / going on the Fawney", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1781, "date_display": "1781", "sort_date": "1781-01-01", "short_gloss": "Earliest known English attestation of ''The FAWNEY RIG'' and ''Fawney'' in George Parker''s A View of Society and Manners, Vol. II (1781), already documenting wholesale fake-ring supply shops", "source_publication": "George Parker, A View of Society and Manners in High and Low Life, Vol. II (London: Printed for the Author, 1781 [M,DCC,LXXXI])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Eighteenth Century Collections Online / British Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_a-view-of-society-and-ma_parker-george_1781_2/page/n173/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. II Title Page (leaf n0, ''M,DCC,LXXXI'') & pp. 166-167 (leaves n173-n174)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 166:] The FAWNEY RIG. A RING-DROPPER; a fellow who has gotten a woman''s pocket, with a pair of scissars, some thread, a thimble, and a housewife with a ring in it, which he drops for some credulous person to pick up. | [p. 167:] THE Fawney says, \"I dare say some poor woman has lost her pocket. Good gracious! here''s a ring, and her wedding-ring too, for here''s a poesy;\" then reads, \"Love me and leave me not,\" or some such thing. ... Queer as this rig may appear, there is a large shop in London where these kind of rings are sold, for the purpose of going on the Fawney.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1781, "masthead_year": 1781, "linguistic_note": "Foundational Anglo-Irish cant borrowing and typographic control: uppercase roman F in ''The FAWNEY RIG'' (p. 166) and crossbarred italic F in ''Fawney'' (p. 167, twice) stand in unmistakable visual contrast to uncrossed medial long-s in ''scissars'', ''some'', ''person'', ''says'', ''say'', ''lost'', ''shop'', ''these'', ''sold'', and ''purpose'', ruling out any 18th-century long-s confusion with ''Sawney''. Notice too that in Parker (1781) ''THE Fawney'' also denotes the ring-dropper himself by metonymy, and the ring already bears a pre-engraved sentimental posy (''Love me and leave me not'') bought at a wholesale London swag shop (''worth about sixpence... intrinsic value of it is not a halfpenny'')—the identical business model attested 121 years later in Fort Wayne''s 1902 ''\"phoney\" ring peddler'' selling $1-a-dozen brass rings engraved ''Joe to Sue'' (CERT-2200-219).", "analysis_notes": "Re-minted cleanly in the 2200-3XX series with fresh 1800px ECCO scans of Vol. II title leaf n0 (''LONDON: PRINTED FOR THE AUTHOR, M,DCC,LXXXI'') and pp. 166-167 (leaves n173-n174). Establishes OED3''s 1781 baseline for fawney rig and confirms that wholesale manufacture of pre-inscribed gilt-brass confidence rings accompanied the word from its very first appearance in English print.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-305.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1781 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-306', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'FAWNEY RIG / FAWNEY. A ring.', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1796, '1788-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_a-classical-dictionary-o_grose-francis_1796/page/n93/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-306.webp', 49238, 'a2ed2b857d210c7c9fad50d39edfc16fef2948fc7951a7c46e9386775fb8a062', '{"id": "CERT-2200-306", "slug": "cert-2200-306", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "FAWNEY RIG / FAWNEY. A ring.", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1796, "date_display": "1788 / 1796 (vs. 1785 1st ed.)", "sort_date": "1788-01-01", "short_gloss": "Edition-by-edition audit of Francis Grose''s Classical Dictionary of the Vulgar Tongue: absent in 1785 1st ed., ''FAWNEY RIG'' added in 1788 2nd ed., and simplex ''FAWNEY. A ring.'' already added in 1796 3rd ed.", "source_publication": "Francis Grose, A Classical Dictionary of the Vulgar Tongue, 1st ed. (London: S. Hooper, 1785), 2nd ed. (London: S. Hooper, 1788), & 3rd ed. (London: Hooper and Wigstead, 1796)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_a-classical-dictionary-o_grose-francis_1796/page/n93/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "1785 1st ed. Title (leaf n4) & sig. I, s.v. FAU (leaf n81); 1788 2nd ed. Title (leaf n5) & leaf n189; 1796 3rd ed. Title (leaf n0) & sig. M3r, s.v. FEN (leaf n93)", "verbatim_quote": "[1785 1st ed., leaf n81:] FAULKNER, a tumbler, juggler, or shewer of tricks, (cant)... [catchword:] FAY [0 Fawney entries] | [1796 3rd ed., leaf n93:] FAWNEY RIG. A common fraud, thus practised: A fellow drops a brass ring, double gilt, which he picks up before the party meant to be cheated, and to whom he disposes of it for less than its supposed, and ten times more than its real, value. See MONEY DROPPER. FAWNEY. A ring.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1796, "masthead_year": 1796, "linguistic_note": "Tightens the lexicographical chronology of Grose''s Vulgar Tongue across all three 18th-century editions: (1) Grose''s 1785 1st edition (https://archive.org/details/grosevulgartongue/page/n81/mode/1up) omits fawney altogether; (2) Grose''s 1788 2nd edition (https://archive.org/details/b2876190x/page/n189/mode/1up) introduces ''FAWNEY RIG. A common fraud, thus practised: A fellow drops a brass ring, double gilt...''; and (3) the posthumous 1796 3rd edition adds standalone simplex ''FAWNEY. A ring.'' directly below ''FAWNEY RIG''—twenty-seven years before Pierce Egan''s 1823 revision often credited in secondary literature.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified side-by-side across Grose''s 1785 1st ed. (MDCCLXXXV, leaf n81), 1788 2nd ed. (M.DCC.LXXXVIII, leaf n189), and 1796 3rd ed. (1796, leaf n93). Notably, Grose specifies the exact metallurgy (''a brass ring, double gilt'') that persists verbatim into Hotten (1859) and American pitchman usage.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-306.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1796 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-307', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'FAWNEY, a ring.', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1795, '1795-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_a-new-dictionary-of-all-_potter-humphry-tristram_1795/page/n34/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-307.webp', 43142, '5408171b08109113cb715c0a9fe66216dd158418e5d6f5f87a34095d6cc23583', '{"id": "CERT-2200-307", "slug": "cert-2200-307", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "FAWNEY, a ring.", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1795, "date_display": "[1795] (reissued 1796, 1797)", "sort_date": "1795-01-01", "short_gloss": "Humphry Tristram Potter''s A New Dictionary of all the Cant and Flash Languages ([1795] 1st ed.; reissued 1796, 1797), p. 27: earliest standalone dictionary headword ''FAWNEY, a ring.''", "source_publication": "Humphry Tristram Potter, A New Dictionary of all the Cant and Flash Languages, Both Ancient and Modern (London: Printed by W. Mackintosh, and Sold by J. Downes, [1795]; 2nd ed. 1796; 3rd ed. 1797)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Eighteenth Century Collections Online)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_a-new-dictionary-of-all-_potter-humphry-tristram_1795/page/n34/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title Page (leaf n0) & Lexicon p. 27 (leaf n34; also 1796 ed. p. 28, leaf n38)", "verbatim_quote": "FAM, a ring. FAMBLES, gloves. FADGE, a farthing. FASTNER, a warrant. FAULKNER, a tumbler, juggler, or shewer of tricks. FEEDER, a spoon. FAWNEY, a ring. FENCE, a receiver of stolen goods.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1795, "masthead_year": 1795, "linguistic_note": "Earliest standalone dictionary lemmatization of simplex cant noun FAWNEY (''a ring''), isolated from the compound phrase fawney rig and placed alongside older English cant FAM (''a ring'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the [1795] Mackintosh/Downes first edition (p. 27, leaf n34) and corroborated on the 1796 second edition (https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_a-new-dictionary-of-all-_potter-humphry-tristram_1796/page/n38/mode/1up; reissued 1797). Antedates Grose''s 1796 3rd edition by one year, James Hardy Vaux''s 1812/1819 Flash Vocabulary by seventeen to twenty-four years, and Pierce Egan''s 1823 Grose by twenty-eight years on the standalone dictionary headword FAWNEY.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-307.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1795 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-302', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'Ainne, s. f. vulg. fainne, a ring | fainne, g. id., pl. -nidhe, m., a ring; f. an phosta, wedding ring', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1817, '1817-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sanasgaoidhilges00orei/page/n55/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-302.webp', 59280, 'beb0b7096fe9656e4c70eba99584b29f7d0508209b7ae526bd0a6d0945794ac8', '{"id": "CERT-2200-302", "slug": "cert-2200-302", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "Ainne, s. f. vulg. fainne, a ring | fainne, g. id., pl. -nidhe, m., a ring; f. an phosta, wedding ring", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1817, "date_display": "1817 (paired w/ 1904)", "sort_date": "1817-01-01", "short_gloss": "Edward O''Reilly (1817) explicitly labeling fainne the vernacular spoken Irish word for ''ring'' (''vulg. fainne, a ring''), paired with Patrick S. Dinneen (1904) restoring headword fainne (''ring; wedding ring'')", "source_publication": "Edward O''Reilly, Sanas Gaoidhilge-Sagsbhearla: An Irish-English Dictionary (Dublin: Printed by John Barlow, 1817 [reissued 1821]); paired with Rev. Patrick S. Dinneen, Focloir Gaedhilge agus Bearla: An Irish-English Dictionary (Dublin: Irish Texts Society, 1904)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sanasgaoidhilges00orei/page/n55/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "O''Reilly 1817 Title leaf (n6), s.v. AIN (leaf n55, col. 2), & s.v. FAI (leaf n247); Dinneen 1904 Title leaf (n5) & p. 294, col. 1 (leaf n314)", "verbatim_quote": "[O''Reilly 1817, s.v. AIN:] Ainn, s. f. a great circle. Ainne, s. f. vulg. fainne, a ring; the dim. of ain. | [Dinneen 1904, p. 294:] fainne, g. id., pl. -nidhe, m., a ring; f. an phosta, wedding ring (nom. also ainne). fainne, g. id., f., a weakening or lessening; comp. of fann, weak.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1817, "masthead_year": 1817, "linguistic_note": "Lexicographical trap resolution and register confirmation: O''Reilly''s explicit sociolinguistic tag ''vulg. fainne, a ring'' (''vulgo'' = in common spoken Irish) proves that f-initial fainne was the everyday vernacular pronunciation carried by 18th- and 19th-century Irish migrants into London and New York, while Dinneen''s collocation ''fainne an phosta, wedding ring'' matches the inscribed gilt ''wedding-ring'' prop of Parker''s 1781 ''Fawney Rig'' and Mayhew''s 1851 ring-droppers.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on O''Reilly''s 1817 Dublin first edition (leaf n55; verified identical in the 1821 Dublin O''Neil reissue, IA bub_gb_lM5EAAAAcAAJ) and Dinneen''s 1904 Irish Texts Society lexicon (p. 294, leaf n314, https://archive.org/details/anirishenglishd00dinngoog/page/n314/mode/1up). Resolves a classic donor-dictionary pitfall: because O''Reilly alphabetized ''ring'' under etymological vowel-initial Ainne (''vulg. fainne, a ring'') and entered only short-vowel ''fainne, s. f. ignorance'' under letter F (leaf n247), a researcher checking only letter F in O''Reilly would falsely conclude that 1817 Irish lacked fainne ''ring''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-302.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1817 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-308', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'FAWNEY, a finger-ring. FAWNIED, or FAWNEY-FAM''D', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1819, '1819-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/memoirsofjamesha02vaux/page/n182/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-308.webp', 38690, '302511b96ad40d5832b7d382061243907ec4821f25494fcc0c2750034bd0ce20', '{"id": "CERT-2200-308", "slug": "cert-2200-308", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "FAWNEY, a finger-ring. FAWNIED, or FAWNEY-FAM''D", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1819, "date_display": "1819 (comp. 1812)", "sort_date": "1819-01-01", "short_gloss": "James Hardy Vaux''s Memoirs, Vol. II (1819 [compiled 1812]), p. 173: ''FAWNEY, a finger-ring. FAWNIED, or FAWNEY-FAM''D, having one or more rings on the finger''", "source_publication": "James Hardy Vaux, Memoirs of James Hardy Vaux, Written by Himself, Vol. II: A New and Comprehensive Vocabulary of the Flash Language (London: Printed by W. Clowes, 1819 [compiled at Newcastle, NSW, 1812])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/memoirsofjamesha02vaux/page/n182/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. II Title Page (leaf n4, dated 1819) & Vocabulary p. 173 (leaf n182)", "verbatim_quote": "FAWNEY, a finger-ring. FAWNIED, or FAWNEY-FAM''D, having one or more rings on the finger.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1819, "masthead_year": 1819, "linguistic_note": "Demonstrates full morphological productivity of fawney inside early 19th-century London and convict-transport flash argot, generating denominal participial adjectives fawnied and fawney-fam''d (''wearing rings on the hand'', echoed in the 22 Sept. 1860 New York Clipper ''faunied famms'', CERT-2200-104).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the 1819 London first edition (Vol. II, title leaf n4; p. 173, leaf n182; dedication dated Newcastle, New South Wales, 17 Sept. 1812). Notably, Vaux prints FAWNEY immediately after FAKE / FAKEMENT (pp. 171-172), showing fawney and fake cohabiting the exact same London pickpocket and swindler lexicon.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-308.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1819 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-309', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'FAWNEY. A ring. FAWNEY RIG | he sports a diamond forney on his little finger', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1823, '1823-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/groseeganvulgartongue/page/n132/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-309.webp', 56920, '175227db4aec41e75b0f2a7f5568a849742b7ec4281f1f8ce46aa70f32bcf613', '{"id": "CERT-2200-309", "slug": "cert-2200-309", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "FAWNEY. A ring. FAWNEY RIG | he sports a diamond forney on his little finger", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1823, "date_display": "1823 & 1830 (1887 ed.)", "sort_date": "1823-01-01", "short_gloss": "Pierce Egan''s Grose (1823) pairing ''FAWNEY'' & ''FAWNEY RIG'', alongside Egan''s Finish to Tom, Jerry, and Logic (1830 [1887 ed., p. 236]) spelling [fo:ni] non-rhotically as ''a diamond forney''!", "source_publication": "Pierce Egan, Grose''s Classical Dictionary of the Vulgar Tongue, Revised and Corrected (London: Sherwood, Neely, and Jones, 1823); paired with Pierce Egan, The Finish to the Adventures of Tom, Jerry, and Logic (London: Reeves & Turner, 1887 [1st ed. London: G. Virtue, 1830]), p. 236", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/groseeganvulgartongue/page/n132/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Egan 1823 Title leaf (n8) & s.v. FAY (leaf n132); Egan Finish (1887 ed. of 1830 text) Title leaf (n4) & p. 236 (leaf n291)", "verbatim_quote": "[Egan 1823, leaf n132:] FAWNEY. A ring. FAWNEY RIG. A common fraud, thus practised:—a fellow drops a brass ring, double gilt... | [Egan 1830 (1887 ed., p. 236):] It is rather singular to remark that, although Any-thing TOMMY does not wash his face once a week, nor comb out his hair in the course of a fortnight, yet his pride is so great, that he sports a diamond forney on his little finger; and in his cameza, which is generally as black as soot, a diamond broach, to give it value.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1823, "masthead_year": 1823, "linguistic_note": "Smoking-gun orthoepic proof for both the vowel of fawney and the origin of the 20th-century ''Forney jewelry'' folk-etymology: in non-rhotic London (and later non-rhotic New York City) English, post-vocalic <or> before a consonant or in eye-dialect represents pure long open-mid [o:], identical to <aw> (just as 1842 New York Whip printed ''farnies'' and Matsell 1859 misread manuscript ''Farney'' as ''PARNEY''). Egan''s 1830 ''diamond forney'' proves that cant speakers and writers spontaneously spelled [fo:ni] (''ring'') as <forney> more than a century before Walter Winchell and H. L. Mencken''s informants reinterpreted non-rhotic [fo:ni] as an eponymous jeweler named ''Forney''!", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on Egan''s 1823 Grose (title leaf n8; leaf n132) and Egan''s Finish to the Adventures of Tom, Jerry, and Logic (1st ed. 1830; verified on Reeves & Turner 1887 scan https://archive.org/details/finishtoadventur00eganuoft/page/n291/mode/1up, p. 236, leaf n291). Notice also the semantic collocation ''diamond forney'' (''diamond ring''), directly anticipating Matsell''s 1859 ''spark-fawney'' (''diamond ring'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-309.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1823 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-310', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'fawneys', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1827, '1827-07-12', 'https://www.oldbaileyonline.org/record/t18270712-33', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-310.webp', 71664, 'c73bb24aa1d909277edbe586c77008b08cf96ffcf0fe71ca631615d6f0de4d03', '{"id": "CERT-2200-310", "slug": "cert-2200-310", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "fawneys", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1827, "date_display": "12 Jul 1827", "sort_date": "1827-07-12", "short_gloss": "Sworn Old Bailey courtroom testimony (12 July 1827, Case 1335: Taylor & Rogers) recording spoken London burglary cant: ''It is all right for the fawneys and the sneezers, meaning the rings and snuff-box''", "source_publication": "The Proceedings of the Old Bailey (Sessions'' Paper, City of London and County of Middlesex, Sixth Session, 12 July 1827, Printed by J. Booth / T. Keys)", "source_archive": "Old Bailey Proceedings Online / HathiTrust Harvard Law School Scan", "source_url": "https://www.oldbaileyonline.org/record/t18270712-33", "page_and_column": "Sixth Session Masthead (p. 499), Indictment Start (p. 531, col. 2), & Witness Testimony (p. 532, col. 2, ref. t18270712-33)", "verbatim_quote": "ABRAHAM LEVISON. I am a working-jeweller. I was near Mr. Lawton''s shop, and saw Keys at the corner of Union-street ... I crossed the road, and heard a conversation between Taylor, Rogers, and the third man—Taylor said, \"It is all right for the fawneys and the sneezers,\" meaning the rings and snuff-box; I crossed over into Good''s shop, and soon after he accomplished his purpose; we ran out and took him.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1827, "masthead_year": 1827, "linguistic_note": "Earliest verbatim courtroom record of plural fawneys in live, unscripted London street speech outside cant glossaries—recorded by shorthand court reporter H. Buckler from a working jeweller who overheard three burglars casing 18 gold rings in a Bishopsgate jeweller''s window.", "analysis_notes": "Re-minted cleanly in the 2200-3XX series from the original 1827 J. Booth Sessions'' Paper scan (p. 499 masthead ''THURSDAY, the 12th of JULY, 1827'', p. 531 indictment for ''stealing 18 gold rings, value 4l., and 1 silver snuff-box, value 20s.'', and p. 532 col. 2 italic ''fawneys and the sneezers''). Antedates OED3''s November 1827 London Morning Chronicle newspaper baseline (''fawney-dropping'') by four months.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-310.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1827 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-102', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'Fawney your famms', 'attestation', 'transatlantic_cant_bridge', 'agent_verified', 1843, '1843-07-22', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_spirit-of-the-times_1843-07-22_13_21/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-102.webp', 92334, '6c40e23b6b18970e24a082e6db81abdbfad98b0c20f43ae1153375af2ad35dd1', '{"id": "CERT-2200-102", "slug": "cert-2200-102", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "Fawney your famms", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "transatlantic_cant_bridge", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1843, "date_display": "22 Jul 1843", "sort_date": "1843-07-22", "short_gloss": "Early antebellum New York sporting-press circulation of flash cant ''Fawney your famms'' (''ring your hands'') in Spirit of the Times", "source_publication": "Spirit of the Times (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_spirit-of-the-times_1843-07-22_13_21/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 13, No. 21, p. 245, col. 1 (leaf 4)", "verbatim_quote": "''Fawney your famms, George!'' cried Ainsworth, becoming suddenly white as a sheet. ''Blow me, if I stand any such nonsense. Why he''d eat the whole lot of us in less time than I could fake a cly?''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1843, "masthead_year": 1843, "linguistic_note": "Flash verbal pun ''Fawney your famms'' (''ring [= wring] your hands'', from cant fawney ''ring'' + famms ''hands''), demonstrating active comprehension of Anglo-Irish flash cant in New York''s flagship sporting newspaper alongside the Oct. 1, 1842 New York Whip & Satirist (''farnies'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on leaf 4 (running masthead ''SPIRIT OF THE TIMES. NEW YORK, SATURDAY, JULY 22, 1843'', p. 245, col. 1) in a satirical sketch portraying William Harrison Ainsworth speaking flash thieves'' cant (''fake a cly'', ''Fawney your famms'') in New York print 16 years before Matsell''s 1859 Vocabulum.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-102.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1843 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-320', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '"Oh no," said Phonny [OCR: Phoney] | First ballot, Foney 107 [for Clerk John W. Forney]', 'rejected_lead', 'proper_name_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1850, '1850-01-12', 'https://archive.org/details/newport-mercury-1850-01-12/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-320.webp', 56922, '01815e167bc168ed990463f7171681bf672b1641f73ed950852f0c99d35ef259', '{"id": "CERT-2200-320", "slug": "cert-2200-320", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "\"Oh no,\" said Phonny [OCR: Phoney] | First ballot, Foney 107 [for Clerk John W. Forney]", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "proper_name_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1850, "date_display": "12 Jan 1850 & c. 1853", "sort_date": "1850-01-12", "short_gloss": "Trap C & B proper-name mirages: Newport Mercury (12 Jan. 1850, p. 2) misprinting Clerk candidate John W. Forney as ''Foney 107'', and Jacob Abbott''s Stuyvesant (c. 1853, p. 122) printing ''Phonny'' (< Alphonzo, OCR ''Phoney'')", "source_publication": "The Newport Mercury (Newport, RI), Vol. 89, 12 Jan. 1850, p. 2, col. 2; paired with Jacob Abbott, Stuyvesant: A Franconia Story (New York: Harper & Brothers, copyright 1853 / 1881 [1904 printing]), p. 122", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/newport-mercury-1850-01-12/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Newport Mercury Masthead p. 1 (leaf n0) & 31st Congress tally p. 2, col. 2 (leaf n1); Abbott Stuyvesant Copyright Verso (leaf n9), Dramatis Personae (leaf n16), & p. 122 (leaf n127)", "verbatim_quote": "[Newport Mercury, 12 Jan. 1850, p. 2:] 31st CONGRESS ... The balloting for clerk was then resumed. First ballot, Foney 107, Campbell 94; second, Foney 107, Campbell 95; third, Foney 106, Campbell 102. No election. ... [Jan. 10:] House.—First ballot for Clerk, Foney 102, Foote 103, scattering 10. | [Abbott, Stuyvesant, p. 122:] \"Oh no,\" said Phonny, \"I can guess near enough.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1850, "masthead_year": 1850, "linguistic_note": "Documents two major Trap C proper-name hazards that masquerade as antebellum 1850-1853 attestations of foney and phoney in full-text digital repositories: (1) In the Jan. 12, 1850 Newport Mercury, the Rhode Island compositor repeatedly dropped the <r> in the surname of Democratic nominee Col. John Wien Forney (1817-1881) during the deadlocked 31st U.S. House Clerkship balloting, printing literal letterpress ''Foney 107... Foney 102''; and (2) In Jacob Abbott''s popular juvenile novel Stuyvesant: A Franconia Story (copyright 1853), the boy character ''ALPHONZO, commonly called Phonny'' (dramatis personae, leaf n16) has his nickname ''Phonny'' misindexed by OCR on p. 122 as ''Phoney'' (nn -> ne).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on The Newport Mercury (Saturday Morning, Jan. 12, 1850, masthead leaf n0; p. 2 col. 2 leaf n1) and Jacob Abbott''s Stuyvesant (https://archive.org/details/stuyvesantfranco00abbo/page/n127/mode/1up, copyright 1853/1881 leaf n9; p. 122 leaf n127). Together with rare census surname entries (Foney / Fawney / Phoney in 19th-century city directories and muster rolls), these proper-noun homographs account for virtually every non-OCR pre-1890 capital-initial hit.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-320.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1850 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-311', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'Fawney dropping line / fawneys are rings / fawney dropping', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1851, '1851-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/londonlabourlond01mayhuoft/page/n400/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-311.webp', 60384, '0d7d7b609463f428616cf5b60bf9a2b70fad936ba04f6eea5c06fcce29c21047', '{"id": "CERT-2200-311", "slug": "cert-2200-311", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "Fawney dropping line / fawneys are rings / fawney dropping", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1851, "date_display": "1851", "sort_date": "1851-01-01", "short_gloss": "Henry Mayhew''s London Labour and the London Poor, Vol. I (1851, pp. 389-390) uniting ''the Fawney dropping line; (fawneys are rings)'' with 7d.-a-dozen V.R.-stamped ''Belchers'' pitched on a Bridge wager", "source_publication": "Henry Mayhew, London Labour and the London Poor, Vol. I: The London Street-Folk (London: 1851 [verified on Charles Griffin & Co. unchanged plate issue])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (University of Toronto)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/londonlabourlond01mayhuoft/page/n400/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. I Title Page (leaf n8) & ''Of the Street-Sellers of Rings and Sovereigns for Wagers,'' pp. 389-390 (leaves n400-n401)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 389:] \"Sometimes, when travelling on foot to a race or fair, I do a little in the Fawney dropping line;\" (fawneys are rings;) \"but that is a dangerous game, I never did it but two or three times.\" | [p. 390:] \"The best sort of rings for fawney dropping is the Belchers. They are a good thick looking ring, and have the crown and V. R. stamped upon them. They are 7d. a dozen. ... ''Now, ladies and gentlemen,'' says I, ''I am not permitted to remain more than ten minutes in one spot. I have rings to sell to decide a wager recently made between two sporting noblemen...''\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1851, "masthead_year": 1851, "linguistic_note": "Captures the exact institutional merger of covert ring-dropping (''fawney dropping'', attested in the London Morning Chronicle Nov. 1827 & 6 Mar. 1830) and open-air street-pitchman patter (''selling rings and sovereigns for wagers'', i.e. Hotten''s 1859 ''fawney bouncing''): the same itinerant racecourse and fairground patterer buys 7d.-a-dozen brass ''Belcher'' rings stamped with a counterfeit hallmark (''the crown and V. R.'') from a Kent Street swag shop and works both games.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on Vol. I, pp. 389-390 (leaves n400-n401; originally issued in two-pence weekly parts and Vol. I in 1851, reissued from identical plates by Griffin, Bohn & Co. / Charles Griffin & Co. in 1861). Notice also Mayhew''s documentation on p. 390 of ''little Ikey'' running the pawnshop-ticket ring-switching game (''present a real gold ring—obtain a loan of 5s., and would present the ticket to the bystanders'')—the exact confidence trick reported forty-six years later in St. Louis as ''learned it was a \"phony\"'' (4 May 1897, CERT-2200-210).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-311.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1851 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-312', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'FAWNEY / FAWNEY BOUNCING / pinched a swell of a fawney... cooter for the sawney', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1859, '1859-04-01', 'https://archive.org/details/vulgartongueagl00anglgoog/page/n53/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-312.webp', 65690, '78d08c223d7af3b33a8a4cd8f42439c78c857f7f8e2ea16ed384aebc31bba0c9', '{"id": "CERT-2200-312", "slug": "cert-2200-312", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "FAWNEY / FAWNEY BOUNCING / pinched a swell of a fawney... cooter for the sawney", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1859, "date_display": "1859 (1st ed. 1857)", "sort_date": "1859-04-01", "short_gloss": "John Camden Hotten (1859, p. 37) on ''FAWNEY'' & ''FAWNEY BOUNCING'', paired with Ducange Anglicus''s The Vulgar Tongue (2nd ed. 1859 [1st ed. 1857], p. 45)—unmasking the exact British flash specimen plagiarized by Matsell (1859)!", "source_publication": "Ducange Anglicus [pseud.], The Vulgar Tongue: A Glossary of Slang, Cant, and Flash Words and Phrases, Used in London, from 1839 to 1859, 2nd ed. (London: Bernard Quaritch, 1859; 1st ed. 1857), pp. 17, 45 (leaves 25, 53)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/vulgartongueagl00anglgoog/page/n53/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Hotten 1859 Title leaf (n8) & p. 37 (leaf n144); Ducange Anglicus 1859 Title leaf (n5), p. 13 (''*FAWNEY—a ring'', leaf n21), & ''Specimens of Flash'' p. 45 (leaf n53)", "verbatim_quote": "[Hotten 1859, p. 37:] FAWNEY, a finger ring. FAWNEY BOUNCING, selling rings for a wager... offering brass, double gilt rings, instead of sovereigns. FAWNEY, or FAWNEY RIG, ring dropping. | [Ducange Anglicus 1859, p. 45:] I speeled to the crib, where I found Jim had been pulling down sawney for grub. He cracked a case last night and fenced the swag. He told me as Bill had flimped a yack and pinched a swell of a fawney, he sent the yack to church and got three finnuffs and a cooter for the sawney.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1859, "masthead_year": 1859, "linguistic_note": "Textual genealogy breakthrough resolving the relationship between British flash books and George W. Matsell''s New York Vocabulum (May 1859): Ducange Anglicus''s ''Specimens of Flash'' (London, 1st ed. 1857; 2nd ed. 1859, p. 45) contains the word-for-word source text that Matsell localized into New York cant on p. 105 of Vocabulum! Even more remarkably, Ducange Anglicus''s London printer misset the final word of the paragraph as ''cooter for the sawney'' (confusing sawney ''bacon'' four lines above with fawney ''ring''), whereas Matsell (1859, p. 105) recognized the London misprint, upgraded ''a fawney'' to ''a spark-fawney'' (''diamond ring''), and actively corrected Ducange''s erroneous final ''sawney'' back to ''for the fawney''!", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified across Hotten''s 1859 first edition (https://archive.org/details/hottenmodernslang/page/n144/mode/1up, title leaf n8, p. 37 leaf n144) and Ducange Anglicus''s 1859 Quaritch edition (title leaf n5, glossary leaf n21 ''*FAWNEY—a ring. Th. 1839'', and dialogue p. 45 leaf n53). Proves that Matsell did not mechanically copy an uncomprehended British word on p. 105: Matsell knew fawney / spark-fawney well enough in 1859 New York to fix Ducange Anglicus''s typo.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-312.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1859 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-313', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'ATTLEBOROUGH / PARNEY. A ring. / spark-fawney / fawney / fawneys', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1859, '1859-05-23', 'https://www.loc.gov/item/2019713446/', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-313.webp', 37860, 'fe80e6bf5833fab6c07c7cea4a5f36682762c491c7a0aef4b1f949ce948c757b', '{"id": "CERT-2200-313", "slug": "cert-2200-313", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "ATTLEBOROUGH / PARNEY. A ring. / spark-fawney / fawney / fawneys", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1859, "date_display": "23 May 1859 (1st ed.)", "sort_date": "1859-05-23", "short_gloss": "Genuine dated 1859 first edition of George W. Matsell''s Vocabulum (verified on the May 23, 1859 SDNY copyright-deposit title leaf & UMich 1859 1st ed.): full audit of ATTLEBOROUGH, FAWNEY, PARNEY, SAWNEY, and SPARK FAWNEY", "source_publication": "George W. Matsell, Vocabulum; or, The Rogue''s Lexicon (1st ed., New-York: Published by George W. Matsell & Co., Proprietors of the National Police Gazette, No. 3 Tryon Row, 1859)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Rare Book Copyright Deposit & HathiTrust / Google Books (Univ. of Michigan 1859 1st Ed.)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/item/2019713446/", "page_and_column": "LoC SDNY Copyright Deposit Title Leaf recto & verso (rbccpmat.copy0034, imgs 2139-2140, ''1859.'' & MS ''Filed May 23. 1859''); UMich 1859 1st ed. (mdp.39015050778318 / euWXIdL12dEC) pp. 9, 31-32, 64, 77, 84, 105, & 126", "verbatim_quote": "[1859 Title Recto:] BY GEORGE W. MATSELL, SPECIAL JUCTICE [sic], CHIEF OF POLICE, ETC., ETC. NEW-YORK: PUBLISHED BY GEORGE W. MATSELL & CO. ... No. 3 Tryon Row. 1859. [Verso MS:] Filed May 23. 1859 | [p. 9:] ATTLEBOROUGH. Not genuine; made to imitate. At the town of Attleborough jewelry is manufactured from the baser metals, or so alloyed as to deceive those who are not good judges of the genuine article. | [p. 64:] PARNEY. A ring. | [p. 105:] Bill had flimped a yack, and pinched a swell of a spark-fawney, and had sent the yack to church, and got half a century and a finnif for the fawney.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1859, "masthead_year": 1859, "linguistic_note": "Complete lexical inventory of the genuine May 23, 1859 first edition of Matsell''s Vocabulum across every target entry: (1) Under letter F (pp. 31-32), headword FAWNEY is OMITTED; instead, p. 64 prints ''PARNEY. A ring.'' (a compositor''s P/F misreading of manuscript ''Farney'', non-rhotic NYC spelling of [fo:ni], cf. Egan 1830 ''forney'' and 1842 NY Whip ''farnies'', contaminated by Romani parney ''rain''); (2) Under letter S, p. 77 prints ''SAWNEY. Bacon; fat pork.'' and p. 84 prints ''SPARK. A diamond. \"Flash your spark,\" show your diamond'' (with NO ''SPARK FAWNEY'' headword under S); (3) In ''Examples'' (p. 105), Matsell prints ''pulling down sawney for grub'' (''stealing some bacon''), ''pinched a swell of a spark-fawney'' (''robbed a well-dressed gentleman of a diamond ring''), and ''got half a century and a finnif for the fawney'' (''$55 for the ring''); (4) In the closing flash poem (p. 126), Matsell prints plural ''The chips, the fawneys, chatty-feeders''; and (5) On p. 9, Matsell enters ''ATTLEBOROUGH. Not genuine; made to imitate'' (with ZERO ''Forney'' entry anywhere in the book).", "analysis_notes": "Resolves the long-standing bibliographic problem surrounding Matsell''s Vocabulum: whereas the widely cited Internet Archive Cornell scan (cu31924073798740, CERT-2200-314) is an undated c. 1877 R. Worthington reissue, primary inspection of the Southern District of New York copyright deposit title leaf in the Library of Congress (item 2019713446, filed May 23, 1859) and the intact dated 1859 University of Michigan copy (https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015050778318 / https://books.google.com/books?id=euWXIdL12dEC) proves that all 130 lexicon and appendix stereotype plates were cast in May 1859, and uncovers a diagnostic first-state title-page misprint (''SPECIAL JUCTICE'') beneath Matsell''s name alongside the intact printed date ''1859.''", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-313.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1859 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-201', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'FAWNEY / FAWNEY BOUNCING / FAWNEY RIG', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1859, '1859-06-30', 'https://archive.org/details/hottenmodernslang/page/n143/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-201.webp', 55202, '2106808bc5428ca1a488086b00ed2ffd2b8a367438dcf3f364183ea5c304649d', '{"id": "CERT-2200-201", "slug": "cert-2200-201", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "FAWNEY / FAWNEY BOUNCING / FAWNEY RIG", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1859, "date_display": "30 Jun 1859", "sort_date": "1859-06-30", "short_gloss": "First edition of Hotten documenting the shift from solo ''fawney rig'' ring-dropping to street-pitch ''fawney bouncing'' of gilt-brass rings", "source_publication": "John Camden Hotten, A Dictionary of Modern Slang, Cant, and Vulgar Words (1st ed., London: John Camden Hotten)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/hottenmodernslang/page/n143/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Preface p. vi (dated 30 June 1859, leaf 15) & p. 37 (leaf 144)", "verbatim_quote": "FAWNEY, a finger ring. FAWNEY BOUNCING, selling rings for a wager. This practice is founded upon the old tale of a gentleman laying a wager that if he was to offer \"real gold sovereigns\" at a penny a piece at the foot of London Bridge, the English public would be too incredulous to buy. ... This tale the FAWNEY BOUNCERS tell the public, only offering brass, double gilt rings, instead of sovereigns. FAWNEY, or FAWNEY RIG, ring dropping.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1859, "masthead_year": 1859, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish cant fawney (''finger-ring'', from Irish fainne ''ring'') extended from covert pavement ring-dropping (fawney rig, 1781) to open-air pitchman patter selling double-gilt brass rings from a tray (fawney bouncing, 1859).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the 1859 London first edition (Preface dated Piccadilly, June 30th, 1859). Establishes the critical occupational shift inside Victorian street cant: whereas the 18th-century ''fawney-dropper'' covertly planted a solitary ring in the street, the mid-19th-century ''fawney bouncer'' became a stationary street pitchman working a crowd from a tray with an elaborate confidence spiel about sacrificing genuine gold for pennies—the direct performance ancestor of the American street-corner ''fawny man'' and ''phoney man''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-201.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1859 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-319', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'THE PHUNNY PHELLOW | THE PHUNNY MAN', 'rejected_lead', 'phonetic_orthography_false_friend', 'agent_verified', 1859, '1859-09-15', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_new-york-times_the-new-york-times_1859-09-15_8_2492/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-319.webp', 54132, '0ceb6abc9b42a2c60a1b14fcc9e223b9e02ca3987832e84aa11031ba02ca4f9f', '{"id": "CERT-2200-319", "slug": "cert-2200-319", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "THE PHUNNY PHELLOW | THE PHUNNY MAN", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "phonetic_orthography_false_friend", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1859, "date_display": "15 Sep 1859 & 1876", "sort_date": "1859-09-15", "short_gloss": "Phonetic-humor orthography false friend: New-York Times (15 Sept. 1859, p. 3 ''THE PHUNNY PHELLOW'') and Josh Billings (1876, p. 329 ''THE PHUNNY MAN''), calibrating 1862 ''phoney [= funny] scrape''", "source_publication": "The New-York Times, Vol. 8, No. 2492 (15 Sept. 1859), p. 3, col. 4; paired with Josh Billings [Henry Wheeler Shaw], The Complete Works of Josh Billings (New York: G. W. Carleton / G. W. Dillingham, 1876 [copyright 1874-1876]), p. 329", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_new-york-times_the-new-york-times_1859-09-15_8_2492/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "NYT 15 Sept. 1859 p. 3 running header & col. 4 (leaf n2); Josh Billings Complete Works Title leaf (n8) & p. 329 (leaf n332)", "verbatim_quote": "[New-York Times, 15 Sept. 1859, p. 3:] NEW PUBLICATIONS. READY TO-DAY, AT 12 O''CLOCK, THE PHUNNY PHELLOW. PRICE, 4 CENTS. [repeated 15 times] | [Josh Billings, 1876, p. 329:] THE PHUNNY MAN. Thare iz hardly ennything that a man iz so vain ov az the humor that iz in him. The phunny man iz seldum an humorist, and never a wit.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1859, "masthead_year": 1859, "linguistic_note": "Calibrates two distinct pre-1890 traps involving <ph-> = /f/ eye-dialect for English funny (''comical, queer, strange''): (1) 1850s-1880s ''Phunny Phellow'' comic orthography (New York publisher Ross & Tousey''s 1859 monthly The Phunny Phellow, where Street & Smith''s young humorists wrote, and Josh Billings''s cacographic phool, phellow, phunny man), which explains both the 1885 Pittsburg Chronicle joke-exchange persona ''the Phony man'' (CERT-2200-225) and Confederate Pvt. Benjamin F. Moody''s Apr. 25, 1862 rural Georgia letter ''a phoney [= funny] scrape and also a serious one, too'' (CERT-2200-105); and (2) Edwin Newman''s casual popular conjecture (Strictly Speaking, 1974 / As Phony as a Three-Dollar Bill, https://archive.org/details/asphonyasathreed1974newm) deriving phony from funny (''funny money''), which fails phonologically (/V/ != /oU/) and ignores the documented fawney -> fauny/foney -> phoney cant chain.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on leaf n2 of the Sept. 15, 1859 New-York Times (masthead header ''The New-York Times. Thursday, September 15, 1859.'') and leaf n332 (p. 329) of Josh Billings''s Complete Works (https://archive.org/details/unset0000unse_k2u2/page/n332/mode/1up).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-319.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1859 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-104', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'fawnies / faunied famms', 'attestation', 'transatlantic_cant_bridge', 'agent_verified', 1860, '1860-09-22', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_the-new-york-clipper_1860-09-22_8_23/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-104.webp', 148188, 'ac4856107f5f23ffc7c1a61ebb5ad00f5181d5acab7acfb426b3dceed28f1055', '{"id": "CERT-2200-104", "slug": "cert-2200-104", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "fawnies / faunied famms", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "transatlantic_cant_bridge", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1860, "date_display": "22 Sep 1860", "sort_date": "1860-09-22", "short_gloss": "Footnoted glossary entry ''34, Fawnies—Rings'' and participial ''faunied famms'' in The New York Clipper (Sept. 22, 1860)", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Illinois Digital Newspaper Collections)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_the-new-york-clipper_1860-09-22_8_23/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 8, No. 23, p. 183, col. 1 (leaf 7; front masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "The fogles(33) and the fawnies(34) prime I did put up the spout,(35) ... Vith strummel faked(44) and faunied famms(45) among the crowd I pressed ... 34, Fawnies—Rings. 35, Pawned. ... 45, Ringed hands.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1860, "masthead_year": 1860, "linguistic_note": "Simultaneous orthographic alternation between <fawnies> (n. pl., glossed ''Rings'') and <faunied> (ppl. adj. in ''faunied famms'', glossed ''Ringed hands''), showing low-back vowel spelling variability (<aw> ~ <au>) in New York theatrical/sporting print.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on front masthead (leaf 0: Vol. VIII, No. 23, New York, Saturday, Sept. 22, 1860) and p. 183, col. 1 (leaf 7), printing ''The Literary Thief'' with numbered flash-cant footnotes for American sporting and variety readers.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-104.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1860 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-105', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'phoney scrape', 'hypothesis', 'orthographic_variant_audit', 'agent_verified', 1977, '1862-04-25', 'https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-968x.2011.01247.x', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-2200-105", "slug": "cert-2200-105", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "phoney scrape", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "orthographic_variant_audit", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1977, "date_display": "25 Apr 1862 [pub. 1977]", "sort_date": "1862-04-25", "short_gloss": "Philological audit of OED3''s bracketed [1862] Civil War letter (''phoney scrape''): naive Southern phonetic spelling of ''funny scrape''", "source_publication": "Pvt. Benjamin Franklin Moody (35th Georgia Infantry, C.S.A.), letter of Apr. 25, 1862, in Mills Lane, ed., ''Dear Mother: Don''t Grieve about Me. If I Get Killed, I''ll Only Be Dead'' (Savannah: Beehive Press, 1977)", "source_archive": "Published Documentary Collection (Beehive Press, 1977; cf. Paul S. Cohen, Transactions of the Philological Society 109.1 [2011]: 1-11)", "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-968x.2011.01247.x", "page_and_column": "p. 116, col. 2 (letter dated April 25, 1862, Yorktown siege lines, Virginia)", "verbatim_quote": "They keep skirmishing along the line. I will tell you of a phoney scrape and also a serious one, too.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1977, "masthead_year": 1977, "linguistic_note": "Syntactic and semantic opposition (''a phoney scrape and also a serious one, too'') proves ''phoney'' stands as the direct antonym of ''serious'' (= funny ''amusing, comical, non-lethal''), reflecting rural Georgia Piedmont semi-phonetic spelling with labialized back-rounded vowel quality before /n/ (cf. Kurath & McDavid 1961) and hypercorrect <ph-> for /f/, not underworld cant phoney (''counterfeit, bogus'').", "analysis_notes": "Audited against OED3 (phoney, adj. and n., Etymology note citing ''[1862 B. Moody Let. 25 Apr. in M. Lane Dear Mother (1977) 116/2]''), Paul S. Cohen (2011), Dave Wilton (Wordorigins.org, 2022), and regimental records for Pvt. Benjamin Franklin Moody, Co. F/G, 35th Georgia Volunteer Infantry (killed June 1862 in the Seven Days Battles; cf. John J. Fox III, Red Clay to Richmond, 2005). Three independent tests exclude genuine cant ''phoney'': (1) binary contrast with ''a serious one, too'' requires ''funny'' (''comical/lighthearted skirmish vs. deadly engagement''), whereas ''counterfeit skirmish'' lacks military sense here; (2) Moody''s letters exhibit vernacular phonetic spellings throughout; and (3) exhaustive multi-archive sweeps across 1862-1889 newspapers, court dockets, and sporting weeklies confirm a 30-year attestation vacuum before 1890/1892. Properly bracketed by OED3 and excluded as an antedating of phoney ''bogus''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1977 (> 1930): scan omitted under pre-1931 public-domain gate; consult external source_url."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-106', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'fawney', 'attestation', 'transatlantic_cant_bridge', 'agent_verified', 1867, '1867-11-26', 'https://panewsarchive.psu.edu/lccn/sn83025925/1867-11-26/ed-1/seq-7/', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-106.webp', 70474, 'e849adea331ddf529db503ebdcffb6925a09fd25173472e13b01f8b0b5bc1745', '{"id": "CERT-2200-106", "slug": "cert-2200-106", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "fawney", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "transatlantic_cant_bridge", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1867, "date_display": "26 Nov 1867", "sort_date": "1867-11-26", "short_gloss": "Post-Civil War Philadelphia daily newspaper report documenting active thieves'' cant ''a ring a \"fawney\"'' (Nov. 26, 1867)", "source_publication": "The Daily Evening Telegraph (Philadelphia, PA)", "source_archive": "Pennsylvania Newspaper Archive (Penn State University Libraries)", "source_url": "https://panewsarchive.psu.edu/lccn/sn83025925/1867-11-26/ed-1/seq-7/", "page_and_column": "Vol. 8, No. 126, p. 7, col. 1 (seq. 7)", "verbatim_quote": "breaking open a till ''hot-snaking,'' it was once ''filkin;'' a handy pickpocket a ''gun,'' a knife a ''chin,'' a head a ''blessy,'' a ring a ''fawney.'' This last is old, as also ''ten commandments'' for fingers", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1867, "masthead_year": 1867, "linguistic_note": "Pairs long-standing Anglo-Irish cant fawney (''ring'') with newly emerging postbellum American criminal lexicon (''gun'' for pickpocket, ''hot-snaking'' for till-tapping), confirming continuous transmission in urban Eastern Seaboard police/underworld circles.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on page 7 running masthead (''THE DAILY EVENING TELEGRAPH. PHILADELPHIA, TUESDAY, NOVEMBER 26, 1867'') and col. 1 physical ink. Demonstrates that ''fawney'' remained recognizable underworld currency in major American cities alongside newer postbellum criminal argot.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-106.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1867 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-322', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'David Roby ... 1 95 [OCR: David phoney] | giving money to the masters [OCR: phoney] | Fancy Articles [OCR: Foney]', 'rejected_lead', 'dirty_ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1875, '1875-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/annualreportalle00alle_38/page/n11/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-322.webp', 43590, '506d72be381482c2ed9d3bbbfbd7260cf0c21c1dd159868b5f9af7d171e343ca', '{"id": "CERT-2200-322", "slug": "cert-2200-322", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "David Roby ... 1 95 [OCR: David phoney] | giving money to the masters [OCR: phoney] | Fancy Articles [OCR: Foney]", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "dirty_ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1875, "date_display": "9 Jun 1838, 24 Oct 1857 & 1875", "sort_date": "1875-01-01", "short_gloss": "Trap B broken-type OCR ghosts refuting pre-1880 mirages: Town of Allenstown [NH] Report (1875, p. 10) ''David Roby'' -> OCR ''David phoney'', NY Evangelist (9 June 1838) ''money'' -> OCR ''phoney'', & Columbia Spy (24 Oct. 1857) ''Fancy'' -> OCR ''Foney''", "source_publication": "Annual Reports of the Selectmen, Treasurer, and Superintending School Committee of the Town of Allenstown [NH] for the Year Ending Feb. 15, 1875 (Concord, 1875), p. 10; paired with New-York Evangelist, Vol. 9, No. 23 (9 June 1838), p. 92 & The Columbia Spy (Columbia, PA), 24 Oct. 1857, p. 4", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/annualreportalle00alle_38/page/n11/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Allenstown 1875 Report Title (leaf n1) & Tax Abatements p. 10 (leaf n11); New-York Evangelist 9 June 1838 p. 92 (leaf n3); Columbia Spy 24 Oct. 1857 p. 4 (leaf n3)", "verbatim_quote": "[Allenstown Report, 1875, p. 10:] Charles Perry ... 95 / David Roby ... 1 95 / Michael Reynolds ... 2 95 | [New-York Evangelist, 9 June 1838, p. 92:] giving money to the masters, and nothing to the slaves ... to give them six years unrequited labor of their slaves, besides the twenty millions. | [Columbia Spy, 24 Oct. 1857, p. 4:] FOR THE LADIES—Rich styles of Fancy and Plain Silks... a general assortment of Perfumery, Fancy Articles, too numerous to mention.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1875, "masthead_year": 1875, "linguistic_note": "Eliminates the earliest raw full-text search mirages of <phoney> and <Foney> in 1838-1875 American print: (1) In the 1875 Allenstown, NH town tax report (p. 10, leaf n11), smudged letterpress ''David Roby ... 1 95'' was hallucinated by OCR as ''David phoney''; (2) In the June 9, 1838 New-York Evangelist (p. 92, https://archive.org/details/sim_evangelist-and-religious-review_1838-06-09_9_23/page/n3/mode/1up), broken initial m in ''money to the masters'' during a British West Indies emancipation report was misread as ''phoney to tho masters''; and (3) In the Oct. 24, 1857 Columbia Spy (p. 4, https://archive.org/details/per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1857-10-24_1/page/n3/mode/1up), display-type ''Fancy'' (''Fancy and Plain Silks'', ''Fancy Articles''; alongside ''pony'' in turf/livestock columns) was misindexed as ''Foney''.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified across all three primary witnesses (Allenstown 1875 leaves n1 & n11; New-York Evangelist 1838 leaf n3; Columbia Spy 1857 leaf n3). Demonstrates why every pre-1890 <phoney>/<foney> candidate requires pixel-level verification of physical ink.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-322.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1875 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-314', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'sawney for grub / spark-fawney / for the fawney [undated c. 1877 Worthington stereotype reissue]', 'contextual_evidence', 'edition_and_plate_state_control', 'agent_verified', 1877, '1877-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924073798740/page/n10/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-314.webp', 56262, '9073af3aa7f2de784c72a843df7135c7ba36f9793de8c57fa93a6e7836f898ac', '{"id": "CERT-2200-314", "slug": "cert-2200-314", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "sawney for grub / spark-fawney / for the fawney [undated c. 1877 Worthington stereotype reissue]", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "edition_and_plate_state_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1877, "date_display": "c. 1877 (1859 plates)", "sort_date": "1877-01-01", "short_gloss": "Bibliographic plate-state proof on the undated c. 1877 R. Worthington (750 Broadway) stereotype reissue of Matsell''s Vocabulum (cu31924073798740), exposing the chiseled 1859 imprint scar and 1876-1877 rear publisher''s catalog", "source_publication": "George W. Matsell, Vocabulum; or, The Rogue''s Lexicon (New-York: George W. Matsell & Co. [reissued undated by R. Worthington, Publisher, 750 Broadway, New York, c. 1877, from 1859 stereotype plates])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Cornell University Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924073798740/page/n10/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Altered Stereotype Title Page (leaf n10), SDNY 1859 Copyright Verso (leaf n11), Examples p. 105 (leaf n120), & Bound-In R. Worthington 750 Broadway ''New Books'' Catalog (leaves n146-n148)", "verbatim_quote": "[Title leaf n10:] BY GEORGE W. MATSELL, SPECIAL JUSTICE, CHIEF OF POLICE, ETC., ETC. ... No. 3 Tryon Row. [date chiseled off plate, leaving faint top-edge ink scars] | [p. 105, leaf n120:] Johnny Doyle had been pulling down sawney for grub... Bill had flimped a yack, and pinched a swell of a spark-fawney, and had sent the yack to church, and got half a century and a finnif for the fawney. | [Catalog leaf n147:] R. WORTHINGTON, Publisher, 750 Broadway, New York. NEW BOOKS... MATSELL (Geo. W.) The Rogues Dictionary... 1 vol. 12mo. Red Cloth. 2 00.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1859, "masthead_year": 1877, "linguistic_note": "Side-by-side plate-state comparison against CERT-2200-313 proves two physical alterations to the title stereotype plate (leaf n10) while confirming that body pages 1-130 are line-for-line identical to the May 1859 first edition: (1) the first-edition typo ''SPECIAL JUCTICE'' was corrected in the plate to ''SPECIAL JUSTICE''; and (2) the bottom date line ''1859.'' below ''No. 3 Tryon Row.'' was routed/chiseled out of the metal plate, leaving visible ghost fragments of the numeral tops.", "analysis_notes": "Pins down the exact issue date of Cornell copy cu31924073798740 via its bound-in 4-page rear publisher''s catalog (''R. WORTHINGTON, Publisher, 750 Broadway, New York. NEW BOOKS'', leaves n146-n148): Worthington moved to 750 Broadway in 1876, and the ''New Books'' list advertises Gladstone''s Bulgarian Horrors (Sept. 1876), Trevelyan''s Life and Letters of Lord Macaulay (1876), J. Drew Gay''s Prince of Wales in India (1877), and Tennyson''s Harold (late 1876/1877), alongside ''MATSELL (Geo. W.) The Rogues Dictionary... $2.00'' (leaf n147)—dating this impression squarely to c. 1877.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-314.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1877 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-317', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'Jewelry Manufacturers ... Attleboro'' / Attleboro'' Falls / No. Attleboro'' / So. Attleboro'' [0 Forney firms]', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1878, '1878-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/massachusettsreg1878bost/page/n490/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-317.webp', 47856, '27a2e73e736c70a48d26edb9b1c22b1cbb7dde7c09d4462e0abfdf008a9c7791', '{"id": "CERT-2200-317", "slug": "cert-2200-317", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "Jewelry Manufacturers ... Attleboro'' / Attleboro'' Falls / No. Attleboro'' / So. Attleboro'' [0 Forney firms]", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1878, "date_display": "1878 & 1875-1892", "sort_date": "1878-01-01", "short_gloss": "Exhaustive negative-corpus directory audit across the 1878 Massachusetts Register (all Attleboro Jewelry Manufacturers) and 9.44M chars of The Jewelers'' Circular (1875-1892): ZERO ''Forney'' manufacturers!", "source_publication": "The Massachusetts Register and Business Directory, 1878 (Boston: Sampson, Davenport, & Co., 1878), p. 525; paired with The Jewelers'' Circular and Horological Review (New York, Vols. 6 [1875], 12 [1881], 13 [1882], & 25 [1892])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/massachusettsreg1878bost/page/n490/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "1878 Massachusetts Directory p. 525 (''Jewelry Manufacturers'', leaf n490); Jewelers'' Circular Vol. 12 No. 1 (Feb. 1881, leaf n6) & Vol. 25 (3 Aug. 1892, p. 12, leaf n15)", "verbatim_quote": "Jewelry Manufacturers. [Attleboro'':] Bennett, Sibley, & Co. ... Sturdy Brothers & Co. [Attleboro'' Falls:] Blackinton V. H. (jet goods), Davidson Brothers, Evans Daniel & Co., Freeman & Co., Mason, Draper, & Co. ... Whitney E. & Co. (fancy gilt, &c.) ... [No. Attleboro'':] Aston Thos. & Co. ... Everett Dennis (glasses), Fisher S. E. & Co., Franklin E. I. & Co., French George W., Holbrook, Whiting, & Albee ... [Plainville:] Faas Charles A. & Co. [0 Forney entries]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1878, "masthead_year": 1878, "linguistic_note": "Empirical trade-directory falsification of the ''Forney brass jewelry manufacturer'' myth: every manufacturing jeweler in Attleboro, Attleboro Falls, North Attleboro, South Attleboro, Leominster (''Joslin & Williams (imitation)''), Mansfield, Plainville, and Wrentham—America''s cheap rolled-plate and gilt-brass jewelry capital (''ATTLEBOROUGH. Not genuine; made to imitate'', Matsell 1859, p. 9)—is listed alphabetically in the 1878 Massachusetts Register (p. 525), jumping directly across the F-slot from ''Evans Daniel & Co.'' to ''Freeman & Co.'' and from ''Fisher S. E. & Co.'' / ''Franklin E. I. & Co.'' / ''French George W.'' to ''Holbrook, Whiting, & Albee'' with ZERO firms named Forney.", "analysis_notes": "Backed by a programmatic full-text sweep across four pre-1893 bound volumes of New York''s flagship trade journal The Jewelers'' Circular and Horological Review totaling 9,442,000 characters—Vol. 6 (1875, PO60176Vol6), Vol. 12 (1881, https://archive.org/details/PO60176Vol12/page/n6/mode/1up), Vol. 13 (1882, PO60176Vol13), and Vol. 25 (1892, https://archive.org/details/jewelerscircular00unse_14/page/n15/mode/1up)—which yields 225 occurrences of ''Attleboro''/''Attleborough'' and ZERO occurrences of any jewelry, watch, or brass-ring manufacturer named ''Forney'' in New York, Newark, Providence, Attleboro, or Chicago.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-317.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1878 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-226', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'forty men [OCR: fony men]', 'rejected_lead', 'dirty_ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1879, '1879-09-28', 'https://archive.org/details/daily-nevada-state-journal-1879-09-28/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-226.webp', 149980, '8d250ec345f603d474d8cf6ac6bf2755d287b149f62789ac969e1987b2d5ea71', '{"id": "CERT-2200-226", "slug": "cert-2200-226", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "forty men [OCR: fony men]", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "dirty_ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1879, "date_display": "28 Sep 1879", "sort_date": "1879-09-28", "short_gloss": "Rejected 1879 ''fony men'' dirty-OCR ghost in Daily Nevada State Journal: physical ink reads ''not less than forty men''", "source_publication": "Daily Nevada State Journal (Reno, NV)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/daily-nevada-state-journal-1879-09-28/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 10, No. 146, p. 2, col. 2 (leaf 1; masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "Military Tournament. (Friday at 10 o''clock, A. M.) Premium $150, and special premium of gold medal, value $100, (gift of President Stevenson) for the best drilled Military Company (no shooting). The company competing shall report with not less than forty men, and will be required to drill in accordance with \"Upton''s U. S. Army Tactics\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1879, "masthead_year": 1879, "linguistic_note": "Classic Trap B broken-lead-type OCR hallucination: smudged medial ''rt'' in ''forty men'' was indexed by Internet Archive full-text OCR as ''fony men'', alongside systematic 1870s-1890s OCR ghosts turning ''fancy goods'' into ''foney goods'' and worn initial M in ''MONEY TO LOAN'' into ''FONEY TO LOAN''.", "analysis_notes": "Representative Trap B falsification across three recurring pre-1895 <foney>/<fony> OCR mirage families audited in this workstream: (1) ''forty men'' -> ''fony men'' (Daily Nevada State Journal, Sept. 28, 1879, p. 2, verified here in physical ink); (2) ''fancy goods'' -> ''foney goods'' (Los Angeles Times, Mar. 21, 1887, p. 1 ''Chinese and Japanese fancy goods''; Fort Wayne Sentinel, Jan. 7, 1889, p. 7; Victoria Daily Times, Nov. 24, 1896, p. 3); and (3) ''MONEY TO LOAN'' / ''Save Money'' -> ''FONEY TO LOAN'' / ''saver. foney'' (Chicago Tribune, Dec. 21, 1886, p. 10; Pittsburg Dispatch, Dec. 1, 1891, p. 2; St. Louis Post-Dispatch, Dec. 21, 1892, p. 5; The Black Cat, Dec. 1900, leaf 118). Also rules out Trap C Texas toponym ''Forney'' misprinted ''Foney'' in The Freeman, Nov. 18, 1899, p. 1.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-226.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1879 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-123', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'money [OCR: phoney]', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_b_broken_type_ocr', 'agent_verified', 1883, '1883-06-11', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1883-06-11_31_306/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-123.webp', 59524, '3c1ed9d3283ee74e0e964135d7a1d54fb4a4d2140abda2cdba911e3ce6beb5eb', '{"id": "CERT-2200-123", "slug": "cert-2200-123", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "money [OCR: phoney]", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_b_broken_type_ocr", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1883, "date_display": "11 Jun 1883", "sort_date": "1883-06-11", "short_gloss": "Trap B OCR Ghost: June 11, 1883 St. Louis Post-Dispatch hit for ''phoney'' is smudged newsprint ''money advanced if desired''", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1883-06-11_31_306/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 31, No. 306, p. 8, col. 1 (leaf 7)", "verbatim_quote": "CHEAPEST and best storage in the city for furniture and goods; money [OCR: phoney] advanced if desired; clean dry warehouse.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1883, "masthead_year": 1883, "linguistic_note": "Classic Trap B broken-lead/ink-bleed OCR hallucination in 6-point classified agate type where initial <m> with an ink speck or ascender bleed is tokenized by Tesseract/ABBYY as <ph> (''money'' -> ''phoney'').", "analysis_notes": "Representative proof plate for the dominant pre-1890 false-positive class across American newspaper databases: identical ink-bleed ghosts were visually falsified in the April 10, 1869 Macon Daily Telegraph (''money'' -> ''phoney''), Dec. 6, 1883 (''MONEY'' -> ''FONEY''), and May 6, 1893 New York Clipper (''Tony Pastor'' -> ''fony Pastor'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-123.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1883 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-321', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'sent by tele- / phone, [OCR: tele-phoney] | Sym- / phony, No. 4 [OCR: phony]', 'rejected_lead', 'dirty_ocr_ghost', 'agent_verified', 1883, '1883-12-08', 'https://archive.org/details/decatur-daily-republican-1883-12-08/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-321.webp', 73020, 'd9f12834c70e98f990bacbc0fbb619c0b43e708338b2620b297a5e155a456192', '{"id": "CERT-2200-321", "slug": "cert-2200-321", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "sent by tele- / phone, [OCR: tele-phoney] | Sym- / phony, No. 4 [OCR: phony]", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "dirty_ocr_ghost", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1883, "date_display": "8 Dec 1883 & 1890-91 (cat. 1884)", "sort_date": "1883-12-08", "short_gloss": "Trap B end-of-line hyphenation OCR ghosts: Decatur Daily Republican (8 Dec. 1883, p. 1) ''sent by tele- / phone,'' -> OCR ''tele-phoney'', and Musical Yearbook (1890-91 [cat. 1884], p. 12) ''Sym- / phony'' -> OCR ''phony''", "source_publication": "The Decatur Daily Republican (Decatur, IL), Vol. 12, No. 214 (8 Dec. 1883), p. 1, col. 4; paired with G. H. Wilson, The Musical Year-Book of the United States, Vol. VIII: Season of 1890-91 (Boston, 1891 [IA cataloged 1884]), p. 12", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/decatur-daily-republican-1883-12-08/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Decatur Daily Republican 8 Dec. 1883 p. 1 Masthead & col. 4 Coal Ad (leaf n0); Musical Yearbook Title leaf (n7, ''Season of 1890-91'') & p. 12 (leaf n20)", "verbatim_quote": "[Decatur Daily Republican, 8 Dec. 1883, p. 1, col. 4:] COAL! COAL!! J. H. VENNIGERHOLZ ... Orders left at Peter Ullrich''s grocery store, or sent by tele- [line break] phone, will be promptly attended to. | [Musical Year-Book, 1890-91, p. 12:] April 18. ... \"Voglein wohin so schnell,\" Lassen (Miss Jahn); Sym- [line break] phony, No. 4, in D minor, Schumann.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1883, "masthead_year": 1883, "linguistic_note": "Exposes the two most prolific 1880s Trap B line-wrap OCR mirage mechanisms across historical newspaper and periodical archives: (1) line-broken ''tele- / phone,'' followed immediately by a comma with a descending tail is systematically misread by OCR engines as ''tele-phoney'' or ''phoney'' (creating a phantom 1883 telephone-slang attestation in Decatur, IL!); and (2) line-broken ''Sym- / phony'' (or ''eu- / phony'', ''caco- / phony'', ''tele- / phony'') strands standalone ''phony'' at the left margin of the next line—compounded in the Musical Year-Book by a 7-year Trap A serial founding-year misdate (cataloged 1884, actual title ''Season of 1890-91'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on leaf n0 of The Decatur Daily Republican (Saturday, December 8, 1883, p. 1, col. 4) and leaves n7 & n20 of The Musical Year-Book of the United States (https://archive.org/details/musicalyearbook04unkngoog/page/n20/mode/1up).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-321.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1883 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-225', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'the Phony man', 'rejected_lead', 'false_friend_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1885, '1885-01-24', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1885-01-24_45/page/n3/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-225.webp', 162508, 'b56c620ed70afa1f868f8bf1b122a01c4589c902e9ad14045fe21483925509c1', '{"id": "CERT-2200-225", "slug": "cert-2200-225", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "the Phony man", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "false_friend_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1885, "date_display": "24 Jan 1885", "sort_date": "1885-01-24", "short_gloss": "Rejected 1885 ''Phony man'' false lead in Chicago Daily Tribune: Pittsburg Chronicle newsroom-humor persona trading puns on milk", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (reprinting Pittsburg Chronicle)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1885-01-24_45/page/n3/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 45, p. 4, col. 3 (leaf 3; masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "The Sly Old Cockroach swaggered up to the Phony man the other day and asked him if he did not want a good thing. \"What is it?\" \"New joke on milk.\" \"O, milk is stale.\" \"This is fresh.\" \"Let''s hear it.\" \"When a milkman asks you to pay a bill tell him to chalk it down,\" and the Sly Old Cockroach laughed viciously.—Pittsburg Chronicle.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1885, "masthead_year": 1885, "linguistic_note": "Trap C homograph / comic newsroom nickname: ''the Phony man'' in an 1885 newspaper-office exchange column (paired with the office paste-pot ''Sly Old Cockroach'' trading puns) denotes either the newsroom telephone-desk reporter (''Phone-y man) or dialectal ''Funny man'' (humor paragrapher), bearing zero semantic connection to bogus jewelry or counterfeiting.", "analysis_notes": "Crucial Trap C guardrail for full-text queries on ''\"phony man\"'': physical letterpress ink on leaf 3 genuinely prints ''Phony man'' on January 24, 1885 (8 years before OED3''s 1893 baseline), but inspection of the full exchange paragraph proves it is a Pittsburg Chronicle joke-column persona swapping a milk pun with the office cockroach, not an underworld fawney/phoney peddler.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-225.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1885 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-203', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'pennyweight-worker (0 phoney/foney/fawney hits)', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1886, '1886-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924096989177/page/n88/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-203.webp', 67598, '287e3cef206b18d815e16a730d9d277150abf09a06920e776362f53cab325ab5', '{"id": "CERT-2200-203", "slug": "cert-2200-203", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "pennyweight-worker (0 phoney/foney/fawney hits)", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1886, "date_display": "1886", "sort_date": "1886-01-01", "short_gloss": "Negative-corpus and occupational control across Byrnes (1886) and 1869-1895 NYC crime corpora: pennyweighters and mock auctions pre-phoney", "source_publication": "Thomas Byrnes, Professional Criminals of America (New York: Cassell & Company, Limited, 1886)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Cornell University Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924096989177/page/n88/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title page (JP2 #0012), pp. 30-35 (''Pennyweighters''), pp. 40-49 (''Confidence and Banco Men'' / ''Sawdust Men''), & p. 68 (JP2 #0089)", "verbatim_quote": "WALTER SHERIDAN is an accomplished thief, a daring forger, pennyweight-worker and counterfeiter. He is also one of the [most clever bank sneaks] in America. ... made a specialty [of pennyweight jewelry thefts].", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1886, "masthead_year": 1886, "linguistic_note": "Establishes the pre-1890 lexical ceiling in formal New York police, detective, and legislative prose: mainstream observers used ''bogus jewelry'', ''mock auctions'', ''Peter Funks'', ''pennyweighters'', and ''green-goods men'', while fawney -> foney -> phoney remained sub-literary oral cant until 1890-1894.", "analysis_notes": "Full-text programmatic sweep across five foundational New York underworld and legislative corpora totaling over 5.2 million characters—Matthew Hale Smith''s Sunshine and Shadow in New York (1869, 1,215,748 chars), Edward Crapsey''s The Nether Side of New York (1872, 698,271 chars), James D. McCabe''s Lights and Shadows of New York Life (1872, 1,761,189 chars), Detective Chief Thomas Byrnes''s Professional Criminals of America (1886, 1,531,066 chars), and the Lexow Committee Police Investigation transcripts including George Appo''s green-goods testimony (1895, Vols. 3 & 5)—yields 0 occurrences of phoney, phony, foney, or fawney. Crucially, however, Crapsey (1872, p. 97), McCabe (1872, p. 512), and Byrnes (1886, pp. 30-35, 68) document the exact urban swindles—Chatham Street mock-auction dollar stores selling brass watches and ''pennyweight-workers'' substituting paste rings on jewelers'' trays—that burst into print under ''phoney''/''foney''/''fauny'' in 1894-1903.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-203.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1886 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-318', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'STAGE JEWELRY (''only we fake and we substitute'') | Contemporaneous Forney Prosopography', 'rejected_lead', 'falsified_eponym_myth', 'agent_verified', 1888, '1888-10-01', 'https://archive.org/details/keystone-v9-n10-oct1888/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-318.webp', 54446, '0eb62ac5e5eb5bacd9acf4223d6598858e3d2d718e8762f68a6e07ff396fbe60', '{"id": "CERT-2200-318", "slug": "cert-2200-318", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "STAGE JEWELRY (''only we fake and we substitute'') | Contemporaneous Forney Prosopography", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "falsified_eponym_myth", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1888, "date_display": "Oct 1888 (paired w/ 1877, 1882)", "sort_date": "1888-10-01", "short_gloss": "Refutes the 1940s-1950s Winchell/Mencken ''Forney brass jewelry'' eponym myth via The Keystone (Oct. 1888, pp. 3 & 12) and prosopography of real contemporaneous Forneys (Col. J. W. Forney & M. N. Forney)", "source_publication": "The Keystone (Philadelphia, PA), Vol. 9, No. 10 (Oct. 1888), pp. 3 & 12; paired with Col. John W. Forney, Forty Years of American Journalism (Philadelphia, 1877) & Matthias N. Forney, Catechism of the Locomotive (New York: Railroad Gazette, 1882)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/keystone-v9-n10-oct1888/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "The Keystone Oct. 1888 Cover Masthead (leaf n0), p. 3, col. 4 (''Stage Jewelry'', leaf n4), & p. 12, col. 2 (''Diamond robberies... paste diamond'', leaf n13); M. N. Forney 1882 Title leaf (n3)", "verbatim_quote": "[Keystone Oct. 1888, p. 3:] STAGE JEWELRY. On Fifth Avenue, in the vicinity of Fourteenth Street, is a noted \"stage jeweler.\" ... \"The eye must be gratified, and we comply; only we fake and we substitute.\"—N. Y. Dramatic News. | [p. 12:] DIAMOND robberies are becoming more frequent every day; not only by direct theft, but by exchange. ... he buys a similar mounting, putting in a paste diamond. The woman then goes back and adroitly makes an exchange.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1888, "masthead_year": 1888, "linguistic_note": "Explains the exact linguistic genesis of the mid-20th-century ''Forney'' eponym myth—reported by correspondents to H. L. Mencken (The American Language: Supplement II, 1948, p. 694n., claiming a ''Forney Jewelry Company'' made cheap watches/rings c. 1900) and popularized by Walter Winchell and syndicated 1950s columns: in non-rhotic New York City speech, older cant fawney [fo:ni] (''imitation ring/jewelry'', cf. Hapgood 1903 ''fauny shop (imitation jewelry)'' and Pierce Egan 1830 ''diamond forney'', CERT-2200-309) was homophonous with the American Pennsylvania-German surname Forney [fo:ni]. Speakers unfamiliar with cant fawney folk-etymologized ''fawney/forney rings'' into a fictitious manufacturer named ''Forney''—exactly like ''Tommy Atkins'', ''Peter Funk'', or ''Sally Lunn'' eponym myths!", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified in Philadelphia''s national retail jewelry organ The Keystone (Oct. 1888, reaching 22,000 jewelers monthly): p. 3 profiles New York''s paste jewelry trade under ''STAGE JEWELRY'', p. 12 describes paste-diamond pennyweight ring-switching with zero mention of ''Forney'', and across the entire national trade press the only retail watchmaker named Forney was a small-town Ohio shopkeeper (J. W. Forney of Steubenville, OH), while the famous Gilded Age bearers of the surname were Philadelphia newspaper proprietor and U.S. Senate Secretary Col. John Wien Forney (1817-1881, https://archive.org/details/fortyyearsofamer00forn/page/n3/mode/1up) and New York locomotive designer Matthias Nagle Forney (1835-1908, inventor of the elevated-railway ''Forney locomotive'', https://archive.org/details/forney-catechism-1882/page/n3/mode/1up).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-318.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1888 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-204', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'prize packages / wrapped it nicely around the soap', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1889, '1889-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/twentyyearshusl00johngoog/page/n88/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-204.webp', 38744, 'a71e760375084e3a225126aa23a18e848aec72113ed5ac8da9ef0768f2fc50c2', '{"id": "CERT-2200-204", "slug": "cert-2200-204", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "prize packages / wrapped it nicely around the soap", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1889, "date_display": "1889 (c. 1887)", "sort_date": "1889-01-01", "short_gloss": "First-person pitchman memoir by J. P. Johnston detailing the circus ''prize soap'' game and street watch auctioneering", "source_publication": "J. P. Johnston, Twenty Years of Hus''ling (Chicago: Hallet Publishing Company, 1889 [copyright 1887])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/twentyyearshusl00johngoog/page/n88/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title & Copyright leaves (JP2 #0009-#0010) & Ch. VI, p. 76 (JP2 #0089; cf. plate on p. 79 ''Selling Prize Soap with a Circus'')", "verbatim_quote": "We purchased a tin box and three large cakes of James S. Kirk''s laundry soap, and some tin-foil. We cut the soap into small, equal sized cakes about three inches long, and a half inch square at the ends. We then cut small strips of writing paper, and after marking 25c on some of them and 50c, 75c, and $1.00 and $2.00 on an occasional one, we pasted a strip of this paper on each cake of soap, some prizes and many blanks. We then cut the tinfoil and wrapped it nicely around the soap and put it into the tin box.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1887, "masthead_year": 1889, "linguistic_note": "Documents the institutional convergence of circus-lot pitchmen, ''prize soap'' tinfoil-wrapper swindlers (wrapping cash slips, bank notes, and gilt rings around soap cakes), and street watch auctioneers in the Midwest during the 1870s-1880s.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the 1889 Hallet Publishing Co. printing (copyright 1887 by J. P. Johnston; calibrated from JP2 #0009-#0010). Johnston''s career—moving directly from selling tinfoil-wrapped ''prize soap'' cakes outside a circus tent at Huntington, Indiana (pp. 76-82) into itinerant jewelry and watch auctioneering—supplies the concrete commercial mechanism linking fake-ring pitching (''fawney bouncing'') and fake-bill wrapping in the same street-pitchman subculture.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-204.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1889 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-205', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'fashino fauny / fauney / Fawney bouncing', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1889, '1889-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/adictionaryofsla01barriala/page/355/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-205.webp', 98176, 'c7d39ad6c3cb5b42320a58f5317a9ad2b12c3ea2715446c3cbe273224e91628b', '{"id": "CERT-2200-205", "slug": "cert-2200-205", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "fashino fauny / fauney / Fawney bouncing", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1889, "date_display": "1889", "sort_date": "1889-01-01", "short_gloss": "Barrere & Leland (1889) recording ''fashino fauny'' (''false gold rings''), ''fauney'', and ''Fawney bouncing''", "source_publication": "Albert Barrere and Charles G. Leland, A Dictionary of Slang, Jargon & Cant, Vol. I: A-K (Ballantyne Press, 1889)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/adictionaryofsla01barriala/page/355/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title page (JP2 #0007, MDCCCLXXXIX) & pp. 355-356 (JP2 #0383-#0384)", "verbatim_quote": "Fashno, fashni, fashioni (gypsy), false, counterfeit; fashni augustrins, false (gold) rings; also fashino fauny. (Fauny is canting.) ... Fawney (thieves), a ring; also \"fauney.\" ... Fawney bouncing (thieves), selling rings for a pretended wager. Fawney dropper (thieves), one who practises the ring-dropping trick.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1889, "masthead_year": 1889, "linguistic_note": "American-born Romani scholar Charles Godfrey Leland (Philadelphia) documents the hybrid Anglo-Romani/cant collocation fashino fauny (''false/counterfeit ring'') alongside variant spellings fauny and fauney, showing how fawney was routinely paired with ''false/snide'' jewelry semantics just prior to ellipsis and vowel-closing in American cant.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the 1889 Ballantyne subscribers'' first edition (pp. 355-356). Compare John S. Farmer and W. E. Henley''s Slang and Its Analogues, Vol. II (London, 1891, p. 386: ''FAWNEY, or FAUNEY... A swindle... A ring (snide) is let drop'') versus Vol. V (1902, where PHONEY / FONEY is still completely absent from British slang lexicography), confirming that the shift from fawney/fauny ''ring (in a fake-ring swindle)'' to foney/phoney ''counterfeit, bogus; fake jewelry'' occurred exclusively in North America.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-205.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1889 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-108', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'QUITE "PHONEY." / quite a "phoney" joke', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1890, '1890-04-04', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83016683/1890-04-04/ed-1/?sp=2', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-108.webp', 129174, '34a5edce61f04f32a2daefe30cbf7c4da1f97ab37d913fa0894b080b446568cf', '{"id": "CERT-2200-108", "slug": "cert-2200-108", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "QUITE \"PHONEY.\" / quite a \"phoney\" joke", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1890, "date_display": "4 Apr 1890", "sort_date": "1890-04-04", "short_gloss": "April 4, 1890 Detroit Plaindealer wire-service baseline (''QUITE \"PHONEY.\"'' / ''quite a \"phoney\" joke'') on a mock telephone marriage", "source_publication": "The Plaindealer (Detroit, MI), Vol. 7, No. 46 (Whole No. 356), Friday, 4 April 1890, p. 2, col. 5 (reprinting Northern Indiana exchange item)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress (Chronicling America)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83016683/1890-04-04/ed-1/?sp=2", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 5 (''QUITE \"PHONEY.\"'')", "verbatim_quote": "QUITE \"PHONEY.\" He Wrote the Name of Agnes in the Sky. Minnie Worley, aged 22, Telephone Exchange operator at South Bend, and Frank Middleton, aged 25, in a like position at Michigan City, became acquainted over the wires during their night watches. Finally Middleton proposed in fun that they get married by telephone, and Minnie consented. A Michigan City justice was called in and performed a legal ceremony, but without the necessary state license. It was passed off as quite a ''phoney'' joke; but it grows serious, when eminent legal council pronounce it valid", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1890, "masthead_year": 1890, "linguistic_note": "Bivalent journalistic wordplay on telephone (''phone + -y) and sham/mock (''proposed in fun... passed off as quite a \"phoney\" joke; but it grows serious''). While primarily motivated by the telephone exchange setting, its <phoney> spelling shows how popular familiarity with phone (''telephone'', 1877-) promoted the <ph-> respelling of spoken /foʊni/ ~ /fɔːni/.", "analysis_notes": "Identified by Dave Wilton (Wordorigins.org, 10 Feb. 2022) and verified on the Library of Congress scan (Vol. 7, No. 46, Whole No. 356, 4 Apr. 1890). The headline ''QUITE \"PHONEY.\"'' and body phrase ''passed off as quite a \\''phoney\\'' joke'' execute a double entendre simultaneously invoking a marriage conducted by telephone (''phone'') and a sham/counterfeit ceremony lacking a state marriage license (''passed off as... a joke''). Rather than proving de novo derivation from telephone alone, this 1890 wire-operator pun captures the exact cultural mechanism—newly ubiquitous tele-phone and phono-graph orthography—that attracted spoken underworld foney (/foʊni/ < fawney) into Neo-Greek <ph-> spelling.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-108.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1890 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-001', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'FAWNEY, or FAUNEY / FORNEY', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1891, '1891-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue03farmuoft/page/n65/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-001.webp', 75976, '444c9a9b18aaaf6d26b1485cab3b2985a3a459f0c92131e998f2f8da1f36b25a', '{"id": "CERT-2200-001", "slug": "cert-2200-001", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "FAWNEY, or FAUNEY / FORNEY", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1891, "date_display": "1891 & 1893", "sort_date": "1891-01-01", "short_gloss": "19th-c. cant lexicon headwords ''FAWNEY, or FAUNEY'' & non-rhotic ''FORNEY'' (''a ring; a variant of FAWNEY'')", "source_publication": "John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues Past and Present, Vol. II: C to Fizzle (London: Printed for Subscribers Only, 1891 [MDCCCXCI]), p. 378 (leaf 385), paired with Vol. III: Fla. to Hyps. (London, 1893 [MDCCCXCIII]), p. 60 (leaf 65)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue03farmuoft/page/n65/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. II (1891), p. 378, col. 2 (leaf 385); Vol. III (1893), p. 60, col. 1 (leaf 65)", "verbatim_quote": "[Vol. II, p. 378:] FAWNEY, or FAUNEY, subs. (common).—1. A ring; Fr., une brobuante; une broquille; un chason; Fourbesque, cerchiosa. 2. A swindle (also called FAWNEY-DROPPING, or RIG), worked as follows:—A ring (snide) is let drop in front of a passer-by, who picks it up, and is confronted by the dropper, who claims to share. ... FAWNEY-BOUNCING = selling rings for a pretended wager; FAWNIED = ringed. | [Vol. III, p. 60:] FORNEY, subs (thieves'').—A ring; a variant of FAWNEY (q.v.). 1871 [1st ed. 1830]. EGAN, Finish of Tom and Jerry, p. 243. He sports a diamond FORNEY on his little finger.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1890, "masthead_year": 1891, "linguistic_note": "Documents both FAWNEY/FAUNEY (''snide'' brass ring & fawney-bouncing street pitch) and the established non-rhotic thieves''-cant headword FORNEY (''a ring; a variant of FAWNEY''), exposing the true lexical seed of the 20th-century ''Forney Jewelers'' folk-eponym", "analysis_notes": "Farmer & Henley''s Vol. II (1891, p. 378) defines the ring-dropping swindle explicitly around ''A ring (snide)'' and ''FAWNEY-BOUNCING = selling rings for a pretended wager'', while Vol. III (1893, p. 60) enters non-rhotic ''FORNEY, subs. (thieves'').—A ring; a variant of FAWNEY (q.v.)'' on the authority of Pierce Egan''s Finish to Tom and Jerry (1830, CERT-2200-309). Because non-rhotic British and Eastern American cant speakers pronounced fawney [fɔːni] identically to a hypothetical rhotic spelling forney, late-19th- and mid-20th-century informants who dimly remembered cheap brass rings being called ''forneys'' retroactively invented a nonexistent manufacturer named ''Forney'' (refuted in Attleboro and trade directories in CERT-2200-317 and CERT-2200-318).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-001.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1891 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-303', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'granya, "ring," f. fainne', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1891, '1891-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/journalgypsylor01socigoog/page/n295/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-303.webp', 49600, 'ed194e6593ec3b0004263d4e02264be84b1a4460ddd43a876ad2cd026cb69c64', '{"id": "CERT-2200-303", "slug": "cert-2200-303", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "granya, \"ring,\" f. fainne", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1891, "date_display": "1890-1891", "sort_date": "1891-01-01", "short_gloss": "Kuno Meyer & John Sampson in Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society (Vol. II, 1890-1891) showing that encrypted Irish Traveller Shelta disguised fainne as granya, whereas Anglo-Irish cant borrowed fainne undisguised as fawney", "source_publication": "Kuno Meyer, ''On the Irish Origin and the Age of Shelta'' (pp. 257-266) & John Sampson, ''Tinkers and their Talk'' (pp. 204-220), Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society, Vol. II (Edinburgh: T. and A. Constable, Jan. 1890 - Apr. 1891)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/journalgypsylor01socigoog/page/n295/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. II Title Page (leaf n11, dated 1891), Sampson p. 207 (leaf n239), & Meyer p. 259 (leaf n295)", "verbatim_quote": "(3.) Substituting another letter or letters for the initial, e.g. slunya, \"glass,\" f. gloine; granko, \"turkey,\" f. francach; Grasano, \"Scotch,\" f. sasanach; graura, \"summer,\" f. samhradh; grarro, \"foal,\" f. searrach; granya, \"ring,\" f. fainne; grenog, \"window,\" f. fuinneog; griwog, \"fairy,\" f. siabhrog", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1891, "masthead_year": 1891, "linguistic_note": "Distinguishes two distinct Celtic-underworld contact channels: in Irish Traveller Shelta (first noted by C. G. Leland, The Gypsies, 1882, pp. 358-372, https://archive.org/details/gypsies00lela/page/358/mode/1up, and analyzed by Sampson 1890, Meyer 1891, and R. A. S. Macalister, The Secret Languages of Ireland, 1937), speakers already fluent in Irish deliberately encrypted fainne by replacing initial f- with gr- to yield Shelta granya (''ring''). By contrast, in English-speaking St Giles / Seven Dials London rookeries, unencrypted vernacular Irish fainne [fo:n''e] was opaque enough to English victims to function directly as thieves'' cant fawney (''ring'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on Vol. II of the Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society (title leaf n11, 1891; Meyer p. 259, leaf n295). Meyer''s phonological table proves two decisive points for TGT-015: (1) Shelta''s -anya rhyme in granya (< Irish fainne) independently confirms the palatalized nasal + schwa/front-offglide perception of Irish -ainne among bilingual Tinkers and Travellers; and (2) fawney entered metropolitan British and American cant directly from vernacular Hiberno-Irish speech rather than via Shelta consonant-prefixing.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-303.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1891 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-109', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'on the phony order', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1892, '1892-05-07', 'https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045462/1892-05-07/ed-1/?sp=12', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-109.webp', 112592, 'd6e22d6e082eef3a6621854eb4abf5320da3bcd8e5a52153e48473ba9d0a812c', '{"id": "CERT-2200-109", "slug": "cert-2200-109", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "on the phony order", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1892, "date_display": "7 May 1892", "sort_date": "1892-05-07", "short_gloss": "Earliest unambiguous non-telephone American print attestation of phony, adj. (''fluky, sham, not genuine'') in The Evening Star (May 7, 1892)", "source_publication": "The Evening Star (Washington, D.C.)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress (Chronicling America)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045462/1892-05-07/ed-1/?sp=12", "page_and_column": "No. 12,175, p. 12, col. 3 (''Weekly Ball Talk'')", "verbatim_quote": "Chamberlain''s home run that won the 7 to 2 game for the Cincinnatis from Washington was a little on the phony order. Ordinarily it would have been a rattling good single", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1892, "masthead_year": 1892, "linguistic_note": "Unquoted Sporting-page idiom ''a little on the phony order'' (''tainted by fluke/sham quality, not a genuine clean home run''), completely independent of any telephone context and already grammatically integrated into the colloquial construction ''on the [adj.] order''.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on page 12 running header (''THE EVENING STAR: WASHINGTON, D. C., SATURDAY, MAY 7, 1892—SIXTEEN PAGES'') and col. 3 physical ink. Antedates OED3''s earliest accepted adjectival quotation (June 29, 1893 Chicago Daily Tribune) by 1 yr, 1 mo, 22 days, and Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s earliest phoney, adj. citation (Nov. 17, 1894 Decatur Daily Review) by 2 yrs, 6 mos.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-109.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1892 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-110', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '''phony'' bookmakers', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1893, '1893-06-29', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1893-06-29_52_180/page/n5/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-110.webp', 92818, '47ca42b6042d4a31034145c7409f45fd5d119aa37b1e09497fac3c31f788299e', '{"id": "CERT-2200-110", "slug": "cert-2200-110", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "''phony'' bookmakers", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1893, "date_display": "29 Jun 1893", "sort_date": "1893-06-29", "short_gloss": "OED3''s June 29, 1893 Chicago Daily Tribune baseline (''Many of the \"phony\" bookmakers in the ring'') at Washington Park racetrack", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1893-06-29_52_180/page/n5/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 52, No. 180, p. 6, col. 2 (leaf 5)", "verbatim_quote": "There were fifty-six books in the Washington Park ring yesterday. Betting was not good. Many of the ''phony'' bookmakers in the ring had not enough play to keep them alive if they were paying their own expenses.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1893, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "Attributive adjectival use (''\"phony\" bookmakers'' = fly-by-night/welshing or house-backed dummy bookmakers in the Chicago betting ring), marking the summer 1893 Chicago World''s Fair racing season as the national diffusion epicenter of phony.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on page 6 running header (''THE CHICAGO DAILY TRIBUNE: THURSDAY, JUNE 29, 1893'', leaf 5) and col. 2 physical ink. Confirms OED3''s primary 1893 baseline verbatim and anchors the July-August 1893 national turf-slang commentary in St. Louis and New York (CERT-2200-111, CERT-2200-112).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-110.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-111', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '''phony'' ticket / ''phony'' horse / phony ride', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1893, '1893-07-06', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1893-07-06_44_336/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-111.webp', 120054, '7f08b7ee6c2e03b0df47fa7c73df1ba31c47eddde2e4f170dd76aac29b0990f6', '{"id": "CERT-2200-111", "slug": "cert-2200-111", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "''phony'' ticket / ''phony'' horse / phony ride", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1893, "date_display": "6 Jul 1893", "sort_date": "1893-07-06", "short_gloss": "Explicit July 6, 1893 metalinguistic definition of ''phony'' (''The word implies an element of fraud'') in the St. Louis Post-Dispatch", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1893-07-06_44_336/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 44, No. 336, p. 3, col. 1 (leaf 2; front masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "The bizarre vocabulary of the turf has been enlarged by a new word—''phony.'' A worthless ticket is a ''phony'' ticket; a bad horse is a ''phony'' horse. The word implies an element of fraud, and when a member of the talent says ''I got a phony ride'' he means that he has witnessed an example of Canadian form.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1893, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "Earliest explicit semantic gloss of phony in print (''The word implies an element of fraud''), attesting three turf collocations simultaneously (''phony'' ticket, ''phony'' horse, ''phony ride'') just seven days after the June 29, 1893 Chicago Tribune citation.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on front masthead (leaf 0) and p. 3 running header (''THURSDAY, JULY 6, 1893'', leaf 2, col. 1). Antedates Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s earliest phoney, adj. quotation (''given a phony ticket by a tout'', Nov. 17, 1894 Decatur Daily Review) by 1 yr, 4 mos, and proves contemporary observers recognized ''phony'' as a newly generalized turf-slang term in mid-1893.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-111.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-112', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '''phony'' ticket / ''phony'' horse', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1893, '1893-08-02', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_new-york-times_the-new-york-times_1893-08-02_42_13086/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-112.webp', 114276, '9b5d8c9d95efbb78797dd87281a16229af4d7516f9af6fcec00f47b7c20e2cc5', '{"id": "CERT-2200-112", "slug": "cert-2200-112", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "''phony'' ticket / ''phony'' horse", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1893, "date_display": "2 Aug 1893", "sort_date": "1893-08-02", "short_gloss": "August 2, 1893 New York Times turf note introducing ''phony'' to New York metropolitan readers (''Who first started the name is a question'')", "source_publication": "The New York Times (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_new-york-times_the-new-york-times_1893-08-02_42_13086/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 42, No. 13,086, p. 3, col. 2 (leaf 2)", "verbatim_quote": "The vocabulary of the turf has been enlarged by a new word—''phony.'' A worthless ticket is a ''phony'' ticket, and a poor horse is a ''phony'' horse. Who first started the name is a question, but the slang of the turf could certainly be improved, to say the least.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1893, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "Demonstrates rapid Midwest-to-East-Coast press transmission (Chicago June 29 -> St. Louis July 6 -> New York Aug. 2, 1893) and confirms that mainstream New York turf writers did not yet recognize its underworld cant pedigree (''Who first started the name is a question'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on page 3 running header (''THE NEW-YORK TIMES, WEDNESDAY, AUGUST 2, 1893'', leaf 2, col. 2). Establishes that by August 1893 ''phony'' had entered national metropolitan broadsheets from the Chicago/Midwestern racing circuit.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-112.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-206', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'fawny men / fawny man / A FAWNY MAN', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-02-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_century-illustrated-monthly-magazine_1894-02_47_4/page/n37/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-206.webp', 96736, '2126348002349ffae3e47b688aa3ee8af26aada40aeca51257ed59f8e54f2602', '{"id": "CERT-2200-206", "slug": "cert-2200-206", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "fawny men / fawny man / A FAWNY MAN", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "Feb 1894", "sort_date": "1894-02-01", "short_gloss": "Josiah Flynt''s Feb. 1894 Century Magazine study and engraving ''A FAWNY MAN'' (''fellows who sell bogus jewelry'', 5 yrs before Green''s 1899)", "source_publication": "Josiah Flynt [Willard], ''The Tramp at Home,'' The Century Illustrated Monthly Magazine, Vol. 47, No. 4 (New York, Feb. 1894)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_century-illustrated-monthly-magazine_1894-02_47_4/page/n37/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Issue header (leaf 0), p. 520, col. 2 (leaf 37) & p. 522 full-page engraving (leaf 39)", "verbatim_quote": "There were two well-dressed tramps whom I immediately recognized as \"fawny men\"—fellows who sell bogus jewelry for more than it is worth. One of these men was a notorious roadster of American birth, who, for purposes best known to himself, went by the name of Liverpool George. He is the most successful fawny man that I have ever met. He earned twenty-two dollars in one day at the fair by selling for two dollars apiece rings which can be bought in Buffalo for two dollars a dozen. ... [p. 522 caption:] A FAWNY MAN.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1894, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "Preserves the American tramp/fairground agentive compound fawny man (''peddler of bogus $2-a-dozen fairground rings'') immediately before and alongside its Mid-Atlantic/Midwestern vowel-raised and <ph->-remodeled doublet phoney man.", "analysis_notes": "Original magazine publication of Josiah Flynt''s tramp fieldwork at the New York State Fair near Syracuse, antedating Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s 1899 book baseline (Tramping with Tramps, pp. 99, 136, 223, 280-281, & glossary p. 376 ''Fawny Man: a peddler of bogus jewelry'') by 5 years and 7 months, and preceding the March 6, 1894 newspaper excerpt (CERT-2200-107) by one month. Includes A. Z. Baker and S. G. Putnam''s 1894 portrait engraving ''A FAWNY MAN.'' on p. 522.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-206.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-107', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'fawny men / fawny man', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-03-06', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1894-03-06_45_210/page/n6/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-107.webp', 95496, 'a038c49f325379ead61b49822c3af41aa3c4d3c8fb038c581b0ce065a4710639', '{"id": "CERT-2200-107", "slug": "cert-2200-107", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "fawny men / fawny man", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "6 Mar 1894", "sort_date": "1894-03-06", "short_gloss": "Josiah Flynt''s March 1894 firsthand tramp account defining ''fawny men—fellows who sell bogus jewelry'' (-5 yrs vs GDoS 1899)", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (reprinting Josiah Flynt, The Century Illustrated Monthly Magazine, March 1894, Vol. 47, No. 5)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1894-03-06_45_210/page/n6/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 45, No. 210, p. 7, col. 2 (leaf 6; Century Magazine March 1894, leaf 72)", "verbatim_quote": "There were two well-dressed tramps whom I immediately recognized as ''fawny men''—fellows who sell bogus jewelry for more than it is worth... He is the most successful fawny man that I have ever met. He earned $22 in one day at the fair by selling for $2 apiece rings which can be bought in Buffalo for $2 a dozen.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1894, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "Crucial morphological and semantic hinge: American tramp cant compound ''fawny man'' (''peddler of $2-a-dozen bogus Buffalo rings at country fairs'') antedates Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s 1899 book citation (Tramping with Tramps, p. 280) by 5 years and pairs directly with CERT-2200-114 (''Phoney'' jewelry man, Aug. 15, 1894, just 5 months later).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on St. Louis Post-Dispatch p. 7 running header (''TUESDAY, MARCH 6, 1894'', leaf 6) and corroborated in The Century Illustrated Monthly Magazine (March 1894, Vol. 47, No. 5, leaf 72, https://archive.org/details/sim_century-illustrated-monthly-magazine_1894-03_47_5/page/n72/mode/1up). Supplies the exact economic mechanism ($2/dozen wholesale brass rings hawked for $2 each) linking Anglo-Irish ''fawney'' to American ''phoney''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-107.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-113', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '''phony'' delegation / ''phony'' ticket', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-05-13', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1894-05-13_45_277/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-113.webp', 118372, '01c7d2eeb659ff8210cc07f6de241bc862462ef7dbd4e4f80ffac4140040d339', '{"id": "CERT-2200-113", "slug": "cert-2200-113", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "''phony'' delegation / ''phony'' ticket", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "13 May 1894", "sort_date": "1894-05-13", "short_gloss": "Transfer of phony from racetrack betting tickets to fraudulent ward-politics convention slates (''the so-called \"phony\" delegation'', May 13, 1894)", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1894-05-13_45_277/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 45, No. 277, p. 5, col. 1 (leaf 4)", "verbatim_quote": "and last, the so-called ''phony'' delegation, headed ''Straight Democratic Ticket,'' with the names of P. J. Kelly, Tim E. Cavanagh and Dr. Thomas F. Carroll... at liberty to count the so-called ''phony'' ticket", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1894, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "Pivotal domain broadening via the polysemous noun ''ticket'' (betting pasteboard -> political ballot slate) and collective human noun ''delegation'', moving phony out of racetrack argot into general civic and political reporting.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on p. 5 running header (''SUNDAY MORNING, MAY 13, 1894'', leaf 4, col. 1). Followed rapidly in the same paper by Aug. 4, 1894 (''phony'' passes, https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1894-08-04_45_360/page/n3/mode/1up) and Nov. 12, 1894 (''Phoney Dice'' headline, https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1894-11-12_46_95/page/n3/mode/1up).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-113.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-114', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '''Phoney'' Jewelry Man / ''Phoney'' jewelry man', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-08-15', 'https://archive.org/details/xt7t7659ft5d/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-114.webp', 109196, 'ffc7fcfcd36208779907eb6e2b5f5f014a47d25fb48db9529d0489b370e4bf36', '{"id": "CERT-2200-114", "slug": "cert-2200-114", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "''Phoney'' Jewelry Man / ''Phoney'' jewelry man", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "15 Aug 1894", "sort_date": "1894-08-15", "short_gloss": "Smoking-gun etymological bridge: Hamilton, Ohio police arrest of a ''\"Phoney\" jewelry man'' selling a $3 ''gold'' watch on the street (Aug. 15, 1894)", "source_publication": "The Evening Bulletin (Maysville, KY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (University of Kentucky / Maysville Public Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/xt7t7659ft5d/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 13, No. 224, p. 1, col. 1 (leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "\"Phoney\" Jewelry Man Arrested. HAMILTON, O., Aug. 15.—Harry Murphy, a \"Phoney\" jewelry man, was arrested on Hight street yesterday in the act of selling a gold watch for $3. Murphy had his pockets full of jewelry and watches, which the police believe to have been stolen. A pal escaped the police.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1894, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "Direct lexical and occupational equivalent of Josiah Flynt''s March 1894 ''fawny man'' (''fellows who sell bogus jewelry'', CERT-2200-107), capturing the exact moment street-peddler cant fawney/fawny man was re-spelled <Phoney> in Ohio Valley police blotter usage 5 yrs, 10 mos before GDoS''s June 30, 1900 National Police Gazette citation (''phony men'').", "analysis_notes": "DECISIVE OCCUPATIONAL SMOKING GUN: Antedates Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s earliest citation for phony man (30 June 1900 National Police Gazette ''phony men, who peddle bogus jewelry'', CERT-2200-216) by 5 years, 10 months, and 15 days. Published in the Ohio River valley just six months after Josiah Flynt defined ''\"fawny men\"—fellows who sell bogus jewelry'' in The Century Magazine (Feb. 1894, CERT-2200-206; reprinted 6 Mar. 1894 in the St. Louis Post-Dispatch, CERT-2200-107), this dispatch catches itinerant street peddler Harry Murphy in Hamilton, Ohio under the exact remodeled occupational compound ''\"Phoney\" jewelry man'' while pitching a $3 ''gold'' watch on the street with an accomplice.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-114.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-115', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '''phony'' stones', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1895, '1895-01-02', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_jck_1895-01-02_29_23/page/n29/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-115.webp', 51570, 'c5df43865c4ba9a8f7490f608ac2f60ad2e7f38e256ffc51932a7c202290e1e5', '{"id": "CERT-2200-115", "slug": "cert-2200-115", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "''phony'' stones", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1895, "date_display": "2 Jan 1895", "sort_date": "1895-01-02", "short_gloss": "Earliest jewelry-trade journal attestation (''substituted two \"phony\" stones for the genuine'') in The Jewelers'' Circular (Jan. 2, 1895)", "source_publication": "The Jewelers'' Circular and Horological Review (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_jck_1895-01-02_29_23/page/n29/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 29, No. 23, Western Supplement, p. 30, col. 3 (leaf 29)", "verbatim_quote": "The Mermod-Jaccard Jewelry Co. reported a daring theft last week. A well dressed young fellow entered the store and asked to see some diamonds. A tray of gems was shown him. He substituted two ''phony'' stones for the genuine and slipped away with brilliants worth $800.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1895, "masthead_year": 1895, "linguistic_note": "Demonstrates immediate uptake of ''phony'' in the professional diamond and jewelry trade for paste/imitation gems swapped during pennyweighter confidence thefts—the core material domain of traditional fawney-dropping.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on page 30 running header (''January 2, 1895. THE JEWELERS'' CIRCULAR AND HOROLOGICAL REVIEW'', leaf 29, col. 3, St. Louis correspondence). Antedates previously cited jewelry-press attestations (such as April 1899 Jewelers'' Review) by 4 yrs, 3 mos.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-115.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1895 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-116', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'phonies', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1895, '1895-07-20', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1895-07-20_46_344/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-116.webp', 108706, '273ae04fc2cee2d951c23f05ab65b2739e67a941e7618fd1051f7fe2113a8536', '{"id": "CERT-2200-116", "slug": "cert-2200-116", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "phonies", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1895, "date_display": "20 Jul 1895", "sort_date": "1895-07-20", "short_gloss": "Major nominal antedating: plural count noun ''phonies'' (''his pasteboards were all \"phonies\"'') in St. Louis Post-Dispatch (July 20, 1895)", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1895-07-20_46_344/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 46, No. 344, p. 8, col. 2 (leaf 7; front masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "when his horse beat Saragossa a neck he went in the ring to cash about $7,000 worth of tickets. The first book he struck was that of Marcus Cartwright, and the St. Louisan''s disappointment can be imagined when the cashier told him his pasteboards were all ''phonies.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1895, "masthead_year": 1895, "linguistic_note": "Elliptical substantivization of adjectival ''phony [tickets]'' -> plural count noun ''phonies'' (''counterfeit betting tickets''), predating OED3 and GDoS''s earliest nominal phoney, n. baseline (1902 Clarence Louis Cullen) by 6 yrs, 8 mos, and OED3''s earliest plural ''phonies'' quotation (Nov. 28, 1916 San Francisco Call & Post) by 21 yrs, 4 mos.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on front masthead (leaf 0) and p. 8 running header (''ST. LOUIS POST-DISPATCH, SATURDAY, JULY 20, 1895'', leaf 7, col. 2). Proves that nominal conversion (''phonies'' = counterfeit items) occurred directly inside American betting-ring speech within two years of adjectival ''phony ticket'' (1893).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-116.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1895 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-208', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'a ''phony''', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1896, '1896-04-09', 'https://archive.org/details/drf1896040901/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-208.webp', 71034, '8b9497d76aa6655d7998285eae432b2c76ae89ec13d8f9f9de0b500c91d6edf9', '{"id": "CERT-2200-208", "slug": "cert-2200-208", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "a ''phony''", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1896, "date_display": "9 Apr 1896", "sort_date": "1896-04-09", "short_gloss": "Singular count noun ''a \"phony\"'' denoting a bogus $5 gold coin (pool check) passed in San Francisco''s Bay District betting ring, 6 yrs before OED3 1902", "source_publication": "Daily Racing Form (Chicago, IL)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Daily Racing Form Archive)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/drf1896040901/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 2, p. 1, col. 2 (leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "Dave Higgins, a bright writer, tells a story to the point about a ''phony'' taking in the Bay District ring. It was about one of George Rose''s book, of which Otis Evans was the blockman, and runs this way: \"Say, Otis, what this!\" asked Henry Flittner as he handed up a pool check of the size of a five-dollar gold piece. \"Did I take that thing in?\" replied Evans.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1896, "masthead_year": 1896, "linguistic_note": "Concrete singular count noun a \"phony\" (''a spurious coin / brass pool token passed off as a $5 gold piece''), demonstrating direct nominal transfer from gilt-brass rings to gilt-brass/metal tokens passed as gold currency.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on p. 1 of the April 9, 1896 Daily Racing Form (masthead ''APRIL 9, 1896. PRICE, 10 CENTS''). Antedates OED3''s and Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s 1902 singular noun baseline (C. L. Cullen, More Ex-Tank Tales, p. 99) by 6 years, and precedes the Dec. 15, 1896 Daily Racing Form racetrack sham-horse noun (''She was a phony'', CERT-2200-118) by 8 months, providing the earliest singular nominal attestation of ''a phony'' in the counterfeit-coin sense.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-208.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1896 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-117', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '''phoney'' Napoleon', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1896, '1896-07-26', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1896-07-26_47_351/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-117.webp', 120458, 'eaef3d720798b89a475600565eee6e6dc4abeb7bd131997a4cf59d2cbc7e1c82', '{"id": "CERT-2200-117", "slug": "cert-2200-117", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "''phoney'' Napoleon", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1896, "date_display": "26 Jul 1896", "sort_date": "1896-07-26", "short_gloss": "Personal sham/pretender adjectival sense (''the \"phoney\" Napoleon from Ohio'' = William McKinley) in St. Louis Post-Dispatch (July 26, 1896)", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1896-07-26_47_351/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 47, No. 351, p. 8, col. 2 (leaf 7; front masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "Immediately after McKinley was nominated Supt. Pape, who is himself a florist, drove in hot haste to Forest Park, and within an hour and a half after the ''phoney'' Napoleon from Ohio had been nominated as the Hanna standard bearer, his name was emblazoned on the soil of Forest Park", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1896, "masthead_year": 1896, "linguistic_note": "Extends adjectival phoney from fraudulent objects/swindles to a pretentious or ersatz public persona (''the \"phoney\" Napoleon from Ohio''), prefiguring 20th-century psychological/social ''phony'' (''insincere person, impostor'') 4 years before George Ade''s More Fables (1900).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on p. 8 running header (''SUNDAY MORNING, JULY 26, 1896'', leaf 7, col. 2). Corroborates Henry M. Blossom Jr.''s 1896 turf novel Checkers: A Hard-Luck Story (Chicago: Herbert S. Stone & Co., p. 224, https://archive.org/details/checkershardluck00blosiala/page/224/mode/1up) and the March 3, 1896 Washington Post (''phoney'' game) in OED3.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-117.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1896 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-002', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '"phoney" ticket', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1896, '1896-10-01', 'https://archive.org/details/checkershardluck00blosiala/page/n245/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-002.webp', 106238, 'e6ac770f5d4d05a8194e294b5443a9df0816a56a28cddf6c7c87ec8eb813ca86', '{"id": "CERT-2200-002", "slug": "cert-2200-002", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "\"phoney\" ticket", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1896, "date_display": "1896 (1901 imp.)", "sort_date": "1896-10-01", "short_gloss": "Bestselling Chicago turf novel diffusing ''the \"phoney\" ticket'' into national American slang", "source_publication": "Henry M. Blossom, Jr., Checkers: A Hard-Luck Story (Chicago & New York: Herbert S. Stone & Co., copyright 1896; 1901 [MDCCCCI] 38th-thousand impression of 1896 plates), p. 224 (leaf 245)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/checkershardluck00blosiala/page/n245/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 224 (leaf 245)", "verbatim_quote": "He rotated between the various \"merry-go-rounds,\" which were open all seasons of the year. The tout''s stock devices—the \"bank-roll\" game, the \"phoney\" ticket, the \"jockey''s cousin\"—he worked with better success than most; but, as a rule, his method was simple.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1896, "masthead_year": 1901, "linguistic_note": "Canonical Green''s Dictionary of Slang & HDAS 1896 literary baseline pairing ''phoney'' with quotation marks in Chicago/Washington Park racetrack tout argot", "analysis_notes": "Cited in Green''s Dictionary of Slang (s.v. phoney, adj.) and Lighter''s HDAS, Henry M. Blossom Jr.''s runaway 1896 Chicago racing novel Checkers (verified on Herbert S. Stone & Co.''s 1896-copyrighted stereotype plates, 38th-thousand impression dated MDCCCCI on leaf 10 and ''COPYRIGHT, 1896, BY HERBERT S. STONE & CO.'' on leaf 11) cataloged ''the \"phoney\" ticket'' among the racetrack tout''s standard confidence tricks—accelerating the word''s leap from Midwestern betting rings (CERT-2200-110, CERT-2200-111) into national middle-class readership.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-002.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1896 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-118', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'a phony', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1896, '1896-12-15', 'https://archive.org/details/drf1896121501/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-118.webp', 110018, '2829d7a0d20de20261543e45df1ceaeeca77bbcd0d0d393d05b51d6fafc6f9ba', '{"id": "CERT-2200-118", "slug": "cert-2200-118", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "a phony", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1896, "date_display": "15 Dec 1896", "sort_date": "1896-12-15", "short_gloss": "Unquoted singular count noun ''a phony'' (''Etack couldn''t untrack herself. She was a phony'') in Daily Racing Form (Dec. 15, 1896)", "source_publication": "Daily Racing Form (Chicago, IL)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Daily Racing Form Archive)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/drf1896121501/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 2, No. 225, p. 3, col. 1 (leaf 2; front masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "Etack couldn''t untrack herself. She was a phony. Early Notice will not do.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1896, "masthead_year": 1896, "linguistic_note": "Fully lexicalized, unquoted singular indefinite count noun ''a phony'' (''an impostor/fraudulent contender that fails to perform as touted''), antedating OED3 and GDoS''s 1902 singular noun baseline (''git away wit'' a phony like dat'', Cullen) by 5 yrs, 4 mos.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on front-page masthead (''DAILY RACING FORM. VOL. II. NO. 225. CHICAGO, TUESDAY, DECEMBER 15, 1896'', leaf 0) and p. 3 New Orleans chart notes (leaf 2, col. 1). Paired with Dec. 2, 1896 Daily Racing Form p. 1 (''Bernard ran like a \"phony.\"'', https://archive.org/details/drf1896120201/page/n0/mode/1up), proving routine singular nominal usage by late 1896.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-118.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1896 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-119', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '''fawny'' watch', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1897, '1897-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/ourrivalrascalfa00eldr/page/220/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-119.webp', 75614, '3b943af613ebdedd93a8db106c223bea33ffbb6eff89b72a71ce688289dd6738', '{"id": "CERT-2200-119", "slug": "cert-2200-119", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "''fawny'' watch", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1897, "date_display": "1897", "sort_date": "1897-01-01", "short_gloss": "Boston Police Inspectors Eldridge & Watts (1897) attesting attributive cant ''\"fawny\" watch'' in the street ''drop game''", "source_publication": "Benjamin P. Eldridge & William B. Watts, Our Rival, the Rascal: A Faithful Portrayal of the Conflict Between the Criminals of This Age and the Defenders of Society—The Police (Boston: Pemberton Publishing Co.)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/ourrivalrascalfa00eldr/page/220/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 220 (leaf 300; title page leaf 11)", "verbatim_quote": "If the ''drop game'' has been worn too thin in any city, it is likely that the ''confidence man'' will vary his performance by pulling out of his pocket what is known to his craft as a ''fawny'' watch.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1897, "masthead_year": 1897, "linguistic_note": "Attributive adjectival conversion of cant fawny (''a \"fawny\" watch'' = brass/bogus confidence watch used in the ring-drop variation), parallel to 1894 ''\"Phoney\" jewelry man'' selling a $3 watch (CERT-2200-114).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on 1897 title page (leaf 11) and p. 220 (leaf 300), calibrating Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s baseline for fawney, adj. (1897) and confirming from senior Boston police detectives that ''fawny'' directly modified bogus watches in confidence-game cant.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-119.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1897 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-209', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'foney coin / "phony" delegations', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1897, '1897-03-07', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1897-03-07_48_210/page/n26/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-209.webp', 206864, '336074e7dac0ad5adeeb42a6832b0d3115b8a35244d06c1e32f0293a041581b5', '{"id": "CERT-2200-209", "slug": "cert-2200-209", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "foney coin / \"phony\" delegations", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1897, "date_display": "7 Mar 1897", "sort_date": "1897-03-07", "short_gloss": "Synchronic co-occurrence in the same March 7, 1897 newspaper issue of phonetic ''foney coin'' (p. 26) and Neo-Greek ''\"phony\" delegations'' (p. 6)", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1897-03-07_48_210/page/n26/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 48, No. 210, p. 26, col. 4 (leaf 26) & p. 6, col. 3 (leaf 6)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 26:] ''Bout 9 o''clock Doolin had t''irteen battles wit'' suckers dat refused tuh perduce, w''at had Hoolan''s tickets, an'' ''e had tuh send Hog-Eye Scanlan down tuh take ''es place an'' give ''im a chanst tuh git duh blood out o'' ''es lamps, so''s ''e wouldn''t make no mistake an'' take in foney coin. [p. 6:] Therefore a large number of \"phony\" delegations were filed yesterday.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1897, "masthead_year": 1897, "linguistic_note": "Captures the orthographic transition fawney -> foney -> phony in vivo inside a single newspaper issue: the vernacular tough-dialect sketch spells /foʊni/ phonetically with initial <f-> (''foney coin''), while the political news page spells the identical word with hypercorrective Neo-Greek <ph-> (''\"phony\" delegations'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified across Front-Page Masthead (Sunday Morning, March 7, 1897, Vol. 48, No. 210), p. 26 (''The Rosy O''Grady Ball Was a Complete Success and It Yielded Coin for Keeps'': ''take in foney coin''), and p. 6 (''\"phony\" delegations'' — correcting raw OCR ''delegates''). Refutes any claim that <foney> was merely a late respelling of <phoney>: <f-> and <ph-> competed side-by-side in 1890s urban newsrooms, with Neo-Greek <ph-> winning in formal/editorial columns on the model of telephone, phonograph, and symphony.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-209.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1897 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-210', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'a "phony"', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1897, '1897-05-04', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1897-05-04_48_268/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-210.webp', 132134, '7f0e7725c64e49d4fce0a17318e69492c5e9f8299a04281333079b6cd6fd520c', '{"id": "CERT-2200-210", "slug": "cert-2200-210", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "a \"phony\"", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1897, "date_display": "4 May 1897", "sort_date": "1897-05-04", "short_gloss": "Earliest scan-verified nominal ''a \"phony\"'' denoting a bogus pawned diamond stud (5 yrs before OED3 1902 noun; 11 yrs before Green''s 1908 ''phoneys'')", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_st-louis-post-dispatch_1897-05-04_48_268/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 48, No. 268, p. 3, col. 4 (leaf 2; masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "BOUGHT A DIAMOND. A Restaurant Man''s Experience in Buying Jewels. ... A Jew, giving the name of Mayenthal came to him with a pawn ticket issued by the Central Loan Office, 204 North Fourth street. The ticket was for a diamond stud which had been pawned for $55. It was easily worth $100, according to the Jew. ... Colby paid the amount and took the stud. Two hours later he learned it was a \"phony.\" He returned to the loan agency and wanted to pawn it again.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1897, "masthead_year": 1897, "linguistic_note": "Nominal count use a \"phony\" (''an imitation diamond stud used in a pawn-ticket confidence game''), directly continuing cant spark-fawney / fawney (''ring/jewel in a confidence swindle'') with shifted nominal semantics (''fake jewel'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on Tuesday Evening, May 4, 1897 (Vol. 48, No. 268, leaf 2). Antedates OED3''s 1902 general noun baseline (Cullen, More Ex-Tank Tales, p. 99) by 5 years and antedates Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s earliest fake-jewel noun citation (1908 Helen Green, The Maison de Shine, p. 10: ''they better not hand you no phoneys, neither... git the stones tested'') by 11 years.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-210.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1897 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-211', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '"phony" rings', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1897, '1897-09-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_jck_1897-09-01_35_5/page/n31/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-211.webp', 97938, '3d067f58ecda8252ea2dc3fdb24dfd2339f7c91c39ec0b68c76ec342b9f57d14', '{"id": "CERT-2200-211", "slug": "cert-2200-211", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "\"phony\" rings", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1897, "date_display": "1 Sep 1897", "sort_date": "1897-09-01", "short_gloss": "The Jewelers'' Circular (Sept. 1, 1897) documenting Chicago brass ''\"phony\" rings'' sold off the finger with a hard-luck story", "source_publication": "The Jewelers'' Circular and Horological Review (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_jck_1897-09-01_35_5/page/n31/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 35, No. 5, p. 30, col. 2 (leaf 31; cover leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "One of the shrewdest thieves in the county jail was discharged Aug. 25 and told to leave town. He was Jim Williams, alias Jim Friend. He was in six months for theft. Soon after being incarcerated Williams had a box of two dozen brass rings, known as \"phony\" rings, sent from Chicago, and he would put one on his finger and tell a pitiful tale to some visitor about its value, that he needed money, etc. The result was he disposed of almost all his stock before he was detected, and made over $100 in one month.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1897, "masthead_year": 1897, "linguistic_note": "Explicit trade-press identification of Wholesale Chicago gilt-brass confidence rings as ''\"phony\" rings'', replacing earlier Jewelers'' Circular trade terminology (''snide jewelry'', e.g. Vol. 27, No. 23, Jan. 3, 1894, p. 22) while preserving the exact 18th/19th-century fawney-rig confidence script.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified in America''s flagship jewelry-trade weekly (Vol. XXXV, No. 5, Sept. 1, 1897, leaf 31; following Jan. 2, 1895 Jewelers'' Circular ''\"phony\" stones'', CERT-2200-115). Demonstrates functional 1-to-1 continuity with Parker (1781) and Hotten (1859): the swindler orders wholesale brass rings (''known as \"phony\" rings'') by the dozen from a Chicago supply house, slips one onto his own finger, and pitches a distressed-owner story to sell it at a huge markup.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-211.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1897 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-212', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '"PHONY MEN." / "phony coin."', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1898, '1898-11-18', 'https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_1898-11-18_50_93/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-212.webp', 216228, '5d7342c142a233faeb5630ce3ac56a5046fcab20dfd4346bd8a2e69e3f1deff1', '{"id": "CERT-2200-212", "slug": "cert-2200-212", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "\"PHONY MEN.\" / \"phony coin.\"", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1898, "date_display": "18 Nov 1898", "sort_date": "1898-11-18", "short_gloss": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch headline ''OVERRUN BY \"PHONY MEN.\"'' extending the occupational compound to counterfeit-money pushers", "source_publication": "St. Louis Post-Dispatch (St. Louis, MO)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_st-louis-post-dispatch_1898-11-18_50_93/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 50, No. 93, p. 2, col. 3 (leaf 1; masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "OVERRUN BY \"PHONY MEN.\" Missouri the Banner State for Counterfeiters. United States District Judge Elmer B. Adams declares Missouri is overrun with counterfeiters, and men engaged in the congenial occupation of passing bogus money. ... It was shown in the evidence Friday that the prisoner had been warned about palming off the \"phony coin.\" His defense was that the bills were given to him in change at his hotel.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1898, "masthead_year": 1898, "linguistic_note": "Demonstrates semantic extension of the occupational plural \"phony men\" from peddlers of spurious jewelry (1894) to passers of counterfeit bank-notes and base coin (''palming off the \"phony coin\"''), corroborated in the May 2, 1903 Decatur Herald (''Looking for Phoney Men. Secret service men are looking for counterfeiters'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on leaf 1 of the Nov. 18, 1898 St. Louis Post-Dispatch (independently noted in CERT-2200-212). Antedates Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s June 30, 1900 National Police Gazette ''phony men'' citation by 1 year and 7 months, and shows how Chicago-to-St. Louis underworld traffic generalized ''phony man'' across both spurious metal trades (gilt jewelry and base/counterfeit currency).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-212.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1898 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-213', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'selling phony jewelry', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1898, '1898-11-30', 'https://archive.org/details/lima-news-1898-11-30/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-213.webp', 183864, '399542e52cc1f5f151177eb6c6162ac2fc3cd9976be29c107af67d612b34e447', '{"id": "CERT-2200-213", "slug": "cert-2200-213", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "selling phony jewelry", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1898, "date_display": "30 Nov 1898", "sort_date": "1898-11-30", "short_gloss": "Single itinerant operative arrested in Lima, Ohio both ''selling phony jewelry'' and carrying 200 plaster-cast counterfeit nickels", "source_publication": "The Lima News (Lima, OH)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/lima-news-1898-11-30/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 5, col. 3 (leaf 4; masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "The confessed counterfeiter is a man perhaps 38 years of age, and has the appearance of a \"shark.\" ... When asked if he lived in Detroit he said that his mother lived there, but that he had traveled about from place to place selling phony jewelry, etc. When arrested about 200 nickels were found on his person, all of which were splendid and none but an expert could have told as to their genuineness. It seems that the coin was made in a plaster of paris cast and after he had molded a big batch, the casts were destroyed.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1898, "masthead_year": 1898, "linguistic_note": "Unquoted journalistic/police-court phrase ''selling phony jewelry'' directly alongside counterfeit coin manufacture in the same criminal biography, explaining why underworld cant transferred phoney seamlessly between bogus jewelry and counterfeit coin.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on leaf 4 of The Lima News (Wednesday, November 30, 1898; masthead leaf 0). Provides concrete prosopographical proof that the same itinerant grifters who peddled ''phony jewelry'' from town to town also molded and shoved counterfeit coins (''shoving the phoney''), uniting the jewelry and counterfeit-money senses in a single practitioner.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-213.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1898 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-214', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'foney watch deal', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1899, '1899-06-28', 'https://archive.org/details/jewelersreviewv00unkngoog/page/n293/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-214.webp', 142126, 'bf55e40c8b528af53a8048c1ecb9cfc065bd3b9c4e0371e01e50325db6efa138', '{"id": "CERT-2200-214", "slug": "cert-2200-214", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "foney watch deal", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1899, "date_display": "28 Jun 1899", "sort_date": "1899-06-28", "short_gloss": "Transitional <foney> spelling in New York''s Jewelers Review (June 28, 1899): pawnbrokers fined $100 each on ''a foney watch deal''", "source_publication": "The Jewelers Review (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/jewelersreviewv00unkngoog/page/n293/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 32, June 28, 1899, p. 827, col. 1 (leaf 293)", "verbatim_quote": "MINNEAPOLIS ... Jacob Levy, Louis Levey and A. B. Washburn, pawnbrokers, were fined $100 each, for swindling a Mr. Yost on a foney watch deal. The police have tried hard to stop the swindling of people who are not judges of watches, or do not suspect dishonest dealing; the jewelers are very delighted at the action Judge Holt took in imposing such a heavy and deserving fine.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1899, "masthead_year": 1899, "linguistic_note": "Unquoted trade-journal attestation of transitional initial-<f> spelling foney (''spurious, sham, fraudulent'') inside pawnbroker and horological trade reporting, preserving the original /f/ grapheme of fawney after vowel closing (/ɔː/ -> /oʊ/).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on p. 827 (leaf 293) of The Jewelers Review with the printed running header ''June 28, 1899 | THE JEWELERS REVIEW | 827'' directly atop the page. Confirms that <foney> was actively printed inside the American jewelry and pawnshop trade press at the end of the 1890s before <phoney>/<phony> standardized.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-214.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1899 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-121', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'phony keys', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1900, '1900-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/talesofextanks0000clar/page/113/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-121.webp', 82384, '62a497a1a8c92d79bb6ec5a8e3c49c6a8edc96a7fb808b71cf0c6be3892ed527', '{"id": "CERT-2200-121", "slug": "cert-2200-121", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "phony keys", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1900, "date_display": "1900 [c. 1899-1900]", "sort_date": "1900-01-01", "short_gloss": "Clarence Louis Cullen''s first Ex-Tank volume (Tales of the Ex-Tanks, 1900, p. 113: ''phony keys'') & bibliographic correction of OED3''s 1902 title", "source_publication": "Clarence Louis Cullen, Tales of the Ex-Tanks: A Book of Hard-Luck Stories (New York: Grosset & Dunlap; copyright 1899 & 1900 by The Sun Printing and Publishing Association)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/talesofextanks0000clar/page/113/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 113 (leaf 119; title and New York Sun 1899-1900 copyright leaves 7-8)", "verbatim_quote": "''You look like zinc money after it''s been run over and stepped on,'' replied the cop with brutal candor, ''and it ain''t no cinch, but what you''d turn a trick with the wedge and the phony keys at that''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1900, "masthead_year": 1900, "linguistic_note": "Unquoted burglar-cant ''phony keys'' (''skeleton/false keys'') originally serialized in the New York Sun (1899-1900), alongside George Ade''s More Fables (1900, p. 138, ''Phoney Acting'', https://archive.org/details/morefables1900ha0000geor/page/138/mode/1up).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on title/copyright leaves 7-8 and p. 113 (leaf 119). Also resolves a bibliographic error in OED3 s.v. phoney, n. (B.1), which cites ''1902 C. L. Cullen Six Ex-tank Tales 99'': Publishers'' Weekly (May 10, 1902, Vol. 61, No. 19, https://archive.org/details/sim_publishers-weekly_1902-05-10_61_19) and GDoS confirm the actual title published April 5, 1902 by J. S. Ogilvie was More Ex-Tank Tales (''Six'' misread from cursive/abbreviated ''More'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-121.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1900 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-216', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'phony men', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1900, '1900-06-30', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_national-police-gazette_1900-06-30_76_1193/page/n5/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-216.webp', 73766, 'fd2863c9dffbaebe404cc46c7776ac6996731ce99d2f597adf38584d1fb3b1bc', '{"id": "CERT-2200-216", "slug": "cert-2200-216", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "phony men", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1900, "date_display": "30 Jun 1900", "sort_date": "1900-06-30", "short_gloss": "Green''s 1900 ''phony men'' baseline scan-verified in National Police Gazette: ''the phony men, who peddle bogus jewelry''", "source_publication": "The National Police Gazette (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_national-police-gazette_1900-06-30_76_1193/page/n5/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 76, No. 1193, p. 6, col. 1 (leaf 5; editorial masthead leaf 1)", "verbatim_quote": "It was to be open house and no denizen of trampdom was excluded. Blanket stiffs, gay cats, stake men, blokies, crooks, phony men, prushuns and ex-prushuns ... were present. ... The crooks are the elite of trampdom. They average higher in intelligence, because they have to exercise their wits to live by dishonest means, and the phony men, who peddle bogus jewelry, are almost in the same class.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1900, "masthead_year": 1900, "linguistic_note": "Clinches lexical identity between Anglo-American tramp cant fawny men and phony men: the Police Gazette''s second-paragraph gloss (''the phony men, who peddle bogus jewelry'') reproduces Josiah Flynt''s 1894/1899 definition (''\"fawny men\"—fellows who sell bogus jewelry'' / ''Fawny Man: a peddler of bogus jewelry'') almost word for word inside the identical Buffalo/New York hobo social hierarchy.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verification and expansion of Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s baseline quotation under fawney-man (also phony-man). Green''s quotes only the first list sentence (''Blanket stiffs, gay cats... phony men''), omitting the decisive definitional clause fifteen lines lower in the same column (''and the phony men, who peddle bogus jewelry, are almost in the same class'')—which occurs at a hobo convention outside Buffalo, NY, the very city where Flynt''s ''fawny man'' Liverpool George bought his $2-a-dozen rings in 1894.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-216.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1900 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-003', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'Phoney Acting', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1900, '1900-11-01', 'https://archive.org/details/morefables00adegiala/page/n151/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-003.webp', 139896, '2cc003a0fcd0aa7a249353ca2b65479c79841c8ff30fbe1c440979a39a8ac02f', '{"id": "CERT-2200-003", "slug": "cert-2200-003", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "Phoney Acting", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1900, "date_display": "1900", "sort_date": "1900-11-01", "short_gloss": "Landmark semantic shift from counterfeit objects/tickets to affected, insincere human behavior (''Phoney Acting'')", "source_publication": "George Ade, More Fables [running title: More Fables in Slang] (Chicago & New York: Herbert S. Stone & Company, 1900 [MDCCCC]), ''The Fable of the Cruel Insult and the Arrival of the Lover from No. 6,'' p. 138 (leaf 151)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/morefables00adegiala/page/n151/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 138 (leaf 151)", "verbatim_quote": "The Sensitive Waitress hurried Away, feeling hurt. \"Overlook all the Phoney Acting by the Little Lady, Bud,\" said the Fireman to the Advance Agent. \"She''s only twenty-seven.\" Producing a small Note-Book, the English Traveler said: \"Gentlemen, I regard this Incident as Most Extraordinary and somewhat Mystifying. I fear that I am not sufficiently acquainted with your Vernacular to grasp the full Purport of what has occurred here.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1900, "masthead_year": 1900, "linguistic_note": "OED3 & Green''s Dictionary of Slang 1900 literary milestone extending adjectival phoney from fraudulent goods/betting slips to social affectation, pretension, and emotional insincerity—explicitly marked as unintelligible to a visiting ''English Traveler''", "analysis_notes": "Verified on the genuine 1900 Herbert S. Stone & Co. first edition (leaf 8 imprint ''MDCCCC''), George Ade''s dialogue performs two crucial philological functions at once: first, ''Phoney Acting'' extends adjectival phoney into psychological and social insincerity (the modern everyday sense later immortalized in J. D. Salinger''s Catcher in the Rye); second, Ade''s visiting ''English Traveler'' takes out a notebook because he cannot understand the American ''Vernacular''—corroborating Farmer & Henley''s 1902 omission (CERT-2200-004) that phoney was strictly a North American remodeling unknown in contemporary Britain.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-003.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1900 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-217', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'a "phony"', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1901, '1901-04-17', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_jck_1901-04-17_42_11/page/n43/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-217.webp', 103482, 'bebc1117cf295f7e43cfa4037f3ef8b2b0189712c626c9fbb63e2e1b64861e32', '{"id": "CERT-2200-217", "slug": "cert-2200-217", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "a \"phony\"", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1901, "date_display": "17 Apr 1901", "sort_date": "1901-04-17", "short_gloss": "Pre-1902 pennyweight ring-switch count noun in Jewelers'' Circular (Apr. 17, 1901): ''$300 diamond ring... taken from the tray and a \"phony\" substituted''", "source_publication": "The Jewelers'' Circular-Weekly (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_jck_1901-04-17_42_11/page/n43/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 42, No. 11, p. 43, col. 2 (leaf 43; cover leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "Loring Andrews & Co. were a few days ago robbed of a $300 diamond ring by an old trick. The ring was taken from the tray and a \"phony\" substituted. The man supposed to have played it was in the store two weeks ago. He was in twice, so it is presumed he called the first time to see the ring he wanted and get a proper substitute.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1901, "masthead_year": 1901, "linguistic_note": "Concrete jewelry-trade count noun a \"phony\" (''a paste replica diamond ring prepared for a pennyweight tray switch''), bridging May 1897 ''a \"phony\"'' (CERT-2200-210) and Hutchins Hapgood''s May 1903 ''fauny shop (imitation jewelry)'' pennyweight narrative (CERT-2200-220).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified in the Cincinnati trade correspondence of The Jewelers'' Circular-Weekly (April 17, 1901, p. 43). Antedates OED3''s 1902 noun baseline by one year and describes step-for-step the identical two-visit pennyweight diamond-switching operation narrated in Hapgood''s Autobiography of a Thief (1903, pp. 56-57), where the thief prices rings on a jeweler''s tray and buys matching paste substitutes at a ''fauny shop''. Compare also Jewelers'' Circular July 4, 1900 (p. 36: ''a lot of \"phony\" jewelry'') and July 1, 1903 (p. 49: rings bought from Brooks Optical Co., Erie, Pa., at $1 a dozen).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-217.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1901 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-004', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'PHOENIX-NEST -> PHYSIC [0 PHONEY/PHONY]', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue05farmuoft/page/n186/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-004.webp', 70268, 'cc263a2f3d2459d5d9013f1eccef154607cacd35621ba4b1fffac83b264b577c', '{"id": "CERT-2200-004", "slug": "cert-2200-004", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "PHOENIX-NEST -> PHYSIC [0 PHONEY/PHONY]", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "1902", "sort_date": "1902-01-01", "short_gloss": "1902 British slang lexicon negative control: Vol. V jumps from PHOENIX-NEST directly to PHYSIC with zero PHONEY entry", "source_publication": "John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues Past and Present, Vol. V: N. to Razzle-dazzle (London: Printed for Subscribers Only, 1902 [MCMII]), p. 183, col. 2 (leaf 186)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue05farmuoft/page/n186/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 183, col. 2 (leaf 186)", "verbatim_quote": "[Running header:] Phiz. 183 Physic. | PHIZ-GIG, subs. (old).—1. An extravagantly dressed old woman ... PHOENIX-MAN, subs. phr. (old).—See quot.—BAILEY (1726); GROSE (1785). ... PHOENIX-NEST, subs. phr. (venery). ... PHYSIC, subs. (venery).—1. Copulation : see GREENS and RIDE.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1890, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "Decisive transatlantic geographic control: whereas Farmer & Henley entered FAWNEY/FAUNEY (1891) and FORNEY (1893), their 1902 P-volume omits PHONEY/PHONY altogether, proving the /fɔːni/ -> /foʊni/ (<phoney>) shift occurred exclusively in North America", "analysis_notes": "Although Farmer & Henley''s seven-volume historical slang dictionary recorded Anglo-Irish/British FAWNEY, FAUNEY, FAWNEY-DROPPING, FAWNEY-BOUNCING (Vol. II, 1891, p. 378), and FORNEY (Vol. III, 1893, p. 60, CERT-2200-001), Volume V (printed in London in 1902 [MCMII], leaf 4; cataloged under the 1890 Vol. I series start date) steps on p. 183 directly from PHOENIX-NEST to PHYSIC without recording PHONEY or PHONY. Ten years after Washington, Chicago, New York, and St. Louis newspapers had normalized phony/phoney (1892–1901), the <ph-> word remained an exclusively American innovation.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-004.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-219', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '"phoney" ring peddler', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1902, '1902-06-17', 'https://archive.org/details/fort-wayne-sentinel-1902-06-17/page/n3/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-219.webp', 155876, '2c3c4a0374347780ee6d97b4bcb21349b8826e4c4de210e27ba9d50476936bbe', '{"id": "CERT-2200-219", "slug": "cert-2200-219", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "\"phoney\" ring peddler", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1902, "date_display": "17 Jun 1902", "sort_date": "1902-06-17", "short_gloss": "Fort Wayne Sentinel (June 17, 1902) profiling a ''\"phoney\" ring peddler'' selling $1-a-dozen gilt-brass rings stamped ''18 k.'' and ''Joe to Sue''", "source_publication": "The Fort Wayne Sentinel (Fort Wayne, IN)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/fort-wayne-sentinel-1902-06-17/page/n3/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 2 (leaf 3; masthead leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "Martin was arrested yesterday afternoon by Superintendent Gorsline and he was proven to be a bad actor. Martin is a \"phoney\" ring peddler and the superintendent yesterday sent out his description to the officers with instructions to pick him up wherever found. Martin''s graft is selling brass rings, marked 18 k., with an engraved mark on the inside. These marks usually are \"M. to L.\" or \"Joe to Sue\" or something of that kind, indicating that the thing is of considerable value, whereas as a matter of fact they can be bought already marked in a variety of ways for about $1 a dozen. They are golded and for a day or two look like real gold.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1902, "masthead_year": 1902, "linguistic_note": "Explicit occupational compound ''\"phoney\" ring peddler'' showing the exact merchandise and deceptive mechanism of the historic fawney-dropper: factory-engraved sentimental inscriptions (''M. to L.'', ''Joe to Sue'') inside 18k-stamped gilt-brass rings sold at $1 a dozen wholesale to simulate lost or pawned personal rings.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on p. 4 (leaf 3) of The Fort Wayne Sentinel (Tuesday, June 17, 1902). Answers how street swindlers made gullible strangers believe a brass ring was a genuine personal heirloom: novelty wholesalers supplied ''golded'' brass rings pre-stamped ''18 k.'' and pre-engraved inside the band with fake initials or lovers'' inscriptions.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-219.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1902 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-220', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'fauny shop', 'attestation', 'prior_attestation_calibration', 'agent_verified', 1903, '1903-05-01', 'https://archive.org/details/autobiographyoft00hapgiala/page/57/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-220.webp', 57880, '5a863ed1274383029fb71f9c6bc4253a14c42c0c2d36c4ba916dbe07d921dfd0', '{"id": "CERT-2200-220", "slug": "cert-2200-220", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "fauny shop", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_attestation_calibration", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1903, "date_display": "May 1903", "sort_date": "1903-05-01", "short_gloss": "Hutchins Hapgood''s Autobiography of a Thief (May 1903) recording NYC thief cant ''fauny shop (imitation jewelry)'' for pennyweight switches", "source_publication": "Hutchins Hapgood, The Autobiography of a Thief (New York: Fox, Duffield & Company, May 1903)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (University of California Libraries)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/autobiographyoft00hapgiala/page/57/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title & Copyright leaves (JP2 #0009-#0010, ''Published May, 1903'') & pp. 56-57 (JP2 #0062-#0063)", "verbatim_quote": "A good graft starts out well dressed, enters a jewelry store and looks at some diamond rings on a tray. He prices them and notes the costly ones. Then he goes to a fauny shop (imitation jewelry) and buys a few diamonds which match the real ones he has noted. Then he and his pal, usually a woman, enter the jewelry store and ask to see the rings. Through some little \"con\" they distract the jeweler''s attention, and then one of them (and at this Sheenie Annie was particularly good) substitutes the bogus diamonds for the good ones; and leaves the store without making a purchase.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1903, "masthead_year": 1903, "linguistic_note": "Crucial semantic link inside New York Pickpocket Jim''s authentic Sixth Ward / Bowery cant: un-remodeled fauny (variant of fawney ''ring'', cf. Barrere & Leland 1889 ''fashino fauny'') ALREADY means ''imitation jewelry'' collectively in ''fauny shop (imitation jewelry)'', proving that the semantic shift ''ring'' -> ''imitation jewelry'' preceded or accompanied the orthographic shift <fauny/foney> -> <phoney>.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the May 1903 first edition (pp. 56-57, JP2 #0062-#0063; cited in Green''s Dictionary of Slang under fawney-shop). Full-text audit of Hapgood''s 468,978-character narrative confirms 0 occurrences of <phoney>/<phony> alongside regular use of older NYC cant ''fauny shop'', ''bang a super'' (steal a watch, p. 45), and ''front'' (watch and chain, p. 46)—showing that veteran New York thieves born in the 1860s-1870s still pronounced/spelled the imitation-jewelry word as ''fauny'' while younger reporters spelled it ''phoney''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-220.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1903 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-221', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '"phoney lay-outs" / the phoney men', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1903, '1903-11-28', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_saturday-evening-post_1903-11-28_176_22/page/n29/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-221.webp', 166802, '3243b8be2af73d96d0ae6be217765f3ff49b9a3026bec00c870fa1998073b633', '{"id": "CERT-2200-221", "slug": "cert-2200-221", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "\"phoney lay-outs\" / the phoney men", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1903, "date_display": "28 Nov 1903", "sort_date": "1903-11-28", "short_gloss": "Saturday Evening Post (Nov. 28, 1903) circus exposé equating ''the phoney men'' and ''\"phoney lay-outs\"'' with traveling circus grifters", "source_publication": "The Saturday Evening Post (Philadelphia, PA)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_saturday-evening-post_1903-11-28_176_22/page/n29/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 176, No. 22, p. 8, col. 2 (leaf 8) & p. 30, col. 1 (leaf 29; cover leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 8:] The circus had barely got its tents pitched before a line of \"phoney lay-outs\" were being set up directly flanking the entrance from the street. ... [p. 30:] The manager kept his temper and informed him that big circuses no longer carried \"lucky boys.\" ... \"They take it out of us just as if we were phoney,\" they will tell you. \"So we might as well have the phoney men, and then take it out of them!\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1903, "masthead_year": 1903, "linguistic_note": "Documents the integration of ''the phoney men'' and ''\"phoney lay-outs\"'' into traveling circus-lot and privilege-car argot alongside ''lucky boys'' (''thimble-riggers and the like'') and ''capping''.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on leaves 0, 8, and 29 of The Saturday Evening Post (Nov. 28, 1903, ''Following the Circus''). Shows how municipal graft pressured smaller traveling tent shows to carry ''the phoney men''—broadening the circus occupational term from midway fake-jewelry pitchmen to crooked midway concessionaires and privilege-car grifters generally.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-221.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1903 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-218', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'selling foney jewelry', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1905, '1905-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/casjhsj_000358/page/n163/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-218.webp', 60848, '62c12c537bdadca7b07f51276460af2640c1c4abe428ad09e45e8b1b53c09f71', '{"id": "CERT-2200-218", "slug": "cert-2200-218", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "selling foney jewelry", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1905, "date_display": "Jan 1905", "sort_date": "1905-01-01", "short_gloss": "Official Joplin, MO police murder-reward circular (Jan. 1905) warning officers that ''professional yegg men'' travel ''selling foney jewelry''", "source_publication": "J. A. McManamy, Chief of Police, Joplin, MO, ''$500 Reward'' Wanted Circular, in Santa Clara County Sheriff''s Office Wanted Fliers Scrapbook", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (San Jose Public Library / California Room)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/casjhsj_000358/page/n163/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Scrapbook p. 156 (leaf 163; duplicate copy at leaf 179)", "verbatim_quote": "$500 REWARD For the Murder or Murderers of Claud Brice one of our Police Officers, December 31, 1904 ... There seems to be no doubt but what these are the right parties. Officers be careful in making arrest for these men are dangerous. These men will no doubt be selling foney jewelry; professional yegg men. There were four in the gang, but we have one of them not described in this circular. Wire all information to J. A. McMANAMY, Chief of Police, Joplin, Missouri.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1902, "masthead_year": 1905, "linguistic_note": "Official law-enforcement circular printing unquoted phonetic <foney> in ''selling foney jewelry'' as the standard cover trade of itinerant tramp safecrackers (''professional yegg men'').", "analysis_notes": "Multimodal Trap A scrapbook calibration: Internet Archive indexes the multi-year Santa Clara County Sheriff''s scrapbook under its opening year ''1902'', but visual inspection of leaf 163 proves this Joplin, Missouri circular advertises a reward ''For the Murder or Murderers of Claud Brice one of our Police Officers, December 31, 1904'', immediately above a second card annotated in red ink ''January 1905''—pinpointing the circular to January 1905. Demonstrates how hobo safecrackers (''yegg men'') routinely carried ''foney jewelry'' peddling stock as a road cover, preserving the <f-> spelling of fawney in provincial police print.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-218.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1905 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-316', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'Most of the green goods swindlers are now fake wire tappers. Selling phony money grew to be a pretty poor calling', 'rejected_lead', 'folk_etymology_telephone', 'agent_verified', 1905, '1905-01-21', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_national-police-gazette_1905-01-21_86_1432/page/n5/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-316.webp', 51400, 'c6ad34145c8ad9f4b3b1286345a62d8320e31a7c68b3c53bb9a3e415fd2cfa67', '{"id": "CERT-2200-316", "slug": "cert-2200-316", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "Most of the green goods swindlers are now fake wire tappers. Selling phony money grew to be a pretty poor calling", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "folk_etymology_telephone", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1905, "date_display": "21 Jan 1905 (paired w/ 1908)", "sort_date": "1905-01-21", "short_gloss": "Falsifies the early-1900s ''telephone / wiretapper poolroom'' origin hypothesis via The National Police Gazette (21 Jan. 1905, p. 6) and Prosecutor Arthur Train''s True Stories of Crime (1908, p. 103)", "source_publication": "The National Police Gazette (New York, NY), Vol. 86, No. 1432 (21 Jan. 1905), p. 6; paired with Arthur Train, True Stories of Crime from the District Attorney''s Office (New York: Charles Scribner''s Sons, 1908), Ch. IV: ''The Last of the Wire-Tappers,'' p. 103", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_national-police-gazette_1905-01-21_86_1432/page/n5/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Police Gazette Masthead p. 2 (leaf n1) & p. 6, cols. 1-2 (leaf n5); Train 1908 Title leaf (n9) & p. 103 (leaf n115)", "verbatim_quote": "[Police Gazette, 21 Jan. 1905, p. 6:] HOW FAKE WIRE TAPPERS —WORKING THEIR GAME ADROITLY— TRIM THEIR VICTIMS ... The wire-tapping game is the successor of the green goods swindle. Most of the green goods swindlers are now fake wire tappers. Selling phony money grew to be a pretty poor calling when the Post-office officials got after the green goods men for using the United States mail. | [Train 1908, p. 103:] Later on he had installed over a dozen deal tables, each fitted with a complete set of ordinary telegraph instruments and connected with wires which, while apparently passing out of the windows, in reality plunged behind a desk into a small \"dry\" battery.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1905, "masthead_year": 1905, "linguistic_note": "Refutes both versions of the de novo ''telephone'' hypothesis—(a) the Dec. 9, 1904 New York Evening Telegram / 1909 Century Dictionary Supplement guess that detectives called gambling gear ''phony'' because it lacked substance like ''a telephone talk with a supposititious friend'' (CERT-2200-224), and (b) the popular underworld claim that ''phony'' arose from fake telephone wiretapping poolrooms: (1) Chronologically and semantically, 1893-1902 attestations overwhelmingly denote tangible physical counterfeits—bogus jewelry, brass rings, paste diamonds, counterfeit coins, fake betting tickets, and sham racehorses—never distorted voices or telephone calls; (2) Orthographically, clipped ''phone (< telephone, attested from the late 1870s) NEVER loses its Neo-Greek <ph-> in print, whereas ''phony'' coexists from 1897 to 1914 with initial-<f> ''foney coin'' (1897), ''foney watch'' (1899), ''fauny shop'' (1903), ''foney jewelry'' (1905), and ''foney wheel'' (1913); and (3) Technically, as both the Jan. 21, 1905 Police Gazette and Manhattan Assistant D.A. Arthur Train (1908, p. 103, https://archive.org/details/truestoriescrim01traigoog/page/n115/mode/1up) prove, turn-of-the-century ''wire-tappers'' staged fake Western Union Morse TELEGRAPH offices (not telephone exchanges), and ex-green-goods men carried the ALREADY-ESTABLISHED counterfeiters'' phrase ''phony money'' into wiretapping!", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the Jan. 21, 1905 National Police Gazette (leaf n1 masthead; leaf n5 p. 6) and Arthur Train''s 1908 True Stories of Crime (title leaf n9; p. 103 leaf n115). Reverses the causal arrow assumed by early 20th-century newspaper columnists: ''phony'' moved from fake rings and green-goods ''phony money'' INTO wiretapper poolrooms, not out of them.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-316.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1905 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-222', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'PASSING PHONY', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1907, '1907-11-08', 'https://archive.org/details/janesville-daily-gazette-1907-11-08/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-222.webp', 135436, '6b2ae21fc20adb26d4b773116bd801652a54731ee560bec318efc64bff4a0c49', '{"id": "CERT-2200-222", "slug": "cert-2200-222", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "PASSING PHONY", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1907, "date_display": "8 Nov 1907", "sort_date": "1907-11-08", "short_gloss": "Uncount mass noun ''PHONY'' denoting imitation diamond jewelry in Janesville Daily Gazette (Nov. 8, 1907): ''GETS YEAR IN PEN FOR PASSING PHONY''", "source_publication": "The Janesville Daily Gazette (Janesville, WI)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/janesville-daily-gazette-1907-11-08/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 51, No. 212, p. 1, col. 3 (leaf 0)", "verbatim_quote": "GETS YEAR IN PEN FOR PASSING PHONY. Man Is Sentenced One Year In Waupun for Selling an Imitation for Genuine Diamond. La Crosse, Wis., Nov. 8.—Pleading guilty to the charge of obtaining money under false pretenses by disposing of a ring set with a glass stone under the representation that it was a diamond, John Hoffman, a stranger, was sentenced to one year at Waupun.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1907, "masthead_year": 1907, "linguistic_note": "Pure uncount/mass nominalization PHONY (''imitation jewelry / paste diamond rings'', in ''PASSING PHONY''), directly exemplifying OED3''s concrete underworld mass-noun sense (''peddler''s cheap imitation jewelry stock'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the front page of The Janesville Daily Gazette (Friday, Nov. 8, 1907, p. 1, col. 3). Without the subhead and body, ''PASSING PHONY'' might be misread as passing counterfeit paper money; the subhead explicitly glosses ''PASSING PHONY'' as ''Selling an Imitation for Genuine Diamond'' (''disposing of a ring set with a glass stone''), confirming mass-noun ''phony'' = fake ring/jewel stock.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-222.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1907 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-224', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'phony [phone + -y1]', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1909, '1909-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/centurydictionar0012benj/page/n242/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-224.webp', 61496, '27ae6c3f047683f0b2eedddb6ced0f3f9416861a1ace6838ab70c966db0e5265', '{"id": "CERT-2200-224", "slug": "cert-2200-224", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "phony [phone + -y1]", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1909, "date_display": "1909 (cit. 9 Dec 1904)", "sort_date": "1909-01-01", "short_gloss": "Century Dictionary Supplement (1909, p. 994) canonizing the false ''[phone + -y¹]'' etymology from a Dec. 9, 1904 Evening Telegram guess", "source_publication": "Benjamin E. Smith (ed.), The Century Dictionary Supplement, Vol. XII (New York: The Century Co., copyright 1909)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/centurydictionar0012benj/page/n242/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title & Copyright leaves (JP2 #0007-#0008, copyright 1909) & Vol. XII, p. 994, col. 2 (JP2 #0242)", "verbatim_quote": "phony (fo''ni), a. [phone + -y1.] Of the character of a ''phone'' or of the talk heard over a ''phone''; unsubstantial; imitational; spurious. [Slang.] When the District Attorney''s men destroyed the paraphernalia found in the palatial gambling den the articles proved to be what the detectives oddly styled \"phony.\" That is a curious word and implies that, as regards merit, a thing so qualified has no more substance than a telephone talk with a supposititious friend. Evening Telegram, Dec. 9, 1904.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1910, "masthead_year": 1909, "linguistic_note": "Unmasks both the lexicographical origin of the popular ''telephone'' folk etymology and the Neo-Greek orthographic pressure that created it: surrounded on p. 994 by learned Neo-Greek coinages (phonotype, phonotypographic, phonozenograph < Gr. phone ''sound''), cant /foʊni/ (< fawney/foney) had been hypercorrigibly respelled <phony>, misleading a 1904 New York journalist and the 1909 Century editors into deriving the detective slang word from ''phone (''telephone'').", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on Vol. XII, p. 994 (JP2 #0242; copyright 1909 on JP2 #0008). Evaluates and weakens the ''telephone'' derivation (acknowledging the April 4, 1890 Detroit Plaindealer ''quite a \"phoney\" joke'' telephone pun in CERT-2200-108 as playful secondary reinforcement, while proving that core 1893-1903 uses denote tangible brass rings, paste diamonds, counterfeit coins, bogus betting tickets, and crooked gambling gear—never acoustic distortion—and retain <f-> in ''foney coin'' [1897], ''foney watch'' [1899], ''fauny shop'' [1903], and ''foney jewelry'' [1905], which clippings of telephone never exhibit).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-224.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1909 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-304', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'Bawn [bawn] (< badhun) / Bawneen (< ban [bawn])', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1910, '1910-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishaswespeak00joycuoft/page/n228/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-304.webp', 43740, '0583dcd2ed168cbb37aa07bcc233a8a3b97f67216a9e1e0660962612c2ee99c0', '{"id": "CERT-2200-304", "slug": "cert-2200-304", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "Bawn [bawn] (< badhun) / Bawneen (< ban [bawn])", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1910, "date_display": "1910", "sort_date": "1910-01-01", "short_gloss": "P. W. Joyce''s English as We Speak It in Ireland (1910, p. 214) documenting regular Anglo-Irish rounding of Irish long a before nasal to <aw> [o:] (bawn, bawneen)", "source_publication": "P. W. Joyce, English as We Speak It in Ireland (London: Longmans, Green, & Co.; Dublin: M. H. Gill & Son, Ltd., 1910)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (University of Toronto)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishaswespeak00joycuoft/page/n228/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf (n6, dated 1910) & Ch. XIII Vocabulary, p. 214 (leaf n228)", "verbatim_quote": "Bawn; an enclosure near a farmhouse for cattle, sheep, &c.; in some districts, simply a farmyard. Irish badhun [bawn], a cow-keep, from ba, cows, and dun, a keep or fortress. ... Bawneen; a loose whitish jacket of home-made undyed flannel worn by men at out-door work. Very general: banyan in Derry. From Irish ban [bawn], whitish, with the diminutive termination.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1910, "masthead_year": 1910, "linguistic_note": "Phonological pathway control for Stages 1 -> 2 -> 3: (1) Southern/Munster Irish backs and rounds long a (/a:/) before nasals toward [o:], regularly rendered <aw> in Anglo-Irish loanwords (ban -> bawn/bawneen, badhun -> bawn, crubeen/traneen/spalpeen, fainne -> fawney [fo:ni]); (2) In 19th-century Mid-Atlantic and Midwestern American English—which lacked a stable open-mid /o:/ contrast before intervocalic nasals in non-native cant items and frequently alternated Hiberno-English /o:/ ~ /oU/ before /n/ (compare bawn/bone, pawn/pone)—cant fawney [fo:ni] raised and closed before nasal /n/ to [foUni], spelled foney / fony (1897-1914); (3) Neo-Greek <ph-> orthographic attraction (telephone, phonograph, symphony) then remodeled <foney> into <phoney> / <phony>.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on the 1910 first edition (title leaf n6; p. 214, leaf n228). Born in Ballyorgan, County Limerick (Munster) in 1827, native Irish speaker P. W. Joyce transcribes Irish ban and badhun directly with English ''[bawn]'', supplying native orthoepic confirmation of the Munster/Anglo-Irish pre-nasal [o:] vowel reflected in 1781 London cant <Fawney>.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-304.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1910 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-323', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'Is the Reliance Life of Pittsburg playing a phony game with solicitors in San Francisco?', 'rejected_lead', 'serial_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1910, '1910-04-01', 'https://archive.org/details/coastreview7819101910sanf/page/n624/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-323.webp', 54560, '8d683432af774c4f09ac8c90638e6c09a24098bfad40639e9359bf11e49d5bdc', '{"id": "CERT-2200-323", "slug": "cert-2200-323", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "Is the Reliance Life of Pittsburg playing a phony game with solicitors in San Francisco?", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "serial_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1910, "date_display": "Apr 1910 (cat. 1871)", "sort_date": "1910-04-01", "short_gloss": "Trap A 39-year serial founding-year misdate: Internet Archive stamps ''1871'' onto Vol. 78 (April 1910) of San Francisco''s The Coast Review (''playing a phony game'')", "source_publication": "The Coast Review: A Monthly Insurance Journal, Vol. 78 (San Francisco, CA, April 1910 [Internet Archive cataloged 1871]), pp. 715-716", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (San Francisco Public Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/coastreview7819101910sanf/page/n624/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 715, col. 1 (''playing a phony game'', leaf n624) & facing p. 716 header (''COAST FIRE LOSSES / April, 1910'', leaf n625)", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 715:] Is the Reliance Life of Pittsburg playing a phony game with solicitors in San Francisco? It is offering, through its managers, Tom and Dick and Harry a \"general agent''s\" commission, with guaranteed salaries, \"office\" expenses and 7 1/2 percent or more renewals contingent on the $50,000 or more written. | [Facing p. 716:] 716 The Coast Review. COAST FIRE LOSSES, April, 1910.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1871, "masthead_year": 1910, "linguistic_note": "Archetypal Trap A bound-serial founding-year misdate (catalog_year 1871 != masthead_year 1910): Internet Archive MARC metadata assigns The Coast Review''s 1871 founding year to Volume 78 (1910), creating a sensational mirage of ''playing a phony game'' in San Francisco twenty-two years before OED3''s 1893 baseline. Facing page 716 prints ''COAST FIRE LOSSES / April, 1910'' in bold display type.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on leaves n624-n625 of coastreview7819101910sanf. Stands alongside four post-1930 Trap A/D catalog misdates also audited and excluded during our sweep: North American Review Vol. 248 (northamreview248miscrich, cataloged 1821 -> actual Winter 1939/1940), Boston College Stylus Vol. 66 (stylus6611unse, cataloged 1883 -> actual 1953), Roget''s International Thesaurus 3rd ed. (rogetsinternatio0000robe_q1h1, cataloged 1834 -> actual 1962), and Fick''s Uber die Zeit (uberdiezeitundde0000fick, cataloged 1871 -> bound with post-1939 World War II ''PHONEY WAR'' clippings).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-323.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1910 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-223', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'foney wheel / Phoney / kiester and a pair of tripes', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1913, '1913-11-29', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1913-11-29_25_48/page/n131/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-223.webp', 157016, '515d0b6da70e51b7b60f6612c72a07e55422f782dd23bd2b3a795fdb090d52ba', '{"id": "CERT-2200-223", "slug": "cert-2200-223", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "foney wheel / Phoney / kiester and a pair of tripes", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1913, "date_display": "29 Nov 1913", "sort_date": "1913-11-29", "short_gloss": "The Billboard (Nov. 29, 1913) uniting ''Pipes for Pitchmen'' (''kiester and a pair of tripes'', ''Slum'') with Show Slang ''foney wheel'' and ''Phoney''", "source_publication": "The Billboard (Cincinnati, OH)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (SIM Microfilm)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_billboard_1913-11-29_25_48/page/n131/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 25, No. 48, p. 81 (''Pipes for Pitchmen'', leaf 79) & p. 133 (''Show Slang'', leaf 131; masthead leaf 1)", "verbatim_quote": "[leaf 131, SHOW SLANG. By Tom L. Wilson:] Eat ''em up—Manner in which patrons buy concession goods or patronize foney wheel. Phoney—Anything spurious in the way of goods. ... [leaf 79, Pipes for Pitchmen:] The last time I saw Gus Williams he was tugging a heavy kiester and a pair of tripes to the depot in Ilion, N. Y.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1913, "masthead_year": 1913, "linguistic_note": "Demonstrates lingering <foney> (''foney wheel'') printed on the exact consecutive line above <Phoney> (''Phoney—Anything spurious in the way of goods'') in America''s premier pitchman and carnival trade paper, alongside canonical street-pitchman kit terms kiester (''display suitcase''), tripes (''tripod stand''), and slum (''cheap prize jewelry'', leaf 78).", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified across leaves 1, 79, and 131 of The Billboard (November 29, 1913; visual inspection restores the word ''goods.'' at the end of ''Phoney—Anything spurious in the way of goods.'' dropped by raw OCR). Also in the same issue (leaf 64): ''Remember the time you bought a \"phoney\" diamond from a kid in Pittsburg and come damnear getting pincht for it?''", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-223.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1913 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-215', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'phony / foney / fony / phoney', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1914, '1914-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYK-566_4/page/n125/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-215.webp', 61418, 'a3c5414b14d31fcd1c018948e3c32a3b155bbc050d19bae246d9ab8dbcadc9ec', '{"id": "CERT-2200-215", "slug": "cert-2200-215", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "phony / foney / fony / phoney", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1914, "date_display": "1914", "sort_date": "1914-01-01", "short_gloss": "American Dialect Society record in Dialect Notes (Vol. IV, Part II, 1914) explicitly cross-listing ''phony'' with ''foney, fony, phoney''", "source_publication": "Dialect Notes: Publication of the American Dialect Society, Vol. IV, Part II (1914)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYK-566_4/page/n125/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. IV Title Page (JP2 #0011, covering Parts I-VII, 1913-1917) & Part II (1914), p. 111 (JP2 #0125)", "verbatim_quote": "phony, adj. Bogus; counterfeit. \"A phony woman in the store window.\" Also foney, fony, phoney.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1917, "masthead_year": 1914, "linguistic_note": "Explicit philological documentation by the American Dialect Society that <foney> and <fony> persisted in regional American usage alongside Neo-Greek <phony> and <phoney>.", "analysis_notes": "Multimodal Trap A date calibration: Internet Archive catalogs the bound Volume IV reprint as ''1917'' (the closing date of Part VII on title page JP2 #0011: ''VOLUME IV (PARTS I-VII, 1913 TO 1917)''), whereas Part II containing the Kansas/Midwestern dialect word-list on p. 111 (JP2 #0125) was issued in 1914. Supplies formal dialectological confirmation of all four orthographic variants (phony, foney, fony, phoney).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-215.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1914 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-227', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', '"fawney-men" [phony-men? — Editor] / "phony spark"', 'rejected_lead', 'serial_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1927, '1927-11-01', 'https://archive.org/details/writersmonthly3019unse/page/n385/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-227.webp', 75348, 'd76f4bf7dc25fde2f2294a94c12a5defd18111c9259d4d8d6fde115605828f56', '{"id": "CERT-2200-227", "slug": "cert-2200-227", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "\"fawney-men\" [phony-men? — Editor] / \"phony spark\"", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "serial_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1927, "date_display": "Nov 1927 (cat. 1912)", "sort_date": "1927-11-01", "short_gloss": "Trap A 15-year serial metadata misdate on The Writer''s Monthly: IA stamps ''1912'' onto a Nov. 1927 issue equating ''fawney-men'' with ''phony-men''", "source_publication": "George Allan England, ''Underworld Lingo,'' The Writer''s Monthly, Vol. 30, No. 5 (Springfield, MA, Nov. 1927 [cataloged 1912])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/writersmonthly3019unse/page/n385/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Issue masthead (JP2 #0354, ''Copyright, 1927'' / Vol. XXX, No. 5, Nov. 1927) & p. 388, col. 1 (JP2 #0386)", "verbatim_quote": "Peddlers of \"slum\" or \"junk\", i. e., fake jewelry, are known as \"fawney-men\" [phony-men? — Editor] while \"pennyweighters\" are jewelry thieves. A \"hoop\", by the way, is a ring.. Diamonds are \"rocks\", \"stones\", \"sparklers\", \"sparks\", or \"ice.\" Of course a \"bum rock\" has flaws in it, and a \"phony spark\" is a paste diamond.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1912, "masthead_year": 1927, "linguistic_note": "Trap A bound-serial founding-year misdate: Internet Archive assigns the journal''s 1912 founding year to Volume XXX, No. 5 (November 1927). Although rejected as a 1912 witness, Editor J. Berg Esenwein''s spontaneous 1927 bracketed query (''\"fawney-men\" [phony-men? — Editor]'') independently illustrates how transparent the occupational equivalence remained to American editors a decade before Peter Tamony''s 1937 article.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on JP2 #0354 (''Copyright, 1927, by The Home Correspondence School'') and TOC/header (''NOVEMBER, 1927, Volume XXX, No. 5''), exposing a 15-year discrepancy against catalog_year 1912 (catalog_year != masthead_year -> FALSIFIED-MYTH / rejected_lead under verification protocol Trap A).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-227.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1927 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-125', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'Sym- / phony [OCR: phony]', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_a_and_c_scrapbook_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1930, '1930-03-12', 'https://archive.org/details/dramaticmusicalc1930hale/page/n18/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-125.webp', 146896, '21b54e07393e4fd989209e1e0c11803fd72477d01246d2023b0439fc5d2de7c9', '{"id": "CERT-2200-125", "slug": "cert-2200-125", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "Sym- / phony [OCR: phony]", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_a_and_c_scrapbook_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1930, "date_display": "12 Mar 1930 [cat. 1889]", "sort_date": "1930-03-12", "short_gloss": "Compound Trap A + Trap C: Philip Hale scrapbook cataloged ''1889'' is dated ''Mch 12 1930'' in ink and wraps ''Sym- / phony''", "source_publication": "Philip Hale, Dramatic and Musical Criticisms [Boston Herald Clippings Scrapbook] (Boston Public Library)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Boston Public Library)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dramaticmusicalc1930hale/page/n18/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Scrapbook leaf 18 (clipping dated in ink ''Mch 12 1930'')", "verbatim_quote": "Mch 12 1930 / Symphony Concert By PHILIP HALE / The fifth concert of the Boston Sym-phony orchestra''s Tuesday series took place yesterday afternoon... Beethoven''s Funeral March... played in memory of William Howard Taft.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1889, "masthead_year": 1930, "linguistic_note": "Double archival trap combining a multi-decade scrapbook start-year misdate (MARC/IA ''1889'' for 1930 clippings) with column-wrap hyphenation of ''Sym- / phony''.", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified on leaf 18: handwritten ink header reads ''Mch 12 1930'', adjacent column reviews Greta Garbo''s 1930 talkie Anna Christie, and the concert memorializes former President William Howard Taft (died March 8, 1930), while ''phony'' is merely the second line of hyphenated ''Sym-phony''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/phoney/CERT-2200-125.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1930 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2200-126', 'TGT-015', 'phoney', NULL, 'phoney', 'phony [slang]', 'rejected_lead', 'trap_d_catalog_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1962, '1962-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.461693', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-2200-126", "slug": "cert-2200-126", "case_slug": "phoney", "headword": "phoney", "attested_form": "phony [slang]", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "trap_d_catalog_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1962, "date_display": "1962 [cat. 1852]", "sort_date": "1962-01-01", "short_gloss": "Trap D Edition Misdate: Digital Library of India Roget''s International Thesaurus cataloged ''1852'' is the 1962 Crowell 3rd edition", "source_publication": "Roget''s International Thesaurus, 3rd ed. (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell Co., 1962 [mis-indexed as 1852])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Digital Library of India)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.461693", "page_and_column": "3rd ed., Sec. 614.13 & Sec. 614.27 (copyright 1962)", "verbatim_quote": "spurious, ungenuine, unauthentic, apocryphal, unreal, fake [coll.], phony [slang], sham, mock, counterfeit, bogus", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1852, "masthead_year": 1962, "linguistic_note": "Modern mid-20th-century American slang thesaurus entry (''phony [slang]'') falsely surfaced in pre-1890 searches because Digital Library of India metadata assigned Peter Mark Roget''s original 1852 British first-edition date to Lester V. Berrey''s 1962 American 3rd edition.", "analysis_notes": "Trap D metadata verification: copyright page explicitly states 3rd edition, Thomas Y. Crowell Company, copyright 1962 (110-year catalog misdate). Properly gated as post-1930 external archive link with hosted_path set to null.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1962 (> 1930): scan omitted under pre-1931 public-domain gate; consult external source_url."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-306', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'SEANNACHAIGHIM', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1768, '1768-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/focalirgaoidhil01brigoog/page/n488/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-306.webp', 81140, 'c21f088fa37a417f03ca1e6d601928e7eac7ccfcda2c83f154fd51aef20bbdd0', '{"id": "CERT-1700-306", "slug": "cert-1700-306", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "SEANNACHAIGHIM", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1768, "date_display": "1768", "sort_date": "1768-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: SEANNACHAIGHIM", "source_publication": "[John O''Brien], Focalóir Gaoidhilge-Sax-Bhéarla, or An Irish-English Dictionary (1st ed., Paris: Printed by Nicolas-François Valleyre, 1768), p. 425, col. 1 (leaf n487) & p. 426, col. 2 (leaf n488); reprinted in 2nd ed., rev. Robert Daly & Michael McGinty (Dublin: Hodges and Smith, 1832), p. 431, col. 2 (leaf n441)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/focalirgaoidhil01brigoog/page/n488/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "1768 1st ed. title leaf n7 (MDCCLXVIII), p. 425 col. 1 (leaf n487), & p. 426 col. 2 (leaf n488); cf. 1832 2nd ed. leaf n6 & p. 431 (leaf n441)", "verbatim_quote": "SEANACH, crafty, cunning, wily; hence the fox is called seanach, or sionach. ... SEANNACH, a fox. SEANNACHAIGHIM, to play the fox. SEANÓIR, an Elder, or Senator.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1768, "linguistic_note": "Tier 2 Qualified/Weakened Pathway (Irish Gaelic Lexicographical Lemma): Bishop John O''Brien (Paris, 1768) already attests both SEANACH (''crafty, cunning, wily; hence the fox is called seanach, or sionach'', SNS = ) and the 1sg denominal verb SEANNACHAIGHIM (''to play the fox'', SNSKN = vs shenanigans / vs singular shenanigan), predating O''Reilly (1817) by 49 years and California shenanigan (1854) by 86 years.", "analysis_notes": "Major 49-year donor-lexicon antedating over Wave 1-3 CERT-500-005 (which dated ''Seannachaighim'' to O''Reilly 1817). Direct inspection of Bishop John O''Brien''s 1768 Paris quarto (focalirgaoidhil01brigoog, p. 425 leaf n487 & p. 426 leaf n488) proves O''Brien printed ''SEANACH, crafty, cunning, wily; hence the fox is called seanach, or sionach'' and ''SEANNACHAIGHIM, to play the fox'' (glossing the 1sg Irish lemma with an English infinitive), reprinted in the 1832 Dublin 2nd ed. (irishenglishdict00obriuoft, leaf n441). Survives harness viability (SNSKN = / ), but remains morphosyntactically strained because -aighim is a synthetic 1sg present verbal ending (''I play the fox'') never borrowed intact as an English mass noun elsewhere.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-306.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1768 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-320', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigans', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_error', 'agent_verified', 1776, '1776-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/10225274bsb/page/n450/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-320.webp', 93856, '28abf963d467ee833c40c38aee2bbb6baed79087346366152d2b1010b06edad2', '{"id": "CERT-1700-320", "slug": "cert-1700-320", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigans", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_error", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1776, "date_display": "1 Jan 1776", "sort_date": "1776-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: shenanigans", "source_publication": "James Macpherson, The History of Great Britain, from the Restoration, to the Accession of the House of Hannover, 2nd ed., Vol. 1 (London: Printed for W. Strahan and T. Cadell, 1776), p. 432 (leaf n450); paired with The Saturday Evening Post (Philadelphia), Vol. 8, No. 418 (August 1, 1829), p. 2, col. 2 (leaf n1)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Bayerische Staatsbibliothek", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/10225274bsb/page/n450/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Macpherson 1776 title leaf n7 (MDCCLXXVI) & leaf n450; Saturday Evening Post 1 Aug. 1829 masthead leaf n0 & p. 2 col. 2 (leaf n1)", "verbatim_quote": "[Raw OCR hallucination on 10225274bsb leaf n450:] privy-council fo felves obliged to iffue a shenanigans covery''. [Actual 1776 physical ink:] that the privy-council found themselves obliged to issue a declaration, containing hopes of his re-covery. [Paired with 1 Aug. 1829 Saturday Evening Post leaf n1: horizontal fold crease over ''The sequel of the image we may'' mis-OCRed as ''Shenanigans''.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1776, "masthead_year": 1776, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "CONCRETE TRAP B RED-TEAM KILL (baseline audit §2): Documents two genuine pre-1854 imprints (1776 and Aug. 1, 1829) in which Internet Archive''s full-text search index hallucinates the exact token ''shenanigans'' out of damaged type. On 10225274bsb (1776, leaf n450), neural language-model OCR hallucinated ''shenanigans'' across faint Caslon type actually reading ''declaration, containing hopes of his re-''; on sim_saturday-evening-post_1829-08-01_8_418 (Aug. 1, 1829, leaf n1), a dark horizontal microfilm crease slicing across ''and worship! The sequel of the image we may'' in an Elias Hicks Quaker polemic generated the ghost word ''Shenanigans'' (just as cursive whaling logbooks turn ''CSS Shenandoah'' into ''shenanigans'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-320.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1776 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-310', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Schienägel', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1812, '1812-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/rotwelschquelle00kluggoog/page/n323/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-310.webp', 97850, 'c7f663228acef847f8b67d2d110817bf68b290a47260f3bf15e5f232adfd687b', '{"id": "CERT-1700-310", "slug": "cert-1700-310", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Schienägel", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1812, "date_display": "1812", "sort_date": "1812-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Schienägel", "source_publication": "Ludwig Pfister, Aktenmäßige Geschichte der Räuberbanden an den beiden Ufern des Mains, im Spessart und im Odenwalde (Heidelberg, 1812), reprinted in Friedrich Kluge, Rotwelsch: Quellen und Wortschatz der Gaunersprache, I (Strassburg: Karl J. Trübner, 1901), Item CXVII, p. 305, col. 1 (leaf n323); paired with Johann Andreas Schmeller, Bayerisches Wörterbuch, Dritter Theil (Stuttgart & Tübingen: J. G. Cotta, 1836), cols. 367-368 (leaves n378-n379, s.v. Die Schin, Der Schinnagel)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/rotwelschquelle00kluggoog/page/n323/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Kluge 1901 p. 305 col. 1 (leaf n323, Pfister 1812 items 781-784, 801); Schmeller 1836 Vol. 3 title leaf n4 & cols. 367-368 (leaves n378-n379)", "verbatim_quote": "CXVII. Pfister 1812. ... 781. Schienägel Arbeit, Schanzarbeit. 782. Schienägels-Bing Knecht. 783. Schienägels-Kitt Zucht. 784. Schienägels-Golem Karren. ... 801. Schlingschinägler Weber. [Collated with Schmeller 1836 Vol. 3, col. 368: Der Schinnagel, im Pinzgau einer der geringern Knechte in einer Bauernwirthschaft.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1812, "linguistic_note": "Tier 3 Weakened/Unlikely Pathway (Pre-1854 Rhine-Main Rotwelsch & Bavarian Dialect): Pfister (1812) attests Schienägel (''Arbeit, Schanzarbeit'' = convict fortification labor) and Schmeller (1828 Vol. 2 / 1836 Vol. 3) attests Schinnagel (''iron wheel-rim nail; basket-splint; lowliest farmhand of all work''; Frommann 2nd ed. 1877 Vol. 2 col. 426 adds Hallein salt-works carter Schinagl). Passes verification audit consonant-skeleton threshold (SNKL = vs shenanigans / vs shenanigan; schinägeln SNKLN = / ), so retained as PHONOLOGICAL-HYPOTHESIS per baseline audit §5.1, but heavily weakened by total semantic mismatch (''hard convict/farm drudgery'' != ''deceitful trickery, humbug'') and unexplained lateral-to-nasal (-l -> -n) shift.", "analysis_notes": "Inspected directly on Kluge p. 305 (leaf n323) and Schmeller Vol. 3 cols. 367-368 (leaves n378-n379; cf. Schmeller Vol. 2, 1828, bayerischeswrt02schmuoft, & Frommann 2nd ed. Vol. 2, 1877, 11369723bsb col. 426). Look at Pfister''s 1812 compound cluster (items 781-784): 781 Schienägel ''entrenchment labor'', 782 Schienägels-Bing ''servant/farmhand'', 783 Schienägels-Kitt ''house of correction (Zuchthaus)'', 784 Schienägels-Golem ''wheelbarrow (Karren)''. Every single pre-1854 German/Rotwelsch attestation denotes grueling physical drudgery (convict trench-digging, wheelbarrow hauling, or menial farm chores)—the exact semantic opposite of crafty political/gambling deception (''come the shenanigan'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-310.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1812 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-301', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Nannicking', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1815, '1815-03-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_monthly-magazine_1815-03-01_39_266/page/n28/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-301.webp', 107262, 'd90796dc0c38531d6eea57c22cd8e0a2a669d6941874df5969441843e2bf14aa', '{"id": "CERT-1700-301", "slug": "cert-1700-301", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Nannicking", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1815, "date_display": "1 Mar 1815", "sort_date": "1815-03-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Nannicking", "source_publication": "H. Narbal (''Jan. 19, 1815''), ''Essex Dialect'', The Monthly Magazine (London), Vol. 39, No. 266 (March 1, 1815), p. 125, col. 1", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_monthly-magazine_1815-03-01_39_266/page/n28/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Masthead leaf n0 (''March 1, 1815, No. 266, Vol. 39'') & p. 125 (leaf n28), col. 1", "verbatim_quote": "SOME time since I observed in your Magazine ''a list of words and phrases peculiar to the inhabitants of Essex,'' collected by Mr. Copsey, of Rochford; and, finding it did not contain the whole of the provincialisms of this county, I send you a few which have escaped the notice of that gentleman ... H. NARBAL. Jan. 19, 1815. Essex Dialect. ... Mongy, Foggy. Nudge, To jog. Nannicking, Trifling. Notchet, A word of contempt for the acquisitions of another.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1815, "masthead_year": 1815, "linguistic_note": "Tier 1 Supporting Pathway (East Anglian / Essex Dialect): participial/gerundial Nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', 1815), expanded in American Gold Rush speech via expressive initial sibilant prefixation (she- + nannicking -> shenannikin -> shenanigan) and mock-Irish -igan suffix remodeling.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verified on fresh composite plate CERT-1700-301 (re-minting Wave 1-3 CERT-403-002). Masthead leaf n0 prints ''THE MONTHLY MAGAZINE. No. 266.] MARCH 1, 1815. [2 of Vol. 39.'' and p. 125 (leaf n28, col. 1) prints ''Nannicking, Trifling.'' in H. Narbal''s ''Essex Dialect'' glossary. Passes verification audit at against plural ''shenanigans'' (SNKNS vs NKN, d=2) and against original 1854 singular ''shenanigan'' (SNKN vs NKN, d=1), with 39-year chronological priority.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-301.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1815 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-307', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Seannachaighim', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1817, '1817-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sanasgaoidhilges00orei/page/n427/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-307.webp', 88906, '5a40bf5e5e03d5668aada18179ef63ecf5dd51f508e8e74c6b67c2ac01d4e22e', '{"id": "CERT-1700-307", "slug": "cert-1700-307", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Seannachaighim", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1817, "date_display": "1817", "sort_date": "1817-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Seannachaighim", "source_publication": "Edward O''Reilly, Sanas Gaoidhilge-Sagsbhearla: An Irish-English Dictionary (1st ed., Dublin: Printed by John Barlow, 1817), leaf n427 col. 2 (s.v. Seannachaighim) & leaf n440 col. 2 (s.v. Sionnach); collated with O''Reilly & John O''Donovan rev. ed. (Dublin: James Duffy, 1864), p. 467, col. 1 (leaf n482, s.v. Sionnach)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sanasgaoidhilges00orei/page/n427/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "1817 title leaf n6, leaf n427 col. 2 (''Seannachaighim''), & leaf n440 col. 2 (''Sionnach''); cf. 1864 rev. ed. title leaf n6 & p. 467 (leaf n482)", "verbatim_quote": "Seannach, see sionnach. Seannachamhuil, see sionnachamhuil. Seannachaighim, v. I play the fox. [Leaf n440 col. 2: Sionnach, s. m. a tub or kieve; a fox. Sionnachamhuil, a. foxlike.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1817, "linguistic_note": "Tier 2 Qualified/Weakened Pathway (Irish Gaelic Noun Stem + Mock-Irish -igan Suffixation): while borrowing O''Reilly''s 1sg verb lemma Seannachaighim (''I play the fox'', SNSKN = ) directly into ''come the shenanigan'' violates Irish-English contact morphosyntax (and verbal noun seannachadh/sionnachadh SNSTK = fails), remodeling the noun sionnach / seannach (''fox, sly trickster'', [ˈʃɪnəx], SNS = ) onto Anglo-Irish surname trisyllables (-anagan/-anigan, cf. Shanahan, Flanagan) in 1849-1854 California remains viable and aligns with the Dec. 31, 1854 San Francisco Wide West testimony calling ''shenanigan'' ''An Irish word... synonymous with humbug''.", "analysis_notes": "Consolidates and corrects Wave 1-3 CERT-403-003 and CERT-500-005 with exact 0-based leaf coordinates verified on scan: in O''Reilly 1817 (sanasgaoidhilges00orei), the Dublin title page is leaf n6, ''Seannachaighim, v. I play the fox'' is on leaf n427 col. 2 (not n428), and ''Sionnach, s. m. a tub or kieve; a fox'' is on leaf n440 col. 2 (not n441); in O''Donovan''s 1864 rev. ed. (irishenglishdict01orei), p. 467 ''Sionnach'' is leaf n482 (fixing the cover-offset leaf n485 = p. 470). Crucially, spoken Irish progressive/nominal syntax uses the verbal noun ''ag sionnachadh / seannachadh'' (SNSTK = < 0.60, lacking any final nasal), proving that the -m of dictionary-only 1sg ''Seannachaighim'' is an artifact of Irish lexicographical lemmatization; only stem ''sionnach'' + Anglo-Irish ''-igan'' remodeling is linguistically natural.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-307.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1817 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-302', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'nannaken', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1823, '1823-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/suffolkwordsand01moorgoog/page/n278/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-302.webp', 84556, '21fb2b6ea941305f04d46a64a190b649308488651bad13daca2748127be5a244', '{"id": "CERT-1700-302", "slug": "cert-1700-302", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "nannaken", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1823, "date_display": "1823", "sort_date": "1823-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: nannaken", "source_publication": "Edward Moor, Suffolk Words and Phrases; or, An Attempt to Collect the Lingual Localisms of That County (Woodbridge: Printed by J. Loder for R. Hunter, London, 1823), p. 244 (leaf n270) & pp. 252-253 (leaf n278)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/suffolkwordsand01moorgoog/page/n278/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title page leaf n7 (1823), p. 244 (leaf n270, s.v. Nannaken), & p. 252 (leaf n278, s.v. NONNAK, NONNAKEN, NONNAKS)", "verbatim_quote": "NONNAK. Light irregular work, or jobs, little better than idling. \"''A cent able to dew a day''s work—''a nonnak about now and ten.\" NONNAKEN. Idling, dawdling. See Dawdle. Nannaken, is sometimes heard. \"Come, store, store, don''t keep nannaken yar time awah.\" Sawneyken, is a similar word. NONNAKS. Jibes—fears. \"Come, none ''a yar nonnaks now.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1823, "linguistic_note": "Tier 1 Supporting Pathway (Suffolk Dialect): Moor (1823) documents both the -aken/-iken participial stem nannaken (''idling, dawdling'', NKN = vs singular shenanigan) and plural noun nonnaks (''jibes'', NKS = vs plural shenanigans) in the negative imperative ''Come, none ''a yar nonnaks now'', syntactically paralleling 1855 ''No shenanigan!'' and 1901 ''no more shenannikin''.", "analysis_notes": "Newly excavated primary 1823 Suffolk evidence missed by Waves 1-3 (where Moor 1823 was searched only under modern spelling ''nannick''). Moor enters ''Nannaken. See Nonnaken'' on p. 244 (leaf n270) and ''NONNAK / NONNAKEN / NONNAKS'' on p. 252 (leaf n278). Crucially, Suffolk dialectal -en/-ken in ''nannaken'' supplies the exact alveolar nasal coda NKN matching ''shenanigan'' (SNKN, sim=) even before velar-nasal G-dropping, while Moor''s ''Come, none ''a yar nonnaks now'' mirrors Gold Rush ''No shenanigan!'' prohibitions.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-302.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1823 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-121', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigans', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_error', 'agent_verified', 1829, '1829-08-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_saturday-evening-post_1829-08-01_8_418/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-121.webp', 165690, 'c4f71df22e5ddb97081cc2a0762dc7c103e12207cb99c3bf2da6bbd227b4a29c', '{"id": "CERT-1700-121", "slug": "cert-1700-121", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigans", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_error", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1829, "date_display": "1 Aug 1829", "sort_date": "1829-08-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: shenanigans", "source_publication": "The Saturday Evening Post (Philadelphia, PA), Vol. 8, No. 418", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sim_saturday-evening-post_1829-08-01_8_418)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_saturday-evening-post_1829-08-01_8_418/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 1 (leaf n1, ''Society of Friends---Elias Hicks'')", "verbatim_quote": "OCR GHOST: IA FTS indexes ''shenanigans'' on leaf n1; physical ink reads: ''the Society of Friends, called \"Westbury and Jericho Monthly Meeting,\" has originated from Elias Hicks'' having publicly inculcated doctrines...''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1829, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "TRAP B KILL (Pre-1854 OCR Ghost): Returned by Internet Archive full-text search for exact ''shenanigans'' prior to 1854 (dated Aug. 1, 1829). Visual inspection of the high-resolution microfilm scan (leaf n1) proves ''shenanigans'' is completely absent from the physical page; heavily foxed/bleed-through newsprint in an article on Elias Hicks and the Westbury and Jericho Monthly Meeting caused the OCR engine to hallucinate the token.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-121.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1829 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-303', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'NONNOCK', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1830, '1830-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/vocabularyeasta04forbgoog/page/n118/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-303.webp', 92722, 'a4acecd746c315ba8cbcd4b1db89bd6d49e737c7352652aa8cc58c94ad63e1d6', '{"id": "CERT-1700-303", "slug": "cert-1700-303", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "NONNOCK", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1830, "date_display": "1830", "sort_date": "1830-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: NONNOCK", "source_publication": "Robert Forby, The Vocabulary of East Anglia, Vol. 2 (London: J. B. Nichols and Son, 1830), p. 236 (leaf n118, s.v. NONNOCK, NONNY); collated with Walter Rye, ed., A Glossary of Words Used in East Anglia, Founded on That of Forby (London: English Dialect Society No. 75, 1895), p. 152 (leaf n165, s.v. *Nonnecking, Nonnock)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/vocabularyeasta04forbgoog/page/n118/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Forby 1830 Vol. 2 title leaf n7 & p. 236 (leaf n118); Rye 1895 title leaf n4 & p. 152 (leaf n165)", "verbatim_quote": "NONNOCK, s. an idle whim; a childish fancy. Connected no doubt with the following. NONNY, v. to trifle; to play the fool. [Collated with Rye 1895 p. 152: *Nonnecking. Full of apish monkey tricks [Johnson]. Nonnock. (1) An idle whim; a childish fancy... (3) To play the fool [Spur.].]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1830, "linguistic_note": "Tier 1 Supporting Pathway (Norfolk / Suffolk Dialect): Forby (1830) records NONNOCK (''idle whim, childish fancy'') beside NONNY (''to trifle; to play the fool''), while Goddard Johnson''s pre-1860 Norfolk MS (printed in Rye''s 1895 E.D.S. 2nd ed., abbreviated ''e.An.1'' in Wright EDD) supplies ''*Nonnecking. Full of apish monkey tricks'' (NKN = vs shenanigans / vs shenanigan) and W. T. Spurdens (1858) supplies ''Nonnock: To play the fool''.", "analysis_notes": "Resolves a subtle bibliographic puzzle in Joseph Wright''s EDD (Vol. 4, p. 225): Wright''s citation ''e.An.1'' under ''Nannicking, ppl. adj. full of apish tricks'' refers jointly to Forby (1830) and Walter Rye''s enlarged 2nd ed. (E.D.S., 1895). Direct scan inspection proves Forby''s 1830 Vol. 2 (p. 236, leaf n118) prints ''NONNOCK, s. an idle whim; a childish fancy'' and ''NONNY, v. to trifle; to play the fool'', whereas the famous gloss ''Full of apish monkey tricks'' comes from Norfolk antiquary Goddard Johnson''s mid-19th-c. MS (''*Nonnecking'', Rye 1895, p. 152, leaf n165) alongside Rev. W. T. Spurdens''s 1858 supplement (''Nonnock... To play the fool'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-303.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1830 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-122', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Shenanigans', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_error', 'agent_verified', 1834, '1834-02-04', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_great-britain-house-of-commons-journal_february-4-december-18-1834_89/page/n804/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-122.webp', 99040, '5290ebce6ba85b9bd649580d305ad90b07824fdb0597f3eb02f39ff5434f6a12', '{"id": "CERT-1700-122", "slug": "cert-1700-122", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Shenanigans", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_error", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1834, "date_display": "4 Feb 1834", "sort_date": "1834-02-04", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Shenanigans", "source_publication": "Journals of the House of Commons (London), Vol. 89 (Session Feb. 4 - Dec. 18, 1834)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sim_great-britain-house-of-commons-journal_february-4-december-18-1834_89)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_great-britain-house-of-commons-journal_february-4-december-18-1834_89/page/n804/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Appendix, p. 805 (leaf n804, ''Customs Duties---United Kingdom'')", "verbatim_quote": "OCR GHOST: IA FTS snippet reads ''of6Lory Shenanigans ——''; physical ink is a 90-degree sideways Parliamentary Customs tariff table reading ''Tallow - - - | Tamarinds - - - | Tapioca - - - | Tar - - - | Tares - - -''.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1834, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "TRAP B KILL (Rotated Tabular OCR Hallucination): Every remaining pre-1854 Internet Archive hit for ''shenanigan(s)'' (including 1834 House of Commons Journal Vol. 89, 1838 Medical Examiner, and 1839-1853 Congressional/statistical tables) is a classic 90-degree landscape table where vertical rules and leader hyphens (''Tallow - - -'') were mis-segmented by modern neural OCR into ''Shenanigans''. Visually verified on leaf n804.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-122.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1834 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-315', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'MANIGANCE', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1835, '1835-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dictionnaired02acad/page/n168/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-315.webp', 113812, '447459e7bdcb7c5d1044485d0dc2ac90ab00a0604847344e123adaab521e6efa', '{"id": "CERT-1700-315", "slug": "cert-1700-315", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "MANIGANCE", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1835, "date_display": "1835", "sort_date": "1835-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: MANIGANCE", "source_publication": "Institut de France, Dictionnaire de l''Académie française, Sixième édition (Paris: Imprimerie et Librairie de Firmin Didot Frères, 1835), Tome 1, p. 311, col. 1 (leaf n351, s.v. CHICANE, CHICANER) & Tome 2, p. 162, col. 1 (leaf n168, s.v. MANIGANCE)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dictionnaired02acad/page/n168/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Tome 1 title leaf n6 & p. 311 col. 1 (leaf n351); Tome 2 p. 162 col. 1 (leaf n168)", "verbatim_quote": "MANIGANCE. s. f. Manoeuvre secrète, procédé artificieux, petite intrigue. Il y a de la manigance dans cette entreprise. Je ne sais pas leur manigance. [Collated with Tome 1, p. 311: CHICANE. s. f. ... Les détours, les ruses de la chicane... CHICANER. v. n. User de chicane en procès.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1835, "linguistic_note": "Falsifies French chicaner / chicane (tentatively floated by Anatoly Liberman, OUP Blog, Jan. 14, 2009) and French manigance / ces manigances: against the original 1854-1877 singular noun shenanigan (SNKN), chicane (SN = ), chicaner (SNL = ; vs shenanigans), and manigance (NKNS = ) all fall below ; furthermore, spoken French [maniɡɑ̃s] / [se maniɡɑ̃s] has a nasalized vowel [ɑ̃] + [s] rather than English [-ən], and documented 1845 American borrowing of chicanery yielded ''shecoonery'' (Bartlett 1859 p. 398; Schele de Vere 1872 p. 51), not ''shenanigan''.", "analysis_notes": "Evaluates both French conjectures on 1835 Académie française primary scans. (1) ''chicane / chicaner'' fails deterministically (SN / SNL = vs shenanigans, vs shenanigan), and American vernacular actually nativized ''chicanery'' as ''shecoonery'' (1845 Chronicles of Pineville p. 47). (2) The folk phrase ''ces manigances'' (''these underhand tricks'') is an orthographic mirage: it relies on treating silent/liaison plural syntax as a monomorphemic loan and aligning French stem-final ''-ce'' [s] with English plural ''-s'', whereas from 1854 (''come the shenanigan'') through 1877 (Bartlett) the American word was singular ''shenanigan'' (SNKN vs NKNS = < 0.60, spoken French [maniɡɑ̃s] lacking any final [-n]).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-315.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1835 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-313', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'CHANADA', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1847, '1847-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/nuevodiccionario00salvuoft/page/n381/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-313.webp', 93144, '2ce94f56209e04653d51460b327c5c38c8b618d3b736d9bdd24d95126303436a', '{"id": "CERT-1700-313", "slug": "cert-1700-313", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "CHANADA", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1847, "date_display": "1847", "sort_date": "1847-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: CHANADA", "source_publication": "Vicente Salvá, Nuevo diccionario de la lengua castellana, que comprende la última edición íntegra, muy rectificada y mejorada, del publicado por la Academia Española, y unas veinte y seis mil voces... Segunda edición (Paris: Librería de Don Vicente Salvá, 1847 [1st ed. 1846]), p. 338, col. 2 (leaf n381); paired with Real Academia Española, Diccionario de la lengua castellana, Décima edición (Madrid: Imprenta Nacional, 1852 [IA catalog Trap A misdated 1843]), p. 212, col. 2 (leaf n223)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/nuevodiccionario00salvuoft/page/n381/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Salvá 1847 title leaf n8 & p. 338 col. 2 (leaf n381); RAE 10th ed. 1852 title leaf n5 & p. 212 col. 2 (leaf n223)", "verbatim_quote": "+CHAN. m. ant. CAN. Acaso es solo ortográfica la diferencia. CHANADA, f. Superchería, chasco. +CHANCACA, p. Am. M. f. RASPADURA. || p. Méj. CHINCATE. [Collated with RAE 10th ed. 1852, p. 212: CHANADA. f. Superchería, chasco.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1847, "linguistic_note": "Falsifies the Spanish chanada / charranada derivation: although Salvá (Paris, 1846/1847, p. 338—marked without + so already in RAE 9th ed. 1843, and retained in RAE 10th ed. 1852, p. 212, with zero entry for *charranada) attests Peninsular Spanish CHANADA (''Superchería, chasco'' = fraud, trick), both chanada (SNT = vs shenanigans / vs singular shenanigan) and charranada (SLNT = / ) fail the articulatory consonant-skeleton gate and leave the disyllabic velar-nasal ending -igan (K-N) wholly unaccounted for.", "analysis_notes": "Re-mints and expands Wave 1-3 CERT-500-004 by inspecting Vicente Salvá''s Nuevo diccionario (2nd ed., Paris, 1847, leaf n381) alongside RAE 10th ed. (1852, leaf n223, misdated 1843 in IA catalog). Crucially, Salvá''s typographic key marks non-RAE additions with ''+'' (as on ''+CHANCACA, p. Am. M. ... p. Méj.''), whereas ''CHANADA, f. Superchería, chasco'' carries NO ''+'' and NO American/Mexican regional label—proving RAE introduced CHANADA as an unmarked Peninsular colloquialism by the 9th ed. (1843), while ''*charranada'' is completely absent from both Salvá 1847 and RAE 1852.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-313.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1847 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-314', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Chancaca', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1852, '1852-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/diccionariodeme00duargoog/page/n163/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-314.webp', 72630, 'c4cfce4b906cf3a9251ed3f2a24d6d7001e1ee685656941ede2c3b379ef1d3e4', '{"id": "CERT-1700-314", "slug": "cert-1700-314", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Chancaca", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1852, "date_display": "1852", "sort_date": "1852-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Chancaca", "source_publication": "Mariano Velázquez de la Cadena, A Pronouncing Dictionary of the Spanish and English Languages (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1852 copyright), Part I, p. 142, col. 3 (leaf n164); paired with Féliz Ramos i Duarte, Diccionario de Mejicanismos: Colección de locuciones i frases viciosas (México: Imprenta de Eduardo Dublán, 1895), p. 158 (leaf n163)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/diccionariodeme00duargoog/page/n163/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Velázquez 1852 copyright leaf n5 & p. 142 col. 3 (leaf n164); Ramos i Duarte 1895 title leaf n6 & p. 158 (leaf n163)", "verbatim_quote": "Chancaca (Méj.), sf. Pan hecho con las zurrapas del azúcar. Del azteca chiancaca, mazapán de la tierra... Chancla (Quer.), sm. i f. Artesano chambón. Chancleta (Camp.), sf. Hembra, niña. [Collated with Velázquez 1852, p. 142: CHAMUSQUINA -> CHANCACA -> CHANCEAR; zero entry for chanada or charranada.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1852, "linguistic_note": "Refutes the Californio/Mexican Gold Rush contact premise for Spanish chanada: neither Mariano Velázquez de la Cadena''s standard Gold Rush-era American Spanish-English dictionary (1852, p. 142: CHAMUSQUINA -> CHANCACA -> CHANCEAR) nor Féliz Ramos i Duarte''s comprehensive Diccionario de Mejicanismos (1895, p. 158: Chancaca -> Chancla -> Chancleta) records chanada or charranada.", "analysis_notes": "Proponents of the Spanish hypothesis routinely assume ''chanada'' was Mexican/Californio mining-camp slang heard by Anglo Forty-Niners. Direct scan inspection of both Velázquez de la Cadena (New York, 1852, leaf n164) and Ramos i Duarte''s Diccionario de Mejicanismos (Mexico City, 1895, leaf n163) reveals zero entries for ''chanada'' or ''charranada'', confirming it was an Andalusian/Peninsular slang term rather than a Mexicanism—compounding its fatal consonant-skeleton failure (SNT = ).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-314.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1852 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-321', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Shanahan', 'rejected_lead', 'proper_noun_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1852, '1852-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/catholic-almanac-1853/page/n240/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-321.webp', 69896, 'b1dc99945412602c45f84224596afbd5065231be159723d4fb0a9181a67b6a0f', '{"id": "CERT-1700-321", "slug": "cert-1700-321", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Shanahan", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "proper_noun_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1852, "date_display": "1852", "sort_date": "1852-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Shanahan", "source_publication": "The Metropolitan Catholic Almanac and Laity''s Directory, for the Year of Our Lord 1853 (Baltimore: Fielding Lucas, Jr., [late 1852]), p. 235, col. 1 (leaf n240); paired with James M. Parker, The San Francisco Directory for the Year 1852-53 (San Francisco: Monson & Valentine, Dec. 1852), p. 52, col. 1 (leaf n79)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/catholic-almanac-1853/page/n240/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Catholic Almanac 1853 title leaf n6 & p. 235 col. 1 (leaf n240); Parker SF Directory 1852-53 title leaf n26 & p. 52 col. 1 (leaf n79)", "verbatim_quote": "Sester, F. N. ........... Blackrock, New York. Sestini, Benedict ........ Frederick city, Maryland. Shawe, Michael E. ........ Detroit, Michigan. Shanahan, John ........... Marysville, California. [Paired with Parker''s 1852-53 San Francisco Directory, p. 52: Flanagan James, boot maker, 168 clay; Flanagan Moses E. comr of emigrants, 92 Jackson.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1852, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "CONCRETE TRAP C RED-TEAM KILL & ONOMASTIC TEMPLATE CONTROL (baseline audit §2): Pre-1854 fuzzy/skeleton searches (SNKN) capture Irish surnames ''Shanahan'' (exact skeleton match with singular ''shenanigan'': SNKN == SNKN!), ''Shenan'' (1852 Massachusetts Register, massachusettsreg1862bost: ''Shenan James, Marlboro''''), and hyphenated toponym ''Shenan-doah'' (1836 Gazetteer, isbn_9780548692080). While excluded as lexical antedatings, documenting ''Shanahan, John ... Marysville, California'' in 1852 (28 miles from Nevada City / Coyoteville!) alongside ''Flanagan'' in the 1852 San Francisco Directory proves that the trisyllabic/tetrasyllabic Irish surname cadence (-ahan/-anagan/-anigan) was salient on the ground in Northern Sierra camps right before August 1854.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-321.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1852 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-124', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', '0 hits (pre-April 1854 negative control)', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'discovered', 1853, '1853-12-31', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_daily-evening-picayune_1851-09-15_2_112', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1700-124", "slug": "cert-1700-124", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "0 hits (pre-April 1854 negative control)", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "discovered", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1853, "date_display": "31 Dec 1853", "sort_date": "1853-12-31", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: 0 hits (pre-April 1854 negative control)", "source_publication": "Pre-April 1854 California Gold Rush & Riverboat Periodical Control Corpus: San Francisco Daily Evening Picayune (1851-1852, 33.8M chars), Tri-Weekly Pacific News & California Revealed Diaries (1848-1853, 285 items), Spirit of the Times (1845-1854, 736 issues), and California Farmer Vol. 1 (Jan.-Apr. 1854)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Chronicling America / Pennsylvania & Texas Newspaper Archives", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_daily-evening-picayune_1851-09-15_2_112", "page_and_column": "Full-text dirty-OCR sweep across 19 orthographic & broken-type variants (1780-1853)", "verbatim_quote": "Negative corpus control across 19 orthographic and broken-type variants (shenanigan, shenanigans, chenanigan, chinanigan, shinanigan, shenanagin, shenanegin, slienanigan, fhenanigan, she-nan-i-gan, nannicking): 0 genuine English lexical attestations prior to April 10/14, 1854 (The Mariposa Chronicle).", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1853, "masthead_year": 1853, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Establishes a sharp empirical terminus post quem in Northern California print: despite tens of millions of digitized 1848-1853 San Francisco, Sacramento, New Orleans/Mississippi sporting (Spirit of the Times), Pennsylvania, and Texas newsprint characters, shenanigan/chenanigan is completely absent before April 1854 and erupts across multiple Northern California sheets between April and December 1854.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1853 (<= 1930): inspect original scan via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-102', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'chenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1854, '1854-04-14', 'https://archive.org/details/cmarm_000116/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-102.webp', 283290, '733608477468e1951f446144b10e85870a239b9d196f14a4d582d1662afc5dff', '{"id": "CERT-1700-102", "slug": "cert-1700-102", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "chenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1854, "date_display": "14 Apr 1854", "sort_date": "1854-04-14", "short_gloss": "chenanigan in The Mariposa Chronicle (Mariposa, Mariposa County, CA), Vol. 1, No. 13", "source_publication": "The Mariposa Chronicle (Mariposa, Mariposa County, CA), Vol. 1, No. 13", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / California Revealed (Mariposa Museum and History Center, cmarm_000116)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cmarm_000116/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 2 (''Letter from \"Borax\"---No. 4'', datelined ''San Francisco, April 10, 1854'')", "verbatim_quote": "It is the intention of this ruse to get the opposition, if possible, to agree to go into Convention, when after some chenanigan at a given signal—most probably the drop of the hat, or, as the General is fond of military music, the tap of the drum—the martial candidate is to be dropped like a hot potatoe, and David rung in. It is to be a regular \"ball game\"—\"now you see it, and now you don''t\"—the \"little joker\" will be nowhere.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1854, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "TIER-A BREAKTHROUGH DISCOVERY: Earliest known English print attestation of ''shenanigan(s)'' in any orthography, written April 10, 1854 in San Francisco by correspondent ''Borax'' (on David C. Broderick''s Tammany-style Democratic convention maneuvering against Gen. James A. McDougall) and printed Friday, April 14, 1854 in The Mariposa Chronicle. Predates the OED''s April 25, 1855 San Francisco Town Talk baseline by 376 days (1 year, 11 days), the canonical August 18, 1854 Nevada Journal baseline by 126 days, and the July 10, 1854 Sacramento Daily Union telegraph dispatch by 87 days. Missed by all prior lexicographers because raw OCR misread italicized ''chenanigan'' as ''chcnanigan'' (e->c confusion). Explicit pairing with thimble-rigging cant (''regular \"ball game\"—\"now you see it, and now you don''t\"—the \"little joker\"'') and initial ''ch-'' spelling strongly illuminate early San Francisco gambling/political cant phonology.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-102.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1854 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-103', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'externally_recognized', 1854, '1854-07-10', 'https://english.stackexchange.com/questions/142498/where-do-shenanigans-come-from', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1700-103", "slug": "cert-1700-103", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "externally_recognized", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": "Cited in public digital historical-lexicography scholarship (Peter Shor, 2013)", "publication_year": 1854, "date_display": "10 Jul 1854", "sort_date": "1854-07-10", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in Sacramento Daily Union (Sacramento, CA)", "source_publication": "Sacramento Daily Union (Sacramento, CA)", "source_archive": "California Digital Newspaper Collection (cited via English Language & Usage StackExchange archival transcription; CDNC behind Cloudflare 403)", "source_url": "https://english.stackexchange.com/questions/142498/where-do-shenanigans-come-from", "page_and_column": "Telegraphic dispatch (''By Telegraph to the Union: San Joaquin Democratic Convention. Stockton, July 9th, 1854'')", "verbatim_quote": "San Joaquin Democratic Convention. Stockton, July 9th, 1854. The Democratic Convention held in this city yesterday passed off with considerable jarring, and no few discordant notes. There was considerable \"shenanigan\" going on amongst them, and it is hardly supposable that the ill-feelings engendered are yet quieted down.", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1854, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Earliest documented standard ''sh-'' spelling (''shenanigan''), dispatched by telegraph from Stockton on July 9, 1854 and printed July 10, 1854 in the Sacramento Daily Union—in the identical California Democratic County Convention register as the April 14, 1854 Mariposa Chronicle (''chenanigan''). Logged honestly as BASELINE-LITERATURE because CDNC (cdnc.ucr.edu) blocks automated retrieval via Cloudflare 403 and no IA/LoC mirror of the July 10, 1854 Sacramento Daily Union is openly hosted.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1854 (<= 1930): inspect original scan via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-104', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'externally_recognized', 1854, '1854-08-18', 'https://www.etymonline.com/word/shenanigan', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1700-104", "slug": "cert-1700-104", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "externally_recognized", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": "Cited in ADS-L (2015), Online Etymology Dictionary, and Green''s Dictionary of Slang", "publication_year": 1854, "date_display": "18 Aug 1854", "sort_date": "1854-08-18", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in The Nevada Journal (Nevada City, Nevada County, CA), Vol. 4, No. 16", "source_publication": "The Nevada Journal (Nevada City, Nevada County, CA), Vol. 4, No. 16", "source_archive": "Online Etymology Dictionary / Wiktionary (citing CDNC The Nevada Journal, Aug. 18, 1854, p. 1, col. 5; CDNC behind Cloudflare 403)", "source_url": "https://www.etymonline.com/word/shenanigan", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 5 (''First performance of the original Dolly Wags at Cayoteville'')", "verbatim_quote": "He then would state some of the principles of the party; First of all he would have them know it was on the square! and why was it on the square? Why? Because it didn''t intend to \"come the shenanigan\"—that was it!", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1854, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Target board TGT-001 reference 1854 baseline witness (Coyoteville / Nevada City mock Know-Nothing fraternal burlesque ''Dolly Wags'', paralleling E Clampus Vitus lodge humor). Checked IA/California Revealed and LoC Chronicling America for open scans of Aug. 18, 1854 Nevada Journal (only 1856-1863 Nevada Democrat is on LoC; CDNC returns HTTP 403), so logged honestly as BASELINE-LITERATURE per protocol. Prosopography (1700-B): The Nevada Journal was founded in April 1851 by Massachusetts native Warren B. Ewer, edited in 1853-1854 by Newburyport-trained printers Aaron A. Sargent and Nathaniel P. Brown alongside E. R. Budd and J. P. Skelton, and aligned with the Coyoteville/Nevada City Whig-Know-Nothing (''Original Dolly Wags'') network.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1854 (<= 1930): inspect original scan via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-105', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Shenanigan', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'externally_recognized', 1854, '1854-11-16', 'https://english.stackexchange.com/questions/142498/where-do-shenanigans-come-from', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1700-105", "slug": "cert-1700-105", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "externally_recognized", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": "Cited in public digital historical-lexicography scholarship (Peter Shor, 2013)", "publication_year": 1854, "date_display": "16 Nov 1854", "sort_date": "1854-11-16", "short_gloss": "Shenanigan in Sacramento Daily Union (Sacramento, CA)", "source_publication": "Sacramento Daily Union (Sacramento, CA)", "source_archive": "California Digital Newspaper Collection (cited via English Language & Usage StackExchange archival transcription; CDNC behind Cloudflare 403)", "source_url": "https://english.stackexchange.com/questions/142498/where-do-shenanigans-come-from", "page_and_column": "Postscript (''San Francisco Correspondence'')", "verbatim_quote": "After a few important preliminaries, a committee consisting of the following gentlemen were named to superintend the drawing, to see, as it was facetiously expressed by one of the \"gods,\" that there was no \"Shenanigan\" about it, namely: Geo. H. Hossefross, J. L. Van Bokkelen, T. K. Battelle, Marshal McKenzie, W. H. Patten, F. M. Pixley, F. E. R. Whitney, R. B. Quayle.", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1854, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Crucial social-network bridge linking San Francisco Volunteer Fire Department leadership (Chief/Foreman George H. Hossefross, Frank M. Pixley, F. E. R. Whitney, J. L. Van Bokkelen) and theatrical gallery ''gods'' to ''Shenanigan'' in Nov. 1854; accompanied in CDNC by Dec. 26, 1854 Sacramento Daily Union (''conducted without any \"shenanigan\"'') and Jan. 1, 1855 (''we don''t want any shenanigan''). Logged as BASELINE-LITERATURE due to CDNC Cloudflare 403.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1854 (<= 1930): inspect original scan via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-106', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1854, '1854-12-31', 'https://archive.org/details/widewest1857bone/page/n135/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-106.webp', 144952, 'a5afe67b1cf348c5a2fde868063c868b3319f3223b00b7dbad826eb6f6d17b7e', '{"id": "CERT-1700-106", "slug": "cert-1700-106", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1854, "date_display": "31 Dec 1854", "sort_date": "1854-12-31", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in The Wide West (San Francisco, CA: Bonestell & Williston), Vol. 1, No. 42", "source_publication": "The Wide West (San Francisco, CA: Bonestell & Williston), Vol. 1, No. 42", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (San Francisco Public Library microfilm digitization, widewest1857bone)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/widewest1857bone/page/n135/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 1 (leaf n135, ''Questions and Answers: Abstrusus'')", "verbatim_quote": "QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS. ABSTRUSUS.—\"In your answers to correspondents last week is given a definition to which I must except: An Irish word which you write ''shenanigan'' is defined as being synonymous with our English word ''humbug,'' thus depriving the natives of ''the Breast-pin of the Ocean'' of the credit of great shrewdness in the use of the word. ... A person of a very common name in Ireland—Shenann—made famous a kind of secret writing in cipher, which is known as ''Ogham,'' (vide Webster) ... when one person has outwitted or nonplussed another, he has practised ''Shenann-Ogham,'' which is evidently the proper orthography.\" Our correspondent is ingenious, but how does he account for the prevalence of the word in question in \"Pike,\" and in \"Pike\" only? Till he can satisfactorily answer this question we must decline accepting his definition.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1854, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan of The Wide West (31 Dec. 1854, Vol. 1, No. 42, p. 2, col. 1; leaf n131 proves Dec. 24, 1854 No. 41 p. 2 is marked ''LACKING'' on the master microfilm). Preserves the earliest known metalinguistic gloss (''synonymous with our English word \"humbug\"''), a facetious folk-etymological spoof (''Shenann-Ogham''), and the editors'' crucial sociolinguistic testimony attributing shenanigan in December 1854 to ''Pike'' (Southwestern/Missouri overland miners) rather than Irish speakers.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-106.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1854 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-107', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'externally_recognized', 1855, '1855-01-10', 'https://www.wordorigins.org/big-list-entries/shenanigans', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1700-107", "slug": "cert-1700-107", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "externally_recognized", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": "Cited in Wordorigins.org and Barry Popik / ADS-L archives", "publication_year": 1855, "date_display": "10 Jan 1855", "sort_date": "1855-01-10", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in Portage County Democrat (Ravenna, Portage County, OH)", "source_publication": "Portage County Democrat (Ravenna, Portage County, OH)", "source_archive": "Wordorigins.org (Dave Wilton, citing Barry Popik / NewsBank America''s Historical Newspapers)", "source_url": "https://www.wordorigins.org/big-list-entries/shenanigans", "page_and_column": "p. 2 (''Our Correspondence'')", "verbatim_quote": "How can an intelligent man say of another, as a politician he is a base, corruptible scoundrel, as a man, a fine, honorable, honest gentleman. This looks like \"shenanigan\" to outsiders.", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1855, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Earliest documented trans-Appalachian / Midwestern newspaper attestation (Ravenna, Ohio, Jan. 10, 1855), appearing within 9 months of the April 1854 San Francisco/Mariposa attestation as Ohio Forty-Niners corresponded with home papers. Verified via Ohio Memory ContentDM API, LoC Chronicling America, and Internet Archive that 1855 issues of Portage County Democrat are exclusive to paywalled NewsBank; logged honestly as BASELINE-LITERATURE. Prosopography (1700-B): Published in Ravenna, Ohio by Lyman W. Hall, John S. Herrick, and Frederick O. Wadsworth (formed April 5, 1854 as a Whig/Free-Soil/Know-Nothing fusion sheet, directly paralleling Nevada City''s Know-Nothing Nevada Journal while Portage County Argonauts corresponded home from the Northern Mines).", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1855 (<= 1930): inspect original scan via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-108', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'externally_recognized', 1855, '1855-04-25', 'https://www.oed.com/dictionary/shenanigan_n', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1700-108", "slug": "cert-1700-108", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "externally_recognized", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": "Cited as opening quotation in Oxford English Dictionary (shenanigan, n.)", "publication_year": 1855, "date_display": "25 Apr 1855", "sort_date": "1855-04-25", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in San Francisco Daily Town Talk (San Francisco, CA)", "source_publication": "San Francisco Daily Town Talk (San Francisco, CA)", "source_archive": "Oxford English Dictionary (OED1-OED3 historical baseline citation; NewsBank Early American Newspapers)", "source_url": "https://www.oed.com/dictionary/shenanigan_n", "page_and_column": "April 25, 1855 issue", "verbatim_quote": "Are you quite sure? No shenanigan?", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1855, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Long-standing OED1/OED2/OED3 earliest print citation (April 25, 1855 San Francisco Daily Town Talk), now superseded by 376 days via our PRIMARY-SCAN-VERIFIED April 14, 1854 Mariposa Chronicle (''chenanigan'', CERT-1700-102) and December 31, 1854 Wide West (''shenanigan'', CERT-1700-106). Daily Town Talk (1855) is absent from open IA/CDNC/LoC repositories; logged honestly as BASELINE-LITERATURE.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1855 (<= 1930): inspect original scan via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-109', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1855, '1855-06-01', 'https://archive.org/details/pioneerorcalifor03sanf/page/n386/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-109.webp', 154148, '353102ad4955ce5901dd7da0d6d87248ac4df26158355c367686858ef3fe5549', '{"id": "CERT-1700-109", "slug": "cert-1700-109", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1855, "date_display": "1 Jun 1855", "sort_date": "1855-06-01", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in The Pioneer; or, California Monthly Magazine (San Francisco, CA: Monson & Valentine), Vol. 3, No. 6", "source_publication": "The Pioneer; or, California Monthly Magazine (San Francisco, CA: Monson & Valentine), Vol. 3, No. 6", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (pioneerorcalifor03sanf)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/pioneerorcalifor03sanf/page/n386/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 375 (leaf n386; ''Editor''s Table'' opening p. 374, ed. Ferdinand C. Ewer)", "verbatim_quote": "One of Professor Moon''s most astonishing \"experiments\" consisted of holding a watch suspended from a short chain at arm''s length, and allowing any one in the pit to pull pistol and \"blaze away\" at the word \"Fire,\" whereupon the watch would most unconscionably disappear. An individual from \"Pike County,\" who had attended several evenings and witnessed the \"experiment,\" suspected, in the classic language of the times, that there was something of \"shenanigan\" in it.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1855, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verification of the June 1855 San Francisco magazine anecdote (explicitly labeling ''shenanigan'' as ''the classic language of the times'' in California and associating it with a ''Pike County'' Gold Rush miner watching magician Professor Moon). Crucially, this exact sketch became a national East Coast vector when reprinted verbatim in Yankee-Notions (New York, Vol. 4, No. 9, Sept. 1855, p. 280, HathiTrust njp.32101079230247). Edited by Ferdinand C. Ewer (Massachusetts-born brother of Nevada Journal founder Warren B. Ewer); explicitly places ''some shenanigan'' in the mouth of ''an individual from \"Pike County\"'' exposing conjurer ''Professor'' John Moon''s planted stage accomplice (''dressed for the nonce in miner''s clothes'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-109.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1855 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-110', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'externally_recognized', 1855, '1855-06-19', 'https://english.stackexchange.com/questions/142498/where-do-shenanigans-come-from', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1700-110", "slug": "cert-1700-110", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "externally_recognized", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": "Cited in public digital historical-lexicography scholarship (Peter Shor, 2013)", "publication_year": 1855, "date_display": "19 Jun 1855", "sort_date": "1855-06-19", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in Daily Alta California (San Francisco, CA)", "source_publication": "Daily Alta California (San Francisco, CA)", "source_archive": "California Digital Newspaper Collection (cited via English Language & Usage StackExchange archival transcription; CDNC behind Cloudflare 403)", "source_url": "https://english.stackexchange.com/questions/142498/where-do-shenanigans-come-from", "page_and_column": "Law Report (''Twelfth District Court'', June 19, 1855)", "verbatim_quote": "A. D. Brown, witness for plaintiff, testified that he had had a conversation with the defendant, Zane, in which Zane said that the plaintiff had been trying to \"shenanigan\" him; that if he, Zane, failed in the action, he intended to have some satisfaction anyhow; that he had employed Judge Heslep as his counsel.", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1855, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Earliest documented transitive verb use (''trying to \"shenanigan\" him''), sworn in San Francisco Twelfth District Court testimony on June 19, 1855—antedating OED''s verb baseline (Oct. 19, 1872 San Francisco Chronicle ''shenaniganing'') by 17 years! Also corroborated in June 1855 Gold Country usage by the ''Rasin and Shenanagan Ditches'' in Todd''s Valley Ravine (June 1, 1855 mortgage recorded in Kimball v. Gearhart, 10 Cal., 1858, HathiTrust hvd.32044078593456) and Aug. 31, 1855 Nevada Journal (''full of tricks and shenanigan''). Logged as BASELINE-LITERATURE due to CDNC Cloudflare 403. Earliest documented verbal conversion (''trying to \"shenanigan\" him'') in sworn San Francisco Twelfth District Court testimony, 17 years ahead of OED''s 1872 verbal noun baseline.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1855 (<= 1930): inspect original scan via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-208', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Town Talk', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sanfranciscobusi00unse/page/n171/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-208.webp', 94484, '895987458a66592327314f20a03855346c8b58c2bfeb026b539d9315c5f72d47', '{"id": "CERT-1700-208", "slug": "cert-1700-208", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Town Talk", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "1 Jan 1856", "sort_date": "1856-01-01", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: Town Talk", "source_publication": "San Francisco Business Directory, for the Year Commencing January 1, 1856 (San Francisco: Baggett, Joseph & Co., 1856), ''Newspapers'', p. 170 (leaf n171; title leaf n6)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sanfranciscobusi00unse)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sanfranciscobusi00unse/page/n171/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "San Francisco Journal, (German)—Ruehl & Co., proprietors. J. Froebell & K. Ruehl, editors; office 66 Merchant street. Town Talk—P. P. Hull & Co., proprietors. W. H. Mantz, editor; office 149 Washington street.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Crucial prosopographical discovery for OED''s April 25, 1855 San Francisco Daily Town Talk baseline (''No shenanigan?''): Colville''s 1856 SF Directory (p. 220) notes Daily Town Talk was founded Nov. 9, 1854 by White & Mantz, became W. H. Mantz & Co. on Dec. 6, 1854, and passed to P. P. Hull & Co. on March 26, 1855—and this Jan. 1, 1856 Directory proves William H. Mantz remained sitting editor under P. P. Hull & Co.! Moreover, Spiritual Telegraph Papers Vol. 2 (1853, IA IAPSOP-spiritual_telegraph_papers_v2_1853, p. 244) proves William H. Mantz arrived in California directly from St. Louis, Missouri (where he edited Light from the Spirit-World and lectured at Concert Hall, St. Louis, Aug. 1853), directly linking Town Talk''s editor to the Missouri/''Pike'' speech corridor!", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-208.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-123', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Nanick', 'rejected_lead', 'proper_noun_homograph', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-02-15', 'https://panewsarchive.psu.edu/lccn/sn85054567/1856-02-15/ed-1/seq-2/', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-123.webp', 181696, '253ce74acca7c6b28ee3204c0cd96a20c598977d3a23f1b780363ffe127bf318', '{"id": "CERT-1700-123", "slug": "cert-1700-123", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Nanick", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "proper_noun_homograph", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "15 Feb 1856", "sort_date": "1856-02-15", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Nanick", "source_publication": "The Daily Morning Post (Pittsburgh, PA), Feb. 15, 1856", "source_archive": "Pennsylvania Newspaper Archive (Open-ONI, Penn State University Libraries, lccn sn85054567)", "source_url": "https://panewsarchive.psu.edu/lccn/sn85054567/1856-02-15/ed-1/seq-2/", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 5 (''Seventh Annual Statement of the Eureka Insurance Company of Pittsburgh'')", "verbatim_quote": "SURNAME OCR GHOST: PA Open-ONI OCR reads ''%A. K. Nanick''; physical ink in the Eureka Insurance Company Directors list reads ''W. K. Nimick'' (prominent Pittsburgh ironmaster William K. Nimick).", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "TRAP C KILL + REGIONAL OPEN-ONI NEGATIVE CONTROL: Across the entire Pennsylvania Newspaper Archive (1780-1857) and Georgia Historic Newspapers (1780-1857) queried across all 19 orthographic/OCR variants, only a single candidate hit surfaced (''nanick'' in Feb. 15, 1856 Pittsburgh Morning Post), which visual inspection of seq-2 col. 5 disproved as Pittsburgh director ''W. K. Nimick''. Similarly, fuzzy prefix queries (''shenan*'') in pre-1854 California papers hit exclusively proper-noun references to the bark/steamer ''Shenandoah'' and ''Shenandoah Valley''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-123.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-111', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanagin', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-05-28', 'https://archive.org/details/xt7cvd6p282q/page/n3/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-111.webp', 118404, 'f582e362ce9967667a03bfea2bb95a157408ef95d020dd230d9a3c9ff4fc52a2', '{"id": "CERT-1700-111", "slug": "cert-1700-111", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanagin", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "28 May 1856", "sort_date": "1856-05-28", "short_gloss": "shenanagin in The Weekly Shelby News (Shelbyville, Shelby County, KY), Vol. 17, No. 22 (Whole No. 854)", "source_publication": "The Weekly Shelby News (Shelbyville, Shelby County, KY), Vol. 17, No. 22 (Whole No. 854)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / University of Kentucky NDNP (xt7cvd6p282q)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/xt7cvd6p282q/page/n3/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 2 (leaf n3)", "verbatim_quote": "For de secun part, de romance lub.—Dis am de kind make Byron such a fool all his life. Dis war de shenanagin dat de brack queen of Egyp knocked down Juleff Ceasar wid, and give old tony, de Roman, de Irish hoist. Dis am de kind dat make jelluffy come.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Earliest PRIMARY-SCAN-VERIFIED Southern / Ohio River basin newspaper attestation (Shelbyville, KY, May 28, 1856) and earliest scan-verified ''-agin'' variant (''shenanagin''). Appears in a circulated minstrel stump-sermon burlesque alongside ''give old tony, de Roman, de Irish hoist'', illustrating theatrical/minstrel exchange circulation into Kentucky by spring 1856.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-111.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-112', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'chinanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-06-14', 'https://archive.org/details/california-history_1930-03_9_1_0/page/62/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-112.webp', 100728, '20b8a9b8e914f02b6d18c95662a6b0a77bccad59277a213c105f284964774b64', '{"id": "CERT-1700-112", "slug": "cert-1700-112", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "chinanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "14 Jun 1856", "sort_date": "1856-06-14", "short_gloss": "chinanigan in The Journals of Charles E. De Long, 1854-1863 (Young''s Hill, Yuba County, CA, entry for Sat., June 14, 1856; published in Cali", "source_publication": "The Journals of Charles E. De Long, 1854-1863 (Young''s Hill, Yuba County, CA, entry for Sat., June 14, 1856; published in California Historical Society Quarterly, Vol. 9, No. 1, March 1930)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (california-history_1930-03_9_1_0)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/california-history_1930-03_9_1_0/page/62/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 62 (diary entry for Saturday, June 14, 1856, ed. Carl I. Wheat)", "verbatim_quote": "Fri. 13,— Had a grand ball at May''s an E Clampsus party; waited upon M J V H to the Ball danced till morning Sat. 14,— Bought J B Stevens out of his Livery Stable, in Co with H C Carpenter; paid $250 down & $250 to be paid in six months tryed to play chinanigan but couldn''t make it rip", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "First-hand Gold Rush private manuscript diary attestation from Young''s Hill, Yuba County, CA (June 14, 1856, the day after an ''E Clampsus'' lodge ball), independently confirming the initial voiceless affricate [tʃ] pronunciation (''chinanigan'') first seen in the April 14, 1854 Mariposa Chronicle (''chenanigan''). Notably, our full-text audit of De Long''s 1854-1855 diaries (CHSQ Vol. 8, Nos. 3-4, 1929) yielded 0 hits prior to June 1856.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-112.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-113', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-08-02', 'https://archive.org/details/casfpjd_000177/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-113.webp', 200202, '4252466565198ba77aa19fa40858673ac095bc652cc8ff4e320a6c04b3ce7fff', '{"id": "CERT-1700-113", "slug": "cert-1700-113", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "2 Aug 1856", "sort_date": "1856-08-02", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in Fireman''s Journal and Military Gazette (San Francisco, CA), Vol. 3, No. 18", "source_publication": "Fireman''s Journal and Military Gazette (San Francisco, CA), Vol. 3, No. 18", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / California Revealed (San Francisco Public Library, casfpjd_000177)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/casfpjd_000177/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 2 (leaf n1, ''Eureka 4'')", "verbatim_quote": "EUREKA 4.—The Sacramento Union of the 26th inst., is under the impression that there has been some \"shenanigan\" in regard to the purchase of the engine for No. 4 of that city, as the appointments of the apparatus seemed to be incomplete. The Union is altogether mistaken; all that was ordered by Mr. Hubbard, who was Chief Engineer of the Sacramento Fire Department when the machine was contracted for, has been received.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verification of San Francisco / Sacramento volunteer fire-company usage on August 2, 1856, directly citing the July 26, 1856 Sacramento Daily Union regarding Eureka Engine Co. No. 4—reinforcing the California Volunteer Fire Department institutional milieu already seen in CERT-1700-105 (Nov. 16, 1854).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-113.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-114', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-09-13', 'https://archive.org/details/casfpjd_000183/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-114.webp', 183460, '19ea349981984193ad3d63c6c8ad04df3100030b601017c5bb7321e1b6d4e7fb', '{"id": "CERT-1700-114", "slug": "cert-1700-114", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "13 Sep 1856", "sort_date": "1856-09-13", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in Fireman''s Journal and Military Gazette (San Francisco, CA), Vol. 3, No. 24", "source_publication": "Fireman''s Journal and Military Gazette (San Francisco, CA), Vol. 3, No. 24", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / California Revealed (San Francisco Public Library, casfpjd_000183)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/casfpjd_000183/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 2 (leaf n1)", "verbatim_quote": "One of the errors gave Mr. Devoe but 201 votes as an Assistant Engineer, while others had thirty and forty more than him, and yet he was elected. Now, in these times of ballot-box stuffing notoriety, our friends at home might think there was some \"shenanigan\" about, and that Mr. Devoe was stuffed in; but such is not the fact, he was fairly elected, and received 261 votes, but it was all through the devil (in the office) the error occurred.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verification explicitly linking ''shenanigan'' with 1856 San Francisco ''ballot-box stuffing notoriety'' (the Second Committee of Vigilance era, where Tammany-trained operatives Billy Mulligan, Yankee Sullivan, Casey, and Ned McGowan dominated ward politics and volunteer fire companies—and remarking ''our friends at home might think there was some \"shenanigan\" about''). Links ''shenanigan'' directly to 1856 San Francisco ballot-box stuffing.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-114.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-115', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-12-17', 'https://archive.org/details/xt7vmc8rcp2m/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-115.webp', 81862, '95db646c94f1dea4d3d4f6b8ffceaf8fb003a3b25a7b12b8279cb3c87d10ddc1', '{"id": "CERT-1700-115", "slug": "cert-1700-115", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "17 Dec 1856", "sort_date": "1856-12-17", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in The Post (Lebanon, Marion County, KY), Vol. 4, No. 52", "source_publication": "The Post (Lebanon, Marion County, KY), Vol. 4, No. 52", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / University of Kentucky NDNP (xt7vmc8rcp2m)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/xt7vmc8rcp2m/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 4 (leaf n0, crediting ''California Herald'')", "verbatim_quote": "''There is your account, sir,'' said the landlord, pretending not to notice Jim''s last remark. ''Two day''s board, $2 62.'' Jim took the bill and eyed the puzzled landlord as tho'' he suspected some ''she- nanigan,'' he broke out: ''Want to see them ar books!''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verification of an exchange-system reprint of a California Gold Rush miner sketch (simultaneously printed in Yankee-Notions, New York, Vol. 5, No. 12, Dec. 1856, p. 363, HathiTrust njp.32101079230247), demonstrating line-break hyphenated ''she- nanigan'' diffusing from California humor columns into interior Kentucky family newspapers by Dec. 1856. Reprints California mining-camp tavern bill-padding dialogue in Kentucky.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-115.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-116', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanegin', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1857, '1857-12-21', 'https://archive.org/details/hutchingsillustr02sanf/page/303/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-116.webp', 220972, '10d2d639e44a0bf93caf49a680311849a52276bb303ca6848ab6e8e1b312b7e3', '{"id": "CERT-1700-116", "slug": "cert-1700-116", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanegin", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1857, "date_display": "21 Dec 1857", "sort_date": "1857-12-21", "short_gloss": "shenanegin in Hutchings'' California Magazine (San Francisco, CA: Hutchings & Rosenfield), Vol. 2, No. 7 (Jan. 1858 issue, article datelined", "source_publication": "Hutchings'' California Magazine (San Francisco, CA: Hutchings & Rosenfield), Vol. 2, No. 7 (Jan. 1858 issue, article datelined ''Nevada [City, CA], Dec. 21, 1857'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (hutchingsillustr02sanf)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/hutchingsillustr02sanf/page/303/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 303, col. 2 (''Quartz Claiming'', signed p. 304 ''Nevada, Dec. 21, 1857'')", "verbatim_quote": "By the laws of this county, the discoverer may claim two hundred feet in length of the vein; and other persons, forming a company, one hundred feet each; but, by a species of shenanegin, the finder and a friend or two set up a claim for as many hundred or even thousand feet as their greediness demands.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1857, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verification of the unstressed medial/final vowel variant ''shenanegin'' written from Nevada City, CA on Dec. 21, 1857 (published Jan. 1858) in a technical exposé of fraudulent dummy-locator quartz-claim staking in Nevada County.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-116.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1857 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-117', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shinanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1858, '1858-04-01', 'https://archive.org/details/hutchingsillustr02sanf/page/478/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-117.webp', 148514, 'febd4732a4f26b474d79e88b2fe3002d09f4d946a35703daeaa2bbfb95572550', '{"id": "CERT-1700-117", "slug": "cert-1700-117", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shinanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1858, "date_display": "1 Apr 1858", "sort_date": "1858-04-01", "short_gloss": "shinanigan in Hutchings'' California Magazine (San Francisco, CA: Hutchings & Rosenfield), Vol. 2, No. 10", "source_publication": "Hutchings'' California Magazine (San Francisco, CA: Hutchings & Rosenfield), Vol. 2, No. 10", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (hutchingsillustr02sanf)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/hutchingsillustr02sanf/page/478/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 478, col. 2 (''An Adventure on a Dark Night'', signed ''A Miner'')", "verbatim_quote": "I arrived at the claim, but saw nobody. I whistled, but nobody ran. Thinks I, there may be no shinanigan here, for the reservoir may be broken. Away I went to the reservoir, half a mile further on. I arrived there in safety, and to my surprise, on striking a light, I found that it was—a gopher hole!", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1858, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan verification of the raised pre-tonic vowel variant ''shinanigan'' (matching Irish sionnach- [ʃɪnəx-] vocalism) in a first-person California placer-miner narrative suspecting foul play (''shinanigan'') when water ceased flowing to his sluice claim.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-117.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1858 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-118', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1858, '1858-07-28', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_new-york-times_the-new-york-times_1858-07-28_7_2138/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-118.webp', 133770, 'd008cd812babdc043510232a0b9c563d2fc0f7202e7aaf5fa64a29212e981236', '{"id": "CERT-1700-118", "slug": "cert-1700-118", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1858, "date_display": "28 Jul 1858", "sort_date": "1858-07-28", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in The New-York Times (New York, NY), Vol. 7, No. 2138", "source_publication": "The New-York Times (New York, NY), Vol. 7, No. 2138", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sim_new-york-times_the-new-york-times_1858-07-28_7_2138)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_new-york-times_the-new-york-times_1858-07-28_7_2138/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 6 (leaf n0, Judge Edward ''Ned'' McGowan San Francisco Vigilance arrest account)", "verbatim_quote": "He continually plied the officer with questions as to his personal safety—if he was in the hands of the Vigilance Committee, &c., and begged for protection. He was, seemingly, not satisfied with the reply of the officer, that good care would be taken of him, and said that he believed there was some shenanigan in the business.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1858, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "Front-page New-York Times attestation (reprinted the next day, July 29, 1858, in The Press of Philadelphia, IA per_the-press_the-press_1858-07-29_1) printing ''shenanigan'' without quotation marks in reporting fugitive Judge Edward ''Ned'' McGowan''s fear of the San Francisco Vigilance Committee—completing the 4-year arc from 1854 San Francisco political slang to unglossed national metropolitan newsprint. Front-page unquoted New York Times uptake quoting fugitive SF Judge Edward ''Ned'' McGowan.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-118.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1858 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-214', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1859, '1859-01-11', 'https://archive.org/details/per_utah-and-the-mormons_kirk-anderson-valley-tan_kirk-anderson_1859-01-11_1_11/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-214.webp', 113812, '3dab206a4f6812a8c46577770f65e8a01be28518576884bdb1d8237421967af8', '{"id": "CERT-1700-214", "slug": "cert-1700-214", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1859, "date_display": "11 Jan 1859", "sort_date": "1859-01-11", "short_gloss": "Shenanigan in Kirk Anderson''s Valley Tan (Great Salt Lake City, UT), Vol. 1, No. 11, Jan. 11, 1859, p. 2, col. 1 (leaf n1; masthead leaf n0)", "source_publication": "Kirk Anderson''s Valley Tan (Great Salt Lake City, UT), Vol. 1, No. 11, Jan. 11, 1859, p. 2, col. 1 (leaf n1; masthead leaf n0)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_utah-and-the-mormons_kirk-anderson-valley-tan_kirk-anderson_1859-01-11_1_11)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_utah-and-the-mormons_kirk-anderson-valley-tan_kirk-anderson_1859-01-11_1_11/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "In the transmission of the President''s Message to California, it looks very like there was some \"Shenanigan\" used, and that the Southern routes, and particularly the Butterfield route, was especially favored.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1859, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Second independent St. Louis -> Western press prosopographical link: editor Kirk Anderson was the former city editor of the St. Louis Missouri Republican (explicitly named 12 lines below in the same column: ''accredited him to the office of the Missouri Republican'') before launching Valley Tan in Salt Lake City in Nov. 1858, applying ''Shenanigan'' to political favoritism in overland mail contracts to California.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-214.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1859 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-224', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigans', 'rejected_lead', 'ocr_error', 'agent_verified', 1860, '1860-08-17', 'https://archive.org/details/per_the-daily-pittsburgh-gazette_the-daily-pittsburgh-gazette_1860-08-17_1/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-224.webp', 279676, '0c099162af69385bc6c5646dab2b37b72a107552782610d55bb78f0600d5c03c', '{"id": "CERT-1700-224", "slug": "cert-1700-224", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigans", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "ocr_error", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1860, "date_display": "17 Aug 1860", "sort_date": "1860-08-17", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: shenanigans", "source_publication": "The Daily Pittsburgh Gazette (Pittsburgh, PA), Vol. 73, No. 220, Aug. 17, 1860, p. 3, col. 6 (Cartwright & Young Truss & Supporter Manufactory advertisement; leaf n2; masthead leaf n0)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Penn State Open-ONI (per_the-daily-pittsburgh-gazette_the-daily-pittsburgh-gazette_1860-08-17_1)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_the-daily-pittsburgh-gazette_the-daily-pittsburgh-gazette_1860-08-17_1/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "[RAW OCR HALLUCINATION]: \"uftrlyoppo.she thennl by lams toeaD shenanigans oftbecalanal tothef.Ol\" -> [ACTUAL PHYSICAL INK]: \"CARTWRIGHT & YOUNG, No. 87 Wood st., [nearly opposite] the old stand, beg leave to call the attention [of the citizens of Pittsburgh...] to the fact that they are the only Manufacturers of Trusses and Supporters\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1860, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TRAP B & TRAP A FALSIFICATION SHIELD: Kills the single earliest apparent 1860s plural ''shenanigans'' hit in raw newspaper OCR (Aug. 17, 1860 Daily Pittsburgh Gazette), proving from physical ink that ''toeaD shenanigans oftbecalanal'' is smudged newsprint inside Cartwright & Young''s Truss & Supporter ad (''call the attention [of the citizens...] to the fact''). Simultaneously documents three sibling false leads purged during our sweep: (1) June 20, 1863 National Anti-Slavery Standard (IA per_national-anti-slavery-standard_national-anti-slavery-standard_1863-06-20_24_6, column-seam OCR garble); (2) Dec. 2, 1871 Pittsburgh Post (''\"shenanigan.\" You'' across a sentence period, not transitive verb); and (3) 200+ Internet Archive congressionalrec* volumes stamped ''1873'' (serial start year of the Congressional Record) that actually date to 1937-1996 (including OED''s Feb. 6, 1964 ''shenaniganing around'' in congressionalrec110aunit), plus 15 saturdayeveningp* volumes stamped ''1839'' and Century Dictionary Vol. XIII (1911) stamped ''1800'' (in.ernet.dli.2015.83597).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-224.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1860 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-311', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'schinageln', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n615/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-311.webp', 105926, 'a27b842741dd7efe5c8343407285257353b37814778a065d84fcde23e59cb251', '{"id": "CERT-1700-311", "slug": "cert-1700-311", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "schinageln", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "1862", "sort_date": "1862-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: schinageln", "source_publication": "Friedrich Christian Benedict Avé-Lallemant, Das deutsche Gaunerthum in seiner social-politischen, literarischen und linguistischen Ausbildung zu seinem heutigen Bestande, Vierter Theil (Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1862), Wörterbuch der Gaunersprache, p. 600 (leaf n615, s.v. Schin)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n615/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf n8 (1862) & Wörterbuch p. 600 (leaf n615, s.v. Schin, lines 9-14)", "verbatim_quote": "Schin, m. (oft d gebraucht als Abbreviatur, der Schliesser im Gefängniss, der Schandarm (Gendarm)... Als Zeitwort findet sich Schin nur verlängert in: schinageln (vom hebr. עגלה, Agole), Schubkarren, Schubwagen; schinaggeln, mit dem Karren schieben, auf der Festung karren; Schinaggler, der Handarbeiter, welcher mit dem Karren fährt), der Gefängnisswärter, Gensdarm.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1862, "linguistic_note": "Decisive semantic refutation from German underworld lexicography: Avé-Lallemant (1862, Vol. 4, p. 600) records no standalone headword *Schenigelei and enters schinageln / schinaggeln solely as a sub-entry under Schin (''turnkey, gendarme'', + Judeo-German Agole < Heb. עגלה ''cart''), defining it strictly as ''to push a wheelbarrow, to haul a convict barrow in a fortress (auf der Festung karren)''—never ''trickery, hoax, or deceit''.", "analysis_notes": "Crucial visual-scan discovery missed by raw Fraktur OCR search (which misread Fraktur ''schinageln'' on p. 600): inspecting physical leaf n615 (printed p. 600) of Avé-Lallemant''s Das deutsche Gaunerthum Vol. 4 (1862) locates ''schinageln / schinaggeln / Schinaggler'' buried inside ''Schin, m.'' and proves two fatal facts against Leo Spitzer''s (1948) German cant theory: (1) Avé-Lallemant postdates 1854 (1862 > 1854, candidate_viable=False) and knows zero noun *Schenigelei; (2) in authentic mid-19th-c. Rotwelsch, ''schinageln / schinaggeln'' meant exclusively ''pushing a convict wheelbarrow in fortress hard labor'', bearing zero semantic overlap with Gold Rush ''shenanigan'' (''humbug, trickery, cheating'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-311.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-215', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-05-01', 'https://archive.org/details/per_the-altoona-tribune_1862-05-01_1/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-215.webp', 222626, 'e03eae9a2a3dfdebe3fac2a7ff7011f2b19dc6dc010b940911c08fc02f6dea02', '{"id": "CERT-1700-215", "slug": "cert-1700-215", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "1 May 1862", "sort_date": "1862-05-01", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in The Altoona Tribune (Altoona, PA: McCrum & Dern), Vol. 7, No. 13, May 1, 1862, p. 3, col. 2 (leaf n2; masthead leaf n0)", "source_publication": "The Altoona Tribune (Altoona, PA: McCrum & Dern), Vol. 7, No. 13, May 1, 1862, p. 3, col. 2 (leaf n2; masthead leaf n0)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Penn State Open-ONI (per_the-altoona-tribune_1862-05-01_1; LCCN sn83031964)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_the-altoona-tribune_1862-05-01_1/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Two of these beat the dictionary, all hollow! These are the verb \"skedaddle,\" to run away, now as commonly used to signify the act of running away, as if it had been used by Johnson and adopted by Webster—and the noun \"shenanigan,\" used on the Western coast, almost universally, to signify secession humbug and trickery.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1862, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Explicit 1862 Civil War metalinguistic testimony predating Bartlett (4th ed., 1877) by 15 years and Schele De Vere (1872) by 10 years: pairs Missouri-born Civil War neologism ''skedaddle'' with Pacific-coast noun ''shenanigan'', confirming both its uncountable singular grammatical status in 1862 (''the noun \"shenanigan\"'') and its perceived Western Coast regional provenance in Eastern Union newspapers (cf. Aug. 11, 1862 Louisville Daily Journal, IA xt7pc824f198, ''by the running \"shenanigan\"'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-215.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-219', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenaniganed', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1864, '1864-08-27', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1864-08-27_12_20/page/n5/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-219.webp', 157652, '9b74043f88dff19f3976b932b0c86d16b3389c553b4941053776b40e10a32e6c', '{"id": "CERT-1700-219", "slug": "cert-1700-219", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenaniganed", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1864, "date_display": "27 Aug 1864", "sort_date": "1864-08-27", "short_gloss": "shenaniganed in The New York Clipper (New York: Frank Queen, ed. & prop.), Vol. 12, No. 20, Aug. 27, 1864, p. 158, col. 1 (leaf n5; masthead", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper (New York: Frank Queen, ed. & prop.), Vol. 12, No. 20, Aug. 27, 1864, p. 158, col. 1 (leaf n5; masthead leaf n0)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1864-08-27_12_20)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1864-08-27_12_20/page/n5/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Everything must have an end, even to the accursed rebellion; hotels that have been patronised and shenaniganed by fashionable frauds will know them no more forever; matrons with marriageable daughters, who have been throwing out their lines to catch some \"noble scion of a noble race\" while passing the summer at some fashionable resort, will have time to reflect on the past", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1864, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TIER-A NOVEL SCAN-VERIFIED OED VERB ANTEDATING: Attests passive transitive past participle ''shenaniganed'' (''swindled, bilked'', paired 5 lines below with ''\"bilk\"'') in Frank Queen''s sporting/theatrical weekly The New York Clipper on Aug. 27, 1864 (following the Clipper''s Oct. 3/31, 1863 pugilistic noun ''I don''t want any shenanigan, but fight''). Antedates OED''s shenanigan, v. earliest citation of any kind (1872 verbal noun ''shenaniganing'') by 8 years, OED''s earliest transitive verb (1894 ''shenan-neganning me'') by 30 years, and OED''s earliest -ed past participle (1932 John Beames Gateway ''shenaniganned'') by 68 years!", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-219.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1864 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-216', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1865, '1865-08-12', 'https://archive.org/details/miningscien11unse/page/n86/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-216.webp', 167140, '32c17e3039ba8966e5ea5f206ce8d3ec89516c373c6cedfae43a96084dd9f79a', '{"id": "CERT-1700-216", "slug": "cert-1700-216", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1865, "date_display": "12 Aug 1865", "sort_date": "1865-08-12", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in Mining and Scientific Press (San Francisco: Dewey & Co.; Warren B. Ewer, Senior Editor), Vol. 11, No. 6, Aug. 12, 1865, p. 83,", "source_publication": "Mining and Scientific Press (San Francisco: Dewey & Co.; Warren B. Ewer, Senior Editor), Vol. 11, No. 6, Aug. 12, 1865, p. 83, col. 1 (''Mining at Reese River''; leaf n86; weekly masthead leaf n84)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (miningscien11unse)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/miningscien11unse/page/n86/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Some of them are so because they have proved to be good, and of course they must be \"spiced\" with an application of Montgomery street, \"shenanigan,\" or \"freeze-out\" game. The poor fellows who discovered them, and were fools enough to allow the controlling interest to pass from their hands, have no business to own rich mines!", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1865, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Equates ''\"shenanigan,\" or \"freeze-out\" game'' (poker sharper''s freeze-out table transferred to San Francisco Montgomery Street mining-stock manipulation, alongside gold-washing/poker ''make the riffle'' and ''come to time'' in the same paragraph) inside the flagship journal edited by Nevada Journal founder Warren Baxter Ewer himself!", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-216.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1865 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-217', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1865, '1865-09-16', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1865-09-16_13_23/page/n5/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-217.webp', 122518, 'ac53bc378e032fe5064b37a3ecdca467f1f861d6948bd065e1d85ac29ea14ebb', '{"id": "CERT-1700-217", "slug": "cert-1700-217", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1865, "date_display": "16 Sep 1865", "sort_date": "1865-09-16", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in The New York Clipper (New York: Frank Queen, ed. & prop.), Vol. 13, No. 23, Sept. 16, 1865, p. 182, col. 2 (leaf n5; masthead", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper (New York: Frank Queen, ed. & prop.), Vol. 13, No. 23, Sept. 16, 1865, p. 182, col. 2 (leaf n5; masthead leaf n0)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1865-09-16_13_23)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_new-york-clipper_1865-09-16_13_23/page/n5/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "How he was three days inserted in a deep bed of snow, / (Invented by Hingston, you very well know), / And chased by the Indians as far as they''d go, / For Arty''s a wonderful man, man, / And no shenanigan, / For Arty''s a wonderful man.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1865, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Direct stage/burlesque prosopographical bridge connecting Gold Rush humorist Artemus Ward (Charles Farrar Browne, 1834-1867, former Western Reserve compositor/humorist) and his theatrical manager Edward P. Hingston (author of The Genial Showman, 1870) immediately after their 1863-1864 San Francisco/Virginia City/Nevada City lecture tour! Notice two phonological/morphological gems in physical ink: (1) rhyming ''man, man'' with ''And no shenanigan'' proves stress on -nan- (/ʃəˈnænɪɡən/), and (2) the very next stanza coins the playful minstrel refrain ''Shenandiddledan''!", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-217.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1865 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-218', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1865, '1865-12-23', 'https://archive.org/details/caaupmd_000179/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-218.webp', 203728, 'c1232ffcecc0cf25c73aa69a9aaf2a8d48f59fe5223303e4ed07a3890aa7ecab', '{"id": "CERT-1700-218", "slug": "cert-1700-218", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1865, "date_display": "23 Dec 1865", "sort_date": "1865-12-23", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in Dutch Flat Enquirer (Dutch Flat, Placer County, CA: E. B. Boust, ed. & prop.), Vol. 6, No. 33, Dec. 23, 1865, p. 2, col. 2 (le", "source_publication": "Dutch Flat Enquirer (Dutch Flat, Placer County, CA: E. B. Boust, ed. & prop.), Vol. 6, No. 33, Dec. 23, 1865, p. 2, col. 2 (leaf n1; masthead leaf n0)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / California Revealed (caaupmd_000179)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/caaupmd_000179/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "time or opportunity to \"ring in a cold deck,\" as the cards were fairly shuffled and dealt, and \"the game was made.\" There probably never has been a similar contest in California, where so little money was used by the successful candidate, and so little chicanery and \"shenanigan\" resorted to by the professional politicians and party wire-pullers, up to the last day of the races. There were three Senatorial nags in the field, Low, Cole, and Sargent", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1865, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Unites all three strands of Subagent 1700-B''s investigation in a single Northern Mines paragraph (Dutch Flat lies on the Placer/Nevada County ridge): explicit faro/poker sharper jargon (''ring in a cold deck'', ''cards were fairly shuffled and dealt'', ''\"the game was made\"'' [faro banking call!]), caucus ''chicanery and \"shenanigan\"'' by ''party wire-pullers'', and former Nevada Journal editor Aaron A. Sargent!", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-218.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1865 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-304', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Nonnaken', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi04wriguoft/page/n234/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-304.webp', 97772, 'ab0d848547c9191c9cd6df106f5e89ebb10a804975b1e6b4d40b749dcbe5102c', '{"id": "CERT-1700-304", "slug": "cert-1700-304", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Nonnaken", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "1866", "sort_date": "1866-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Nonnaken", "source_publication": "John Greaves Nall, Great Yarmouth and Lowestoft ... With a Glossary of the East Anglian Dialect (London: Longmans, Green, Reader, & Dyer, 1866), p. 594 (leaf n624, s.v. Nonnacks, Nonnaken); collated with Joseph Wright, ed., The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. 4 (London: Henry Frowde, 1903), p. 225, col. 2 (leaf n234, s.v. NANNICK, v. and sb.)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi04wriguoft/page/n234/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Nall 1866 title leaf n10 & p. 594 (leaf n624); Wright EDD Vol. 4 (1903) title leaf n6 & p. 225 (leaf n234), col. 2", "verbatim_quote": "Nonnacks. Light, irregular work, idle whims. Nonnaken, idling, dawdling. Possibly a cor. of no maks, do nothing. Nonny. To trifle, play the fool. [Collated with Wright EDD Vol. 4, p. 225: NANNICK, v. and sb. Glo. e.An. ... 1. v. To play the fool... Ess. Now children, no nannicking, sit still at table! Hence Nannicking, ppl. adj. full of apish tricks; trifling. e.An.1 Ess. Monthly Mag. (1815) I. 125... 3. sb. An idle whim... Suf. He''s allus full of his nannicks.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "East Anglian & Gloucestershire paradigm collation (1815-1903): documents full regional spread across Essex, Suffolk, Norfolk, and Gloucestershire (''no nannicking!'', ''full of his nannicks'', ''nanickin''''), synthesized by Eric Partridge (The World of Words, 1938, p. 313) as she- + nannicking.", "analysis_notes": "Collates Nall (1866, p. 594, leaf n624) and Wright''s EDD Vol. 4 (1903, p. 225, leaf n234; note Wave 1-3 CERT-403-002 cited leaf n235, which is actually printed p. 226 ''NAP''—corrected here to physical leaf n234 = printed p. 225). EDD documents ten East Anglian/Gloucestershire vowel variants (nannick, nanick, nannack, nannak, nannock, nannuck, nonek, nonnak, nonneck, nonnuck) anchored chronologically in 1815 Narbal, 1823 Moor, and 1830 Forby.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-304.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-119', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shananigan', 'rejected_lead', 'misdated_source', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-10-01', 'https://archive.org/details/westminsterrevi03unkngoog/page/n454/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-119.webp', 158068, '4cbf6413dbee8e6ac526a7f0967fcb9fdc9d69afe7312ade9e88caa15e5c8de5', '{"id": "CERT-1700-119", "slug": "cert-1700-119", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shananigan", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "misdated_source", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "1 Oct 1866", "sort_date": "1866-10-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: shananigan", "source_publication": "The Westminster Review (London: Trübner & Co.), New Series, Vol. XXX (July & October 1866)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Google Books (westminsterrevi03unkngoog, cataloged under series-inception year 1852)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/westminsterrevi03unkngoog/page/n454/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 444 (leaf n454, Art. VII: ''Our North-Pacific Colonies'')", "verbatim_quote": "When a \"claim\" disappoints the hopes of the proprietors, it has \"fizzled out.\" Credit is \"jawbone,\" or as it is otherwise expressed, \"shooting off the face.\" Deceit in business is \"shananigan.\" When one has run off to elude his creditors, he has \"vamoosed the ranch.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1852, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "TRAP A KILL (Bound-Serial Metadata Poisoning): Both Google Books and Internet Archive index westminsterrevi03unkngoog as ''date: 1852'' (the launch year of The Westminster Review''s New Series), creating a seductive illusion of an 1852 British attestation of ''shananigan''. Visual inspection of the bound title page (leaf n5) proves it is New Series Vol. XXX, July & October 1866 (reviewing 1862-1865 Vancouver Island / British Columbia Gold Rush books by R. C. Mayne and Matthew Macfie, importing California Forty-Niner slang northward).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-119.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-204', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Nevada Journal', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1867, '1867-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/beanshistorydire00bean/page/n55/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-204.webp', 90390, '2f0cb1f222429e87e92645453e90a4c7c8d5b5aa7cc4f3c76de3827d9a672bda', '{"id": "CERT-1700-204", "slug": "cert-1700-204", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Nevada Journal", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1867, "date_display": "1 Jan 1867", "sort_date": "1867-01-01", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: Nevada Journal", "source_publication": "Edwin F. Bean (comp.), Bean''s History and Directory of Nevada County, California (Nevada City: Daily Gazette Book and Job Office, 1867), ''The Press'' (by Edwin G. Waite), p. 40 (leaf n55; title leaf n10)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (beanshistorydire00bean)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/beanshistorydire00bean/page/n55/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "The Nevada Journal, the first paper published in the county, and one of the first ever published in the mountains of the State, made its appearance at Nevada in April, 1851, under the auspices of W. B. Ewer & Co. It was always controlled by Whigs, or men of Whig antecedents, down to its suspension in the Fall of 1861. ... Ewer did not long remain in the concern, being succeeded as editor by A. A. Sargent, who, except at short intervals, furnished its editorials till July, 1855, when he was succeeded by E. G. Waite as editor, who generally directed the course of the paper down to October, 1861.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1867, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Primary prosopographical anchor written by Nevada Journal editor Edwin Greene Waite himself (b. Granville, Washington Co., NY, 1826; arrived SF Aug. 4, 1849 via Panama). Confirms The Nevada Journal was founded April 1851 by Massachusetts native Warren Baxter Ewer (1815-1906), edited 1852-July 1855 by Newburyport printer Aaron Augustus Sargent (1827-1887; with Edwin Ruthvin Budd & Southern printer John P. Skelton in Aug. 1854), and edited from July 1855 by E. G. Waite, who was elected Whig Assemblyman in 1854 and Know-Nothing (''American'') State Senator in 1855 (Bean 1867, p. 35), explaining the Aug. 31, 1855 Nevada Journal Know-Nothing ''eagle-eyed natives... no shenanigan is played'' editorial.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-204.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1867 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-205', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Coyoteville', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1867, '1867-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/beanshistorydire00bean/page/n94/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-205.webp', 101452, '6b6cffba3fb40657cb5f6a1b332948a67ce6bf5fe98855782f5411e6e07ee7de', '{"id": "CERT-1700-205", "slug": "cert-1700-205", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Coyoteville", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1867, "date_display": "1 Jan 1867", "sort_date": "1867-01-01", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: Coyoteville", "source_publication": "Edwin F. Bean (comp.), Bean''s History and Directory of Nevada County, California (Nevada City: Daily Gazette Book and Job Office, 1867), ''Sketch of Nevada Township'', p. 79 (leaf n94; with B. P. Avery Dec. 20, 1866 memoir on pp. 73-76, leaves n88-n91)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (beanshistorydire00bean)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/beanshistorydire00bean/page/n94/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "A town called Coyoteville sprang up on the gravel hills, well endowed with saloons and monte-banks, and flourished for a year or two exceedingly. Its site was on the eastern end of Lost Hill, and is now almost washed away; but here miners growing rich congregated, politicians flocked, and noisy demagogues brayed to indifferent or ignorant listeners.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1867, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Reconstructs the exact social, dialectal, and gambling ecology of Coyoteville—the mining camp immediately adjoining Nevada City where the ''Original Dolly Wags'' performed on Aug. 18, 1854 (''on the square... come the shenanigan''). On pp. 73-75 (leaves n88-n90) of the same chapter, New York-born journalist Benjamin Parke Avery (1828-1875; sailed round Cape Horn 1849; later editor of North San Juan Hydraulic Press, SF Bulletin, and Overland Monthly) recounts camping at Coyoteville among ''Southwestern men'' who viewed him with ''a bit of contempt for the smooth-faced \"Yorker\"'', while p. 79-80 (leaves n94-n95) documents Coyoteville''s ''saloons and monte-banks'', ''faro, roulette, vingt-et-un and poker'', and stump politicians.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-205.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1867 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-220', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1868, '1868-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924022108264/page/n141/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-220.webp', 111696, 'c7bf6e175619506a76df32521c13b26b959c76294cdecc8775e79bbd923d305d', '{"id": "CERT-1700-220", "slug": "cert-1700-220", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1868, "date_display": "1 Jan 1868", "sort_date": "1868-01-01", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in [Ina Marie Porter], Randolph Honor (New York: Richardson and Company, 1868 [copyright 1867]), p. 137 (leaf n141; title leaf n6", "source_publication": "[Ina Marie Porter], Randolph Honor (New York: Richardson and Company, 1868 [copyright 1867]), p. 137 (leaf n141; title leaf n6)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Cornell University Library (cu31924022108264)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924022108264/page/n141/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Union and Old Flag indeed! Can''t come that shenanigan over me! Union been played out for a coon''s age, and as to the old flag, since them ''publicans taken it into their dirty hands, its something I ain''t got no use for.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1868, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Documents the transitive-prepositional phrasal expansion ''come [that] shenanigan over [someone]'' (from 1854 Nevada Journal ''come the shenanigan'') inside a deliberately rendered Arkansas/Missouri border-South dialect monologue (''played out'', ''git euchred'', ''slam them at this game'', ''up thar in Missourah''), again pairing shenanigan with card-playing (''euchred'', ''slam'') and ''Missourah'' speech (title imprint 1868, copyright verso 1867; attested_year conservatively recorded as 1868 per imprint rule).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-220.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1868 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-221', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigans', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1869, '1869-02-03', 'https://archive.org/details/per_reading-daily-eagle_reading-daily-eagle_1869-02-03_1/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-221.webp', 301136, 'bf733fe5c0b6f00aa3253bcd81a6002d48ea0ac7abceba84d232daf5315ef86e', '{"id": "CERT-1700-221", "slug": "cert-1700-221", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigans", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1869, "date_display": "3 Feb 1869", "sort_date": "1869-02-03", "short_gloss": "shenanigans in Reading Daily Eagle (Reading, PA: Ritter & Co.), Vol. 2, No. 6, Feb. 3, 1869, p. 3, col. 3 (leaf n2; masthead leaf n0)", "source_publication": "Reading Daily Eagle (Reading, PA: Ritter & Co.), Vol. 2, No. 6, Feb. 3, 1869, p. 3, col. 3 (leaf n2; masthead leaf n0)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Penn State Open-ONI (per_reading-daily-eagle_reading-daily-eagle_1869-02-03_1; LCCN sn84026345)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_reading-daily-eagle_reading-daily-eagle_1869-02-03_1/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "I wonder whether the owner of the property has invested anything in this shaving enterprise? Now, Lewy, none of your shenanigans. Take care or else your mother''s pet pig will get into a scrape.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1869, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "TIER-A NOVEL SCAN-VERIFIED PLURAL NOUN ANTEDATING: Earliest physically verified plural ''shenanigans'' found across our entire 1848-1875 archival sweep (after killing the 1860 Pittsburgh Gazette and 1863 National Anti-Slavery Standard OCR ghosts, CERT-1700-224). Antedates Green''s Dictionary of Slang''s earliest plural (1929 L. Thomas Woodfill of the Regulars) by 60 years and OED2''s plural examples (1938 Rodale ''Shenanigins'' / 1969 ''shenanigans'') by 69-100 years! Shows exact syntactic re-analysis from mass noun (cf. July 1868 Overland Monthly p. 296 ''all kinds of shenanigan'') to count/plural noun under the analogical frame ''none of your [tricks/pranks/monkeyshines] -> none of your shenanigans''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-221.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1869 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-317', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Shenannigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1871, '1871-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/americanismseng00veregoog/page/n635/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-317.webp', 62270, '0fe325c4ca74b78c46db12635b12885a2a601a807878b246bce2690f175a7c09', '{"id": "CERT-1700-317", "slug": "cert-1700-317", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Shenannigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1871, "date_display": "1871", "sort_date": "1871-01-01", "short_gloss": "Shenannigan in [Lyman Hotchkiss Bagg] (''A Graduate of ''69''), Four Years at Yale (New Haven: Charles C. Chatfield & Co., 1871), p. 47 (leaf n", "source_publication": "[Lyman Hotchkiss Bagg] (''A Graduate of ''69''), Four Years at Yale (New Haven: Charles C. Chatfield & Co., 1871), p. 47 (leaf n60); paired with Maximilian Schele de Vere, Americanisms; The English of the New World (New York: Charles Scribner & Company, 1872 [entered 1871]), pp. 631-632 (leaves n635-n636)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/americanismseng00veregoog/page/n635/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Bagg 1871 title leaf n4 & p. 47 (leaf n60); Schele de Vere 1872 title leaf n5, p. 631 (leaf n635), & p. 632 (leaf n636)", "verbatim_quote": "Shenannigan, probably a purely fictitious word (though a Dutch origin has been claimed for it), frequently heard in the South and West, and denoting groundless bragging for the purpose of getting the better of another. \"Shenannigan means any kind of chaff, foolery, nonsense, advanced to cover some little scheme or game.\" Hence Miss Vinnie Ream... \"No shenannigan, Vinnie!\" (January, 1871.)", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1871, "linguistic_note": "Earliest published lexicographical entries (1871 Bagg, 1872 Schele de Vere): Schele de Vere explicitly judges Shenannigan ''probably a purely fictitious [i.e., expressive] word'' while recording the earliest published foreign-donor rumor (''though a Dutch [= Deutsch/German or Dutch] origin has been claimed for it'').", "analysis_notes": "Side-by-side scan comparison of Bagg (1871, p. 47, leaf n60) and Schele de Vere (1872, pp. 631-632, leaves n635-n636; note UofT scan americanismsengl00scheuoft accidentally skipped filming pp. 630-631, recovered here from Google scan americanismseng00veregoog leaf n635!) proves that Schele de Vere''s quoted definition (''Shenannigan means any kind of chaff, foolery, nonsense, advanced to cover some little scheme or game'') was copied almost verbatim from Bagg''s 1871 Yale college slang glossary, paired with a January 1871 Chicago Evening Post quote (''No shenannigan, Vinnie!'') and the earliest known etymological note.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-317.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1871 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-318', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1877, '1877-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dictionaryameri05bartgoog/page/n661/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-318.webp', 54622, 'abf329a42aed7201d74ce20081b4d84a6ef13b41c92db411440cc6913f66151b', '{"id": "CERT-1700-318", "slug": "cert-1700-318", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1877, "date_display": "1877", "sort_date": "1877-01-01", "short_gloss": "Shenanigan in John Russell Bartlett, Dictionary of Americanisms: A Glossary of Words and Phrases Usually Regarded as Peculiar to the United", "source_publication": "John Russell Bartlett, Dictionary of Americanisms: A Glossary of Words and Phrases Usually Regarded as Peculiar to the United States, 4th ed. (Boston: Little, Brown, and Company, 1877), p. 592 (leaf n661) & Addenda p. 813 (leaf n882); contrasted with 2nd ed. (Boston: Little, Brown, and Company, 1859), p. 398 (leaf n432)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dictionaryameri05bartgoog/page/n661/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "1859 2nd ed. title leaf n5 & p. 398 (leaf n432: SHEER -> SHELL-BARK); 1877 4th ed. title leaf n9 & p. 592 (leaf n661, s.v. Shenanigan)", "verbatim_quote": "Shenanigan. 1. A trick in which there is cheating. California. 2. Chaff; foolery; nonsense, especially when advanced to cover some scheme or little game. Yale College. [Addenda s.v. Hornswoggle, quoting Philadelphia Times, Nov. 5, 1877: \"Its best Eastern equivalent is shenanigan, although the less complicated word hornswoggling rather directly translates it.\"]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1877, "linguistic_note": "Edition-by-edition verification of Bartlett (1848, 1859, 1860, 1877): proves Shenanigan was absent in 1859 (2nd ed., p. 398 leaf n432, which records SHECOONERY and jumps from SHELL-BARK to SHERRYVALLIES) and 1860 (3rd ed.), and was first added in the 4th edition (1877, p. 592 leaf n661), where Bartlett gives NO foreign etymology, localizing Sense 1 (''A trick in which there is cheating'') to California and Sense 2 (from Bagg 1871) to Yale College.", "analysis_notes": "Answers Prompt Item 3 directly on verified 1859 and 1877 scans: Bartlett omitted ''shenanigan'' in his 1st (1848), 2nd (1859, dictionaryameri03bartgoog leaf n432), and 3rd (1860, dictionaryameri04bartgoog) editions—confirming its very recent 1854-1855 California emergence had not yet reached Providence/New York lexicography by March 1859—and first admitted ''Shenanigan'' in the 4th edition (1877, dictionaryameri05bartgoog leaf n661), abstaining from any Irish, Spanish, or German derivation.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-318.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1877 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-222', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigans', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1877, '1877-09-08', 'https://archive.org/details/per_the-carbon-advocate_the-carbon-advocate_1877-09-08_1/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-222.webp', 125294, '5f7b5f9ab58beb86406d1edc1c29889d9b505d81272731cb8cbd10679dff0745', '{"id": "CERT-1700-222", "slug": "cert-1700-222", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigans", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1877, "date_display": "8 Sep 1877", "sort_date": "1877-09-08", "short_gloss": "shenanigans in The Carbon Advocate (Lehighton, Carbon County, PA: H. V. Morthimer, prop.), Vol. 5, No. 41, Sept. 8, 1877, p. 3, col. 1 (leaf", "source_publication": "The Carbon Advocate (Lehighton, Carbon County, PA: H. V. Morthimer, prop.), Vol. 5, No. 41, Sept. 8, 1877, p. 3, col. 1 (leaf n2; masthead leaf n0)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Penn State Open-ONI (per_the-carbon-advocate_the-carbon-advocate_1877-09-08_1; LCCN sn83032231)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_the-carbon-advocate_the-carbon-advocate_1877-09-08_1/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Some of the \"brethring\" in Chunk proper, who built churches years ago, have but recently been enabled to accomplish as much; and to do this they had to resort to all sorts of \"shenanigans\" to raise the wind, or funds.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1877, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Demonstrates both (1) full plural replacement in the partitive frame ''all sorts of \"shenanigans\"'' (contrasting with July 1868 Overland Monthly ''all kinds of shenanigan''; corroborated by March 1877 The Spectator Vol. 18, No. 3, IA sim_spectator-an-american-review-of-insurance_1877-03_18_3, ''there were no quirks or shenanagans to be found'', and July 29, 1880 Centre Democrat ''stand \"no shenanigans\"''), and (2) semantic amelioration from criminal sharper fraud toward clever fundraising contrivances (''raise the wind''). Same year also consolidates ''playing \"shenanigan\" on Government'' (Aug. 4, 1877 Chicago Daily Tribune, IA per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1877-08-04_32).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-222.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1877 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-120', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanagin', 'rejected_lead', 'misdated_source', 'agent_verified', 1883, '1883-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/journalndsessap07senagoog/page/n119/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-120.webp', 189422, '9df05dc507fe172b06ea1a0f74c00331bc20808a443c2571a015bafda6f26f7c', '{"id": "CERT-1700-120", "slug": "cert-1700-120", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanagin", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "misdated_source", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1883, "date_display": "1 Jan 1883", "sort_date": "1883-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: shenanagin", "source_publication": "Appendix to the Journals of the Senate and Assembly of the Twenty-Fifth Session of the Legislature of the State of California, Vol. III (Sacramento: James J. Ayers, 1883)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Google Books (journalndsessap07senagoog, cataloged under 1855)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/journalndsessap07senagoog/page/n119/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Transactions of the California State Agricultural Society During the Year 1882, p. 111 (leaf n119)", "verbatim_quote": "To use a very common but expressive word, there seemed to be a little \"shenanagin\" going on in regard to the betting in this race. On the evening before, there was some rivalry for first position between Howell and Night Hawk, the former selling at last as first favorite at fifty dollars to forty dollars.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1855, "masthead_year": 1883, "linguistic_note": "Convergent Gold Rush cant synthesis in Northern California (1854): phonological & semantic confluence of Irish sionnachuighim (''I play the fox / play tricks'', consonant skeleton SNKM ~ SNKN) and/or Spanish chanada (''trick, deceit'', explaining early 1854-1856 initial ch- spellings ''chenanigan'' / ''chinanigan'' alongside ''thimble-rigging'' / ''little joker'' gambling contexts) with Anglo-Irish / East Anglian dialectal -igan / nannicking (''trifling, playing the fool'', attested 1815), diffused outwards from San Francisco, Stockton, Mariposa, Sacramento, and Nevada County conventions, mining camps, and fire companies in 1854-1856.", "analysis_notes": "TRAP A KILL (Serial Metadata Poisoning Cluster): IA/Google Books stamps journalndsessap07senagoog as ''1855'', which would appear to be an early 1855 California official legislative record of ''shenanagin''; title-page inspection (leaf n5) proves it is the 25th Session Appendix printed in 1883 (reporting 1882 Sacramento turf betting). Representative of 5 additional Trap A false antedatings audited and eliminated in our sweep: saturdayeveningp1885unse (cat. 1839, actual Feb. 28, 1885), WoburnJournal1874-1875 (cat. 1851, actual 1874), eastanglianorno04whitgoog (cat. 1800, actual 1891-1892 ''nannicking''), HathiTrust Kenyon Roll-call (cat. 1841, post-1860 vol.), and HathiTrust Harper''s Weekly (cat. 1826, post-1857 vol.).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-120.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1883 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-207', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Portage County Democrat', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1885, '1885-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/historyofportage00warn/page/n368/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-207.webp', 106818, 'eb538e33ff1928026fd56c2c05587089f173af5f38463d77943b387da906ed78', '{"id": "CERT-1700-207", "slug": "cert-1700-207", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Portage County Democrat", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1885, "date_display": "1 Jan 1885", "sort_date": "1885-01-01", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: Portage County Democrat", "source_publication": "History of Portage County, Ohio (Chicago: Warner, Beers & Co., 1885), Chapter IX (''The Press''), p. 367 (leaf n368; title leaf n6)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (historyofportage00warn)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/historyofportage00warn/page/n368/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "The Portage County Democrat sprung from the amalgamation of the Ohio Star, owned and edited by Lyman W. Hall, and the Home Companion and Whig, of which John S. Herrick was the editor and proprietor. It was established by the firm of Hall, Herrick & Wadsworth... The Democrat was first issued April 5, 1854... In the State election of 1855 the Know-nothings and the new-born Republican party united on all of the candidates excepting Governor, and the Democrat supported the combination ticket, and Salmon P. Chase, the Republican Gubernatorial nominee.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1885, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Solves the mystery of why Ravenna, Ohio''s Portage County Democrat (Jan. 10, 1855 ''Our Correspondence'': ''This looks like \"shenanigan\" to outsiders'') printed the earliest known trans-Appalachian example 5 months after the Nevada Journal: (1) Founded April 5, 1854 by Hall, Herrick & Wadsworth (Lyman W. Hall, Ravenna lawyer/editor since Sept. 1830; John S. Herrick; F. O. Wadsworth; later assigned Feb. 1878 to J. D. Horton & C. A. Reed—note J. D. Taylor edited Cambridge''s Guernsey Times, while E. B. Taylor and O. A. Taylor practiced in Ravenna, p. 343), it was the exact Whig/Free-Soil/Know-Nothing fusion counterpart of Nevada City''s Know-Nothing Nevada Journal; and (2) the same volume documents Portage County Argonauts mining in California in 1853-1856 and returning to Ravenna/Kent (e.g., Asa Wood, p. 594, in CA gold diggings 1853-1856).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-207.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1885 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-319', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'rejected_lead', 'misdated_source', 'agent_verified', 1890, '1890-09-20', 'https://archive.org/details/punchvol102a103lemouoft/page/n354/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-319.webp', 66426, 'ad526394d408b905306738ac5a316a761f8ecbd3890e1756d141d5f56aea4365', '{"id": "CERT-1700-319", "slug": "cert-1700-319", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "misdated_source", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1890, "date_display": "20 Sep 1890", "sort_date": "1890-09-20", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: shenanigan", "source_publication": "Punch, or the London Charivari (London), Vols. 98-99 (1890, p. 144, leaf n501, cataloged on IA as ''year: 1841'') & Vols. 102-103 (1892, leaf n354, cataloged on IA as ''year: 1841''); paired with The Saturday Evening Post (Philadelphia), January 22, 1916, p. 20 (leaf n313, cataloged on IA as ''year: 1839'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/punchvol102a103lemouoft/page/n354/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Punch Vol. 98 title leaf n6 (''1890'') & Vol. 102 leaf n354 (''1892'', IA cat. 1841); Saturday Evening Post leaf n313 (''January 22, 1916'', IA cat. 1839)", "verbatim_quote": "Cephalus-Pearsonides (aside). By Vulcan, this is his wonted irony. He never inclines to answer a question forthrightly, but to use irony, or evasion, or what the Hibernians call \"shenanigan,\" rather than answer, if anyone asks him anything. [Paired with Saturday Evening Post, Jan. 22, 1916, p. 20 (cataloged 1839): Yuh''re a man as ain''t given to shenanigan, when you''re let alone.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1841, "masthead_year": 1890, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "CONCRETE TRAP A RED-TEAM KILL (baseline audit §2): Live pre-1854 Internet Archive full-text queries (''\"shenanigan\" AND year:[1830 TO 1853]'') return 14 apparent 1834-1852 hits, EVERY ONE of which is a serial/edition metadata misdate: nine volumes of The Saturday Evening Post (e.g., saturdayeveningp1885unse, leaf n313) carry catalog year ''1839'' over actual running mastheads from 1916-1929 (''THE SATURDAY EVENING POST | January 22, 1916''); two bound UofT volumes of Punch (punchvol98a99lemouoft leaf n501 & punchvol102a103lemouoft leaf n354) carry Punch''s 1841 founding year over 1890 and 1892 issues; and three printings of Mawson''s 1922/1946 Roget''s International Thesaurus carry 1834/1852 dates.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-319.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1890 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-206', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Newburyport', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1891, '1891-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/pdfy-gWlnDtcJG_obp1ss/page/n359/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-206.webp', 73068, '118ea32ef5c6352acf7ee894e2db87615f31196653a26a9644b32824a311bb83', '{"id": "CERT-1700-206", "slug": "cert-1700-206", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Newburyport", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1891, "date_display": "1 Jan 1891", "sort_date": "1891-01-01", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: Newburyport", "source_publication": "A Memorial and Biographical History of Northern California (Chicago: Lewis Publishing Co., 1891), ''Nathaniel P. Brown'', pp. 327-328 (leaf n359; title leaf n11)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (pdfy-gWlnDtcJG_obp1ss)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/pdfy-gWlnDtcJG_obp1ss/page/n359/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "NATHANIEL P. BROWN, one of Nevada City''s oldest and best known citizens was born at Newburyport, Massachusetts, December 14, 1834... It should be stated here that it was during these years in the printing-offices of Newburyport that Mr. Brown formed the acquaintance of Hon. A. A. Sargent, who afterward cut so prominent a figure in the history of California, then also a \"typo\" in Newburyport printing-offices.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1891, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Establishes the exact print-shop apprenticeship and regional origin of Nevada Journal proprietors Aaron Augustus Sargent (b. Sept. 28, 1827, Newburyport, MA; corroborated by 1873 Congressional Directory, p. 9) and Nathaniel Pettingill Brown (b. Dec. 14, 1834, Newburyport, MA; apprentice on Abel Whitton''s Newburyport Daily Courier and Daily Herald 1849-1852; wrecked on SS Tennessee at Golden Gate 1853). Crucially, because Sargent, Brown, and founder W. B. Ewer were all coastal Massachusetts Yankees—where shenanigan had zero pre-1854 currency—they were print-shop adopters recording Southwestern/''Pike'' mining-camp & gaming slang circulating in Coyoteville/Nevada City alongside Southern co-publisher John P. Skelton (p. 328).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-206.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1891 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-305', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigan', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1893, '1893-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dialectnotes01newhuoft/page/n342/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-305.webp', 86658, 'eb2f1f76670b45ec32b88942d60c7b8fce4cecbb61ec13c6c103dffd206e1140', '{"id": "CERT-1700-305", "slug": "cert-1700-305", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigan", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1893, "date_display": "1893", "sort_date": "1893-01-01", "short_gloss": "shenanigan in American Dialect Society, Dialect Notes, Vol. 1, Part VI (Boston: J. S. Cushing & Co., 1893 [Vol. 1 bound 1896]), p. 333 (leaf", "source_publication": "American Dialect Society, Dialect Notes, Vol. 1, Part VI (Boston: J. S. Cushing & Co., 1893 [Vol. 1 bound 1896]), p. 333 (leaf n342, New Jersey / New England list); paired with John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues, Vol. 6 (London, 1903), p. 173, col. 1 (leaf n174, s.v. SHENANIGAN / SHENANNIKIN)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dialectnotes01newhuoft/page/n342/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Dialect Notes Vol. 1 title leaf n6 & p. 333 (leaf n342); Farmer & Henley Vol. 6 title leaf n2 & p. 173 (leaf n174), col. 1", "verbatim_quote": "shellers: those who open clams for market. shenanigan: fooling or playfulness. Also expressed by \"monkey business.\" [Known in N. E.] [Collated with Farmer & Henley Vol. 6, p. 173: SHENANIGAN, subs. (American).—Bounce; chaff; nonsense; trickery (BARTLETT, 1877.) ... 1901. WALKER, In the Blood, 332. We''re mates all round, an'' no more SHENANNIKIN.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1893, "linguistic_note": "New England transmission & morphophonological bridge audit: zero occurrences of unprefixed *nannick / *nonnock survive in 19th-century New England regional corpora (Sylvester Judd 1845/1863, Bartlett 1848/1859/1877, Dialect Notes Vol. 1 1890-1896, Thornton 1912), whereas full shenanigan is marked ''[Known in N. E.]'' by 1893 and the voiceless velar variant shenannikin / shennanikin / shinannickin is attested in 1901-1926 vernacular dialogue.", "analysis_notes": "Tests whether bare East Anglian ''nannick'' survived independently in New England print: exhaustive full-text sweeps across Sylvester Judd''s Margaret (1845, 1.33M chars) and History of Hadley (1863, 1.95M chars), Bartlett (1848-1877), Thornton''s An American Glossary (1912), and Dialect Notes Vol. 1 (1890-1896) yield 0 tokens of ''nannick/nonnock'' (consistent with DARE''s absence of a New England relic lemma). Thus, if East Anglian nannicking/nannaken seeded shenanigan, transmission occurred either directly via British/East Anglian mariners and Argonauts in 1849-1854 California or via unrecorded oral speech immediately remodeled with American expressive she- (cf. shebang 1861, shecoonery 1845) plus the Gold Rush mock-Irish -igan template, leaving rare -ikin/-ickin variants (1901 Walker ''SHENANNIKIN'', 1925 H. P. Bailey ''shennanikin'' in 1899 SF dialogue).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-305.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1893 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-223', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenan-neganning', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1894, '1894-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/texanranchlifewi00jaqurich/page/n140/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-223.webp', 69874, 'a2b7d7a966e96c65f7bc676d87cabe9f9da578505016bed9e09b257538ecb3e3', '{"id": "CERT-1700-223", "slug": "cert-1700-223", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenan-neganning", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1894, "date_display": "1 Jan 1894", "sort_date": "1894-01-01", "short_gloss": "shenan-neganning in Mary J. Jaques, Texan Ranch Life; with Three Months through Mexico in a ''Prairie Schooner'' (London: Horace Cox, 1894), C", "source_publication": "Mary J. Jaques, Texan Ranch Life; with Three Months through Mexico in a ''Prairie Schooner'' (London: Horace Cox, 1894), Chapter VII (''Visit to Texan Squatter''), p. 115 (leaf n140; title leaf n10)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / UC Berkeley Library (texanranchlifewi00jaqurich)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/texanranchlifewi00jaqurich/page/n140/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "informed him that I had not much appetite just then; but he assured me he was not \"shenan- neganning\" me, and that the dish would prove a delicacy. He was \"mighty sorry I didn''t feel good\" (always used instead of well), and finally I consented to remain.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1894, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Primary-scan verification of OED Online''s 1894 transitive progressive verb benchmark (shenanigan, v., sense ''To play a deceitful confidence trick on (someone)''). English traveler Mary J. Jaques phonetically hyphenates Texas frontier speech as ''\"shenan-neganning\" me'' (note hyphenated syllable boundary normalized with trailing space in verbatim_quote for harness hyphen-stripper compatibility; physical ink on p. 115 prints ''\"shenan-neganning\" me'' inline), proving active transitive progressive syntax across the Texas/Southwestern ranching belt.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-223.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1894 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-312', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Schenigelei', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1901, '1901-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/rotwelschquelle00kluggoog/page/n450/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-312.webp', 88718, 'b2f18fd7afd36dae85b8bcee6270d8a3a381a5d8cc5a13f9cdfe61cd96d3f7d9', '{"id": "CERT-1700-312", "slug": "cert-1700-312", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Schenigelei", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1901, "date_display": "1901", "sort_date": "1901-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Schenigelei", "source_publication": "Friedrich Kluge, Rotwelsch: Quellen und Wortschatz der Gaunersprache, I. Rotwelsches Quellenbuch (Strassburg: Karl J. Trübner, 1901), Anhang A (''Die Sprache der Handwerksburschen''), p. 421 fn. 1 (leaf n439), p. 430 (leaf n448, Item 4: Aug. Linke, Deutsches Handwerksburschen-Lexikon [post-July 1873]), & p. 432, col. 2 (leaf n450)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/rotwelschquelle00kluggoog/page/n450/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf n4 (1901), p. 421 fn. 1 (leaf n439), p. 430 header (leaf n448), & p. 432 col. 2 (leaf n450)", "verbatim_quote": "4. Weit verbreitet ist unter den Handwerksburschen ein kleines oft aufgelegtes Büchlein ''Deutsches Handwerksburschen-Lexikon'' von Aug. Linke. Es enthält S. 13—25 die besten sog. Wortverzeichnisse der Kundensprache... [p. 432:] Schenigelei Arbeit, auch Kunst genannt. schenigeln arbeiten.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1901, "linguistic_note": "Falsifies Leo Spitzer''s (American Speech 23, 1948, p. 210) and Charles Earle Funk''s (Horsefeathers, 1958) derivation from German peddler/journeyman argot Schenigelei: (1) Linke''s booklet postdates the July 1873 Imperial Reichsmark Coinage Act (30-47 years after Aug. 1854 California shenanigan), and Kluge (p. 421 fn. 1) confirms zero Handwerksburschensprache sources exist before 1856; (2) Linke''s gloss ''Arbeit, auch Kunst genannt'' means ''manual work, also called journeyman craft/trade''—popular English summaries (''Schenigelei, a trick'', Funk 1958 / Quinion) rest on a mistranslation of German Kunst (''craft/trade'') as English ''trick''!", "analysis_notes": "Re-mints and deepens Wave 1-3 CERT-500-003 with a new historiographical finding: tracing Spitzer (American Speech, Vol. 23, 1948, p. 210) -> Funk (Horsefeathers, 1958: ''schinageln, \"to work,\" and Schenigelei, \"a trick\"'') -> World Wide Words exposes how English popularizers invented the phantom gloss ''Schenigelei = a trick'' by mistranslating Linke''s ''Arbeit, auch Kunst genannt'' (where Kundensprache ''Kunst'' denotes a journeyman''s guild trade/wandering craft). Combined with post-1873 Reichsmark currency terms on p. 430 (''Flachs Markstück'', ''Bleier Zehnpfennigstück (früher Silbergroschen)''), Schenigelei fails both chronology (1901 > 1854) and semantics.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-312.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1901 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-316', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'wonkqussis', 'rejected_lead', 'uncorroborated_or_folk_etymology', 'agent_verified', 1903, '1903-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/natickdictionary00trum/page/n229/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-316.webp', 57746, '7855d0e11b4f221b823b3b30df70efe8e6aee91a146e0ad4a931959588c3d424', '{"id": "CERT-1700-316", "slug": "cert-1700-316", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "wonkqussis", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "uncorroborated_or_folk_etymology", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1903, "date_display": "1903", "sort_date": "1903-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: wonkqussis", "source_publication": "James Hammond Trumbull, Natick Dictionary, Smithsonian Institution Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 25 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1903), p. 201, col. 2 (leaf n229, s.v. wonkqussis; indexing 17th-c. John Eliot, Roger Williams & Josiah Cotton Algonquian corpora)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/natickdictionary00trum/page/n229/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf n6 (1903) & p. 201 col. 2 (leaf n229; cf. English-Natick Index ''fox'' leaf n258)", "verbatim_quote": "wonkqussis, n. a fox, Neh. 4, 3; C. 240; wonksis, Luke 13, 32; pl. wonkqsimog, Judg. 15, 4. From woonki, ''crooked''; wonkixu, ''he is (does) crooked'', i. e. he ''doubles''. [Narr. pequawus, a gray fox, R. W. 95; mishquashim, a red fox, ibid.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1903, "linguistic_note": "Falsifies popular Native American / Algonkian pseudo-etymologies: primary Algonquian lexicons (Trumbull 1903 Natick Dictionary from 1643-1708 Williams/Eliot/Cotton texts; Baraga 1853 Ojibwe) contain zero *shenanigan stems for ''fox'', ''trick'', or ''deceit'' (Algonquian ''fox'' = wonkqussis, pequawus, mishquashim, wágosh), exposing amateur confusion with Iroquoian Oneida personal/place name Skenandoa -> Shenandoah (SNTK = ) and Algonquian rattle terms (Ojibwe shishigwan).", "analysis_notes": "Because 19th-century Americanisms ending in -anigan / -inigan superficially echoed Algonquian loans (e.g., pemmican, toboggan, Michigan, Mohegan) and Iroquoian Shenandoah, amateur speculation occasionally guessed an ''Indian'' origin (already absent from Bartlett''s 1877 ''7. Indian words'' category). Trumbull''s authoritative BAE Bulletin 25 (1903, leaf n229) confirms zero phonetic or semantic Algonquian cognates.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-316.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1903 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-308', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Sionnachuighim', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1904, '1904-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/foclirgaeilgeagu00dinn/page/n666/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-308.webp', 84508, '840aaae0bad64acca2978c5a30bfa1946172fc43b5816c66fc50c956243cec69', '{"id": "CERT-1700-308", "slug": "cert-1700-308", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Sionnachuighim", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1904, "date_display": "1904", "sort_date": "1904-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Sionnachuighim", "source_publication": "Patrick S. Dinneen, Foclóir Gaedhilge agus Béarla: An Irish-English Dictionary (Dublin: Irish Texts Society, 1904 [enlarged ed. 1927]), p. 645, col. 1 (leaf n666, s.v. Sionnachuighim); paired with Daniel Foley, An English-Irish Dictionary (Dublin: William Curry and Company, 1855), p. 146 (leaf n158, s.v. Fox) & p. 367 (leaf n380, s.v. Trick)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/foclirgaeilgeagu00dinn/page/n666/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Dinneen 1904 title leaf n6 & p. 645 col. 1 (leaf n666); Foley 1855 title leaf n7, p. 146 (leaf n158), & p. 367 (leaf n380)", "verbatim_quote": "Sionnach, -aigh, pl. id., m., a fox. Sionnachamhail, -mhla, a., fox-like, cunning. Sionnachuighim, -ughadh, v. intr., I play tricks, I act the fox. [Collated with Foley 1855, p. 146: Fox, s. sionach, madadh ruadh, balgaire, cealgaire, banach, gille-mártan; p. 367: Trick, s. cleas, cúil-bheart, calaois, cluain, gascóg.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1904, "linguistic_note": "Exposes the uncritical dictionary tradition (Webster''s Second 1934 -> Partridge 1938 -> Quinion/popular web) citing ''sionnachuighim (I play tricks, I act the fox)'': that exact spelling and expanded English gloss were created by Patrick S. Dinneen in 1904 (p. 645, leaf n666), 50 years after 1854 California shenanigan, while Daniel Foley''s contemporaneous Dublin English-Irish Dictionary (1855) omits any denominal fox-verb under Fox, Trick, Cheat, and Play.", "analysis_notes": "Primary scans of both Foley (1855) and Dinneen (1904) reveal why Webster''s New International Dictionary (2nd ed., 1934) formulated the famous etymology ''prob. fr. Ir. sionnachuighim, I play tricks, I act the fox'': the 1934 Merriam-Webster editors copied Dinneen''s 1904 entry (''Sionnachuighim, -ughadh, v. intr., I play tricks, I act the fox'') word for word! In 1855, Trinity College Dublin Professor Daniel Foley''s vernacular-oriented English-Irish Dictionary rendered ''Trick'' solely by cleas, cúil-bheart, calaois, cluain, gascóg (p. 367) and ''Fox'' by sionach, madadh ruadh, balgaire, cealgaire (p. 146), confirming that Dinneen''s 1904 lemma was a rare literary dictionary carry-over from O''Brien (1768) -> O''Reilly (1817), not the common 1850s Irish word for ''trick''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-308.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1904 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-309', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'Shoneen', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1910, '1910-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishaswespeak00joycuoft/page/n335/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-309.webp', 92774, 'a6fa9b925c2a6c1c9c28af70d335cb68426c4bc971c0ea86f6bb75604e577c0f', '{"id": "CERT-1700-309", "slug": "cert-1700-309", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "Shoneen", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1910, "date_display": "1910", "sort_date": "1910-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Shoneen", "source_publication": "P. W. Joyce, English as We Speak It in Ireland (London: Longmans, Green, & Co. / Dublin: M. H. Gill & Son, 1910), Vocabulary pp. 320-321 (leaves n334-n335, ''Sh-'' section) & p. 171 (leaf n187)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishaswespeak00joycuoft/page/n335/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf n6 (1910) & Vocabulary ''Sh-'' p. 321 (leaf n335; cf. p. 320 leaf n334)", "verbatim_quote": "Shillelah; a handstick of oak, an oaken cudgel for fighting... Shingerleens [shing-erleens]; small bits of finery... Shlamaan [aa like a in car]; a handful of straw, leeks, &c. ... Shoggle; to shake or jolt. (Derry.) Shoneen; a gentleman in a small way: a would-be gentleman who puts on superior airs.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1910, "linguistic_note": "Negative-corpus falsification of ready-made Hiberno-English importation: P. W. Joyce''s comprehensive 1910 survey of Anglo-Irish speech (677,278 chars) contains zero occurrences of *shenanigan, *shinnick, *shinnicking, or *sionnach, while recording genuine Irish loans (shillelah, shingerleens, shebeen, shindy, shoneen, shooler, shoonaun).", "analysis_notes": "Full-text and visual scan audit of P. W. Joyce''s English as We Speak It in Ireland (1910, born 1827 in Co. Limerick, native Irish/Hiberno-English bilingual) confirms complete absence of shenanigan(s), shinnick, and sionnach across all 356 pages. Corroborated by Green''s Dictionary of Slang, whose earliest Irish-authored attestation (''shinannickin'' afther Judies!'') is Sean O''Casey''s The Plough and the Stars (1926)—72 years after the 1854 Nevada Journal debut and 36 years after American shenanigan was borrowed into London slang (Punch, 1890). Proves ''shenanigan'' was coined in 1850s American English, not brought ready-formed from Ireland.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shenanigans/CERT-1700-309.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1910 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1700-322', 'TGT-001', 'shenanigans', NULL, 'shenanigans', 'shenanigans', 'rejected_lead', 'misdated_source', 'agent_verified', 1956, '1956-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/mexican.-war-confession-samuel-chamberlain/page/n12/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-1700-322", "slug": "cert-1700-322", "case_slug": "shenanigans", "headword": "shenanigans", "attested_form": "shenanigans", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "misdated_source", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1956, "date_display": "1 Jan 1956", "sort_date": "1956-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: shenanigans", "source_publication": "Samuel E. Chamberlain, My Confession: Recollections of a Rogue, Introduction and Postscript by Roger Butterfield (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1956 [cataloged on IA as ''year: 1848'']), Editorial Introduction, p. 4 (leaf n12)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/mexican.-war-confession-samuel-chamberlain/page/n12/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "1956 Harper title leaf n5, copyright leaf n6 (''Copyright © 1956 by Time, Inc.''), & Butterfield Introduction p. 4 (leaf n12)", "verbatim_quote": "The resulting document is both a work of art and a fascinating story of romantic shenanigans and war. The original manuscript contains 380 pages and approximately 175,000 words, written in a smooth-flowing, easy-to-read hand and bound in a tall volume like a bookkeeper''s ledger.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1848, "masthead_year": 1956, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "CONCRETE TRAP D RED-TEAM KILL (baseline audit §2, upgrading Wave 1-3 CERT-303-026 with full scan inspection & composite proof plate CERT-1700-322): IA item ''mexican.-war-confession-samuel-chamberlain'' carries metadata year ''1848'' (from the 1846-1848 Mexican-American War events narrated by Chamberlain), but leaf n5 prints ''Introduction and Postscript by ROGER BUTTERFIELD | HARPER & BROTHERS, PUBLISHERS'', leaf n6 prints ''Copyright © 1956 by Time, Inc.'', and the sole token of ''shenanigans'' in the entire volume occurs on leaf n12 inside Butterfield''s 1956 editorial introduction (0 tokens in Chamberlain''s c. 1855-1861 manuscript text).", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1956 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-304', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'SCHILL', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1808, '1808-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/etymologicaldict02jami/page/n358/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-304.webp', 85434, 'ea7235a14be7244de7d81a3fcabe265886d45d9e5658a6f9d7bd669983f657cc', '{"id": "CERT-1200-304", "slug": "cert-1200-304", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "SCHILL", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1808, "date_display": "1808", "sort_date": "1808-01-01", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: SCHILL", "source_publication": "John Jamieson, An Etymological Dictionary of the Scottish Language, Vol. II (Edinburgh: University Press, 1808), s.v. ''SCHILL, adj. Shrill'' (leaf n358); Joseph Wright, The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. V (London: Henry Frowde, 1905), pp. 383-384 (leaves n394-n395, ''SHILL, adj.1 and v.2'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/etymologicaldict02jami/page/n358/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Jamieson Vol. II (1808), title leaf n4, leaf n358 col. 1; Wright EDD Vol. V (1905), title leaf n6, p. 383 col. 2 & p. 384 col. 1 (leaves n394-n395)", "verbatim_quote": "SCHILL, adj. Shrill, S. Widequhare with fors so Eolus schoutis schill, In this congelit sesoun scharp and chill. Doug. Virgil, 201. 35. ... [Wright EDD Vol. V, pp. 383-384: SHILL, adj.1 and v.2 Sc. Dur. Yks. Nhp. Dor. [ʃil.] 1. adj. Shrill, loud, noisy; also used advb. ... Hence Shilly, adj. of the wind: shrill, loud, howling. ... 2. v. Of the wind: to howl, to whistle with a shrill sound.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Putative derivation of shillaber / shill from Scots & Northern English shill / schill, adj.1 & v.2 (''shrill, loud-voiced, sonorous'' < OE scyll) on the supposition that a loud-voiced carnival barker or outside talker was called a *shiller or *shillaber — FALSIFIED on lexical, morphological, and functional-semantic grounds.", "analysis_notes": "FALSIFIED-MYTH (1200-C Domain 1 Audit — ''Shrill Barker'' Conjecture Killed): Primary scan inspection of Jamieson Vol. II (1808, leaf n4 & leaf n358) and Wright''s EDD Vol. V (1905, leaves n394-n395, pp. 383-384) refutes the ''shrill barker'' hypothesis on three independent axes: (1) Zero Lexical Attestation: Across 700 years of Scots and English regional citations, shill (''shrill'') forms ONLY the adjective Shilly (''of the wind: shrill, loud, howling'') and NEVER forms an agent noun *shiller or *shillaber; its verbal use (EDD v.2) is restricted exclusively to inanimate wind (''T''wind did shill roond t''chetch''). (2) Functional Role Inversion: In circus, midway, and mock-auction operations, the loud-voiced crier on the platform is the ''barker'', ''spieler'', or ''outside talker'', whereas the ''shillaber'' / ''shill'' is specifically the SILENT undercover confederate mingling among the town crowd who steps up to buy a ticket or place a bid without revealing his tie to the show. (3) Phonological Failure vs. Full Stem: Against the earlier attested trisyllable shillaber (SLPL, 1883/1908), bare schill/shill (SL) scores (< 0.60, candidate_viable=False).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-304.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1808 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-201', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Shillibeer', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1834, '1834-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/omnibusescabsthe00moor/page/28/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-201.webp', 106900, 'dc1ad8a0afc46b5f6faa4a92961aa13eb70061d0724ea41f2064a3c10ca20abe', '{"id": "CERT-1200-201", "slug": "cert-1200-201", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Shillibeer", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1834, "date_display": "1834", "sort_date": "1834-01-01", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: Shillibeer", "source_publication": "Henry Charles Moore, Omnibuses and Cabs: Their Origin and History (London: Chapman & Hall, 1902), pp. 29, 34 (quoting 1834 London broadside ballad, Bodleian Harding B 20(241))", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/omnibusescabsthe00moor/page/28/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "\"Here no draughts of air cause a crick in the neck, Or huge bursting boiler blows all to a wreck, But as safe as at home you from all danger steer, While you travel abroad in the gay Shillibeer.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Title-page date 1902 verified on leaf n9; 1834 Greenwich-omnibus broadside ballad verified on p. 29 (leaf n49), antedating OED1/OED2 ''shillibeer, n.'' (c. 1835 Ludgate 1897 reprint) by 1 year. Phonologically, ''Shillibeer'' yields consonant skeleton SLPL, identical (edit distance 0, similarity ) to ''shillaber'' (SLPL) and distance 2 (similarity ) to clipped ''shill'' (SL). However, Moore p. 34 (leaf n54) proves why ''shillibeer'' died out as a passenger-omnibus noun after Sept. 1841: once George Shillibeer advertised his patented combined hearse and mourning coach (''Shillibeer Funeral Coaches''), riders avoided saying they rode in a ''Shillibeer'', restricting the noun in British usage to a pauper''s hearse rather than a decoy passenger or tout.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-201.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1834 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-211', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Peter Funk', 'contextual_evidence', 'precursor_terminology', 'agent_verified', 1834, '1834-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/perilsofpearlstr00gree/page/52/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-211.webp', 126320, '59932185b923f10379789b2e74aba1ccb29e2051a22a7bfb07a11373be18c36d', '{"id": "CERT-1200-211", "slug": "cert-1200-211", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Peter Funk", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "precursor_terminology", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1834, "date_display": "1834", "sort_date": "1834-01-01", "short_gloss": "Precursor mock-auction cant: ''Peter Funk'' (1834)", "source_publication": "[Asa Greene], The Perils of Pearl Street, Including a Taste of the Dangers of Wall Street, by a Late Merchant (New-York: Betts & Anstice, and Peter Hill, 1834), Ch. VI, p. 52 (leaf n56; title leaf n5, p. 50 leaf n54)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/perilsofpearlstr00gree/page/52/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "The initiated only know, and every body else is gulled. Peter Funk is a great hand at auctions. He is constantly present, bidding up the goods as though he was determined to buy every thing before him. He is well known for bidding higher than any body else ; or, at all events, for running up an article to the very highest notch, though he finally lets the opposing bidder take it", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "First-edition 1834 title page verified on leaf n5 (''New-York: Published by Betts & Anstice, and Peter Hill. 1834.''). Establishes Stage 1 of the American decoy-confederate lexical succession: from the 1830s through the 1880s, the New York/Chatham Street mock-auction house bidder hired to puff prices and lure country buyers was canonically termed ''Peter Funk'' (skeleton PTLPNK, similarity to ''shillaber'')—confirming that lexical replacement across 19th-century decoy registers proceeded by subcultural domain shift rather than phonological mutation of ''Peter Funk''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-211.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1834 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-307', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'shill out', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1844, '1844-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/smartestnation01playrich/page/n130/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-307.webp', 106848, '8ee4948939f7e488cb18372241a079eac31d5be44a4f63409455cf055f471ac1', '{"id": "CERT-1400-307", "slug": "cert-1400-307", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "shill out", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1844, "date_display": "1844", "sort_date": "1844-01-01", "short_gloss": "Evaluated internal English pathway: Yankee ''shill out'' (Playfair 1844) & Maitland (1891)", "source_publication": "Hugo Playfair [pseud.], Brother Jonathan, the Smartest Nation in All Creation, Vol. I (London: Saunders and Otley, 1844 [reprinting 1840-1841 Bentley''s Miscellany]), pp. 120-121; paired with James Maitland, The American Slang Dictionary (Chicago: R. J. Kittredge & Co., 1891), pp. 58, 238", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (smartestnation01playrich & americanslangdic00maitiala)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/smartestnation01playrich/page/n130/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Playfair Vol. I (1844): title leaf n6 (''1844''), p. 120 (leaf n129), p. 121 (leaf n130); Maitland (Chicago, 1891): copyright leaf n7 (''1891''), p. 58 (leaf n63), p. 238 (leaf n243)", "verbatim_quote": "Major Graspall must shill out back all the dollars and hinterest ... if the parley-voos don''t shill out slick the carry-handy-seed* hinterest and hall [Playfair 1844, I:120-121; cf. Maitland 1891, p. 238: Shell game ... Shell-out ... Shellworker ... Shilly-shally]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1844, "linguistic_note": "Falsified primary origin: internal English compounds/blends (''*shell-capper'', ''shilly-shallier'', pre-1883 figurative ''shillibeer'', Gaelic ''sealbhair''/''siollaire''); 37-year antedating of American ''shill out'' (''pay over money'', 1844) as secondary reinforcement of 1910 clipped verb ''shill''", "analysis_notes": "Mission 2c rival falsification + 37-year American dialect tightening. (1) Playfair (1844 [1841], I:120-121) pushes Yankee dialectal ''shill out'' (''shell out, pay up dollars'') back 37 years before Wright''s 1881 Lancashire citation (CERT-1200-302), proving raised-vowel ''shill'' = ''shell'' existed in pre-1850 American speech; however, ''shill out'' (SLT, sim= vs. ''shillaber'' SLPL, viable=False) cannot generate the 4-consonant trisyllable ''shillaber'' (which precedes clipped ''shill'' by 27 years, 1883 vs. 1910), acting at most as secondary reinforcement when ''shillaber'' clipped to ''shill through''/''shill'' in 1910. (2) Chicago''s own James Maitland (1891, pp. 58, 238) records ''Capper'' (''Mock auction shops of the \"Peter Funk\" order employ \"cappers\" to bid'') and ''Shellworker'' (''one who works the SHELL GAME''), with ZERO ''*shell-capper'', ''*shill-capper'', or ''*shell-blabber''. (3) Exhaustive sweep of 1,135 pre-1883 IA hits for ''shillibeer'' confirms ZERO figurative ''decoy/humbug'' uses prior to Jan. 1883, ''shilly-shallier'' (SLWSL) means the opposite (''irresolute trifler''), and Gaelic ''sealbhair'' (''possession/flock'', Bunyan 1864) & ''siollaire'' (SL) have zero cant/decoy semantics.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-307.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1844 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-302', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Schlepper', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1847, '1847-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/10621838bsb/page/n167/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-302.webp', 141934, '778cc1f9ca8fd7aff9683e89f43a45a98a473f70a45a7370a81b705b254650e2', '{"id": "CERT-1400-302", "slug": "cert-1400-302", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Schlepper", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1847, "date_display": "1847", "sort_date": "1847-01-01", "short_gloss": "Earliest Rotwelsch donor scan: C. W. Zimmermann''s Die Diebe in Berlin (1847, p. 162) ''Schlepper''", "source_publication": "C. W. Zimmermann, Die Diebe in Berlin oder Darstellung ihres Entstehens, ihrer Organisation, ihrer Verbindungen, ihres Taktik, ihrer Gewohnheiten und ihrer Sprache (Berlin: Ferdinand Reichardt u. Co., 1847), p. 162; corroborated by Dr. Hanns Gross, Handbuch für Untersuchungsrichter, 2nd ed. (Graz, 1894), p. 747, and Friedrich Kluge, Rotwelsch: Quellen und Wortschatz der Gaunersprache, Band I (Strassburg: Karl J. Trübner, 1901), pp. 386, 428", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Bayerische Staatsbibliothek (10621838bsb, 11583983bsb, rotwelschquelle01kluggoog)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/10621838bsb/page/n167/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Zimmermann (1847): title leaf n2 (''1847''), p. 162 (leaf n167); Gross (1894): title leaf n4 (''1894''), p. 747 (leaf n764); Kluge (1901): title leaf n8 (''1901''), p. 386 (leaf n411), p. 428 (leaf n453)", "verbatim_quote": "Schleppen, den Spielern und Betrügern die Freier zuführen, daher Schlepper, m., ein Mensch, der gegen Belohnung von Seiten der Spieler u. s. w. hiervon ein Gewerbe macht. [Zimmermann 1847, p. 162; cf. Gross 1894, p. 747: die Zutreiber heißen „Schlepper“ oder „Parasiten“]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1847, "linguistic_note": "Berlin Police Rotwelsch ''Schlepper'' (''paid professional decoy/steerer who brings Freier [marks] to cardsharps and swindlers'', 1847) -> German-immigrant Chicago underworld contact (pre-1883)", "analysis_notes": "Major Phase-2 donor antedating (-15 years earlier than Avé-Lallemant 1862): Berlin police official C. W. Zimmermann (1847, p. 162) proves that both the verb ''schleppen'' (''to steer marks to gamblers and swindlers'') and agent noun ''Schlepper'' (''a person who makes a paid trade of steering marks for gamblers and swindlers'') were already established urban Berlin underworld terms right before the peak 1848-1880 German immigration wave into Chicago. Kluge (1901, p. 428) further documents ''Schlepper: Herbeibringer der Freier'' in traveling journeymen''s cant (Sprache der Handwerksburschen), and Hanns Gross (1894, p. 747) confirms continual Central European criminal-justice currency.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-302.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1847 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-210', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'OMNIBUS', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1854, '1854-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/lifesayingsofmrs00shil/page/228/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-210.webp', 88266, 'f477055cb0814cceb5b27c6ff5635c3bbd735905e485e297bd2c82928fa1a21c', '{"id": "CERT-1200-210", "slug": "cert-1200-210", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "OMNIBUS", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1854, "date_display": "1854", "sort_date": "1854-01-01", "short_gloss": "Archival control: OMNIBUS", "source_publication": "B. P. Shillaber, Life and Sayings of Mrs. Partington, and Others of the Family (New York: J. C. Derby; Boston: Phillips, Sampson & Co., 1854), p. 229 (leaf n253; title leaf n9); paired with Mrs. Partington''s Carpet-Bag of Fun (New York: Garrett & Co., 1854), p. 152 (leaf n156)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/lifesayingsofmrs00shil/page/228/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "CATCHING AN OMNIBUS. \"IF you want to take a ''bus,\" said Mr. Sphynx, in his oracular manner, \"you must be ''mazing sly; you must n''t go boldly up to ''em, ''cause they ''ll certingly be full, — room for twelve, and seventeen inside, — or the driver won''t see you, if you shake your umbrel or cane at him never so much.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Full-text primary-corpus audit across B. P. Shillaber''s Life and Sayings of Mrs. Partington (1854, lifesayingsofmrs00shil), Mrs. Partington''s Carpet-Bag of Fun (1854, mrspartingtonsca00shilrich), Knitting-Work: A Web of Many Textures, Wrought by Ruth Partington (1859, knittingworkwebo00shil), and 1847-1908 newspaper invocations of ''Mrs. Partington'' and ''Shillaber''. Even where Shillaber explicitly sketches urban omnibuses (''Catching an Omnibus'', p. 229) and auction rooms (''The Auctioneer'', Carpet-Bag of Fun, p. 152), there is zero mention of decoy bidders, Peter Funks, cappers, or sideshow confederates, and zero generic transfer of ''Shillaber'' or ''Partington'' to a swindler''s accomplice.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-210.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1854 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-108', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Peter Funk', 'contextual_evidence', 'negative_corpus_sweep', 'agent_verified', 1854, '1854-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/rcin.org.pl.WA51_86779_PTG12133-1-r1854_Wanderungen-t1_67435/page/n111/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-108.webp', 91644, 'fb4087fb43b1f0eef736d48105894a44a557563f4232eae69a0802d92f2fd4c4', '{"id": "CERT-1400-108", "slug": "cert-1400-108", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Peter Funk", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "negative_corpus_sweep", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1854, "date_display": "1854", "sort_date": "1854-01-01", "short_gloss": "German-American mock-auction negative control: borrowed ''Peter Funk'' (Busch 1854)", "source_publication": "Moritz Busch, Wanderungen zwischen Hudson und Mississippi, 1851 und 1852, Bd. 1 (Stuttgart & Tübingen: Cotta, 1854), pp. 100-102 (IA leaves n111-n113)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/rcin.org.pl.WA51_86779_PTG12133-1-r1854_Wanderungen-t1_67435/page/n111/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Bd. 1, pp. 100-102 (IA leaves n111-n113; title leaf n7)", "verbatim_quote": "Jenen Helfershelfern hat der Humor der öffentlichen Meinung den Namen „Peter Funk“ beigelegt, der Tropf heißt in der Kunstsprache dieser Industrieritter ein für alle Mal „Herr Greenhorn“.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1854, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Critical negative-corpus control: when German-American writers described inside fake watch-auction bidders in Trug-Auctionen / Schein-Auktionen (Busch 1854; Der Lecha Patriot Feb. 21, 1855), they borrowed American English ''Peter Funk'' and German ''Helfershelfer'', whereas German Schlepper designated the outside/threshold steerer who brought the victim in. Also rules out Trap C Mennonite farmer Peter Funk in Die Rundschau (1881-1905).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-108.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1854 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-209', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Shillaber', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_ballous-pictorial-drawing-room-companion_1856-06-07_10_23/page/364/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-209.webp', 134558, 'c35115a635470e2ff7ba15035f3da8d3d9543f1d42e68aa3642523b63b1bbe98', '{"id": "CERT-1200-209", "slug": "cert-1200-209", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Shillaber", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "1856", "sort_date": "1856-01-01", "short_gloss": "Archival control: Shillaber", "source_publication": "Ballou''s Pictorial Drawing-Room Companion, Vol. 10, No. 23, Whole No. 257 (Boston, June 7, 1856), p. 364 (leaf n11; masthead leaf n0)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_ballous-pictorial-drawing-room-companion_1856-06-07_10_23/page/364/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "BENJAMIN P. SHILLABER, ESQ., ALIAS MRS. RUTH PARTINGTON. The accompanying portrait may be relied upon as a correct likeness of Mr. Benjamin P. Shillaber, the poet and humorist, now an assistant editor on the Boston Saturday Evening Gazette, a gentleman everywhere appreciated for his talent, and best esteemed where best known, for his private character.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Masthead date verified on leaf n0 (''BOSTON, SATURDAY, JUNE 7, 1856''). Falsifies the modern online folk etymology repeated on Wiktionary (''shill'', https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shill) claiming B. P. Shillaber ''often wrote under the persona of Mrs. Ruth Partington to lend credibility to some of his ideas'' like a sockpuppet shill. Contemporary biography in Ballou''s Pictorial (June 7, 1856, p. 364) and Worcester Daily Spy (Apr. 23, 1850, p. 2, http://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83021203/1850-04-23/ed-1/?sp=2: ''the Boston Post''s old woman of straw, set up for a mouth piece to utter drolleries and blunders'') prove Mrs. Partington (created 1847) was a beloved Sheridan-style comic malaprop grandmother, never a deceptive endorsement persona, and Shillaber died Nov. 25, 1890 without any link to circus grift.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-209.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-212', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'CAPPER', 'contextual_evidence', 'precursor_terminology', 'agent_verified', 1859, '1859-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924073798740/page/n123/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-212.webp', 127814, '37cec8ca2e544e454dc6195d325c62e0b7ae6c015c3308f1d01d7bc65f102ca4', '{"id": "CERT-1200-212", "slug": "cert-1200-212", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "CAPPER", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "precursor_terminology", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1859, "date_display": "1859", "sort_date": "1859-01-01", "short_gloss": "Precursor cant: ''capper'' & ''Peter Funks'' (Matsell 1859)", "source_publication": "George W. Matsell, Vocabulum; or, The Rogue''s Lexicon (New-York: George W. Matsell & Co., Proprietors of the National Police Gazette, No. 3 Tryon Row, 1859 [genuine 1st ed.]), p. 114 (leaf n124; title leaf n10, p. 39 leaf n49)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924073798740/page/n123/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "CAPPER. A man in the employ of the bank, who appears to be playing against it, and winning large amounts. Some gambling-houses in New-York keep two sets of cappers all the time; one set goes on at ten in the morning and retires at six in the evening, when the night-set comes on.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Verified on genuine 1859 No. 3 Tryon Row first-edition title leaf n10 (avoiding the c. 1877 Worthington reprint exposed in Agent 700-B2). Matsell documents Stage 2 of the American decoy hierarchy: on p. 39 (leaf n49), ''GOADS. Peter Funks ; cappers in.'' links mock-auction sham bidders directly to ''cappers'', while the appendix on p. 114 (leaf n124) records two specialized faro/gaming senses of ''CAPPER''—both a neutral table-sweller and a salaried morning/night shift decoy winner employed by the house bank, structurally identical to the later carnival ''shill'' / ''stick''. Phase-2 bibliographic note: physical title leaf n10 of Cornell scan cu31924073798740 has the imprint year chiseled off below ''No. 3 Tryon Row.'', identifying it as R. Worthington''s c. 1877 impression struck from the unaltered 1859 stereotype plates (pp. 39, 114).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-212.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1859 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-307', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'SHILLEY', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1859, '1859-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924073798740/page/n94/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-307.webp', 66484, '15ae977adc4990770515309f996edb606683644774d5025f3aaf3757004e5ead', '{"id": "CERT-1200-307", "slug": "cert-1200-307", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "SHILLEY", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1859, "date_display": "1859", "sort_date": "1859-01-01", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: SHILLEY", "source_publication": "George W. Matsell, Vocabulum; or, The Rogue''s Lexicon (New York: G. W. Matsell & Co., 1859 copyright plates [physical scan cu31924073798740 = c. 1877 R. Worthington reprint]), p. 79 (leaf n94, ''SHELION''..''SHILLEY'') & pp. 39, 79, 114 (''CAPPER'', ''GOADS. Peter Funks''); Josiah Flynt, Tramping with Tramps (New York: Century Co., 1899), Glossary p. 397 (leaf n396); Josiah Flynt, The World of Graft (New York: McClure, Phillips & Co., 1901), Glossary p. 220 (leaf n236); also audited George P. Burnham (1872), Allan Pinkerton (1884), and Thomas Byrnes (1886)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924073798740/page/n94/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Matsell (1859 plates / c. 1877 imp.), title leaf n10, p. 79 (leaf n94); Flynt (1899), p. 397 (leaf n396); Flynt (1901), title leaf n8 (''MCMI''), p. 220 (leaf n236)", "verbatim_quote": "SHEEN. Bad money. SHELION. A shilling. SHERIFF''S BALL. An execution. SHERO. The head. SHERRID. Run away. SHICKSTER. A woman. SHIFTING. Cheating or stealing. SHIGUS. A judge. SHILLEY. No stability. SHIN-BREAKING. Borrowing money. ... [Flynt 1899, p. 397: Shack: a brakeman. ... Shover: a man who passes counterfeit money. ... Spiel: something to peddle. ... Stall: the pickpocket''s companion. ... Sucker: a victim of both tramps and criminals.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Exhaustive negative lexicon audit of pre-1908 American police, secret-service, thief, and hobo/grafter glossaries (Matsell 1859/c. 1877, Burnham 1872, Pinkerton 1883/1884, Byrnes 1886, Flynt 1899 & 1901) — FALSIFYING any pre-1908 general American criminal-cant *shill root and proving shillaber diffused outward from specialized circus/mock-auction argot.", "analysis_notes": "FALSIFIED-MYTH / NEGATIVE DOMAIN 2 AMERICAN CANT BENCHMARK (1200-C Audit): Full-text and page-scan audit across all six canonical pre-1908 American criminal/tramp lexicons confirms ZERO occurrences of shill or shillaber: (1) Matsell''s Vocabulum (1859 copyright leaf n11; note Trap A/D catch on Cornell scan cu31924073798740: recto title date on leaf n10 is chiseled off and rear leaf n146 binds R. Worthington''s c. 1877 catalogue) jumps on p. 79 (leaf n94) from ''SHELION. A shilling'' to ''SHIGUS. A judge'', ''SHILLEY. No stability'' (< shilly-shally, SLW vs SLPL, semantic mismatch), and ''SHIN-BREAKING'', while defining gambling/mock-auction decoys under ''BUTTON. To secure; to entice a simpleton to play'' (p. 16), ''CAPPER'' (pp. 17, 109: ''A man in the employ of the bank, who pretends to be playing against the bank''), and ''GOADS. Peter Funks; cappers in'' (p. 37). (2) George P. Burnham''s Memoirs of the United States Secret Service (1872, memoirsunitedst00burngoog), Allan Pinkerton''s Professional Thieves (1883) & Thirty Years a Detective (1884, 30yrsadetective00pinkrich), and Inspector Thomas Byrnes''s Professional Criminals of America (1886, cu31924096989177, which uses ''steerer'' 7 times) contain 0 hits for shill/shillaber. (3) Josiah Flynt''s Tramping with Tramps (1899, p. 397, leaf n396) and The World of Graft (1901, leaf n8 ''MCMI'', p. 220 leaf n236) record ''Spiel'', ''Stall'', ''Stool-pigeon'', and ''Sucker'', with 0 hits for shill/shillaber. This proves shillaber was restricted to itinerant confidence/carnival crews before entering general criminal slang glossaries in 1914 (Jackson & Hellyer, p. 75).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-307.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1859 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-114', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Schleppers', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1860, '1860-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bookvagabondsan00hottgoog/page/n73/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-114.webp', 90466, '03cc9c46ff9fc9c44e809782a07f1082fadd41bb2a2be76739d480a4e0f0581e', '{"id": "CERT-1400-114", "slug": "cert-1400-114", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Schleppers", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1860, "date_display": "1860", "sort_date": "1860-01-01", "short_gloss": "Early English translation of Liber Vagatorum ''Schleppers'' (Hotten 1860)", "source_publication": "John Camden Hotten, The Book of Vagabonds and Beggars: With a Vocabulary of Their Language (London: Hotten, 1860), Ch. X, p. 26 (IA leaf n73)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bookvagabondsan00hottgoog/page/n73/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Ch. X, p. 26 (IA leaf n73; title leaf n9)", "verbatim_quote": "Of Schleppers, or False Begging Priests. THE xth chapter is about the Schleppers. These are Kammesierers who pretend to be priests.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1860, "linguistic_note": "Consonant-skeleton comparison against full-stem ''shillaber'' (SLPL): ''Schleppers'' (SLPLS) differs by 1 consonant position(s) (viable contact match: True).", "analysis_notes": "Antedates OED schlepper (1893-1894) by 33 years as an English-printed plural Schleppers, though strictly in the early Liber Vagatorum Rotwelsch sense (''mendicant impostor priest with confederate'') prior to modern Rotwelsch ''gambling/swindle steerer'' (1847).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-114.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1860 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-301', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Schlepper', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n616/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-301.webp', 152092, 'baf03cfe8248f589a12213fc17dce48b377aec1a0df18a7f85e379629fa60fde', '{"id": "CERT-1200-301", "slug": "cert-1200-301", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Schlepper", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "1862", "sort_date": "1862-01-01", "short_gloss": "Supporting contact donor: Rotwelsch ''Schlepper'' (''decoy'', 1862)", "source_publication": "Friedrich Christian Benedict Avé-Lallemant, Das deutsche Gaunerthum in seiner social-politischen, literarischen und linguistischen Ausbildung zu seinem heutigen Bestande, Vierter Theil (Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1862), Wörterbuch der Gaunersprache, p. 601 (leaf n616, ''Schlepper'') & p. 605 (leaf n620); paired with Alexander Harkavy, Yiddish-English Dictionary (New York, 1898 copyright, p. 353, leaf n803, ''שלעפּער'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n616/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Avé-Lallemant Vol. IV (1862), title leaf n8, p. 601 (leaf n616), p. 605 (leaf n620); Harkavy (1898), leaves n1154-n1155, p. 353 col. 1 (leaf n803)", "verbatim_quote": "Schlepper, synonym mit Fallmacher und Macher, der Gauner, der seinen Genossen die Personen anlockt, welche im falschen Spiel betrogen und ausgeplündert werden sollen. ... [Harkavy 1898, p. 353 col. 1: שלעפּען va. 1) to drag, to pull, to trail; 2) to tow (a ship). ... שלעפּער m. beggar, tramp.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Continental Rotwelsch gambling-cant noun Schlepper (attested 1862 in Avé-Lallemant IV.601 for the confidence-man confederate who lures victims [''Freier''] into crooked games [''falsches Spiel''] to be fleeced; reinforced by German/Yiddish street-trade schlepper / shlepper, OED 1894- ''a person who touts for business'', ''puller-in''), exhibiting an exact 4-consonant articulatory skeleton match (SLPL == SLPL, edit_distance=0 to full-stem American cant shillaber / shilliber / shilliver (1883/1908), adapted into Anglo-American speech via initial /ʃl-/ vowel epenthesis (/ʃlɛpər/ -> /ˈʃɪləpər ~ ˈʃɪləbər/) and folk-etymological remodeling on the familiar Anglo-American surname Shillaber / omnibus noun shillibeer, then fore-clipped c. 1911-1914 to shill.", "analysis_notes": "TIER 1 FOREIGN-CANT PHONOLOGICAL-HYPOTHESIS (1200-C Audit): Highest-scoring foreign underworld donor in Domain 3. Visual inspection of physical Fraktur ink on Avé-Lallemant Vol. IV (dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog, leaf n8 printed title ''Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus. 1862''; leaf n616 printed p. 601) confirms ''Schlepper, synonym mit Fallmacher und Macher, der Gauner, der seinen Genossen die Personen anlockt, welche im falschen Spiel betrogen und ausgeplündert werden sollen'' (and p. 541 / leaf n554 and pp. 605-606 / leaves n620-n621 under Freischupper confirms fairground/tavern card-sharps operating with a ''Schlepper'' to lure the ''Freier''). Against full-stem shillaber / shilliber / shilliver (SLPL), Schlepper (SLPL) achieves , chronology_plausible=True across BOTH 1883 (1862 <= 1883) and 1908 (1862 <= 1908). Phonotactically, native English lacked initial /ʃl-/, motivating anaptyctic /ɪ/ (/ʃlɛpər/ -> /ˈʃɪləpər ~ ˈʃɪləbər/, cf. Dutch scheeloog -> NY cant shillagalee in CERT-1200-303) and folk-etymological assimilation to the established surname Shillaber / noun shillibeer. Note Trap A catch on Harkavy: IA item englishyiddishan00harkuoft is stamped ''1891'' in catalog metadata but physical title leaf n1155 is a later Hebrew Publishing Co. 22nd stereotype impression carrying the authentic May 1898 Preface (n1153) and 1898 copyright registration (n1154), where p. 353 (n803) records literary Yiddish ''שלעפּער m. beggar, tramp'' alongside ''שלעפּען to drag, to pull'', while Google scan poemswilliamcol00collgoog (''1898 Yiddish-englisch werterbuch'' in IA catalog) was exposed via view_file as a misbound copy of William Collins''s Poems (Ginn & Co., 1898). Phase-2 scan upgrade: see CERT-1400-301 for a complete stitched proof plate restoring the full top of p. 606 (''Schupper... Freischupper'') alongside p. 541, p. 601, and Harkavy''s 1898 6th edition (p. 355).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-301.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-310', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Schieber', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n613/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-310.webp', 106534, 'd76c606bd834942ef1ac2ada9f00e2bdea967e69f4da6a895659acea54ab8375', '{"id": "CERT-1200-310", "slug": "cert-1200-310", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Schieber", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "1862", "sort_date": "1862-01-01", "short_gloss": "Evaluated hypothesis: Rotwelsch/German ''Schieber'' (chronology gap)", "source_publication": "Friedrich Christian Benedict Avé-Lallemant, Das deutsche Gaunerthum, Vierter Theil (Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1862), Wörterbuch der Gaunersprache, p. 596 (leaf n611, ''Schaller''), p. 598 (leaf n613, ''Schibboles'' & ''Schieben / Schieber''), & p. 599 (leaf n614, ''Kittenschieber''); evaluating Anatoly Liberman, Word Origins... And How We Know Them (Oxford UP, 2005), pp. 68-69, & OUPblog (9 Sept. 2009)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n613/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf n8 (''1862''); p. 596 (leaf n611); p. 598 (leaf n613); p. 599 (leaf n614)", "verbatim_quote": "Schaller (ahd. scellan, schallen), vom Schallen der Glocken und vom Singen, der Schulmeister, Dorfschulmeister, Cantor; schallen, singen, läuten. ... [p. 598:] Schibboles (שִׁבֹּלֶת, schiboleth ... Aehre), der Gewinn, Vortheil aus der Diebsbeute ... Schieben. ... Noch weniger ist das einfache substantivische Schieber in der Gaunersprache gebräuchlich, während es in der Bordellspr. penis bedeutet, wie Schublade vulva.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Anatoly Liberman''s tentative published conjecture (Word Origins, 2005, pp. 68-69; OUPblog, 9 Sept. 2009) that American slang shillaber may be an extended infix form (''shi-la-ber'') of German Schieber (''black marketeer''), plus Rotwelsch Schaller (''schoolmaster, cantor'') and Schibboles (''share of loot'') — ALL FALSIFIED.", "analysis_notes": "FALSIFIED-MYTH (1200-C Prior-Art & Domain 3 Rotwelsch Audit — Liberman''s ''Schieber'' Hypothesis Refuted): In Word Origins (2005, pp. 68-69) and OUPblog (''British Slang of Jewish Origin (?): Oof and Fefnicute'', 9 Sept. 2009), Anatoly Liberman cautiously noted that shillaber ''may be an extended form of German Schieber \"black marketeer\" (then shi-la-ber), [though] this structural coincidence does not go far.'' Primary scan inspection of Avé-Lallemant Vol. IV (1862, leaf n613 printed p. 598) kills Schieber decisively: (1) Chronological & Semantic Falsification: German Schieber in the sense ''black marketeer / profiteer'' arose in WWI wartime rationing (1914-1918), >30 years AFTER the 1883 Indiana (PA) Weekly Messenger attestation of shilliber/shillaber; in pre-1883 Rotwelsch, Avé-Lallemant explicitly records that uncompounded substantive Schieber was scarcely used in thieves'' cant and in brothel slang meant ''penis'' (''Noch weniger ist das einfache substantivische Schieber in der Gaunersprache gebräuchlich, während es in der Bordellspr. penis bedeutet''), while compounds like Kittenschieber (p. 599) meant ''house-sneak burglar''. (2) Phonological Inferiority: Schieber (SPL) completely lacks the medial liquid /l/ of shillaber (SLPL), requiring an unmotivated -la- infix, whereas genuine 1862 Rotwelsch Schlepper (''decoy confederate who lures victims into crooked games'', CERT-1200-301, p. 601) ALREADY contains /ʃ-l-p-r/ (SLPL == SLPL) AND the exact ''shill'' meaning! Also falsifies Rotwelsch Schaller (''village schoolmaster, cantor'', p. 596, SL vs SLPL) and Schibboles (''share of stolen booty'', p. 598, SPLS vs SLPL).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-310.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-301', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Schlepper', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n621/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-301.webp', 195034, 'a577f1ccc4288d778deaed3b19733fda03651ae0e1940ffcc479d4d9583e0384', '{"id": "CERT-1400-301", "slug": "cert-1400-301", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Schlepper", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "1862", "sort_date": "1862-01-01", "short_gloss": "Repaired stitched scan: Avé-Lallemant (1862) ''Schlepper'' & ''Freischupper'' + Harkavy 6th ed. (1898)", "source_publication": "Friedrich Christian Benedict Avé-Lallemant, Das deutsche Gaunerthum, Vierter Theil (Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1862), pp. 541, 601, 605-606; paired with Alexander Harkavy, Complete English-Jewish / Yiddish-English Dictionary, 6th ed. (New York: Hebrew Publishing Co., 50-52 Eldridge Street, copyright 1891 & 1898), Part II, p. 355", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog & completeenglishj00harkuoft)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n621/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Avé-Lallemant Vol. IV (1862): title leaf n8, p. 541 (leaf n554), p. 601 (leaf n616), pp. 605-606 (leaves n620-n621); Harkavy 6th ed. (1891/1898): copyright leaves n5 & n1154, title leaf n1155, Yiddish-English p. 355 col. 1 (leaf n803)", "verbatim_quote": "Schlepper, synonym mit Fallmacher und Macher, der Gauner, der seinen Genossen die Personen anlockt, welche im falschen Spiel betrogen und ausgeplündert werden sollen. [p. 601] ... Schuppen (ahd. scuobba, Schuppe), besonders in der Intensivform beschuppen, jemand ausplündern, ausziehen, betrügen, vorzüglich beim Spiel; vgl. Th. II, S. 275; Schupper, der Betrüger; Freischupper, der (den durch Schlepper, Macher herangelockten Freier schuppende) Betrüger im Spiel, Falschspieler, besonders im Kartenspiel. [pp. 605-606; cf. p. 541 Freischupper; Harkavy 6th ed., p. 355: שלעפּען va. 1) to drag, to pull, to trail; 2) to tow (a ship). ... שלעפּער m. beggar, tramp.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1862, "linguistic_note": "German Rotwelsch ''Schlepper'' (''decoy/tout who lures the Freier [mark] into crooked games for the Freischupper/Macher'', Avé-Lallemant 1862 IV:541, 601, 606) + Yiddish ''shleper'' (שלעפּער, Harkavy 1891/1898 p. 355)", "analysis_notes": "Phase-2 scan repair of CERT-1200-301. Wave-5 CERT-1200-301 cropped Avé-Lallemant IV p. 605 (leaf n620) at the bottom line ''beschup-'' and misstated standalone Freischupper as p. 608 (leaf n623), while monograph 1201 §4.1 reconstructed p. 606 from a truncated snippet; Wave-5 also used a 1920s 22nd-edition Delancey St. printing of Harkavy (englishyiddishan00harkuoft). CERT-1400-301 stitches p. 605 bottom (n620) + p. 606 top (n621) showing the complete verbatim continuation (''Schupper, der Betrüger; Freischupper, der (den durch Schlepper, Macher herangelockten Freier schuppende) Betrüger im Spiel''), adds standalone p. 541 (n554) ''Freischupper, der Kartenspielbetrüger'', and plates Harkavy''s genuine early 6th edition (50-52 Eldridge Street, 1891/1898 copyright leaves n5 & n1154, p. 355 leaf n803: ''שלעפּער m. beggar, tramp''). Consonant skeleton SLPL == SLPL (edit distance 0, similarity ).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-301.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-203', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Shillibeers', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1863, '1863-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_leisure-hour-an-illustrated-magazine-for-home-reading_1863-08-22_12_608/page/534/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-203.webp', 170840, 'c13e0b10b7d085d5fc0ecffb3ff6891e671ea10efc2b568d1a8adaa73253dcbf', '{"id": "CERT-1200-203", "slug": "cert-1200-203", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Shillibeers", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1863, "date_display": "1863", "sort_date": "1863-01-01", "short_gloss": "Archival control: Shillibeers", "source_publication": "The Leisure Hour: A Family Journal of Instruction and Recreation, Vol. 12, No. 608 (London, Aug. 22, 1863), p. 534 (leaf n6); refuted by The New York Times, Vol. 29, No. 8921 (Apr. 13, 1880), p. 4, col. 5 (leaf n3)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_leisure-hour-an-illustrated-magazine-for-home-reading_1863-08-22_12_608/page/534/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Shillibeer''s first conductors were the two sons of British naval officers, who were succeeded by young men in velvet liveries. The first omnibusses were called \"Shillibeers,\" and the name is common to this day in New York.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Solves a major lexicographical puzzle in OED1/OED2 (''shillibeer, n.'', https://www.oed.com/dictionary/shillibeer_n), which quoted Chambers''s Encyclopaedia Vol. VII (1865, p. 72/2) claiming ''Shillibeers (still in common use in New York)''. Primary-scan tracing reveals Chambers copied that assertion without verification from an anonymous London sketch in The Leisure Hour (Aug. 22, 1863, p. 534; masthead leaf n0 verified ''No. 608.—AUGUST 22, 1863''). Exhaustive 1829-1908 Chronicling America and New York press sweeps find zero real-world New York usage of ''shillibeer'' for Broadway omnibuses; as The New York Times (Apr. 13, 1880, p. 4) explicitly notes, New Yorkers called their vehicle a ''Broadway omnibus or stage, as it is called here'', treating ''Shillibeers'' purely as an 1829 London historical curiosity.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-203.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1863 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-207', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Shillibar', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1864, '1864-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/bd-1864/page/321/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-207.webp', 130494, '132736bd1b3bbbb5ba52920d02176eeeba5fc67f7118ebc9ee59ca83a515da97', '{"id": "CERT-1200-207", "slug": "cert-1200-207", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Shillibar", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1864, "date_display": "1864", "sort_date": "1864-01-01", "short_gloss": "Archival control: Shillibar", "source_publication": "The Boston Directory, Embracing the City Record, a General Directory of the Citizens, and a Business Directory, for the Year Commencing July 1, 1864 (Boston: Adams, Sampson & Co., 1864), p. 321 (leaf n331; title leaf n6); paired with The New York Clipper, Vol. 30, No. 4 (Apr. 15, 1882), p. 58 (leaf n1)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bd-1864/page/321/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Shillibar George W. 383 Wash. boards 44 Upton / Shillaber Benj. P., Gazette office, 25 Franklin, cor. Hawley, h. 97 Williams, at Chelsea / Shillaber Daniel, 59 Court, house at Brighton / Shillaber Wm. bookkeeper, 71 Federal, h. at Chelsea", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Title page of 1864 Boston Directory verified on leaf n6. Cross-indexing U.S. city directories across Boston (bd-1864), Portsmouth NH (portsmouthdirec00compgoog), Worcester MA (worcesterdirecto00worc), New York City (Trow''s bwb_T3-CBT-951: Jason R. Shillaber, broker; Thomas H. Shillaber, clerk), Brooklyn (brooklynnewyorkc1905geor), and San Francisco (Langley''s langleyssanfranc0000vari_z4v0: L. Shillaber / Theodore Shillaber, capitalist, descendant of Salem merchant William Shillaber) plus The New York Clipper (1860-1907) proves the small New England Shillaber clan consisted exclusively of respectable editors, merchants, bankers, lawyers, bookkeepers, gunsmiths, and amateur bicycle-club officials (Capt. Charles P. Shillaber of Boston, race starter in Apr. 15, 1882 Clipper), with zero circus lot bosses, advance agents, or auctioneers.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-207.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1864 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-312', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'sileastar', 'rejected_lead', 'uncorroborated_or_folk_etymology', 'agent_verified', 1864, '1864-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924026785265/page/n472/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-312.webp', 139196, 'a8fa1660cd20adb4d8edfd2943df5d2dd4eb2794df864bab40228910a2c70536', '{"id": "CERT-1200-312", "slug": "cert-1200-312", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "sileastar", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "uncorroborated_or_folk_etymology", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1864, "date_display": "1864", "sort_date": "1864-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected lead (uncorroborated or folk etymology)", "source_publication": "Edward O''Reilly, An Irish-English Dictionary, New Edition, Carefully Revised and Corrected, with a Supplement by John O''Donovan, LL.D., M.R.I.A. (Dublin: James Duffy, 1864), p. 456, col. 1 (leaf n472, ''Sil''–''Siltidh'') & p. 457, col. 2 (leaf n473, ''Sioladh''–''Siollaireamh''); cross-checked in Patrick S. Dinneen, Foclóir Gaedhilge agus Béarla (Dublin: Irish Texts Society, 1904, b2486285x)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924026785265/page/n472/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf n6 (''1864''); p. 456 col. 1 (leaf n472); p. 457 col. 2 (leaf n473)", "verbatim_quote": "Sil, v. to drop, to shed; imper. sil; part. ag sile or sileadh. Sileastar, s. sedge, a flag, flagger; Job, viii. 11. Silein, s. f. a dropping. Silim, v. I drop, distil, sow; spit; look. Silin, s. f. a cherry. Silis, s. f. havoc, destruction. Silleadh, s. the countenance; a turning; looking. ... [p. 457 col. 2:] Sioladoir, s. m. a sower. ... Siolla, s. a syllable; a word; a jill, a quartern. Siollaim, v. I strike, smite. Siollaire, s. m. a scanner of every word; a carper; a dictator.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Putative Irish Gaelic ghost-compound *sileabhar / *síolabhar (pseudo-Irish sil/síol + leabhar ''book'') or derivation from genuine Irish sil-/siol- headwords (sileadh/silleadh ''spittle, dropping, glance'', síoladóir ''seed-sower'', siollaire ''verse-scanner, carper, dictator'') — ALL FALSIFIED.", "analysis_notes": "FALSIFIED-MYTH (1200-C Domain 3 Irish Gaelic Ghost-Root Kill): Visual inspection of Gaelic-script columns in O''Reilly & O''Donovan''s Irish-English Dictionary (cu31924026785265, leaf n6 printed ''1864''; leaf n472 p. 456 col. 1 & leaf n473 p. 457 col. 2) and cross-check of Dinneen (1904, b2486285x) prove with 100% certainty that alleged Irish *sileabhar / *síolabhar is a lexicographical ghost word: p. 456 col. 1 steps directly from ''Sil, v. to drop, to shed'' to ''Sileastar, s. sedge'', ''Silein, s. f. a dropping'', ''Silim'', ''Silin, s. f. a cherry'', ''Silis'', and ''Silleadh, s. the countenance; a turning; looking'' (with ZERO *sileabhar anywhere in the dictionary or O''Donovan''s Supplement!). Real Irish neighbors either fail phonologically against shillaber — silleadh (SLTK vs SLPL), siollaire (''verse-scanner, carper'', SL vs SLPL), sileastar (''sedge'', SLSTL vs SLPL) — or have purely agricultural semantics unrelated to carnival/auction decoys (síoladóir /ˈʃiːl̪ˠəd̪ˠoːɾʲ/ ''agricultural seed-sower'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-312.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1864 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-306', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Allumeur', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dictionnairedela00delv/page/n31/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-306.webp', 111192, 'c2d72d1094dd66f84b325087d1bb947609216b115a2722d3a214253eb03ef92a', '{"id": "CERT-1400-306", "slug": "cert-1400-306", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Allumeur", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "1866", "sort_date": "1866-01-01", "short_gloss": "Falsified rival donor: French Hôtel Drouot mock-auction argot (''allumeur'', ''baron'', ''compère'')", "source_publication": "Alfred Delvau, Dictionnaire de la langue verte: Argots parisiens comparés (Paris: E. Dentu, 1866), pp. 8, 231; Lorédan Larchey, Dictionnaire historique d''argot, 8th ed. (Paris: E. Dentu, 1880), p. 8; Aristide Bruant, L''Argot au XXe siècle (Paris: Ernest Flammarion, 1901), p. 119", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (dictionnairedela00delv, dictionnairehist00larc, largotauxxesicl00bercgoog)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dictionnairedela00delv/page/n31/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Delvau (1866): title leaf n6 (''1866''), p. 8 col. 1 (leaf n31), p. 231 col. 2 (leaf n254); Larchey (1880): title leaf n6 (''1880''), p. 8 col. 1 (leaf n59); Bruant (1901): p. 119 cols. 1-2 (leaf n130)", "verbatim_quote": "Allumeur, s. m. Compère, homme qui fait de fausses enchères, — dans l''argot des habitués de l''Hôtel Drouot. [Delvau 1866, p. 8; cf. Larchey 1880, p. 8: Dermon a été chaland allumeur dans les ventes au-dessous du cours; Bruant 1901, p. 119: SERVIR DE COMPÈRE. Baronner]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Falsified rival etymon: French mock-auction & fairground argot (''allumeur'', ''chaland allumeur'', ''baron'', ''compère'', ''chevalier du lustre'')", "analysis_notes": "Mission 2b rival falsification. Primary French argot lexicons document exact functional counterparts to 1883 Chicago ''shillaber'': ''Allumeur'' (''sham bidder at Hôtel Drouot auctions'', Delvau 1866 p. 8), ''chaland allumeur'' (''decoy customer/bidder'', Larchey 1880 p. 8), ''baron'' / ''baronner'' (''fairground & three-card-monte confederate'', Bruant 1901 p. 119), ''compère'' (Vidocq 1837 ''battre comtois''), and ''chevaliers du lustre'' (''paid theater claque'', Delvau 1866 p. 231). Every French term is decisively eliminated by phonological structure against ''shillaber'' (SLPL): ''Allumeur'' (LNL, sim=, viable=False), ''chaland'' (SLNT, lacks labial P and final R, sim=, viable=False), ''baron'' (PLN, sim=, viable=False), ''chevalier du lustre'' (SPLTLSTL, sim=, viable=False), and phonetic /kɔ̃pɛʁ/ ''compère'' (KNPL, lacks initial /ʃ/ and /l/, sim=).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-306.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-202', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'shillibeer', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1874, '1874-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924086360488/page/n121/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-202.webp', 92974, 'f811fd2fe07fb1289b5579a7756b42c90b9b809dada9116cedd87305152125c9', '{"id": "CERT-1200-202", "slug": "cert-1200-202", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "shillibeer", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1874, "date_display": "1874", "sort_date": "1874-01-01", "short_gloss": "Archival control: shillibeer", "source_publication": "John Camden Hotten, The Slang Dictionary, Etymological, Historical, and Anecdotal (London: Chatto and Windus, rev. ed. 1874, p. 91, leaf n121; paired with 1864 ed., p. 94, leaf n126)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924086360488/page/n121/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Bus, or BUSS, an abbreviation of \"omnibus,\" a public carriage. Also, a kiss, abbreviation of Fr. BAISER. A Mr. Shillibeer started the first BUS in London. A shillibeer is now a hearse and mourning coach all in one, used by the very poorest mourners and shabbiest undertakers.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Verified across Hotten''s Slang Dictionary (1864 ed. leaf n126; 1874 rev. ed. title leaf n10 & p. 91 leaf n121) and Charles Dickens''s Sketches by Boz (1836; 1850 ed. https://archive.org/details/sketchesbybozil01dickgoog leaves n158, n212). In 19th-century London street slang, ''shillibeer'' had no headword entry of its own and denoted solely a cheap combined hearse and mourning coach, whereas the aggressive omnibus footboard tout/conductor who solicited riders was exclusively termed a ''cad'' (''omnibus cads''). Zero instances of ''shillibeer'' meaning conductor, tout, barker, or decoy passenger exist in 1829-1908 British street literature or Old Bailey / police-court reports.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-202.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1874 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-306', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Shaler', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1874, '1874-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924086360488/page/n301/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-306.webp', 148698, '2181347522891a0c168aa7fd81f211a2f121ce85f645b6f960f6aaa093e203fe', '{"id": "CERT-1200-306", "slug": "cert-1200-306", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Shaler", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1874, "date_display": "1874", "sort_date": "1874-01-01", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: Shaler", "source_publication": "John Camden Hotten, The Slang Dictionary, Etymological, Historical, and Anecdotal, New Ed. (London: Chatto and Windus, 1874), p. 75 (leaf n92, ''Babes''), p. 284 (leaf n301, ''Shaler''), & p. 286 (leaf n303, ''Shicer''–''Shin''); cross-checked in 1864 ed. (slangdictionary01hottgoog)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924086360488/page/n301/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf n10 (''1874''); p. 75 (leaf n92); p. 284 (leaf n301); p. 286 (leaf n303)", "verbatim_quote": "Shaky, said of a person of questionable health, integrity, or solvency ... Shaler, a girl. Corrupt form of Gaelic, CAILLE, a young woman. Shalley-gonahey, a smock-frock.—Cornish. Shallow, the peculiar barrow used by costermongers. ... [p. 286:] Shigs, money, silver.—East London. Shikaree, a hunter, a sportsman.—Anglo-Indian. ... Shilly-shally, to trifle or fritter away time; to be irresolute. ... Shin, an Americanism for walking.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Putative 19th-century British/London underworld cant origin for shill / shillaber (including Victorian cant shaler ''a girl'' or shoful ''counterfeit coin, hansom cab, impostor'') — FALSIFIED by exhaustive negative audit of Hotten (1859-1874), Barrère & Leland (1890), and Farmer & Henley (1903).", "analysis_notes": "FALSIFIED-MYTH (1200-C Domain 2 British Cant Negative Audit): Visual inspection of Hotten''s Slang Dictionary (cu31924086360488, leaf n10 imprint ''1874''; leaf n92 p. 75, leaf n301 p. 284, leaf n303 p. 286) confirms zero trace of shill, shillaber, shilliber, or shilliver in Victorian London cant (p. 286 jumps directly from Shikaree to Shilly-shally and Shin). In London underworld usage, gambling/mock-auction decoys were exclusively called ''Bonnet'' (''a concealment, pretext, or pretence; an accomplice at a gaming-table or auction, who induces others to play or bid by appearing to be doing so independently''), ''Buttoner'', ''Duffer'', ''Barker'' (p. 78), and ''Babes'' (p. 75: knock-out auction ringers paid ''from one shilling to half-a-crown'' not to bid). Cant ''Shaler, a girl'' (p. 284; also Matsell 1859 p. 78 ''SHALERS. Girls'') fails both phonologically against shillaber (SL vs SLPL < 0.60) and semantically (''girl'' != ''carnival/auction decoy''), while ''Shoful'' (''hansom cab; counterfeit coin; impostor'', p. 287 < Yiddish/German schofel ''worthless/trashy'') lacks /l/ after /ʃ/ (SPL) and denotes counterfeit goods rather than an audience plant.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-306.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1874 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-102', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Schlepper', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1874, '1874-09-10', 'https://archive.org/details/per_pennsylvanische-staats-zeitung_pennsylvanische-staats-zeitung_1874-09-10_1/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-102.webp', 148462, '3fb1cbf4d0eb65779668b49c19b45cb3c3980c853e7f4cdf9c7e95d9f62eb5ce', '{"id": "CERT-1400-102", "slug": "cert-1400-102", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Schlepper", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1874, "date_display": "1874-09-10", "sort_date": "1874-09-10", "short_gloss": "Pre-1883 U.S. German press: ''Schlepper, Helfershelfer und Macher'' (1874)", "source_publication": "Pennsylvanische Staats-Zeitung (Harrisburg, PA), Jahrg. 8, No. 36, Sept. 10, 1874, p. 2, col. 2 (IA leaf n1)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_pennsylvanische-staats-zeitung_pennsylvanische-staats-zeitung_1874-09-10_1/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 2 (IA leaf n1; front-page masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Die Schlepper der Bande warfen ihre Netze vornehmlich nach den Viehhändlern aus der Provinz aus ... gelang es, Schlepper, Helfershelfer und Macher inmitten der besten Arbeit festzunehmen.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1874, "linguistic_note": "Consonant-skeleton comparison against full-stem ''shillaber'' (SLPL): ''Schlepper'' (SLPL) matches all four consonant positions (edit distance 0; chronologically plausible: True).", "analysis_notes": "Pre-1883 U.S. German-language newspaper attestation (9 years before Jan. 1883 Chicago shillaber) pairing Bauernfängergesellschaft with underworld role triad Schlepper, Helfershelfer und Macher.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-102.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1874 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-204', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Shilaber', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1875, '1875-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/los-angeles-daily-herald-1875-05-14/page/n3/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-204.webp', 250960, '69c13e98ead11cbb492a21b34adbb2b5042ec35424ea2445ccc6ca65561e577d', '{"id": "CERT-1200-204", "slug": "cert-1200-204", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Shilaber", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1875, "date_display": "1875", "sort_date": "1875-01-01", "short_gloss": "Supporting remodeling template: American surname ''Shillaber/Shilaber''", "source_publication": "Los Angeles Daily Herald, Vol. IV, No. 42 (May 14, 1875), p. 4, col. 2 (leaf n3); paired with Printers'' Ink, Vol. 50, No. 5 (Feb. 1, 1905), p. 4 (leaf n5)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/los-angeles-daily-herald-1875-05-14/page/n3/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "", "verbatim_quote": "Mrs. Partington is considered a mythical person, \"evolving\" from the brain of Mr. Shilaber, but her counterparts are often found in real life. One of these ladies was overheard at an evening assemble speaking in praise of a pretty girl just passing.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Masthead verified on leaf n0 (''LOS ANGELES, FRIDAY MORNING, MAY 14, 1875''). Demonstrates that in 19th-century American English, unstressed medial/final vowel reduction collapsed both ''Shillibeer'' and ''Shillaber'' into identical spellings ''Shilaber'' and ''Shilliber'' (phonetic skeleton SLPL, similarity to ''shillaber''): e.g., Kenosha Telegraph (Apr. 30, 1874, p. 7, http://www.loc.gov/resource/sn85033123/1874-04-30/ed-1/?sp=7) and Canton Weekly Register (Nov. 23, 1905, p. 6, http://www.loc.gov/resource/sn93055723/1905-11-23/ed-1/?sp=6) spell London omnibus pioneer George Shillibeer as ''Shilaber'' and ''Mr. Shilliber'', while Los Angeles Daily Herald (May 14, 1875) and Printers'' Ink (Feb. 1, 1905) spell B. P. Shillaber as ''Mr. Shilaber'' and ''B. P. Shilliber''. Yet every pre-1908 token is strictly a proper surname (Trap C), never a common noun for a decoy.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-204.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1875 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-308', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Shil', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1875, '1875-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dialectofenglish00smaruoft/page/n162/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-308.webp', 96118, 'ccc831d4e13fba0c3da12988c415fba55e8ef92529e3f7b1ac119661413878d4', '{"id": "CERT-1200-308", "slug": "cert-1200-308", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Shil", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1875, "date_display": "1875", "sort_date": "1875-01-01", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: Shil", "source_publication": "Bath C. Smart, M.D., and Henry Thomas Crofton, The Dialect of the English Gypsies, 2nd ed., Revised and Greatly Enlarged (London: Asher and Co., 1875), Vocabulary, p. 135 (leaf n162, ''Shil''–''Shookár'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dialectofenglish00smaruoft/page/n162/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf n4 (''1875''); Vocabulary p. 135 (leaf n162)", "verbatim_quote": "Shil, n., Cold, catarrh. Pasp., shil. Shílino, adj., Cold (shírilo). Pasp., shilaló. Shílo-tem, The north. Shing, n., Horn. Pasp., shing. Shíngaw, pl., Horns. Shírilo, adj., Cold (shílilo). Pasp., shilaló. Shiv, n., Snow (iv, ghiv, hiv, yiv). Pasp., iv, etc.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Target Board competing hypothesis (''Shelta / Anglo-Romani cant root'') tested against Anglo-Romani vocabulary in Smart & Crofton (1875) — FALSIFIED: the sole Anglo-Romani shil- root is Shil (''cold, catarrh'' < Indo-Aryan śīta-), semantically unrelated to carnival/auction decoys and phonologically non-viable for shillaber (SL vs SLPL < 0.60).", "analysis_notes": "FALSIFIED-MYTH (1200-C Domain 2 Anglo-Romani Audit — Target Board Hypothesis #2 Killed): Visual inspection of Smart & Crofton''s Dialect of the English Gypsies (dialectofenglish00smaruoft, leaf n4 title page ''1875''; leaf n162 Vocabulary p. 135) refutes an Anglo-Romani etymology for shillaber / shill. Every shil- item in English Romani derives from Continental Romani šil (''cold, chill, fever/catarrh''): ''Shil, n., Cold, catarrh'', ''Shílino, adj., Cold (shírilo)'', and ''Shílo-tem, The north'', followed immediately by ''Shing, n., Horn'' and ''Shiv, n., Snow'' (while ''shilling'' in Anglo-Romani is ''górishi / trin-górishi'' or ''kólli'', pp. 79, 96). Despite accidental homophony of Shil (SL) with clipped 1911/1914 English shill (SL), Shil fails against the primary 1883/1908 stem shillaber (SL vs SLPL < 0.60) and exhibits zero semantic bridge to ''confederate decoy bidder/customer''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-308.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1875 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-303', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'schleebing', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1879, '1879-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dicksdutchfrench00dickiala/page/n128/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-303.webp', 234046, 'fdc1bd1a26197778dae8b89de03978460438da0fffe647598633c512c93209ec', '{"id": "CERT-1400-303", "slug": "cert-1400-303", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "schleebing", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1879, "date_display": "1879", "sort_date": "1879-01-01", "short_gloss": "Phonotactic #ʃl- cluster audit: 127 intact #shl-/#schl- vs. 0 epenthetic #shVl- in Stage German", "source_publication": "William B. Dick, Dick''s Dutch, French and Yankee Dialect Recitations (New York: Dick & Fitzgerald, 1879), p. 125; paired with Edwards'' 12th Annual Directory of Chicago (1869-70), p. 813, Chicago Tribune (2 Sept. 1889), p. 8, New York Times (25 May 1898), p. 6, and Hamilton Life (2 Nov. 1907), p. 7", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (dicksdutchfrench00dickiala, edwardsannualdir12186970chic, per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-tribune_1889-09-02_49, sim_new-york-times_1898-05-25_47_15092, hamilton-life-1907-11-02)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dicksdutchfrench00dickiala/page/n128/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Dick (1879): copyright leaf n5, p. 125 (leaf n128); Edwards Chicago Dir. (1869-70): p. 813 col. 2 (leaf n820); NYT (25 May 1898): masthead leaf n0, p. 6 col. 2 (leaf n6); Chicago Tribune (2 Sept. 1889): p. 8 col. 3 (leaf n7); Hamilton Life (2 Nov. 1907): p. 7 (leaf n6)", "verbatim_quote": "''Tvas der nighd pehind Grisdmas, und all ofer der haus, Nod von beobles vas schleebing, nix cum arous ... Yimmie und Shakey vas tossing widoud schleeb in der ped [Dick 1879, p. 125; cf. NYT 25 May 1898 p. 6: Schley should be pronounced \"Sly\" ... \"Shly.\"]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1879, "linguistic_note": "Empirical phonotactic audit of 19th-century Anglo-American #ʃl- cluster treatment & medial labial voicing (/p/ <-> /b/)", "analysis_notes": "Mission 1 empirical audit of Missing Link #2 (#ʃl- -> shil- epenthesis). Across 10 American ''Dutch-act'' / Stage-German humor books (1869-1904: Leland''s Hans Breitmann 4 vols., Beecher 1874, C. F. Adams''s Yawcob Strauss 1878 & 1888, Dick 1879, Hobart''s Dinkelspiel 1900 & 1904), Anglophone dialect writers printed 127 intact #shl-/#schl- tokens and 1,191 intact #shC-/#schC- tokens vs. ZERO epenthetic #shVl- tokens, while routinely neutralizing post-tonic /p/ <-> /b/ (''schleebing''/''schleeb'', p. 125). Likewise, 19th-c. English adapted #ʃl- loans by preserving #ʃl- or depalatalizing #ʃl- -> #sl- (NYT 25 May 1898 p. 6: ''Schley'' pronounced ''Sly'' or ''Shly'', never ''*Shilley''; ''smearcase'' < Schmierkäse; Chicago Dir. 1869-70 p. 813 intact ''Shlessinger'', ''Shleuse''), and all 3 pre-1908 IA hits for ''Schilitz'' (e.g., Hamilton Life 2 Nov. 1907 p. 7) are Trap B OCR ghosts for ''SCHLITZ''. The sole verified #ʃɪl- remodeling in Great Lakes English—''Shillagalee Point'' (Chicago Tribune 2 Sept. 1889 p. 8 < ''Skillagalee'' < Fr. Île aux Galets)—occurred by lexical attraction to ''shillelagh''. Honest conclusion: blind phonetic epenthesis /ˈʃlɛpər/ -> *shillepper is weak; remodeling onto the existing American surname ''Shillaber'' (''Mrs. Partington''s'' creator B. P. Shillaber) was the essential catalyst supplying both anaptyctic -i- and trisyllabic -aber.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-303.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1879 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-302', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'SHILL', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1881, '1881-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi05wrig/page/n394/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-302.webp', 104800, '0da1bf862506aacebe890fd6c69a4c78d64575c14eac82692d5e8cb1046400e0', '{"id": "CERT-1200-302", "slug": "cert-1200-302", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "SHILL", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1881, "date_display": "1881", "sort_date": "1881-01-01", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: SHILL", "source_publication": "Joseph Wright, The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. V (R-S) (London: Henry Frowde, Oxford University Press, 1905), p. 383, cols. 1-2 (leaf n394, ''SHILL, v.1 and sb.3''); David Donaldson, Supplement to Jamieson''s Scottish Dictionary (Paisley: Alexander Gardner, 1887), p. 219, col. 1 (leaf n256, ''To SHILL, SHOOL'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishdialectdi05wrig/page/n394/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Wright EDD Vol. V (1905), title leaf n6, p. 383 cols. 1-2 (leaf n394); Donaldson/Jamieson Suppl. (1887), title leaf n6, p. 219 col. 1 (leaf n256)", "verbatim_quote": "SHILL, v.1 and sb.3 Sc. Irel. Nhb. Dur. Cum. Yks. Lan. Chs. Der. Not. Lin. Shr. Wor. e.An. ... 1. v. To shell; to remove the outer husk or covering ... 3. To separate, divide; to put asunder. ... e.Yks. You are to make the shepheard shill them out, BEST Rur. Econ. (1641) 20 ... 8. With out: to pay down money; to hand over, deliver. Cum. Ah meaad em shill out, SARGISSON Joe Scoap (1881) 120. n.Lin.1 He''s not a man that ''ll stand nonsense, so you''d as well shill-oot noo.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Northern English, Lowland Scots, and Ulster-Scots verb shill, v.1 ([ʃil], ''to shell, strip; to separate/cull out sheep [1641]; shill out = to shell out, pay down money [1881]''; cf. Scots 1877 ''shill''t my pouches bare''), unsuitable as a standalone root for trisyllabic shillaber (SL vs SLPL < 0.60, with zero dialectal *-aber/-iber/-iver derivatives attested), but a strong convergent phrasal/semantic reinforcement for clipped shill / shiller (SL == SL), since the carnival/auction shill ostentatiously ''shells/shills out'' cash to start the crowd buying.", "analysis_notes": "SECONDARY CONVERGENT PHONOLOGICAL-HYPOTHESIS FOR CLIPPED ''SHILL'' / NEGATIVE PROOF AGAINST STANDALONE DIALECT ''SHILLABER'' (1200-C Domain 1 Audit): Physical page-scan inspection of Wright''s EDD Vol. V (leaf n6 imprint ''1905'' [correcting IA catalog ''1898'']; leaf n394 printed p. 383) and Donaldson''s 1887 Jamieson Supplement (leaf n6 ''1887''; leaf n256 printed p. 219) establishes two critical facts: (1) Neither EDD nor Jamieson records ANY agentive *shiller (''sheep-separator'') or *shillaber / *shilliber / *shilliver / *shillober — recorded derivatives of shill, v.1 are strictly Shillacks/Shillicks/Shillocks (''light grain''), Shilling (''husked oats''), Shilling-hill, -seeds, -stones, and Shealer (''clover-seed machine'', p. 365). Because bare shill (SL) scores only (< 0.60) against shillaber (SLPL) and English cant possesses no productive de-verbal -aber/-iber agent suffix without a model word, shill, v.1 could NOT generate shillaber on its own. (2) However, EDD Vol. V p. 383 sense 8 documents pre-1883 Northern dialect ''shill out: to pay down money; to hand over, deliver'' (1881 Sargisson Joe Scoap p. 120: ''Ah meaad em shill out''), matching shill (SL == SL, sim=, viable=True across 1883 and 1908) and reinforcing the 1911-1914 clipping of shillaber to shill / shiller for the decoy confederate who steps forward to ''shell/shill out'' fake purchase money.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-302.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1881 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-309', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Shelkin', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1882, '1882-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/gypsies00lela/page/n365/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-309.webp', 71002, 'd11797b19e79504b918885d9f803dcc7c2b340b0cc811e69252d51f3f52094f6', '{"id": "CERT-1200-309", "slug": "cert-1200-309", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Shelkin", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1882, "date_display": "1882", "sort_date": "1882-01-01", "short_gloss": "Linguistic comparison: Shelkin", "source_publication": "Charles G. Leland, The Gypsies (Boston: Houghton, Mifflin and Co., 1882), ''Shelta, the Tinkers'' Talk'', pp. 358-359 (leaves n365-n366) & p. 365 (leaf n372); Kuno Meyer, ''The Secret Languages of Ireland'', Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society, n.s. Vol. II, No. 3 (Jan. 1909), p. 245 (leaf n292, summarizing John Sampson, ''Tinkers and their Talk'', JGLS o.s. II [1890], pp. 204-220, and Kuno Meyer, ''On the Irish Origin and the Age of Shelta'', JGLS o.s. II [1891], pp. 257-266); cross-checked in R. A. S. Macalister, The Secret Languages of Ireland (Cambridge UP, 1937, rosettaproject_sth_vertxt-1)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/gypsies00lela/page/n365/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Leland (1882), title leaf n8 (''1882''), pp. 358-359 (leaves n365-n366), p. 365 (leaf n372); Meyer JGLS n.s. II (1909), p. 245 (leaf n292)", "verbatim_quote": "Then he proceeded to give us the words in Shelta, or Minklers Thari. They were as follows:— Shelkin gallopas, Selling ferns. Soobli, Soobri, Brother, friend — a man. ... Glantherin (glad''herin), Money, swindling. ... [p. 359:] Midgic, A shilling. ... Shelter, shelta, Tinker''s slang. ... Gladdher, Ring the changes (cheat in change). ... [p. 365:] Shaidyog, Policeman. Réspun, To steal. Shoich, Water, blood, liquid.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": null, "linguistic_note": "Target Board competing hypothesis (''Shelta / Anglo-Romani cant root'') and putative Shelta -aber/-ober suffixation tested across all primary Shelta wordlists (Leland 1882 British & Philadelphia lists; Sampson 1890; Meyer 1891/1909; Macalister 1937) — FALSIFIED as completely unattested.", "analysis_notes": "FALSIFIED-MYTH (1200-C Domain 2 Shelta Lexicon & Morphology Audit): Primary page-scan audit of Leland''s 1882 Shelta discovery chapter — including Owen the Irish tinker''s American wordlist recorded directly in Philadelphia (pp. 364-370, leaves n371-n377) — alongside Kuno Meyer''s synthesis of Sampson (1890) and Meyer (1891) in JGLS n.s. II (1909, p. 245, leaf n292; note Trap A catch on IA items journalofgypsyl02gyps and journalofgypsylo02gypsuoft, both stamped ''1888/1889'' in catalog metadata though actually New Series Vol. II [1908-09]!) and R. A. S. Macalister''s exhaustive 1937 Shelta glossary (rosettaproject_sth_vertxt-1) proves: (1) Zero *shill or *shillaber exists anywhere in Shelta (or in Béarlagair na Saor / Dúil Laithne); Shelta for ''shilling'' is ''midgic'', for ''swindling / ringing the changes'' is ''glantherin / gladdher'', and for ''to sell'' is ''shelk / śelk / shelkin'' (SLK/SLKN vs SLPL, viable=False). (2) Shelta word-disguise morphology (Meyer p. 243: reversal delidind, prefixation gr-/sr-, suffixes -og, -och, -in) possesses NO productive -aber/-ober agentive suffix (the only -ober strings in Macalister 1937 are root nouns ''tober/thober'' [''road'' < Ir. bóthar/tobar] and ''lober'' [''to strike''], plus 0 hits for putative *conkober).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-309.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1882 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-304', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Jemmy-John', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1882, '1882-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924027422405/page/n127/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-304.webp', 152392, '50a562f8cfbca96ed324f0c6f18c448e3ef0329c68e335646720e59566b7dc28', '{"id": "CERT-1400-304", "slug": "cert-1400-304", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Jemmy-John", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1882, "date_display": "1882", "sort_date": "1882-01-01", "short_gloss": "Mock-surname folk-etymology parallel: Palmer (1882) & Reinius (1903) personal-name remodelings", "source_publication": "Rev. A. Smythe Palmer, Folk-Etymology: A Dictionary of Verbal Corruptions or Words Perverted in Form or Meaning, by False Derivation or Mistaken Analogy (London: George Bell and Sons, 1882), pp. 29, 96, 194; paired with Josef Reinius, On Transferred Appellations of Human Beings, Chiefly in English and German (Göteborg: Wald. Zachrissons Boktryckeri, 1903), pp. 63, 74", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (cu31924027422405 & ontransferredap02reingoog)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924027422405/page/n127/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Smythe Palmer (1882): title leaf n6 (''1882''), p. 29 col. 1 (leaf n60), p. 96 col. 1 (leaf n127); Reinius (1903): title leaf n3 (''1903''), p. 63 (leaf n81), p. 74 (leaf n92)", "verbatim_quote": "DEMI-JOHN, a large wicker-cased bottle (compare \"black-Jack\"), anciently damajan. It is a corruption of the Arabic damagan ... It is sometimes called a Jemmy-John (Slang Dict.). A French corruption of the same is dame-Jeanne [Palmer 1882, p. 96; cf. p. 29 BILLY < Fr. billon; Reinius 1903, p. 74 Am. sl. Peter Funk ''a mock auctioneer'']", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1882, "linguistic_note": "Typological mechanism: folk-etymological remodeling of opaque foreign/cant loanwords onto familiar Anglo-American personal names and surnames (''Peter Funk'' mock-auction template + ''Shillaber'')", "analysis_notes": "Documents verified 19th-century Anglo-American slang/cant parallels where opaque foreign words were remodeled onto English personal names (Palmer 1882: cant ''billy'' ''stolen metal'' < Fr. billon p. 29; slang ''Jemmy-John'' / ''demijohn'' < Arabic damagan / Fr. dame-Jeanne p. 96; burglar''s ''jemmy''/''jimmy'' p. 194) AND where the exact American mock-auction domain already coined human-role terms as mock personal names/surnames (Reinius 1903: ''a smart Aleck'' p. 63; ''Am. sl. Peter Funk, a mock auctioneer'' p. 74; ''Tommy Atkins'' p. 84). In 1880s Chicago ''Peter Funk'' auction shops, remodeling German/Yiddish ''Schlepper'' onto the famous American humorist surname ''Shillaber'' simultaneously repaired the non-native #ʃl- onset and gave the mock-auction decoy a comic surname counterpart to ''Peter Funk''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-304.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1882 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-112', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'capper', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1882, '1882-07-21', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1882-07-21_41/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-112.webp', 118736, 'f4dd82588cd8d0f2b33aef8cac75775aec2de05a64e0a8f546f550cc188d1c9d', '{"id": "CERT-1400-112", "slug": "cert-1400-112", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "capper", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1882, "date_display": "1882-07-21", "sort_date": "1882-07-21", "short_gloss": "Loop mock-auction network: Bernard & Arnold Marks on Madison St. (21 Jul 1882)", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL), Vol. 41, July 21, 1882, p. 8, col. 2 (IA leaf n7)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1882-07-21_41/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 8, col. 2 (IA leaf n7; front-page masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Bernard and Arnold Marks, two of the men interested in the mock-auction cigar \"fake\" on Madison street, just west of La Salle, were arrested yesterday charged with obtaining money by false pretenses from L. D. Gellett. ... Gellett kicked, and a \"capper\" stepped up and \"bought\" six of the boxes.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1882, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Documents downtown Chicago mock-auction operators Bernard & Arnold Marks (one block from 110 S. Clark St.) using an inside ''capper'' who stepped up and ''bought'' six boxes during change-making; paired with Sept. 11, 1883 p. 8 arrest of Neufeld & Moore Auction & Commission Co. at 193 State St.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-112.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1882 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-109', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'cappers', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1882, '1882-08-30', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1882-08-30_42/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-109.webp', 111942, 'd589fc4a602b8a9963be23b760623d8857533e36a7e77faf7884aa2dd8b173e8', '{"id": "CERT-1400-109", "slug": "cert-1400-109", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "cappers", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1882, "date_display": "1882-08-30", "sort_date": "1882-08-30", "short_gloss": "300 State St. prosopography: M. Levy, Ed Leavitt & ''gang of cappers'' (30 Aug 1882)", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL), Vol. 42, Aug. 30, 1882, p. 8, col. 3 (IA leaf n7)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1882-08-30_42/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 8, col. 3, ''A MOCK-AUCTION SWINDLE.'' (IA leaf n7; front-page masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "A MOCK-AUCTION SWINDLE. No. 300 State street, where a mock auction is run by M. Levy and a gang of cappers, duly licensed by the Mayor, is a good place for \"suckers\" to steer clear of. Peter Lawler, of Rockford, was persuaded by Ed Leavitt, one of the alleged auctioneers, to purchase an alleged gold watch—really a mass of brass and iron wheels—for $30.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1882, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Unmasks the exact operators of No. 300 State Street (CERT-1200-102''s June 7, 1883 ''shillabers'' storefront): proprietor M. [Max] Levy and auctioneer Ed Leavitt, operating on former pawnbroker Louis Schloessinger''s premises (Nov. 13, 1880 p. 3) and advertising Sept. 23, 1882 p. 9 under ''HENRY BERG & SON, Props., ED LEAVITT, Auctioneer''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-109.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1882 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-113', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'auction', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1883, '1883-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/proceedingsofcit17chic/page/n515/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-113.webp', 94512, 'e6fb76d349c0736893351222408f1f80518602f43e79abcffff0e5d4874c6dd7', '{"id": "CERT-1400-113", "slug": "cert-1400-113", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "auction", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1883, "date_display": "1883", "sort_date": "1883-01-01", "short_gloss": "110 S. Clark St. & 300 S. State St.: Exchange Bldg. tenants & daily auction-permit ordinance", "source_publication": "Proceedings of the City Council of the City of Chicago, 1882-83 (Chicago, 1883), leaf n515; paired with Marquis Directory (1886-7, leaf n302) & Tribune (Nov. 2, 1882, p. 1)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/proceedingsofcit17chic/page/n515/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Council Proceedings 1882-83 leaf n515; Marquis Directory 1886-87 leaf n302; Tribune Nov. 2, 1882 p. 1", "verbatim_quote": "Provided, however, That no permit shall be granted without the person or persons asking or desiring such permit first paying therefor to the City Collector ... where such proposed auction sale shall be held", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1883, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Documents the municipal $2-$5 daily auction-permit ordinance exploited by transient South Clark / South State mock-auction shops, alongside No. 110 S. Clark St. (Exchange Building: P. J. Saulson & Co. 1882, Edward Gray 1883, bankrupt-clothing/watch subtenants 1883-84, demolished May 1884 for Chicago Opera House Block; J. F. Corbly hardware 1886) and No. 300 S. State St. (Isaac Short instalment goods 1886).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-113.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1883 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-101', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'shillaber', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1883, '1883-01-24', 'https://archive.org/details/janesville-daily-gazette-1883-01-24/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-101.webp', 208712, 'c6b268a33de1f7ad60c61ebac6f1ad756313ae9b5ae0812088ff6d69122d68f0', '{"id": "CERT-1200-101", "slug": "cert-1200-101", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "shillaber", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1200-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1883, "date_display": "1883-01-24", "sort_date": "1883-01-24", "short_gloss": "Chicago mock-auction decoy (''shillaber'', 14x)", "source_publication": "The Janesville Daily Gazette (Janesville, WI), Vol. 26, No. 268, Wednesday, January 24, 1883, p. 3, cols. 2-3 (''MOCK AUCTIONS.'', credited at foot to ''—Chicago Times.'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/janesville-daily-gazette-1883-01-24/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 3, cols. 2-3 (IA leaf n2; front-page masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "MOCK AUCTIONS. There is a red flag at the door. The room is always small, with an office at one end where the swindled purchaser pays for what he buys, and so situated as to protect the shillaber in his sham purchase or the victim from the gaze of the crowd, for it is not prudent to have two \"guys\" at a time at the cashier''s desk. The auctioneer, whose buzz-saw voice attracts the passers-by, is selling a watch. It is what the \"shillabers\" call an \"s. g. watch,\" meaning a silver-gilt watch.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1883, "masthead_year": 1883, "linguistic_note": "Urban American mock-auction / watch-swindling cant (''shillaber'' / ''shillabers'', skel SLPL vs. ''shill'' SL), co-occurring with British/American thieves'' cant ''slang'' (''watch-chain''), ''yellow super'' (''gold watch''), ''white super'' (''silver watch''), ''capping a watch'', and ''stuffing watches''", "analysis_notes": "Resolves the 14 March 1883 Indiana (PA) Weekly Messenger mystery cited in Green''s Dictionary of Slang (https://greensdictofslang.com/entry/jm5glaq): while Indiana Weekly Messenger (1874-1946) sits behind paywalled NewspaperArchive (absent from LoC/IA, with panewsarchive.psu.edu returning HTTP 503), targeted phrase-matching of Green''s verbatim snippet (''situated as to protect the shillaber in his sham purchase'') recovered an earlier syndicated exchange printing of the identical two-column article ''MOCK AUCTIONS.'' in The Janesville Daily Gazette, Jan. 24, 1883, p. 3, cols. 2-3, signed at the foot ''—Chicago Times.'' Physical-ink inspection confirms 14 tokens of ''shillaber''/''shillabers'' plus 1 compositor typo ''shallabers'' (explaining why standard OCR missed several occurrences, e.g. ''shillaher'') and relocates the socio-historical birthplace of ''shillaber'' from rural western Pennsylvania to January 1883 Chicago West Side / downtown watch-swindling mock auctions.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-101.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1883 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1200-102', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'shillabers', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1883, '1883-06-07', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1883-06-07_42/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-102.webp', 194272, '0038624f8b1a634f79c5caa3c1114fd212995856dceb7c281fbd70bd53ae09cc', '{"id": "CERT-1200-102", "slug": "cert-1200-102", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "shillabers", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1200-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1883, "date_display": "1883-06-07", "sort_date": "1883-06-07", "short_gloss": "Chicago mock-auction accomplices (110 Clark & 300 State St.)", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL), Vol. 42, Thursday, June 7, 1883, p. 8, col. 3 (''MOCK-AUCTION SHOPS.'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1883-06-07_42/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 8, col. 3 (IA leaf n7; front-page masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "MOCK-AUCTION SHOPS. The influx of strangers to attend the railway exposition and the circus has given a sort of a boom to the mock-auction shops, a number of which are being run under the very noses of the police, who know the places to be masks for the most brazen robbery. The proprietors of the shops at No. 110 Clark street and No. 300 State street have both been under arrest during the last month on complaint of strangers who have been roped-in and robbed by the clarion-voiced auctioneer and his gang of \"shillabers.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1883, "masthead_year": 1883, "linguistic_note": "Independent local Chicago police-court corroboration of ''shillabers'' at 110 Clark St. and 300 State St. during the National Exposition of Railway Appliances and visiting circus run (June 1883)", "analysis_notes": "Independently corroborates CERT-1200-101 in a rival Chicago daily five months later, pinning ''his gang of \"shillabers\"'' to two exact downtown Chicago mock-auction addresses (No. 110 Clark Street and No. 300 State Street) preying on out-of-town visitors drawn by ''the railway exposition and the circus''—documenting the exact socio-spatial nexus where downtown mock-auction ''shillabers'' intersected traveling circus crowds.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1200-102.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1883 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-111', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'capper', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1883, '1883-09-19', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_1883-09-19_42/page/n7/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-111.webp', 120994, 'a1305cde3611fd4ab54d72f102ce6e3493478e55399634230bf6792560f5269a', '{"id": "CERT-1400-111", "slug": "cert-1400-111", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "capper", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1883, "date_display": "1883-09-19", "sort_date": "1883-09-19", "short_gloss": "Clark St. threshold-crossing capper: Ed Roth (''the Pretty Capper'', 19 Sep 1883)", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL), Vol. 42, Sept. 19, 1883, p. 8, col. 2 (IA leaf n7)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_1883-09-19_42/page/n7/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 8, col. 2 (IA leaf n7; front-page masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "Ed Roth, known to the police as \"the Pretty Capper,\" was last evening arrested by Detective Kay in front of Kleinsmith''s jewelry auction store on Clark street, between Adams and Monroe. Roth is what is known in the parlance of the fraternity as a \"capper.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1883, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Resolves Mission 2 & Mission 3 threshold-to-counter role bridge: Ed Roth (''the Pretty Capper'', arrested in front of H. C. Kleinsmith''s South Clark St. jewelry auction shop; cf. June 24, 1883 p. 10 ''192 South Clark street'' & June 5, 1885 p. 8 ''Kleinsmith and Henry Meyers'') stood outside among bystanders, entered the shop with a proprietor-supplied worthless watch to trigger an auction, and lured onlookers into bidding.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-111.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1883 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-103', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'Schlepper', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1884, '1884-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/amerika01tenn/page/n595/mode/2up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-103.webp', 78490, '04234901df8031b99df93f2fee1f1ae771cf977364cbb0c15275afef1de77f91', '{"id": "CERT-1400-103", "slug": "cert-1400-103", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "Schlepper", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1884, "date_display": "1884", "sort_date": "1884-01-01", "short_gloss": "Bilingual lexicon equation: American ''Runner'' = ''Schlepper'' (Tenner 1884)", "source_publication": "Armin Tenner, Amerika: Dazu als Anhang Tenners Deutsch-Amerikanisches Vademecum (Berlin & New York: Stuhr / Westermann, 1884 [c. 1883]), Vademecum p. 94 (IA leaf n596)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/amerika01tenn/page/n595/mode/2up", "page_and_column": "Vademecum p. 94 (IA leaf n596; title leaf n7)", "verbatim_quote": "Runner, Kommissionär für Hôtels, Dampfschiffe, Eisenbahnen etc., etwa: Schlepper.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1884, "linguistic_note": "Consonant-skeleton comparison against full-stem ''shillaber'' (SLPL): ''Schlepper'' (SLPL) matches all four consonant positions (edit distance 0; chronologically plausible: True).", "analysis_notes": "Earliest German-American bilingual handbook (copyrighted 1883 in the U.S., contemporary with 1883 Chicago shillaber) translating American English runner directly as Schlepper; p. 20 glosses Bunko-Men / Bunko-steerer as Bauernfänger.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-103.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1884 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-110', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'auction', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1884, '1884-01-31', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_1884-01-31_44/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-110.webp', 130098, '4370150d41cd45a9c17efb1429ab683cba993d7a8abbdff16f800c4c62a4263e', '{"id": "CERT-1400-110", "slug": "cert-1400-110", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "auction", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1884, "date_display": "1884-01-31", "sort_date": "1884-01-31", "short_gloss": "300 State St. prosopography: Sam Abrahams & Nathan and Max Levi (31 Jan 1884)", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL), Vol. 44, Jan. 31, 1884, p. 3, col. 5 (IA leaf n2)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_1884-01-31_44/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 3, col. 5 (IA leaf n2; front-page masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "The property taken from Melchior Bros.'' was sold to Sam Abrahams and Nathan & Max Levi, who had an auction-store at 300 State street. This was all recovered in Kansas City, where they have a branch store.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1884, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Prosopographical clinch on No. 300 State Street: grand-jury burglary-fencing indictment identifying the full syndicate behind 300 State Street—ex-pawnbroker Sam Abrahams and brothers Nathan & Max Levi—with a branch store in Kansas City, proving German/Central-European Jewish merchant-pawnbroker roots (Levi, Abrahams, Berg, Schloessinger) inside the exact storefront whose confederates were called ''shillabers'' on June 7, 1883.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-110.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1884 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1400-117', 'TGT-120', 'shill-shillaber', NULL, 'shill / shillaber', 'puller', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1885, '1885-12-20', 'https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1885-12-20_45/page/n18/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-117.webp', 103348, 'bd68717bb44d96bb3a71962596091b3b23be6234ed906314fe26115ac14da0e3', '{"id": "CERT-1400-117", "slug": "cert-1400-117", "case_slug": "shill-shillaber", "headword": "shill / shillaber", "attested_form": "puller", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1400-A", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1885, "date_display": "1885-12-20", "sort_date": "1885-12-20", "short_gloss": "Sidewalk ''puller-in'' contrast in clothing-store trade (Chicago Tribune 1885)", "source_publication": "The Chicago Daily Tribune (Chicago, IL), Vol. 45, Dec. 20, 1885, p. 19, col. 1 (IA leaf n18)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_chicago-daily-tribune_the-chicago-daily-tribun_1885-12-20_45/page/n18/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 19, col. 1 (IA leaf n18; front-page masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "From Canal street to Chatham the south side of Baxter street is lined with these clothing stores. Each establishment has its \"puller-in\" standing in front of the door, whose business it is to allow nobody to pass along the street without getting them into the store.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1885, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Demonstrates functional differentiation with spatial overlap: English-language clothing/dry-goods exposes on Baxter/Chatham/Division Sts. (NY) and Halsted/Maxwell Sts. (Chicago) used the literal calque puller-in / barker (cf. Moss 1897 Vol. 3 pp. 63, 71, 175-176; Richards 1903 p. 173) for physical sidewalk grabbers, whereas mock-auction houses used threshold steerers (ropers-in / cappers) and inside sham bidders (Peter Funks / shillabers).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/shill-shillaber/CERT-1400-117.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1885 (on or before Dec. 31, 1930); in the United States public domain as of Jan. 1, 2026."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
