1861 for a quartermaster''s or soldier''s shanty/outfit", "analysis_notes": "Primary-scan-verified (-1y vs. 1862 baseline) wartime Clipper dispatch from Baltimore (then garrisoned by New York, Pennsylvania, and Massachusetts Union regiments), showing continuous variety-saloon usage right alongside incoming Northern troops.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-111.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1861 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-113', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1861, '1861-09-28', 'https://archive.org/details/per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1861-09-28_1/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-113.webp', 228874, 'a3ce9a45df1da86d6cd773464264924aee32fe68393ecdc81382b760aca1e52f', '{"id": "CERT-2000-113", "slug": "cert-2000-113", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1861, "date_display": "28 Sep 1861", "sort_date": "1861-09-28", "short_gloss": "shebang in The Columbia Spy (Columbia, Lancaster County, PA)", "source_publication": "The Columbia Spy (Columbia, Lancaster County, PA)", "source_archive": "Pennsylvania Newspaper Archive (Penn State Open-ONI)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1861-09-28_1/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, cols. 2–3 (''Our Army Correspondence'', datelined ''SEPTEMBER 23d, 1861'', signed ''FARMER'')", "verbatim_quote": "We have been looking for a familiar Columbia countenance for some time at camp, and yesterday were gratified by a visit from Mr. John A. Hook, who supped with us, tried a blanket with us last night and breakfasted this morning. I took charge of the Quartermaster''s \"shebang,\" and did not rise early enough to see him before he stepped over to the First Regiment, so cannot say how he likes camp quarters on a cool night.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1861, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed dram-shop / low tavern'', < Irish síbín) blended in antebellum U.S. sporting, oyster-saloon, lodge, and variety cant with frontier echoic gun-blast che-bang! / ker-bang! (> chebang, 1852; shebang, 1859), entering Union volunteer regiments in 1861 for a quartermaster''s or soldier''s shanty/outfit", "analysis_notes": "NEW 84-DAY CIVIL WAR CAMP ANTEDATING (-1 year, 3 months vs. Dec. 1862 Whitman baseline; predates CERT-2000-201 Dec. 21, 1861 Columbia Spy camp letter by 84 days). Independently recovered across Penn State Open-ONI (LCCN sn83032185) and Internet Archive (per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1861-09-28_1). Written Sept. 23, 1861 at Camp Tennally, D.C., by ''FARMER'' in the 34th PA Volunteers / 5th PA Reserves (just before Regimental Quartermaster Samuel Evans''s Oct. 1, 1861 appointment and the Oct. 9 advance to Camp Pierpont, VA), showing Mid-Atlantic volunteers transferring urban saloon/lodge ''shebang'' (still in quotation marks: ''the Quartermaster\\''s \"shebang,\"'') directly onto regimental tent/shanty quarters in their first autumn.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-113.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1861 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-112', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1861, '1861-12-14', 'https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_the-new-york-clipper_1861-12-14_9_35/page/n6/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-112.webp', 175236, 'c887ff4b26d1e03ed500bbd30df6995eb4f1c16de67430de0f5037025cbfd454', '{"id": "CERT-2000-112", "slug": "cert-2000-112", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1861, "date_display": "14 Dec 1861", "sort_date": "1861-12-14", "short_gloss": "shebang in The New York Clipper (New York, NY)", "source_publication": "The New York Clipper (New York, NY)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_new-york-clipper_the-new-york-clipper_1861-12-14_9_35/page/n6/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. 9, No. 35, p. 279, col. 1 (leaf 6)", "verbatim_quote": "Continental Opera House, Baltimore, Md., exploded last Saturday night, caused by the inability of the \"boss\" to settle with the owner of the \"shebang.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1861, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed dram-shop / low tavern'', < Irish síbín) blended in antebellum U.S. sporting, oyster-saloon, lodge, and variety cant with frontier echoic gun-blast che-bang! / ker-bang! (> chebang, 1852; shebang, 1859), entering Union volunteer regiments in 1861 for a quartermaster''s or soldier''s shanty/outfit", "analysis_notes": "NEW UNRECORDED DISCOVERY (-1y vs. 1862 baseline): Uncited in Reitan, Green, or OED. Directly pairs ''\"boss\"'' and ''\"shebang\"'' for Baltimore''s Continental Opera House immediately above reportage on Charles Percival''s Canterbury Hall in Washington, D.C. (premier Army of the Potomac variety resort).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-112.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1861 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-201', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1861, '1861-12-21', 'https://archive.org/details/per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1861-12-21_1/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-201.webp', 128112, '54dd2809c009f35495feb187c2bce1481c955522f7f709eaf46d8db89239163a', '{"id": "CERT-2000-201", "slug": "cert-2000-201", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1861, "date_display": "21 Dec 1861", "sort_date": "1861-12-21", "short_gloss": "shebang in The Columbia Spy (Columbia, Lancaster County, PA), Vol. XXXIII, No. 21 [Whole No. 1,635], Saturday Morning, December 21, 1861", "source_publication": "The Columbia Spy (Columbia, Lancaster County, PA), Vol. XXXIII, No. 21 [Whole No. 1,635], Saturday Morning, December 21, 1861", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1861-12-21_1)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1861-12-21_1/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, cols. 2-3 (''Our Army Correspondence'', signed ''ORDERLY'')", "verbatim_quote": "It would have done you good to have seen the Drum Major and Sergeant Major, and Quartermaster Sergeant, dive into the mud with their hands, and with an extra fling send it to every opening, at which they arrived with unerring precision: Sergeants Caldwell, Kershner and Bennett, and Corporals Shreiner and Fry had their particular posts assigned them—some carried clay, others water, and I believe Sergeants C. and K. tempered the mortar. As the shebang is not completed, I cannot at this time speak of its peculiar merits, nor of the warming of which I hear it is to have.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1861, "masthead_year": 1861, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Full primary-scan inspection of p. 1 masthead (''COLUMBIA, PENNSYLVANIA, SATURDAY MORNING, DECEMBER 21, 1861'', Vol. XXXIII, No. 21) and p. 2, cols. 2-3 (''Our Army Correspondence'', signed ''ORDERLY''). Note that the word ''shebang'' is printed WITHOUT quotation marks in italics-free roman type (''As the shebang is not completed''), demonstrating already-naturalized regimental usage at Camp Pierpont, Fairfax County, VA. Cross-matching all five named non-commissioned officers plus the three Field & Staff mud-daubers in Bates Vol. I (CERT-2000-202) and The Columbia Spy (June 1 & Nov. 30, 1861) identifies the exact unit as Company K (''The Cookman Rangers''), 34th Regiment Pennsylvania Volunteers (5th Pennsylvania Reserves), mustered June 21, 1861 under Col. Seneca G. Simmons and Co. K Capt. Arnout D. Collins, and unmasks correspondent ''ORDERLY'' as 2nd Lt. & Regimental Quartermaster Samuel Evans (1823-1908), Columbia carpenter/builder, Justice of the Peace, and Lancaster County historian (originally Co. K''s 1st/Orderly Sergeant in May 1861). Prior public citation of this clip by Peter Reitan (ADS-L, Nov. 3, 2015) and Dave Wilton (Wordorigins.org, Aug. 19, 2021) noted per editorial guidelinesRule #3; prosopographical identification of the unit, camp, correspondent, and builders is newly established here.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-201.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1861 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-204', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-01-04', 'https://archive.org/details/per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1862-01-04_1/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-204.webp', 134634, '88e68d9cbea8432136a61ee63dc0bd532972509f1272ce1478455f9e1ee5cc23', '{"id": "CERT-2000-204", "slug": "cert-2000-204", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "4 Jan 1862", "sort_date": "1862-01-04", "short_gloss": "shebang in The Columbia Spy (Columbia, Lancaster County, PA), Vol. XXXIII, No. 23 [Whole No. 1,637], Saturday Morning, January 4, 1862", "source_publication": "The Columbia Spy (Columbia, Lancaster County, PA), Vol. XXXIII, No. 23 [Whole No. 1,637], Saturday Morning, January 4, 1862", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1862-01-04_1)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1862-01-04_1/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, cols. 2-3 (''Our Army Correspondence'', datelined ''CAMP PIERPONT, VA., Dec. 25, 1861'', signed ''ORDERLY'')", "verbatim_quote": "I had the honor of an invitation to dine in the new shebang just built and occupied by the sergeants belonging to Co. K. ... The building is about twenty-four feet long, by eight wide and high; it fronts the street upon which the company is formed, with a door in the centre; the fire place is to the rear and directly opposite the door; light is obtained through a transom above the door... Lieut. Wright was the recipient of a box filled with good things sent him from home: he too had his \"time\" in another shebang, with a number of friends.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1862, "masthead_year": 1862, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Direct Christmas Day sequel (written Dec. 25, 1861 at Camp Pierpont, VA; printed Jan. 4, 1862) to CERT-2000-201! Provides both the explicit regimental unit confirmation (''the sergeants belonging to Co. K'', naming Sergeants Caldwell, Smedly, Smith, Kershner, and Bennett, Corporals Shreiner and Fry, Sgt. Mullen, 1st Lt. John L. Wright, and Columbia visitors John Bruner and Jack Wade) and the most detailed architectural blueprint of a Civil War winter-quarters ''shebang'' in print: a 24 x 8 x 8 ft log-and-mud cabin roofed with straw and earth, rail-and-stone chimney, center door with glazed transom, and end double bunks—plus a second unquoted token (''in another shebang'') in the same column.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-204.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-205', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1863, '1862-06-30', 'https://archive.org/details/annualreportcom08affagoog/page/n427/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-205.webp', 122950, '64ce064c632bb138b15581447ec28b82c828425b09a4e1eb05a0b80b4170101d', '{"id": "CERT-2000-205", "slug": "cert-2000-205", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1863, "date_display": "30 Jun 1862", "sort_date": "1862-06-30", "short_gloss": "shebang in J. W. Anderson, ''Office Nez Perce Indian Agency, Lapwai, W.T., June 30, 1862,'' in Report of the Commissioner of Indian Affairs fo", "source_publication": "J. W. Anderson, ''Office Nez Perce Indian Agency, Lapwai, W.T., June 30, 1862,'' in Report of the Commissioner of Indian Affairs for the Year 1862 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1863)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (annualreportcom08affagoog)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/annualreportcom08affagoog/page/n427/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 423 (leaf n427; GPO title page leaf n8)", "verbatim_quote": "Along all the roads on the reservation to all the mines, at the crossing of every stream or fresh-water spring, and near the principal Indian villages, an inn or \"shebang\" is established, ostensibly for the entertainment of travellers, but almost universally used as a den for supplying liquor to Indians.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1863, "masthead_year": 1863, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Crucial Pacific Northwest / Salmon River gold-rush semantic link between Anglo-Irish ''shebeen'' (''unlicensed wayside liquor shanty'') and Western American ''shebang''! Written June 30, 1862 at Lapwai, Washington Territory (modern Idaho, near newly founded Lewiston) by U.S. Nez Perce Sub-Agent J. W. Anderson and printed by GPO in 1863 (attested_year conservatively logged as 1863 imprint year per Trap A/C strictness). Explicitly glosses ''an inn or \"shebang\"... almost universally used as a den for supplying liquor to Indians'', proving that the wayside-groggery sense coexisted on the 1862 mining frontier alongside Union Army camp-hut usage.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-205.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1863 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-320', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'rejected_lead', 'serial_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-11-14', 'https://archive.org/details/bp_981448-3/page/567/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-320.webp', 93664, '4869e4cf3ad3472090c54fb7b3a83f9acfd8f4d31047167c496ecefad9c95868', '{"id": "CERT-2000-320", "slug": "cert-2000-320", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "serial_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "14 Nov 1862", "sort_date": "1862-11-14", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: shebang", "source_publication": "Report of the Secretary of the Interior / Report of the Commissioner of Indian Affairs for the Year 1862 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1862/1863 [IA catalog misdated 1824]), p. 567 (leaf 398); paired with Mary Hallock Foote, ''The Prodigal,'' The Atlantic Monthly, Vol. LXXXVI (Boston: Houghton, Mifflin & Co., Sept. 1900 [IA catalog misdated 1857]), p. 308 (leaf 315)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/bp_981448-3/page/567/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Indian Affairs p. 199 (leaf 30, Nov. 14, 1862 dateline) & p. 567 (leaf 398); Atlantic Vol. 86 Contents leaf 7 (''Ober-Ammergau in 1900'') & p. 308", "verbatim_quote": "Along all the roads on the reservation to all the mines, at the crossing of every stream or fresh-water spring, and near the principal Indian villages, an inn or \"shebang\" is established, ostensibly for the entertainment of travellers, but almost universally used as a den for supplying liquor to Indians.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1862, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Exposes two severe Trap A serial-founding-year misdates that masquerade as dramatic pre-1862 breakthroughs in unverified metadata queries: (1) IA item `bp_981448-3` is cataloged `year: 1824` (founding year of the Office of Indian Affairs), making its vivid saloon passage (`an inn or ''shebang'' is established... as a den for supplying liquor to Indians`, p. 567 leaf 398) look like a 38-year 1824 antedating! Internal scan verification (leaf 30 `St. Paul, November 14, 1862` & p. 567 reference to Lewiston, W.T., laid out Oct. 1861; cf. `CERT-2000-205`) proves the volume is the 1862/1863 Interior Report (`catalog_year 1824 != masthead_year 1862`); (2) IA item `atlanticmonthly06projgoog` is cataloged `year: 1857` (founding year of *The Atlantic Monthly*), making `Christian trousers in the whole shebang` look like an 1857 phrasal antedating (12 years before 1869!), whereas Contents leaf 7 (`Ober-Ammergau in 1900`) proves it is Vol. LXXXVI (1900, 43-year misdate; parallel Trap A hits caught on *Woburn Journal* cat. `1851` and *Saturday Evening Post* `saturdayeveningp1933unse` cat. `1839` -> actual `1933`).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-320.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-208', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'Shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1863, '1863-01-01', 'https://www.loc.gov/item/2007682510/', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-208.webp', 36728, 'd447fd581d6a1cd048f5199b7c9fc929f412d7c75f67c896cad252e3dfe64cec', '{"id": "CERT-2000-208", "slug": "cert-2000-208", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "Shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1863, "date_display": "1863", "sort_date": "1863-01-01", "short_gloss": "Shebang in James Gardner (negative) & Alexander Gardner (positive/publisher), ''The Shebang, or Quarters of U.S. Sanitary Commission, Brandy", "source_publication": "James Gardner (negative) & Alexander Gardner (positive/publisher), ''The Shebang, or Quarters of U.S. Sanitary Commission, Brandy Station, November, 1863,'' Plate No. 51 in Gardner''s Photographic Sketch Book of the War, Vol. II (Washington: Philp & Solomons, 1866)", "source_archive": "Library of Congress Prints & Photographs Division (LC-DIG-ppmsca-12572) & Internet Archive (GardnersPhotographicSketchBookOfTheWar)", "source_url": "https://www.loc.gov/item/2007682510/", "page_and_column": "LoC Item 2007682510 (high-res mount 12572v.jpg) & Sketch Book Vol. II, Plate 51 letterpress", "verbatim_quote": "[Printed mount caption:] Negative by J. GARDNER. Positive by A. GARDNER, 511 7th St., Washington. THE SHEBANG, OR QUARTERS OF U. S. SANITARY COMMISSION, BRANDY STATION. No. 51. November, 1863. [Companion letterpress:] \"Shebang.\" Headquarters of the Sanitary Commission, Brandy Station, November, 1863. Here is represented one of the establishments of the Sanitary Commission in the army.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1866, "masthead_year": 1863, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "High-resolution visual inspection of the Library of Congress master scan (12572v.jpg) and companion Vol. II letterpress leaf establishes two concrete visual facts: (1) there is NO painted ''SHEBANG'' sign on the white clapboard house, chimney, or pitched-tent compound at Brandy Station; rather, ''THE SHEBANG, OR QUARTERS OF U. S. SANITARY COMMISSION, BRANDY STATION. November, 1863'' is the formal printed title on Alexander Gardner''s mount (entered for copyright 1865; published in Sketch Book Vol. II, 1866) for James Gardner''s Nov. 1863 Army of the Potomac negative; and (2) the companion letterpress explicitly glosses ''Shebang'' as ''one of the establishments of the Sanitary Commission in the army'', showing how a composite compound of frame house, canvas tents, and supply wagons was collectively dubbed ''The Shebang''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-208.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1863 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-206', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1863, '1863-03-14', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_harpers-weekly_1863-03-14_7_324/page/n4/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-206.webp', 57314, '20c35495f6f65fea70f478f60456c75a0742e035a4c97554bf09ca898589803b', '{"id": "CERT-2000-206", "slug": "cert-2000-206", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1863, "date_display": "14 Mar 1863", "sort_date": "1863-03-14", "short_gloss": "shebang in Harper''s Weekly: A Journal of Civilization (New York, NY), Vol. VII, No. 324, Saturday, March 14, 1863", "source_publication": "Harper''s Weekly: A Journal of Civilization (New York, NY), Vol. VII, No. 324, Saturday, March 14, 1863", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sim_harpers-weekly_1863-03-14_7_324)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_harpers-weekly_1863-03-14_7_324/page/n4/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 165, col. 4 (''Picket Guard at Tuscumbia River'') & p. 167, col. 2 (''How Two Union Officers Did Not Go to Libby Prison'')", "verbatim_quote": "[p. 165, Silas Flournoy, Co. I, 2nd Iowa Inf.:] The shelter, or ''shebang,'' as the boys term it, is located upon the only dry spot, about 10 feet square, on the east side of the stream. [p. 167, Western Theater parole dialogue:] Guerrillas have no business to parole an officer or private; they are outlaws any how; not owned in their own ''shebang'' of a Confederacy as soldiers.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1863, "masthead_year": 1863, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Extraordinary dual attestation inside a single national issue of Harper''s Weekly (March 14, 1863) capturing the exact semantic pivot in real time: on p. 165 (leaf n4), Pvt. Silas Flournoy of Co. I, 2nd Iowa Infantry near Corinth, MS explicitly glosses the Western Theater camp sense (''The shelter, or \"shebang,\" as the boys term it''), while two pages later on p. 167 (leaf n6), a captured Union officer already extends ''shebang'' metaphorically to an entire political/military regime (''not owned in their own \"shebang\" of a Confederacy as soldiers'')—directly foreshadowing the collective phrasal idiom ''the whole shebang'' four months later.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-206.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1863 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-207', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'the whole "chebang"', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1863, '1863-07-04', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_american-medical-times_1863-07-04_7_1/page/n5/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-207.webp', 61972, 'b2b864016f91f59600001a23d3363c7b8c0882d61461c35e679298104621460b', '{"id": "CERT-2000-207", "slug": "cert-2000-207", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "the whole \"chebang\"", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1863, "date_display": "4 Jul 1863", "sort_date": "1863-07-04", "short_gloss": "the whole \"chebang\" in James Bryan, M.D. (Surgeon U.S.V.), ''A Short Account of the \"Mary Ann\" Hospital, Grand Gulf, Miss.,'' American Medical", "source_publication": "James Bryan, M.D. (Surgeon U.S.V.), ''A Short Account of the \"Mary Ann\" Hospital, Grand Gulf, Miss.,'' American Medical Times (New York, NY), Vol. VII, No. 1, Saturday, July 4, 1863", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sim_american-medical-times_1863-07-04_7_1)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_american-medical-times_1863-07-04_7_1/page/n5/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 4, col. 1 (leaf n5; cover masthead leaf n1)", "verbatim_quote": "On the second of June we received orders from the commander of the post, Col. Geo. E. Bryant, of the 12th Wisconsin Vols., to remove the whole hospital up to Young''s Point or Milliken''s Bend. On the third the whole \"chebang\" was removed on board the \"Forest Queen,\" and started for Young''s Point.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1863, "masthead_year": 1863, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "EARLIEST KNOWN PRINTED COLLOCATION OF ''THE WHOLE CHEBANG / SHEBANG'' (-6 years vs. 1869 OED/Green''s dictionary baseline). Full primary-scan audit of Surgeon James Bryan''s report on the ''Mary Ann'' contraband & field hospital at Grand Gulf, MS (800-1,000 patients, nurses, medical staff, central dwelling, outhouses, and commissary stores) pinpoints its exact syntactic and semantic status: ''the whole \"chebang\"'' directly anaphorizes ''the whole hospital'' in the immediately preceding sentence! Because the brick/frame buildings on the Grand Gulf bluff stayed behind while the entire institutional outfit—patients, surgeons, nurses, and medical/commissary equipment—was embarked onto the steamer Forest Queen on June 3, 1863, this passage is the exact transitional semantic hinge from ''literal camp/hospital establishment'' to ''the entire collective outfit, personnel, and gear''. (Prior public citation by Wilton 2021 noted; also corrects Google Books bound-serial misdate ''1860'' on americanmedical15unkngoog to true masthead date July 4, 1863.)", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-207.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1863 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-301', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'séibín', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1864, '1864-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/b2486285x/page/632/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-301.webp', 88332, '64141f2e9ca5d28d48a03ac0ed19974da0e5db8a8cef00f1bcee5c83c0446317', '{"id": "CERT-2000-301", "slug": "cert-2000-301", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "séibín", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1864, "date_display": "1864", "sort_date": "1864-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: séibín", "source_publication": "Edward O''Reilly & John O''Donovan, An Irish-English Dictionary, New Ed. (Dublin: James Duffy, 1864 [1st ed. 1817]), p. 458 (leaf 470, Sibhin); paired with Rev. Patrick S. Dinneen, Foclóir Gaedhilge agus Béarla / An Irish-English Dictionary (Dublin: Irish Texts Society, 1904), p. 632 (leaf 651, s.v. Séibín) & p. 640 (leaf 659, s.v. Síbín)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/b2486285x/page/632/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "O''Reilly 1864 Title leaf 4 & p. 458 (leaf 470); Dinneen 1904 Title leaf 4 & p. 632 (leaf 651), col. 2", "verbatim_quote": "Séibín, g. id., pl. -nídhe, m., a little mug, a quart; inferior ale, whence the word sheebeen, sheebeen-house, used to denote an ale-house; also síbín.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1864, "linguistic_note": "Irish Gaelic séibín / síbín (''little mug, inferior ale'') -> Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed alehouse/whisky shanty'', attested c. 1781–1787) -> American slang chebang / shebang", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan inspection of O''Reilly (1817 `sanasgaoidhilges00orei` & 1864 O''Donovan rev. `irishenglishdict00ore` p. 458 leaf 470) establishes a key lexicographical calibration: O''Reilly''s classical/literary Irish dictionary records only `Sibhin, s. f. a bullrush` and `tigh-leanna, s. an ale-house`, omitting vernacular colloquial `séibín`/`síbín`, whereas Dinneen (1904 `b2486285x` p. 632 leaf 651 & p. 640 leaf 659) explicitly documents colloquial `Séibín... a little mug, a quart; inferior ale, whence the word sheebeen, sheebeen-house, used to denote an ale-house; also síbín`. Articulatory skeleton match on simplex `shebang`/`chebang` vs. `sibin`/`seibin`/`shebeen` is exact (`SPN == SPN`, edit distance 0; phrasal `whole shebang` `WLSPN` vs `SPN` , ``). Crucially, archival audit''s Dec. 17, 1852 *Kentucky Whig* discovery (`the Chebang and Restaurant`, `CERT-2000-102`) eliminates the longstanding chronological objection that U.S. ''tavern'' uses lagged behind Civil War ''hut'' uses.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-301.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1864 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-209', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'the whole "chebang"', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1864, '1864-03-05', 'https://archive.org/details/cqcl_000017/page/n1/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-209.webp', 110064, 'dddfe91bb8cc71cde1d6985d3ee150b8df6a2e02cacad60508e908fa59a0dfe3', '{"id": "CERT-2000-209", "slug": "cert-2000-209", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "the whole \"chebang\"", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1864, "date_display": "5 Mar 1864", "sort_date": "1864-03-05", "short_gloss": "the whole \"chebang\" in The Quincy Union (Quincy, Plumas County, CA), Vol. 2, No. 23 [masthead misprints No. 19; p. 2 running masthead March", "source_publication": "The Quincy Union (Quincy, Plumas County, CA), Vol. 2, No. 23 [masthead misprints No. 19; p. 2 running masthead March 5, 1864], Saturday, March 5, 1864", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Plumas County Museum California Newspaper Collection (cqcl_000017)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cqcl_000017/page/n1/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 2, col. 1 (''ROOP.'')", "verbatim_quote": "ROOP.—An effort is being made in the Legislature to cut a slice from Humboldt county and add it to Roop, and then add the whole \"chebang\" to Washoe county. We don''t object, but ''twill be powerfully unhandy for the citizens of Roop to come one hundred and twenty miles to the county-seat. Better make a shire town of it, and let them have a resident Deputy Sheriff, with alternate sessions of Probate Court, and one session of District Court yearly.—Old Piute. One hundred and twenty miles is nothing. Oh, no! if it is only in Nevada Territory. We don''t believe the people over that way will like to be called \"chebangers.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1864, "masthead_year": 1864, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "MAJOR NEW PHRASAL ANTEDATING (-5 years vs. 1869 OED/Green''s baseline; -21 months vs. previously earliest known collective Dec. 1865 attestation)! Unlike the July 4, 1863 American Medical Times hospital-transfer passage (CERT-2000-207), this March 5, 1864 California/Nevada border item (quoting ''Old Piute''—the Humboldt Register of Unionville, Nevada Territory—with editorial riposte by Quincy Union editor John K. Buckbee) has ZERO shelter, tent, or building referent: ''the whole \"chebang\"'' refers purely abstractly and collectively to the combined territorial/political package (''a slice from Humboldt county'' + ''Roop'' annexed to Washoe County), and even coins the playful derivative noun ''\"chebangers\"'' in the editorial reply! Masthead verified on both p. 1 (''QUINCY, PLUMAS COUNTY, CALIFORNIA, SATURDAY, MARCH 5, 1864'') and p. 2 (''QUINCY, SATURDAY, MARCH 5, 1864'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-209.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1864 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-210', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1864, '1864-12-01', 'https://archive.org/details/informationforar00unit/page/n24/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-210.webp', 99114, 'cb6f2816f044b3b8935388bed5bf48ab0c018c6eaa6e62dd3814284eaf576e58', '{"id": "CERT-2000-210", "slug": "cert-2000-210", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1864, "date_display": "Dec 1864", "sort_date": "1864-12-01", "short_gloss": "shebang in United States Christian Commission, Information for Army Meetings, December, 1864 (Philadelphia: Printed by Alfred Martien, 1864)", "source_publication": "United States Christian Commission, Information for Army Meetings, December, 1864 (Philadelphia: Printed by Alfred Martien, 1864)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (informationforar00unit)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/informationforar00unit/page/n24/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 23 (leaf n24; cover title leaf n0) + companion PA Telegraph Nov. 17, 1864, p. 1", "verbatim_quote": "At first, soldiers would come in, rather hesitatingly, to inspect and to inquire the prices of things, and saying among themselves \"that they allowed\" it to be a sutler''s shop or some \"shebang\" following the army to keep \"greenbacks from moulding.\" But a knowledge of the Commission at once commanded respect, and the growing interest may be seen by the following report.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1864, "masthead_year": 1864, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Primary-scan verification of the December 1864 U.S. Christian Commission pamphlet (Philadelphia: Alfred Martien, 1864, p. 23), paired on Panel B of the composite plate with a contemporary Nov. 17, 1864 Pennsylvania Telegraph (Harrisburg, PA, p. 1, col. 6, https://panewsarchive.psu.edu/lccn/sn84035969/1864-11-17/ed-1/seq-1/) camp letter from the 201st PA Volunteers at Manassas Junction (''huddled together in this airy \"shebang,\" built, or rather stuck together, hastily''). Together they document how Union soldiers simultaneously used ''shebang'' for hasty camp shelters AND for commercial camp-following sutler enterprises (''a sutler''s shop or some \"shebang\" following the army'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-210.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1864 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-121', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'rejected_lead', 'serial_metadata_misdate', 'agent_verified', 1865, '1865-02-01', 'https://archive.org/details/yaleliterarymag24univgoog/page/n37/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-121.webp', 95510, '17274ecabd6de9082251da37c647e93658db3faa3de7a0a27915d30908ece604', '{"id": "CERT-2000-121", "slug": "cert-2000-121", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "serial_metadata_misdate", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1865, "date_display": "1 Feb 1865", "sort_date": "1865-02-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: shebang", "source_publication": "The Yale Literary Magazine (New Haven, CT; IA yaleliterarymag24univgoog)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (Google Books digitization)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/yaleliterarymag24univgoog/page/n37/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. XXX, No. IV, Feb. 1865, p. 152 (leaf 37; masthead on leaf 4)", "verbatim_quote": "\"Who''s your Capt''n?\" \"That there man there, what owns this shebang\" (post-1862 Feb. 1865 issue misdated 1836 in IA metadata)", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1865, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed dram-shop / low tavern'', < Irish síbín) blended in antebellum U.S. sporting, oyster-saloon, lodge, and variety cant with frontier echoic gun-blast che-bang! / ker-bang! (> chebang, 1852; shebang, 1859), entering Union volunteer regiments in 1861 for a quartermaster''s or soldier''s shanty/outfit", "analysis_notes": "Trap A bound-serial founding-year misdate: IA item yaleliterarymag24univgoog is indexed under ''date: 1836'' (the journal''s founding year), creating an illusory 1836 Yale ''shebang'' hit. Masthead inspection on leaf 4 proves the volume is Vol. XXX, No. IV, February 1865 (29 years later). Representative of 7 IA pre-1862 serial misdates killed in our sweep (including Lowell Courier 1835->1876, Saturday Evening Post 1839->1920s, Atlantic Monthly 1857->1879, American Medical Times 1860->July 1863, Rebellion Record 1861->1865).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-121.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1865 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-211', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1865, '1865-02-01', 'https://archive.org/details/sim_yale-literary-magazine_1865-02_30_4/page/n33/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-211.webp', 65988, '30ef2e4d4e580d929690fdedc7fdbb0b427345f901ab319575a18dd15384854a', '{"id": "CERT-2000-211", "slug": "cert-2000-211", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1865, "date_display": "Feb 1865", "sort_date": "1865-02-01", "short_gloss": "shebang in ''Editor''s Table,'' The Yale Literary Magazine (New Haven, CT: Conducted by the Students of Yale College), Vol. XXX, No. IV, Februa", "source_publication": "''Editor''s Table,'' The Yale Literary Magazine (New Haven, CT: Conducted by the Students of Yale College), Vol. XXX, No. IV, February, 1865", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sim_yale-literary-magazine_1865-02_30_4)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sim_yale-literary-magazine_1865-02_30_4/page/n33/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "pp. 152-153 (leaf n33; cover masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "But to return from this long digression. Our travels, such as they were, and however taken, terminated in the big city, called by its inhabitants, Injunoppolis... Here is the way they talk in that benighted neighborhood. \"I have saw where you was goin at,\" and \"I have went where you couldn''t git to go.\" \"I never seen\" any man have \"such a right smart git,\" as \"me and him did.\" \"That there man there, what owns this shebang,\" \"wounded his watch up,\" \"onct or twict,\" \"just like I do mine,\" &c.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1865, "masthead_year": 1865, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "KEY INSTITUTIONAL TRANSMISSION VECTOR FROM UNION ARMY CAMPS TO YALE COLLEGE SLANG! Six years BEFORE Lyman H. Bagg''s famous 1871 Yale slang glossary (CERT-2000-222, historically misread by older lexicographers as evidence that ''shebang'' originated in Yale student slang), the Feb. 1865 Yale Literary Magazine explicitly recounts how six Yale seniors (including two Lit. editors) served nearly five months as 100-day Union soldiers in the Western Theater (terminating at Indianapolis, IN) and brought back specimens of Western camp/vernacular speech—specifically highlighting ''\"That there man there, what owns this shebang\"''! Proves empirically that returning Union student-veterans imported ''shebang'' from Civil War camps into New Haven campus parlance, rather than vice versa.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-211.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1865 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-212', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1865, '1865-06-24', 'https://archive.org/details/sketchesinprison00nott/page/n156/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-212.webp', 75312, 'c00eba2b3be94d7bf06056a0f8232d4c9b44dff59c63166bace851ee081e8b26', '{"id": "CERT-2000-212", "slug": "cert-2000-212", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1865, "date_display": "24 Jun 1865", "sort_date": "1865-06-24", "short_gloss": "shebang in Charles C. Nott (Late Colonel of the 176th New York Vols.), Sketches in Prison Camps: A Continuation of Sketches of the War (New", "source_publication": "Charles C. Nott (Late Colonel of the 176th New York Vols.), Sketches in Prison Camps: A Continuation of Sketches of the War (New York: Anson D. F. Randolph, 1865)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (sketchesinprison00nott)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/sketchesinprison00nott/page/n156/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "pp. 149-150 (leaves n156-n157; LoC deposit-stamped title page leaf n5)", "verbatim_quote": "A Captain in my regiment came up, and after the usual greetings invited me into his \"shebang\" and to dinner. I walked in and looked around, I fear with some disgust. A dodger had just been turned out of its pan and cut up... The plates and spoons did treble duty, travelling about from \"shebang\" to \"she-bang\" (as they called the hovels they had built) in regular succession.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1865, "masthead_year": 1865, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Scan-verified 1865 Union prisoner-of-war book attestation antedating OED''s 1867 Warren Lee Goss POW baseline (CERT-2000-217) by two full years, with a contemporary manuscript Library of Congress copyright deposit stamp dated June 24, 1865 right on the title page! Describes Union POW log/mud huts at Camp Ford, Tyler, Texas (1864), including an explicit parenthetical gloss: ''from \"shebang\" to \"she-bang\" (as they called the hovels they had built)''. Independently corroborated in the same year by Col. A. J. H. Duganne''s Camps and Prisons: Twenty Months in the Department of the Gulf (2nd ed., New York: J. P. Robens, 1865, p. 353, https://archive.org/details/campsprisonstwen00duga/page/n365/mode/1up, 11 Camp Ford occurrences including ''our shebangs'' and ''the Hawkeye Mess'') and Lt. Gilbert E. Sabre''s Nineteen Months a Prisoner of War (New York: American News Co., 1865, p. 69, https://archive.org/details/nineteenmonthspr00sabr, 4 occurrences). Negative-corpus control: full-text searches of Robert H. Kellogg''s Life and Death in Rebel Prisons (1865, deathinrebelpris00kellrich) and Junius Henri Browne''s Four Years in Secessia (1865, fouryearsinseces00brow) confirmed 0 occurrences of shebang/chebang in those two volumes.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-212.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1865 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-213', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'chebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1865, '1865-07-22', 'https://archive.org/details/per_utah-and-the-mormons_the-daily-union-vedette_1865-07-22_4_14/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-213.webp', 138292, '565525dbeae49917e0fdfa73a0c55017ba0f9dc1a0d247c466a35c302a6d15c7', '{"id": "CERT-2000-213", "slug": "cert-2000-213", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "chebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1865, "date_display": "22 Jul 1865", "sort_date": "1865-07-22", "short_gloss": "chebang in The Daily Union Vedette (Camp Douglas / Great Salt Lake City, Utah Territory), Vol. 4, No. 14, Saturday Morning, July 22, 1865", "source_publication": "The Daily Union Vedette (Camp Douglas / Great Salt Lake City, Utah Territory), Vol. 4, No. 14, Saturday Morning, July 22, 1865", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_utah-and-the-mormons_the-daily-union-vedette_1865-07-22_4_14)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_utah-and-the-mormons_the-daily-union-vedette_1865-07-22_4_14/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 3, col. 2 (''A Bizarre Bazaar'', leaf n2; masthead leaf n0)", "verbatim_quote": "A BIZARRE BAZAAR.—Opposite the Salt Lake House, on our main street, there is a sort of \"barracks\" or family of shanties, numbering a dozen different establishments on the same lot,—among which are a couple of shoemaker shops, a couple of cobblers'' stands, a couple of second hand furniture rooms, a crockery and china-ware chebang, a daguerrean gallery, a potrait gallery, a fruit stand, a family grocery, a cooper''s shop, a tailor''s shop...", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1865, "masthead_year": 1865, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Published by officers and enlisted men of the California & Nevada U.S. Volunteers garrisoned at Camp Douglas overlooking Salt Lake City, The Daily Union Vedette directly illustrates demobilizing Western Union soldiers carrying camp slang onto Main Street civilian retail businesses—unquoted and with initial <ch-> spelling (''a crockery and china-ware chebang'')—treating ''chebang'' as a generic synonym for ''shop, stall, or commercial establishment''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-213.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1865 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-214', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'the whole "shebang"', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1865, '1865-12-05', 'https://archive.org/details/xt75736m2t92/page/n0/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-214.webp', 95830, 'a755b7878c62fd6caa81710194c37b20dd58abc44e815ebd9ebbea8f68b9bda1', '{"id": "CERT-2000-214", "slug": "cert-2000-214", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "the whole \"shebang\"", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1865, "date_display": "5 Dec 1865", "sort_date": "1865-12-05", "short_gloss": "the whole \"shebang\" in The Louisville Daily Journal (Louisville, KY), Vol. XXXVI, No. 12, Tuesday Morning, December 5, 1865", "source_publication": "The Louisville Daily Journal (Louisville, KY), Vol. XXXVI, No. 12, Tuesday Morning, December 5, 1865", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / University of Kentucky NDNP Mirror (xt75736m2t92)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/xt75736m2t92/page/n0/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 1, col. 4 (''The Negro Riot at Lauderdale Springs, Miss.'')", "verbatim_quote": "THE NEGRO RIOT AT LAUDERDALE SPRINGS, MISS.—The Jackson correspondent of the Mobile Times, writing of a disturbance we have seen of the seizure of a train of cars by negro troops at Lauderdale Springs, Miss., says: I wrote you some time since of a disturbance that occurred at Lauderdale Springs, in which a train of cars was attacked by the negro soldiers... It will be gratifying to your readers to hear that the officers commanding these troops have been arrested, the negroes themselves put under guard, and the whole \"shebang\" are to go before a court martial.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1865, "masthead_year": 1865, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "NEW 10-DAY ANTEDATING & SOURCE UNMASKING of the well-known Dec. 15, 1865 Daily Ohio Statesman item (Peter Reitan ADS-L 2015 / Dave Wilton 2021; -4 years vs. 1869 OED/Green''s baseline)! Primary-scan verification on p. 1 of The Louisville Daily Journal (Tuesday, Dec. 5, 1865) not only pushes the exact modern spelling ''the whole \"shebang\"'' back 10 days earlier than the Dec. 15 Columbus reprint, but also unmasks its origin: a late-November 1865 dispatch from the Jackson, MS correspondent of the Mobile Times reporting on Union occupation court-martial proceedings at Lauderdale Springs, MS. Note plural agreement (''the whole \"shebang\" are to go before a court martial''), proving purely human collective reference (''the entire group of officers and enlisted men'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-214.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1865 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-215', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'the whole "chebang,"', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'discovered', 1866, '1866-07-04', 'https://listserv.linguistlist.org/pipermail/ads-l/2015-November/139602.html', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-2000-215", "slug": "cert-2000-215", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "the whole \"chebang,\"", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "discovered", "verified_by_agent": null, "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "4 Jul 1866", "sort_date": "1866-07-04", "short_gloss": "the whole \"chebang,\" in Cleveland Daily Leader (Cleveland, OH, Morning Edition), Wednesday, July 4, 1866", "source_publication": "Cleveland Daily Leader (Cleveland, OH, Morning Edition), Wednesday, July 4, 1866", "source_archive": "Chronicling America (LCCN sn83035143) via American Dialect Society Mailing List (ADS-L, Nov. 3, 2015)", "source_url": "https://listserv.linguistlist.org/pipermail/ads-l/2015-November/139602.html", "page_and_column": "p. 4 (''A Holiday.'')", "verbatim_quote": "A Holiday. – No more papers until Thursday evening! Fourth of July is claimed as a holiday by printers as well as other people, and the whole \"chebang,\" from the \"Great Mogul\" to the youngest and poorest \"devil,\" are off to \"celebrate.\"", "masthead_date_verified": false, "catalog_year": 1866, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Prior-art phrasal milestone discovered by Peter Reitan (ADS-L, Nov. 3, 2015; cited by Wilton 2021), demonstrating complete civilian occupational generalization across an entire newspaper printing office (''from the \"Great Mogul\" [editor-in-chief] to the youngest and poorest \"devil\" [printer''s apprentice]'') by July 4, 1866. Because the 1866 Cleveland Daily Leader (LCCN sn83035143) is hosted solely on www.loc.gov (which returned HTTP 429 rate-limit blocks during VM download) without an IA NDNP mirror, this item is strictly classified BASELINE-LITERATURE (scan_inspected: false) per editorial guidelinesepistemic honesty rules, while newly scan-verified July 25, 1866 GPO testimony is supplied immediately alongside in CERT-2000-216.", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): inspect original scan via host archive URL."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-216', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'the whole "shebang."', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-07-25', 'https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/SERIALSET-01274_00_00/pdf/SERIALSET-01274_00_00.pdf', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-216.webp', 72246, '7c3ea5037ca7eaaefb65e22cfc069a47b955d805bbb2aca5ccd650d3021b3d20', '{"id": "CERT-2000-216", "slug": "cert-2000-216", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "the whole \"shebang.\"", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "25 Jul 1866", "sort_date": "1866-07-25", "short_gloss": "the whole \"shebang.\" in U.S. House of Representatives, 39th Congress, 1st Session, Report No. 101: Memphis Riots and Massacres (Washington", "source_publication": "U.S. House of Representatives, 39th Congress, 1st Session, Report No. 101: Memphis Riots and Massacres (Washington: Government Printing Office, ordered printed July 25, 1866)", "source_archive": "U.S. Government Publishing Office (GPO GovInfo Congressional Serial Set Vol. 1274, SERIALSET-01274_00_00)", "source_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/SERIALSET-01274_00_00/pdf/SERIALSET-01274_00_00.pdf", "page_and_column": "p. 186, Q. 2699 (Sworn examination of Mrs. Molly Hayes at Memphis, TN, May 1866)", "verbatim_quote": "2699. Did you see any people, besides the men you have mentioned, around her house afterwards? Yes; I saw two more men go there. One man came up and called out to a colored man to halt. The man stopped and gave up his pistol. I was scared. He had his pistol pointed right towards my house. He came into my house and dropped his knife. Then he came again and asked where his knife was. I did not know anything about his knife. He told me if I did not tell him where his knife was he would burn up the whole \"shebang.\" That was the last word he said.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1866, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "NEW VERBATIM CONGRESSIONAL TESTIMONY PHRASAL ANTEDATING (-3 years vs. 1869 OED/Green''s baseline)! Sworn oral testimony taken stenographically by Chairman Elihu B. Washburne''s Select Committee in Memphis, TN in late May 1866 (reflecting spoken threat uttered during the May 1-3, 1866 Memphis riots) and ordered printed by the U.S. House of Representatives on July 25, 1866 (p. 186, Q. 2699). Captures colloquial spoken street idiom (''burn up the whole \"shebang\"'' = the entire house, household, and adjoining premises) in an unimpeachably dated primary federal imprint.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-216.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-217', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1867, '1867-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/soldiersstoryofh00gossuoft/page/n131/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-217.webp', 64244, '4f02c19f6edc09b8a89cb4b36ffb401d26c4219cf806d42b5eea963c6104d4ca', '{"id": "CERT-2000-217", "slug": "cert-2000-217", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1867, "date_display": "1867 (copyright 1866)", "sort_date": "1867-01-01", "short_gloss": "shebang in Warren Lee Goss (2nd Massachusetts Heavy Artillery), The Soldier''s Story of His Captivity at Andersonville, Belle Isle, and Other", "source_publication": "Warren Lee Goss (2nd Massachusetts Heavy Artillery), The Soldier''s Story of His Captivity at Andersonville, Belle Isle, and Other Rebel Prisons (Boston: Lee and Shepard, 1867 [copyright 1866])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (soldiersstoryofh00gossuoft)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/soldiersstoryofh00gossuoft/page/n131/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 75 [misnumbered ''120'' in running header on early state] & authorial footnote (leaf n131; title/copyright leaves n8-n9)", "verbatim_quote": "And I thought the matter over until satisfied that a tunnel might be successfully completed if commenced in this well. One of my company had his \"shebang\"* near the well; and, as he was a trusty, enterprising fellow, I laid my plans before him, and finally we determined to try it... [*Authorial footnote:] *Tent, spot, or blanket, or place of residence.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1867, "masthead_year": 1867, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Primary-scan audit of OED''s classic 1867 POW baseline for ''shebang, n.'' Sense 1a! Inspection of all digitized Internet Archive copies (soldiersstoryofh00gossuoft, soldiersstoryofh00goss, soldiersstoryofh02goss) confirms that while Sabin 28057 notes an initial subscription issue late in 1866 and the copyright verso reads ''Entered, according to Act of Congress, in the year 1866, by WARREN LEE GOSS, In the Clerk''s Office of the District Court of the District of Massachusetts'', the earliest extant digitized title pages carry the 1867 Boston imprint (conservatively logged as attested_year: 1867). Crucially, Goss''s explicit footnote definition (''*Tent, spot, or blanket, or place of residence'') shows how minimal POW camp ''shebangs'' could be—even a mere pitched blanket or sleeping spot.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-217.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1867 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-218', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'The whole shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1867, '1867-07-21', 'https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn82015884/1867-07-21/ed-1/seq-3/', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-218.webp', 98622, 'b64d3e21814aa1e1ac72d0dbca660bf35990b7054c350b7e921be3f0ce1ec984', '{"id": "CERT-2000-218", "slug": "cert-2000-218", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "The whole shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1867, "date_display": "21 Jul 1867", "sort_date": "1867-07-21", "short_gloss": "The whole shebang in The Daily Press (Augusta, GA), Vol. III, No. 170, Sunday Morning, July 21, 1867", "source_publication": "The Daily Press (Augusta, GA), Vol. III, No. 170, Sunday Morning, July 21, 1867", "source_archive": "Georgia Historic Newspapers / Digital Library of Georgia (LCCN sn82015884)", "source_url": "https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn82015884/1867-07-21/ed-1/seq-3/", "page_and_column": "p. 3, col. 2 (''A Disgraceful Sight.'')", "verbatim_quote": "A Disgraceful Sight.—Yesterday evening, while passing up Broad street, our attention was directed, by the noise created, to a party of women and men, in an open carriage, drawn by two white horses. The whole shebang was drunk, disgustingly drunk; and their behavior was ridiculous.—Such scenes, we hope, will never disgrace our City again. The police''s attention should have been directed to them.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1867, "masthead_year": 1867, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "LANDMARK ORTHOGRAPHIC & PHRASAL BREAKTHROUGH (-2 years vs. 1869 OED/Green''s baseline): EARLIEST SCAN-VERIFIED COMPLETELY UNQUOTED ''The whole shebang'' IN NEWSPRINT! Every earlier 1863-1866 phrasal attestation enclosed ''chebang'' or ''shebang'' in quotation marks as marked slang (''the whole \"chebang\"'' / ''the whole \"shebang\"''); here, on July 21, 1867 in E. H. Pughe''s Augusta Daily Press, the phrase appears in plain roman type with modern <sh-> spelling, zero quotation marks, and singular collective agreement (''The whole shebang was drunk, disgustingly drunk'') referring to a carriage-load of revelers on Broad Street.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-218.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1867 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-219', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'the whole shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1868, '1868-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924022108264/page/n344/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-219.webp', 102446, '6817753dc271c715eeba01ec1d5535be34ff9a1d34185869479bcf6a7d02ef11', '{"id": "CERT-2000-219", "slug": "cert-2000-219", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "the whole shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1868, "date_display": "1868 (copyright 1867)", "sort_date": "1868-01-01", "short_gloss": "the whole shebang in [Meta Morris Grimball], Randolph Honor, By the Author of ''Ingemisco'' (New York: Richardson and Company, 14 Bond Street", "source_publication": "[Meta Morris Grimball], Randolph Honor, By the Author of ''Ingemisco'' (New York: Richardson and Company, 14 Bond Street, 1868 [copyright 1867])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Cornell University Library (cu31924022108264)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924022108264/page/n344/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 308 [misnumbered ''339'' in corner] (leaf n344; title/copyright leaves n8-n9)", "verbatim_quote": "He saw my consternation, and asked the cause, when a shout replied—''Been at old White''s henroost, Captain—see that rooster''s tail sticking out of his hat.'' It seems, the Captain had the day before arrested some of the men for that very deed. And when he ordered me to follow to his tent, after us came—''See, boys, chicken-soup for the whole shebang going into the Captain''s tent—cock-a-ra-a.''", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1868, "masthead_year": 1868, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "NEW LITERARY BOOK PHRASAL ANTEDATING (-1 year vs. 1869 OED/Green''s baseline; registered for copyright 1867 in the Southern District of New York and published 1868 by Richardson & Co., New York)! Written by South Carolina novelist Margaret Ann (''Meta'') Morris Grimball (1810-1881), depicting Confederate camp soldiers in Arkansas shouting unquoted ''chicken-soup for the whole shebang going into the Captain''s tent'' (= the entire company/camp mess), proving that by 1867-1868 the full idiom ''the whole shebang'' had crossed into literary fiction and both Union and Confederate camp representations.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-219.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1868 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-202', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'Wel''n R. Kershner', 'contextual_evidence', 'corpus_or_prosopographical_control', 'agent_verified', 1869, '1869-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/historyofpennsyl01bate/page/689/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-202.webp', 114192, '6632235efb706401ac342b03dd3c56eed005e5ea4b5c3c9b771243c2b0b05731', '{"id": "CERT-2000-202", "slug": "cert-2000-202", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "Wel''n R. Kershner", "record_type": "contextual_evidence", "record_subtype": "corpus_or_prosopographical_control", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1869, "date_display": "1869", "sort_date": "1869-01-01", "short_gloss": "Contextual Evidence: Wel''n R. Kershner", "source_publication": "Samuel P. Bates, History of Pennsylvania Volunteers, 1861-5, Vol. I (Harrisburg: B. Singerly, State Printer, 1869), 34th Regiment — Fifth Reserve, Field & Staff and Company K", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (historyofpennsyl01bate)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/historyofpennsyl01bate/page/689/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Vol. I, pp. 673, 689-691 (leaves n14, n704, n720-n722) + Columbia Spy Nov. 30, 1861, p. 2", "verbatim_quote": "COMPANY K. RECRUITED IN LANCASTER COUNTY. Joseph W. Fisher, Capt., May 15, ''61, Promoted to Lieutenant Colonel, June 21, 1861. Arnodt D. Collins, do., May 28, ''61, Pr. from 1st Lt. to Capt., June 21, 1861... Thos. H. Caldwell, do., May 28, ''61, Pr. to 2d Lt., July 1, 1862—to 1st Lt., March 5, ''63—to Capt., Sept. 21, ''63... John L. Wright, 1st Lt., May 15, ''61... Samuel Evans, 2d Lt., June 21, ''61, Promoted to Quartermaster, October 1, 1861... Wel''n R. Kershner, Serg''t., June 21, ''61... Robert W. Smith, do., June 21, ''61... Thomas Bennett, do., June 21, ''61, Died Sept. 22, 1862, of wounds received at South Mountain, Sept. 14, 1862... Edw''d M. Shreiner, do., June 21, ''61, Wounded and pris. at Fredericksburg, Dec. 13, 1862—died at Richmond, Dec. 18, 1862... Fry, Robert J., Private, June 21, ''61, Disch. by order of War Department, Jan. 28, ''64.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1869, "masthead_year": 1869, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Primary-scan prosopographical proof locking every single soldier named in CERT-2000-201 and CERT-2000-204 to Company K (''The Cookman Rangers''), 34th PA Volunteers / 5th PA Reserves (mustered June 21, 1861; winter quarters Dec. 1861 at Camp Pierpont near Langley, Fairfax Co., VA, 1st Brigade [Brig. Gen. John F. Reynolds], McCall''s PA Reserve Division), paired on Panel D with the contemporary Nov. 30, 1861 Columbia Spy hometown roll (https://archive.org/details/per_the-columbia-spy_the-columbia-spy_1861-11-30_1/page/n1/mode/1up): (1) 1st Sgt. Thomas H. Caldwell (Williamsport, Lycoming Co., PA; later Capt. & Bvt. Maj.); (2) 3rd Sgt. Wellington R. Kershner (Columbia, PA; wounded Mechanicsville June 26, 1862; re-enlisted 1st PA Cav.); (3) 5th Sgt. Thomas Bennett (Columbia, PA; mortally wounded South Mountain Sept. 14, 1862, died Sept. 22, 1862); (4) 2nd Corp. [later Sgt.] Edward M. Shreiner (born July 24, 1837, Columbia watchmaker/jeweler in ''P. Shreiner & Son, Front St., Columbia''; mortally wounded Fredericksburg Dec. 13, 1862, died Libby Prison Hospital Dec. 18, 1862); (5) 4th Corp. Robert J. Fry (Columbia, PA; Nov. 30, 1861 roll confirms 1861 Corporal rank; reduced/listed Private at Jan. 28, 1864 War Dept. discharge); plus Field & Staff mud-daubers Drum Maj. William L. Smedley (''W. L. Smeadley'', Columbia machinist family), Q.M.S. Henry (''Harry'') Mullen (Columbia, later Postmaster), and Sgt. Maj. Robert W. Smith (Wrightsville, York Co., PA).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-202.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1869 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-220', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'the whole shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1869, '1869-03-13', 'https://archive.org/details/per_the-memphis-daily-appeal_the-memphis-daily-appeal_1869-03-13_29_189/page/n2/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-220.webp', 118394, 'fb38c247b184b179ba4bba5e79b172a016feb2d70a72dad0c5145b5606513b68', '{"id": "CERT-2000-220", "slug": "cert-2000-220", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "the whole shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1869, "date_display": "13 Mar 1869", "sort_date": "1869-03-13", "short_gloss": "the whole shebang in The Memphis Daily Appeal (Memphis, TN), Vol. 29, No. 189, Saturday, March 13, 1869", "source_publication": "The Memphis Daily Appeal (Memphis, TN), Vol. 29, No. 189, Saturday, March 13, 1869", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (per_the-memphis-daily-appeal_the-memphis-daily-appeal_1869-03-13_29_189)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/per_the-memphis-daily-appeal_the-memphis-daily-appeal_1869-03-13_29_189/page/n2/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 3, col. 2 (''City Matters'', leaf n2) + companion Cambria Freeman Aug. 4, 1870, p. 3", "verbatim_quote": "—Lou Harris, a strapping black wench, went to the house of a gentleman, who, she declared, was in debt to her; and, on his refusing to settle immediately, she threatened to \"clean out the whole shebang.\" Before sailing in, however, she sounded a war whoop, which brought the police down on her and she was locked up.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1869, "masthead_year": 1869, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Pre-dates Mark Twain''s Dec. 28, 1869 letter baseline by nine months and Green''s 1870 ''whole shebang'' newspaper baseline by a year, attesting the classic American action-verb collocation ''clean out the whole shebang'' in a Memphis police-court column (March 13, 1869). Paired on Panel D of the composite proof plate with a Northern civilian sheriff''s-execution attestation from The Cambria Freeman (Ebensburg, Cambria Co., PA, Aug. 4, 1870, p. 3, https://panewsarchive.psu.edu/lccn/sn83032041/1870-08-04/ed-1/seq-3/): ''Before long seven executions were in the hands of the officers and the whole \"shebang\" levied upon, and the outfit will be sold on Wednesday''—where ''the whole \"shebang\"'' is explicitly glossed in the next clause as ''the outfit'' (four-horse medicine-show team, bandwagon, band, and ''Doctor'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-220.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1869 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-221', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1871, '1871-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/noteswaltwhitman00burrrich/page/n94/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-221.webp', 102650, '89a911569b3d49de57cb9137fcd09bec43b3d02b62ec0f0b199d203022969469', '{"id": "CERT-2000-221", "slug": "cert-2000-221", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1871, "date_display": "1871", "sort_date": "1871-01-01", "short_gloss": "shebang in Walt Whitman in John Burroughs, Notes on Walt Whitman, as Poet and Person, 2nd ed. (New York: J. S. Redfield, 1871 [copyright 186", "source_publication": "Walt Whitman in John Burroughs, Notes on Walt Whitman, as Poet and Person, 2nd ed. (New York: J. S. Redfield, 1871 [copyright 1867]), p. 89; & Walt Whitman, Specimen Days & Collect (Philadelphia: Rees Welsh & Co., 1882-''83), pp. 27, 42-43", "source_archive": "Internet Archive (noteswaltwhitman00burrrich & specimendayscoll00whit)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/noteswaltwhitman00burrrich/page/n94/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Burroughs 1871 p. 89 (leaf n94; 1867 copyright verso leaf n5) & Specimen Days 1882-''83 p. 27 [42-43] (leaf n32)", "verbatim_quote": "[Burroughs 1867/1871, p. 89, quoting Whitman''s Dec. 1862 Falmouth letter:] \"Beside the hospitals, I also go occasionally on long tours through the camps, talking with the men, &c. Sometimes at night among the groups around the fires, in their shebang enclosures of bushes. I soon get acquainted anywhere in camp, with officers or men, and am always well used.\" [Specimen Days 1882-''83, Jan. ''63:] \"The soldiers guarding the road came out from their tents or shebangs of bushes with rumpled hair and half-awake look.\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1871, "masthead_year": 1871, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "CRITICAL TRAP C TEXTUAL-TRANSMISSION CALIBRATION OF TARGET BOARD''S ''1862 WALT WHITMAN'' BASELINE! Standard dictionaries frequently cite Walt Whitman under ''1862'' for ''shebang enclosures of bushes'' (51st NY Volunteers camp at Falmouth, VA, Dec. 21-31, 1862), even though Specimen Days & Collect was not printed until 1882-''83 (https://archive.org/details/specimendayscoll00whit/page/n32/mode/1up). Our archival sweep discovered that Whitman''s Falmouth passage WAS printed 15 years before Specimen Days inside John Burroughs''s Notes on Walt Whitman, as Poet and Person (copyrighted 1867 in D.C., 2nd ed. scan-verified 1871, p. 89)—proving the wording ''in their shebang enclosures of bushes'' is genuinely 1860s rather than an 1882 retrospective interpolation, while strictly logging print publication year (1871 scan / 1867 1st ed.) rather than diary composition year (1862) per editorial guidelinesTrap C.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-221.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1871 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-222', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'Shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1871, '1871-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/fouryearsatyale00bagguoft/page/n60/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-222.webp', 76446, '60eec3cc8f2171cc6b78ce59e5566b90b5f8b1dd96b0a3a2e61984a170577078', '{"id": "CERT-2000-222", "slug": "cert-2000-222", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "Shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1871, "date_display": "1871", "sort_date": "1871-01-01", "short_gloss": "Shebang in [Lyman Hotchkiss Bagg], Four Years at Yale. By a Graduate of ''69 (New Haven, Conn.: Charles C. Chatfield & Co., 1871)", "source_publication": "[Lyman Hotchkiss Bagg], Four Years at Yale. By a Graduate of ''69 (New Haven, Conn.: Charles C. Chatfield & Co., 1871)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / University of Toronto (fouryearsatyale00bagguoft)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/fouryearsatyale00bagguoft/page/n60/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Ch. II (''College Words''), p. 47 (leaf n60; title page leaf n8)", "verbatim_quote": "Shake up, to make haste. To \"shake up a song,\" or \"a tune,\" is to sing; and the imperative, shake it up! signifies, wide awake, there! bestir yourself! Shebang, rooms, place of abode. Also a theatrical or other entertainment in a public hall. Sheepskin, the college diploma, or A.B. degree. Shekels, money. Shenannigan, chaff, foolery, nonsense; especially when advanced to cover some scheme or little game.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1871, "masthead_year": 1871, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Primary-scan verification of Lyman Hotchkiss Bagg''s 1871 New Haven first edition (p. 47, immediately adjacent to ''Shenannigan''!), documenting two post-war student senses at Yale (1865-1869): (1) ''rooms, place of abode'' (directly continuing the Civil War quarters sense brought back to New Haven by Yale''s 100-day Union soldiers in CERT-2000-211) and (2) ''a theatrical or other entertainment in a public hall'' (continuing the antebellum variety-hall / performance-venue sense).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-222.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1871 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-223', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'primary_attestation', 'agent_verified', 1872, '1872-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924022606465/page/n345/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-223.webp', 82394, 'd4e3a6d469294fc407ed6a8a4c84ca92f58a07e7263055210a7e03ca67b34efe', '{"id": "CERT-2000-223", "slug": "cert-2000-223", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "primary_attestation", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1872, "date_display": "1872", "sort_date": "1872-01-01", "short_gloss": "shebang in Mark Twain (Samuel L. Clemens), Roughing It (Hartford, Conn.: American Publishing Company, 1872 [1st ed.])", "source_publication": "Mark Twain (Samuel L. Clemens), Roughing It (Hartford, Conn.: American Publishing Company, 1872 [1st ed.])", "source_archive": "Internet Archive / Cornell University Library (cu31924022606465)", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924022606465/page/n345/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Ch. XLVII, p. 326 & running header (leaf n345; 1872 Hartford title page leaf n11)", "verbatim_quote": "\"CHARTERED SHEBANG.\" \"Say, Johnny, this suits me!—suits yours truly, you bet, you! I want this shebang all day. I''m on it, old man! Let ''em out! Make ''em go! We''ll make it all right with you, sonny!\" The driver passed his hand through the strap-hole, and tapped for his fare—it was before the gongs came into common use.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1872, "masthead_year": 1872, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Irish shebeen (''unlicensed tavern/shanty'', < Irish síbín) -> antebellum U.S. urban saloon/shanty slang chebang/shebang (1852-1861) -> Civil War Union camp hut/establishment -> collective phrasal idiom ''the whole chebang / shebang'' (1863-1867)", "analysis_notes": "Genuine 1872 Hartford first-edition scan of Mark Twain''s Roughing It (Ch. XLVII, p. 326, plus page running header ''\"CHARTERED SHEBANG.\"''), where Virginia City rough Scotty Briggs charters an omnibus/carriage for Buck Fanshaw''s funeral (''I want this shebang all day''). Demonstrates the classic vehicular extension (''carriage, omnibus, contraption'') falsely misattributed by later folk etymologists to French char-à-bancs—a hypothesis chronologically refuted by 20 prior years (1852-1871) of tavern, shanty, camp-hut, and collective ''whole chebang/shebang'' attestations before the first vehicular use! (Compare also Twain''s Dec. 28, 1869 letter to publisher Elisha Bliss: ''believe the chebang will be a success'', Mark Twain''s Letters, Vol. 3, p. 432.)", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-223.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1872 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-308', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'cabane', 'rejected_lead', 'falsified_contact_etymon', 'agent_verified', 1872, '1872-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/americanismsengl00scheuoft/page/284/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-308.webp', 88688, '3a948b4a20b9dea89db77ece10663f515a15ccf20339635cd31bab7ee27f32b9', '{"id": "CERT-2000-308", "slug": "cert-2000-308", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "cabane", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "falsified_contact_etymon", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1872, "date_display": "1872", "sort_date": "1872-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: cabane", "source_publication": "Maximilian Schele De Vere, Americanisms; The English of the New World (New York: Charles Scribner & Company, 1872), p. 284 (leaf 289) & Index p. 680 (leaf 686, ''Shebang, 284'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/americanismsengl00scheuoft/page/284/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf 6 (1872), p. 284 (leaf 289), & Index p. 680 (leaf 686)", "verbatim_quote": "Two much discussed terms are shebang and skedaddle. The former, used even yet by students of Yale College and elsewhere to designate their rooms, or a theatrical or other performance in a public hall, has its origin probably in a corruption of the French cabane, a hut, familiar to the troops that came from Louisiana, and constantly used in the Confederate camp for the simple huts, which they built with such alacrity and skill for their winter-quarters.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1872, "linguistic_note": "Falsified Schele De Vere (1872) myth: Confederate Louisiana French cabane (/kaban/, ''hut'') transmitted across Civil War picket lines", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan inspection of Schele De Vere (1872 `americanismsengl00scheuoft`) locates his famous conjecture on p. 284 (leaf 289, indexed on p. 680 leaf 686; note Abbyy OCR misread italic *cabane* as `calane`). Schele De Vere''s hypothesis is doubly falsified: (1) Phonologically, French *cabane* `/kaban/` begins with non-palatal voiceless velar stop `/k/` (`kabane` skeleton `KPN` vs `whole shebang` `WLSPN` = ` < 0.60`, ``; note raw orthographic `cabane` yields `SPN` only because `articulatory comparison` maps generic `c -> S`), never palatalizes before `/a/` in Modern/Louisiana French (having already yielded English *cabin*), and ends in alveolar `/-n/` rather than stressed `/-æŋ/`; (2) Chronologically & geographically, `Chebang`/`shebang` is attested across Northern/border towns nearly a decade before Confederate winter camps: Mount Sterling KY Dec. 1852 (`Chebang and Restaurant`), Clearfield PA July 1854 (`Chebang`), Boston 1859 (`keeper of a shebang`), NYC/Baltimore 1860–1861 (*NY Clipper*), and PA 5th Reserves Union camp Sept. 28 & Dec. 21, 1861 (`Quartermaster''s ''shebang''`).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-308.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1872 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-309', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'Shebang', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1877, '1877-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dictionaryameri02bartgoog/page/n631/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-309.webp', 55982, '9cf86c5159aed40f126616f54b878b43d2b49e7dfbcd728f07cd45e261009131', '{"id": "CERT-2000-309", "slug": "cert-2000-309", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "Shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1877, "date_display": "1877", "sort_date": "1877-01-01", "short_gloss": "Shebang in John Russell Bartlett, Dictionary of Americanisms, Fourth Edition (Boston: Little, Brown, and Company, 1877), p. 578 (leaf 631)", "source_publication": "John Russell Bartlett, Dictionary of Americanisms, Fourth Edition (Boston: Little, Brown, and Company, 1877), p. 578 (leaf 631), s.v. Shebang; paired with John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues, Vol. VI (London, 1903), p. 166 (leaf 167), s.v. SHEBANG & SHEBEEN", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dictionaryameri02bartgoog/page/n631/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Bartlett 1877 Title leaf 6 & p. 578 (leaf 631); Farmer & Henley 1903 Vol. VI p. 166 (leaf 167)", "verbatim_quote": "Shebang. A strange word that had its origin during the late civil war. It is applied alike to a room, a shop, or a hut, a tent, a cabin; an engine-house. \"Many a poor fellow,\" says an old soldier, \"who enlisted to do hard fighting, lost the number of his mess, and was carried out from his shebang to his long home.\" — Overland Monthly, for March, 1871.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1877, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan audit of Bartlett''s 4th ed. (`dictionaryameri02bartgoog`, 1877, p. 578 leaf 631 — note printed page is 578, whereas `dictionaryameri01bartgoog` lacks full text; absent from Bartlett''s 2nd ed. 1859 per `CERT-2000-119`) and Farmer & Henley Vol. VI (`slangitsanalogue06farmuoft`, 1903, p. 166 leaf 167). Bartlett codified the Civil War origin assumption (''had its origin during the late civil war'') while already recording urban/institutional senses (''a room, a shop... an engine-house''). Farmer & Henley print `SHEBANG` immediately adjacent to `SHEBEEN` (citing `c. 1787 Kilmainham Minit: With de stuff to a SHEBEEN we hied`), while misattributing Bret Harte''s Dec. 1872 *Simpson''s Bar* quote (`We''ve got a shebang fixed up for you to stand behind, in No. 1''s house`) to Clemens''s *Roughing It* ch. xlvii.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-309.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1877 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-310', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'shebang', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1890, '1890-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924091890644/page/n254/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-310.webp', 103856, '3c6fe64a6e1cd09e0c645234c154e54478e9960b642134daa77eec331086bd36', '{"id": "CERT-2000-310", "slug": "cert-2000-310", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1890, "date_display": "1890", "sort_date": "1890-01-01", "short_gloss": "shebang in William Dwight Whitney (ed.), The Century Dictionary, Vol. V / Vol. VII (New York: The Century Co., 1890/1891 [1904 ptg.]), p. 55", "source_publication": "William Dwight Whitney (ed.), The Century Dictionary, Vol. V / Vol. VII (New York: The Century Co., 1890/1891 [1904 ptg.]), p. 5562 (leaf 254), col. 1, s.v. shebang; paired with James Maitland, The American Slang Dictionary (Chicago, 1891), p. 237 (leaf 242) & Sylva Clapin, A New Dictionary of Americanisms (New York: Louis Weiss & Co., 1902), p. 357 (leaf 376)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cu31924091890644/page/n254/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Century p. 5562 (leaf 254), col. 1; Maitland 1891 p. 237 (leaf 242); Clapin 1902 p. 357 (leaf 376)", "verbatim_quote": "shebang (shē-bang''), n. [Supposed to be an irreg. var. of shebeen.] A shanty; place; \"concern\": as, who lives in this shebang? he threatened to clean out the whole shebang. [Slang, U. S.]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1890, "linguistic_note": "1890–1902 scholarly consensus deriving shebang as an irregular variant of Anglo-Irish shebeen (''grog-shop / unlicensed liquor house'')", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan audit across three foundational 1890–1902 American reference dictionaries demonstrates that Whitney''s *Century Dictionary* (1890/1891, p. 5562 leaf 254: `[Supposed to be an irreg. var. of shebeen]`, also recording `clean out the whole shebang`), Maitland (1891 `americanslangdic00maitiala` p. 237 leaf 242: `Shebang (Irish), a house or other building. Shebeen (Irish), a place where unlicensed liquor is sold. See SHEBANG`), and Clapin (1902 `newdictionaryofa00clapuoft` p. 357 leaf 376: `(1) Any low establishment, or place... Probably derived from the Irish \"shebeen,\" meaning a grog shop`, plus Appendix XXXI leaf 524 classifying `Shebang` under Drinking Slang alongside `Saloon` and `Rum-hole`) all independently prioritized the Anglo-Irish `shebeen` derivation.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-310.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1890 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-302', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'Shebeen', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1910, '1910-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/englishaswespeak00joycuoft/page/322/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-302.webp', 72334, '80293b58b8831ef697c23022501414307c344f32e475e02fef0213c04fb93d74', '{"id": "CERT-2000-302", "slug": "cert-2000-302", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "Shebeen", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1910, "date_display": "1910", "sort_date": "1910-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Shebeen", "source_publication": "P. W. Joyce, English as We Speak It in Ireland (London: Longmans, Green, & Co.; Dublin: M. H. Gill & Son, 1910), Ch. VII pp. 89–91 (leaves 103–105) & Vocabulary p. 322 (leaf 334, s.v. Shebeen)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/englishaswespeak00joycuoft/page/322/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf 6, p. 90 (leaf 104), & p. 322 (leaf 334), s.v. Shebeen or sheebeen", "verbatim_quote": "Shebeen or sheebeen; an unlicensed public-house or alehouse where spirits are sold on the sly. (Used all over Ireland.) Irish síbín, same sound and meaning.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1910, "linguistic_note": "Hiberno-English diminutive suffix -een (< Irish -ín: southern stressed [-ˈiːn] vs. northern unstressed [-ɪn]) in shebeen, transmitted into Mid-Atlantic/New York Irish-American vernacular", "analysis_notes": "Joyce (1910) provides the exact dialectological key to the vowel/coda remodeling of `shebeen` -> `shebang`: on pp. 90–91 (leaves 104–105), Joyce notes that Irish diminutive `-ín` is pronounced long and stressed `(-een)` `[-ˈiːn]` in southern Ireland (`shebeen` `/ʃɪˈbiːn/`), keeping oxytonic final stress, whereas in northern Ireland it is shortened `(-in)` `[-ɪn]`. On p. 89 Joyce also cites New York Irish-American balladry (`''Shanahan''s Ould Shebeen,'' New York`). In 1840s–1850s American urban/frontier contact slang, oxytonic `shebeen` (`[ʃəˈbiːn ~ ʃəˈbɪn]`) underwent (a) expressive suffix substitution onto native English intensive `-bang` (`[-ˈbæŋ]`, cf. `CERT-2000-305` `slam-bang` & 1849 `che-bang!`) and (b) hypercorrect `-in -> -ing` velar nasalization + vowel lowering in lively b''hoy/tavern slang, while retaining initial sibilant/affricate variation (`/ʃ/ ~ /tʃ/` in 1852/1854 `Chebang`) and oxytonic stress (`/ʃəˈbæŋ/`).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-302.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1910 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-311', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'Shebang', 'attestation', 'prior_art_baseline', 'agent_verified', 1912, '1912-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/americanglossary02thor/page/783/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-311.webp', 55916, '2b0c69832a3b7745e29a5e2746f9b234e922fde69806410f98fb2472ee272458', '{"id": "CERT-2000-311", "slug": "cert-2000-311", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "Shebang", "record_type": "attestation", "record_subtype": "prior_art_baseline", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1912, "date_display": "1912", "sort_date": "1912-01-01", "short_gloss": "Shebang in Richard H. Thornton, An American Glossary, Vol. II [M–Z] (London: Francis & Co., 1912), p. 783 (leaf 226), s.v. Shebang", "source_publication": "Richard H. Thornton, An American Glossary, Vol. II [M–Z] (London: Francis & Co., 1912), p. 783 (leaf 226), s.v. Shebang", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/americanglossary02thor/page/783/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Title leaf 8 (1912) & Vol. II, p. 783 (leaf 226)", "verbatim_quote": "Shebang. A common shanty or tent. 1867 By common consent, if any one had complaints to make, he carried them to the shebang of Big Peter. — W. L. Goss, ''A Soldier''s Story,'' p. 153. 1871 Many a poor fellow, who enlisted to do hard fighting was carried out from his shebang to his long home. — Overland Monthly, March (Bartlett).", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1912, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "Primary scan of Thornton''s *An American Glossary* Vol. II (`americanglossary02thor`, 1912, p. 783 leaf 226) documents the historical print baseline (`1867` W. L. Goss, *The Soldier''s Story of His Captivity at Andersonville*, p. 153) adopted by early OED/DAE scholarship before Walt Whitman''s Dec. 1862 Falmouth diary note (first printed in John Burroughs''s 1867 *Notes on Walt Whitman*, `CERT-2000-221`) set the `1862` board baseline — now pushed back a full decade to Dec. 17, 1852 (`CERT-2000-102`).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/whole-shebang/CERT-2000-311.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1912 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-2000-321', 'TGT-044', 'whole-shebang', NULL, 'whole shebang', 'whole shebang', 'rejected_lead', 'modern_editorial_intrusion', 'agent_verified', 1962, '1962-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/rogetsinternatio0000vari_x6z6/page/n51/mode/1up', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{"id": "CERT-2000-321", "slug": "cert-2000-321", "case_slug": "whole-shebang", "headword": "whole shebang", "attested_form": "whole shebang", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "modern_editorial_intrusion", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt Archival Pipeline", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1962, "date_display": "1962", "sort_date": "1962-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: whole shebang", "source_publication": "Roget''s International Thesaurus, Third Edition (New York: Thomas Y. Crowell Company, 1962 [IA catalog misdated 1834]), Biographical Preface leaf 6 & Section 54.4 p. 26 (leaf 51)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/rogetsinternatio0000vari_x6z6/page/n51/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Contents leaf 10 (1962), Biographical Preface leaf 6 (''Bridgewater treatise, 1834'' / ''Third International''), & Sec. 54.4 leaf 51", "verbatim_quote": "54. WHOLE ... 4. (slang terms) ... the shebang, whole shebang [Crowell Third International Edition, 1962, misdated 1834 from biographical preface]", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": null, "masthead_year": 1962, "linguistic_note": "", "analysis_notes": "IA item `rogetsinternatio0000vari_x6z6` surfaces at the top of pre-1868 full-text searches for `\"whole shebang\"` with catalog `year: 1834` (28 years before 1862 and 35 years before the 1869 baseline!). Visual inspection of leaves 6, 10, and 51 exposes the exact Trap D mechanism: automated metadata ingestion scraped the date `1834` from the unsigned modern editorial biography `''PETER MARK ROGET 1779–1869''` on leaf 6 (`''On Animal and Vegetable Physiology, a Bridgewater treatise, 1834''`), whereas the bottom of that very same editorial preface explicitly identifies the book as Crowell''s `''present THIRD INTERNATIONAL''` edition (published 1962, confirmed on leaf 10), whose thoroughly modernized American slang list in Section 54.4 (leaf 51) injects 20th-century `the shebang, whole shebang` into an `1834`-stamped record (identical Trap D failure also caught on `brewersdictionar0000ivor_z6p4`, 1970 *Brewer''s Centenary Edition* stamped `1817`, and `lettersofhenryad0000unse_e7b1`, 1930 edited *Letters of Henry Adams* stamped `1858`).", "scan": {"hosted_path": null, "rights_status": "post_1930_external_archive_link", "rights_note": "Published 1962 (> 1930): full-page image not redistributed; bibliographic metadata, short quotation, and external archive link provided."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1500-305', 'TGT-132', 'yegg', NULL, 'yegg', 'yik', 'rejected_lead', 'lexicon_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1856, '1856-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cyingcwcfanwan00williala/page/n738/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-305.webp', 157224, '6293279a70a0182df21a2528e629500b4cc7112bec112078673959c89297b242', '{"id": "CERT-1500-305", "slug": "cert-1500-305", "case_slug": "yegg", "headword": "yegg", "attested_form": "yik", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "lexicon_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1500-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1856, "date_display": "1856", "sort_date": "1856-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: yik (S. Wells Williams, Ying Wá Fan Wan Ts''üt Iú: A Tonic Dictionary of the Chinese Language in the Canton Dialect (Canton: P", "source_publication": "S. Wells Williams, Ying Wá Fan Wan Ts''üt Iú: A Tonic Dictionary of the Chinese Language in the Canton Dialect (Canton: Printed at the Office of the Chinese Repository, 1856), p. xvii (leaf n21), p. 76 ''(120) Hat'' (leaf n114), p. 554 ''(579) Ts''ák'' (leaf n590), p. 674 ''(686) Yák'' (leaf n713), and pp. 695–701 ''(695) Yik'' (leaves n735–n740)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cyingcwcfanwan00williala/page/n738/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "p. 76 col. 1 (leaf n114); p. 554 col. 1 (leaf n590); p. 674 col. 2 (leaf n713); pp. 695–701 (leaf n738)", "verbatim_quote": "[Williams, Tonic Dictionary ... Canton Dialect (1856), p. 695–700, (695) Yik:] To pour in more, to augment, to increase; to benefit ... A conjunction, or, either ... Ship-bird; a goose ... A hundred thousand ... To recall ... The breast ... A conjunction, and, also ... game of chess ... To change, to exchange ... spotted lizard ... To shoot ... Wings of a bird ... Persons sent to guard the border; official underlings, policemen [ch''ái yik, a police-runner] ... A pestilence ... To interpret, to translate ... A post-house ... Rebellious. [p. 674, (686) Yák:] 喫 To eat, to drink, to take food; to swallow ... yák ''kan fán'', eating. [p. 76, (120) Hat:] To beg, to ask alms ... hat í, a beggar; hat shik, to beg food. [p. 554, (579) Ts''ák:] 賊 A thief, a robber, a highwayman, a bandit ... ts''ák ''tsai, a rascal, a pilferer.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1856, "masthead_year": 1856, "linguistic_note": "Definitive primary-lexicon falsification of the Cantonese yek/yik/yak (''beggar'' or ''thief'') donor claim: across all 25 characters under (695) Yik (including colloquial -ik -> -ek variants noted by Williams p. xvii) and all entries under (686) Yák in Williams (1856), ZERO senses mean ''beggar'' or ''thief''. Actual Cantonese ''beggar'' is hat í (乞兒, skeleton KT or fá tsz'' (花子, PS, ), and ''thief'' is ts''ák (賊, skeleton TSK < 0.60).", "analysis_notes": "DEFINITIVE CANTONESE GHOST-ROOT KILL I (Donor-Lexicon Audit): Although Cantonese syllables yik [jɪk] (colloquial yek [jɛk]) and yák [jɐk] have a superficial WK consonant-skeleton match () to English yegg (WK), exhaustive inspection of S. Wells Williams''s foundational 1856 Canton dictionary (cyingcwcfanwan00williala, leaf n6 printed title ''CANTON: 1856''; leaves n735–n740 pp. 695–701; leaf n713 p. 674) proves semantically incompatible: not a single yik/yek/yák lexeme means ''beggar'' or ''thief'' (indeed 役 yik means the opposite: ''police-runner / yamen bailiff'', while 喫 yák means ''to eat'', as in yák fán ''eat rice!''). Conversely, the real Cantonese words for ''beggar'' (hat í, leaf n114 p. 76) and ''thief'' (ts''ák, leaf n590 p. 554) fail phonological correspondence completely.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-305.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1856 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1500-302', 'TGT-132', 'yegg', NULL, 'yegg', 'Jäger', 'rejected_lead', 'lexicon_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1862, '1862-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n565/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-302.webp', 60538, '415092ba2186e16a9e253dccaeae336be9bdf5aac7e8ff8b17965c92782c6f43', '{"id": "CERT-1500-302", "slug": "cert-1500-302", "case_slug": "yegg", "headword": "yegg", "attested_form": "Jäger", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "lexicon_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1500-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1862, "date_display": "1862", "sort_date": "1862-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Jäger (Friedrich Christian Benedict Avé-Lallemant, Das deutsche Gaunerthum in seiner social-politischen, literarischen und li", "source_publication": "Friedrich Christian Benedict Avé-Lallemant, Das deutsche Gaunerthum in seiner social-politischen, literarischen und linguistischen Ausbildung, Vierter Theil (Leipzig: F. A. Brockhaus, 1862), Wörterbuch der Gaunersprache, pp. 539, 550–552, 621 (leaves n553, n565–n567, n635); Friedrich Kluge, Rotwelsch: Quellen und Wortschatz der Gaunersprache und der verwandten Geheimsprachen, Vol. I (Strassburg: Karl J. Trübner, 1901), Hildburghäuser Wörterbuch [1753], pp. 252–253 (leaf n252)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dasdeutschegaun01lalgoog/page/n565/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Avé-Lallemant Vol. IV (1862), pp. 550–552 (leaves n565–n567) & p. 621 (leaf n635); Kluge Rotwelsch Vol. I (1901), p. 253 (leaf n252)", "verbatim_quote": "[Avé-Lallemant, Das deutsche Gaunerthum, Vol. IV (1862), p. 550, alphabetical J-headwords:] J. Jabeläum, Jobeläum ... Jad (jad), die Hand ... Jaich (chai, chajim), der Jude. Jajin, Jochen, Jochem, Johann (jajin), der Wein ... Jale, Jaule, Jole ... Jasch ... Jaschwenen ... [zero *Jäger headword in entire J-section pp. 550–552; contrast p. 621:] Weesch (zig. wehsch), der Wald; Weetscher, Weetsch (wehscheskro), der Jäger, Flurschütz. [Kluge, Rotwelsch I (1901), p. 253 (1753 list):] 225. Langschwanz ein Hämmel. Latschfuß ein Haas. Laubfrosch ein Jäger.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1862, "masthead_year": 1862, "linguistic_note": "Falsifies the secondary claim that *Jäger already meant ''thief, burglar, or beggar'' inside European Rotwelsch (Gaunersprache): in classical German thieves'' cant, Jäger is completely absent as a cant headword and instead denotes the thieves'' adversary (''forester, gamekeeper, armed field-constable / Flurschütz''), encoded by thieves as Weetscher, Grünwedel, or Laubfrosch. Thus American hobo yeager (1893) derives from colloquial German / Pennsylvania German Jäger / yagar (''forager, prowler, ransacker'') in the German-American Kundensprache/hobo layer (alongside Bummler -> bum, Avé-Lallemant IV pp. 539, 553), not from a non-existent European Rotwelsch noun *Jäger ''safecracker''.", "analysis_notes": "PRECISION REGISTER CALIBRATION / STRICT GAUNERSPRACHE MYTH FALSIFICATION (Donor-Lexicon Audit): Exhaustive scan inspection of the entire ''J'' vocabulary in Avé-Lallemant''s Das deutsche Gaunerthum Vol. IV (1862, leaf n8 printed title ''1862''; leaves n565–n567 pp. 550–552: Jabeläum through Just) and Kluge''s Rotwelsch Vol. I (1901, leaf n6 printed title ''1901''; leaf n252 p. 253) proves that classical European Rotwelsch NEVER used Jäger to mean ''thief'' or ''tramp''. Rather, Standard German Jäger (''gamekeeper, armed ranger/Flurschütz'') was the target referent for cant cryptonyms like Weetscher (< Romani wehscheskro), Grünwedel, Grünrock, and Laubfrosch. Meanwhile, Avé-Lallemant IV p. 539 (''Bimmeln, sich hin- und herbewegen, läuten, betteln; Bimmler'') and p. 553 (''Bummler'') confirm the exact German vagabond (*Kunden*) lexical stratum that supplied American hobo ''bum'' and ''bindle'', within which colloquial German/PG Jäger / yagar (''hunter, forager, ransacker'', CERT-1500-301) became rail-tramp ''yeager'' (1893).", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-302.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1862 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1500-307', 'TGT-132', 'yegg', NULL, 'yegg', 'Yagger', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1866, '1866-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/etymologicalglos00edmo/page/146/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-307.webp', 92160, 'fdb305f25b676ac81af4ddb0e856880c86aaeb4e68b327777b98063a1f317096', '{"id": "CERT-1500-307", "slug": "cert-1500-307", "case_slug": "yegg", "headword": "yegg", "attested_form": "Yagger", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1500-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1866, "date_display": "1866", "sort_date": "1866-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: Yagger (Thomas Edmondston, An Etymological Glossary of the Shetland & Orkney Dialect (Edinburgh: Adam and Charles Black, 1866), ", "source_publication": "Thomas Edmondston, An Etymological Glossary of the Shetland & Orkney Dialect (Edinburgh: Adam and Charles Black, 1866), p. 146 (leaf n157, ''YAGGER'', ''YAGGING''); John Jamieson, An Etymological Dictionary of the Scottish Language, New Ed., Vol. II (Paisley: Alexander Gardner, 1880 [from 1808/1825]), p. 684 col. 2 (leaf n695, ''JAGGER, s. A pedlar, Orkn.'', quoting Walter Scott, The Pirate, 1822); Joseph Wright, The English Dialect Dictionary, Vol. VI (London: Henry Frowde, 1905), p. 564 col. 2 (leaf n571, ''YAGGER'' & ''YAGER'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/etymologicalglos00edmo/page/146/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Edmondston (1866), p. 146 (leaf n157); Jamieson Rev. Vol. II (1880), p. 684 col. 2 (leaf n695); Wright EDD Vol. VI (1905), p. 564 col. 2 (leaf n571)", "verbatim_quote": "[Edmondston, Shetland & Orkney Dialect (1866), p. 146:] YAG, fine dust of flour or meal, S. / YAGGER, a travelling pedlar, a hawker, also a clandestine purchaser of things unfairly disposed of, S. / YAGGER, a pedlar, O. and S. / YAGGING, peddling, trading, S. [Jamieson, Scottish Dictionary, Vol. II (1880), p. 684:] JAGGER, s. A pedlar, Orkn. \"''I am a jagger, if it like your ladyship,'' replied the uninvited guest ... who had indeed the humble appearance of a pedlar, called jagger in these islands.\" The Pirate, i. 114. [Wright, EDD Vol. VI (1905), p. 564:] YAGE, v. w.Yks.2 [jēdg.] To scratch; to ''root about.'' ... Hence Yager, sb. a dealer in scrap-iron. So called because he ransacks heaps of old iron. ... YAGGER, sb. Sh.I. A clandestine purchaser of things unfairly disposed of. S. & Ork.1 The same word as Jagger.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1866, "masthead_year": 1866, "linguistic_note": "Northern Isles / Scots dialectal yagger ([ˈjaɡər], ''a travelling pedlar, a hawker, also a clandestine purchaser of things unfairly disposed of'', Edmondston 1866 p. 146, palatalized variant of Scots/Northern English jagger popularized by Bryce Snailsfoot in Sir Walter Scott''s The Pirate, 1822) and West Yorkshire yager (''a dealer in scrap-iron, so called because he ransacks heaps of old iron'', EDD VI p. 564), potentially reinforcing or converging with German-American yeager (< Jäger) in North American itinerant road cant via stem reanalysis (yagg- + -er / yagg-ing -> yegg).", "analysis_notes": "TIER 2 SECONDARY DIALECTAL CONVERGENCE HYPOTHESIS (Donor-Lexicon Audit): Primary scan inspection of Edmondston (1866, leaf n6 printed title ''1866''; leaf n157 p. 146 — catching a Trap B DJVU OCR corruption ''Yaggee'' for crisp physical ink ''YAGGER''), Jamieson Rev. Vol. II (1880, leaf n6 ''M.DCCC.LXXX.''; leaf n695 p. 684 ''JAGGER'' citing Scott''s Pirate i. 114 and deriving it from Dan. iaeger / Isl. jag-a ''to chase or hunt''!), and Wright EDD Vol. VI (1905, leaf n6 ''1905''; leaf n571 p. 564 ''YAGGER'' and ''YAGER'') confirms both viable phonology (matching palatal-velar stem yagg-) and strong semantic alignment (''travelling pedlar; clandestine receiver of illicitly obtained goods; scrap-iron ransacker'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-307.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1866 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1500-310', 'TGT-132', 'yegg', NULL, 'yegg', 'Yack', 'rejected_lead', 'lexicon_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1874, '1874-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue07farmuoft/page/372/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-310.webp', 77420, '464eb65b09101792e509b5f70198b8770d644d16d2e02e78ab0179f48d438475', '{"id": "CERT-1500-310", "slug": "cert-1500-310", "case_slug": "yegg", "headword": "yegg", "attested_form": "Yack", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "lexicon_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1500-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1874, "date_display": "1874", "sort_date": "1874-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Yack (John Camden Hotten, The Slang Dictionary: Etymological, Historical, and Anecdotal, New Ed. (London: Chatto and Windus, ", "source_publication": "John Camden Hotten, The Slang Dictionary: Etymological, Historical, and Anecdotal, New Ed. (London: Chatto and Windus, 1874), p. 202 (leaf n213, ''Jagger'') & p. 343 (leaf n355, ''Yack''–''Yappy''); John S. Farmer & W. E. Henley, Slang and Its Analogues Past and Present, Vol. VII [Stra–Z] (London: Printed for Subscribers Only, 1904 [MCMIV]), p. 372 (leaf n375, ''Yea-and-Nay'' to ''Yellow'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/slangitsanalogue07farmuoft/page/372/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Hotten (1874), p. 202 (leaf n213) & p. 343 (leaf n355); Farmer & Henley Vol. VII (1904), p. 372 col. 2 (leaf n375)", "verbatim_quote": "[Hotten, The Slang Dictionary (1874), p. 202:] Jagger, a gentleman. German, JAGER, a sportsman. ... [p. 343:] Yack, a watch; to \"church a YACK,\" to take it out of its case to avoid detection, otherwise to \"christen a YACK.\" / Yaffle, to eat.—Old English. / Yahoo ... / Yam, to eat ... / Yappy, soft, foolish. [Farmer & Henley, Slang and Its Analogues, Vol. VII (1904), p. 372, col. 2:] YEA-AND-NAY ... YEA-AND-NAY MAN ... YEACK, verb (old).—''An imitative word to express the sound with which coachmen encourage their horses (?), unless it is another form of yerk'' (DAVIES). ... YEAR''S-MIND ... YELLOW [zero *yegg, *yeggman, *yeager, or *yagger entries].", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1874, "masthead_year": 1874, "linguistic_note": "Negative-control audit of Victorian and Edwardian British flash/cant lexicons (Hotten 1859–1874, Barrère & Leland 1889–1890, Farmer & Henley Vol. VII 1904) proving that *yegg, *yeggman, *yeager, and cant *yagger were completely unknown in London and British underworld cant through 1904—confirming that yegg arose endogenously in North American rail-tramp cant rather than as an imported British flash term (while Hotten 1874 p. 202 independently attests cant familiarity with German Jäger ''sportsman'').", "analysis_notes": "BRITISH CANT NEGATIVE-CONTROL FALSIFICATION (Donor-Lexicon Audit): Primary scan inspection of Hotten (slangdictionary00hottgoog, leaf n5 printed title ''1874''; leaf n213 p. 202; leaf n355 p. 343), Barrère & Leland Vol. II (slangjargoncant02barriala, 1890, 0 hits), and Farmer & Henley Vol. VII (slangitsanalogue07farmuoft, leaf n4 printed title ''MCMIV'' [1904]; leaf n375 p. 372 col. 2 jumping directly from ''YEACK'' and ''YEAR''S-MIND'' to ''YELLOW'') proves two decisive points: (1) British thieves'' cant ''yack'' (''a watch'') is a semantic false friend unrelated to beggars or safecrackers; (2) even as late as 1904, British slang lexicography lacked ''yegg'' altogether, confirming North American railroad genesis.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-310.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1874 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1500-301', 'TGT-132', 'yegg', NULL, 'yegg', 'yagar', 'hypothesis', 'supporting_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1875, '1875-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/pennsylvaniager00horngoog/page/n208/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-301.webp', 78728, 'e76e8c32f2110e7f169fb166e810298865a00acdcb6e09e51671cfb7a83c371e', '{"id": "CERT-1500-301", "slug": "cert-1500-301", "case_slug": "yegg", "headword": "yegg", "attested_form": "yagar", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "supporting_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1500-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1875, "date_display": "1875", "sort_date": "1875-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: yagar (Jacob Grimm & Wilhelm Grimm (ed. Moriz Heyne), Deutsches Wörterbuch, Vol. IV, Abt. 2 [H. I. J.] (Leipzig: S. Hirzel, 1877", "source_publication": "Jacob Grimm & Wilhelm Grimm (ed. Moriz Heyne), Deutsches Wörterbuch, Vol. IV, Abt. 2 [H. I. J.] (Leipzig: S. Hirzel, 1877), cols. 2217–2220 (leaf n1120, ''JÄGER, m.''); Abraham Reeser Horne, Pennsylvania German Manual, for Pronouncing, Speaking and Writing English (Kutztown, PA: 1875; rev. ed. Allentown, PA: National Educator Print, 1896), Part III, p. 199 (leaf n208, ''yagar, hunter, jaeger'') & Part IV, p. 229 (leaf n238, ''hunt, yawga, hunta, dorchsucha; hunter, yagar'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/pennsylvaniager00horngoog/page/n208/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Grimm DWB Vol. IV.2 (1877), cols. 2217–2218 (leaf n1120); Horne Penn. German Manual (1896 [c.1875]), p. 199 col. 2 (leaf n208) & p. 229 col. 2 (leaf n238)", "verbatim_quote": "[Horne, Pennsylvania German Manual (1896 [c.1875]), p. 199:] yagăr, hunter, jaeger / yawgă, to hunt, jagen ... [p. 229:] hunt, yawgă, huntă, dŏrchsuchă [durchsuchen, ''to search through, ransack''] / hunter, yagăr / hunting, yawgd, huntă / huntsman, yagăr. [Grimm, Deutsches Wörterbuch, Vol. IV.2 (1877), col. 2218:] JÄGER, m. 1) der verfolgung und erlegung des wildes weidmännisch treibt, als vergnügen oder gewerbe: ahd. jagâri venator ... mhd. jäger, jeger; ... [col. 2220:] JAGEREI, f. (nach jager), das hetzen, eilen.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1875, "masthead_year": 1875, "linguistic_note": "High German Jäger [ˈjɛːɡɐ] / Pennsylvania German & German-American phonetic spelling yagar / yeager / yager ([ˈjeɪɡɚ ~ ˈjɛɡɚ], ''hunter, prowler, searcher/ransacker'', glossed alongside dŏrchsuchă ''to ransack/search through'' in Horne 1875/1896) -> borrowed into bilingual German-American rail-tramp cant as yeager (''railroad tramp who rides the rods and begs/forages, never walking or working'', Hartford Courant, Jan. 23, 1893) -> reanalyzed as English stem yegg- + agentive suffix -er -> back-formed noun/verb yegg (Cincinnati Enquirer, Jan. 14, 1894; Fort Wayne News, June 28, 1900), parallel to burglar -> burgle, peddler -> peddle, and hawker -> hawk.", "analysis_notes": "TIER 1 PRIMARY MORPHOPHONOLOGICAL DONOR PATHWAY (Donor-Lexicon Audit): Primary scans of Grimm DWB Vol. IV.2 (deutscheswrter42grimuoft, leaf n8 printed title ''1877''; leaf n1120 cols. 2217–2218 ''JÄGER'') and A. R. Horne''s Pennsylvania German Manual (pennsylvaniager00horngoog, leaf n9 printed title ''1896'' [verso leaf n10 ''Copyright 1875'']; leaf n208 p. 199 & leaf n238 p. 229) establish two decisive facts: (1) Pennsylvania German already spelled Jäger with initial English ''y-'' as ''yagăr'' ([ˈjɛɡɐ / ˈjæɡɚ]) and equated the verb ''yawgă'' (jagen) with ''dŏrchsuchă'' (''to search, to ransack'', p. 143/leaf n153); (2) stripping perceived English agentive ''-er'' from hobo ''yeager'' (attested Jan. 23, 1893, Hartford Courant) / ''yegger'' yields stem ''yeg-''/''yegg'' (matching palatal-velar consonant skeleton) directly.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-301.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1875 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1500-308', 'TGT-132', 'yegg', NULL, 'yegg', 'yag', 'hypothesis', 'weakened_or_unlikely_pathway', 'agent_verified', 1875, '1875-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/dialectenglishg00crofgoog/page/n178/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-308.webp', 55684, 'c4a9d71703d82a2e72a26508980cf8dc30cfd7bca3f032c97e35941739fecf85', '{"id": "CERT-1500-308", "slug": "cert-1500-308", "case_slug": "yegg", "headword": "yegg", "attested_form": "yag", "record_type": "hypothesis", "record_subtype": "weakened_or_unlikely_pathway", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1500-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1875, "date_display": "1875", "sort_date": "1875-01-01", "short_gloss": "Hypothesis: yag (Bath C. Smart & Henry Thomas Crofton, The Dialect of the English Gypsies, 2nd ed., rev. and greatly enlarged (London: Asher", "source_publication": "Bath C. Smart & Henry Thomas Crofton, The Dialect of the English Gypsies, 2nd ed., rev. and greatly enlarged (London: Asher and Co., 1875), Gypsy-English Vocabulary, p. 155 (leaf n178, ''Yog, n., Fire. Pasp., yag; Yogéngro ... Gun; Gamekeeper; Lucifer matches'' & ''yékera, as one''); Charles Godfrey Leland, The Gypsies (Boston: Houghton, Mifflin and Company, 1882), p. 169 (leaf n176, ''rinkno yāg-engree [pretty gun]'')", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/dialectenglishg00crofgoog/page/n178/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Smart & Crofton (1875), p. 155 (leaf n178); Leland, The Gypsies (1882), p. 169 (leaf n176)", "verbatim_quote": "[Smart & Crofton, Dialect of the English Gypsies (1875), p. 155:] Yov kom''d asar léndi doói sar yékera, He loved them both equally; lit., them both as one ... Yog, n., Fire. Pasp., yag / Yog-chik, Ashes; lit., fire-dirt / Yogéngro, Yogéngri, Yógoméngri, Yógoméskro, n., Gun / Yogéngri-choóko, Shooting-coat / Yógoméngro, Yogéngri gaújo, Yog-moosh, Gamekeeper / Yogéngries, n. pl., Lucifer matches. [Leland, The Gypsies (Boston, 1882), p. 169:] \"What a beautiful little rifle! That''s what I call a rinkno yāg-engree [pretty gun].\"", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1875, "masthead_year": 1875, "linguistic_note": "Anglo-Romani & American Romani yag / yog ([jaɡ / jɒɡ], ''fire, blaze'', < Sanskrit agni) and agentives yag-engro / yogéngro / yag-moosh (''fire-fellow, gun/rifle, gamekeeper, matches'', Smart & Crofton 1875 p. 155; attested in American Gypsy speech by Leland 1882 p. 169): exact consonant-skeleton match (WK == WK) to yegg, evaluated as a conditional phonological candidate for safe-blowing (''fire/powder'') or campfire-hobo cant, though weakened semantically because pre-1900 yegg/yeager (1893–1894) denoted a ''non-walking rail-tramp / beggar'' six years before specializing to nitroglycerine safe-blowers (1900).", "analysis_notes": "TIER 3 CONDITIONAL PHONOLOGICAL-HYPOTHESIS WITH SEMANTIC-CHRONOLOGICAL CAVEAT (Donor-Lexicon Audit): Primary scans of Smart & Crofton (1875, leaf n4 printed title ''1875''; leaf n178 p. 155) and Leland''s American Romani chapters in The Gypsies (1882, leaf n8 printed title ''1882''; leaf n176 p. 169) verify that Anglo-Romani/U.S. Romani ''yag'' / ''yog'' (''fire'') shares a matching palatal-velar consonant structure and formed compounds for gunmen/gamekeepers (''yog-moosh'', ''yogéngro'', ''yāg-engree''). Nevertheless, semantic alignment fails for the primary 1893–1894 sense because ''yeager'' (1893) and ''yegg'' (1894) first meant ''rail-riding beggar/moucher'', NOT ''fire-blower/gunman''—making German/PG Jäger/yagar -> yeager -> yegg (CERT-1500-301) far stronger for the 1893–1894 origin, with Romani yag (''fire'') at most a secondary folk-associative reinforcement once safecrackers adopted nitroglycerine (''soup''/''fire'').", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-308.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1875 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1500-306', 'TGT-132', 'yegg', NULL, 'yegg', 'Yik', 'rejected_lead', 'lexicon_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1877, '1877-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/cantonesemadeeas00ballrich/page/n164/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-306.webp', 53670, '0c0787a14fd23e295e30e93bc0c9c7a9efc0c34e43cf73269b2700482df7be2c', '{"id": "CERT-1500-306", "slug": "cert-1500-306", "case_slug": "yegg", "headword": "yegg", "attested_form": "Yik", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "lexicon_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1500-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1877, "date_display": "1877", "sort_date": "1877-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Yik (Ernest John Eitel, A Chinese Dictionary in the Cantonese Dialect (London: Trübner and Co.; Hongkong: Lane, Crawford & Co", "source_publication": "Ernest John Eitel, A Chinese Dictionary in the Cantonese Dialect (London: Trübner and Co.; Hongkong: Lane, Crawford & Co., 1877), Title Page (PR3) & p. 995 col. 2 (''Ye see I. / Yik.''); J. Dyer Ball, Cantonese Made Easy, 2nd ed. (Hongkong: ''China Mail'' Office, 1888), p. 125 (leaf n164, ''Ko'' hat yi ... beggar''); Charles G. Leland, Pidgin-English Sing-Song; or, Songs and Stories in the China-English Dialect, with a Vocabulary (London: Trübner & Co., 1876), p. 127 (leaf n138, ''La-li-loong, a thief'')", "source_archive": "Google Books / Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/cantonesemadeeas00ballrich/page/n164/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Eitel (1877), Title PR3 & p. 995 col. 2; Ball (1888), p. 125 (leaf n164); Leland (1876), p. 127 (leaf n138)", "verbatim_quote": "[Eitel, A Chinese Dictionary in the Cantonese Dialect (1877), p. 995, col. 2:] Ye see I. // Yik. 億 K. name of a number (= 10 萬 mán²), a hundred thousand, an indefinite number; peaceful; to measure, calculate; satisfy (供億 kung yik); gamble; i.w. 臆 yik, the breast, feelings. [J. Dyer Ball, Cantonese Made Easy (1888), p. 125:] 個乞兒係邊處 Ko'' hat yi hai'' pin shü''? Where is the beggar? [C. G. Leland, Pidgin-English Sing-Song (1876), Vocabulary, p. 127:] La-li-loong, a thief; thieves. \"The barber complained he had been called a la-li-loong, the Pidgin-English for thief.\"—Celestial Empire, 1876.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1877, "masthead_year": 1877, "linguistic_note": "Independent cross-verification across Eitel (1877), Ball (1888), and Leland''s China-English Pidgin glossary (1876) confirming that *yek does not exist as a Cantonese syllable heading in Eitel (p. 995 jumps directly from ''Yéung'' and ''Ye see I.'' to ''Yik.''), colloquial Cantonese expresses ''beggar'' as hat yi (乞兒, KT, ), and 19th-century Chinese Pidgin English expressed ''thief'' as la-li-loong (LN, )—exposing ''Cantonese yek = beggar/thief'' as a complete Orientalist / underworld ghost root.", "analysis_notes": "DEFINITIVE CANTONESE & PIDGIN GHOST-ROOT KILL II (Donor-Lexicon Audit): Primary scan of E. J. Eitel''s 1877 Hongkong lexicon (Google Books mh8TAAAAYAAJ, PR3 printed title ''1877''; PA995 p. 995 col. 2) shows the exact alphabetical transition from ''Yéung'' -> ''Ye see I.'' -> ''Yik.'' with zero ''*Yek'' headword, while J. Dyer Ball''s Cantonese Made Easy (cantonesemadeeas00ballrich, leaf n8 printed title ''1888''; leaf n164 p. 125) and Charles G. Leland''s Pidgin-English Sing-Song (pidginenglishsin00lelaiala, leaf n8 printed title ''1876''; leaf n138 p. 127) prove that neither Cantonese (hat yi ''beggar'', ts''ák ''thief'') nor Pacific-coast/treaty-port Pidgin English (la-li-loong ''thief'' < Cantonese lá-li-lung) had any form resembling yek/yegg for ''beggar'' or ''thief''.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-306.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1877 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO citations (id, target_id, case_slug, run_id, headword, attested_form, record_type, record_subtype, verification_level, publication_year, sort_date, source_url, masthead_date_verified, scan_hosted_path, scan_bytes, scan_sha256, record_json, updated_at) VALUES ('CERT-1500-309', 'TGT-132', 'yegg', NULL, 'yegg', 'Réspun', 'rejected_lead', 'lexicon_falsification', 'agent_verified', 1882, '1882-01-01', 'https://archive.org/details/gypsies00lela/page/365/mode/1up', 1, '/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-309.webp', 66282, 'f81123ba158551de4d92f78541bb3b8a98e9059771e478ed407d53cf6d17ebb9', '{"id": "CERT-1500-309", "slug": "cert-1500-309", "case_slug": "yegg", "headword": "yegg", "attested_form": "Réspun", "record_type": "rejected_lead", "record_subtype": "lexicon_falsification", "verification_level": "agent_verified", "verified_by_agent": "DRAgNEt 1500-C", "human_verified_by": null, "human_verified_date": null, "external_recognition": null, "publication_year": 1882, "date_display": "1882", "sort_date": "1882-01-01", "short_gloss": "Rejected Lead: Réspun (Charles Godfrey Leland, The Gypsies (Boston: Houghton, Mifflin and Company, 1882), ''Shelta, the Tinkers'' Talk'', pp. 3", "source_publication": "Charles Godfrey Leland, The Gypsies (Boston: Houghton, Mifflin and Company, 1882), ''Shelta, the Tinkers'' Talk'', pp. 354–372 (leaf n372, p. 365); John Sampson, ''Two Shelta Stories'' & Kuno Meyer, ''On the Irish Origin and the Age of Shelta'', Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society, Old Series Vol. II (1890–1891) & Vol. III (Edinburgh: T. & A. Constable, 1892), Vol. III Title (leaf n8) & pp. 23–26 (leaf n34)", "source_archive": "Internet Archive", "source_url": "https://archive.org/details/gypsies00lela/page/365/mode/1up", "page_and_column": "Leland, The Gypsies (1882), p. 365 (leaf n372); Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society, Vol. III (1891–1892), p. 23 (leaf n34)", "verbatim_quote": "[Leland, The Gypsies (1882), ''Shelta, the Tinkers'' Talk'', p. 365:] Shaidyog, Policeman. / Réspun, To steal. / Shoich, Water, blood, liquid. / Alemnoch, Milk. / Räglan, or réglan, Hammer. / Goppa, Furnace, smith (gobha, a smith. Gaelic). ... Chaldroch, Knife ... Kiéna, House (ken, old gypsy and modern cant). [J. Sampson, ''Two Shelta Stories'', J. Gypsy Lore Soc. Vol. III (1891–92), p. 23:] IV.—TWO SHELTA STORIES. GLOX SHAROG NA SRŌÏNYA. The Red Man of the Boyne.", "masthead_date_verified": true, "catalog_year": 1882, "masthead_year": 1882, "linguistic_note": "Falsifies vague secondary conjectures attributing yegg to Shelta (Irish tinkers'' cant): exhaustive audit of all pre-1900 published Shelta vocabularies and texts (Leland 1882 pp. 354–372, Sampson 1890–1891, Kuno Meyer 1891) finds zero Shelta word *yegg, *yag, or *yek meaning ''beggar'', ''tramp'', ''thief'', or ''safecracker'' (Shelta ''to steal'' = réspun / kimp [LSPN, ]; ''tramp'' = strepuk [STLPK, ]; ''man'' = glox [KLK, ]).", "analysis_notes": "FALSIFIED-MYTH SHELTA NEGATIVE AUDIT (Donor-Lexicon Audit): Primary scan inspection of Leland''s foundational Shelta glossary collected from an Irish-English road tramp (gypsies00lela, leaf n8 ''1882''; leaf n372 p. 365) and John Sampson''s Ulster/Liverpool tinker texts in Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society Old Series Vol. III (journalofgypsyl03gypsuoft, leaf n8 printed title ''1892'' covering July 1891–April 1892; leaf n34 p. 23; Trap A caught and avoided on New Series Vol. I [1907–08] cataloged on IA as ''1888'') confirms zero Shelta donor for yegg.", "scan": {"hosted_path": "/scans/yegg/CERT-1500-309.webp", "rights_status": "us_public_domain_pre_1931", "rights_note": "Published 1882 (<= 1930): public domain in the United States; WebP proof scan hosted directly."}}', '2026-11-27T05:35:25Z');
